var e={"sequim-wa":{why:`Southwesterlies lift Pacific air up the Olympic Range, wringing rain and snow onto the Hoh and Quinault before the same air descends, compresses, and dries toward the Dungeness prairie. Sequim sits in that lee, a band perhaps 30 km wide of genuinely Mediterranean summers on a coast that is otherwise rainforest. The Strait of Juan de Fuca still moderates every season: winters stay milder than the interior, summers cooler than eastern Washington. Elevation near 55 m does not make the climate. The mountain wall and the strait do. Nearby Forks, on the windward slope, is the same latitude and a different planet of rain.`,history:[`In 1977 a Sequim resident named Emanuel Manis was digging a pond on his property when his backhoe turned up a pair of mastodon tusks. Archaeologists who came to look found something stranger: a bone projectile point lodged in one of the animal's ribs. Dated to roughly 13,800 years ago, the Manis Mastodon is one of the oldest records of people hunting in North America, and it was found on the same dry prairie that S'Klallam families later burned each autumn to keep camas meadows open and deer browse fresh. The town's name comes from a S'Klallam word usually rendered as a place for going to shoot — a hunting ground, then as now.`,`George Vancouver sailed past in 1792 and, thinking the long sand spit off the river mouth looked like the shingle at Dungeness in Kent, named it New Dungeness. The 1855 Treaty of Point No Point took most of the land and reserved fishing rights; settler fences did the rest. In 1874 a S'Klallam leader known as Lord James Balch persuaded families to pool their savings and buy 210 acres on the bay outright rather than accept removal, and Jamestown still stands on that purchase. Meanwhile homesteaders discovered the catch of the rain shadow: glorious sun and almost no water. In 1895 a farmer the neighbours nicknamed Crazy Callen talked them into digging an irrigation ditch from the Dungeness River, and on 1 May 1896 water spilled onto the prairie. Sequim has thrown a party for that day ever since — the Irrigation Festival, the oldest continuously running festival in Washington.`,`Ditches turned the prairie into dairy country for most of the next century. John Wayne fell for Sequim Bay while anchoring his converted minesweeper, the Wild Goose, and his family later gave the land that became the John Wayne Marina. When milk prices sank in the 1990s, growers noticed that the same dry, cool summers that suit a cow pasture suit Provençal lavender even better; the first Lavender Festival in 1997 turned purple fields into the town's postcard. Retirees chasing sunshine followed, and the old farm grid filled with cul-de-sacs. The Jamestown S'Klallam Tribe, federally recognised again in 1981, is now one of the area's largest employers — and the snowpack that feeds the ditches is shrinking, which makes every summer a negotiation over the river.`],deepTitle:`Ditch and lavender`,deep:[`The Manis site rewrote a textbook. In the 1970s the reigning story held that the first Americans were Clovis big-game hunters who arrived around 13,000 years ago; a hunted mastodon in Washington, centuries older, was hard to square with that. The bone point was argued over for decades until a 2011 re-analysis using CT scans and protein evidence confirmed it was made from mastodon bone and driven into a living animal. The prairie where the find was made is unremarkable to look at — which is part of the point. People have been reading this rain-shadow lowland as good hunting ground for a very long time.`,`Lord James Balch earned his English nickname from settlers; his own people knew him as a persuasive headman who could see where the land rush was going. The 1874 purchase was an act of quiet defiance: by buying title under the newcomers' own rules, the families kept a shoreline village while other S'Klallam bands were pushed toward reservations far from home. Jamestown sat outside federal recognition for more than a century, running its affairs on that private land until the tribe regained its government-to-government standing in 1981. Today it operates a health clinic, a casino resort at Blyn, and salmon-restoration work on the very river the settlers ditched.`,`D.R. Callen's ditch was dug largely by hand with plows, horses and stubbornness, and the first irrigation company was soon joined by a dozen more until the valley was laced with laterals carrying snowmelt from the Olympics onto the outwash gravels. The Irrigation Festival began as a picnic for the ditch-diggers in 1896 and grew a parade, a royalty court and a logging show; it has run, by local count, every spring since. The ditches mattered because the rain shadow is a genuine anomaly: the town averages well under a fifth of the rain that soaks the Hoh rainforest on the other side of the same mountains.`,`Lavender arrived as an experiment. A handful of farms planted it in the mid-1990s, the 1997 festival drew crowds, and within a decade Sequim was billing itself as the lavender capital of North America, with July weekends that fill every motel on the peninsula. Behind the purple there is arithmetic. The Dungeness carries less water in late summer than it once did, salmon runs are listed, and farmers, tribes and new subdivisions have spent years hammering out water-sharing agreements. The sunny pocket that sells the town is small, and everyone who lives in it is drawing on the same mountain snow.`]},"portal-az":{why:`The Chiricahuas rise about 1,700 m above the San Simon and Animas basins, intercepting winter Pacific storms and summer monsoon moisture on a Madrean sky island. Lapse-rate cooling drops summit temperatures by roughly 10 °C relative to the surrounding desert, and orographic lift roughly doubles the rain. Portal itself sits in Cave Creek Canyon, where shelter and cold-air drainage off the peaks make a climate distinct from both the creosote floor and the fir-and-aspen crest. The village is not a generic Arizona basin town. It is the desert's front porch to a forested mountain, with a long drive for groceries as the everyday price.`,history:[`On the morning of 4 September 1886, Geronimo rode into Skeleton Canyon in the Peloncillo Mountains, a day's ride east of where Portal now stands, and surrendered to General Nelson Miles. It was the end of the Apache wars and of Chiricahua freedom in the range that still carries their name; within days the band was on a train to Florida. For centuries before that, the Chiricahuas had been a refuge and a pantry. Their canyons held water when the San Simon flats did not, the oak and pine slopes fed deer and acorn gathering, and the peaks served as lookouts over every approach. Earlier Mogollon farmers had used the same staircase of climates long before anyone wrote down a treaty.`,`The vacuum filled quickly and not always respectably. North of Cave Creek, near Turkey Creek, the silver camp of Galeyville boomed in 1880 and briefly became a hangout for Curly Bill Brocius and the rustlers known as the Cowboys, the same crowd that would end up on the wrong side of the Earps in Tombstone. Stephen Reed's family built one of the first settler cabins in the canyon in 1879, and the mining camp of Paradise sprang up after 1900 with its own saloons and dreams of copper that never quite paid. Portal, at the canyon mouth, took its name from being exactly that — the doorway into the mountains — and grew into a post office, a store and a scattering of ranches.`,`The scientists came in 1955, when the American Museum of Natural History bought the old Painted Canyon Ranch with help from David Rockefeller and opened the Southwestern Research Station. The attraction was the climate staircase itself: in a single drive you climb from creosote desert to Douglas-fir, crossing half a dozen life zones that elsewhere are separated by hundreds of kilometres. Birders discovered the elegant trogon, a coppery tropical bird at the northern tip of its range, and Cave Creek became a pilgrimage. In 2011 the Horseshoe Two fire burned roughly 90,000 hectares of the range; the floods and debris flows that followed now shape the canyon's year as much as the birds do.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Chiricahua Apache were not one tribe but several bands — the Chokonen under Cochise, the Bedonkohe and Chihenne, the Nednhi of the Sierra Madre — and these mountains sat at the centre of their world. After Cochise made peace in 1872, the government promised a reservation here, then broke it up in 1876 and moved people north to San Carlos. That decision set off the decade of breakouts, raids and pursuits that ended in Skeleton Canyon. The descendants of those prisoners of war still live in Oklahoma and New Mexico; the mountains they were taken from now draw visitors from around the world.`,`Galeyville lasted only a couple of years. John Galey's smelter failed, the ore was thinner than promised, and the town's most enduring reputation was as a place where stolen Mexican cattle changed hands and nobody asked questions. Paradise, its successor up the canyon, had a brief copper-and-silver fling after 1901 and then dwindled to a few cabins, a famous birding lodge and a cemetery. Neither town ever made the money Tombstone or Bisbee did, which is partly why the canyon kept its sycamores and its cliffs instead of tailings piles.`,`The research station turned a dead-end ranch road into one of the most-cited addresses in field biology. Generations of graduate students have studied spiders, snakes, bats, moths and frogs here precisely because the mountain packs so many climates into so little horizontal space. The station's records also captured the arrival of new problems: drier winters, hotter Junes and longer fire seasons in a range where the forest on the peaks is already a relic of a cooler past.`,`Living in Portal means accepting distance. Groceries, hospitals and most services are an hour or more away, across the state line in New Mexico or out on the interstate. In exchange you get dark skies, a canyon that funnels cool air down from the peaks at dawn, and the odd experience of watching a monsoon storm pour onto one ridge while the house stays dry. After the 2011 burn, residents also learned to read the creek: a big July storm on the blackened slopes can send a wall of mud and boulders down a channel that was dry an hour earlier.`]},"huachuca-az":{why:`About 1,500 m of relief, monsoon-season orographic lift, and a living biogeographic continuity with Mexico's Sierra Madre stack climate zones from San Pedro mesquite to Miller Peak fir in a compact range. The river corridor adds a rare hydrologic exception to an otherwise arid basin: cottonwood-willow gallery forest beside a desert floor that still cooks in June. Sierra Vista and the fort sit on the bajada, not on the crest, so summer heat is real even while the canyons hold Mexican-affinity birds. The Huachucas are a sky island with a military town at the door, not a generic Arizona valley.`,seasons:{summer:`June is the furnace month — afternoons near 35°C, the grass bleached to straw, every ridge a fire risk. Then the monsoon breaks, usually in early July: anvil clouds stack over Miller Peak by noon, thunder rolls down Ramsey and Miller canyons, and the evening air smells of wet creosote. Nights stay mild near 18°C, and the canyons fill with hummingbirds riding the rains north from Mexico.`},history:[`Thirteen thousand years ago, hunters with fluted Clovis points waited at the marshy edges of the San Pedro River for mammoths to come and drink. At the Lehner and Murray Springs sites, a few kilometres from today's Sierra Vista, archaeologists have found the bones of the animals they killed, the stone points that killed them, and even a bone tool for straightening spear shafts. The river valley has been a highway ever since: Sobaipuri O'odham farmers grew corn along its banks, and in 1540 Francisco Vázquez de Coronado probably marched north up this valley looking for the Seven Cities of Gold — a memorial now marks the pass at the range's southern tip. The mountains' name is popularly glossed as place of thunder, which anyone who has sat through a July afternoon here will find easy to believe.`,`On 3 March 1877, Captain Samuel Whitside led two companies of the 6th Cavalry into a well-watered canyon mouth and set up tents. The aim was to watch the Apache routes into Sonora and protect settlers in the San Pedro valley. Camp Huachuca became a fort in 1882, played its part in the final Geronimo campaign of 1886, and then did something most frontier posts failed to do: it survived. The border kept it useful. From 1913 the 10th Cavalry — the Buffalo Soldiers — made it their home for nearly two decades, and Colonel Charles Young, the first Black officer to reach that rank, served here in the era of the 1916 expedition into Mexico after Pancho Villa.`,`During the Second World War, Fort Huachuca became one of the main training bases for Black soldiers in the segregated army, hosting both the 92nd and 93rd Infantry Divisions. After the war it reinvented itself as an electronics and intelligence centre, and Sierra Vista grew up around its gate as the fort's civilian twin. The same decades brought birders to Ramsey Canyon, where Mexican-affinity hummingbirds earned the city its claim as the hummingbird capital of the United States, and in 1988 the San Pedro Riparian National Conservation Area was set aside to protect one of the last living rivers of the desert Southwest. The question that now ties the base, the town and the birds together is the aquifer under the valley.`],deepTitle:`Fort and river`,deep:[`The Murray Springs site is unusual because it preserves a single bad day for a family of mammoths in remarkable detail. Researchers found butchered bones, a mammoth trackway pressed into the old spring mud, a bison kill nearby and a campsite where the hunters resharpened their points. A black organic layer, nicknamed the black mat, seals the whole scene and marks a sudden climate shift around 12,900 years ago that coincides with the disappearance of the mammoths and the end of the Clovis toolkit across the continent. The San Pedro, in other words, is one of the places where scientists argue about how the ice age ended.`,`The Old Post at Fort Huachuca, with its adobe and stone quarters around a shaded parade ground, is among the best-preserved Indian Wars cantonments in the country and a National Historic Landmark. The Buffalo Soldiers who lived there patrolled a border that was violent in both directions; Black troopers fought at Nogales in 1918 in one of the last real skirmishes on the line. The fort's museum keeps their story in the middle of an active base — visitors drive past drone hangars and intelligence schools to reach it.`,`Ramsey Canyon's fame came from a quirk of geography. The Huachucas are the first high, wet mountains north of the border, so species that live in the Sierra Madre of Mexico — the elegant trogon, Rivoli's and blue-throated hummingbirds, painted redstarts — ride the canyons north each spring. The Nature Conservancy has looked after the Ramsey Canyon preserve since the 1970s, and birders keep life lists here that would be impossible almost anywhere else in the United States.`,`Every one of those stories depends on water. The fort was sited where the canyon springs ran; the river's cottonwoods depend on groundwater that the growing city also pumps. For decades the Army, Sierra Vista and conservation groups have argued, sued and finally cooperated over recharge projects and pumping limits, because a dried-up San Pedro would hurt all three. In a warming climate with hotter Junes and less reliable monsoons, that bargain is going to be renegotiated again.`]},"monterey-ca":{why:`The California Current and intense summer upwelling keep sea-surface temperatures near 12 °C even in August. Cold water under warmer air aloft builds a durable marine inversion, capping a 300–600 m stratus layer that owns the peninsula's summer. Inland valleys a short drive away escape the marine layer and heat normally, one of the continent's sharpest short-distance climate gradients. Fog and kelp are not scenery. They are the pantry and the thermostat. The open Pacific at the head of Monterey Canyon, with the Santa Lucias rising south, is the whole mechanism.`,history:[`Monterey began with a sales pitch. In 1602 the Spanish explorer Sebastián Vizcaíno anchored here, named the bay for the Count of Monterrey, and reported a magnificent harbour sheltered from every wind. When Gaspar de Portolá marched up the coast in 1769 to claim it, the description was so inflated that he stood on the shore, failed to recognise the place, and kept walking — stumbling instead onto San Francisco Bay. He came back the next year, and on 3 June 1770 he and Father Junípero Serra founded the presidio and mission on land where Rumsen Ohlone families had lived for thousands of years, harvesting mussels, abalone, acorns and the endless fish of a cold, upwelling sea.`,`For three-quarters of a century Monterey was the capital of Alta California, first Spanish and then Mexican, with a custom house where every ship trading on the coast had to pay its duties. On 7 July 1846 Commodore John Sloat raised the American flag over that custom house, and three years later delegates crowded into the new Colton Hall to write California's first constitution. Robert Louis Stevenson turned up in 1879, broke and lovesick, to chase the woman he would marry, and wrote about the town's fog and adobe. By then Chinese fishermen had built a village at Point Alones and pioneered night fishing for squid by torchlight, and Italian, Japanese and Portuguese crews were following them onto the water.`,`Sardines made the twentieth century. Canneries lined Ocean View Avenue, reduction plants filled the air with a smell residents called the smell of money, and Ed Ricketts collected tide-pool creatures in his lab between them while his friend John Steinbeck turned the whole scene into Cannery Row in 1945. Within a few years the sardines were gone, fished out as the ocean shifted under them. The street took Steinbeck's title in 1958, the Monterey Jazz Festival started that same year, and in June 1967 Jimi Hendrix set his guitar on fire at the Monterey Pop Festival. In 1984 the Monterey Bay Aquarium opened inside the shell of the old Hovden cannery, and the cold water that once fed the canneries became the town's main attraction.`],deepTitle:`Presidio to aquarium`,deep:[`Mission San Carlos Borromeo did not stay in Monterey for long. Serra moved it to the Carmel River in 1771, partly to get his converts away from the soldiers at the presidio and partly because the river valley had better soil and water. The mission records that survive there read as both a religious archive and a demographic catastrophe: baptisms of Rumsen and Esselen people, marriages, and then, year after year, burials as disease and forced labour took their toll. Serra himself is buried beneath the church floor in Carmel. Rumsen descendants still live on the peninsula and in the valleys behind it.`,`Mexican Monterey was a small, cosmopolitan port where Yankee traders married into Californio families, hides and tallow went out by the shipload, and the wide-verandahed Monterey Colonial style was born when New England carpentry met adobe walls. Thomas Larkin's house, built in the 1830s, is the classic example. Walter Colton, the first American alcalde, co-founded the state's first newspaper here in 1846 and then built the stone hall that hosted the constitutional convention, paying for it partly with fines levied on gamblers and drunks.`,`The Chinese village at Point Alones burned in 1906 under circumstances that many in the community considered no accident, and its residents were pushed off land the Pacific Improvement Company wanted for development. The sardine canneries that followed employed thousands, many of them immigrant women working shifts timed to the arrival of the boats. When the catch collapsed, scientists argued for years over whether overfishing or a natural ocean cycle was to blame; the honest answer is both, and the debate helped give birth to modern fisheries science.`,`Today the peninsula runs on salt water and language schools. The Naval Postgraduate School occupies the grand old Hotel Del Monte, the Defense Language Institute teaches dozens of languages on the presidio hill, and the Monterey Bay National Marine Sanctuary, created in 1992, protects the deep canyon offshore that makes the upwelling possible. Fort Ord's closure in 1994 turned a vast army base into a university and a national monument. The fog that once kept tourists away now sells a cool-summer refuge, and housing on the peninsula is priced accordingly.`]},"black-mountain-nc":{why:`On still nights the Southern Appalachians drain cold air into cove bottoms, and the Swannanoa is a textbook case. Mid-slope homes near 700–900 m often stay several degrees warmer than the valley floor on radiative winter mornings, with a growing season a few weeks longer — the thermal belt orchardists settled on purpose. The Black Mountains, including Mount Mitchell, add lapse-rate cooling and extra orographic rain just to the north. Summer is still humid Carolina, not New England. The difference is a slope that can hold bloom when the hollow frosts, and a river that can still flood the same town.`,history:[`In June 1857 Elisha Mitchell, a stubborn professor from the University of North Carolina, set out alone across the Black Mountains to settle an argument. He had measured the range's highest summit years earlier and declared it the tallest peak east of the Mississippi; a former student turned congressman, Thomas Clingman, claimed the honour for himself and a different peak. Mitchell slipped at a waterfall in the dark and drowned in the pool below. His body was later reburied on the summit of the mountain that now bears his name, and he lies there still, at 2,037 m, in the spruce-fir forest that gives the Black Mountains their name and their colour.`,`Long before the surveyors, the Swannanoa valley was a Cherokee route east, and its name is usually traced to a trail toward the country of the Suwali people. In 1776 General Griffith Rutherford's militia came through the gap to burn Cherokee towns, and treaties soon pushed the boundary west. The real change came with the railroad. Convict labourers, many of them Black men leased from the state, blasted the Swannanoa Tunnel through the Blue Ridge in 1879 at a terrible cost in lives, and once trains could climb the escarpment, Black Mountain grew up around the depot selling the one thing the lowlands lacked — cool summer air.`,`Churches bought that air in bulk. A Congregationalist-led assembly founded Montreat in 1897 (the Presbyterians took it over a decade later), the YMCA opened its Blue Ridge Assembly in 1906, and the Baptists built Ridgecrest soon after, turning the valley into a kind of Protestant Chautauqua. Billy Graham made his home in Montreat for more than seventy years. In 1933 something entirely different arrived: Black Mountain College, an experimental school where Josef and Anni Albers taught design, John Cage staged what is often called the first happening, and Buckminster Fuller tried to raise his first geodesic dome. It closed in 1957 but its influence on American art is enormous. In September 2024, Hurricane Helene's rains sent the Swannanoa through the valley in a flood that tore away homes and roads, a reminder that a mild mountain town still sits in a steep, wet watershed.`],deepTitle:`Assembly and college`,deep:[`The Mitchell–Clingman feud was a very Victorian fight — about barometric readings, priority and pride. Mitchell had first climbed into the range in 1835 and used a barometer to estimate heights; Clingman, a politician with a talent for publicity, claimed a higher measurement in the 1850s. Mitchell's fatal trip was meant to prove his original route. Later surveys confirmed that the summit he had measured was indeed the highest, and Clingman got a consolation prize in the Smokies: Clingmans Dome. Mitchell Falls, where he died, is still marked on trail maps below the crest.`,`The Swannanoa Tunnel was the hardest piece of the Western North Carolina Railroad. Work crews of convicts dug from both ends through hard rock, sometimes by candlelight, and collapses and disease killed many of them; the exact toll was never properly recorded. When the headings met in 1879 the railway could finally reach Asheville, and Black Mountain's boarding houses, inns and later its summer conference grounds all trace back to that hole in the mountain.`,`Black Mountain College began in 1933 in borrowed buildings at the YMCA's Blue Ridge Assembly, then moved to its own campus at Lake Eden in 1941, where students helped build the classrooms and grow the food. The roster reads like a history of postwar American culture: Robert Rauschenberg, Ruth Asawa, Cy Twombly, Willem de Kooning for a summer, the poet Charles Olson as rector in the final years. Fuller's first dome, built from venetian-blind slats in 1948, famously sagged instead of rising — students called it the supine dome — but he was back the following summer with a version that stood.`,`The orchards and truck farms that once used the thermal belt — the band of mid-slope ground where frost on still nights slides past to the valley floor — have mostly given way to houses, galleries and breweries, and the town now functions as Asheville's quieter neighbour. Helene's flood in 2024 showed the other side of the valley's geography: a short, steep river that can rise metres in hours. Recovery, insurance and the price of a cottage above the flood line are now as much a part of local conversation as the autumn colour.`]},"bishop-ca":{why:`The Sierra Nevada rises more than 3,000 m immediately west, wringing Pacific storms on the windward slope, then dumping dry, compressed air into the Owens Valley so Bishop sits in a bright rain shadow at the foot of 4,000-metre granite. Yearly precipitation drops from roughly 550 mm at Mammoth Lakes, much of it as deep snow, to about 130 mm at Bishop only some 50 km away. Nights cool hard because the valley is a high desert trench, not a coastal plain. The same topography that makes the light famous also made the river worth stealing. Elevation near 1,253 m stretches the diurnal range; it does not bring rain.`,history:[`On 5 November 1913, forty thousand people gathered at the foot of the San Fernando Valley to watch water from the Owens River pour down a concrete spillway. William Mulholland, the engineer who had built the Los Angeles Aqueduct, reportedly told the crowd: There it is. Take it. Some four hundred kilometres to the north, Bishop's farmers had just watched their river leave. Before the city came, the Owens Valley Paiute — the Nüümü — had already been irrigating it for generations, diverting Sierra creeks to water fields of wild seed and edible tubers, one of the few native irrigation systems recorded in the Great Basin.`,`Ranchers followed the snowmelt in the 1860s. A cattleman named Samuel Bishop drove his herd to a creek below the Sierra in 1861, and the creek — and later the town — took his name. Within two years the valley was at war: settlers and soldiers fought the Paiute through 1862 and 1863, and in July 1863 nearly a thousand people were marched south over the mountains to Fort Tejon. Many slipped back home in the following years, and Bishop grew into a ranching and supply town for the silver camps of the region, with orchards and alfalfa fields stretching across the valley floor.`,`Then Los Angeles quietly bought the land and the water rights to go with it. By the 1920s furious ranchers were dynamiting the aqueduct, and in November 1924 they seized the Alabama Gates and turned the city's water back into the river. The rebellion collapsed when the local bank failed in 1927 and its owners went to prison for embezzlement. Owens Lake dried into an alkali flat that became the largest single source of dust pollution in the United States. During the Second World War, Manzanar, an hour south, held some ten thousand Japanese Americans behind barbed wire. Bishop today is a climbing, fishing and packing town that throws the world's biggest party for mules every Memorial Day.`],deepTitle:`Water war`,deep:[`The story of how Los Angeles got the Owens River is a tale of insiders. Fred Eaton, a former mayor, bought ranches and options in the valley while a federal reclamation engineer, Joseph Lippincott, was surveying the same water for a local irrigation project. Residents believed the government was coming to help them; instead the land ended up in the hands of the city. The aqueduct itself was an engineering triumph — gravity carries the water the entire way — and a political scar that the valley has never stopped picking at. The 1974 film Chinatown borrowed the outline of the story and invented the rest.`,`The so-called water wars were serious but rarely deadly. Men with dynamite blew holes in the aqueduct more than a dozen times between 1924 and 1927; armed ranchers occupied the Alabama Gates spillway for four days while their families brought picnics. The Watterson brothers, whose Inyo County Bank financed the resistance, were found to have been embezzling from it, and when their bank collapsed in 1927 so did much of the valley's ability to fight. Most of Bishop's farms were sold to the city within a few years.`,`Owens Lake was once a working inland sea, with steamboats hauling silver bullion from Cerro Gordo across its surface in the 1870s. By 1926 it was dry, and its windblown salts and heavy metals were drifting across the valley. Since the late 1990s Los Angeles has spent well over a billion dollars on dust control — shallow flooding, gravel blankets and managed vegetation — making the dry lake one of the most expensive pieces of land in the American West to keep still.`,`Bishop's identity now leans on its mountains. Climbers come for the granite boulders of the Buttermilks and the volcanic tablelands, anglers for the trout streams, and everyone eventually drives up to the White Mountains to see bristlecone pines that were already growing when the pyramids of Giza were built. Mule Days, started in 1969 to celebrate the packers who supply the backcountry, draws tens of thousands of visitors. Underneath it all, the city of Los Angeles still owns most of the valley's land, and the Paiute nations of Bishop, Big Pine and Lone Pine still press their own claims to the water.`]},"flagstaff-az":{why:`At 2,106 m on the Colorado Plateau, Flagstaff is a ponderosa island above the desert heat that defines most of Arizona. Elevation lapse, a monsoon-edge position, and the San Francisco Peaks together wring extra snow and summer storms; the town sees on the order of 560 mm of precipitation a year, nearly triple Phoenix. Summer afternoons stay warm rather than brutal, then nights drop hard under dry air and altitude. You feel the plateau, not the latitude. Drought in the pines and a fire-adapted forest are the bill for those cool summers.`,history:[`On the Fourth of July 1876, a party of travellers camped near a spring at the foot of the San Francisco Peaks stripped the branches off a tall ponderosa pine and ran an American flag up it to mark the country's hundredth birthday. The pole stood for years as a landmark for wagon trains, and when the Atlantic and Pacific Railroad arrived in 1882 the camp around it took the obvious name. The mountain above was already sacred ground. To the Hopi it is Nuvatukya'ovi, home of the katsinam who bring the rain; to the Diné it is Dook'o'oosłííd, the sacred mountain of the west. Eight centuries earlier, the eruption of Sunset Crater had blanketed the land with ash, and Sinagua farmers moved in to plant the fresh cinders around Wupatki.`,`The railroad turned the pines into money. The Riordan brothers built a lumber empire and a matching log-and-stone mansion, and in 1894 a wealthy Bostonian named Percival Lowell built an observatory on Mars Hill because the dry, thin air made the stars sharp. Lowell spent years sketching canals on Mars that did not exist, but his observatory did real science: Vesto Slipher measured the redshifts of distant galaxies there in the 1910s — early evidence that the universe is expanding — and on 18 February 1930 a young Kansas farm boy named Clyde Tombaugh found Pluto on a pair of photographic plates. To keep the skies dark, the town passed the world's first outdoor-lighting ordinance in 1958.`,`In the 1960s the US Geological Survey made Flagstaff its astrogeology headquarters, blasted craters into the Cinder Lake volcanic field to mimic the Moon, and trained Apollo astronauts on the lava flows nearby. Route 66 brought road-trippers, the normal school became Northern Arizona University, and in 2001 Flagstaff was named the world's first International Dark Sky City. The forest that built the town is now its greatest worry: a century of fire suppression left it crowded with fuel, and after the 2010 Schultz Fire sent floods through neighbourhoods below the Peaks, residents voted to tax themselves to thin national forest land — a first for any American city.`],deepTitle:`Pines and planets`,deep:[`Sunset Crater blew sometime around 1085, the youngest volcano in the San Francisco volcanic field. Rather than ruin the land, the ash acted as a mulch that held moisture in the soil, and for a century or so the area around Wupatki became one of the busiest farming districts on the plateau, with multistorey pueblos, a ball court and trade goods from as far away as Mexico. By around 1250 the villages had emptied, their people moving on to join the communities the Hopi count among their ancestors.`,`Lowell's obsession with Mars was both his folly and the observatory's engine. He hired Andrew Ellicott Douglass to scout sites, settled on Flagstaff, and spent his fortune on telescopes and publicity. Douglass, meanwhile, began counting tree rings in the local ponderosas and founded the science of dendrochronology. Lowell's other hunt, for a Planet X beyond Neptune, ended fourteen years after his death when Tombaugh blinked two photographs and saw a faint dot move. The observatory's director at the time cabled the news around the world, and the name Pluto was suggested by an eleven-year-old schoolgirl in Oxford.`,`Snow in Flagstaff is a serious business — the town is among the snowiest cities in the United States — and the Arizona Snowbowl ski area on the Peaks has spent decades in court with the Hopi, Navajo and other nations over expanding on sacred ground, and later over making artificial snow from reclaimed wastewater. The ski area won; the arguments over what can be done to a holy mountain have not gone away.`,`Since the Schultz Fire, the town's relationship with its forest has been rewritten. Thinning crews and prescribed burns now work the slopes above the city, the Museum Fire of 2019 and the Tunnel and Pipeline fires of 2022 tested the plans, and post-fire flooding has become a recurring summer hazard during the monsoon. Elevation still buys Flagstaff its cool July nights; keeping the ponderosa forest around those nights alive has become a civic project.`]},"silver-city-nm":{why:`Silver City sits where Madrean woodland meets Chihuahuan desert on a bench high enough that nights ease and monsoon storms stack on the Pinos Altos and Mogollon ranges. Elevation near 1,813 m supplies on the order of 10 °C of cooling relative to the basin, and the Gila–Mogollon uplift intercepts both winter Pacific storms and summer monsoon, producing enough rain for piñon-juniper and, with care, real gardens. The elevation is doing the air-conditioning; the mountains are doing the rain. Nearby mines and the Gila headwaters make that geography economic as well as scenic.`,history:[`Somewhere around the year 1000, potters in the Mimbres valley east of today's Silver City began painting bowls unlike anything else in the ancient Americas: black-on-white fish, cranes, bats, rabbits and people, drawn with a geometry and wit that collectors now pay fortunes for. Many were buried with the dead, upside down over the face, with a small hole punched through the bottom. The Mimbres villages faded by the 1100s, and the mountains passed to the Chihenne Apache, whose leader Mangas Coloradas fought the miners who swarmed into Pinos Altos after gold was found in 1860. He was seized under a flag of truce in 1863 and killed in custody.`,`Copper came first. In the early 1800s Spanish miners opened the Santa Rita del Cobre workings, reportedly after an Apache showed them the ore, and mule trains carried copper south toward the mint in Mexico City. Silver followed in 1870, and a boomtown spread across the hills. One of its residents was a skinny teenager named Henry McCarty, whose mother died of tuberculosis in town in 1874. The next year he was jailed for a petty theft and escaped up the chimney — his first jailbreak, years before the world knew him as Billy the Kid. The town also learned a harder lesson: after the hillsides were stripped of trees, floods in 1895 and the early 1900s tore Main Street into a gully more than 10 m deep, now a park known as the Big Ditch.`,`Grant County became one of the great copper districts of the West. The Chino open pit near Santa Rita grew so large that it eventually swallowed the town of Santa Rita itself, birthplace of Apollo 17 astronaut Harrison Schmitt. In 1950 Mexican-American zinc miners at nearby Hanover struck for equal pay and conditions, and when an injunction barred them from the picket line, their wives took their places; the story became Salt of the Earth, a 1954 film made by blacklisted Hollywood artists and itself blacklisted for years. Meanwhile, in 1924, the forester Aldo Leopold persuaded the Forest Service to set aside the Gila as the world's first designated wilderness area, and Silver City slowly became a gateway for hikers, artists and retirees as well as a mine town.`],deepTitle:`Ore and wilderness`,deep:[`Mimbres bowls ended up shaping a twentieth-century art market and an ethical argument. Early collectors dug burials for them, and many of the finest pieces were taken from graves without any record. Today archaeologists read the designs as a window into Mimbres life — fish species from the Gulf of California, astronomical events, ceremonial dress — while descendant Pueblo communities ask that the dead and their offerings be left alone.`,`Billy the Kid's Silver City years were short but formative. His mother, Catherine Antrim, ran a boarding house before her death and is buried in the town cemetery. After the chimney escape the boy fled to Arizona, killed his first man in a saloon fight near Fort Grant in 1877, and drifted into the Lincoln County War. Silver City still has the site of his family's cabin, the cemetery, and a steady trade in Kid lore that he would hardly recognise.`,`The Empire Zinc strike lasted fifteen months and ended with real gains for the miners, but its larger fame came from the film. Director Herbert Biberman and screenwriter Michael Wilson, both blacklisted, shot Salt of the Earth with the actual strikers playing themselves; the production was harassed, its Mexican lead actress Rosaura Revueltas was deported mid-shoot, and projectionists' unions refused to screen it. Rediscovered decades later, it is now preserved in the Library of Congress National Film Registry.`,`Leopold's wilderness idea was radical for its time: a piece of land kept roadless so people could travel it for weeks on foot or horseback. The Gila still feels that way, and the Gila Cliff Dwellings, built in the 1280s in shallow caves above a creek, are reached by a winding mountain road that takes far longer than the map suggests. Fire is now as much a part of the Gila as the cliff dwellings; the 2012 Whitewater-Baldy fire was then the largest in New Mexico's history, and smoke seasons are part of life in town.`]},"hood-river-or":{why:`The Cascade rain shadow begins right at Hood River. Rainfall drops from about 1,900 mm at Cascade Locks to about 800 mm here to about 350 mm at The Dalles, only 30 km east. The Columbia's only sea-level gap through the range funnels a venturi wind that cools the waterfront and sells the kite scene, while river-valley moderation and mid-slope thermal belts keep orchard country from behaving like the dry plateau. Mount Hood's glacial stream supplies irrigation the rain shadow withholds. It is a hinge climate: marine air still leaks from the west, and continental dryness is already visible down-gorge.`,history:[`For thousands of years the Columbia Gorge was one of the great fishing grounds of North America. Wasco, Wishram, Yakama, Umatilla and Warm Springs fishermen stood on wooden scaffolds over the falls at Celilo, east of Hood River, dipping nets into the spring salmon run, and traders came from across the West to meet them. Lewis and Clark floated through in 1805 and described the noise and the crowds. In 1792 a British lieutenant, William Broughton, had already sighted the great volcano to the south and named it for Admiral Samuel Hood; the river that drains it into the gorge eventually took the same name — though local tradition says early pioneers called it Dog River, after a hungry winter when they ate their own dogs.`,`The first settler family, the Coes, arrived in 1854, and in the following decades homesteaders discovered that the valley's volcanic soil, sunny summers and cold winter nights grew superb fruit. Irrigation ditches drew on glacial water from Mount Hood, and Hood River apples won prizes across the country until a savage freeze in 1919 killed thousands of trees; many growers replanted in pears, which is why the valley is now one of the largest winter-pear districts in the world. Japanese immigrants cleared land and built some of the most productive orchards in the valley — and then, in 1942, were sent to incarceration camps. In 1944 the local American Legion post struck the names of sixteen Japanese American servicemen from the county's honour roll, a gesture so shameful that it drew national condemnation and was reversed the following year.`,`The gorge changed again in the twentieth century. The Columbia River Highway, opened in stages from 1916, made the waterfalls a tourist route; Bonneville Dam in 1938 and The Dalles Dam in 1957 tamed the river and drowned Celilo Falls in a single afternoon. In the early 1980s windsurfers discovered that the summer wind howling through the gorge — the same pressure-driven gale that makes the waterfront feel cool — was among the best sailing wind on the continent, and Hood River became a world capital of the sport, later of kiteboarding. In 2017 a teenager's fireworks started the Eagle Creek Fire, which burned across the gorge for months.`],deepTitle:`Pears and gorge wind`,deep:[`Celilo Falls fell silent on 10 March 1957, when the gates of The Dalles Dam closed and the reservoir rose over the fishing platforms in a matter of hours. Thousands of people gathered on the banks to watch. For the river nations, it was the loss of a place that had been a fishery, a market and a meeting ground for at least ten thousand years; treaty fishing rights survived on paper and in the courts, and tribal fishers still work platforms and set nets along the Columbia today.`,`The 1919 freeze was the kind of event that reshapes a valley. Temperatures in the orchards plunged far below anything the apple trees could survive, and growers who replanted chose pears — Anjou, Bosc, Bartlett — that suited the cold nights and long sunny days. The valley's packing houses and fruit-stand Fruit Loop still follow that pivot a century later.`,`The honour-roll scandal of 1944 involved soldiers who were, at that very moment, fighting in Europe and the Pacific. One of them, Frank Hachiya, an army interpreter, was killed in the Philippines only weeks after his name was erased. After the war many Japanese American families came home to find their land sold or neglected and their neighbours hostile; some stayed and rebuilt orchards that their grandchildren still farm.`,`The windsurfing boom of the 1980s brought gear shops, board builders and a new kind of resident, and the money that followed has priced many farmworkers out of the town their labour sustains. The gorge's geography sets the pattern: marine air from the west, dry continental air from the east, and a funnel between them that blows hardest on the warmest summer days. In recent summers it has also funnelled smoke from fires burning across the Cascades.`]},"ashland-or":{why:`Ashland sits in a west-east Rogue Valley pocket where marine air still leaks through the Cascades but the Siskiyous block the worst coastal gray. Summers run warmer and drier than the Oregon coast; winters stay milder than the high Cascades. The valley floor develops a notable diurnal swing — hot afternoons, cool nights — that rewards tree fruit and vineyard work. Lithia water and theatre made the climate famous; the rain shadow and valley inversion made it true. Smoke can still cancel outdoor Shakespeare, which is the honest review of a drying Bear Creek corridor.`,history:[`In July 1935 a young English teacher named Angus Bowmer wanted to stage Shakespeare in the roofless shell of Ashland's old Chautauqua hall. The city fathers agreed on one condition: that boxing matches be held on the same stage in the afternoons, to cover the losses the plays were sure to make. The plays sold out and paid for the boxing. From that bargain grew the Oregon Shakespeare Festival, which now runs most of the year, employs hundreds of people and is the reason a town of about twenty thousand fills its hotels for most of the year.`,`The valley's first story is older and harsher. The Takelma and Shasta peoples lived along Bear Creek and the Rogue River, harvesting salmon and acorns from the oak savanna, until gold was found at nearby Jacksonville in 1851 and miners poured in. The Rogue River Wars of 1855–56 ended with survivors marched and shipped north to the Siletz and Grand Ronde reservations. A flour mill on Ashland Creek gave the new town its first name, Ashland Mills, after a settler's home county in Ohio. In December 1887 a golden spike was driven at Ashland to join the Oregon and California railroads, linking Portland to San Francisco by rail for the first time.`,`Boosters then tried to turn the town into a spa. In the 1910s voters approved bonds to pipe sulphurous, faintly fizzy lithia water from springs outside town into fountains on the plaza, and hired John McLaren, the designer of San Francisco's Golden Gate Park, to lay out a park of nearly a hundred acres along the creek. The spa never quite took off — the water is an acquired taste, and the fountains still let visitors find that out for themselves — but Lithia Park became the town's living room, and the festival gave visitors a better reason to come. In September 2020 the Almeda Fire raced down the Bear Creek valley through Talent and Phoenix, destroying some 2,500 homes just a few kilometres from the plaza.`],deepTitle:`Bard and lithia`,deep:[`The gold rush that opened the Rogue Valley was violent even by the standards of its time. Miners and volunteer militias attacked Takelma villages, Takelma warriors struck back, and federal troops ended the war in 1856 by removing nearly every Native family from the valley. Descendants of those families are now part of the Confederated Tribes of Siletz Indians and the Grand Ronde community, and some of the valley's place names have begun to change as their histories are told.`,`The Chautauqua movement brought lecture circuits, music and summer education to small towns across America, and Ashland built a domed hall for it in 1893. When the dome was replaced, the circular wall that remained reminded Bowmer of an Elizabethan playhouse. The festival won a special Tony Award for regional theatre in 1983, and its outdoor Allen Elizabethan Theatre still stands roughly where the Chautauqua tents once rose.`,`The lithia water scheme was the dream of a few promoters who imagined Ashland as a western Saratoga Springs. The mineral water comes from springs a few kilometres east of town and is piped to the plaza fountains, where it tastes strongly of salt and sulphur. The grand hotel and bathhouses never got built; what remains is the park, with its duck ponds, Japanese garden and creekside trails, and a fountain that has embarrassed generations of first-time drinkers.`,`Ashland sits in one of Oregon's sunniest corners, and that same dry summer climate is what feeds the fires that now define the late season. The Almeda Fire started on the north edge of Ashland on a windy September morning and ran north for kilometres through mobile-home parks and neighbourhoods. Smoke from fires across southern Oregon and northern California has also forced the festival to cancel or move outdoor shows in several recent summers.`]},"santa-fe-nm":{why:`Santa Fe's high-desert bench at 2,194 m cuts heat that Albuquerque's lower Rio Grande corridor still feels, while the Sangre de Cristo range wrings extra moisture and throws cold air toward the piñon foothills. Nights drop fast. July stays warm rather than brutal; January is honestly cold. The monsoon arrives with some reliability, and the mountains amplify both winter snow and afternoon showers. The light is high-elevation light, not a marketing slogan. Drought, fire in the Sangres, and a shrinking snowpack are the capital-city constraints on that amenity.`,seasons:{summer:`June is the dry, windy, brilliant month — afternoons near 29°C and the mountains on fire watch. Then in early July the monsoon arrives: clouds pile over the Sangre de Cristos by early afternoon, a short hard storm rattles the rain spouts on adobe roofs, and the town smells of wet piñon and damp earth. Evenings fall back to around 12°C — sweater weather for the open-air opera.`},history:[`On 10 August 1680, runners carrying knotted cords reached every pueblo along the Rio Grande. Each knot counted down a day, and when the last one was untied, the Pueblo peoples rose together against Spanish rule. The revolt, organised by a Tewa leader named Po'pay of Ohkay Owingeh, besieged Santa Fe for days, drove the colonists all the way back to El Paso and kept them out for twelve years. It is the most successful Indigenous uprising in North American history, and it happened on the plaza where tourists now buy turquoise from Pueblo artists under the portal of the Palace of the Governors.`,`That palace was already seventy years old by then. Santa Fe was founded as the capital of the Spanish province of New Mexico around 1610, which makes it the oldest capital city in the United States, and the adobe building on the north side of the plaza is the oldest public building in continuous use in the country. Diego de Vargas led the Spanish back in 1692, and the city still celebrates its fiesta every September, complete since 1924 with the burning of Zozobra, a towering puppet stuffed with residents' written worries. After Mexican independence the Santa Fe Trail opened in 1821, and wagons from Missouri rolled into the plaza loaded with calico and hardware; in 1846 General Stephen Kearny marched in and claimed the city for the United States without firing a shot.`,`The railroad snubbed Santa Fe in 1880 — the main line went through Lamy, about 30 km away — and for a while the capital looked like a backwater. It reinvented itself after statehood in 1912 by deliberately rebuilding in an adobe Pueblo-Spanish style and writing that style into law, drawing painters, writers and eventually opera lovers. During the Second World War the city kept one of the century's great secrets: every scientist bound for the Manhattan Project checked in at an unmarked office at 109 East Palace Avenue before being driven up to Los Alamos. Today the city's water still depends on snow in the Sangre de Cristos, and recent megafires have shown how close the forest is to the capital.`],deepTitle:`Revolt and adobe`,deep:[`The Pueblo Revolt was a war of ideas as well as arms. Po'pay called for a return to traditional ways — churches were burned, Christian names abandoned, and Spanish crops and livestock rejected in some places — but the alliance of dozens of pueblos speaking several languages proved hard to hold together once the Spanish were gone. When de Vargas returned, some pueblos made terms and others fought; the colony that followed was more cautious, and the Franciscans never again wielded quite the same power.`,`Fiesta de Santa Fe began in 1712 as a commemoration of the reconquest, and its meaning has been contested ever since. The Entrada pageant re-enacting de Vargas's return was quietly dropped in 2018 after years of protests from Native residents. Zozobra, invented by the artist Will Shuster in 1924 as a lighthearted send-up, has grown into a crowd of tens of thousands cheering as Old Man Gloom burns.`,`The Manhattan Project's front door was run by Dorothy McKibbin, a Santa Fe widow who greeted physicists, handed out passes and fielded questions she could not answer from 1943 until the end of the war. Klaus Fuchs, the physicist who passed atomic secrets to the Soviet Union, met his courier on a Santa Fe bridge. Few residents knew what was going on up on the mesa until the news of Hiroshima.`,`Santa Fe's look is a deliberate twentieth-century creation. After 1912 museum director Edgar Lee Hewett and his allies promoted the Pueblo-Spanish Revival style, and a 1957 ordinance made it compulsory in the historic district — flat roofs, earth tones, projecting vigas. Underneath the style the older system still runs: acequias, communal irrigation ditches managed by elected mayordomos, still water gardens and small farms along the river in a tradition that goes back to the first Spanish settlers and the Pueblo irrigation they adapted.`]},"santa-barbara-ca":{why:`A south-facing coastal bench under the Santa Ynez Mountains keeps Santa Barbara in a narrow marine band: cool summers by California-interior standards, brief winters, and a sundowner wind when desert air spills over the ridge. The coastline turns east–west here, so the city faces due south with a 1,200 m wall within about 10 km. The Channel is a moat. The mountains are a wall and a fire chimney. Marine-layer presence is shorter-lived than farther north; winter freezes are essentially absent. The thermometer is gentle. The hills are not.`,history:[`On 23 February 1942, as President Roosevelt delivered a fireside chat, a Japanese submarine surfaced off the Ellwood oil field just west of Santa Barbara and fired some sixteen shells at the storage tanks. The damage was trivial, but it was the first enemy attack on the American mainland in the Second World War, and it set off a coast-wide panic. The oil it targeted was nothing new: natural tar seeps had been bubbling onto these beaches for millennia, and the Chumash used that tar to seal the planked canoes — tomols — they paddled across the channel to trade with island villages.`,`The Chumash town of Syuxtun stood on this shore when Spanish ships arrived. Sebastián Vizcaíno named the channel for Saint Barbara in December 1602, and in the 1780s Spain built a presidio and the mission later known as the Queen of the Missions. In 1824 Chumash neophytes at Santa Barbara, Santa Inés and La Purísima rose in revolt against mission abuses, holding La Purísima for a month before soldiers retook it. Under Mexico the town became a sleepy hide-trading port, described by Richard Henry Dana in Two Years Before the Mast, and under the United States a health resort and, from 1912 to 1921, the home of the Flying A film studio, one of the biggest silent-movie studios in the world.`,`On 29 June 1925 an earthquake flattened much of downtown. Civic leaders led by Pearl Chase seized the chance to rebuild the whole centre in a single Spanish Colonial Revival style — white walls, red tile — and the result, crowned by the 1929 county courthouse, is the look the city now sells. In January 1969 a blowout on Union Oil's Platform A spilled millions of litres of crude into the channel and coated beaches in black, galvanising the environmental movement that led to the first Earth Day. Fire has written the most recent chapters: the Thomas Fire of December 2017 burned the mountains above town, and a month later a downpour on the burn scar sent debris flows through Montecito that killed 23 people.`],deepTitle:`Tar, tile, and fire`,deep:[`Chumash tomols were among the most sophisticated watercraft in pre-contact North America — planks of driftwood redwood sewn together with plant fibre and caulked with a mix of tar and pine pitch. Crews paddled them across 30 km of open water to Santa Cruz Island, carrying shell bead money, acorns and fish. The craft nearly vanished after the mission era; in 1976 Chumash descendants built a new tomol, and since 2001 an annual crossing to Santa Cruz Island has revived the voyage.`,`The Chumash Revolt of 1824 started at Santa Inés after a soldier flogged a young man, spread to La Purísima, where rebels held the mission until March, and reached Santa Barbara, whose residents fled into the hills and across the mountains to the San Joaquin Valley. Some never returned to the missions. The revolt is often skipped in mission-tourism brochures; it was the largest organised resistance to the California mission system.`,`The Ellwood shelling was carried out by the submarine I-17 under Commander Kozo Nishino, who reportedly had visited the oil field before the war as a tanker captain. Its military effect was nil — a damaged pump and a pier — but the following night, jittery anti-aircraft gunners in Los Angeles fired some 1,400 shells at nothing in the so-called Battle of Los Angeles.`,`Santa Barbara's mild climate comes with a sharp edge. On a few evenings each year, downslope sundowner winds rush off the Santa Ynez Mountains and push fires toward the sea, as they did in the Painted Cave Fire of 1990 and the Jesusita Fire of 2009. After a big burn, the steep, bare slopes can turn a heavy winter storm into a river of mud and boulders. The 2018 Montecito debris flows changed building rules, evacuation planning and insurance across the south coast.`]},"driggs-id":{why:`Teton Valley sits in a high, west-of-the-range pocket at 1,866 m: the Tetons wring storms on the Wyoming side and throw a rain shadow and gap winds toward Driggs, while cold air drains off the range and the Big Hole Mountains into a closed basin with no easy outlet. Winter inversions are among the most reliable in North America. Summers stay sharp; nights cool hard. It feels like the Tetons' quieter back door because it is. The pass is still a weather gate, and the basin still traps dense cold on clear, still nights.`,history:[`In July 1832 this quiet hay valley hosted one of the wildest gatherings in the history of the American West. Trappers, traders and hundreds of Nez Perce and Salish families converged on what the mountain men called Pierre's Hole — named for an Iroquois trapper, Pierre Tevanitagon — for the annual fur-trade rendezvous: weeks of trading pelts for powder, coffee and whiskey, horse races and brawls. As the parties broke up, a band of Gros Ventre ran into a group of trappers at the south end of the valley, and the Battle of Pierre's Hole left dozens dead on both sides. French-speaking trappers, looking at the peaks from this side, had already given the range its cheeky name: Les Trois Tétons.`,`The valley belonged to Shoshone and Bannock bands long before the trappers came, part of a seasonal round that took families to camas meadows, buffalo country and the hot springs of Yellowstone. The 1868 Fort Bridger Treaty confined them to the Fort Hall reservation on the Snake River plain, and in the late 1880s Latter-day Saint settlers arrived to plough the high, frost-prone flats. They named their main town for Benjamin Driggs, a church leader, and laid it out on the wide-streeted Mormon grid. Life ran on hay, dairy cattle and seed potatoes — crops that can survive a growing season in which a hard frost is possible in almost any month.`,`For most of the twentieth century Teton Valley was the quiet back side of famous mountains. That began to change in 1969, when Grand Targhee ski area opened on the Wyoming slope above town, selling deep powder at lower prices than Jackson Hole. Then Jackson's property prices exploded, and workers, climbers and second-home buyers began crossing Teton Pass to live on the Idaho side. Today Driggs is part farm town and part bedroom community for one of the most expensive resort valleys in America, with the old drive-in, the grain elevators and the potato fields still in view of new subdivisions.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The 1832 rendezvous drew the biggest names of the mountain fur trade: William Sublette, Jim Bridger, Nathaniel Wyeth's party of greenhorn New Englanders and agents of the rival American and Rocky Mountain fur companies. When the Gros Ventre group was spotted, a trapper and a Flathead ally rode out under a show of truce and shot the Gros Ventre leader; the fight that followed lasted most of a day around a willow fort in the creek bottom. Sublette was wounded. Accounts disagree about the death toll, but the battle passed into mountain-man legend and the valley's name stuck to maps for decades.`,`The Shoshone-Bannock tribes at Fort Hall retained treaty hunting rights on unoccupied federal land, and families still hunt and gather in the mountains around the valley. Grand Targhee itself is named for Targhee, a nineteenth-century Bannock leader. The settler grid, the irrigation ditches and the wooden churches came later and filled in the valley floor; the high country above stayed public land.`,`The Teton River drains the valley north through a deep canyon, and in June 1976 the newly built Teton Dam on that canyon failed as its reservoir filled for the first time. The flood that poured out swept through Rexburg and Sugar City downstream, killing eleven people and destroying thousands of homes. The dam was never rebuilt, and its broken abutments are still visible from the canyon rim — a sobering monument in a landscape usually admired for its peaks.`,`Driggs's climate is famously extreme for its elevation. The valley floor is a closed basin, so on clear, calm winter nights dense cold air drains off the mountains and pools on the flats, and the town can be one of the coldest places in the lower forty-eight while the ski hill above sits in milder air. Summers are the reward: dry, sunny, wildflower-bright and cool at night, which is exactly what drew the newcomers — and why housing in a hay valley now costs what it does.`]},"traverse-city-mi":{why:`Grand Traverse Bay holds summer heat and winter cold in a lake-effect engine: cherries survive because the water delays bloom past frost, then buffers July, while west winds off Lake Michigan can bury the same slopes in snow that insulates soil. The deep bay and the open lake to the west stretch autumn into October. The fruit belt is a thermal trick of shoreline geometry, a narrow band of lake-moderated country that inland counties a few kilometres away do not get. Ice-cover decline is a slow-motion threat to the same frost protection that made cherries rational.`,seasons:{summer:`Long, bright, and lake-cooled — afternoons near 26°C, nights near 14°C, and a breeze off Grand Traverse Bay that keeps inland humidity at arm's length. July is cherry month: shaker machines strip the tart Montmorency trees on the peninsulas and the National Cherry Festival takes over the waterfront. By August the shallow East Bay is warm enough for long swims, and thunderstorms roll in off Lake Michigan a few evenings a month.`},history:[`In 1852 a Presbyterian missionary named Peter Dougherty planted a few cherry trees beside his mission house on the long finger of land that splits Grand Traverse Bay. Everyone said it was too far north for fruit. The trees thrived, because the deep water on both sides of the peninsula holds back spring bloom until the danger of frost has passed and keeps autumn mild into October. A century and a half later Traverse City calls itself the Cherry Capital of the World, and Michigan grows most of America's tart cherries on the same lake-tempered slopes.`,`The bay's first name was Anishinaabe. Odawa and Ojibwe families fished, planted and gathered maple sugar here, and French voyageurs crossing the wide mouth of the bay by canoe called the passage la grande traverse — the long crossing. Treaties in 1836 and 1855 took most of the land. Then came the lumbermen. Perry Hannah, the 'father of Traverse City', made a fortune cutting the white pine forests, and his firm laid out the town and built its brick downtown. In 1885 the state opened the Northern Michigan Asylum here, a vast Italianate hospital whose superintendent believed that beauty was therapy and surrounded patients with gardens, farms and a prize-winning dairy herd.`,`When the pines were gone, the cutover land went to orchards, and by the 1920s the town was celebrating its blossoms with a festival that became the National Cherry Festival. In 1980 the Grand Traverse Band of Ottawa and Chippewa Indians became the first tribe to win federal acknowledgement under the government's new recognition process. Wine followed cherries on the same slopes from the 1970s, the old asylum was restored into shops and apartments, and Sleeping Bear Dunes to the west drew summer crowds. The lake still sets the terms: in 2012 a freakish March heatwave coaxed the trees into bloom and an April freeze killed most of the state's tart cherry crop.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Old Mission Peninsula is barely three kilometres wide in places and more than 30 km long, with water on both sides — about as close as a farm can get to living on a ship. Cold air drains downhill into the bays on spring nights instead of settling on the orchards, and the lake's slow warm-up delays buds until late frosts are less likely. The same geography made the peninsula one of the first places in Michigan to grow European wine grapes, starting in the 1970s.`,`Dougherty's mission was itself a casualty of the treaties. After 1852 the Odawa families he served moved across the bay to the Leelanau Peninsula, where they could buy land, and he followed them to found a new mission at Omena. The community that stayed rooted there became the core of the Grand Traverse Band, whose federal recognition in 1980 restored a government that had been dissolved on paper since the 1870s.`,`The asylum, later Traverse City State Hospital, was a world unto itself, with its own power plant, greenhouses and herd of Holsteins — one of whom, a champion milk producer named Traverse Colantha Walker, was honoured with a gravestone on the grounds. The hospital closed in 1989 and sat empty for years before a developer restored the yellow-brick buildings as the Village at Grand Traverse Commons, now one of the largest historic-preservation projects in the Midwest.`,`Sleeping Bear Dunes takes its name from an Anishinaabe story: a mother bear and two cubs swam across Lake Michigan to escape a forest fire; the mother reached shore and lay down to wait, becoming the great dune, while the exhausted cubs drowned and became the North and South Manitou Islands. The dunes rise more than 100 m above the lake, and the national lakeshore protecting them was created in 1970.`]},"ithaca-ny":{why:`Cayuga Lake is a long heat reservoir in a steep glacial trough: autumn stays open, spring comes late, and gorges cut into the plateau dump cold air and waterfalls into town. The largest Finger Lakes are some 60 km long and more than 100 m deep — enough thermal mass to keep shores frost-free weeks longer than the plateau above. Cornell's hill is a different climate from the inlet. Slopes oriented to winter sun add warmth the uplands do not get. The lakes are doing the work, not the latitude alone.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, green, and thundery — afternoons near 27°C, nights near 15°C, and humid air that makes the gorges the best place in town. Waterfalls thin to lace by August, swimming opens at the state-park pools below the falls, and storms build over the hills most weeks. Cayuga Lake keeps the shore a degree or two cooler than the plateau, and the farmers' market on the inlet runs at full tilt.`},history:[`In September 1779, an American army under General John Sullivan marched through the Finger Lakes on George Washington's orders to destroy the towns of the Haudenosaunee nations allied with Britain. At the south end of Cayuga Lake the soldiers burned Coreorgonel, a village where Cayuga families had taken in refugee Tutelo and Saponi people from the south, along with its orchards and cornfields. The campaign broke the Cayuga hold on their homeland. After the war New York surveyed the region into lots for veterans, and the state surveyor general, Simeon De Witt, sprinkled it with classical names — Ithaca, Ovid, Ulysses, Homer, Hector — so the frontier still reads like a Greek atlas.`,`Ithaca grew as a lake port at the foot of steep hills, shipping salt, gypsum and timber north by boat toward the Erie Canal. In 1865 Ezra Cornell, who had made his fortune building telegraph lines that became Western Union, joined with the scholar Andrew Dickson White to found a university on the hill above town, with the declared aim of offering instruction to any person in any study. Cornell admitted women from its early years and taught engineering and agriculture alongside the classics. In the 1910s the town briefly became a film capital: the Wharton brothers shot silent serials such as The Exploits of Elaine on location in Ithaca's gorges, parks and lake shore.`,`The twentieth century gave Ithaca its reputation as an outpost of independent thought. Vladimir Nabokov wrote much of Lolita while teaching at Cornell, Carl Sagan made the university his home, and the Moosewood Restaurant, founded as a collective in 1973, turned vegetarian cooking into a national bestseller. In 1991 the town even printed its own local currency, Ithaca Hours. The bumper-sticker slogan Ithaca Is Gorges points at the landscape that underlies all of it: more than a hundred waterfalls within a short drive, carved by streams falling off the plateau into a lake that glaciers dug so deep its floor lies below sea level.`],deepTitle:`Gorges and gown`,deep:[`The Sullivan campaign destroyed some forty Haudenosaunee towns and thousands of acres of crops in a single season, and the Seneca and Cayuga called Washington Town Destroyer for generations afterward. The Cayuga Nation lost almost all of its land in later dealings with New York State, and its people scattered to Seneca and Six Nations communities. A small Cayuga land base has since been bought back near the north end of the lake, and the nation's claims are still being argued.`,`Ezra Cornell was a self-taught carpenter and mechanic who got into the telegraph business by designing a trenching plough for buried wire — it failed, so he strung wire on poles instead and made a fortune. His campus on East Hill sits above deep gorges cut by Fall Creek and Cascadilla Creek, and students have been crossing them on footbridges, and occasionally falling into them, ever since. The university's chimes have rung across the valley since 1868, from McGraw Tower since 1891.`,`The Wharton studio operated from 1914 to about 1919 in what is now Stewart Park on the lake shore, and the gorges gave its cliffhanger serials their cliffs. Stars rode horses along the lake, staged rescues in the creeks and tumbled over waterfalls; the industry eventually left for the reliable sun of southern California, taking with it the cloudy Finger Lakes' brief claim to be Hollywood.`,`Taughannock Falls, just north of town, drops about 65 m in a single plunge — taller than Niagara — and Cayuga Lake itself is more than 130 m deep, its bottom below sea level. That deep water stores summer heat and releases it in autumn, which is why vineyards and orchards line the lake slopes and why the shore can stay frost-free weeks longer than the plateau above. The Cayuga Lake Wine Trail, founded in 1983, is often described as the oldest in the United States.`]},"grand-marais-mn":{why:`Grand Marais faces the longest fetch of cold water in the Lower 48: Lake Superior delays spring, knocks the top off summer heat, and can throw fog and gales on a schedule that inland Minnesota never sees. Summer highs along the immediate shore often sit on the order of 10 °C below inland counties. The highland behind town is boreal. The harbor is a climate machine. Fog forms offshore on warm days and drifts inland; gales and seiches remind a small town that the lake is also a hazard, not only a view.`,history:[`Every summer in the late 1700s, the shore 50 km east of Grand Marais became the busiest place in the North American interior. At Grand Portage, voyageurs paddling birchbark freight canoes up from Montreal met the northern crews who had wintered in the fur country beyond Lake Superior, and for a few frantic weeks the North West Company's stockade swarmed with clerks, traders, Ojibwe families and partners dining on wine and beef in the great hall. Then everyone shouldered packs of 40 kg and carried the trade goods up a 13-km trail around the falls of the Pigeon River — the grand portage itself.`,`Grand Marais was a smaller stop on that world, a sheltered double harbour whose Ojibwe name is usually translated as great double water; the French name — great marsh — came from the wetland behind the beach. After the treaties of the 1850s opened the North Shore to settlement, Norwegian and Swedish fishermen built shacks along the harbour and hauled herring and lake trout from water so cold that bodies of the drowned rarely surface. Logging camps stripped the pine from the hills, and in the 1890s a rough wagon road began pushing inland toward the lakes along the Canadian border. It became the Gunflint Trail, named for the flinty chert along its route, and it opened the canoe country that is now the Boundary Waters.`,`In 1947 a painter named Birney Quick started a summer art school on the harbour, now the Grand Marais Art Colony, the oldest in Minnesota, and artists have been chasing the North Shore light ever since. The North House Folk School, founded in 1997 in a row of old fishing buildings, teaches boatbuilding, timber framing and other northern crafts. The lake and forest still write the town's calendar: on 4 July 1999 a derecho flattened hundreds of thousands of acres of forest along the border, and in 2007 the Ham Lake Fire roared down the Gunflint Trail and burned well over a hundred homes, cabins and outbuildings.`],deepTitle:`Harbour and portage`,deep:[`The Grand Portage Band of Lake Superior Chippewa has lived on this shore throughout every one of those chapters, and its reservation still surrounds the old fur post, which is now a national monument staffed partly by band members. The band kept its land in the 1854 treaty by insisting on it, and today its lodge, casino and businesses are much of the economy at the tip of the state. The reconstructed great hall and canoe warehouse sit beside a living community, not a ghost town.`,`Commercial fishing on the North Shore boomed until the 1950s, when overfishing and the arrival of the parasitic sea lamprey through the shipping canals crashed the lake trout population. Fisheries managers spent decades poisoning lamprey larvae and restocking trout before the native populations recovered. A few licensed families still set nets for herring off Grand Marais, and smoked fish is sold from the same harbour where the old net houses stood.`,`The Gunflint Trail made legends of a few people who refused to leave. Justine Kerfoot ran Gunflint Lodge on the border lakes for most of the twentieth century, guiding, trapping and dog-sledding through winters when the road was impassable for weeks. Canoe outfitters along the trail still send thousands of paddlers into the Boundary Waters every summer, and Grand Marais is where they buy their last groceries.`,`Lake Superior holds about 10 percent of the world's fresh surface water and stays cold enough at depth that its shore feels like the sea. On summer mornings fog can sit on the harbour while the hills behind town bake in sunshine; in November, gales build waves several metres high that break over the breakwall to the lighthouse. Ice cover on the lake has become less reliable in recent decades, which changes winter storms and the fishing season — and the town watches the lake the way coastal towns watch the ocean.`]},"highlands-nc":{why:`Highlands sits on a southern Blue Ridge plateau high enough that orographic cloud and a nearly temperate-rainforest wetness replace Piedmont heat. The escarpment is the first high wall moist Gulf air meets riding north, so annual precipitation regularly tops 2,000 mm. Elevation near 1,230 m keeps summer afternoons human; winter can glaze. The plateau is a climate island at southern latitude, which is the whole point. Lush is not the same as safe: landslides, wet roads, and a fire risk that surprises people who think rainforest cannot burn are the fine print.`,immersive:`On the Highlands plateau the air often feels washed. Clouds snag on the Blue Ridge escarpment, drip through rhododendron, and keep July from turning into Atlanta. Streets stay damp after storms that never reached the foothills. Winter is not northern-severe, but it is real: glaze, a little snow, woodsmoke in the coves. Afternoon light through wet forest can feel like a higher latitude wearing a southern address. People came here in the 1870s for exactly this, and the forest still makes that bargain audible in every dripping afternoon.`,seasons:{summer:`The South's natural air-conditioning — afternoons near 25°C while the Piedmont below swelters in the 30s, and nights near 15°C that call for a light blanket. The price is rain: clouds pile against the escarpment most afternoons and close to 600 mm can fall across the summer, so the waterfalls roar, ferns and rhododendron drip, and fog often fills the hollows by evening.`},history:[`According to local lore, Highlands was founded with a ruler. In 1875 two Kansas developers, Samuel Kelsey and Clinton Hutchinson, drew a line on a map from New York to New Orleans and another from Chicago to Savannah, noticed that they crossed on a high plateau in the North Carolina mountains, and concluded that it was bound to become a great crossroads of commerce. It never did — the railroads went elsewhere — but the plateau they bought turned out to have something rarer than trade: cool, wet, healthy air at 1,230 m in a region where lowland summers brought heat, humidity and malaria.`,`The land had belonged to the Cherokee, whose towns lined the Little Tennessee valley below; the great mound at Nikwasi in nearby Franklin still marks the heart of one of them. Removal in 1838 forced most Cherokee west on the Trail of Tears, though some families stayed hidden in the mountains and became the ancestors of today's Eastern Band. Kelsey's town grew instead as a summer refuge for wealthy families from Atlanta, Augusta, Charleston and New Orleans, who built shingled cottages along the ridges and hiked to waterfalls like Cullasaja and Bridal Veil. Golfer Bobby Jones helped found the Highlands Country Club in 1928.`,`Scientists came for the rain. The plateau sits on the Blue Ridge escarpment where moist air from the Gulf of Mexico is forced suddenly upward, and it catches well over 2,000 mm of precipitation a year — temperate rainforest in the southern United States. The Highlands Biological Station, founded in 1927, turned the dripping forests into a laboratory for salamanders, mosses and rare plants found almost nowhere else. The same geography carries risk: in 2024 Hurricane Helene's rains triggered landslides and floods across the southern mountains, and steep roads down the escarpment can close for weeks after a big storm.`],deepTitle:`Town in the clouds`,deep:[`Kelsey and Hutchinson were promoters from the Kansas prairie who had already founded a town out west; Highlands was their bet on the mountains. They advertised widely, sold lots to New Englanders and southerners alike, and within a few years the town had a hotel, a school and summer residents from across the country. The legend of the two crossing lines may be too neat to be entirely true, but it captures their optimism — and the plateau's stubborn isolation, which kept Highlands small while the crossroads they imagined grew up somewhere else.`,`Bridal Veil Falls, just west of town, once had a highway running behind it: the old road passed beneath the overhang so that drivers motored through the spray. The highway was later rerouted, and the old loop behind the falls is now open only to people on foot. Farther down the Cullasaja gorge the road clings to cliffs above a series of cascades, and Whiteside Mountain's sheer granite face, where peregrine falcons nest, rises nearly 230 m above the trees.`,`The southern Appalachians are the world's capital of salamanders, and the wet forests around Highlands are a hotspot within that hotspot. Biologists at the station have documented species that live on single mountains and breathe entirely through their skin, needing the constant moisture that the escarpment provides. Rare plants like the Oconee bell grow in shaded coves nearby, and the old-growth hemlocks that once shaded the streams have been battling an invasive insect, the hemlock woolly adelgid.`,`The summer colony that Kelsey dreamed of has become a luxury retreat, with second homes worth millions on ridges where cattle once grazed. Most of the people who work in Highlands's restaurants and shops commute up from lower, cheaper towns. The population roughly swells several times over between winter and summer, and on a foggy November weekday the main street can feel almost empty.`]},"boulder-co":{why:`Boulder leans on the first steep rise of the Front Range: west winds drop, compress, and warm as chinooks, while the Flatirons wring storms and throw a sharp lee. Sudden 20 °C warm-ups can erase snow in a day; summer hail and winter grass can share a week. Elevation near 1,655 m tempers afternoon heat, and dry continental air keeps humidity low. The mountain wall is the climate. Chinooks still melt snow in a day. Grass fires under the same wind can undo a neighborhood in an afternoon.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot, dry mornings climbing toward 30°C, then — on many afternoons — thunderheads boiling up over the Flatirons and a short, violent storm with hail and lightning before the sky clears for sunset. Dry air at 1,655 m lets nights drop to around 14°C, so windows open after dark. Early summer brings snowmelt high and cold down Boulder Creek; later, fire watch in the foothills is part of the season.`},history:[`There is a story, still told in Boulder, about the Curse of Niwot. Niwot — Left Hand, in English — was a leader of the Southern Arapaho who wintered his band in the Boulder Valley and greeted the first gold seekers in 1858 peacefully, even though they were trespassing on land guaranteed by treaty. People who see the beauty of this valley, the legend has him saying, will want to stay, and their staying will be the undoing of the beauty. The real history is grimmer than the legend: Niwot was at Sand Creek in November 1864, when Colorado militia massacred a peaceful Cheyenne and Arapaho camp, and is believed to have died of his wounds.`,`Gold seekers founded Boulder City in 1859 as a supply town for the mines in the mountains above, and it soon became a farming centre too, watered by ditches from Boulder Creek. The University of Colorado opened in 1877 with a single building, and in 1878 hired Mary Rippon, one of the first women to hold a professorship at a state university in the country. In 1898 Texas teachers looking for a cool summer retreat helped launch the Colorado Chautauqua at the foot of the Flatirons, where the dining hall and auditorium still host summer concerts.`,`Boulder's modern identity began with a series of votes. In 1959 residents drew a 'Blue Line' along the mountain front above which the city would not supply water, and in 1967 they became the first city in the United States to tax themselves to buy open space; later rules capped building heights so the mountains stayed in view. Federal science arrived at the same time — the National Bureau of Standards, NOAA, and the National Center for Atmospheric Research, whose mesa laboratory was designed by I.M. Pei — giving the town an unusual density of meteorologists. Two disasters have since tested it: the September 2013 flood, and the Marshall Fire of December 2021, which was driven by winds of more than 160 km/h and destroyed over a thousand homes in neighbouring towns.`],deepTitle:`Blue Line town`,deep:[`Niwot spoke English and travelled widely, and he believed that peace with the Americans was possible. He visited Denver and even wintered near Boulder with miners nearby, and the town of Niwot north of Boulder and Left Hand Canyon carry his name. The Sand Creek Massacre, in which some 230 people, mostly women, children and elderly, were killed, is now a national historic site in eastern Colorado; Arapaho and Cheyenne descendants travel there each year for a healing run.`,`Mary Rippon taught German and French at the university for decades and became one of its best-loved figures; an outdoor theatre on campus is named for her. She also kept a secret that would have ended her career had it come out: a marriage to a former student and a daughter raised far from Boulder. The story only came to light long after her death and is now part of the university's own telling of its history.`,`The Blue Line and the open-space tax changed the look of the whole Front Range. The city now owns or protects more than 18,000 hectares of grassland, mesa and mountain around Boulder, and the Flatirons rise above public land instead of subdivisions. The policies also made the town famously expensive, since housing has nowhere to spread, and many people who work in Boulder live in towns to the east.`,`The Marshall Fire was a new kind of disaster for Colorado. It started in dry grass on the edge of open space on 30 December, a day with almost no snow on the ground, and a downslope windstorm drove it into suburban streets in Louisville and Superior faster than people could evacuate. It burned through neighbourhoods rather than forest, and it showed that the same winter chinooks that make Boulder's climate famously mild can turn a grass fire into a firestorm.`]},"grand-marais-mi":{why:`Winter wind here has a 300 km run across open Lake Superior before it hits sand and forest, so snow is not a decoration but a structural season. Prevailing northwesterlies over unfrozen water produce intense lake-effect convection; Redfield-class totals are the U.P. version of that engine. Summers stay cool and short. The same lake that dumps metres of snow is the air conditioner that makes July exquisite. Ice loss may eventually change the snow machine — or make lake-effect more intense in windows when the lake stays open. Either way, a harbor this small feels the experiment first.`,immersive:`Winter winds cross 300 km of open Lake Superior before reaching this sandy shore, dumping colossal snow totals. Summers are short, cool, and exquisite; winters are defining. Communities along the Grand Marais–Munising shore measure snow in meters, not centimeters. Pictured Rocks rise just west; the harbor itself is a dune-and-ice working edge, with July highs near 23°C because the lake is a heat sink. October gales can return the snow machine before the maples are fully bare. Isolation is structural: one highway, volunteer-scale services, and a lake that does not forgive a late crossing. Ice loss may change the snow bands, or intensify them in windows when Superior stays open.`,seasons:{winter:`Deep, white, and wind-driven — afternoons just under freezing, nights near −10°C, and lake-effect squalls marching in off open Superior on every northwest wind until snowbanks top the car roofs. Skies stay gray for days while the lake is unfrozen; in a hard winter, when ice finally locks the shore, the bands slacken and the sun returns. On some weekends snowmobiles outnumber cars.`,summer:`Short, bright, and cool — afternoons near 23°C, nights near 11°C, and a lake far too cold for long swims outside the shallow harbour. When warm air slides over the cold water, fog banks drift ashore and blot out the dunes for an hour or a day, but most days are clear, with sunsets after half past nine in late June and the occasional thunderstorm rolling off the land.`},contrasts:[{label:`Seney flats (≈40 km S, inland)`,note:`Away from the shore the lake-effect snow thins out, winter nights run colder, and summer afternoons warmer — a continental interior a short drive from a maritime harbour.`},{label:`Au Sable Point (≈20 km W)`,note:`The exposed headland takes Lake Superior's full fetch: gales, fog, and the shipwrecks that put a lighthouse there in 1874.`}],history:[`Ernest Hemingway never actually fished the Two Hearted River. In the summer of 1919, back from the First World War with a leg full of shrapnel, he took the train to Seney and fished the Fox River in the cut-over pine country south of Grand Marais. When he turned the trip into his great short story Big Two-Hearted River, he borrowed the name of the stream that runs into Lake Superior east of town because, he later said, it sounded better. That mix of real and borrowed geography suits Grand Marais, a village that has reinvented itself several times on the same sand.`,`For centuries the harbour here — the only good shelter on a long, cliff-bound stretch of the south shore — served Ojibwe families as a fishing camp and a stop on canoe journeys along Gitche Gumee, the big lake. French voyageurs named it for the marsh behind the beach. In the lumber boom of the late nineteenth century the town filled with mills and saloons, and loggers on the Grand Sable Dunes sent pine logs thundering down a steep sand chute straight into the lake for shipment; the Log Slide is still there. When the timber ran out around 1910, the mills closed and most of the population left almost as fast as it had arrived.`,`What stayed was the lake. Lake Superior's long fetch and sudden gales wrecked so many ships along this coast that a lighthouse went up at Au Sable Point in 1874 and a life-saving station at Grand Marais a generation later; the ribs of old wrecks still surface on the beach after storms. In 1966 Congress made the cliffs and dunes to the west into Pictured Rocks, the first national lakeshore in the country, and Grand Marais became its quiet eastern gateway. Today the village lives on summer hikers, autumn colour and winter snowmobilers, with close to four metres of snow in a typical year.`],deepTitle:`Dune harbour`,deep:[`The Grand Sable Dunes are perched dunes: sand blown up onto a bluff that already stands high above the lake, so they rise roughly 90 m from the water. The lumbermen's Log Slide used that drop as a free conveyor. A slide down was quick; the climb back up is a famous trap for tourists who underestimate it.`,`Au Sable Light, built in 1874, guarded a reef that had already claimed many ships. Keepers lived there through the shipping season, cut off by sand and water, and the station's foghorn sounded for days at a time when cold lake air met warmer summer winds. The wrecks of the steamers Sitka, Gale Staples and Mary Jarecki lie in the shallows nearby, and their timbers appear and disappear with shifting sand.`,`Hemingway's Upper Peninsula stories are full of the burned, cut-over country he saw in 1919 — the town of Seney nearly gone, blackened stumps, grasshoppers turned dark by the fire. That landscape is now the Seney National Wildlife Refuge, a vast marsh of cranes, loons and trumpeter swans, and the Fox River still runs through it.`,`Living in Grand Marais means planning around snow and distance. The village has a few hundred year-round residents, one road south, and a winter that can close it for days at a time when lake-effect bands stall overhead. Summer brings campers, boaters and agate hunters combing the beaches; by November the motels are shuttered and the lake takes over again.`]},"apalachicola-fl":{why:`Apalachicola sits where a great alluvial river meets a shallow, marsh-fringed bay: sea breezes knock a few degrees off Gulf heat, while the estuary — not the beach — sets humidity, smell, and storm surge. Bay moderation and river-delta air buffer both extreme heat and cold relative to the interior panhandle. It is maritime Florida without the resort geometry. The town is a working waterfront first. A single Gulf hurricane can still rearrange the whole ledger, which Hurricane Michael already demonstrated next door.`,immersive:`Apalachicola sits where the Apalachicola River meets its eponymous bay — one of the most productive estuaries on the Gulf. The climate is subtropical but the marine moderation and onshore breezes keep summer afternoons below the Deep-South interior inferno. Oyster reefs, tupelo-honey swamps, and pine flatwoods together paint a distinctly regional picture. Humidity is the medium; the bay, not a resort beach, sets the smell and the surge line. A single Gulf hurricane can still rearrange the ledger, as Hurricane Michael already showed next door at Mexico Beach. The town remains a working waterfront first: oysters, shrimp, and a slower main street on a river that Georgia dams still argue over. St. Vincent and St. George islands are the storm buffers and the real-estate temptation.`,seasons:{winter:`Soft and changeable — afternoons near 19°C, nights near 7°C, and cold fronts that sweep through every few days with a day of rain and a hard north wind that drains the bay down to its oyster bars. Behind them come bright, cool, bugless days; frost is rare but not unknown. Winter has always been the working season on the water.`,summer:`Hot, heavy, and thundery — afternoons near 33°C, nights that barely dip below 23°C, and humidity you can wear. Sea breezes off the Gulf grow into towering afternoon storms most days, so summer rain runs to well over 500 mm and the early morning is the time to be on the water. Hurricane season runs right through it.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for oysters shucked on the dock, empty barrier-island beaches on St. George and St. Vincent, kayaking the estuary, and an old cotton port that still moves at the pace of the tide.`,contrasts:[{label:`Tallahassee (≈110 km NE, inland)`,note:`Away from the Gulf, summer afternoons run hotter and winter nights noticeably colder, with far more frost — the bay breeze that softens Apalachicola never reaches it.`},{label:`St. George Island (across the bay)`,note:`The barrier island takes the full Gulf breeze, runs a touch cooler on summer afternoons, and is first in line when a hurricane's surge comes ashore.`}],history:[`In the 1840s an Apalachicola doctor named John Gorrie, trying to cool his feverish yellow-fever patients in the stifling Gulf summer, built a machine that compressed air, let it expand and froze water into ice. He patented the first mechanical ice machine in 1851 and died a few years later, ridiculed and broke, never knowing that he had invented the principle behind refrigeration and air conditioning — the technology that would make the modern American South livable. His museum stands a few blocks from the waterfront, in a town that still knows exactly what he was up against.`,`Gorrie's Apalachicola was a boomtown. Cotton from the plantations of Georgia and Alabama floated down the Chattahoochee and Apalachicola rivers on steamboats and was loaded onto ocean ships here, and by the 1830s and 1840s the town was one of the largest cotton ports on the Gulf, lined with brick warehouses whose walls still stand. The river had a darker history too. In 1816, upstream at Prospect Bluff, an American gunboat's red-hot cannonball struck the magazine of a fort held by some three hundred formerly enslaved people and their Choctaw allies, killing most of them in a single explosion — the bloodiest single shot in American military history.`,`The railroads bypassed the town after the Civil War, and Apalachicola turned to the bay. Sponge divers, lumber schooners and, above all, oyster tongers kept it alive; by the late twentieth century the bay produced most of Florida's oysters and a sizeable share of the nation's. Then the reefs failed. Drought, low river flows held back by upstream dams in Georgia, and disease brought the harvest to collapse in the 2010s, and the state closed the wild fishery entirely in 2020. Florida's long lawsuit against Georgia over the river's water reached the Supreme Court and was lost in 2021, and Hurricane Michael's Category 5 landfall at nearby Mexico Beach in 2018 showed what the Gulf can do.`],deepTitle:`Ice, cotton, oysters`,deep:[`The fort at Prospect Bluff was built by the British during the War of 1812 and left to the Black people and Native allies who had fought with them. To slaveholders in Georgia it was an intolerable beacon of freedom — runaways knew where to go — and General Andrew Jackson ordered it destroyed. After the explosion on 27 July 1816, survivors were returned to slavery. The site, later known as Fort Gadsden, is now marked in the Apalachicola National Forest.`,`Gorrie was a physician, postmaster and mayor of Apalachicola who believed, like many in his time, that tropical fevers came from bad air. His machine cooled hospital rooms by blowing air over buckets of ice, and he imagined a future of artificial cold for every home. Investors were scarce and newspapers in the North mocked a crank who thought he could make ice better than God. Florida later chose Gorrie as one of its two statues in the US Capitol, where he has stood since 1914.`,`Apalachicola's oysters grew in a rare balance of salt water from the Gulf and fresh water from one of the largest rivers in the Southeast. When too little river water arrived — in droughts, and as Atlanta's growing thirst drew down the reservoirs upstream — the bay grew salty, predators and disease moved in, and the reefs died. Watermen who had tonged oysters from small boats for generations lost their livelihood. Restoration crews are now rebuilding reefs with shell and limestone in the hope of reopening the fishery.`,`The river swamps above town produce one of the world's most prized honeys. White tupelo trees bloom for a few weeks each spring, and beekeepers move their hives onto platforms and barges along the river to catch the flow; pure tupelo honey is so high in fructose that it barely crystallises. The 1997 film Ulee's Gold, starring Peter Fonda as a tupelo beekeeper, was set in these swamps and made the trade briefly famous.`]},"santa-cruz-felton-ca":{why:`Felton sits in a north-facing redwood canyon where marine air and summer fog drip through the canopy while the San Lorenzo River drains a steep Santa Cruz Mountain catch. Redwoods require that fog drip; valley orientation feeds marine air inland; microclimates shift over tens of metres from sunny bench to dripping floor. The floor is darker and damper than Santa Cruz beach, a few kilometres and a climate class away. Winter is a different animal: atmospheric rivers hit the range, the canyon roars, and slide-prone slopes remind you that a temperate rainforest on a faulted range is not a spa.`,immersive:`The San Lorenzo Valley carves through coast-redwood forest above Santa Cruz. Summer marine layer pours up the valley each evening, feeding the tallest trees on Earth. Walk fifteen minutes from a sunny canyon bench into a fog-dripping redwood cove and the temperature drops 5°C. The floor is darker and damper than Santa Cruz beach, a few kilometres and a climate class away. Winter is another animal: atmospheric rivers hit the range, the canyon roars, and slide-prone slopes remind you that a temperate rainforest on a faulted range is not a spa. The 2020 CZU fire scar still sits on the upland flanks. Living here is a bet on canopy, slope, and a river that does not care about the café.`,seasons:{winter:`Wet and green — afternoons near 17°C on clear days, nights near 5°C, and atmospheric-river storms that can drop a month's rain in a weekend, swelling the San Lorenzo and loosening hillsides. Between storms the redwoods steam in low sunshine, and frost settles in the canyon bottoms on clear, still nights while the ridges stay milder.`,summer:`Two climates a few hundred metres apart: fog pours up the San Lorenzo valley most evenings and drips from the redwood needles until mid-morning, while sunny benches above the grey reach 27°C by afternoon. Nights fall to around 12°C. It almost never rains between June and September, so the fire watch runs all season.`},contrasts:[{label:`Santa Cruz waterfront (≈10 km S)`,note:`Right on the bay the marine layer holds summer afternoons cooler and greyer and keeps winter nights gentler; Felton's canyon heats more by day and chills more at night.`},{label:`San Jose & the Santa Clara Valley (≈40 km NE)`,note:`Across the ridge the fog rarely arrives — summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter, and the year brings roughly a third of Felton's rain.`}],history:[`In 1903 Theodore Roosevelt came to Felton to see the redwoods. The grove he walked through, then a private park called Big Trees, held trees that had been growing since before the Norman conquest, and the grove later became the heart of Henry Cowell Redwoods State Park. The president was lucky: by his time most of the old-growth forest in the San Lorenzo valley had already been cut, hauled out by flumes and narrow-gauge trains, or burned in kilns to make lime. The redwoods that survived owe their existence to fog, the sea mist that rolls up the valley on summer evenings and keeps the giants watered through a rainless season.`,`The valley's first people were Awaswas-speaking Ohlone, who lived in villages along the river and harvested acorns, salmon and steelhead. After Mission Santa Cruz was founded in 1791, missionaries and soldiers drew them down to the coast, where disease and forced labour took a terrible toll. In the 1850s the hills filled with loggers and lime burners. Limestone outcrops in the Santa Cruz Mountains fed kilns that produced much of the lime mortar used to build San Francisco, and in 1875 a narrow-gauge railroad and a long wooden flume began carrying lumber down the San Lorenzo from Boulder Creek to Felton and on to the sea.`,`When the timber and lime ran out, Felton turned to summer visitors and later to commuters. The Felton Covered Bridge, built in 1892 and one of the tallest of its kind in the country, still crosses the river, and since 1963 the Roaring Camp steam railroad has hauled tourists up Bear Mountain on the old logging grades. The valley has paid repeatedly for its steep, wet geography. In January 1982 a rain-soaked hillside above Ben Lomond collapsed and killed ten people; the 1989 Loma Prieta earthquake was centred in these mountains; and in August 2020 the CZU Lightning Complex fire burned nearly a thousand homes in the hills around Felton and gutted the headquarters of Big Basin, California's oldest state park.`],deepTitle:`Lime and redwood`,deep:[`The Big Trees grove had been a tourist attraction since the 1870s, when the railroad stopped beside it and passengers climbed down to gaze at trees taller than a twenty-storey building. One hollow giant was named the Frémont Tree on the story that John C. Frémont camped inside it in 1846 — a tale historians treat with some suspicion. Henry Cowell's son Samuel Cowell donated land around the grove to the state in 1953, and the park took the family name.`,`Lime-making was hot, dangerous and hungry for wood. Quarrymen blasted limestone from the hillsides, burned it in stone kilns for days at a time, and fed the fires with redwood and oak cut from the surrounding slopes; one kiln could consume a small forest in a season. The ruins of the old kilns still stand in the woods of the Pogonip and the UC Santa Cruz campus, which was built on the former Cowell ranch in the 1960s.`,`The San Lorenzo flume was a feat of rough engineering: a wooden trough on trestles carrying water and sawn lumber down a steep canyon. The South Pacific Coast Railroad later pushed a line through tunnels to Los Gatos and the Santa Clara Valley, and Felton became a junction town with hotels and summer cabins until the automobile, and then the 1940 closure of the mountain tunnels, made the trains obsolete.`,`Fire is part of the redwood forest's life — the thick bark of old trees survives most burns, and many of the giants in Big Basin are resprouting from blackened trunks — but people living among those trees face a different calculation. After the CZU fire, insurers retreated, rebuilding costs soared, and residents of the San Lorenzo valley began organising their own neighbourhood evacuation and fuel-reduction groups. The atmospheric-river winters of 2017 and 2023 followed with floods and landslides on the same slopes.`]},"wenatchee-wa":{why:`Wenatchee occupies the Columbia–Wenatchee confluence in a deep Cascade rain shadow: summers are hot, dry, and brilliant while the river and irrigation keep orchards alive. Precipitation falls to around 240 mm here where Stevens Pass, 80 km upwind, can take more than 2,500 mm. Clear skies maximize radiative cooling at night, producing the diurnal swings that set fruit sugars. The mountains to the west take the rain. The trench takes the sun. Climate here is a production input, with smoke weeks as the honest addendum.`,history:[`On the morning of 5 October 1931, a battered orange monoplane called Miss Veedol came out of the sky over East Wenatchee with no landing gear and skidded to a halt on its belly on a hilltop. The pilots, Clyde Pangborn and Hugh Herndon, had just flown 41 hours non-stop from northern Japan — the first non-stop flight across the Pacific Ocean. Pangborn had grown up in the area, and he chose to land in the dry, open hills he knew. The local airport is named for him, and a piece of Misawa, the Japanese town where the flight began, is a sister city.`,`For thousands of years before that, the confluence of the Wenatchee and Columbia rivers was a gathering place. The Wenatchi, or P'squosa, people fished for salmon at the river mouth and at the forks upstream where Icicle Creek joins the Wenatchee, and each spring their neighbours came to share the first salmon and the first roots. The 1855 treaty promised them a small reservation at that upstream fishery; it was never set aside, and most Wenatchi families eventually moved to the Colville Reservation to the north. Their fishing rights on the river remain a matter of law and memory.`,`James J. Hill's Great Northern Railway arrived in 1892, irrigation canals soon carried water from the rivers onto the dry benches, and apple trees transformed sagebrush into one of the most famous orchard districts in the world. Wenatchee started calling itself the Apple Capital of the World and has held an Apple Blossom Festival every spring since 1919. In 1933 Rock Island Dam, just downstream, became the first dam to span the Columbia, followed by the great federal dams that remade the river. The orchards still depend on cold winters, warm dry summers and an army of seasonal workers, and in recent decades wildfire — including the 2015 Sleepy Hollow Fire, which burned homes inside the city — has become part of the summer.`],deepTitle:`Apples and the river`,deep:[`Miss Veedol's flight was a gamble from start to finish. Pangborn and Herndon had been detained and fined in Japan for photographing military areas without permission, and they jettisoned their landing gear after take-off to cut drag so the plane could carry enough fuel to cross the ocean. Fog and cloud closed in over their first choices of landing field, so they turned toward Wenatchee, where Pangborn's mother watched him slide to a stop on the sandy hilltop.`,`The Wenatchapam fishery at the mouth of Icicle Creek was promised to the Wenatchi in the 1855 Yakama Treaty and then simply never surveyed. Settlers filed homesteads on the land instead. In the twenty-first century Wenatchi descendants have pressed for the fishing rights the treaty guaranteed, and the river's dams and hatcheries now shape what salmon remain.`,`Apple country was a product of engineering. The Highline Canal, finished in the early 1900s, carried water along the hillsides above the valley floor so that gravity could irrigate the benches, and packing houses and cold-storage warehouses rose beside the railroad tracks. Generations of Mexican and Mexican-American families have done much of the picking and packing since the mid-twentieth century, and their communities now make up a large share of the valley's population.`,`The valley's geography concentrates both sun and smoke. The Cascades block Pacific storms, so summers are hot and almost rainless, but when fires burn in the forests to the north and west, smoke drains into the river trench and can sit there for days under a lid of warm air. Heat waves stress both pickers and fruit, and growers have begun planting newer apple varieties and installing shade netting to cope.`]},"ellensburg-wa":{why:`The Kittitas Valley is a wind gap: pressure differences between wet western Washington and the Columbia Plateau drive air through Ellensburg until the town's personality is the blow. Cross-Cascade geometry plus a broad basin accelerate winds through an otherwise ordinary rain-shadow valley. Summers are bright and hot-dry in a way Seattle never is; winters are colder and clearer, with snow that the wind redistributes rather than politely stacks. Rain-shadow dryness and that corridor wind make a climate you hear as much as you feel. Gap winds also turn grass into a fuse.`,immersive:`Ellensburg sits at a classic gap: the Kittitas Valley funnels pressure differences between the wet west side and the dry Columbia Plateau into persistent, often howling wind. Summers are hot-dry-sunny and winters are cold but clearer than west of the crest. The wind is the signature — ranchers joke that the day it stops blowing everyone in town goes to see a doctor. Rain-shadow dryness and that corridor blow make a climate you hear as much as you feel. Grass and sage become a fuse when the same wind throws embers; the 2012 Taylor Bridge fire was a local lesson. Irrigation greened parts of the shrub-steppe, but the gap has not quieted. The wind dries laundry, wrecks fences, and drives fire weather.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and often grey — afternoons a few degrees above freezing, nights near −5°C, and valley inversions that trap low cloud and freezing fog for days while the ridges above sit in sunshine. Snow comes in modest helpings, and the wind that defines the town turns an ordinary cold day into a bitter one; on the breezy days, at least, the fog blows out.`,summer:`Hot, dry, and breezy — afternoons near 28°C, nights cooling quickly to around 12°C, and almost no rain from late June to September. The westerly wind through the Cascade gap picks up most afternoons, drying hay and laundry in an hour and fanning any spark in the grass; heat waves can push past 38°C.`},contrasts:[{label:`Snoqualmie Pass (Cascade crest, ≈90 km W)`,note:`At the crest the storms that miss Ellensburg unload many metres of snow, and the year's precipitation is several times the Kittitas Valley's.`},{label:`Yakima (≈60 km S)`,note:`Lower and deeper in the rain shadow, Yakima runs hotter in summer, gets less snow, and sits in calmer air than the wind gap at Ellensburg.`}],history:[`Ellensburg's first store was a log trading post opened around 1870 and nicknamed Robbers' Roost, which gives a fair idea of the clientele. When John Shoudy laid out a proper town a few years later, he named it for his wife, Mary Ellen — Ellen's burgh. The valley he built it in was home to the Kittitas people, whose name survives on the county; they had long harvested roots in the spring meadows, fished the Yakima River, and traded with neighbours across the passes. Under the 1855 Yakama Treaty most of their land passed to the United States, and many Kittitas families joined the Yakama Nation to the south.`,`In 1889 Ellensburg was booming. The Northern Pacific Railroad had arrived, the town was a cattle and hay shipping centre, and its boosters were campaigning to become the capital of the new state of Washington. Then, on the Fourth of July, a fire started in a grocery store and destroyed most of the downtown. Olympia led the capital vote that autumn and won the runoff in 1890. The town rebuilt in brick — much of it still standing — and the consolation prize arrived in 1891: a state normal school that grew into Central Washington University.`,`Two things have defined Ellensburg since. One is the rodeo, held every Labor Day weekend since 1923, which fills the town and brings members of the Yakama Nation in to dance at the grounds. The other is wind. The Kittitas Valley sits at the eastern end of a gap through the Cascades, and air pouring from the cool, moist west side toward the hot interior makes it one of the windiest towns in the Northwest — now lined with wind turbines on the ridges. In 2012 that wind drove the Taylor Bridge Fire through dozens of homes west of town. And for more than two decades, the university was home to Washoe, the first chimpanzee taught to communicate in American Sign Language.`],deepTitle:`Wind, hay, and rodeo`,deep:[`The 1889 fire started on the evening of 4 July in a building on Main Street and spread through the wooden business district in hours, helped by a stiff wind and a water system that could not keep up. Around ten blocks burned. Merchants rebuilt quickly in brick and stone, and the Davidson Building and other ornate commercial blocks from that rebuilding still give downtown its late-Victorian look.`,`Washoe arrived at Central Washington University in 1980 with the researchers Roger and Deborah Fouts, who founded a chimpanzee and human communication institute there. Washoe and her adopted son Loulis, along with several other chimps, signed with one another and with their human companions; Loulis was observed learning signs from Washoe rather than from people. Washoe died in Ellensburg in 2007, and the remaining chimpanzees were later moved to a sanctuary.`,`The valley's other treasure is a stone. Ellensburg Blue, a rare sky-blue agate, is found only in the hills north of town and has been prized by collectors since the early twentieth century; it sells for many times the price of ordinary agate. Rockhounds still walk the ridges after spring thaws looking for it.`,`Irrigation from the Yakima Project turned much of the valley floor into fields of timothy hay, much of it pressed into bales and shipped across the Pacific to feed livestock in Asia. The same dry, windy climate that cures hay so well also makes the grasslands around town quick to burn, and the wind that powers the turbines on the ridges is the one that throws embers ahead of a fire.`]}},t={"forks-wa":{why:`Pacific storms hit the Olympic west slope first. Moist marine air is forced up the range and drops extraordinary rain on the Hoh, Bogachiel, and Quillayute drainages before the same air dries in the Sequim rain shadow a short drive east. Cool sea-surface temperatures and persistent onshore flow keep summer from becoming a heat story. Sitka spruce, western hemlock, and moss are the vegetation of that orographic sink, not a decoration applied to a generic coast. Forks sits at the foot of the lift, so the climate is the mountain's windward machine rather than a Seattle average copied westward.`,immersive:`Forks and the Hoh Valley sit at the foot of the Olympics on the Pacific-facing side. Storms hit the west slope first, unleashing their moisture as they climb — producing one of the wettest climates in the contiguous US. Moss drips from every surface; temperatures barely vary from about 2 to 22°C; sunshine is rare and precious. Sitka spruce and western hemlock hold that rain as a dripping interior, especially along the Hoh, where maple and nurse logs stay wet even in July. And yet the same storm system that gives Forks about 400 mm in December gives Sequim, about 95 km east, roughly 60 mm. Fire is less the daily story than windthrow, flood, and a two-lane highway that winter storms can close. Drainage, not drought, is what a garden here has to solve.`,seasons:{winter:`Rain by the metre — afternoons near 8°C, nights just above freezing, and storm after storm off the Pacific; a single winter can bring well over a metre of rain. Days are short and the light comes filtered through cloud and moss. Snow rarely lasts at the town's low elevation, while the Olympics behind it pile it deep, and the rivers run brown and fast for the winter steelhead.`,summer:`The dry season, by Forks standards — afternoons near 20°C, nights near 10°C, ocean fog in the mornings, and a few weeks in July and August when the moss actually dries out. It still rains now and then, but the rainforest trails are at their most inviting and the sea stacks at Rialto Beach and La Push glow at sunset.`},contrasts:[{label:`Sequim (≈100 km E by road)`,note:`On the dry side of the same mountains, Sequim gets roughly a fifth of Forks's rain and far more summer sun.`},{label:`Hoh Rain Forest (≈50 km SE)`,note:`Up the Hoh valley, deeper into the mountain wall, yearly rain climbs past 3,500 mm and the mossed maples drip even in summer.`}],history:[`The Quileute say their first people were made from wolves. In the old story the Transformer, K'wati, came to the mouth of the Quillayute River, found no people there, and changed a pair of wolves into the ancestors of the nation. For thousands of years the Quileute and their Hoh relatives lived along these rivers and beaches, fishing salmon and smelt, hunting seals and the occasional whale from cedar canoes, and gathering camas on the prairie where Forks now stands. In 1889, while many Quileute families were away working the hop harvest inland, a settler who coveted the village site at La Push burned it to the ground. That same year the government finally reserved a single square mile there for them.`,`Homesteaders reached Forks Prairie in the 1870s and named the settlement for the river forks that drain the west side of the Olympics. Farming was a losing battle with rain and stumps; logging paid. In the First World War the army sent soldiers into the peninsula's forests to cut Sitka spruce, a light, strong wood prized for building warplanes, and built a railroad to haul it out — finishing the line just after the war ended. Theodore Roosevelt had already protected the high country as a national monument in 1909, partly to save the elk that had been named for him in 1897, and in 1938 his cousin Franklin signed Olympic National Park into law. Forks itself stayed a timber town and proudly called itself the logging capital of the world.`,`The listing of the northern spotted owl as a threatened species in 1990 and the end of big federal timber sales hit Forks hard. Then, in 2005, a novelist named Stephenie Meyer set a vampire romance called Twilight in the rainiest town she could find on a map, and gave her werewolves to the Quileute. Tourists arrived by the busload looking for characters who had never existed. The Quileute and Hoh tribes, meanwhile, have spent recent decades moving their villages away from the tsunami zone and fighting to protect the rivers they have fished since K'wati's time. The town remains a long, wet drive from the nearest hospital, airport or big store.`],deepTitle:`Wolves and spruce`,deep:[`The fire at La Push in September 1889 destroyed some two dozen houses and generations of carved and woven belongings. The settler responsible had filed a claim on the village land, and the burning was meant to drive the Quileute out for good. It failed: families rebuilt on the same beach, and the reservation set aside that year — tiny, wedged between river and ocean — has remained their home. In 2012 Congress transferred more upland to the tribe so that the school and houses could move above the reach of a tsunami.`,`The Spruce Production Division was one of the strangest military projects of the First World War. Tens of thousands of soldiers were sent into Northwest logging camps to cut the clear, straight-grained spruce that aircraft frames required, and in the Olympics the army built a railroad around Lake Crescent toward the west-end forests at breakneck speed. The Armistice came before the first trainload of spruce rolled. Part of the old grade is now the Spruce Railroad Trail.`,`Forks's logging years left a culture of chainsaws, log trucks and gyppo outfits that still shapes the town's sense of itself, along with memories of a 1951 forest fire that came close enough to send residents fleeing toward the coast. When the spotted owl rules arrived, the conflict was personal: bumper stickers, protests and a long decline in mill jobs. Some forests regrew; much of the land around town is still worked as timber plantations on a short rotation.`,`The Twilight boom brought an unlikely revival. Shops sold vampire souvenirs, the chamber of commerce hosted a festival on the fictional heroine's birthday, and fans flew in from around the world. The Quileute Nation used the attention to tell its own story, insisting that the real people of La Push be seen as more than a novelist's werewolves. When the tourists go home, the rain stays — close to three metres of it in an average year.`]},"astoria-or":{why:`River, ocean, and Coast Range meet at a single bar. Pacific storms ride the Columbia estuary inland while marine stratus and fog stall against the coastal hills, so sunshine is scarce and the annual temperature range stays unusually tight. Orographic lift on the Coast Range wrings extra rain from the same systems that soak the Olympic west slope farther north. The bar itself, where river current collides with swell, is why the town still feels like a working storm port rather than a mild Cfb average copied from inland Oregon.`,seasons:{winter:`Storm season on the bar — afternoons near 10°C, nights just above freezing, and Pacific gales that drive rain sideways up the hillside streets. Daylight is short and sunshine scarcer still; ships wait offshore for the bar to calm, and storm-watchers gather at the jetties to see waves explode against the rocks. Snow is a rare novelty.`,summer:`Mild and breezy — afternoons near 20°C, evenings near 11°C, and a marine layer that often hangs over the river mouth until midday before burning back. Air conditioning is almost unknown: when Portland bakes, Astoria is a sweater town, and the long evenings belong to the waterfront trolley and the sea lions on the docks.`},contrasts:[{label:`Portland (≈150 km E, up the Columbia)`,note:`Behind the Coast Range, summers run far hotter and sunnier, winters a little colder, and the year brings well under half of Astoria's rain.`},{label:`Long Beach Peninsula (across the river mouth)`,note:`The open-ocean shore north of the bar takes the Pacific wind and storm surf head-on — the same mild temperatures with even more exposure.`}],history:[`Lewis and Clark spent the winter of 1805–06 in a log stockade a few kilometres from present-day Astoria, and they did not enjoy it. By their count it rained on all but a dozen of the hundred-odd days they spent at Fort Clatsop; their clothes rotted, fleas infested their furs, and they lived largely on elk and on fish and roots bought from their Clatsop neighbours, who had been trading with European ships at the river mouth for years. Chinook and Clatsop villages controlled the lower Columbia, and their headman Comcomly was a shrewd broker for everyone who arrived by sea.`,`Five years later the fur magnate John Jacob Astor sent the ship Tonquin around Cape Horn to found a trading post at the river's mouth. Eight men drowned trying to find a channel across the bar, and after landing the colonists the Tonquin sailed north to trade, where its abrasive captain insulted a local chief; attackers swarmed the ship, and a wounded crewman blew up its powder magazine, killing everyone aboard. Fort Astoria survived — the first American settlement on the Pacific coast — but during the War of 1812 Astor's partners sold it to the British North West Company. When a British warship arrived to capture it, the captain found the fort had already changed hands and could only run up his flag.`,`In the late nineteenth century salmon made Astoria rich. Dozens of canneries lined the waterfront on pilings, staffed largely by Chinese crews and supplied by Finnish, Swedish and Norwegian fishermen who filled the hillside neighbourhoods with churches, saunas and union halls. The salmon runs collapsed under overfishing and dams, the canneries closed one by one, and Astoria settled into its role as a small, steep port with a lot of history and a famous view. Hollywood found it in the 1980s — The Goonies was filmed here — and the Columbia River Maritime Museum now tells the story of the bar that has sunk around two thousand ships.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Washington Irving immortalised the Astor venture in his 1836 book Astoria, turning a commercial failure into an epic of the frontier. The Tonquin disaster happened at Clayoquot Sound on Vancouver Island, where the Tla-o-qui-aht people had reason to resent the captain's contempt. The fur post at the river mouth, renamed Fort George under the British, became a minor depot of the Hudson's Bay Company after it moved its headquarters upriver to Fort Vancouver, and the United States only gained clear title when the boundary was settled in 1846.`,`The Columbia River Bar, where the river's current collides with Pacific swells over shifting sand, earned the name Graveyard of the Pacific. Since 1846 a specialised corps of bar pilots has met every large ship offshore and guided it across. The rusted ribs of the Peter Iredale, a British sailing ship driven ashore in 1906, still stick out of the sand at Fort Stevens, and the Coast Guard trains its surf rescue crews on the bar because there is no harder water in the country.`,`On the night of 21 June 1942 a Japanese submarine, I-25, surfaced off the river mouth and fired seventeen shells at Fort Stevens, the old army post guarding the Columbia's south shore. Nobody was hurt and the fort's commander chose not to fire back, but it made Fort Stevens the only military installation in the contiguous United States attacked by an enemy during the Second World War.`,`Astoria's Victorian houses climb a hill so steep that some streets are staircases, and the Astoria Column on top, built in 1926 with a painted spiral frieze of local history, gives a view across the estuary to Washington. The 1966 Astoria–Megler Bridge, more than six kilometres long, ended the town's days as a dead end. Today the docks host sea lions, fishing boats and cruise ships, and the town's main worry is less the bar than the housing squeeze as remote workers discover a cool, cheap-ish, foggy port.`]},"port-townsend-wa":{why:`Olympic rain shadow and a Strait gap share the same hinge. Storms dump on the west Olympics, then the air descending toward Discovery Bay and Admiralty Inlet arrives drier and often sunnier than Seattle, only sixty kilometres south. The Strait of Juan de Fuca also channels wind and marine cloud, so mildness comes with exposure rather than stillness. Port Townsend's Victorian streets sit in that double geography: less rain than the west-end rainforest, more wind than a sheltered Puget cove, and long June evenings at forty-eight degrees north.`,immersive:`Port Townsend sits in the same Olympic rain shadow as Sequim but with an exposed hinge-point character: the Strait of Juan de Fuca channels both sun and wind in from the west. The result is a mild, partly-sunny maritime climate with roughly half the rainfall of Seattle only 60 km south. Fort Worden's bluff takes that strait wind full in the face; the Victorian streets a kilometre inland feel kinder. Garry oak and madrone mark the drier ground. June evenings run long at 48°N, a light people move here for. The preserved downtown is a byproduct of the place's late-19th-century boomtown misfire; the climate is the daily reason people stay, wind and ferry weather included.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey but gentle — afternoons near 8°C, nights near 2°C, and storms that arrive already wrung out by the Olympics, so the town gets roughly half of Seattle's winter rain. The real weather is wind: southeasterly gales funnel up Admiralty Inlet and cancel ferries to Whidbey Island when the chop builds. Snow comes most winters but rarely lasts.`,summer:`Bright, breezy, and seldom hot — afternoons near 22°C, nights near 11°C, and long northern evenings that stay light until almost ten. A westerly off the Strait of Juan de Fuca fills the sails in the bay and cools the bluff at Fort Worden; fog sometimes slides in at dawn and burns away by mid-morning.`},contrasts:[{label:`Seattle (≈60 km SE)`,note:`Outside the rain shadow, Seattle gets about twice Port Townsend's yearly rain and cloudier winters, but warmer summer afternoons.`},{label:`Sequim (≈50 km W)`,note:`Deeper in the Olympic lee, Sequim is slightly drier and calmer; Port Townsend trades a little extra rain for its constant Strait breeze.`}],history:[`In the 1880s Port Townsend was certain it was about to become the San Francisco of the north. It was the official port of entry for Puget Sound, every ship coming in from the Pacific stopped there to clear customs, and the railroads, everyone agreed, would surely end at its deep harbour. Speculators threw up brick hotels, banks and mansions on the bluff, and the waterfront filled with saloons, brothels and boarding houses where sailors were sometimes drugged and shanghaied onto outbound ships. Then the railroads chose Seattle and Tacoma, the depression of 1893 arrived, and the boom died almost overnight — leaving one of the best-preserved Victorian seaports in America.`,`The S'Klallam village of qatáy stood on the beach long before any of that, facing the meeting of the Strait of Juan de Fuca and Admiralty Inlet. Its leader, Chetzemoka, whom settlers nicknamed the Duke of York, befriended the first Americans who arrived in 1851 and tried for years to keep the peace as their numbers grew. After the 1855 Treaty of Point No Point, officials pressed the S'Klallam to move to a distant reservation, and in 1871 an Indian agent burned the remaining houses on the beach. Many families returned to nearby shores anyway; their descendants form the Jamestown, Port Gamble and Lower Elwha Klallam tribes.`,`At the turn of the century the army built Fort Worden on the bluff above town, one corner of a triangle of gun batteries meant to stop enemy fleets from entering Puget Sound. The guns never fired in anger, and the fort later became a state park — and the set for the 1982 film An Officer and a Gentleman. The Victorian buildings that nobody could afford to tear down became the town's fortune, drawing artists, wooden-boat builders and writers; the Wooden Boat Festival has filled the harbour every September since 1977. The paper mill on the edge of town still runs, and the ferry to Whidbey Island remains the town's front door.`],deepTitle:`City of dreams`,deep:[`Port Townsend's boom was built almost entirely on expectation. Businessmen raised the Hastings Building, the Hotel Waterstreet and grand homes like the Ann Starrett Mansion, and when rumours spread that a transcontinental line was coming, prices soared; the connection never arrived. By the mid-1890s many of the brick blocks stood half empty, and the town shrank from several thousand people to a fraction of that. Its nickname, the City of Dreams, was meant at first without irony.`,`Chetzemoka was born at qatáy and visited San Francisco as a young man, returning convinced that the newcomers could not be driven away and must be lived with. He is buried in the town cemetery, and a park on the bluff carries his name. The village site is now marked by an interpretive trail with S'Klallam place names, and canoe families from across the Salish Sea land on the beach at Fort Worden during the annual Tribal Canoe Journeys.`,`Fort Worden, Fort Flagler on Marrowstone Island and Fort Casey on Whidbey Island were built in the 1890s and early 1900s with disappearing guns that rose over concrete walls to fire and then dropped back out of sight. Aircraft made them obsolete within a generation. After the army left, the barracks, officers' row and the balloon hangar were turned into an arts centre, conference grounds and the Port Townsend Marine Science Center.`,`The waterfront's rowdy reputation was earned. Sailors, loggers and fishermen crowded the bars on Water Street, while respectable families lived uptown on the bluff and, legend has it, had a separate set of stairs to avoid walking through the lower town. The rain-shadow sunshine that the town now advertises is real enough: Port Townsend gets roughly half of Seattle's yearly rain, and noticeably more of its summer sun.`]},"hood-river-gorge":{why:`A sea-level gap through the Cascades is the rarest kind of mountain pass: the Columbia cut a trench the weather can use. West of Hood River, marine air and gorge cliffs still feel like a rainforest edge; east toward The Dalles, the same corridor opens onto shrub-steppe. Pressure differences between cool coast and hot interior drive the famous gap winds. Orchards sit in that transect because irrigation and river-moderated winters work, while the wind that fills sails is the same gradient that dries the east end and funnels summer fire weather.`,seasons:{winter:`The gap works in reverse — cold air from the interior plateau pours west through the gorge on east winds, so winter here can bite harder than either Portland or the high desert suggests: afternoons near 5°C, nights below freezing, and the occasional ice storm when Pacific rain falls through that river of cold air and glazes every branch. Some winters it closes the interstate; between storms, fog lies along the water.`,summer:`Hot, dry, and wind-whipped — afternoons near 28°C, nights near 12°C, and a west wind that strengthens through the afternoon as the interior heats, filling the river with windsurf and kite sails. Rain almost disappears from late June to September, and in bad fire years smoke fills the corridor for days at a time.`},contrasts:[{label:`Bonneville & Multnomah Falls (west gorge)`,note:`Marine air keeps the western gorge wet and green, with two to three times the rain and mossy cliffs dripping year-round.`},{label:`The Dalles (east gorge, ≈30 km E)`,note:`Already sagebrush country — around 350 mm of rain a year, hotter summer afternoons, and colder, clearer winter nights.`}],history:[`Around 15,000 years ago, an ice dam in what is now the Idaho Panhandle gave way and released a lake holding more water than Lake Ontario in a matter of days. The water roared across eastern Washington and funnelled into the Columbia Gorge in a flood hundreds of metres deep, stripping the walls down to bare basalt and leaving its side streams to pour off the cliffs as waterfalls high above the river. It happened again and again, perhaps dozens of times. When a geologist named J Harlen Bretz proposed the idea in the 1920s, his colleagues laughed at him; it took forty years for aerial photographs and giant ripple marks to prove him right.`,`People have lived in the gorge since the floods receded, and its rapids made it the greatest fishing and trading corridor in the Northwest. Around 1450 an enormous landslide off the north wall dammed the river near today's Cascade Locks; the river eventually cut through, and the Cascades rapids that resulted lie behind the legend of the Bridge of the Gods, a land bridge that the Klickitat say collapsed when two mountain brothers fought over a beautiful third. Lewis and Clark lowered their canoes through those rapids in 1805. Oregon Trail families later tried to float their wagons down the Columbia on rafts, and so many drowned that a new road around Mount Hood opened in 1846 to give them a safer choice.`,`Engineers spent the next century taming the gap. The first steam locomotive in the Northwest, the little Oregon Pony, hauled freight around the Cascades rapids in 1862; locks opened in 1896; the Columbia River Highway hung a road of stone bridges and tunnels on the cliffs by 1922. Then in 1938 Bonneville Dam drowned the rapids for good. Three years later the government hired a young folk singer named Woody Guthrie for a month to write songs promoting the dams' cheap power; he produced twenty-six, including Roll On, Columbia. In 1986 Congress made the whole gorge a National Scenic Area, freezing its cliffs against development — and the wind that blows through it every summer afternoon turned it into a world capital of windsurfing.`],deepTitle:`Floods and dams`,deep:[`Bretz first described the scablands of eastern Washington in 1923 — channels and dry falls and gravel bars far too big for any river. He argued that only a single catastrophic flood could explain them, which sounded like biblical catastrophism to geologists trained to believe in slow, gradual change. The missing piece was the source: glacial Lake Missoula, whose ancient shorelines are still visible as horizontal lines on the hills above Missoula. When the evidence finally convinced the profession, Bretz was in his nineties; he was awarded the Geological Society of America's highest honour in 1979.`,`The Bonneville landslide, which geologists date to around the mid-1400s, pushed the river south and created a dam of rock and debris. Native accounts describe a time when people could cross the river on dry land. The story of Wy'east and Pahto — Mount Hood and Mount Adams — quarrelling over Loowit, Mount St. Helens, and destroying the bridge in their anger, joined the geology so neatly that it is now told at the steel Bridge of the Gods, a toll bridge built across the river at Cascade Locks in 1926.`,`Woody Guthrie's month with the Bonneville Power Administration in 1941 is one of the oddest episodes in American music. The agency drove him around the Northwest to see the dams and the farms they would water, and he wrote at a furious pace — Pastures of Plenty, Grand Coulee Dam, Roll On, Columbia. The film the songs were meant for was delayed by the war, but the songs outlived it, and Roll On, Columbia is now Washington's official folk song.`,`The National Scenic Area Act of 1986 drew a line around 1,200 square kilometres of gorge and gave towns like Hood River, The Dalles and Cascade Locks room to grow while keeping new houses off the cliffs. The Eagle Creek Fire of 2017 burned across the west gorge for months and closed the historic highway; the slopes it scorched have since sent rockfalls and debris flows onto the roads below. The climate gradient that makes the gorge so dramatic — rainforest at one end, sagebrush at the other — is now also a fire gradient.`]},"eureka-ca":{why:`Cold California Current water wells up offshore all summer, and onshore wind pastes that chill onto Humboldt Bay. The Coast Range walls the marine layer in place, so Eureka's annual temperature range stays tiny: fog and stratus in July, rain rather than frost in January. Redwood and Sitka spruce on the nearby slopes are fog-drip forests, not heat-driven woodland. Upwelling is the engine; the bay is the harbor that let mills ship the trees; the range is the fence that keeps inland heat from rewriting the waterfront.`,immersive:`Summer stratus slides over Humboldt Bay until the Victorian cupolas on Second Street disappear. Gulls stand on cannery roofs, and the air smells of mudflat and diesel rather than dust. Afternoon does not warm into a California beach day; the upwelling keeps the water in the low teens °C and the wind onshore, so sweaters stay on the chair. Winter is the rain season, not a freeze. That unchanging cool is why redwood lumber could be worked year-round here and why heat-seeking travelers often last a weekend, then drive inland for sunshine.`,history:[`For more than two centuries, ships sailed past Humboldt Bay without noticing it. The entrance is narrow, the bar is rough, and summer fog hides the coast for weeks at a time; an American fur trader slipped inside in 1806, and his discovery was promptly forgotten. It was found again from the land side, in December 1849, by a half-starved party of explorers led by Josiah Gregg, who had struggled for weeks through the redwoods from the Trinity River gold fields. Within months, ships loaded with gold seekers and merchants were crossing the bar, and Eureka — named from the Greek for I have found it — was laid out on the shore in 1850.`,`The Wiyot had found it long before. Their villages ringed the bay, and on Tuluwat, the island at its centre, they held the World Renewal ceremony that marked the centre of their world. On the night of 26 February 1860, as families slept after the ceremony, a group of settlers crossed to the island and murdered dozens of people, mostly women, children and elders, with hatchets and knives; other villages around the bay were attacked the same night. A young newspaper editor in nearby Arcata named Bret Harte wrote a furious condemnation of the killings and was forced to flee to San Francisco, where he went on to become one of the most famous writers in America.`,`Fort Humboldt had been built above the bay in 1853 to control the region's Native peoples. One of its officers, a homesick and hard-drinking captain named Ulysses S. Grant, resigned from the army there in 1854. Redwood made the city rich: mills lined the shore, and the lumber baron William Carson built the gingerbread mansion on Second Street that is now one of the most photographed Victorian houses in the country. As the old-growth forests were cut out, Eureka leaned on fishing, then on the university, then — in the hills behind it — on cannabis. In 2019 the city returned Tuluwat to the Wiyot Tribe, one of the first voluntary land returns by an American city.`],deepTitle:`Fog bay`,deep:[`Josiah Gregg was a famous author of Santa Fe trade lore who had come west for gold, and his expedition to find a seaport for the mines nearly killed everyone in it. The men quarrelled constantly — Gregg insisted on stopping to take scientific measurements while the others starved — and after they reached the bay the party split up. Gregg died of exhaustion and hunger on the return journey in 1850, before the town that grew from his discovery had even been named.`,`The Wiyot have spent decades working to get Tuluwat back. After the massacre the island was diked for a dairy and later used as a shipyard, leaving toxic waste in the ancient shell mounds. In 2000 the tribe bought back a first small piece with money it raised itself; the city returned forty acres in 2004 and the rest of its holdings in 2019, and the World Renewal ceremony was held on the island again in 2014 for the first time since 1860.`,`Grant's months at Fort Humboldt were among the unhappiest of his life. Separated from his wife and children by thousands of kilometres and bored by frontier garrison duty, he drank, clashed with his commanding officer and resigned his captain's commission in April 1854, leaving the army apparently for good. He returned to it seven years later at the start of the Civil War and ended it as the general who won the war and later president.`,`The Carson Mansion took a hundred men more than two years to build in the 1880s, largely to keep the mill crews employed during a slow season, and it was finished in redwood throughout. It now houses a private club and cannot be toured, which has only made it more famous. Across the bay, the Samoa Cookhouse still serves lumber-camp breakfasts at long tables, the last of the great logging cookhouses on the West Coast.`]},"point-reyes-ca":{why:`The peninsula juts into the California Current, so upwelling and marine stratus hit the outer ridges first. Summer fog frequency at the lighthouse is among the highest on the West Coast; a few kilometres inland, Olema Valley can be sunlit and several degrees warmer on the same afternoon. The San Andreas Fault is the reason for that geometry: the point is a granitic block sliding northwest, with steep aspects and a narrow neck at the fault trough. Dairy pasture persists because the cool never really lifts.`,immersive:`Dairy fog drips from bishop pine onto a ridge fence while the outer beach is a white roar you hear before you see. Tule elk graze Tomales Point in wind that never quite dries the grass. Drive eight kilometres toward Point Reyes Station and the stratus tears; cowbirds and sun appear as if a different county began. That gradient is peninsula geometry plus upwelling, not a mood. The ranches work because summer stays forage-cool, and the lighthouse keeps its reputation because the point sticks into the current like a pier into a refrigerator.`,seasons:{winter:`The green season — afternoons near 15°C, nights near 6°C, and Pacific storms that turn the dry gold hills emerald within a few weeks. Between storms the air is the clearest of the year. From the lighthouse in January you can watch grey whales migrating south, and elephant seals haul out to fight and give birth on the beaches below Chimney Rock.`},contrasts:[{label:`Point Reyes Lighthouse (outer headland)`,note:`Often buried in fog and wind on summer afternoons when the village is sunny — one of the foggiest, windiest spots on the Pacific coast.`},{label:`Petaluma (≈35 km E, inland)`,note:`Past the coastal ridges the marine layer thins; summer afternoons run several degrees warmer and clear nights fall colder.`}],history:[`In June 1579 Francis Drake, halfway through his voyage around the world and with his ship the Golden Hind leaking and crammed with Spanish treasure, put into a bay on this coast to repair her. He stayed about five weeks, traded with the local people, claimed the land for Queen Elizabeth as Nova Albion, and nailed a brass plate to a post. Historians have argued over exactly where he landed for four centuries, but many believe it was Drakes Bay, under the white cliffs of Point Reyes — the cliffs, Drake's men wrote, reminded them of England. Sixteen years later a Spanish galleon, the San Agustín, was wrecked in the same bay; the Coast Miwok salvaged its Chinese porcelain, and shards still turn up in their village sites.`,`The Coast Miwok had lived around Tomales Bay and the peninsula for thousands of years. The mission at San Rafael, founded in 1817, drew most of them away within a few years, and Mexican land grants carved the peninsula into ranchos. After the American conquest a San Francisco law firm, Shafter, Shafter, Park and Heydenfeldt, won the long fight over titles and in the 1860s divided the land into dairy ranches it leased to tenants, named alphabetically from A to Z. Butter from Point Reyes, carried by schooner across the bay, became famous in San Francisco.`,`On 18 April 1906 the San Andreas Fault, which runs right through the valley beside Point Reyes Station, ruptured and threw the peninsula several metres northwest in seconds; a fence near Olema was famously offset by about five metres. In 1962 President John F. Kennedy signed the bill creating Point Reyes National Seashore, protecting the peninsula from the subdivisions planned for it, and the park bought out the ranches but leased many back to the families who worked them. In 1979 the Point Reyes Light, a tiny weekly newspaper, won the Pulitzer Prize for exposing the violent Synanon cult. Today the tension is over elk, cattle and water quality, and over who the seashore is really for.`],deepTitle:`Drake's bay`,deep:[`In 1936 a brass plate inscribed with Drake's claim was found near San Francisco Bay and hailed as the greatest relic in California history. Decades later scientific tests showed it was a modern forgery, made as a prank by members of a historical society to fool a gullible professor — the joke got out of hand when he announced it to the world. The real plate, if it existed, has never been found, and the landing site debate continues; in 2012 the government listed Drakes Bay as a National Historic Landmark for the landing anyway.`,`The San Agustín was a Manila galleon commanded by Sebastián Rodríguez Cermeño, sent to scout the California coast for a safe harbour. When it sank in a storm in November 1595, Cermeño and his crew sailed a small open boat all the way to Mexico. A few years later, on the feast of the Epiphany in 1603, the explorer Sebastián Vizcaíno named the headland for the Three Kings — Punta de los Reyes.`,`Point Reyes Station was a railroad town for the North Pacific Coast line, which carried passengers and milk until the 1930s, and the village still has the feel of a cow town with good coffee. The Point Reyes Light's Synanon investigation, by the husband-and-wife owners Dave and Cathy Mitchell and the Berkeley sociologist Richard Ofshe, ran when the cult was still powerful and threatening; soon afterward a Synanon member left a rattlesnake in the mailbox of a lawyer suing the group.`,`Tule elk were reintroduced to Tomales Point in 1978, more than a century after hunters wiped them out, and the herd grew until droughts trapped many behind the fence that kept them off the ranches. In 1995 the Vision Fire burned through Inverness and destroyed dozens of homes, and in 2014 the Drakes Bay oyster farm closed after a long legal fight over its lease in a wilderness area. Through it all the fog keeps coming: the lighthouse at the end of the point, built in 1870, sits in one of the foggiest and windiest places on the Pacific coast.`]},"truckee-ca":{why:`Truckee sits in a high Sierra basin where cold air drains off granite and lodges on the river flats. Clear nights radiate heat away; surrounding ridges can be markedly warmer than the town on still winter mornings. Lake Tahoe, a short drive south, stores heat and breaks up that pooling, which is why lakeshore nights feel different from the depot. Elevation near 1810 m sets the snow climate; basin geometry sets the frost. Wildfire on the surrounding mixed-conifer slopes is the summer counterpart to that winter machine.`,immersive:`Dawn frost silvers the Truckee River while the depot clock is still in shadow and the ridges already catch sun. Exhaust from idling snowplows hangs in the inversion until a breeze down canyon tears it. Twenty minutes toward Tahoe's shore, the same night is milder because the lake holds heat the basin floor cannot. That contrast is cold-air drainage, not a forecast quirk. Summer can still freeze in the hollows, which is why gardens are a gamble and why the town's identity is winter — rail, snow, and a valley that keeps the cold overnight.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, dry afternoons near 27°C and nights that crash to around 7°C — on the stillest mornings in the river flats, frost is possible even in July. Thunderstorms build over the crest some afternoons; the rest is clear mountain air, cold river swims, and a fire season that keeps everyone watching the ridgelines.`},contrasts:[{label:`Lake Tahoe shore (≈25 km S)`,note:`The lake stores heat and stirs the night air, so lakeshore mornings run several degrees milder than the frost-prone basin floor at Truckee.`},{label:`Reno (≈50 km E, across the crest)`,note:`About 440 m lower in the Sierra rain shadow, Reno gets a fraction of Truckee's snow, under a quarter of its precipitation, and hotter summer afternoons.`}],history:[`In the first days of November 1846, a wagon train of Midwestern families reached the lake just below the pass west of Truckee and found the way blocked by snow. They were late, having taken a disastrous shortcut, and the storm that caught them was the first of many in one of the snowiest winters on record. Over the next four months the Donner Party starved in crude cabins and tents; a desperate party on snowshoes tried to cross the mountains, and survivors on both sides of the pass ate the dead. Of the eighty-seven people in the party, roughly half survived to be rescued. The lake and the pass have carried their name ever since.`,`The Washoe had lived around Lake Tahoe and the Truckee River for thousands of years, fishing the lake in summer and moving to lower valleys in winter, and they knew exactly how much snow the pass could hold. The river itself was named for a Northern Paiute leader, called Truckee by the emigrants, who guided the first wagon party over the Sierra in 1844. Two decades later Chinese crews working for the Central Pacific Railroad blasted tunnels through the granite at the summit and laid track down the canyon, and in 1868 the railroad camp at Coburn's Station became the town of Truckee. Lumber, ice cut from ponds and shipped to Sacramento and San Francisco, and railroad work kept it alive — along with a famously rough main street of saloons.`,`The town's darker chapter came in 1886, when a local newspaper editor, Charles McGlashan, led a boycott that drove Truckee's large Chinese community out of town — a campaign so effective that it was copied across the West as the Truckee Method. The same McGlashan had written the first serious history of the Donner Party. In the twentieth century Truckee became a Hollywood stand-in for Alaska — Charlie Chaplin filmed scenes for The Gold Rush here in 1924 — and then a gateway to skiing after the 1960 Winter Olympics at nearby Squaw Valley. Interstate 80 and Bay Area money transformed it into a mountain town of second homes; it incorporated in 1993.`],deepTitle:`Pass and railroad`,deep:[`The Donner Party's ordeal was largely a matter of timing and bad advice. Leaving Illinois in the spring of 1846, they took Lansford Hastings's untested cut-off across the Wasatch and the Great Salt Lake desert, lost weeks and wagons, and reached the Sierra just as winter came early. The pioneer cabin site is now part of Donner Memorial State Park, where a monument's pedestal stands as high as the snow lay that winter: about 6.7 m deep.`,`Building the Central Pacific over Donner Pass was one of the great engineering feats of the century. Thousands of Chinese labourers drilled the Summit Tunnel through solid granite, working through winters in which avalanches buried camps; snowsheds kilometres long were later built over the tracks to keep the line open. The labourers were paid less than white workers and are barely named in the company records, but the tunnels they cut are still in use, and the old ones can be walked.`,`The pass has never stopped testing travellers. In January 1952 the luxury streamliner City of San Francisco was trapped by drifts near Donner Pass with more than two hundred people aboard; it took three days to dig them out, and two railroad men died in the rescue effort. Interstate 80 still closes in big storms, with chain controls and spun-out trucks a winter ritual.`,`Truckee regularly records some of the coldest temperatures in the lower forty-eight. The town sits in a basin where cold air slides off the surrounding mountains on still nights and pools along the river, so a clear winter morning can be far colder than the lake shore just over the hill. The ice ponds of the nineteenth century were built to exploit exactly that.`]},"mammoth-lakes-ca":{why:`Mammoth sits on the Sierra's east flank, high enough to catch Pacific storms as snow before the air drops into the Owens Valley rain shadow. Elevation near 2424 m dominates temperature; the partial lee of higher crests to the west keeps the sky oftener clear than the west slope. Long Valley Caldera's volcanic plateau adds pumice soils, geothermal seeps, and a rain-shadow steppe a few minutes downhill. UV is intense, nights are cold, and the ski hill is simply the place where orographic snow still stacks on that eastern ramp.`,immersive:`Chairlift cables tick over pumice while the Minarets hold a hard blue that west-side forests rarely see. Snowpack is the water here, not summer rain, and the sun at this elevation is a physical weight on snow and skin. Down-canyon toward Bishop the sage begins in minutes; uphill, lodgepole and red fir still own the caldera rim. That is east-Sierra geography in one drive: storm snow on the ramp, rain shadow in the valley, volcanic ground underfoot. Night cold arrives fast when the sky clears, which it often does after the flakes stop.`,seasons:{summer:`Bright and thin-aired — afternoons near 25°C, nights near 6°C, and a sun at 2,400 m that burns faster than the thermometer suggests. Snow lingers on the high peaks into July while the lakes open for fishing and the shuttle runs down to Devils Postpile. Thunderheads sometimes build over the crest in the afternoon, and smoke from Sierra fires is the season's wildcard.`},contrasts:[{label:`Bishop (≈65 km SE, 1,200 m lower)`,note:`Down in the Owens Valley rain shadow, summer afternoons run about 10°C hotter and the year brings roughly a third of Mammoth's precipitation.`},{label:`Yosemite Valley (across the crest)`,note:`On the west slope and far lower, Yosemite catches more Pacific rain but little lasting snow on the valley floor, and its summers run much hotter.`}],history:[`About 760,000 years ago, the ground under Mammoth Lakes blew apart in one of the largest volcanic eruptions in North American history. Hundreds of cubic kilometres of ash and pumice spread across the West, and the roof of the emptied magma chamber collapsed into a basin 30 km long — the Long Valley Caldera, whose rim the ski area now sits on. The volcano is not entirely asleep. In May 1980 four magnitude-6 earthquakes rattled the town within two days, the ground bulged, and the county built a new road, still called the Scenic Loop, as an emergency escape route. In the 1990s carbon dioxide seeping from below began killing trees around Horseshoe Lake.`,`For thousands of years Paiute and Mono families came up into these mountains in summer to hunt, gather pine nuts and quarry obsidian, the volcanic glass that made the sharpest arrowheads in the West and was traded far across the Sierra. Prospectors arrived chasing the legend of the Lost Cement Mine, a vein supposedly studded with gold nuggets, and in 1878 a boomtown called Mammoth City sprang up with a mill, hotels and a thousand hopeful miners. Within a few years the ore had run out and the town was abandoned. In 1911, President Taft protected the great columns of basalt at Devils Postpile, which had been slated to be blasted for a dam.`,`Mammoth's modern history belongs largely to one man. Dave McCoy, a Los Angeles water department hydrographer who measured the snowpack on skis, began running rope tows in the eastern Sierra in the 1930s and won a Forest Service permit for Mammoth Mountain in 1953, after other investors had decided it was too high, too windy and too remote. His first chairlift opened in 1955. The family company grew into one of the biggest ski areas in America, McCoy sold it in 2005, and he lived to be 104. The town incorporated in 1984 and still lives on snow, Southern California weekends and summer visitors to the lakes and hot springs.`],deepTitle:`Caldera ski town`,deep:[`The eruption that formed Long Valley left a layer of welded ash called the Bishop Tuff across the Owens Valley, and its ash has been found as far away as Nebraska. Geologists have watched the caldera closely since the 1980 earthquakes, and the U.S. Geological Survey still runs a volcano observatory network here. The hot springs and geothermal power plants around Mammoth exist because the heat is still close to the surface.`,`Mammoth City's boom attracted a mill hauled in at great expense, a newspaper, and plans for a railroad that never came. Its brief life is typical of the high-Sierra mining camps: optimistic claims, hard winters, thin ore and quick abandonment. A few ruins and the name of Mammoth Rock survive; the Lost Cement Mine has never been found.`,`McCoy's partner in building the mountain was his wife, Roma, and his early crew included local ski racers and returning soldiers. The family ran the area informally for decades, with McCoy known for driving the snowcats himself. Mammoth became famous as one of the few places in California where people could ski into June or even July in big snow years.`,`The town sits right at the edge where Pacific storms drop their last snow before the Owens Valley rain shadow begins, so a winter can bring towering snowbanks while Bishop, an hour south, sees almost none. That makes Mammoth's economy hostage to the snowpack: drought winters leave thin seasons and empty condos, while giant winters like 2023 bury cars and close roads for days.`]},"borrego-springs-ca":{why:`Borrego sits in a down-dropped desert bowl walled by the Santa Rosa and Vallecito mountains, so Pacific storms are wrung out before they arrive. The Colorado Desert subdivision of the Sonoran is already dry; basin geometry makes it drier and, in summer, a heat sink. Winters are clear because subsiding air and distance from the coast suppress cloud. Isolation from urban light is why the night sky is a civic asset. The same walls that create the rain shadow also trap summer heat and make the valley a seasonal town in practice.`,immersive:`Noon in July turns the valley floor into a vibrating sheet; the only reasonable motion is toward shade, then toward darkness when the Santa Rosas go indigo. Winter afternoons reverse the script: sharp shadows on ocotillo, a wind you can walk in, and a horizon that holds every star later. That seasonal split is basin rain shadow plus low elevation, not a spa slogan. Surrounding peaks near two kilometres high wring storms dry, then radiate heat back onto the playa. People who stay through summer are negotiating with a furnace; people who come in February are using the same geometry as a gift.`,seasons:{summer:`A furnace — afternoons near 40°C and higher, nights that only fall to about 23°C, and a dry heat that drives most winter residents away until October. Life shifts to dawn and after dark, when the Milky Way blazes over an almost lightless valley. Once in a while a monsoon surge pushes in humid air, distant lightning, and a flash flood down the washes.`},contrasts:[{label:`Julian (≈30 km W, in the mountains)`,note:`Up in the oaks and pines at about 1,300 m, Julian gets several times Borrego's rain, occasional winter snow, and summer afternoons about 10°C cooler.`},{label:`Palm Springs (≈90 km N)`,note:`Another low-desert furnace in the lee of the San Jacinto range, but with a city's worth of lights — Borrego keeps the dark sky.`}],history:[`On Christmas Eve 1775, in a cold canyon at the northern end of Borrego Valley, a woman named María Gertrudis Rivas, wife of the soldier Ignacio Linares, gave birth to a son. She was one of about 240 colonists Juan Bautista de Anza was leading from Sonora to found the Spanish settlement at San Francisco, and the baby, Salvador Ignacio Linares, is often counted among the first children of European descent born in Alta California. Anza had scouted the route through Coyote Canyon the year before. The canyon's springs had long been known to the Cahuilla and Kumeyaay peoples, who moved between desert palm oases in winter and mountain oak groves in summer.`,`After the Spanish passed through, the valley stayed remote. The Butterfield Overland Mail ran stagecoaches across the desert to the south in the late 1850s, and a trapper and horse thief named Thomas Peg Leg Smith told a story about finding black-crusted gold nuggets on a desert butte — a lost mine that prospectors have been hunting ever since. Homesteaders and cattlemen trickled into Borrego Valley in the early twentieth century, and in 1933 California created a desert state park around them; merged with a neighbouring park in 1957, Anza-Borrego became the largest state park in California, with the town as a private island in the middle.`,`After the Second World War, Borrego Springs grew into a winter resort of golf courses, citrus groves and retirees, all drinking from an aquifer with almost no natural recharge. In 2009 it became California's first International Dark Sky Community, and its unlit streets now draw astronomers. Since 2008 an artist named Ricardo Breceda has filled the surrounding desert with giant rusted-steel sculptures of mammoths, sloths and a sea serpent that seems to swim through the sand. The town's future hangs on water: state law now requires pumping from the overdrawn aquifer to fall by around three-quarters, which will retire most of the farms.`],deepTitle:`Desert inholding`,deep:[`Anza's 1775–76 expedition was one of the great overland migrations of the Spanish empire: families, soldiers, priests and around a thousand head of livestock walking more than 1,600 km from Sonora to the Pacific. Coyote Canyon, with its running water and willows, was a welcome stop after the dry crossing of the Colorado Desert, and the expedition spent several days there in a winter storm. The route is now a state historic trail, and the canyon is one of the few places in the desert where water flows above ground all year.`,`Peg Leg Smith's lost gold has inspired a peculiar local tradition. Since 1975 Borrego Springs has hosted the Peg Leg Smith Liars Contest, in which contestants compete to tell the most outrageous tall tale about the lost mine, preferably around a campfire and within a strict time limit. A stone pile at the edge of the park marks the spot where hopeful prospectors are invited to add a rock for luck.`,`The park's badlands record millions of years of climate change. Fossil beds in the Borrego Badlands and Vallecito Creek have yielded mammoths, camels, horses, giant ground sloths and sabre-toothed cats from a time when the region was wetter and grassy — which is partly why Breceda's sculptures depict those animals. After wet winters the desert floor erupts in wildflower superblooms that draw hundreds of thousands of visitors.`,`The groundwater reckoning is the most important story in town. For decades farms, golf courses and homes pumped far more water than seeped back in, and the water table dropped many metres. Under California's Sustainable Groundwater Management Act, the valley must bring its pumping into balance, and most of the citrus groves that once lined the road into town are being fallowed. Residents now debate what kind of town a dark-sky desert village can be without its farms.`]},"fort-davis-tx":{why:`The Davis Mountains are a volcanic pile lifted hundreds of metres above the Chihuahuan floor, so pine-oak and cooler nights appear where the basins still bake. Elevation near 1478 m is the first control; monsoon moisture lifting over the massif is the second, yielding August storms the desert flats miss. Atmospheric transparency on this isolated range is why astronomers claimed Mount Locke. The sky island is therefore a temperature and humidity island, not a metaphor, with a dry grassland sea around it.`,immersive:`Evening on the scenic loop smells of juniper and warm basalt after a monsoon cell dies over Mount Livermore. The desert floor to the south still holds the day's heat, but in town the air has already slipped toward sweater weather. Stars come on hard because the range sits far from city light and above much of the basin dust. That is volcanic uplift plus isolation: a Madrean woodland perched in West Texas, with McDonald Observatory's domes as the obvious monuments to the same clear, dry column of air. Ranch gates along the loop still outnumber hotels, which is the point of the isolation.`,history:[`In 1880 a second lieutenant named Henry Ossian Flipper reported for duty at Fort Davis. Born into slavery in Georgia, he had just become the first Black graduate of West Point, and he was the only Black officer in a post whose enlisted ranks were full of Black cavalrymen. He served well — he engineered a drainage ditch that kept malaria at bay at another fort — but in 1881 he was accused of embezzling commissary funds, acquitted of the charge, and dismissed from the army anyway for conduct unbecoming an officer. He spent the rest of his life as a surveyor and engineer, trying to clear his name. In 1999, nearly sixty years after his death, President Clinton granted him a full pardon.`,`The fort he served at was built in 1854 to guard the road from San Antonio to El Paso, where the springs of Limpia Creek offered water, grass and timber in a land controlled by Mescalero Apache and Comanche raiders. It was named for the Secretary of War, Jefferson Davis, who would later lead the Confederacy. In 1859 the army even tested camels in these mountains, marching a caravan through the Trans-Pecos to see if they could replace mules in the desert; they could, but the Civil War ended the experiment. Abandoned during that war, the post was rebuilt in stone after 1867 and garrisoned by the Black regiments known as Buffalo Soldiers until the raiding ended and the army closed it in 1891.`,`In 1926 a reclusive banker from Paris, Texas, named William Johnson McDonald left most of his fortune to the University of Texas to build an astronomical observatory. His relatives sued, arguing that a man who left his money to stargazing must have been insane; the university won. Astronomers chose the summit of Mount Locke above Fort Davis for its dark, dry, steady air, and in 1939 the McDonald Observatory dedicated what was then the second-largest telescope in the world. The ruined fort became a national historic site in 1961, and today the tiny town lives on stargazers, birders, and travellers on the scenic loop through the highest mountains in Texas outside the Guadalupes.`],deepTitle:`Camels and stars`,deep:[`The Buffalo Soldiers of the 9th and 10th Cavalry and the 24th and 25th Infantry spent much of their time at Fort Davis building, scouting and escorting mail and wagons. In 1880 they played a central role in the campaign against the Apache leader Victorio, guarding the water holes of the Trans-Pecos so that his band could not survive in Texas; Victorio was driven back into Mexico and killed there that October. Their barracks, officers' row and hospital, rebuilt by the National Park Service, now form one of the best-preserved frontier forts in the Southwest.`,`The camel experiment was the brainchild of Jefferson Davis himself, who believed the animals could carry supplies across the deserts of the new Southwest. The army imported them from the Mediterranean in 1856 and tested them in Texas and California; one 1859 expedition passed through Fort Davis on its way to the Rio Grande, where the camels outlasted the mules on long dry marches. After the war the herd was sold off or turned loose, and feral camels were reportedly sighted in the desert for decades.`,`McDonald Observatory grew from that disputed bequest into one of the world's major research sites. The 82-inch Otto Struve telescope of 1939 was followed by larger instruments, including the Hobby-Eberly Telescope, one of the largest optical telescopes in the world. To protect the dark that makes all this possible, the surrounding counties adopted lighting ordinances, and the observatory's public star parties now draw tens of thousands of visitors a year to a town of about a thousand people.`,`The Davis Mountains are an old volcanic range lifted high enough to catch summer storms and support oaks and pines above the desert. In the 1930s the Civilian Conservation Corps built Indian Lodge, a pueblo-style hotel in Davis Mountains State Park that still takes guests. Most of the mountains remain private ranch land, which is one reason the night sky is so dark — and why the scenic loop drive, rather than hiking trails, is how most visitors see them.`]},"marfa-tx":{why:`Marfa occupies a high Chihuahuan plain between the Davis and Chinati mountains, some 650 m above the Rio Grande. Elevation takes the edge off basin heat; nights radiate hard through dry air, so diurnal swings are large. Summer monsoon cells build into anvils you can watch from the courthouse lawn because the horizon is empty. Continentality, not a marine layer, sets the climate. The famous sky is a function of altitude, aridity, and sparse light — the same ingredients that draw astronomers a half hour north to Fort Davis.`,immersive:`Marfa sits on a high grassland at 1470 m, some 650 m above the Rio Grande at Presidio. The altitude tempers what would otherwise be desert heat; summer afternoons peak near 33°C while nights drop to around 17°C as dry air radiates through a sky with almost no lid. Winter brings occasional snow and freezing fog. From the courthouse lawn you can watch monsoon cells build into anvils because the horizon is empty. That sky is altitude, aridity, and sparse light — the same ingredients that draw astronomers a half hour north to Fort Davis — with thunderstorms that can climb 15 km. Continentality, not a marine layer, sets the year. Water on the high plain is limited; Alpine remains the grocery-and-hospital fallback.`,seasons:{winter:`Sunny days and freezing nights — afternoons near 17°C under a sky with hardly a cloud, then a plunge toward 0°C after dark as the dry air lets the day's warmth escape. Now and then a blue norther or a rare dusting of snow sweeps across the grassland; wind and dust are the more common nuisances.`,summer:`Hot, dry June days near 34°C give way in July to the monsoon: anvil clouds tower over the Chinati and Davis mountains by afternoon, curtains of rain sweep across the grassland where you can watch them for kilometres, and evenings cool to about 18°C under a sky scrubbed clean.`},history:[`In 1883 a cowhand named Robert Reed Ellison, driving cattle across the grassland east of town, saw lights flickering on the horizon toward the Chinati Mountains and assumed they were Apache campfires. When he rode out to look, there was nothing there. The Marfa Lights have been reported ever since — glowing orbs that bob, split, and vanish — and explained by everything from ghosts to swamp gas to car headlights on a distant highway refracted through layers of cold and warm air. The town built them a viewing area on the highway, and on any clear night there is someone parked there, waiting.`,`Marfa itself began that same year as a water stop on the railroad being pushed across West Texas, in Apache and Comanche country that Spanish and Mexican ranchers had never managed to hold. A railroad executive's wife chose the name from a novel she was reading — local legend says The Brothers Karamazov, though later researchers think it was a Jules Verne adventure. The town became the county seat, built a grand domed courthouse, and grew around ranching and the army post of Fort D.A. Russell, which housed cavalry during the Mexican Revolution and became a training post and prisoner-of-war camp in the Second World War. In 1955 Hollywood arrived: Elizabeth Taylor, Rock Hudson and James Dean stayed at the Hotel Paisano while filming Giant.`,`When the army left in 1946, Marfa shrank. Then in the 1970s the minimalist sculptor Donald Judd, fed up with New York's galleries, came looking for space and light. He bought ranch land, an old bank and much of the abandoned fort, and filled its artillery sheds with a hundred gleaming aluminium boxes and its fields with giant concrete ones, all aligned with the horizon. The Chinati Foundation opened in 1986, Judd died in 1994, and the art pilgrims kept coming. Today a town of under two thousand people has galleries, a famous radio station and food trucks — and a short drive northwest, a fake Prada boutique sits alone in the desert as an art installation.`],deepTitle:`Lights and boxes`,deep:[`Scientists have studied the Marfa Lights more seriously than you might expect. Students from the University of Texas set up instruments in 2004 and found that most of the lights they recorded matched the headlights of cars on U.S. 67 many kilometres away, bent and magnified by the sharp temperature layers that form over the grassland on still nights. The explanation has not dented the legend, and nobody has convincingly explained Ellison's sighting, made long before there were cars.`,`Fort D.A. Russell began as Camp Marfa during the border troubles of the 1910s, when cavalry patrolled against raids during the Mexican Revolution, and grew into a major post; in the Second World War, the nearby Marfa Army Air Field trained thousands of pilots on the high, clear plain. After both were closed, the buildings sat empty until Judd and the Dia Art Foundation bought them. That accident — a rancher's county inheriting enormous empty military buildings — is why art on Judd's scale could land here at all.`,`Giant made Marfa briefly famous. The cast and crew filled the hotel and rented rooms in local homes during the summer of 1955, and townspeople worked as extras. James Dean was killed in a car crash weeks after filming ended, before the movie came out. Later directors returned for the same big, empty landscape: parts of No Country for Old Men and There Will Be Blood were filmed nearby in 2006.`,`The Prada Marfa installation, built in 2005 by the artists Elmgreen and Dragset outside the hamlet of Valentine, was meant to slowly decay in the desert as a comment on consumerism. Instead it was broken into within days, repaired, and became one of the most photographed sites in Texas. Back in town, the old Hispanic neighbourhoods south of the tracks, with their adobe houses, are older than the art scene and have felt the rising rents most sharply.`]},"sedona-az":{why:`Sedona occupies a bench where the Colorado Plateau breaks off the Mogollon Rim into the Verde Valley. At 1372 m it sits between Phoenix heat and Flagstaff snow. Red-rock canyons steer sun and shade: south faces bake, creek bottoms drain cold air at night, and summer upslope breezes slide along the cliffs. Monsoon cells stack against the sandstone and turn into the photogenic storms. The Goldilocks reputation is elevation plus canyon geometry, not a spiritual climate unique to vortex maps.`,immersive:`Sedona sits at 1372 m in the shelter of the Mogollon Rim's red-rock cliffs. It is high enough to escape Phoenix's 45°C summers — Sedona tops out around 35°C — and low enough to avoid Flagstaff's real winter, with only rare snow and occasional freezes. The canyon geometry channels cool upslope afternoon breezes in summer and drains cool air into Oak Creek at night, so a south-facing wall and a creek bottom can be different climates a short walk apart. The monsoon delivers thunderstorms against the cliffs, one of the West's most photogenic weather shows, and also the flash-flood risk the photographs omit. Oak Creek is the desert rarity that made farms, then wells, then a crowded trail town.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot and bright — June afternoons near 35°C under a hard sky, the red rock radiating heat into the evening, and swimming holes along Oak Creek crowded by noon. The monsoon arrives in July: storms tower over the Mogollon Rim, lightning flickers across the cliffs, and flash floods can roar down washes that were dry an hour before. Nights cool to around 16°C.`},history:[`In 1902 a Missouri couple named Carl and Sedona Schnebly applied to open a post office in their farmhouse on Oak Creek. Carl's suggestions — Oak Creek Crossing, Schnebly Station — were rejected as too long to fit on a cancellation stamp. His brother told him to name it after his wife, and the postmaster general approved it. Few American towns owe their name to the size of a rubber stamp.`,`The red-rock country had been home to people for far longer. Between about 1150 and 1300, Sinagua farmers built cliff dwellings in the alcoves at Palatki and Honanki and painted the rock walls around them; Hopi tradition remembers a red-rock place called Palatkwapi. Later the Yavapai and Western Apache hunted and farmed along Oak Creek, until the army forced them out. In the winter of 1875 about 1,500 Yavapai and Apache people were marched some 300 km from the Verde Valley to the San Carlos reservation; many died on the way. The Yavapai-Apache Nation, which later won back land near Camp Verde, remembers the march each year as Exodus Day.`,`Homesteaders planted apple and peach orchards along the creek, and the cliffs soon caught Hollywood's eye: dozens of westerns were shot here from the 1920s on. The surrealist painter Max Ernst lived in Sedona with Dorothea Tanning after the Second World War, and in 1956 the Chapel of the Holy Cross was built into the rocks south of town. In August 1987 thousands of people gathered on the red rocks for the Harmonic Convergence, and Sedona's reputation as a centre of energy vortexes took off. The town incorporated in 1988 and now hosts millions of visitors a year — its building rules so strict that the local McDonald's arches are turquoise instead of gold.`],deepTitle:`Red rock to vortex`,deep:[`The Sinagua — the name is Spanish for without water — were dry farmers who made the most of thin soils and seasonal rain. Their alcove villages at Palatki and Honanki were tucked into cliffs where the rock stored warmth in winter and shaded the rooms in summer, and their pictographs layer over even older rock art. Montezuma Castle, a five-storey cliff dwelling down the Verde Valley, belongs to the same people.`,`The 1875 removal was ordered over the objections of the army officers on the ground, and the march was made in winter, across swollen rivers, with little food. The Yavapai and Apache were not allowed to return to the Verde Valley for decades; when families began coming back around 1900, the best land had been taken. Today the Yavapai-Apache Nation runs a casino and businesses near Camp Verde, and its members are among the few residents of the area whose ties to the red rocks go back before the post office.`,`Max Ernst and Dorothea Tanning built a small house in West Sedona in 1946 and lived there for several years, painting the strange rock formations into their work; Ernst's sculpture Capricorn was made there. They were part of a trickle of artists who settled in the town, later joined by the Cowboy Artists of America, founded in Sedona in 1965 by a group of western painters in a local bar.`,`Sedona's vortex reputation dates largely from the 1980s, when psychics identified spots where the earth's energy supposedly swirled — Bell Rock, Cathedral Rock, Airport Mesa. The Harmonic Convergence of 1987 brought international attention and a wave of crystal shops, healers and tour jeeps. Geologists point instead to the layered sandstone of the Supai and Schnebly Hill formations, stained red by iron oxide. Either way, the visitors keep coming, the trailheads overflow, and Oak Creek — a rare year-round stream in the desert — remains the reason anyone could live here at all.`]},"prescott-az":{why:`Granite of the Bradshaw Mountains lifts Prescott some 1,300 metres above the Phoenix basin, into ponderosa and a four-season year by Arizona standards. Elevation lapse rate is the main machine: summer afternoons stay moderate while the desert floor is still a furnace. The monsoon still arrives as stacked afternoon storms. Winter can frost and occasionally snow. It is a transition-zone island, not a different desert, which is why Phoenix residents have used it as a heat refuge since territorial days.`,immersive:`Courthouse plaza shade is a different climate from the paved lot two blocks away: granite, ponderosa, and elevation doing the work air-conditioning does in Phoenix. July builds thunderheads over the Bradshaws every afternoon, then throws a brief cold rain on Whiskey Row. Winter nights can freeze; snow dusts Thumb Butte and is gone from the plaza by noon. That is mile-high Arizona — not alpine, not Sonoran floor. People who summer here from the Valley are following the lapse rate uphill, the same reason the territorial capital sat in timber instead of cactus.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm and bright through June — afternoons near 31°C, the forest at peak fire danger — then the monsoon: by July thunderheads build over the Bradshaws most afternoons, a short, cold downpour rattles the elms on the courthouse plaza, and evenings fall to about 15°C. It is the summer escape Phoenix drives up for, with the Fourth of July rodeo as its peak.`},contrasts:[{label:`Phoenix (≈150 km SE, 1,300 m lower)`,note:`Down in the low desert, summer afternoons run about 10°C hotter, nights barely cool, and the year brings less than half of Prescott's rain.`},{label:`Flagstaff (≈140 km NE, ≈470 m higher)`,note:`Up on the Colorado Plateau, Flagstaff runs cooler in summer and buries itself in several times Prescott's snow.`}],history:[`On the afternoon of 14 July 1900, a fire broke out in a miners' hotel on Montezuma Street, the row of saloons Prescott calls Whiskey Row, and swept the whole block. The customers of the Palace Saloon did not waste time: they picked up the bar — a massive slab of carved wood — carried it across the street onto the courthouse plaza, and carried on drinking while the town burned. The Palace was rebuilt in brick within a year, the bar was moved back inside, and you can still order a drink at it.`,`Prescott owes its existence to gold. In 1863 a party of prospectors led by the frontiersman Joseph Walker found placer gold in the creeks of the Bradshaw Mountains, and the new Arizona Territory's officials — wary of Confederate-leaning Tucson — chose to build their capital beside the strikes. They named it for William H. Prescott, a Boston historian of the Spanish conquest who never came west. Fort Whipple guarded the camp, and the Yavapai people who had lived along Granite Creek for centuries were driven out; in 1875 they were marched to the San Carlos reservation with the Apache. The capital moved to Tucson, came back, and finally left for Phoenix in 1889, but Prescott kept its territorial air: a courthouse square, Victorian houses and a rodeo that claims, since 1888, to be the world's oldest.`,`One of its mayors, William Owen 'Bucky' O'Neill, a gambler, sheriff and newspaperman, raised a troop for Theodore Roosevelt's Rough Riders in 1898 and was killed by a sniper in Cuba; his bronze statue on horseback stands on the plaza. In the twentieth century the old fort became a veterans' hospital, the Yavapai-Prescott Tribe regained a small reservation beside it, and retirees discovered the mile-high climate. On 30 June 2013 a wind shift trapped nineteen of the town's Granite Mountain Hotshots in a box canyon near Yarnell, south of town; all of them died, the deadliest day for American wildland firefighters in eighty years.`],deepTitle:`Gold and Whiskey Row`,deep:[`The Walker party's discovery set off one of the classic western rushes, with miners panning Lynx Creek and the Hassayampa River, which according to local legend makes anyone who drinks from it incapable of telling the truth. The territorial governor, John Goodwin, took the officials north to the diggings in early 1864 rather than risk Tucson, and they laid out a town of wooden buildings in the pines. The log Governor's Mansion survives today on the grounds of the Sharlot Hall Museum.`,`Sharlot Hall herself is one of Prescott's best stories: a ranch girl who came to Arizona by wagon in 1882, became a poet and historian, and in 1909 was appointed territorial historian, the first woman to hold a salaried territorial office in Arizona. She spent years collecting the stories and artefacts of pioneer families, and in the 1920s she rescued the abandoned governor's mansion and turned it into the museum that bears her name.`,`Prescott Frontier Days, held around the Fourth of July since 1888, started as a contest among cowboys from local ranches and has run almost every year since. Whiskey Row, rebuilt after the 1900 fire, kept its reputation through Prohibition and beyond, and the courthouse plaza, shaded by elms planted in the nineteenth century, is still where the town gathers for parades, concerts and the Christmas lights.`,`The Granite Mountain Hotshots were a city fire crew, unusual for an elite wildland team, and the loss of nineteen of its twenty members devastated the town. A memorial state park now follows the trail to the place where they died. The tragedy changed how crews are trained to watch for wind shifts in chaparral and focused attention on the thousands of houses built among the ponderosa pines around Prescott, where a growing population has moved into forests that evolved to burn.`]},"cloudcroft-nm":{why:`The Sacramento Mountains rise in one steep pitch from the Tularosa Basin, so Cloudcroft's mixed conifer and aspen sit more than 1300 vertical metres above the Tularosa floor. Elevation lapse rate does most of the work: summer afternoons stay mild while Alamogordo bakes. The range also lifts monsoon moisture, stacking thunderstorms on the crest that the gypsum basin below barely receives. It is classic Madrean sky-island behavior — a forest island over a desert sea — which is why El Paso once rode a train uphill for blankets in July.`,immersive:`Switchbacks off Alamogordo trade creosote for ponderosa in half an hour, and the air in the village smells of resin and woodsmoke even in July. Clouds snag on the Sacramento crest the way the railroad's name promised. Afternoon monsoon turrets build over the ridge while the Tularosa Basin stays a white glare far below. That contrast is the whole place: a 2667 m forest balcony over gypsum desert. Winter actually snows here. People come up from the basin for that reversal, then discover how thin the grocery shelves are when the highway ices.`,seasons:{winter:`Real mountain winter above the desert — afternoons near 6°C, nights near −5°C, and snow that piles up in the pines and lingers on north-facing slopes, with the little ski hill open when the storms cooperate. Between storms the sky is the hard blue of high altitude; down on the basin floor, Alamogordo stays bare and sunny.`,summer:`The great escape from desert heat — afternoons near 22°C while the basin below tops 35°C, nights near 10°C, and almost daily monsoon thunderstorms from July that soak the meadows; summer brings close to half of the year's rain. Mornings are clear and pine-scented; by afternoon, carry a raincoat.`},contrasts:[{label:`Alamogordo (≈25 km W, ≈1,300 m lower)`,note:`On the Tularosa Basin floor, summer afternoons run about 13°C hotter and the year brings roughly half of Cloudcroft's rain.`},{label:`White Sands (basin floor)`,note:`A sea of gypsum dunes glaring in the heat, seen from the village overlooks as a pale band far below the pines.`}],history:[`In 1898 the railroad builder Charles Eddy needed timber for his line across the desert from El Paso, and the nearest big trees grew on the crest of the Sacramento Mountains, more than 1,300 m above the Tularosa Basin. So he built a railroad straight up the mountain. The Alamogordo and Sacramento Mountain Railway climbed grades as steep as six per cent, doubled back on itself through a switchback and crossed some fifty-eight wooden trestles to reach the top in 1900, where a survey party had camped in the clouds and named the spot Cloudcroft — a croft being a clearing. Locals called it the Cloud-Climbing Railroad.`,`The mountains were the homeland of the Mescalero Apache, a high refuge above the desert where families hunted deer and elk and gathered piñon and the agave they roasted in pits; their reservation still covers much of the range to the north. Eddy, however, saw a second business in the cool air. Alongside the log trains he ran excursions for El Paso families escaping the summer heat, and built a grand resort hotel, The Lodge, on the summit. It burned in 1909 and was rebuilt, and in the 1930s it was owned for a time by Conrad Hilton. Beside it lies one of the highest golf courses in the country.`,`Trucks replaced the trains, and the last run down the mountain came in 1947. Cloudcroft settled into life as a summer village of cabins and a small ski hill, with Lincoln National Forest all around. In 1947 the government also began studying the sun from nearby Sacramento Peak, where the clear air made a solar observatory possible. In September 2018 that observatory, at the village of Sunspot, was suddenly evacuated and closed for eleven days with FBI agents on site, setting off a worldwide frenzy of alien conspiracy theories; the real reason turned out to be a criminal investigation into a janitor. Fire is the mountain's more serious threat, and the winding highway down to the basin is the only easy way out.`],deepTitle:`Rails to the clouds`,deep:[`The Mexican Canyon trestle, a curving timber span about 100 m long, is the best-preserved piece of the old railroad and can be seen from an overlook beside the highway. The line was a feat of mountain engineering built in about a year, with gangs of workers hand-laying track on grades that would have been impossible for most railroads. Logging trains brought timber down; excursion trains brought people up, and passengers sometimes got off on the steepest grades to walk alongside.`,`The Lodge has a resident ghost. According to the hotel's story, a red-haired chambermaid named Rebecca vanished in the 1930s after her lumberjack lover found her with another man, and she has been seen and heard in the building ever since; the hotel restaurant is named after her. The story is told with relish, and nobody has ever been able to identify the real Rebecca.`,`The Mescalero Apache Nation's lands to the north of the village hold Sierra Blanca, a sacred mountain whose summit rises above 3,600 m, and the tribe runs its own ski area and resort. In the nineteenth century the Mescalero were confined to a reservation at Bosque Redondo with the Navajo in the 1860s, escaped, and won a reservation in their own mountains in 1873. Their history of the range is far longer than the railroad's.`,`The road up from Alamogordo passes through the only highway tunnel in New Mexico, and in thirty minutes climbs from creosote and dunes to pine and aspen — one of the most dramatic climate transects in the Southwest. That same road is the village's lifeline. Ice, snow and fire can close it, and when the forest burns on the slopes below, smoke climbs straight up into the village from the basin.`]},"taos-nm":{why:`Taos sits on a basalt plateau at 2124 m beneath the southern Sangre de Cristo range, with the Rio Grande gorge cut nearby. Elevation and dry air make huge day-to-night swings; monsoon moisture hitting the mountains yields the dramatic afternoon storms. Winter is genuine at this height, then erased by intense sun. The famous light is thin, dry atmosphere over pale adobe and sage, not a gallery invention. Continentality plus altitude is the mechanism; the gorge and peaks are the local modifiers.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot sun and cool nights — afternoons near 28°C in thin, dry air, then a plunge to around 9°C after dark, a swing of nearly 20°C that makes evenings jacket weather even in July. Monsoon storms build over the Sangre de Cristos most afternoons from July, sending lightning and short, drenching downpours across the sage plateau, and the smell of wet sage drifts through the plaza.`},history:[`In 1898 two young painters heading from Denver to Mexico, Ernest Blumenschein and Bert Phillips, broke a wagon wheel on a mountain road north of Taos. They flipped a coin to decide who would ride into town to get it fixed; Blumenschein lost and made the ride, and the light, the mountains and Taos Pueblo so overwhelmed him that the two never left — or at least kept coming back. By 1915 they and their friends had formed the Taos Society of Artists, and their paintings of adobe, cottonwoods and Pueblo people helped sell the American Southwest to the rest of the country.`,`Taos Pueblo was already ancient by then. Its multistorey adobe houses, home to the Northern Tiwa people, have stood beside the stream from Blue Lake for many centuries, and the pueblo held trade fairs where Comanche, Apache and Spanish traders met long before the United States existed. It was at Taos that the leader Po'pay planned much of the Pueblo Revolt of 1680. In January 1847, soon after the American conquest, Pueblo and Hispano rebels killed the new territorial governor, Charles Bent, in his house in Taos; American troops answered by shelling the pueblo's church, where many had taken refuge, and killed more than a hundred and fifty people. Kit Carson's house stands a short walk from Bent's.`,`In 1917 the New York heiress and salon hostess Mabel Dodge arrived, married a man from the pueblo named Tony Lujan, and drew a stream of famous guests: D.H. Lawrence, Georgia O'Keeffe, Ansel Adams, Willa Cather. Decades later, after filming scenes of Easy Rider nearby, Dennis Hopper bought her house. The pueblo, meanwhile, fought for sixty years to recover Blue Lake, its most sacred site, which the government had absorbed into a national forest in 1906; in 1970 President Nixon signed a law returning it. In 1992 the pueblo became a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and it is still a living town with no electricity or running water inside its historic walls.`],deepTitle:`Pueblo and painters`,deep:[`Blumenschein and Phillips arrived at a town that was already a crossroads of cultures: the Tiwa pueblo three kilometres to the north, the Hispano plaza of Don Fernando de Taos, and a scattering of Anglo traders and mountain men. Kit Carson had made Taos his home base for decades, marrying into a local Hispano family; his house is now a museum. The painters' Taos was romantic and often staged — they posed Pueblo models in costumes and invented scenes — but it made the town an art colony that still has dozens of galleries.`,`D.H. Lawrence lived on a ranch in the mountains north of Taos in the mid-1920s, a gift from Mabel Dodge to his wife Frieda, and wrote there about the light and the pueblo. He died in France in 1930. Years later Frieda had his ashes brought to the ranch and, by the most persistent local story, mixed them into the cement of the small memorial shrine on the hillside so that nobody could steal them. The ranch now belongs to the University of New Mexico.`,`The return of Blue Lake was a landmark. For decades Taos leaders petitioned Washington, insisting the lake was the source of their life and religion and could not be paid for with money. The 1970 law returned some 19,000 hectares of mountain watershed to the pueblo — the first time the federal government returned a large area of land to a tribe for religious reasons — and it became a turning point for Native rights across the country.`,`Since then Taos has grown in layers. Ernie Blake, a German-Swiss immigrant who scouted the mountains from a small plane, opened Taos Ski Valley in 1955. Michael Reynolds began building off-grid Earthships from tyres and bottles on the mesa west of town in the 1970s, and the Rio Grande Gorge Bridge, opened in 1965, carries traffic about 200 m above the river. Through it all the pueblo keeps its own calendar, closing to visitors for ceremonies, and its people still farm with water carried in the old acequias.`]},"gunnison-co":{why:`The upper Gunnison is a high, broad valley with an outlet too small to flush winter air. Cold drains off the West Elk and Sawatch ranges and pools on the sage floor under clear Rocky Mountain skies. Light winds let the inversion thicken for days. Ridges can be dramatically warmer than town on those mornings. Elevation near 2347 m sets a short summer; basin geometry sets the famous cold. Monsoon storms punctuate July, then the pool rebuilds as soon as the nights lengthen.`,history:[`Captain John Williams Gunnison never saw the town that bears his name. In the late summer of 1853 he led an army survey across these high sage flats, looking for a route for a railroad to the Pacific, and pronounced the canyon below the valley too rough for tracks. A few weeks later, camped beside the Sevier River in Utah, his party was attacked at dawn by Pahvant Ute warriors avenging a killing by emigrants; Gunnison and seven of his men died. The river he had surveyed kept his name, and so did the county and the town that grew up beside it a generation later.`,`This was Ute country — the hunting grounds of the Tabeguache and other bands, who followed elk and bison across the basin and wintered in the lower valleys. Gold and silver strikes in the surrounding mountains in the 1870s brought prospectors pouring in; treaties carved the land away piece by piece, and after the Meeker Massacre of 1879 the cry 'the Utes must go' ended with most of the bands marched off to Utah in 1881. That same year the Denver and Rio Grande's narrow-gauge line climbed over Marshall Pass and reached Gunnison, which became a supply centre for mining camps and the cattle ranches that outlasted them. The upper Gunnison country also produced Colorado's most infamous tale: in the spring of 1874 the prospector Alferd Packer walked alone out of the snowbound mountains near what became Lake City, having set out with five companions, and was later convicted of killing them — and suspected of eating them.`,`In 1901 the state founded a teachers' college here; it became Western Colorado University, and its giant W, laid out in white stone on Tenderfoot Mountain, looks down on the town. Biologists from the college helped start the Rocky Mountain Biological Laboratory in the ghost town of Gothic in 1928. In the 1960s federal dams downstream created Blue Mesa Reservoir, the largest body of water entirely in Colorado. Through it all the basin's real claim to fame has stayed constant: on still winter nights, cold air drains off the surrounding ranges and pools on the valley floor, making Gunnison one of the coldest towns in the lower forty-eight.`],deepTitle:`Survey and sage`,deep:[`Gunnison's survey party was part of the great Pacific Railroad Surveys that mapped possible routes across the West in the 1850s. His men measured the Black Canyon downstream and concluded no railroad could follow it; when the Denver and Rio Grande did eventually squeeze a narrow-gauge line partway through in 1882, it was hailed as an engineering marvel. The deepest part of the canyon, now a national park, is so narrow and sheer that some stretches of the river see only minutes of direct sunlight a day.`,`The Packer case has never quite let go of the region. Packer said he killed only one man, in self-defence, after another had killed the rest; he was convicted of murder at Lake City in 1883, retried and convicted of manslaughter, and eventually paroled. A legend grew that the judge sentenced him by saying there had been only a handful of Democrats in the county and Packer had eaten them — the line was invented by a saloon wit, but it is still quoted. The University of Colorado named its student cafeteria grill after him.`,`The basin's wildlife includes one of America's rarest birds. The Gunnison sage-grouse, recognised as a separate species only in 2000, lives almost entirely in the sagebrush around Gunnison; in spring the males gather on leks at dawn to puff out white chests and pop air sacs in a strutting display that birders travel across the country to watch. The species is federally protected, and ranchers and the county have spent years negotiating how to keep its habitat intact.`,`Living in Gunnison means respecting January. Temperatures far below −30°C are not unusual on clear, calm mornings, cars need block heaters, and wood smoke hangs in the inversion over town. Summers, by contrast, are dry, sunny and mild, with cool nights that make the town a base for fishing on the Gunnison and Taylor rivers and for the ski and bike trails up the valley at Crested Butte.`]},"crested-butte-co":{why:`Crested Butte occupies a higher East River pocket, about 360 m above Gunnison, surrounded by peaks near 3500 m. Elevation makes summers short and winters deep; orographic snow on the west Elk Range feeds the ski hill. Paradoxically, on strong inversion nights the village can sit above Gunnison's colder pool. Monsoon-fed wildflower meadows are a July fact of snowmelt plus summer rain. The climate is subalpine Dfc, not a softer marketing season pasted onto a mining town.`,immersive:`July paintbrush and lupine crowd the Gothic Road while the butte's namesake summit still holds a snow seam. The air is thin enough that afternoon walking is a slower gear, then a thunderstorm erases the sun for twenty minutes and the petals steam. Winter reverses the palette to spruce, powder, and a grid of colored miners' houses. That swing is elevation in a closed high valley. Gunnison, downriver, can be colder at dawn; here the snow is deeper. The flowers are not a festival logo. They are snowpack plus monsoon on subalpine till.`,seasons:{winter:`Deep and frozen — afternoons hovering around freezing, nights near −15°C and far colder on still mornings, and snow that stacks up all winter until banks bury the miners' cabins to the eaves. Storms rolling in from the west unload on the Elk Mountains; between them the sky turns a thin, brilliant blue. The road over Kebler Pass closes for the season, and the valley becomes a cul-de-sac.`,summer:`Short, cool, and flowering — afternoons near 23°C, nights near 6°C with frost never quite ruled out, and meadows that erupt in lupine, paintbrush and mule's ears through July. Thunderstorms roll over the peaks most monsoon afternoons, so hikers and bikers aim to be off the ridges by early afternoon.`},contrasts:[{label:`Gunnison (≈45 km S, ≈360 m lower)`,note:`Down in the broad basin, Gunnison gets less than half the precipitation and far less snow, warmer summer afternoons — and, on still winter nights, the colder pool.`},{label:`Aspen (≈30 km N across the Elk Mountains)`,note:`Just over the range as the crow flies — a long hike or a very long drive — with a similar subalpine climate and a far bigger resort economy.`}],history:[`In the autumn of 1976 a group of Crested Butte locals took their battered old balloon-tyred bicycles over Pearl Pass, a rough 3,800-m mining road, and coasted down the other side into Aspen, partly to prove that they could and partly to tweak the swankier town's nose. Riders from Marin County in California joined them in 1978, and the Pearl Pass Tour became one of the founding rides of mountain biking. For a quarter of a century the town was home to the Mountain Bike Hall of Fame, which opened here in 1988 — an unexpected legacy for a place that had nearly been a ghost town thirty years before.`,`The East River valley was Ute summer country until the 1870s, when silver and gold strikes in the Elk Mountains filled the hills with prospectors and the Utes were pushed out of western Colorado. Crested Butte was founded as a supply town for the mining camps, and in 1881 the Denver and Rio Grande arrived to haul away something more durable than silver: high-quality coal for the steel mills in Pueblo. On 24 January 1884 an explosion in the Jokerville Mine killed fifty-nine men, many of them boys. The town survived on coal anyway. Welsh, Italian, Slovenian and Croatian miners built lodges, saloons and the brightly painted wooden houses that line the streets today, and the Big Mine ran from 1894 until it closed in 1952.`,`When the mine closed, the railroad tore up its tracks and the town nearly died. Then in 1961 two businessmen, Fred Rice and Dick Eflin, bought a ranch below the mountain and started a ski area. Through the 1970s the town fought off a giant molybdenum mine planned for Mount Emmons, the peak locals call the Red Lady, which overlooks Main Street. The poverty of the 1950s left the Victorian mining town intact, and it became a National Historic District in 1974. In 1990 the state legislature named Crested Butte the Wildflower Capital of Colorado; in 2018 the ski area was sold to Vail Resorts.`],deepTitle:`Coal, bikes, flowers`,deep:[`The Jokerville disaster was one of the worst mining accidents in Colorado's history. Coal dust and methane exploded early in the morning shift, and rescuers spent days bringing out bodies; the dead were buried in the town cemetery, where their graves are still visited. The mine was reopened but never prospered, and the disaster fed the labour organising that made Crested Butte a union town for the rest of its coal years.`,`The Big Mine, run by the Colorado Fuel and Iron Company, produced coking coal that fed blast furnaces across the state. At its peak hundreds of men worked underground, and the company dominated the town's life until steelmaking changed and the need for Crested Butte coal disappeared almost overnight. By the mid-1950s many storefronts were empty — which is exactly why so many nineteenth-century buildings were still standing when skiers and artists arrived to save them.`,`The Red Lady fight became a model for small-town environmental campaigns in the West. When the mining company AMAX proposed a molybdenum mine on Mount Emmons in the late 1970s, residents organised, lobbied and sued, arguing that the mine would poison the water and destroy the town's character. The company eventually withdrew, and decades later the mineral claims were permanently retired in a deal that protected the mountain.`,`Gothic, an abandoned silver camp a few kilometres up the valley, has been home since 1928 to the Rocky Mountain Biological Laboratory, where scientists study wildflowers, marmots and pollinators. Their long records are among the best evidence anywhere of how mountain springs are arriving earlier: snow is melting and flowers are blooming weeks ahead of where they were half a century ago, which has consequences for every hummingbird and bee that depends on them.`]},"leadville-co":{why:`Leadville occupies the upper Arkansas Valley at 3094 m, between the Sawatch and Mosquito ranges, so every climate variable starts with thin air. Oxygen is about seventy percent of sea level; UV is intense; summer nights freeze easily because the basin still radiates under clear skies. Snow lasts. The surrounding fourteeners make their own weather, but the town's daily feel is elevation first, cold-air drainage second. Nothing about this is a typical Colorado ski-town climate — it is alpine-edge inhabited.`,immersive:`Harrison Avenue's brick cornices sit under a sky that always feels closer than in Denver. Walking two blocks at 3094 m is a lung lesson; July shade can still bite after sundown. The Sawatch wall to the west holds snow into summer while the valley floor tries to grow a lawn and mostly fails. Winter is the default season, long and bright. People lived here because silver and molybdenum paid for the thin air. The downtown is a mining ledger in masonry, not a resort invented for the altitude. Afternoon UV on brick can feel like a high desert even when the peaks still hold snow.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, bright, and brutally cold at night — afternoons barely reaching freezing, nights near −14°C and far lower on still mornings, and a sun at 3,100 m strong enough to melt a south-facing step while the shade stays frozen. Snow falls in steady instalments from October into May, the passes in and out ice over, and the brick storefronts on Harrison Avenue wear icicles for months.`,summer:`Brief and brilliant — afternoons near 20°C, nights near 4°C with frost possible in any month, and monsoon thunderstorms building over the Sawatch fourteeners nearly every afternoon. The thin air makes a stroll up Harrison Avenue feel like exercise, and by late August the aspens are already thinking about autumn.`},contrasts:[{label:`Denver (≈120 km E, ≈1,500 m lower)`,note:`Down on the plains summer afternoons run about 10°C hotter, winters are milder, and every breath carries noticeably more oxygen.`},{label:`Climax & Fremont Pass (≈20 km NE)`,note:`Higher still on the Continental Divide, the molybdenum mine at Climax sits in one of the coldest and snowiest spots in Colorado.`}],history:[`In April 1882 Oscar Wilde, on a lecture tour of America, came to Leadville and talked about art to a hall full of silver miners. They took him down a mine shaft in a bucket, he ate supper at the bottom with the miners, and in a saloon afterwards he saw a sign over the piano that he later claimed was the most rational art criticism he had ever met: Please do not shoot the pianist. He is doing his best. Leadville in 1882 was the wildest town in America — a silver city of perhaps thirty thousand people at more than 3,000 m, with dozens of saloons, a brand-new opera house, and Doc Holliday soon to arrive.`,`It had started with gold. Prospectors found placer gold in California Gulch in 1860 and built a camp called Oro City, but the gold ran out and miners cursed the heavy black sand that clogged their sluices. In 1877 someone thought to have the sand assayed: it was lead carbonate laced with silver. Within two years the most famous boom in Colorado history had built Leadville. A shopkeeper named Horace Tabor, who had grubstaked two prospectors in exchange for a share of whatever they found, became one of the richest men in America when they struck the Little Pittsburg mine. He built the Tabor Opera House, divorced his wife Augusta, and married a young divorcée known as Baby Doe in a wedding attended by the President.`,`When Congress stopped propping up the silver price in 1893, the fortunes vanished. Tabor died broke in 1899; Baby Doe moved into a cabin beside his played-out Matchless Mine and lived there in poverty for more than thirty years, until she was found frozen to death in 1935. Leadville survived on zinc, lead and later molybdenum from the vast Climax mine on Fremont Pass, which supplied much of the world's molybdenum until a price collapse shut it down in 1982 and threw thousands out of work. In the Second World War the army's ski troops of the 10th Mountain Division trained at nearby Camp Hale, and after the war its veterans went on to found much of the American ski industry. Today the town calls itself the highest incorporated city in the United States and hosts a famously brutal hundred-mile mountain race.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Margaret Tobin arrived in Leadville in 1886 as a teenager from Missouri, married a mining engineer named J.J. Brown, and became rich when his mine struck gold in 1893. Years later, as Molly Brown, she survived the sinking of the Titanic, helped row and rally the passengers of Lifeboat 6, and organised aid for other survivors once they reached New York — earning the nickname the Unsinkable Molly Brown. Her small house in Leadville is a reminder that the town made more than one legend.`,`Doc Holliday drifted to Leadville in 1882 after the gunfight at the O.K. Corral, dealing faro and sinking deeper into tuberculosis and debt. In 1884 he shot a bartender who had threatened him over a five-dollar loan, and was acquitted of attempted murder. The thin, cold air did his lungs no favours; he left for the hot springs at Glenwood Springs, where he died in 1887.`,`In the winter of 1895–96, desperate to revive the town after the silver crash, Leadville's businessmen built an ice palace: a castle of blocks cut from frozen lakes, with towers nearly 30 m high, an indoor skating rink and a ballroom. It opened on New Year's Day 1896 to great fanfare, but a warm spell in March began to melt it, the crowds never matched the hopes, and by summer it was gone.`,`Camp Hale, built in 1942 in a valley north of town, housed some fifteen thousand soldiers training to fight on skis and climb in the mountains. The 10th Mountain Division later fought in the Apennines in Italy; its veterans returned to build ski areas such as Vail and Arapahoe Basin and to found ski schools across the country. In 2022 the camp and surrounding mountains became a national monument. A century of mining, meanwhile, left acid drainage and metals in the gulches around Leadville, and the cleanup of California Gulch as a federal Superfund site has run since 1983.`]},"durango-co":{why:`Durango occupies a north–south Animas River valley at 1988 m, tucked between San Juan peaks and the Colorado Plateau. The valley orientation and river corridor take the edge off Rocky Mountain winter compared with higher parks. Southern latitude helps. Summer monsoon still builds on the San Juans and throws thunderstorms down the valley. It is a foothill-mountain hybrid: cold enough for snow, mild enough that January afternoons can feel surprisingly open. Smoke from regional fires is the newer summer modifier.`,immersive:`Narrow-gauge steam hangs in the Animas corridor on a cool morning while the San Juans still hold last night's snow on the high peaks. Downtown brick warms faster than the canyon mouths; by afternoon a monsoon tower may be blooming over Engineer Mountain. Winter is real but shorter-feeling than in Gunnison or Leadville because the valley opens south toward Mesa Verde country. That is river-valley moderation at the mountain front. Fire season now paints the same pretty light a dirty orange, a reminder that the forested walls are fuel as well as scenery.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot, dry June afternoons near 30°C, then the monsoon: from early July, thunderheads build over the San Juans by lunchtime and roll down the Animas valley with lightning, gusts and a short, hard downpour that leaves the streets steaming. Nights fall to about 11°C, the river runs cold and green for tubers, and the steam train's whistle echoes up the canyon every morning — though in bad fire years the forest closes and smoke settles in.`},contrasts:[{label:`Silverton (≈60 km N, ≈850 m higher)`,note:`At the end of the railway in a high mountain bowl, Silverton runs about 8°C cooler in summer, gets several times Durango's snow, and stays white well into spring.`},{label:`Farmington, NM (≈75 km S, ≈350 m lower)`,note:`Down on the high desert of the San Juan Basin, summers run hotter and the year brings roughly half of Durango's precipitation.`}],history:[`In 1880 the Denver and Rio Grande Railway was pushing its narrow-gauge tracks toward the silver mines of Silverton and needed a town in the Animas valley. There already was one — Animas City, a few kilometres up the river — but its citizens, sensing a payday, held out for a better deal. The railroad's answer was to found a rival town of its own just downstream, lay out lots, and run the tracks straight past Animas City without stopping. Durango was booming within months. Animas City withered, and in 1948 the town that had tried to drive a hard bargain was quietly annexed by the one that replaced it.`,`The valley's history runs much deeper. Ancestral Puebloan farmers built pit houses along the Animas and, a short ride west, the cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde. Spanish explorers in the eighteenth century gave the river its haunting full name, El Río de las Ánimas Perdidas en Purgatorio — the River of Lost Souls in Purgatory. The Utes hunted and wintered here until treaties and the mining rush of the 1870s pushed them onto the narrow reservations that the Southern Ute and Ute Mountain Ute tribes still hold to the south. Durango itself was named by a former territorial governor who admired the city of Durango in Mexico.`,`The Silverton branch opened in July 1882, threading the Animas canyon on ledges blasted into the cliffs, and for decades it carried ore down to Durango's smelter. When mining faded, the line survived on its scenery, playing a supporting role in westerns from How the West Was Won to Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid. In 1956 Fort Lewis College moved onto the mesa above town, bringing a Native student tradition with roots in an old army post and boarding school. And on 5 August 2015, a federal cleanup crew accidentally breached the Gold King Mine above Silverton, sending a plume of mustard-yellow water down the Animas and through the middle of Durango.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Durango and Silverton Narrow Gauge Railroad is one of the longest-running steam operations in America, still pulling coal-fired locomotives built in the 1920s up 72 km of track from 1882. It has also turned into a sporting rival. In 1972 a cyclist named Tom Mayer challenged his brother Jim, a brakeman on the train, to a race to Silverton over two high passes. Tom won, and the Iron Horse Bicycle Classic has been run every Memorial Day weekend since, with thousands of riders trying to beat the steam engine up the mountain.`,`During the Second World War, the old smelter site on the edge of town was reworked into a mill that extracted vanadium and uranium from Colorado Plateau ore, some of it destined for the Manhattan Project. The radioactive tailings sat in piles beside the river for decades before they were hauled away in the 1980s and 1990s. Durango's later reputation as a clean, outdoorsy mountain town was built partly on that cleanup.`,`Fort Lewis began in 1878 as a cavalry post meant to watch the Utes, became a federal boarding school for Native children in 1891, and was handed to the state on the condition that Native students would always be educated there free of charge. The college that grew from it honours that promise today, and a large share of its students come from dozens of tribal nations — an unusual legacy for an institution born as a fort.`,`The Gold King spill released about eleven million litres of acidic, metal-laden water from an abandoned mine, turning the river an eerie orange-yellow for days and forcing the Navajo Nation, far downstream, to stop drawing water for its farms. It led to the Bonita Peak Mining District Superfund site, which wraps dozens of old leaking mines into one cleanup. Three years later the 416 Fire, blamed on cinders from the steam train, burned for weeks in the forest north of town and shut down the railroad for much of a summer.`]},"cody-wy":{why:`Cody sits on the east foot of the Absaroka Range, in the Bighorn Basin's rain shadow, so annual moisture is scant and sun is abundant. Chinook winds descending the Absarokas can erase snow in hours, which is why winter here is interrupted rather than continuous. Elevation near 1555 m cools the nights; the basin still warms in summer. The Shoshone River is the irrigated exception in a sage sea. Dry, windy, and bright is the mechanism — foehn plus shadow — not a Yellowstone climate copied downhill.`,immersive:`A January chinook arrives as a wall of warm dust off the Absarokas and the packed snow on Sheridan Avenue turns to running water by supper. Sage on the benches looks silver in that wind; cottonwoods along the Shoshone roar. Summer is a different dryness: bright, irrigated lawns against basin tawny, with Yellowstone's mountains a blue rumor to the west. That interruption of winter is foehn physics, air forced down the range and heated by compression. People who hate wind will hate the gift. People who want sun in January will understand why Buffalo Bill sited a town here.`,feel:`Cody feels like the Old West with a mountain wind at its back. Buffalo Bill's town sits on a high, dry sagebrush plain where the Shoshone River bursts out of a canyon in the Absaroka Range, about 80 km from the east gate of Yellowstone, under a sky that is huge and usually blue. At about 1,550 m, the air is thin and dry; summers are warm and bright with cool nights, and winters are cold but strangely interrupted, as chinook winds roar down from the mountains and melt the snow in a day.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, dry, and constantly interrupted — afternoons near 3°C, nights near −9°C, and light snow that rarely lasts, because a chinook roaring down off the Absarokas can lift the temperature 15°C in a few hours and turn the streets to slush by supper. Wind is the real winter weather, scouring the benches and stacking drifts against fences; between blows the sky over the basin is clear and very blue.`,spring:`The wettest and most unsettled season — afternoons climbing from about 8°C in March to about 20°C by late May, with upslope storms that can still drop wet snow into May and soak the foothills green. The east gate to Yellowstone opens in early May, and on the warm days between storms the cottonwoods along the Shoshone leaf out almost overnight.`,summer:`Dry heat and long light — afternoons near 28°C, nights cooling to about 11°C, low humidity, and short thunderstorms that blow in off the mountains with more wind and lightning than rain. The rodeo runs every night, the road to Yellowstone fills with traffic, and smoke from distant fires sometimes turns the evening sun copper.`,autumn:`Golden and quickly cooling — September afternoons near 23°C fading to about 8°C by November, nights dropping below freezing by October, cottonwoods turning gold along the Shoshone River, and the first real snows in late October. Elk bugle in the high country, and hunting season fills the motels.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the east gate of Yellowstone, the nightly summer rodeo, the five museums of the Buffalo Bill Center of the West, and the wild drive up the Wapiti Valley through red-rock hoodoos.`,contrasts:[{label:`Yellowstone Lake (≈80 km W, ≈800 m higher)`,note:`Up on the park plateau, winter is far snowier and colder, the lake stays frozen into late spring, and summer nights can drop to near freezing.`},{label:`Powell (≈35 km NE, basin floor)`,note:`Out on the irrigated floor of the Bighorn Basin, the year brings only about two-thirds of Cody's precipitation, with fewer of the chinook gales that rake the foothills.`}],history:[`On 28 January 1912, a baby named Paul Jackson Pollock was born in a house in Cody. His family moved away before he was a year old, and he grew up to drip paint across canvases on the floor of a barn on Long Island and become the most famous American painter of his century. Cody did not exactly claim him at first — this was a town built on the paintings of cowboys and horses, not abstract splatter — but the Buffalo Bill Center of the West now owns one of his works, a western homecoming for a man who left as an infant.`,`The town owed its existence to an even more famous showman. In 1896 William F. Cody — Buffalo Bill, then touring the world with his Wild West show — joined a group of investors to buy water rights on the Shoshone River and lay out a town in the sagebrush at the foot of the Absaroka Range. The river was then called the Stinking Water, for the sulphur springs that boiled up along its banks, the same steaming ground that the trapper John Colter had stumbled through in 1807 and that skeptical easterners mocked as Colter's Hell. The Crow, whose name for themselves gave the Absarokas their name, had hunted here for generations. Cody had the river renamed, brought in the railroad in 1901, built the Irma Hotel for his daughter, and promoted the road up the valley to Yellowstone's east entrance as the most scenic route in America.`,`His canal company could not raise enough money to water the basin, so in 1904 he turned the rights over to the new federal Reclamation Service, which built the Shoshone Dam in the canyon west of town; when it was finished in 1910, it was among the tallest dams in the world. Cody died broke in Denver in 1917, and to Wyoming's lasting irritation he was buried on Lookout Mountain in Colorado rather than above his town. During the Second World War, some 14,000 Japanese Americans passed through the Heart Mountain camp on the irrigated land northeast of town — among them a boy named Norman Mineta, who met a Cody boy named Alan Simpson at a Scout jamboree there. Both grew up to serve in Congress, and remained friends for life.`],deepTitle:`Buffalo Bill's town`,deep:[`Buffalo Bill Cody was one of the most recognised people on earth at the turn of the twentieth century, a former scout and buffalo hunter who staged reenactments of the frontier for audiences that included Queen Victoria. His Wild West show, with Lakota performers, sharpshooters like Annie Oakley and mock stagecoach attacks, toured Europe for years. He poured much of the money into Wyoming ventures — the town, a hunting lodge called Pahaska Tepee near the park, ranches and mines — and lost most of it.`,`Shoshone Dam, renamed Buffalo Bill Dam in 1946, was built between 1905 and 1910 by crews working in a narrow granite canyon through brutal winters; the first contractor gave up partway through. At about 99 m high it was briefly the tallest concrete dam on earth, and its water turned the basin floor around Powell into farms of beans, barley and sugar beets. In the 1990s it was raised 7.6 m higher.`,`Heart Mountain held its incarcerated families in tarpaper barracks on a windswept bench, where winter temperatures fell far below freezing. In 1944 sixty-three young men from the camp refused to be drafted until their families' rights as citizens were restored; they were convicted in the largest mass trial in Wyoming history, and pardoned by President Truman in 1947. The camp's interpretive centre tells their story, and Mineta and Simpson helped build it.`,`Cody still calls itself the Rodeo Capital of the World and has held a rodeo every summer night since 1938, along with the big Cody Stampede around the Fourth of July since 1919. The Buffalo Bill Center of the West, grown from a memorial association founded in 1917 and a log-cabin museum of 1927, now holds five museums under one roof, including one devoted to the Plains Indians. On summer evenings a staged gunfight still breaks out in front of the Irma, and Buffalo Bill would surely approve of the ticket sales.`]},"missoula-mt":{why:`Five valleys meet at Missoula, a broad basin ringed by ranges, so winter high pressure can lid cold air hundreds of metres deep while ridgetops stay sunlit and warmer. The Clark Fork, Bitterroot, Blackfoot, and related waters supply the moisture and the fog. Summer reverses the script into heat and, increasingly, wildfire smoke that uses the same bowl. River-valley moderation is real compared with the high Divide; inversion geometry is why the city can feel trapped. It is a textbook mountain-valley climate, not a generic northern Rockies average.`,immersive:`January fog sits on Higgins Avenue while the Rattlesnake ridgetops shine in sun a person can see but not feel. Woodsmoke and exhaust hang until a front finally scours the bowl. August can invert the discomfort: heat in the afternoon, then a smoke pall from a canyon fire that uses the same closed geometry. Between those seasons the rivers glitter and the larch on the benches turn gold. That is confluence topography. The university on the hill sits just high enough to remind the flats that altitude is a local escape from the pool.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey, still, and bottled up — afternoons barely above freezing, nights near −7°C, and long spells when a lid of cold air settles over the valley and holds fog, frost and wood smoke against the town for days while the surrounding ridges bask in sunshine. Arctic air sometimes pours in through the Hellgate canyon on a howling east wind. Snow comes in modest helpings, and the ski hill above the inversion is often the sunniest place in the county.`,summer:`Hot, dry and long-evening — afternoons near 30°C in July and August, nights cooling to about 10°C, and very little rain once June is out. People float the Clark Fork in inner tubes and fish the Blackfoot and Bitterroot, but August is also fire season, and in bad years smoke from fires in the surrounding forests pools in the same valley that traps winter fog.`},contrasts:[{label:`Ridgetops above town (≈600 m higher)`,note:`On winter inversion days Mount Sentinel and the Snowbowl ridges sit in bright sun above the fog, often warmer than the frosty valley floor below.`},{label:`Hamilton (≈75 km S, Bitterroot Valley)`,note:`Sheltered by the Bitterroot Range, Hamilton is a little drier and sunnier, with less of the lingering winter fog that clogs Missoula's basin.`}],history:[`On the afternoon of 5 August 1949, fifteen smokejumpers from the Forest Service base at Missoula parachuted onto a grass fire in Mann Gulch, a steep side canyon of the Missouri north of Helena. As they walked down the slope, the fire jumped the gulch below them and came roaring uphill. Their foreman, Wag Dodge, did something none of them had seen before: he lit a fire in the grass ahead of him, lay down in its ashes, and shouted at the others to join him. They ran instead. Two outran the blaze, Dodge survived in his escape fire, and twelve smokejumpers and a fire guard died. Missoula's Norman Maclean, the writer of A River Runs Through It, spent his last years investigating the tragedy; Young Men and Fire was published after his death.`,`Missoula sits where five valleys meet, a crossroads the Salish, Pend d'Oreille and Kootenai peoples had travelled for thousands of years to reach the bison plains, the camas meadows and the fishing grounds of the Clark Fork. The narrow canyon east of town became known as Hell Gate, after the Blackfeet war parties that waylaid travellers there. In July 1855 the territorial governor Isaac Stevens summoned the tribes to Council Grove, west of today's city, and pressed them into a treaty that ceded the valley and created the Flathead Reservation to the north. The Bitterroot Salish under Chief Charlo refused to leave their home valley for decades, until they were finally moved in 1891.`,`A trading post at Hell Gate became a town after 1864, when two merchants built a sawmill and a flour mill on Rattlesnake Creek, and the Mullan Road brought wagon traffic between Fort Benton and the Columbia. The Northern Pacific arrived in 1883, the University of Montana was founded in 1893, and lumber and the railroad built the town. In 1916 Jeannette Rankin, who grew up on a ranch outside Missoula, became the first woman elected to Congress — four years before women could vote nationwide. She voted against entering both world wars, and in December 1941 was the only member of Congress to vote no.`],deepTitle:`Five valleys`,deep:[`In the summer of 1877 the Nez Perce were fleeing the army across the Bitterroot Mountains toward the buffalo country, and soldiers from the brand-new Fort Missoula threw up a log barricade across the Lolo trail to stop them. The Nez Perce climbed a ridge, walked around it and continued south, and the barricade was mockingly known ever after as Fort Fizzle. A few weeks later the army caught up with them at the Big Hole, and the long retreat continued toward its end in the Bears Paw Mountains.`,`Missoula's smokejumpers trace their history to 1940, when two men from the base, Rufus Robinson and Earl Cooley, made the first parachute jump onto a wildfire, in the Nez Perce National Forest of Idaho. The town became the national centre of fire science, with laboratories studying how fire behaves and a training base that still sends jumpers across the West. The Mann Gulch tragedy shaped that science: Dodge's escape fire, then unknown, became a technique taught to firefighters.`,`In the Second World War Fort Missoula became an internment camp for more than a thousand Italian men, mostly merchant seamen and World's Fair workers stranded in America when war broke out, and about a thousand Japanese immigrant men taken from their communities. The Italians, nicknamed the Bella Vista boys for the view, mostly remembered the place fondly; some married local women and stayed.`,`The Clark Fork carried more than water to Missoula. In 1908 a flood washed tonnes of mining waste from Butte and Anaconda downstream, where it settled behind the new Milltown Dam above town and slowly leaked arsenic into the groundwater. The dam was finally removed in 2008 as part of a giant Superfund cleanup, and the river now runs free again at the confluence. The valley that once collected mine waste still collects its own air, which is why Missoula has spent decades trying to tame woodstove smoke in its winter inversions.`]},"bozeman-mt":{why:`The Gallatin Valley sits between the Bridger, Gallatin, and Tobacco Root ranges, a foothill basin that catches chinooks and rain-shadow sun. Elevation near 1474 m keeps summer afternoons moderate; surrounding mountains wring moisture that the valley floor only partly shares. Winters are cold but interrupted by downslope warming. It is drier and brighter than Missoula's western-valley woods, cooler in summer than Montana's eastern plains. Yellowstone weather is next door, not overhead — the town's climate is the valley's, not the park's.`,immersive:`Late light on the Bridgers turns the limestone wall salmon while the Gallatin Valley floor is still a windy gold. A chinook can strip a January sidewalk to wet concrete overnight; the next week may be a hard north cold. Summer evenings cool fast enough for a sweater on the MSU mall. That swing is basin-and-range Montana: sun, elevation, and foehn. The university's brick sits on a bench that was supposed to hold a capitol. Growth has filled the benches with roofs, but the wind still owns the gaps between the ranges.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, bright and changeable — afternoons near 2°C and nights near −9°C, with arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −25°C for days, then a chinook that strips the sidewalks to wet concrete overnight. Snow arrives in light, frequent helpings in town and much deeper ones on the Bridgers, where Bridger Bowl's ridge collects the cold smoke powder the locals guard jealously.`,summer:`Warm days and sweater evenings — afternoons near 28°C by late July, nights dropping to about 10°C, and the brightest sunshine of the year. June is green and stormy, with thunderstorms building over the Gallatins; by August the valley browns, the rivers run low and clear for fly-fishing, and wildfire smoke can drift in and soften the light for days.`},contrasts:[{label:`Livingston (≈40 km E, over Bozeman Pass)`,note:`Where the Yellowstone River leaves the mountains, Livingston sits in one of the windiest corridors in America — milder winter afternoons, but gusts that can top 100 km/h.`},{label:`Big Sky (≈55 km S, ≈800 m higher)`,note:`Up in the Madison Range the ski resort gets several times Bozeman's snow, runs about 6°C cooler in summer, and keeps winter well into April.`}],history:[`In July 1806, on the long road home from the Pacific, William Clark led a party east across the Gallatin Valley with the young Shoshone woman Sacagawea, who had been captured near here as a child. Facing a wall of mountains, she pointed out a low gap that led to the Yellowstone River. Clark took her advice, and the gap — now Bozeman Pass, crossed by Interstate 90 — became the way east. Lewis and Clark had already named the valley's river the year before, after the Treasury Secretary Albert Gallatin, at the place where three rivers join to form the Missouri.`,`The valley was rich hunting ground for the Crow, shared and contested with the Blackfeet, Shoshone, Salish and others, and buffalo jumps in the surrounding hills show how long people had driven bison over the cliffs here. In 1863 a restless Georgian named John Bozeman blazed a shortcut from the Oregon Trail to the new gold camps at Virginia City, cutting straight through Crow, Lakota and Cheyenne lands; the trail set off Red Cloud's War, and the army abandoned it in 1868. Bozeman himself laid out a town in the valley in 1864 to sell food to the miners, and was killed on the Yellowstone in 1867 — by Blackfeet raiders, said his companion, though some historians have wondered about the companion.`,`In 1866 the merchant Nelson Story drove about a thousand Texas longhorns north through the Bozeman Trail's war zone, defying army orders to stop, and founded Montana's cattle industry; his fortune later helped give land for the agricultural college that opened in 1893 and became Montana State University. The Northern Pacific arrived in 1883, and Fort Ellis, east of town, launched both the 1870 expedition that helped make Yellowstone the first national park and, earlier that same year, the massacre of a peaceful Piegan Blackfeet camp on the Marias River. In the 1970s the television newsman Chet Huntley built Big Sky resort to the south, and in recent decades Bozeman has become one of the fastest-growing small cities in America — and one of its most expensive.`],deepTitle:`Valley of flowers`,deep:[`The Gallatin Valley's nickname, the Valley of the Flowers, is often said to come from its Native names, and in early summer the hills around it still bloom with lupine, balsamroot and bitterroot. The Madison Buffalo Jump, a limestone cliff west of town, was used for some two thousand years: hunters disguised in wolf and bison skins lured herds toward the edge and stampeded them over it, then butchered the carcasses in camps below.`,`The Bozeman Trail was a short road with a long, violent history. In 1866 the army built forts along it to protect travellers, and the Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho, under Red Cloud, laid siege to them; Captain William Fetterman and his eighty men were wiped out near Fort Phil Kearny that December. The 1868 Treaty of Fort Laramie closed the trail and gave the Lakota the Powder River country — one of the few wars the United States plainly lost to Native nations, at least for a time.`,`Nelson Story's cattle drive became Montana legend, partly because he ran it at night past the forts to avoid the army's order to halt; it is said to be one of the drives that inspired Larry McMurtry's Lonesome Dove. Fort Ellis's officers, meanwhile, joined the 1870 Washburn expedition into the Yellowstone country, whose accounts of geysers and canyons helped persuade Congress to create the national park in 1872. Lieutenant Gustavus Doane's official report was the first government description of the wonders.`,`Montana State's Museum of the Rockies made Bozeman famous in another field. Its curator of paleontology, Jack Horner, discovered nesting grounds of the duck-billed dinosaur Maiasaura in the late 1970s — proof that some dinosaurs cared for their young — and later advised the filmmakers of Jurassic Park. The museum's halls of Montana dinosaurs, including Tyrannosaurus rex fossils, are among the largest anywhere. Outside, the valley's hayfields keep giving way to subdivisions, and the town argues constantly about how to grow without losing the views that brought everyone here.`]},"logan-ut":{why:`Cache Valley is a closed basin between the Bear River Range and Wellsville Mountains, a textbook cold-air bowl. Winter high pressure lids fog, frost, and particulate matter for days or weeks; ridgelines above the inversion can be bright and milder. Lake Bonneville sediments on the floor hold farms and, in stagnation, emissions. Summer mixes; the same walls that trap January air then make a warm, irrigated, well-ventilated agricultural valley. The split personality is geometry, not a forecasting quirk.`,immersive:`A December afternoon in Logan can be a gray lid you could walk on, streetlights haloed, the temple hill barely a rumor. Drive the canyon toward Beaver Mountain and at a sharp altitude the car pops into blue, with the valley's fog now a lake below. July inverts the lesson: canal water glittering, dry heat, and evening drainage winds off the Wasatch that reset the night. That seasonal opposition is the enclosed basin. People who garden the Bonneville silt in summer are using the same bowl that fails its air-quality tests in January.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot, dry and green — afternoons near 32°C in July, the irrigated fields and orchards a shock of green against the brown foothills, and nights that cool to about 13°C as air drains out of Logan Canyon. Rain is scarce once June ends, with only an occasional thunderstorm, and the canyon's cold river and Bear Lake's turquoise water an hour over the mountains are where people go to cool off.`},contrasts:[{label:`Logan Canyon summit & Bear Lake (≈50 km NE, ≈500 m higher)`,note:`Up the canyon, winter inversion days are often sunny above the valley fog, snow is much deeper, and summer afternoons run about 5°C cooler.`},{label:`Salt Lake City (≈130 km S)`,note:`At a similar elevation in a bigger basin beside the Great Salt Lake, Salt Lake City's winters are slightly milder, but it fights its own winter inversions.`}],history:[`In the winter of 1824–25, so the story goes, a group of fur trappers camped in this valley argued about where the Bear River went after it left the mountains. A twenty-year-old trapper named Jim Bridger was sent to find out. He floated a bullboat down the river until it emptied into a vast body of water, tasted it, and found it salty. He came back convinced he had reached an arm of the Pacific Ocean; he had in fact become one of the first white men to see the Great Salt Lake. The trappers held summer rendezvous here in the 1820s, and buried their furs in pits to keep them safe — caches that gave the valley its name.`,`For the Northwestern Shoshone, the valley was a home of willows, rivers full of fish, and good grass for the horse herds; they wintered in sheltered spots along the Bear River. Mormon settlers led by Peter Maughan began farming the valley in 1856, and within a few years the herds and fences were crowding the Shoshone off their food. On a bitterly cold morning in January 1863, Colonel Patrick Connor's California Volunteers attacked a Shoshone winter village on the Bear River just north of the valley, killing some 250 to 400 men, women and children — among the deadliest massacres of Native Americans in the history of the West, and for a century one of the least remembered.`,`Logan was laid out in 1859 and grew into the valley's capital. Its Mormon settlers built canals, a tabernacle and, on a bench above town, a white temple that took seven years of community labour to finish and was dedicated in 1884. In 1888 the territorial legislature founded an agricultural college on the hill opposite; Utah State University has since grown into a research university with a space laboratory that builds instruments for satellites and an ice cream shop famous across the state. The valley's dairy farms made its cheese famous, and the closed mountain basin that shelters the farms has also earned Logan an unwanted record: on still winter weeks its air has measured among the dirtiest in America.`],deepTitle:`Caches and willows`,deep:[`The Bear River Massacre was ignored or celebrated as a battle for generations; Connor was promoted to brigadier general for it. Only in the late twentieth century, as historians and Shoshone descendants pressed for the truth, was it recognised as a massacre. The site, just across the Idaho line near Preston, is a national historic landmark, and the Northwestern Band of the Shoshone Nation has bought back land there to build an interpretive centre and restore the river bottoms where the village once stood.`,`Among the survivors was a leader named Sagwitch, wounded in the attack. He and many of his people later joined the Mormon church and founded a farming settlement at Washakie in northern Utah, where they worked their own land for decades. Their descendants form the core of today's Northwestern Band, which still keeps Sagwitch's story at the centre of its own history.`,`The Logan Temple was raised by volunteer workers from across Cache Valley, who quarried the stone from the canyon and cut timber in the mountains, and it was only the second temple the church completed in Utah. Utah State's Old Main, across the valley on its own hill, faces it, and the two buildings still define the skyline. The college's farm experiments helped create the valley's dairy and cheese industry, and Aggie Ice Cream, made from its creamery's milk since the 1920s, has been the town's favourite reward for generations.`,`Logan Canyon, which climbs from the edge of town toward Bear Lake, is a gallery of limestone cliffs and one of Utah's great drives. A side trail leads to the Jardine Juniper, a gnarled tree thought to be about 1,500 years old, and at the top the road drops to Bear Lake, whose milky turquoise water and raspberry shakes are a summer pilgrimage. In winter, when the inversion fills the valley with cold grey murk, drivers climbing the canyon pop out into sunshine above it — the best view in Utah of the trap they just escaped.`]}},n={"moab-ut":{why:`Spanish Valley is a canyon-floor oasis cut by the Colorado River through stacked Mesozoic sandstones. Afternoon sun heats Entrada and Navajo cliff faces; the rock then re-radiates, so the floor runs hotter than the plateau above. In winter those same walls, plus the river’s thermal mass, blunt Arctic air that scours the La Sals. Moisture is scarce because successive high plateaus wring Pacific storms before they arrive. The lived climate is therefore a rain-shadow desert with a riparian ribbon, milder January days than elevation alone would suggest, and summer heat that is a wall-amplified basin effect rather than a simple latitude story.`,immersive:`Red-rock walls close around the Colorado’s crossing at Moab, and the stone keeps working after sunset. Heat stored in the cliffs returns to the valley floor on summer nights, while winter mornings stay milder than the open Colorado Plateau a few hundred meters higher. Cottonwoods mark the river and Mill Creek; beyond them, slickrock and saltbush take over. Spring and autumn are the usable outdoor seasons, when canyon shade and dry air make long days on stone possible. Drought, flash floods in side washes, and the press of park traffic are the practical limits. The place is a desert oasis only as long as the river and the canyon geometry hold.`,feel:`Moab feels like a green ribbon stitched into a sea of red stone. The Colorado River and Mill Creek keep a strip of cottonwoods alive on the valley floor, while the sandstone walls soak up the sun all day and give it back all night — so summer evenings stay warm, winter mornings stay milder than the plateau above, and spring and autumn are about as close to perfect as desert weather gets.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold nights and bright, mild days — afternoons near 7°C and nights a few degrees below freezing, with a dusting of snow that often melts off the red rock by noon and leaves the La Sal Mountains gleaming white above the valley. The trails empty out, the light is low and golden, and an icy morning on the slickrock can give way to shirtsleeve sun.`,spring:`Moab's high season — afternoons climbing from about 17°C in March to near 28°C by late May, nights cool and clear, cottonwoods leafing out along the river and snow still on the La Sals. Wind is the catch: spring storms passing to the north can whip up red dust for a day at a time. Over Easter thousands of jeeps descend on the trails for the Jeep Safari.`,summer:`A furnace between cool canyon walls — afternoons near 38°C in July, nights that only fall to about 20°C as the rock radiates the day's heat, and almost no humidity. Hikers start at dawn and the river becomes the place to be. From late July, monsoon storms can build over the canyons, sending flash floods roaring down dry washes, sometimes from a storm the people in the canyon never see.`,autumn:`The second golden season and the wettest — afternoons near 30°C in September easing to about 14°C by November, nights cool and starry, and occasional storms that dump the year's most rain in a few downpours. Cottonwoods along the river and aspens on the La Sals turn gold together, and the red rock glows in the low light.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Arches and Canyonlands, slickrock mountain biking, rafting the Colorado and jeep trails that climb into sandstone fins, and night skies as dark as any in the lower forty-eight.`,residentFit:`It suits climbers, river guides, mountain bikers and desert lovers who can live with blistering summers, a tourist-season town that roars from March to October, and housing that seasonal workers struggle to afford.`,contrasts:[{label:`La Sal Mountains (≈25 km E, peaks ≈2,600 m higher)`,note:`The snowy range on Moab's skyline holds snowfields into June, and summer afternoons up on the loop road run about 15°C cooler than in town.`},{label:`Island in the Sky (≈30 km SW, ≈550 m higher)`,note:`Up on the mesa top in Canyonlands, summers run a few degrees cooler but windier, and winter nights fall colder than on the sheltered valley floor.`}],history:[`In the summer of 1952, a broke geologist named Charlie Steen was living with his wife and four sons in a tarpaper shack at Cisco, northeast of Moab, chasing uranium in a stretch of desert that the government's experts had written off. He had borrowed money from his mother to buy a worn-out drilling rig. In July his drill bit snapped at about 60 m, and when he pulled up the core he found it streaked with black rock he did not recognise. A gas station Geiger counter told him what it was: pitchblende, high-grade uranium ore. His Mi Vida mine made him a millionaire almost overnight. He built a hilltop mansion above town, threw parties to which everyone in Moab was invited, and set off the biggest uranium rush in American history.`,`People had lived in this valley for thousands of years before that. Ancestral Puebloan and Fremont farmers grew corn along the creeks and left granaries and rock art on the canyon walls; the Utes and Southern Paiutes followed, and the Old Spanish Trail crossed the Colorado River here on its way from Santa Fe to Los Angeles. In 1855 Mormon missionaries built a stone fort by the ford, but after Ute raids killed three of their men that September, they abandoned it. Farmers and ranchers returned for good in the late 1870s, and the town took its name from the biblical land of Moab, a kingdom at the edge of the promised land.`,`Uranium crowded the valley with prospectors, mills and motels in the 1950s, but the boom faded by the 1960s, and Moab found a new fortune in its scenery. Arches had become a national monument in 1929 and turned into a national park in 1971; Canyonlands was created in 1964. Hollywood had been coming since John Ford shot westerns here in 1949, and later the opening of Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade and the final plunge of Thelma and Louise were filmed nearby. In the 1980s mountain bikers discovered the Slickrock Trail, laid out in 1969 for motorcycles, and Moab became the most famous trail town in the world.`],deepTitle:`Uranium to arches`,deep:[`Steen's success turned the Colorado Plateau into a frenzy. The Atomic Energy Commission paid bonuses for new discoveries and bulldozed roads into the canyons to bring out the ore, and weekend prospectors with Geiger counters bought claims by the thousand. Steen built a uranium mill beside the river at Moab in 1956, served in the state senate, and later lost most of his fortune in bad investments. His mill, later run by the Atlas company, left a pile of some sixteen million tonnes of radioactive tailings on the riverbank, which the federal government has been hauling by train to a safe site north of town since 2009.`,`Edward Abbey worked two seasons as a ranger at Arches in the late 1950s, living in a trailer at the end of a dirt road. His book about those years, Desert Solitaire, published in 1968, attacked what he called industrial tourism and became a founding text of the environmental movement. Abbey's dirt road is now paved, and Arches receives so many visitors that it has used a timed-entry reservation system to manage the crowds.`,`Delicate Arch, now on Utah's licence plates, stands above the ranch that a Civil War veteran named John Wesley Wolfe homesteaded in 1898 with his son. Wolfe's log cabin, the petroglyphs of horsemen on a nearby rock panel and the trail to the arch make one of the classic walks of the Southwest. The arches themselves are made from Entrada Sandstone eroded along cracks into fins and windows; Wall Arch collapsed in 2008, and geologists say others will follow.`,`Moab's modern economy is built on jeeps, bikes, rafts and climbing, and the Easter Jeep Safari, held since 1967, draws thousands of vehicles to the backcountry trails. Growth has brought problems familiar to every popular western town — expensive housing, crowded trails, and a long debate about how much water the valley's aquifer can spare. Through it all, the cottonwoods along the river still mark the line between the oasis and the slickrock beyond.`]},"canaan-valley-wv":{why:`Canaan is a high Allegheny plateau basin, a canoe-shaped trough near 980 m ringed by Cabin, Canaan, and Brown mountains. Cold air drains off those ridges on clear nights and ponds on the peat-rich floor, where sphagnum and cranberry muck conduct heat poorly. The geometry is a frost hollow more typical of boreal country than of 39° north. Elevation already cuts summer warmth; the closed basin then adds inversions that can frost any month. Relict red spruce, balsam fir, and bog plants persist because this cold pool has outlasted the retreat of Pleistocene climate from the surrounding ridges.`,immersive:`Walk the valley boardwalks and the air feels a latitude farther north: red spruce, yellow birch, and cranberry in standing water, with fog hanging after a clear night. Ridges around the basin trap radiatively cooled air so that dawn on the peat flats can be sharp even in July. Davis, on the Blackwater rim, sits slightly above the deepest pool, yet the same Allegheny height keeps afternoons modest. Snow lingers on north-facing Cabin Mountain slopes into spring, which is why downhill skiing took root here. The tradeoff is a short, uncertain growing season and a wetland complex that floods, freezes, and stores water in ways a typical Appalachian cove does not.`,seasons:{summer:`Cool and green, with a northern edge — afternoons near 24°C, but nights that drop to about 12°C on the valley floor and occasionally flirt with frost even in July, when cold air drains off the ridges into the bogs. Thunderstorms roll through often, mist lifts off the wetlands at dawn, and wild blueberries ripen on the high ground by August.`},history:[`In October 1746 a survey party struggled across these mountains to mark the western boundary of Lord Fairfax's vast Virginia estate, and planted a stone at the source of the Potomac's North Branch. One surveyor, Thomas Lewis, kept a journal that is one of the best complaints ever written about the Alleghenies: endless rain, laurel thickets so dense the men had to crawl, horses tumbling down rocks, and the general impression of a country nobody in their right mind would try to settle. For a long time, nobody did. The high valley beside his route stayed a wilderness of red spruce, bogs and rhododendron for more than a century, and the settlers who eventually ventured in named it, perhaps hopefully, Canaan — the promised land.`,`The Shawnee and Seneca had hunted these ridges, but the cold, wet valley floor never held villages; the last elk in the eastern mountains survived here until the nineteenth century. Then came the railroad. In 1884 the West Virginia Central reached the Blackwater River, and the coal baron Henry Gassaway Davis — later, at eighty, the oldest major-party candidate for vice president in American history — laid out a town named after himself. Lumber companies cut the great spruce forests of the valley in a few decades, and fires that followed burned not only the slash but the thick organic soil beneath it, leaving bare rock and stumps where the forest had stood for millennia.`,`By the 1920s the timber was gone and the valley was a burnt-over pasture with a few farms and hunting camps. Its revival came slowly: a state park in 1963, a state-park ski resort in 1971 (private ski areas had run in the valley since 1951), and a long fight in the 1970s and 1980s over an electric utility's plan to flood the valley for a pumped-storage power project. The plan was defeated, and in 1994 Canaan Valley became the five-hundredth national wildlife refuge in the country, protecting the largest wetland in West Virginia — and the coldest, most northern-feeling valley in the eastern United States.`],deepTitle:`Spruce, fire, refuge`,deep:[`The Fairfax Stone, which Lewis's party set up in 1746, marked one of the strangest boundaries in colonial history. Thomas, Lord Fairfax, owned some 21,000 square kilometres of northern Virginia granted to his family by the king, and the stone fixed the western end of a line that defined his empire. A young surveyor named George Washington worked for Fairfax a few years later, and the stone's site is now a tiny state park a short drive north of Davis.`,`Henry Gassaway Davis built a fortune in coal, timber and railroads and served as a U.S. senator from West Virginia. His company towns of Davis and Thomas boomed with mills and miners, and his railroad carried the valley's spruce out by the trainload. In 1904, already eighty years old, he was the Democratic nominee for vice president alongside Alton Parker; they lost in a landslide to Theodore Roosevelt.`,`Logging on the scale practised here destroyed more than the trees. Without the dense canopy, the deep humus that had built up over centuries dried out and burned, sometimes smouldering underground for months. The Blackwater River, stained dark as tea by the tannins of hemlock and spruce, still pours over Blackwater Falls just outside Davis, and red spruce has slowly returned to parts of the valley, but ecologists consider the forests only partly recovered a century later.`,`The battle over the pumped-storage project ran for nearly two decades. Allegheny Power wanted to dam the Blackwater and turn much of the valley into a reservoir; conservationists argued that the wetlands were irreplaceable. Federal agencies eventually denied the key permit, and the land passed to the Fish and Wildlife Service. Today the refuge protects woodcock, beaver, and boreal plants such as cranberry, which persist this far south only because the valley's cold-air pool keeps nights so chilly — a refuge that warming summers may test.`]},"redfield-ny":{why:`Tug Hill is a cuesta of sedimentary rock rising east of Lake Ontario, a plateau that intercepts northwest flow after a long over-water fetch. Cold air crossing unfrozen Ontario picks up heat and moisture, then is forced upward over several hundred meters of uplift. That orographic kick turns lake-effect bands into some of the heaviest persistent snows east of the Rockies. Redfield sits on the snowy core rather than the milder lake plain. Poor soils and a short growing season follow from the same height and cloudiness that make winter the region’s defining season.`,immersive:`Tug Hill is the textbook lake-effect amplifier. Cold continental air sweeps southeast across 250 km of unfrozen Lake Ontario, picks up moisture and heat from the relatively warm water, then is forced to rise over the plateau's 500 m uplift. The result is among the heaviest snowfalls in North America outside of the Pacific mountain ranges. Redfield and Boylston routinely record 6 m of annual snowfall; single storms of 1.5 m are not unusual. Summers are mild and wet, with a short growing season on thin soils. Hamlets here budget for unplowed roads, snowmobile traffic, and isolation when a band stalls. The plateau is a cuesta first and a community second, and the weather still writes the budget.`,seasons:{summer:`Mild, green and quietly humid — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 14°C, frequent thunderstorms, and forests so lush it is hard to picture the snowbanks that buried the road signs a few months earlier. Black flies and mosquitoes own June; by July the streams run clear, the Salmon River draws anglers, and the hamlet's only traffic jam is a logging truck.`},history:[`On 11 and 12 January 1997, a lake-effect snow band parked over the village of Montague, in the heart of the Tug Hill Plateau a few kilometres from Redfield. A local weather observer measured the snowfall six times as it piled up and reported 196 cm of snow in twenty-four hours, which would have been a North American record. A national panel of experts examined the claim and threw it out, not because they doubted the snow had fallen but because the observer had measured it too often, so that the settling of the fresh snow did not count against him. The episode only confirmed what the people of Tug Hill already knew: nowhere east of the Rockies gets snow quite like this.`,`The plateau was hunting and fishing ground for the Oneida and Onondaga nations of the Haudenosaunee, who kept their villages on the lakes and rivers below. After the American Revolution the state sold their lands to speculators such as Alexander Macomb and William Constable, whose giant purchase of 1791 covered most of northern New York, and settlers began trickling up onto the hill. Redfield, named after one of its first settlers, was organised as a town in 1800. Farmers cleared the forest, cut timber, and raised dairy cattle and sheep, and the population of the surrounding region peaked around 1870. Some say the name Tug Hill comes from the long tug it took a team of horses to haul a wagon up the slope.`,`The farms never had much of a chance. Thin soils, short summers and the snow drove most families off the high plateau by 1930, and the state bought many of their abandoned fields to plant as state forests. In 1913 a dam on the Salmon River flooded some of Redfield's best farmland to create a reservoir. Today the hill is one of the largest unbroken forests in New York outside the Adirondacks, and Redfield's winter economy runs on the snow itself — snowmobile trails, hunting camps, and a stream of visitors coming to see what it looks like when a lake-effect band unloads. In February 2007 an unofficial 358 cm fell on Redfield in ten days.`],deepTitle:`The snow machine`,deep:[`Lake-effect snow happens when frigid air sweeps across Lake Ontario's unfrozen water, soaking up heat and moisture over some 250 km of open lake. When that air reaches the shore and is forced up the slope of Tug Hill, it cools and dumps its load in narrow bands that can drop several centimetres an hour while places a few kilometres away see sunshine. The snowiest parts of the plateau average somewhere around 5 to 6 m of snow a year, and in the great winter of 1976–77 the hamlet of Hooker measured nearly 12 m of snow.`,`Living with that much snow is a craft. Roofs are built steep and strong, plough crews work around the clock during storms, and residents keep their cars stocked with shovels and sand. Schools close rarely; roads close more often, especially Route 177 across the top of the plateau, when whiteouts make driving impossible. The snow's depth turns ordinary houses into snow caves and mailboxes into buried treasure.`,`The Salmon River below Redfield is famous among anglers for its runs of Chinook and coho salmon and steelhead, which come up from Lake Ontario every autumn. The salmon are not native: they were stocked by the state from the 1960s to replace the Atlantic salmon that had disappeared from the lake in the nineteenth century, and the river's hatchery at Altmar keeps the fishery going. On peak days the riverbanks near Pulaski are lined with anglers shoulder to shoulder.`,`The Tug Hill Commission, created by the state in 1972, helps the plateau's small towns plan together rather than see the hill carved into scattered lots or locked up as a park. Much of the land remains working forest, managed for timber and open to hunters and snowmobilers. The future hangs partly on the lake: as Lake Ontario freezes less often in a warming climate, the lake-effect machine may run even longer into the winter, bringing more snow to Tug Hill before it brings less.`]},"syracuse-ny":{why:`Syracuse sits on the Ontario plain about 50 km south of the lake, in the path of northwest flow that needs no mountain to make snow. Open water supplies heat and moisture; the city is simply downstream. Onondaga Lake is too small to drive the snow machine, yet it adds local humidity and, in the industrial era, a chemical history. Compared with Tug Hill, there is little orographic lift, so totals are large for a major city but smaller than the plateau. Summer humidity and lake-moderated cold are the other half of the same water-body geometry.`,immersive:`Lake-effect bands slide off Ontario and stall over Syracuse as if the city were a catcher’s mitt: wet snow on cinder streets, lake cloud on otherwise dry cold days, and a downtown that learned to move regardless. Onondaga Lake lies at the north edge, a sacred Haudenosaunee water that later became a chemical sump and is now a Superfund recovery. Summers are humid and milder than interior snowbelt towns farther from water. The Finger Lakes sit just south, a different lake story of deep troughs and vineyards. Living here means treating snow as a municipal utility and the lake as both origin story and cleanup site.`,feel:`Syracuse feels like a city that has made a civic religion out of snow. Lake Ontario sits upwind, and every cold northwest wind that crosses its open water arrives loaded — so winter here is not a storm now and then but a season of steady, soft snow that the plough fleets attack day and night. The payoff comes in summer and autumn: humid green summers, long golden Septembers, and some of the best fall colour in the East.`,seasons:{winter:`The snowiest big city in America — afternoons barely above freezing, nights near −8°C, and lake-effect bands off Ontario that can drop 30 cm in an afternoon on one side of town and nothing on the other. Grey skies are the norm and the sun a rare event. The city keeps moving anyway: sidewalks ploughed, schools rarely closed, and a long-running rivalry with Buffalo and Rochester over who got the most.`,spring:`Late, raw and grudging — afternoons climbing from about 5°C in March to near 20°C by late May, with April snowstorms still possible and plenty of chilly rain in between. Then everything turns green almost at once: magnolias bloom on the campus hill, the lake breeze softens, and by Memorial Day the city's cafés are out on the sidewalks.`,summer:`Warm, humid and green — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 16°C, and frequent thunderstorms rolling off the lake plain. Heat waves are short and the evenings are long. The State Fair fills the end of August, and the Finger Lakes, just to the southwest, offer wineries, waterfalls and cold, clear swimming.`,autumn:`The best season — September afternoons near 23°C, nights crisp, and a blaze of maple and oak colour across the hills by mid-October. The lake stays warm long after the land cools, so the first lake-effect showers arrive with the November winds, and by Thanksgiving the plough trucks are back on the roads.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Finger Lakes wineries and gorges to the south, the Erie Canal heritage, the New York State Fair in late summer and Syracuse University basketball under the roof of the JMA Wireless Dome.`,residentFit:`It suits families and university people who want an affordable, four-season city and genuinely enjoy snow, because Syracuse gets more of it than almost any other large city in the United States.`,contrasts:[{label:`Tug Hill Plateau (≈70 km N)`,note:`Where the Ontario snow bands slam into rising ground, the snowiest hamlets on the plateau can get roughly twice Syracuse's snow.`},{label:`Ithaca & the Finger Lakes (≈80 km SW)`,note:`Sheltered from the main lake-effect bands, Ithaca gets about half Syracuse's snow, with slightly warmer summers in the deep lake valleys.`}],history:[`In October 1869 two well diggers on a farm in Cardiff, a few kilometres south of Syracuse, struck something hard about a metre down. It was a man — a giant over 3 m tall, apparently turned to stone. Within days thousands of people were paying fifty cents a head to see the Cardiff Giant, and preachers proclaimed it proof that giants had walked the earth in biblical times. It was a hoax: a cigar maker named George Hull had it carved from a block of gypsum in Chicago and buried on his cousin's farm, apparently to mock a Methodist preacher who had insisted to him that the Bible's giants were literally true. When P.T. Barnum could not buy the giant, he made a copy and exhibited it as the real thing, and the two fakes ended up drawing crowds in the same city at the same time.`,`The land around Syracuse is the heart of the Haudenosaunee world. By Onondaga tradition, on the shore of Onondaga Lake the Peacemaker and Hiawatha united five warring nations into the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, and the Onondaga have kept the Confederacy's council fire ever since. They showed the French Jesuits the lake's salt springs in 1654, and those brine springs built the city. The state seized the springs after the Revolution, and by the mid-1800s Syracuse — incorporated in 1825 beside the new Erie Canal — was producing much of the nation's salt. It was also a stronghold of abolition. In October 1851 a crowd broke into the city police office and freed William Henry, known as Jerry, a man arrested as a fugitive slave, and smuggled him to Canada; the Jerry Rescue became one of the most famous acts of defiance against the Fugitive Slave Act.`,`Salt gave way to soda ash, typewriters, cars, air conditioners and electronics. The Solvay Process Company opened beside the lake in 1884, Carrier moved its air-conditioning business to the city in the 1930s, and Syracuse University, founded in 1870, grew into a major research school. In 1954 Danny Biasone, the owner of the Syracuse Nationals basketball team, invented the 24-second shot clock to keep teams from stalling, and arguably saved professional basketball. Heavy industry left the lake so poisoned with mercury and chemical waste that it was placed on the Superfund list in 1994. The cleanup has brought it slowly back to life.`],deepTitle:`Salt and snow`,deep:[`In 1656 French Jesuits and soldiers built a mission called Sainte-Marie among the Iroquois on the shore of Onondaga Lake. Two years later, hearing rumours of a plot against them, they decided to leave without being stopped. They secretly built boats, then threw a huge feast at which, by custom, guests were expected to eat everything served. When their Onondaga hosts fell asleep stuffed, the French slipped away down the river at night. The mission site on the lakeshore is now the Skä•noñh Great Law of Peace Center, which tells the story from the Onondaga side.`,`Salt boiling made the shore of Onondaga Lake an industrial landscape of kettles, vats and solar evaporation sheds. The state leased the springs to manufacturers and collected a tax on every bushel, and that revenue helped pay for the Erie Canal, which in turn carried Syracuse salt across the country to pack pork and preserve fish. Production peaked during the Civil War, when Union armies needed salt and Southern supplies were cut off, and the city still calls itself the Salt City.`,`The Cardiff Giant now lies in a pit at the Farmers' Museum in Cooperstown, while Barnum's copy has toured fairs and museums for more than a century. The hoax gave American English one of its most famous lines: the remark that there is a sucker born every minute, often credited to Barnum, was probably first said by David Hannum, one of the bankers who owned the original giant, about the crowds lining up to see Barnum's fake.`,`The Onondaga Nation remains a sovereign nation on its territory just south of the city, with its own government and the council fire of the Confederacy. In 2005 it filed a land rights action asking the courts to recognise that its lands had been illegally taken and to order the cleanup of Onondaga Lake; the courts dismissed it, but the nation continues to press its case, and it has become a strong voice in the effort to restore the lake it considers sacred.`]},"marquette-mi":{why:`Marquette occupies a granite peninsula on Lake Superior’s south shore, where a cold inland sea sets the seasonal clock. Summer highs stay modest because the lake is a heat sink; autumn warmth lingers, then winter lake-effect snow bands train in off open water. The harbor’s rocky point and the Huron Mountains inland create local wind and cloud differences, but the dominant mechanism is Superior’s thermal mass at 46.5° north. Delayed springs, heavy snow, and a short, brilliant summer are the peninsula bargain, not a generic Upper Midwest continental pattern.`,immersive:`Marquette sits on a granite peninsula on Lake Superior's south shore. The lake moderates summer — July highs around 23°C — and delivers heavy lake-effect snow, close to 4 m a year in the city and around 5 m on the higher ground inland, when cold air trains in off open water. Winter is deep and long; summer is brief, beautiful, and maritime-feeling despite being 1000 km from the ocean. Spring arrives late, with ice and fog still in the harbor when inland counties have already greened. The town's character is shaped by its identity as a Great Lakes port at a latitude where most of North America is already truly northern. A short drive inland, summers warm and the snow machine weakens. The peninsula is the whole bargain.`,feel:`Marquette feels like a seaport on an ocean of fresh water. Lake Superior is so big and so cold that it sets the town's clock: it keeps summer afternoons cool and breezy, holds autumn warm into October, and then turns into a snow machine once the arctic air arrives. The red sandstone downtown, the granite headlands and the old ore dock looming over the harbour all feel built to withstand a lake that can throw waves taller than a house.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, deep and white — afternoons below freezing, nights near −11°C, and lake-effect snow that keeps coming from November into April, piling banks higher than cars. Superior's open water steams in the arctic air, and the harbour's breakwater wears a coat of ice. Cross-country skiers glide through town, and the snow is part of the local pride.`,spring:`Slow and cold — Superior's ice-cold water holds the shore back, so afternoons climb only from about 3°C in March to near 17°C by late May, with fog rolling in off the lake and snow possible into May. Ice can linger in the harbour into April, and the leaves come out weeks after they do downstate.`,summer:`Cool, bright and lake-breezy — afternoons near 24°C, nights around 13°C, and a lake that stays too cold for most swimmers except on the hottest days. A shift in the wind from land to lake can drop the temperature ten degrees in an hour. Long evenings light up the sky past ten o'clock in late June, and the beaches, trails and Pictured Rocks fill with visitors.`,autumn:`Colourful and stormy — September afternoons near 20°C cooling to about 5°C by November, maples and birches turning red and gold, and the famous gales of November rolling across Superior with waves that crash over the breakwater. The first lake-effect snow usually arrives by mid-November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the sandstone cliffs of Pictured Rocks, the wild shore of Lake Superior, the ore dock and lighthouse, mountain biking in summer, and cross-country skiing and the UP 200 sled-dog race in winter.`,residentFit:`It suits outdoor people who treat a long, snowy winter as the main event, value a lively university town with real wilderness at the door, and do not mind being a long drive from a big city.`,contrasts:[{label:`Ishpeming & Negaunee (≈20 km W, ≈250 m higher)`,note:`Up on the iron range away from the lake's moderating hand, summers run warmer by day, winter nights colder, and the snow often deeper.`},{label:`Green Bay, WI (≈250 km S)`,note:`Out of the Superior snow belt, Green Bay gets well under half of Marquette's snow, with hotter, more humid summers.`}],history:[`On the night of 31 July 1952, an army lieutenant named Coleman Peterson walked into the Lumberjack Tavern in the village of Big Bay, north of Marquette, and shot the owner dead, claiming the man had raped his wife. Peterson's lawyer was a local attorney and former county prosecutor named John Voelker, who won an acquittal by persuading the jury that his client had been driven by an irresistible impulse. Voelker turned the trial into a novel, Anatomy of a Murder, published under the pen name Robert Traver; it became a bestseller, and in 1959 Otto Preminger filmed it in and around Marquette, with James Stewart as the lawyer and a score by Duke Ellington. Locals crowded around to watch, many appeared as extras, and the courthouse where the real trial took place stars in the movie.`,`The shore had been Anishinaabe country for centuries, the home of Ojibwe bands who fished Superior and travelled its rivers by canoe. In 1844 a surveyor named William Burt was running lines near Teal Lake when his compass needle began swinging wildly. He looked around and found the cause: outcrops of iron ore. An Ojibwe leader named Marji-Gesick guided prospectors to the deposits a year later. In 1849 the Marquette Iron Company founded a town on the harbour, naming it the following year after the French Jesuit explorer Jacques Marquette, and after the locks at Sault Ste. Marie opened in 1855, ore boats could carry the red rock from Marquette to the furnaces of Ohio and Pennsylvania.`,`Marquette grew into the capital of the Upper Peninsula's iron range, a town of sandstone banks and churches built with mining money, and its great ore docks loaded thousands of freighters. Peter White, who arrived as an eighteen-year-old in 1849, became the town's banker, booster and benefactor. The teachers' college founded in 1899 grew into Northern Michigan University, and when the mines declined, the university, the hospital and tourism took up the slack. The town's waterfront now mixes ore docks, bike trails and beaches on one of the most beautiful stretches of the Great Lakes.`],deepTitle:`Ore docks and ice`,deep:[`Burt's discovery happened because he was using an instrument he had invented himself: a solar compass that found true north by the sun, so he could keep surveying even when the magnetic needle went haywire. The iron range he found, together with the Mesabi in Minnesota, would feed American steelmaking for a century. The first ore was hauled to the harbour by mule and wagon, then by plank road and railway, and finally dropped into ships from towering wooden docks.`,`Marji-Gesick was promised a share in the Jackson Mining Company for showing the company where the iron lay. He never received it, and after his death his daughter Charlotte Kawbawgam spent years in the courts pursuing the claim; the Michigan Supreme Court ruled on the case in 1889, and her persistence is now a celebrated chapter in Ojibwe history. She and her husband, Charles Kawbawgam, lived on Presque Isle, where their grave still lies in the park.`,`Lake Superior's November storms have sunk hundreds of ships, and Marquette's harbour of refuge, behind its long breakwater, saved many more. The lake's coastal shipwreck lore inspired generations of sailors' songs and Gordon Lightfoot's ballad of the Edmund Fitzgerald, which sank in 1975 in the lake's eastern basin. Marquette's lighthouse, standing on its red rock point since the 1860s, is a reminder of how much weather the town has watched.`,`Northern Michigan University built the Superior Dome in 1991, one of the largest wooden domes in the world, and its Olympic training centre has trained athletes in winter sports for decades. Marquette also gained a reputation as one of the best small cities in America for outdoor life — mountain biking on trails once used by miners, kayaking in the harbour and cross-country skiing in deep lake-effect snow. The old Lower Harbor ore dock, no longer used, stands as a monument to the iron that built it all.`]},"houghton-mi":{why:`The Keweenaw is a peninsula thrust far into Lake Superior, so lake-effect snow can arrive from almost any wind that has crossed open water. Houghton sits on the Portage Lake ship canal that splits the peninsula, a dredged waterway that adds local moisture and fog but is secondary to the big lake. Orographic lift along the spine raises totals further inland toward the Copper Country’s snow belt. Multiple fetch directions on a small landmass are the mechanism; the result is among the heaviest seasonal snows east of the Rockies, with maritime-cool summers on the same exposure.`,immersive:`Portage Lake’s lift bridge and the stacked streets of Houghton-Hancock sit in a notch cut through the Keweenaw, but the weather still belongs to Superior. Snow can arrive on north, east, or west winds because water surrounds the peninsula on three sides; lake bands do not need a single favored direction. Winter is long, dark, and operational: plow budgets, Michigan Tech’s calendar, and a culture that treats meters of snow as ordinary. Summers turn mild and bright on the same water that buried the towns in January. Stamp-sand beaches and mine rock remain underfoot, a copper district that learned to live with a lake that both isolates and feeds it.`,seasons:{winter:`Snow, then more snow — afternoons below freezing, nights near −11°C, and lake-effect bands off Superior that can bury the peninsula from almost any wind direction. Houghton's streets climb steeply up from the water, so plough crews work around the clock and snowbanks tower over parked cars. Michigan Tech's Winter Carnival in February fills the campus with giant snow sculptures that students build through the night.`,summer:`Short, bright and lake-cooled — afternoons near 24°C, nights around 12°C, and long evenings when the light lingers past ten. The lake is warm enough for brave swimmers by August, the ferry runs to Isle Royale, and wildflowers and thimbleberries crowd the roadsides along the peninsula.`},contrasts:[{label:`Calumet & the Keweenaw spine (≈15 km N, ≈150 m higher)`,note:`Up on the ridge toward the tip of the peninsula, snowfall climbs higher still; a roadside gauge at Delaware marks the record winter of 1978–79 at about 10 m.`},{label:`Duluth, MN (≈250 km W)`,note:`At Superior's western end, out of the main lake-effect bands, Duluth gets less than half of the Keweenaw's snow, though its winters are colder.`}],history:[`On Christmas Eve 1913, the striking copper miners of the Keweenaw held a party for their children on the second floor of Italian Hall in Calumet, a few kilometres up the peninsula from Houghton. The miners had been on strike since July for an eight-hour day and better pay. In the middle of the party someone shouted fire. There was no fire, but the crowd rushed down the narrow stairway and piled up against the doors at the bottom; seventy-three people died, fifty-nine of them children. Who shouted, and why, has never been proven, but the strikers blamed the mining companies' allies. Woody Guthrie later wrote a song about it, and the tragedy broke the strike's spirit within months.`,`Copper had made the Keweenaw famous long before that. Native peoples had dug pure copper out of the rock here and on Isle Royale for thousands of years, hammering it into tools and ornaments that were traded across the continent; a huge copper boulder near the Ontonagon River was so revered that it was carted off to Washington in 1843 as a curiosity. Douglass Houghton, the state geologist, reported the copper riches in 1841; four years later he drowned when his boat capsized in a Lake Superior storm, and the town on the Portage waterway was named in his honour. The Cliff Mine began paying dividends in 1849 and set off America's first great mining boom.`,`For decades in the nineteenth century the Copper Country produced most of America's copper, its giant mines worked by Cornish, Irish, German, Italian, Finnish and Croatian immigrants. The Quincy Mine above Hancock, nicknamed Old Reliable, ran from 1848 to 1945, and the Michigan Mining School, opened in Houghton in 1885 to train mining engineers, grew into Michigan Technological University. After the mines closed, the university became the area's lifeline, and the Keweenaw National Historical Park, established in 1992, now preserves the mines and the company towns. Visitors come for the history, the waterfalls, the snow and the Cornish pasties that miners carried underground.`],deepTitle:`Copper Country`,deep:[`The Keweenaw's copper is unusual: much of it is native copper, almost pure metal formed when ancient lava flows were filled with copper-rich fluids more than a billion years ago. Some masses were so large that miners had to cut them apart with chisels for weeks. The Ontonagon Boulder, a single piece of copper weighing more than a tonne, now sits in the Smithsonian, and the tribes of the region have long asked for its return.`,`The Quincy Mine's No. 2 shaft eventually reached nearly 3 km down along the slope of the lode, and to haul men and rock up from such depths the company installed a steam hoist in 1918 that was the largest ever built. The hoist house still stands on the hill above the Portage, and visitors can ride a tram down to a lower level of the old mine and see where miners worked by candlelight.`,`The Keweenaw received the largest concentration of Finnish immigrants in the United States, and Finnish saunas, churches and cooperatives shaped the region's culture; Hancock's Finnish American Heritage Center keeps that history. Cornish miners brought the pasty, a meat-and-vegetable pie that could be kept warm underground. The two cultures mingled so thoroughly that local Finns made pasties their own.`,`Michigan Tech's Winter Carnival, held since 1922, is famous for its enormous snow statues, carved by student groups who work through the night in subzero weather to finish them before judging. The Portage Lake Lift Bridge, which opened in 1959 to link Houghton and Hancock, is one of the heaviest lift bridges in the world; its central span rises to let ships through and drops to a lower deck in winter so snowmobiles can cross.`]},"viroqua-wi":{why:`The Driftless Area escaped the Pleistocene ice sheets, so southwestern Wisconsin keeps a dissected maze of ridges and coulees instead of glacial till plains. Cold air drains into valley bottoms on still nights; south-facing slopes green up weeks before north faces. Spring creeks fed by limestone hold steady temperatures for trout. Viroqua sits in that relief, where a few hundred feet of local elevation and aspect create farm-scale climates unusual in the Midwest. Unglaciated topography, not a different air mass, is why orchards, organic vegetables, and dairy can occupy neighboring folds of the same county.`,immersive:`From a Vernon County ridge the land falls away into wooded coulees, not the table-flat corn country to the east. Morning fog sits in the Kickapoo folds while ridgetop barns are already in sun; frost collects in the bottoms and spares the benches. Spring creeks stay cold and clear enough for trout because they are groundwater, not snowmelt ditches. Amish buggies, organic vegetable trucks, and conventional dairy tanks share the same narrow roads. Flooding in the Kickapoo remains the honest seasonal risk. The Driftless does not offer a mild winter; it offers terrain that lets small farms pick their aspect.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and quiet, with the valleys colder still — afternoons below freezing, nights near −12°C, and on clear, calm mornings the coulee bottoms can run 5 to 10°C colder than the ridgetop town as frigid air drains downhill and pools in the hollows. Snow is moderate, the spring creeks steam in the cold, and Amish buggies leave narrow tracks along the ridge roads.`,summer:`Warm, humid and lush — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 15°C, and the wettest stretch of the year, with thunderstorms that can pour several centimetres into the steep coulees in an hour and send the Kickapoo over its banks. Morning fog fills the valleys while the ridges sit in sunshine, and farmers' markets overflow with organic vegetables.`},contrasts:[{label:`Kickapoo valley bottoms (≈15 km E, ≈100 m lower)`,note:`Down in the winding valleys, cold air pools on still nights, so frosts come weeks earlier in autumn and later in spring than on Viroqua's ridge.`},{label:`La Crosse (≈45 km NW, on the Mississippi)`,note:`Down on the broad river floodplain, summer afternoons run a little hotter and muggier, and river fog is a regular morning visitor.`}],history:[`On the morning of 2 August 1832, a band of exhausted Sauk and Meskwaki families reached the Mississippi at the mouth of the Bad Axe River, about 30 km west of today's Viroqua, and tried to cross to safety in Iowa. They were what remained of the followers of the war leader Black Hawk, who had been chased across Illinois and Wisconsin all summer. A steamboat, the Warrior, blocked the river and fired on them with cannon, and soldiers attacking from the bluffs shot those who tried to swim. Hundreds of people, many of them women and children, were killed. Black Hawk escaped, only to be handed over to the army by Ho-Chunk men weeks later. The killing at the Bad Axe ended the Black Hawk War, the last Native war fought in the old Northwest.`,`The ridges and coulees around Viroqua had long been Ho-Chunk country, and after the war the Ho-Chunk were pressured into ceding it and removed again and again, though many families kept slipping back. The first settlers, from Ohio and New York, arrived in 1846, and the county seat went through several names before settling on Viroqua in 1854 — most likely borrowed from the heroine of a sentimental novel published a few years earlier. Norwegian immigrants filled the surrounding valleys, growing tobacco and raising dairy cattle, and a community of free Black and mixed-race farmers settled the Cheyenne Valley nearby in the 1850s, one of the most remarkable rural communities in nineteenth-century Wisconsin.`,`Viroqua's land is unusual: the glaciers of the last ice ages flowed around this corner of the Midwest but never covered it, leaving a maze of ridges, deep coulees and cold spring creeks instead of flat plains. That terrain was hard to farm on a big scale, so small family farms survived here longer than elsewhere. In 1988 seven farmers in the Kickapoo Valley founded a cooperative to sell their organic produce; it grew into Organic Valley, one of the largest farmer-owned organic cooperatives in North America. Amish families moving in since the 1960s have added produce auctions and horse-drawn ploughs, and Viroqua has become one of the most surprising rural food towns in America.`],deepTitle:`Driftless coulees`,deep:[`The Driftless Area covers some 40,000 square kilometres of Wisconsin, Minnesota, Iowa and Illinois. Geologists still debate exactly why the ice sheets missed it — the deep valley of the Mississippi and the hard rocks of northern Wisconsin may have diverted the ice — but the result is a landscape that looks older and steeper than anything around it. Its limestone-fed spring creeks stay cold and clear all year, making it one of the best trout-fishing regions in the Midwest.`,`In the 1960s the Army Corps of Engineers began building a flood-control dam on the Kickapoo River at La Farge, buying up farms and moving families out of the valley. By 1975, with the dam half-built, rising costs and protests from environmentalists halted the project, leaving an unfinished concrete tower standing in the woods. In 1996 Congress handed the land to the state and the Ho-Chunk Nation, and today the Kickapoo Valley Reserve is managed jointly, a rare example of land returned to a tribe after being taken for a public project.`,`The Cheyenne Valley settlement, near Hillsboro, was founded by free Black families from the East who bought land and farmed alongside European immigrants and Native neighbours; marriages across those lines were common. The community built churches and schools, and its farmers became respected members of the county — a story of rural integration that was largely forgotten until historians revived it.`,`In August 2018 storms dropped record rain on the Kickapoo watershed, and several small flood-control dams built in the coulees decades earlier failed, sending torrents through farms and villages. The floods were a reminder that the steep, beautiful landscape that protects small farms can also turn violent in a warming climate. Many farmers responded by planting more grass and trees on slopes to hold the soil, a practice the area's conservationists had been urging for nearly a century.`]},"asheville-nc":{why:`Asheville occupies a basin at 650 m on the French Broad River between the Black Mountains and the Pisgahs. Elevation knocks several degrees off Piedmont heat; surrounding ridges blunt the worst winter intrusions without turning the city into a snowbelt. Valley fog and cold-air drainage still collect on the river flats, while thermal belts on the slopes stay milder at night. Cove forests and orchard benches follow those aspects. The mechanism is basin elevation plus Blue Ridge shelter, not a coastal or Piedmont climate relocated to the mountains.`,immersive:`The French Broad slides through a mountain basin where summer afternoons feel closer to a highland than to Charlotte’s Piedmont glare. Ridges take the snow that downtown only samples; river neighborhoods collect fog and, in extreme storms, floodwater. Cove hardwoods, public porches, and a dense craft economy grew on that milder four-season pitch. The Blue Ridge Parkway and Biltmore’s forested estate make the same topography legible as scenery. After Hurricane Helene in 2024, the basin’s other face—steep tributaries, saturated soils, a river with too little room—became equally legible. Mildness here was never the same as safety on the water.`,feel:`Asheville feels like a mountain city that found the gentlest corner of the Appalachians. At 650 m in the French Broad basin, the heat that flattens the Piedmont in summer softens into warm afternoons and cool, misty mornings; winter mostly spares the city while the surrounding peaks take the snow. Ridges of hardwood forest rise on every side, and the result is a long, easy four-season climate with spring and autumn as the stars — though the same steep mountains can channel catastrophic floods into the valleys when a tropical storm stalls overhead.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild by mountain standards — afternoons near 10°C, nights just below freezing, and only light, occasional snow in town while the peaks of the Blue Ridge turn white and the Parkway closes at high elevations. Cold snaps come and go quickly. Clear days give long views through the bare trees, and the city's breweries and music halls take up the slack.`,spring:`A slow green explosion that climbs the mountains — afternoons warming from about 15°C in March to 24°C by late May, dogwoods and redbuds blooming in April, and the new leaves creeping up the slopes week by week. Rain showers come often, and the occasional late frost can still nip the gardens in the valleys.`,summer:`Warm but rarely sweltering — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 17°C, and afternoon thunderstorms building over the ridges several days a week. Humidity is high but the elevation keeps the worst of the Southern heat away. Mornings often start in fog along the river, and people escape to waterfalls and the cool tunnel of the Parkway.`,autumn:`Asheville's showpiece — dry, sunny days near 24°C in September easing to about 15°C by November, with a wave of red, orange and gold leaf colour that starts on the high peaks in early October and rolls down into the valleys by month's end. Leaf-peepers fill the Parkway, and the cool, clear nights make the colour sharper.`},residentFit:`It suits creative remote workers, craft brewers, artists and retirees who want mountain air and a lively food and music scene, and who can manage steep housing costs and the lingering scars of Hurricane Helene.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mount Mitchell (≈30 km NE, ≈1,400 m higher)`,note:`The highest peak east of the Mississippi runs about 10°C cooler, wears rime ice and snow through winter, and is cloaked in spruce-fir forest like Canada's.`},{label:`Charlotte (≈160 km E, ≈450 m lower)`,note:`Down in the Piedmont, summer afternoons run several degrees hotter and muggier, and nights stay much warmer.`}],history:[`In 1929 a young writer from Asheville named Thomas Wolfe published his first novel, Look Homeward, Angel, about a boy growing up in a mountain town called Altamont. Everyone in Asheville recognised it as their town — and recognised themselves, from Wolfe's own family, who ran a boarding house, to the town's lawyers, doctors and drunks, often under barely disguised names. The city was furious. The public library refused to stock the book, and Wolfe received threats and did not come home for eight years. When he finally did, in 1937, he was greeted as a hero. He died the next year at thirty-seven and is buried in Asheville, and his mother's boarding house is now a state historic site.`,`The French Broad valley was Cherokee hunting ground, and Spanish explorers under Hernando de Soto passed through the region in 1540. After the Revolution, settlers pushed in on land taken from the Cherokee, and in 1797 the village was named for the governor, Samuel Ashe. The Buncombe Turnpike, opened in 1827, made it a stop for drovers herding hogs, cattle and turkeys by the hundreds of thousands to markets in South Carolina. The railroad arrived in 1880, and Asheville became a health resort. Doctors sent tuberculosis patients to breathe its mountain air, and grand hotels filled with the wealthy.`,`In 1888 George Vanderbilt, grandson of the railroad magnate, came to Asheville with his mother and fell in love with the mountains. He bought some 500 square kilometres of land and built Biltmore House, the largest private house in America, which opened on Christmas Eve 1895. The patent-medicine millionaire Edwin Wiley Grove built the Grove Park Inn of giant granite boulders in 1913, and the city boomed until the stock market crash. When its banks failed in 1930, Asheville was left with the highest per-capita debt of any city in the country. It refused to default and spent until 1976 paying it off — which left no money to tear down the old buildings, so the Art Deco downtown survived intact. In September 2024 Hurricane Helene's floods devastated the city's river districts, and Asheville began rebuilding once again.`],deepTitle:`Biltmore, Altamont`,deep:[`Biltmore was a project on a scale America had never seen. Vanderbilt hired the architect Richard Morris Hunt to design a French Renaissance château of 250 rooms and the landscape architect Frederick Law Olmsted to plan the gardens and forest. Olmsted persuaded Vanderbilt to restore the worn-out farmland as forest, and a young Gifford Pinchot, later the first chief of the U.S. Forest Service, was hired to manage it; his successor, Carl Schenck, founded the first school of forestry in America on the estate in 1898. After Vanderbilt's death, his widow sold much of the land to the government, and it became the heart of Pisgah National Forest.`,`The Grove Park Inn attracted presidents, inventors and writers. F. Scott Fitzgerald stayed there in 1935 and 1936 while his wife, Zelda, was being treated at Highland Hospital, a psychiatric hospital in Asheville. Zelda died in a fire at the hospital in 1948. The hotel's giant fireplaces, big enough for a person to walk into, are still a sight on a cold night.`,`Black Mountain College, founded in 1933 in the hills east of the city, was one of the most influential experiments in American education, drawing artists and thinkers such as Josef and Anni Albers, John Cage, Merce Cunningham, Robert Rauschenberg and Buckminster Fuller, who experimented with his first geodesic domes there. It closed in 1957, but its spirit helped shape Asheville's arts scene, which revived in the 1980s and 1990s as artists and musicians moved into the empty downtown.`,`Floods are part of Asheville's history. In July 1916 two tropical storms in a row sent the French Broad surging to record heights, destroying bridges, factories and railroads. Hurricane Helene in 2024 broke that record, destroying the River Arts District, cutting off the city's water supply for weeks and killing dozens of people in the region. The mountains that give Asheville its mild climate also funnel heavy rain into narrow valleys, and the city's rebuilding has forced hard questions about where to build.`]},"boone-nc":{why:`Boone sits at 995 m on the Blue Ridge Escarpment, high enough that lapse rate alone produces summers more like New England than like the Carolina Piedmont below. Orographic lift on the escarpment wrings extra rain and snow from passing systems; cloudy, windy days are common. Northern hardwoods and a real winter follow the height. The town is not in a deep frost hollow, but ridgelines and coves still differ by aspect. Extreme elevation for 36° north is the whole mechanism: a climate transect you can drive in an hour from foothill heat to cabbage-and-apple country.`,immersive:`Appalachian State’s brick and the New River’s headwater folds sit just under a thousand meters, where summer afternoons stay bearable and winter actually ices the streets. Cloud rakes the escarpment; spruce-fir hangs on higher Balds while the campus lawns grow northern hardwoods. Ski slopes at Beech and Sugar depend on that height and on a latitude that still surprises visitors from Charlotte. Helene’s 2024 floods showed how quickly steep Watauga catchments can fail. The High Country feels northern because it is high, wet, and exposed, not because the South ended at a cultural border.`,feel:`Boone feels like New England dropped onto a Southern mountaintop. At nearly 1,000 m on the crest of the Blue Ridge, summer afternoons stay mild while the Piedmont below swelters, winters bring real snow and ice, and clouds often pour over the escarpment and wrap the town in fog. It is a college town where students ski in the morning and fiddlers play on the sidewalks at night, set among rolling Christmas-tree farms and ancient, rounded peaks.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and often icy — afternoons near 7°C, nights below freezing, and about a metre of snow over the season, often in quick storms followed by thaws. Wind rips across the ridges, and freezing fog coats the trees on the high peaks with rime. The ski slopes at Beech and Sugar make their own snow when nature fails to.`,spring:`Late, cool and changeable — afternoons warming from about 11°C in March to 21°C by late May, with snow possible into April and plenty of rain. Wildflowers carpet the coves in April and May, trout streams run high, and the leaves don't fully arrive on the higher ridges until well into May.`,summer:`The great escape from the Carolina heat — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 15°C, and frequent afternoon thunderstorms that boil up over the ridges. Fog often fills the valleys in the morning. The Blue Ridge Parkway, the outdoor drama Horn in the West and summer music festivals fill the High Country with visitors.`,autumn:`Early and spectacular — leaf colour peaks around mid-October, often a week or two ahead of the lowlands, and afternoons ease from about 22°C in September to 12°C by November. The first frosts arrive early in the month, the Parkway fills with leaf-lookers, and Christmas-tree farms on the hillsides start their harvest season.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Blue Ridge Parkway and Grandfather Mountain, the Appalachian State college-town energy, bluegrass and old-time music, fall colour, and the ski slopes at Sugar and Beech mountains in winter.`,residentFit:`It suits families, students and summer-heat refugees from the Piedmont who want cool mountain summers and real winters, and who do not mind fog, ice storms and winding mountain roads.`,contrasts:[{label:`Grandfather Mountain (≈20 km S, ≈800 m higher)`,note:`The jagged summit just south of town is among the windiest places in the East, wrapped in cloud much of the year and far colder than Boone.`},{label:`Wilkesboro (≈50 km E, ≈700 m lower)`,note:`Below the Blue Ridge escarpment, summer afternoons run about 6°C hotter, and snow is a rare event rather than a season.`}],history:[`In the first decades of the twentieth century, Boone and the counties around it were known as the Lost Provinces — so cut off from the rest of North Carolina by the escarpment that it was easier to reach Tennessee than Raleigh. In 1919 a narrow-gauge railway, the East Tennessee and Western North Carolina, finally climbed into town. Its little steam locomotives whistled so shrilly through the hollows that locals called it Tweetsie. A flood in 1940 washed the line out for good. One of its engines was later bought by the singing cowboy Gene Autry, then returned, and since 1957 it has been running in circles at a Wild West theme park called Tweetsie Railroad, near Blowing Rock.`,`Long before that, the Blue Ridge was Cherokee hunting ground. Daniel Boone, the frontiersman, hunted in these mountains in the 1760s, and the town that took his name was incorporated in 1872 — though it remained a tiny, isolated county seat for decades. Scottish, Irish and German settlers farmed the steep land, raised cattle and grew cabbage and potatoes; they preserved the ballads and fiddle tunes their ancestors had brought from the British Isles, a musical heritage that would make the region famous. In 1899 two brothers, B.B. and D.D. Dougherty, opened a small academy to train teachers for mountain schools, and it grew into Appalachian State University.`,`Arthel Doc Watson, born in 1923 in nearby Deep Gap and blind from infancy, learned guitar on a farm and became one of the greatest flatpicking guitarists in American music, a hero of the folk revival of the 1960s. His statue sits on a bench on King Street in Boone. Ski resorts opened in the 1960s, and Christmas trees became a major crop, with the High Country growing millions of Fraser firs for the national market. In September 2024 Hurricane Helene tore through the mountains, washing out roads and cutting Boone off for days — a reminder that the steep land that gives the High Country its cool, green charm also funnels water with terrible force.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The name Watauga, the county and the river, comes from the Cherokee. In the 1770s the Watauga settlements, just over the line in what is now Tennessee, set up one of the first independent governments in America, and in 1780 the frontier militia known as the Overmountain Men gathered there and marched across these mountains to defeat a Loyalist army at the Battle of Kings Mountain. Their trail is now a national historic trail.`,`Appalachian State's early years were frugal. The school ran its own farm and built a small wooden dam on the New River in 1915 to generate electricity, and students worked to pay their tuition. It became part of the University of North Carolina system in 1971. In 2007 its football team shocked the sports world by beating the University of Michigan, then ranked fifth in the country, in one of the biggest upsets in college football history.`,`Grandfather Mountain, south of Boone, is one of the oldest and most biologically diverse mountains in the Appalachians. In 1952 its owner, Hugh Morton, built the Mile High Swinging Bridge across a chasm near the summit, and it became a classic tourist attraction. Morton was also a passionate conservationist, and the mountain is now protected as a state park and a United Nations biosphere reserve, home to rare plants and animals found nowhere else.`,`Doc Watson founded MerleFest in 1988 in memory of his son and musical partner, Merle, who was killed in a tractor accident; the festival, held each spring in nearby Wilkesboro, has become one of the largest roots-music gatherings in America. In Boone, the music tradition goes on in jam sessions at shops and cafés, and the outdoor drama Horn in the West has retold the story of the frontier every summer since 1952.`]},"key-west-fl":{why:`Key West is a low coral island at about 24.5° north, fully wrapped by Florida Strait and Gulf water that holds heat through winter. Trade winds and sea breeze suppress the daily extremes that interiors at this latitude would see. Frost is outside the historical record because there is no radiational cold source and no elevation. Seasonality is rainfall and hurricane risk: a wet, storm-prone summer and autumn, a drier winter. The isothermal, tropical feel is ocean surround plus latitude, not a magic of the town’s architecture.`,immersive:`Trade wind and harbor light are the first facts: a flat limestone island, streets of gumbo-limbo and royal poinciana, water on every horizon. Winter days stay swim-warm because the surrounding sea never lets the air forget it is tropical. Summer’s work is humidity, afternoon storms, and watching the Atlantic basin. The reef that made wreckers rich is still offshore, now a tourist and research commons under hurricane rearrangement. Drinking water arrives by aqueduct from the mainland; elevation is a couple of meters. The climate is genuine tropics with a U.S. municipal government, which means insurance, Navy leases, and sea-level planning sit on the same porch as the dry-season glare.`,contrasts:[{label:`Miami (≈210 km NE)`,note:`On the mainland, winter cold fronts can push nights near 10°C a few times a year; Key West, surrounded by warm water, has never recorded frost.`},{label:`Dry Tortugas (≈110 km W)`,note:`Out on the open sea at Fort Jefferson, the climate is even more purely oceanic, with steadier trade winds and slightly less rain.`}],history:[`In April 1982, the US Border Patrol set up a roadblock on the Overseas Highway, the only road into the Florida Keys, stopping every car to search for drugs and undocumented migrants. Traffic backed up for kilometres and tourists stayed away. Key West's mayor, Dennis Wardlow, went to federal court to have the roadblock removed, and when that failed he announced that the Keys would secede from the United States. On 23 April, at a ceremony in Mallory Square he proclaimed the Conch Republic, broke a loaf of stale Cuban bread over the head of a man dressed in a naval uniform, and surrendered to the United States about a minute later — after which he requested a billion dollars in foreign aid. The roadblock was soon lifted. The Conch Republic still celebrates its independence every April.`,`The Calusa and other South Florida peoples fished the Keys for centuries, and the Spanish called the island Cayo Hueso, Bone Key, perhaps for the bones they found scattered there; English-speaking sailors turned that into Key West. In 1821 an Alabama businessman bought the island for two thousand dollars, and the next year the US Navy raised the flag. Key West's first fortune came from shipwrecks. Ships passing through the Florida Straits ran aground on the reef with grim regularity, and licensed wreckers raced out to salvage their cargoes, which by law had to be auctioned in Key West. For a time the island was by some reckonings one of the richest towns per person in America.`,`Cuban cigar makers arrived after 1868, fleeing war on their island, and by the 1880s Key West was rolling millions of cigars a year; José Martí raised money among its cigar workers for Cuban independence. Henry Flagler's Overseas Railroad reached the island in 1912, hopping from key to key on bridges across the open sea, but the Labor Day Hurricane of 1935 wrecked it, and the highway that replaced it opened in 1938. Ernest Hemingway lived here in the 1930s, writing in a studio behind his house on Whitehead Street, and President Truman made the naval station's commandant's quarters his Little White House. Today the island lives on tourism, a free-spirited reputation, and an increasingly uneasy relationship with the rising sea.`],deepTitle:`Wreckers and conchs`,deep:[`The wrecking business was regulated by a federal court in Key West from 1828, which decided how much of each cargo the salvagers could keep. Wreckers waited with their schooners at the ready and raced each other to the reef when a ship went aground. Rumours that they lured ships onto the reef with false lights were mostly legend, but they did make fortunes; the lighthouses built along the reef in the nineteenth century slowly put them out of business.`,`Hemingway's years in Key West were among his most productive. His wife Pauline's uncle bought them the Spanish colonial house on Whitehead Street, and there he worked on Death in the Afternoon, Green Hills of Africa, The Snows of Kilimanjaro and To Have and Have Not, his novel of Key West rum-runners in the Depression. He fished the Gulf Stream, drank at Sloppy Joe's bar and boxed in the back yard. The house is now a museum, famous for its population of six-toed cats, said to descend from one given to him by a sea captain.`,`Harry Truman first came to Key West in 1946 on doctor's orders to rest, and made eleven visits in all, spending a total of 175 days of his presidency there. He played poker, swam, walked in loud Hawaiian shirts and held cabinet meetings in the white house on the naval base. In 1985 a treasure hunter named Mel Fisher, after sixteen years of searching, found the mother lode of the Spanish galleon Nuestra Señora de Atocha, lost in a hurricane off the Keys in 1622, with tonnes of silver and gold. Much of it is on display in his museum in Key West.`,`Key West is the southernmost city in the continental United States, closer to Havana than to Miami, and its climate is truly tropical — it has never recorded a frost. That same position puts it squarely in the path of hurricanes. Hurricane Irma in 2017 struck the Lower Keys with devastating force, and rising seas now flood the island's lowest streets on the highest tides of the year. All of its fresh water arrives through a pipeline from the mainland.`]},"charleston-sc":{why:`Charleston occupies the peninsula where the Ashley and Cooper rivers meet a tidal estuary, a few meters above the Atlantic. Afternoon sea breezes slide up that harbor and knock the edge off Lowcountry heat that inland South Carolina still feels. Tidal exchange keeps winter nights milder than the interior. The same geometry exposes the city to storm surge and to humidity that the breeze cannot erase. Estuarine fetch plus a reliable coastal wind is the mechanism; hurricane track is the risk that organizes architecture, insurance, and memory.`,immersive:`Spartina, palmetto, and a harbor breeze arrive together on the Charleston peninsula: salt in the air by mid-morning, thunderheads over the Atlantic by afternoon, live oaks holding the shade. The Ashley and Cooper are tidal rivers, so the city feels the ocean twice a day even when the wind is light. Summer relief is that sea breeze, not dry air. Winters are gentle enough for camellias; the serious season is hurricane autumn, when the same estuary that moderates heat becomes a surge funnel. Gullah Geechee language and food are as native to this water as the dolphin at the harbor mouth. Elevation is almost none; culture is everything built on top of that fact.`,feel:`Charleston feels like a city steeped in heat, history and salt marsh. Its pastel row houses, church steeples and wrought-iron gates crowd a low peninsula between two rivers, shaded by live oaks draped in Spanish moss, and the sulphurous smell of pluff mud drifts in with every tide. The air is soft and humid for much of the year: summers are long, hot and steamy, broken by thunderstorms, winters are short and mild, and spring and autumn are glorious, with gardens in bloom and long evenings on the piazzas.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and often damp — afternoons near 17°C, nights near 6°C, and gentle rain more often than cold, though a few frosty nights each winter can still cover the marshes in white. Camellias bloom in the gardens, the oyster roasts begin, and the low winter light turns the pastel houses of Rainbow Row golden. Snow comes perhaps once a decade, and the city stops when it does.`,spring:`The city's showpiece season — afternoons rising from about 21°C in March to 28°C by late May, azaleas, wisteria and jasmine blooming in the walled gardens, and the Spoleto Festival filling the theatres and churches in late spring. The humidity creeps back by May, along with the first afternoon thunderstorms.`,summer:`Hot, heavy and stormy — afternoons near 32°C, nights that barely fall below 24°C, and humidity that makes the air feel like warm soup. Afternoon thunderstorms roll in off the swamps almost daily, briefly flooding the low streets at high tide. The sea breeze is the saving grace near the harbour and on the islands, and people head for the beaches or the shade of live oaks draped in Spanish moss.`,autumn:`Two seasons in one — August and September are the heart of hurricane season, hot and watchful, with storms sometimes spinning up the coast; then October brings Charleston's finest weather, with afternoons near 25°C, lower humidity, clear blue skies and cool nights. By November the marsh grass turns gold and the shrimp boats are bringing in the last of the season's catch.`},residentFit:`It suits people who love history, food and the Lowcountry water — boaters, chefs, preservationists and retirees — and who accept hot, humid summers, tidal flooding on downtown streets and a real hurricane plan.`,contrasts:[{label:`Folly Beach & the sea islands (≈15 km S)`,note:`On the barrier islands the ocean breeze trims summer afternoons by a few degrees and keeps winter nights milder than on the inland marshes.`},{label:`Columbia (≈170 km NW)`,note:`Inland in the Midlands, summer afternoons run about 3°C hotter and winter nights are noticeably colder, without the sea breeze to temper either.`}],history:[`On the night of 17 February 1864, eight Confederate sailors squeezed into an iron tube barely more than a metre high, cranked its propeller by hand, and crept out of Charleston harbour toward the Union warship Housatonic, part of the fleet blockading the city. The H.L. Hunley rammed a torpedo into the ship's side and sank it, becoming the first submarine in history to sink an enemy warship. Then it vanished. Its fate was a mystery for 131 years until divers found it in 1995 buried in sand off Sullivan's Island; it was raised in 2000, and the remains of its crew were buried in Charleston with full honours. Why the Hunley sank, no one knows for sure.`,`The Kiawah and other Cusabo peoples welcomed English settlers to the Ashley River in 1670, and by 1680 Charles Town had moved to the peninsula between the Ashley and Cooper rivers. It grew rich on rice, grown in tidal fields by enslaved Africans whose knowledge of rice farming made the Lowcountry one of the wealthiest places in the colonies. As many as two in five of the enslaved Africans brought into British North America may have arrived through its harbour. The Gullah Geechee culture, with its own language and traditions, grew on the sea islands, and resistance never stopped: in 1739 the Stono Rebellion broke out south of the city, and in 1822 a free Black carpenter named Denmark Vesey was hanged for planning an uprising.`,`In April 1861 Confederate guns in Charleston fired on Fort Sumter and started the Civil War. A year later an enslaved pilot named Robert Smalls took over the Confederate steamer Planter while its white officers slept ashore, picked up his family and sailed past the forts to the Union fleet; he later served five terms in Congress. After the war the city was poor, and on 31 August 1886 an earthquake wrecked much of it; the iron bolts used to brace damaged buildings are still visible on many houses. Charleston gave its name to the dance craze of the 1920s, and Porgy and Bess grew from a novel set in its alleys. Hurricane Hugo in 1989 and rising seas have tested it since, and in 2015 a gunman killed nine worshippers at Emanuel AME Church, the congregation Vesey had helped to build.`],deepTitle:`Rice and ruin`,deep:[`Rice cultivation in the Lowcountry depended on a sophisticated system of dikes, canals and floodgates that let planters flood and drain their fields with the tide. Many historians believe the knowledge came from the enslaved people themselves, many of whom were taken from the rice-growing regions of West Africa. The work was brutal, the mortality high, and the wealth it produced built the mansions of the planter elite that line Charleston's streets today.`,`Robert Smalls's escape on the Planter in May 1862 was one of the most daring acts of the war. Dressed in the captain's straw hat and imitating his stance, Smalls gave the correct signals as he passed Fort Sumter, then raised a white sheet as he approached the Union ships. He went on to pilot the Planter for the Union navy, bought his former owner's house in Beaufort after the war, and fought for the rights of Black Carolinians in Congress.`,`The earthquake of 1886, estimated at around magnitude 7, was the most destructive ever recorded in the southeastern United States. It killed about sixty people and damaged nearly every building in the city. Scientists still study the source fault, which is buried deep beneath the coastal plain, and the city's buildings now follow stricter rules to withstand the next one.`,`In 1925 the Charleston writer DuBose Heyward published Porgy, a novel about Black life on a courtyard he called Catfish Row, based on a real alley called Cabbage Row. George Gershwin spent the summer of 1934 on Folly Beach, listening to Gullah music in the churches of nearby James Island, and the opera Porgy and Bess opened in New York the next year. Today Charleston is one of America's most visited cities, and the Gullah Geechee Cultural Heritage Corridor works to protect the culture and land of the descendants of the people who built it.`]},"joseph-or":{why:`Wallowa Valley lies at about 1,290 m in Oregon’s remote northeast, a glacial trough at the foot of the Wallowa Mountains. Those peaks wring Pacific moisture, so the valley floor is sunnier and drier than the state’s west-side forests. Continentality is real—warm summers, cold winters—but the basin is less severe than the Snake River Plain or the open Rockies because ridges block the worst wind and trap a bit of heat. Ranch irrigation from the Wallowa River and lake, not rainfall, greens the hay fields. Rain-shadow sanctuary with alpine relief is the climate, not a Swiss import.`,immersive:`Wallowa Lake’s moraine dams a cold blue trough, and the peaks behind Joseph rise with a sharpness that explains every Alps comparison without needing one. The valley floor is ranch grass and irrigation ditches, dry enough that summer smells of sage and hay rather than moss. Winter snow is reliable on the high lakes and passes, yet the town itself is a sunny basin, not a cloud forest. Bronze sculptures on Main Street catch that light. Services are thin; Enterprise and a long drive to La Grande are the practical geography. The Wallowas make their own weather in the cirques, while the valley lives in the rain shadow those cirques create.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and still — afternoons around 3°C, nights near −6°C, and enough snow to bury the valley in white from December to March, with much deeper drifts in the Wallowas above. Cold air settles on the valley floor on calm nights and fog can linger for days. The lake freezes at the edges, cross-country skiers take to the meadows, and the peaks glow pink at sunset.`,summer:`Dry, sunny and spacious — afternoons near 28°C in July and August, nights cooling to about 10°C, and almost no rain once the June thunderstorms have passed. Wildflowers fill the high basins of the Eagle Cap, Wallowa Lake fills with kayaks, and the rodeo comes to town in late July. Smoke from wildfires is the season's wild card.`},contrasts:[{label:`Eagle Cap high country (≈20 km S, peaks ≈1,700 m higher)`,note:`The granite peaks above Wallowa Lake hold snow well into summer, and their high basins see frost even in July.`},{label:`Hells Canyon floor (≈50 km E, ≈800 m lower)`,note:`Down in the deepest river gorge in North America, summer afternoons can top 38°C while Joseph enjoys mountain air.`}],history:[`On 5 October 1877, after a fighting retreat of more than 1,800 km across Idaho and Montana, the Nez Perce leader Heinmot Tooyalakekt — known to Americans as Chief Joseph — surrendered to the US Army in the Bears Paw Mountains, just short of the Canadian border. The words attributed to him there are among the most famous ever spoken by a Native American: From where the sun now stands, I will fight no more forever. He had been fighting for this valley. His father, Old Joseph, had refused to sign away the Wallowa country and told his son never to sell the land where his parents were buried. The son never saw it as his home again.`,`The Wallowa Valley was the summer and autumn home of the Wallowa band of the Nimiipuu, or Nez Perce, who fished the lake for red salmon, hunted in the mountains and raised famous herds of horses. A treaty in 1855 reserved the valley for them, but after gold was found in Idaho, a new treaty in 1863 shrank the reservation and left the Wallowa outside it; Old Joseph's band never signed it. Settlers began moving in during the 1870s, and in 1877 the army ordered the band onto the reservation in Idaho. Killings of settlers by a few young warriors set off a war, and the band's long retreat toward Canada ended in surrender. Joseph was sent into exile and never allowed to return to live in the valley.`,`The town that took his name was laid out in 1883, and its people lived on cattle, sheep and timber. In 1896 three men robbed the town's bank, and a gunfight broke out in the street as citizens chased them down; the robbery is reenacted every summer. The mills closed in the 1980s, and in 1982 a bronze foundry opened in town, giving laid-off millworkers new jobs casting sculpture. Joseph now has galleries and bronze statues on its main street. The Nez Perce have returned too, buying back land near the town and holding an annual powwow in the valley.`],deepTitle:`The Wallowa band`,deep:[`Old Joseph, Tuekakas, had been one of the first Nez Perce to become a Christian, but when the 1863 treaty took his land he tore up his New Testament and refused to deal with the government again. He died in 1871 and was buried near the Wallowa River. In 1926 his remains were moved to a burial ground at the north end of Wallowa Lake, where his grave can still be visited.`,`The Nez Perce War of 1877 was one of the most remarkable military campaigns in American history. About 750 people, including women, children and elders, with some 2,000 horses, outmanoeuvred several US Army columns over three months, winning several battles and travelling through what is now Yellowstone National Park. Their surrender came only after General Nelson Miles cut them off with a fresh force. Joseph spent his later years on the Colville Reservation in Washington. In 1899 he visited the Wallowa and asked to buy a small piece of land; the settlers refused. He died in 1904.`,`Wallowa Lake, a glacial lake enclosed by enormous moraines, has been a summer resort since the early twentieth century. The Wallowa Lake Tramway, opened in 1970, carries visitors up to the summit of Mount Howard, more than 1,000 m above the lake, and is one of the steepest gondolas in North America.`,`To the east, Hells Canyon, carved by the Snake River, is the deepest river gorge in North America, more than 2,400 m deep in places. In the spring of 1877, obeying the order to move to the reservation, the Wallowa band swam their families and herds across the flooding Snake at its edge, weeks before the war began; it remains one of the wildest places in the American West. The area's remoteness, which kept industry small, now draws visitors looking for quiet mountain towns and empty trails.`]},"fairbanks-ak":{why:`Deep-interior continentality, basin geometry, and polar-jet access make Fairbanks a trapped bowl. Far from any moderating ocean, with the Alaska Range blocking Pacific moisture and the Brooks Range blocking Arctic storms, the Tanana Valley radiates heat freely in summer and stratifies into ice fog in winter. The sun-latitude flip — from about twenty-one hours of daylight to about four — amplifies both extremes. January nights routinely plunge far below anything a coastal Alaskan town of the same latitude would see. The ridge above town is a different climate from the floodplain. Continentality is the whole engine.`,history:[`In August 1901 a trader named E.T. Barnette was trying to get a load of goods up the Tanana River to set up a trading post where the trail to the Yukon gold fields crossed it. The river was low, and the steamer he had chartered, the Lavelle Young, turned into a shallow side stream, the Chena, and ran aground. The captain refused to go any further and put Barnette, his wife and their supplies ashore on the riverbank, in the middle of nowhere. Barnette was furious. Then, eleven months later, an Italian prospector named Felix Pedro struck gold on a creek about 25 km away, and Barnette's stranded trading post became the only store for hundreds of kilometres. Fairbanks was born of a bad-tempered steamboat captain and a lucky strike.`,`The Lower Tanana Dene, Athabascan people who had lived along these rivers for thousands of years, fished, hunted and trapped across the valley; the ridge where the University of Alaska now stands was a place they called Troth Yeddha', where they gathered wild potato roots. After Pedro's strike, thousands of miners poured in. A federal judge, James Wickersham, persuaded Barnette to name the camp after Charles Fairbanks, a senator from Indiana who later became vice president. Barnette became the first mayor and founded a bank, which collapsed in 1911 with the depositors' money; by then he had left town, and he was eventually acquitted of all but a minor charge.`,`Gold dredges worked the creeks for decades, and in 1923 President Warren Harding came north to drive the golden spike on the Alaska Railroad at Nenana; he died just a few weeks later. In the Second World War, American pilots flew nearly eight thousand Lend-Lease warplanes from Ladd Field in Fairbanks to Soviet crews, who flew them across Siberia to the Eastern Front. Then came oil. In the 1970s, Fairbanks was the base for building the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, and the town boomed again. Today it is a university, military and research town that lives with some of the most extreme temperatures in North America — summer days of endless sunlight, and winters where the thermometer can drop below −40°C.`],deepTitle:`Gold, ice and light`,deep:[`The University of Alaska was founded in 1917 as an agricultural college and school of mines, and opened in 1922 with six students. It has become one of the world's leading centres for the study of permafrost, the aurora borealis and the Arctic climate. Its location, beneath the auroral oval, makes the northern lights a regular winter visitor, and in recent years tourists, especially from Asia, have come in their thousands to see them.`,`Since 1906 the town has celebrated the summer solstice with a baseball game that starts late in the evening and is played without artificial lights. The Midnight Sun Game, played by the Alaska Goldpanners in recent decades, has never needed them; the sun barely sets. In winter the opposite holds: on the shortest days the sun is above the horizon for less than four hours.`,`Fairbanks's worst disaster was not cold but water. In August 1967, heavy rains sent the Chena River over its banks and flooded most of the town; thousands of people were evacuated, many to the university on its ridge. The flood led to the building of a dam and flood-control project upstream, which has protected the city ever since.`,`Living through a Fairbanks winter takes special skills. Below about −35°C, the moisture from cars, houses and power plants freezes into ice fog that can hang over the city for days. Cars must be plugged into electric heaters overnight, and people learn to dress in many layers. The Yukon Quest sled-dog race, run between Fairbanks and Whitehorse since 1984, and the World Ice Art Championships, where carvers sculpt giant blocks of ice so clear it is called Arctic diamond, celebrate the cold rather than flee it.`]},"sitka-ak":{why:`Sitka faces the open Pacific on Baranof Island, where the Alaska Current delivers relatively warm water even in winter. Steep coastal mountains force almost continuous orographic rain; the Sitka spruce–western hemlock forest is that rainfall made visible. Latitude is subarctic, but marine moderation keeps winter near freezing at sea level and summer only modestly warm. Snow that would bury an Interior town of the same latitude rarely sticks on the tide line. The mechanism is a warm current plus mountain lift plus an outer-coast exposure, producing a hyper-maritime climate rather than a continental Alaskan one.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, dark and very wet — afternoons near 4°C, nights hovering around freezing, and Pacific storms that roll in one after another with driving rain, gales and the occasional snowfall that rarely lasts more than a few days. Days are short, with barely seven hours of light at the solstice, and the cloud-capped cone of Mount Edgecumbe comes and goes across the water.`,summer:`Cool, green and long-lit — afternoons near 17°C, nights around 10°C, and a soft, misty rain that falls even on summer days, though a run of clear days turns the islands and peaks brilliant. Daylight lasts nearly eighteen hours in June. Humpback whales feed in Sitka Sound, salmon fill the rivers, and cruise ships call at the harbour.`},history:[`On the afternoon of 18 October 1867, Russian and American soldiers stood at attention on the hill above Sitka for the ceremony that transferred Alaska to the United States. As the cannons fired, a Russian sailor began lowering the double-headed eagle flag of the tsars — and it tangled around the flagpole and refused to come down. A sailor had to be hoisted up the pole to free it; he threw it down and it landed on the bayonets of the Russian soldiers below, and the wife of the Russian governor is said to have fainted. The Tlingit, who had lived here for thousands of years, watched from their canoes. Their leaders pointed out that the Russians had never owned the land they had just sold.`,`The Tlingit called the place Sheet'ká, and the Kiks.ádi clan had long fished its rich waters for herring and salmon. In 1799 Alexander Baranov of the Russian-American Company built a fort nearby to control the trade in sea-otter furs. In 1802 the Tlingit destroyed it; in 1804 the Russians returned with a warship and besieged the Tlingit fort at Indian River for days, until the Kiks.ádi, running out of gunpowder, slipped away by night on a long march across the island. The Russians built a new town, New Archangel, and made it the capital of Russian America — a settlement of onion-domed churches, a fur trading company and, by some accounts, more culture than any other port on the northern Pacific.`,`After 1867 the town remained the capital of American Alaska until 1906, when the seat of government moved to Juneau. It became a centre of Native activism: the Alaska Native Brotherhood, which fought for civil rights for Alaska Natives, was founded in Sitka in 1912. In the Second World War the navy built an air station across the harbour, and in 1959 a pulp mill opened south of town, employing hundreds until it closed in 1993. Today Sitka lives on fishing, tourism, health care and the Coast Guard, and its Tlingit, Russian and American layers sit together in a town that the rainforest still crowds against the sea.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Battle of Sitka in 1804 is now commemorated in Sitka National Historical Park, where a trail through the rainforest leads past carved totem poles to the site of the Tlingit fort. The Kiks.ádi retreat across Baranof Island, known as the Survival March, is remembered by clan members as a strategic withdrawal rather than a defeat. The clan returned to Sitka about twenty years later, and Tlingit and Russians lived uneasily side by side for the rest of the Russian era.`,`St. Michael's Cathedral, built in the 1840s, was the seat of the Russian Orthodox church in the Americas. In January 1966 a fire swept through downtown Sitka and destroyed the cathedral, but townspeople ran into the burning building and rescued almost all of its icons and treasures. The cathedral was rebuilt to the same design and still stands in the middle of the main street. The Russian Bishop's House, completed in 1843, is one of the oldest buildings of the Russian colonial era in the Americas.`,`On 1 April 1974, residents of Sitka looked across the sound and saw black smoke pouring from the crater of Mount Edgecumbe, a dormant volcano. Some thought it was erupting. It was a prank: a local man known as Porky Bickar had spent years planning it, and finally flew dozens of old tyres into the crater by helicopter and set them on fire. He had warned the authorities in advance, and spray-painted the words APRIL FOOL in giant letters on the snow beside the crater. The prank became a legend of Alaskan humour.`,`Sitka's rainforest, part of the Tongass National Forest, is part of the largest temperate rainforest on earth. Its giant Sitka spruces and hemlocks grow in a climate that brings almost 3,000 mm of rain a year, much of it in autumn storms. Logging of the old-growth forest fed the pulp mill for decades, and debates over how much of the forest to protect still divide the town.`]},"valdez-ak":{why:`Valdez occupies the head of a deep fjord where the Chugach Mountains rise more than 3,000 m almost from tidewater. Pacific storms hit that wall and have nowhere to go but up, wringing prodigious snow; Thompson Pass inland is even snowier. Katabatic air can drain off the icefields on clear winter nights, funneling down the fjord. Summers stay cool and often overcast because the same marine fetch persists. Orographic collision, not lake effect, is the engine. The port is ice-free relative to the Interior, which is why oil and gold rushes both needed this particular hole in the coast.`,seasons:{winter:`Buried — afternoons a little below freezing, nights near −8°C, and snow that comes in astonishing quantities as Pacific storms slam into the Chugach Mountains: several metres fall in town over a winter, and much more up on Thompson Pass. Cold winds pour down the valleys from the interior, roofs must be shovelled, and the town's snow-removal crews are among the best in the world.`,summer:`Cool and rainy but magnificent — afternoons near 17°C, nights around 9°C, and frequent rain that keeps hundreds of waterfalls pouring off the mountains around the fjord. Long days of nearly nineteen hours of light at the solstice bring salmon runs, fishing derbies, glacier kayaking and wildlife cruises on Prince William Sound.`},contrasts:[{label:`Thompson Pass (≈40 km NE, ≈850 m higher)`,note:`The pass above town holds the Alaskan records for snow — about 25 m in the winter of 1952–53 — and draws heli-skiers from around the world.`},{label:`Glennallen (≈190 km N, across the Chugach)`,note:`In the dry interior basin beyond the mountains, winters are far colder, often below −30°C, but bring only a fraction of Valdez's snow.`}],history:[`At 5:36 in the afternoon of Good Friday, 27 March 1964, the freighter Chena was unloading at the Valdez dock while townspeople watched from the pier, as they often did. Then the strongest earthquake ever recorded in North America began — magnitude 9.2. The ground under the waterfront, built on loose glacial silt, slid into the fjord, taking the dock and everyone on it with it. A wave lifted the Chena high in the air, dropped it into the hole where the dock had been, and then lifted it again; the ship's crew managed to get it out to sea. Thirty-two people on the dock were killed. The townsite was condemned, and over the next few years the people of Valdez moved their entire town about 6 km west to firmer ground.`,`The fjord had been travelled by Sugpiaq (Chugach) people for thousands of years, and the Ahtna came down from the Copper River country to trade with them. Spanish explorers named the port in 1790 after Spain's navy minister, Antonio Valdés. In 1898, during the Klondike gold rush, steamship companies promoted an all-American route to the gold fields through Valdez, and some four thousand people arrived to cross the Valdez Glacier. Many suffered snow blindness, frostbite and scurvy, and few reached the gold fields. But a trail was built north to the interior, later becoming the Richardson Highway, and Valdez became a gateway to Alaska.`,`Just after the town rebuilt, it was chosen as the southern terminus of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, which carries oil 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay on the Arctic coast. The first tanker sailed from Valdez in 1977. On 24 March 1989 one of those tankers, the Exxon Valdez, ran aground on Bligh Reef, about 40 km from town, spilling some 41 million litres of crude oil into Prince William Sound — one of the worst environmental disasters in American history. Valdez became the base for the cleanup. Today the town lives on oil, fishing and tourism, and every winter on staggering amounts of snow.`],deepTitle:`Quake, oil, snow`,deep:[`The 1964 earthquake shook the ground for about four and a half minutes, and its effects were felt across Alaska; Anchorage lost much of its downtown and the tsunami killed people as far away as California. In Valdez, landslides beneath the water also triggered waves that swept the waterfront. The new town of Valdez, built in the late 1960s, won national recognition for the way its people had rebuilt, but the old townsite still lies abandoned east of the modern town.`,`The gold rush of 1898 was a case of false advertising. The route over the Valdez Glacier was promoted as an easy way to reach the Klondike without passing through Canada, but in reality it meant hauling supplies over a vast field of ice and crevasses in deep snow. Men died of exposure and disease. The army sent expeditions to find a better route, and their trail through Keystone Canyon and over Thompson Pass laid the foundation for the road north.`,`The Exxon Valdez spill spread across about 2,000 km of coastline, killing hundreds of thousands of seabirds, thousands of sea otters and hundreds of seals. Thousands of workers spent months scrubbing beaches by hand and with hot water. Some species have never fully recovered; the herring fishery in Prince William Sound collapsed a few years later and has not come back. The disaster led to new laws requiring double-hulled tankers and escort tugs, which now shepherd every tanker out of Valdez.`,`Valdez's snow has made it a destination for extreme sports. The World Extreme Skiing Championships were held here in the 1990s, and heli-skiers and snowboarders from around the world come to ride the steep, snow-loaded Chugach peaks. In winter, frozen waterfalls in Keystone Canyon become a classic destination for ice climbers.`]},"death-valley-ca":{why:`Furnace Creek sits below sea level in a pull-apart basin between the Panamint Range to the west and the Amargosa Range to the east. Pacific storms must cross several high ranges before arriving, so rain is a rarity. Dense air in the sub-sea-level trough heats and cannot easily lift away; rock and salt pan reradiate. Summer is a heat engine; winter is mild, bright, and the season humans actually use the valley floor. Four-range rain shadow plus basin geometry, not a single desert cliché, explains why this is among the hottest, driest inhabited points in North America.`,history:[`Around Christmas 1849, a hundred or so emigrants bound for the California gold fields came down into this valley by a shortcut that a map — and a lot of optimism — had promised would save them weeks. Instead they found a sink of salt and sand ringed by mountains they could not cross with wagons. They slaughtered their oxen, burned their wagons to dry the meat, and waited while two young men, William Lewis Manly and John Rogers, walked some 400 km to a ranch north of Los Angeles and back to lead them out. As the survivors climbed over the Panamint Range in early 1850, one of them, by tradition, looked back and said: Goodbye, Death Valley. The name stuck, though remarkably only one of their party is known to have died here.`,`The Timbisha Shoshone had been living in the valley for a thousand years before that, wintering at the springs on the valley floor, gathering mesquite beans, and moving up to the Panamint Range in summer to harvest pine nuts. They knew where the water was. The miners who came after the forty-niners did not. Silver and gold camps rose and fell, but the strike that lasted was borax, the white mineral crust on the salt flats. From 1883 to 1888 the Harmony Borax Works shipped it out in giant wagons pulled by twenty-mule teams, which hauled more than 30 tonnes per trip across 270 km of desert to the railroad at Mojave. The twenty-mule team became the trademark of the borax company and the symbol of the valley.`,`When the borax mines moved elsewhere, the company decided to sell the desert itself. It built the Furnace Creek Inn in 1927, and in 1933 President Hoover proclaimed Death Valley a national monument. On 10 July 1913, a thermometer at Furnace Creek had recorded 56.7°C, still listed as the hottest air temperature ever measured on earth, though some meteorologists doubt it; readings above 54°C in recent summers are not in dispute. In 1994 Death Valley became a national park, the largest in the lower forty-eight, and in 2000 the Timbisha Shoshone finally won back a homeland of their own inside it — the first time a reservation had been created within a national park.`],deepTitle:`Borax and Scotty`,deep:[`The twenty-mule teams were actually eighteen mules and two horses, hitched to two enormous wagons and a water tank. The trip took about ten days each way, with summer temperatures on the valley floor that made work impossible during the hottest months. Despite the danger, the company liked to boast that no wagon was ever lost. The image of the twenty-mule team was later used to sell borax soap for nearly a century, and a radio and television show, Death Valley Days, sponsored by the company, ran for decades; one of its hosts was Ronald Reagan.`,`The valley's most famous resident was a con man. Walter Scott, known as Death Valley Scotty, was a former trick rider in Buffalo Bill's Wild West show who claimed to have a secret gold mine in the valley. In 1905 he hired a special train and set a speed record from Los Angeles to Chicago, spending money that came from investors in his imaginary mine. One of them, a Chicago insurance millionaire named Albert Johnson, came to see the mine, discovered the desert was good for his health, and spent the 1920s and 1930s building a Spanish-style mansion in the northern part of the valley. Scotty told everyone it was his castle. Johnson, apparently amused, let him.`,`The Timbisha Shoshone's road to a homeland was long. When the monument was created, the government restricted them to a small village at Furnace Creek, with no title to the land and at times under pressure to leave. They were federally recognised as a tribe only in 1983. The Timbisha Shoshone Homeland Act of 2000 placed about 3,100 hectares in trust for the tribe, including land at Furnace Creek, and recognised their right to use traditional areas within the park.`,`Death Valley holds the record for the lowest point in North America, Badwater Basin, 86 m below sea level, and lies only about 140 km from the highest point in the lower forty-eight, Mount Whitney. Rain is so rare that the valley averages about 50 mm a year, but when it falls, it can be catastrophic: in October 2015 a single storm sent floods through Scotty's Castle, closing it for years, and in August 2023 the remnants of Hurricane Hilary dropped more rain in a day than the valley often receives in a year. After the rarest wet winters, the valley floor bursts into superblooms of wildflowers.`]},"stanley-id":{why:`Stanley sits in a broad, high valley at nearly 2,000 m, ringed by the Sawtooths and drained by the Salmon. On clear, calm nights, cold air slides off 3,000 m peaks and ponds on the valley floor under an inversion lid. Dry continental air radiates heat to space efficiently; peat and grass do not store it. Frost can occur in any month, and winter readings often undercut much of the northern Plains. The town is an inhabited cold sink of the same family as other Rocky Mountain frost hollows, tightened by elevation and enclosure.`,seasons:{winter:`Brutally cold on clear nights — afternoons hovering around freezing, nights near −13°C, and still mornings when cold air pools in the basin and the thermometer can plunge below −35°C, making Stanley the coldest spot in the lower forty-eight on the national weather map. Snow piles up steadily, the Sawtooths glitter, and the town of barely a hundred people hunkers down while snowmobilers and backcountry skiers come for the powder.`,spring:`A slow thaw — afternoons climbing from about 2°C in March to 15°C by late May, but nights stay well below freezing well into May, and snow lingers in the forests into June. The Salmon River swells with snowmelt, elk and deer return to the valley meadows, and the first wildflowers appear in the sagebrush.`,summer:`Warm, dry days and freezing nights — afternoons near 26°C, but nights that can drop to around 0°C even in July, with frost possible any month. Clear skies and thin air make for brilliant stargazing. River rafters and hikers fill the valley, and wildfire smoke can drift in from late July.`,autumn:`Short, golden and frosty — September afternoons near 21°C but nights already below freezing, aspens and willows turning gold along the river, and the first snow often dusting the Sawtooths by late September. By November afternoons barely reach 5°C, snow is settling in, and the town empties until the snowmobilers arrive.`},history:[`In the summer of 1992, biologists at Redfish Lake, just south of Stanley, waited for the year's run of sockeye salmon to return from the Pacific. These fish make the longest and highest migration of any sockeye in the world, swimming some 1,450 km up the Columbia, Snake and Salmon rivers and climbing nearly 2,000 m higher to reach the lake where they were born. The lake got its name because in the nineteenth century so many spawning salmon crowded its shallows that the water seemed to turn red. That year, only one fish came back: a male that the biologists nicknamed Lonesome Larry. His genes went into a captive-breeding program that has kept the run alive, and in good years, hundreds of sockeye now make it home.`,`The Sawtooth Valley was the home of the Tukudeka, the Mountain Shoshone known as Sheepeaters for the bighorn sheep they hunted; they fished salmon and moved with the seasons through the mountains. Fur trappers crossed the basin in the 1820s but found few beaver. In 1863 a party of gold prospectors led by Captain John Stanley, a Civil War veteran, explored the valley and left behind his name. Mining camps sprang up in the surrounding hills — Custer, Bonanza, Vienna — and in 1879 the army fought the Sheepeater War, forcing the Tukudeka onto reservations. When the mines played out, the camps were abandoned, and a few ranchers stayed on in the cold valley.`,`Stanley stayed tiny; its streets were not even surveyed until 1919, and it still has only about a hundred year-round residents. In the late 1960s a mining company proposed a huge open-pit molybdenum mine in the White Cloud Mountains east of the valley, and the fight against it helped elect an Idaho governor and persuaded Congress to protect the area. In 1972 President Nixon signed the law creating the Sawtooth National Recreation Area, which preserves both the mountains and the ranching character of the valley. In 2017 the region became the first International Dark Sky Reserve in the United States, protecting the brilliant starry skies above Stanley.`],deepTitle:`Salmon and sawteeth`,deep:[`The decline of Idaho's sockeye was caused largely by the dams built on the Snake and Columbia rivers in the twentieth century. Young salmon heading downstream to the sea must pass eight dams, and many die along the way. In 1991 the Snake River sockeye became the first salmon in the Columbia basin listed as endangered under the Endangered Species Act. Debates over whether to breach the four lower Snake River dams to help the salmon have gone on for decades, and Stanley's own future as a fishing destination is tied to their outcome.`,`The mining camp of Custer, in the Yankee Fork valley north of Stanley, boomed in the 1880s and was abandoned by 1911; its buildings are preserved as a ghost-town museum. Nearby sits the Yankee Fork Gold Dredge, a giant floating machine that worked the river from 1940 to 1952, leaving behind kilometres of gravel tailings. The dredge is now a museum too, and the river is being restored for salmon.`,`The Sheepeater War of 1879 began after the deaths of several Chinese miners and two settlers were blamed on the Tukudeka, though the evidence was thin. Army troops chased small bands through some of the most rugged country in the West for months; the Sheepeaters, numbering only a few dozen fighters, eventually surrendered and were sent to the Fort Hall reservation. It was one of the last Indian wars in the Pacific Northwest.`,`The fight over the White Clouds in the late 1960s made Cecil Andrus, who opposed the mine, governor of Idaho in 1970; he later served as Secretary of the Interior. Senator Frank Church guided the Sawtooth bill through Congress. The White Clouds themselves were finally protected as wilderness in 2015. Stanley's ranches, rustic lodges and view of the jagged Sawtooths, rising more than 1,000 m above the valley floor, have changed little since.`]},"mount-washington-nh":{why:`The summit stands in the Presidential Range where three storm tracks converge, at an altitude that reaches into the free troposphere, on a ridge that nozzles wind like a venturi. No other peak in the northeastern United States combines that traffic of lows, that height, and that terrain focusing. The result is alpine tundra, rime, and hurricane-force winds at a latitude that grows hardwoods only a few vertical kilometers below. Elevation lapse rate starts the cold; geometry and jet-stream exposure do the rest. It is a maritime-arctic mountain climate stranded in New England.`,feel:`Mount Washington feels like a piece of the Arctic dropped onto New England. Its summit sits where three storm tracks converge and the jet stream often roars overhead, and with no higher ground anywhere nearby to block the wind, it gets the full force of every storm that passes. Fog hides the top most days, rime ice grows like feathers on every surface, and the weather can change from pleasant to deadly within an hour — even in July.`,seasons:{winter:`Arctic and violent — afternoons near −8°C, nights near −17°C, and winds that reach hurricane force on many winter days, pushing windchills far below −40°C. Rime ice coats the summit buildings in thick feathers that grow into the wind. Fog and blowing snow make visibility near zero for days at a time, and the observatory staff work in shifts, venturing outside only roped up.`,spring:`Winter in disguise — afternoons climbing from about −5°C in March to near 7°C by late May, storms still dropping heavy snow, and fog clinging to the summit. On the mountain's flanks, Tuckerman Ravine fills with deep snow that draws daring skiers to its steep headwall into June.`,summer:`Cool, foggy and changeable — afternoons near 13°C, nights around 6°C, and fog covering the summit on most days. Thunderstorms and cold fronts can bring hail, sleet and dangerous winds without warning. On clear days the view stretches to the Atlantic and into Canada, and hikers, cog railway riders and drivers crowd the summit.`,autumn:`Fast and harsh — the first snow can fall in September, and by October the summit is back in winter, with afternoons near freezing and ice already forming. Below, the valleys blaze with autumn colour while the summit wears a white cap. Storms grow more frequent and the Auto Road closes for the season by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come to ride the Cog Railway or drive the Auto Road to the summit, hike the Presidential Range, visit the Mount Washington Observatory's weather museum and see for themselves the place once famed for the fastest surface wind ever recorded.`,residentFit:`Nobody lives on the summit except the observers of the Mount Washington Observatory, who work week-long shifts in one of the harshest weather stations on earth; the valley towns below suit hikers, skiers and mountain guides.`,history:[`On the afternoon of 12 April 1934, three men in a small wooden building on the summit of Mount Washington watched their wind instruments with growing disbelief. A storm had been strengthening all day, and the gusts kept climbing. Wendell Stephenson, Alex McKenzie and Salvatore Pagliuca took readings again and again, iced over their instruments, chipped them clear, and checked the calibration. At 1:21 pm a gust hit 372 km/h — the fastest wind speed ever recorded on the surface of the earth. It held the world record for more than sixty years, until a cyclone off Australia beat it in 1996, and it is still the highest wind ever measured by people watching the instrument.`,`The Abenaki called the mountain Agiocochook and considered it the home of the Great Spirit, and they were in no hurry to climb it. In 1642 a colonist named Darby Field made the first recorded ascent, with a few Abenaki guides who stopped short of the top. The mountain was named for George Washington in 1784, five years before he became president. In the nineteenth century tourists began to come in large numbers: a stone hotel, the Tip-Top House, opened on the summit in 1853, a road for horse-drawn carriages was completed in 1861, and in 1869 the Mount Washington Cog Railway, the first mountain-climbing rack railway in the world, began carrying passengers to the top. Its inventor, Sylvester Marsh, had been told by the state legislature that he might as well build a railway to the moon.`,`Scientists spent the winter of 1870–71 on the summit to see if a person could survive there, and in 1932 the Mount Washington Observatory was founded to staff the summit year-round. More than 160 people have died on the mountain, many of them hikers caught by sudden storms. At the mountain's foot, in the grand Mount Washington Hotel in Bretton Woods, delegates from 44 nations met in July 1944 to design the postwar financial system, creating the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank. Today the summit is a state park with a modern weather station, and the observatory's crew, along with its resident cat, still record some of the world's worst weather.`],deepTitle:`Worst weather`,deep:[`The first recorded death on the mountain came in 1849, when an English traveller named Frederick Strickland lost his way in a storm. In 1855 a young woman named Lizzie Bourne died of exposure just a few hundred metres from the summit hotel while climbing with her uncle and cousin; a memorial marks the spot. The mountain's weather can turn from calm and sunny to freezing, foggy and windy in minutes, and its many deaths have made it one of the most dangerous small mountains in the world.`,`The Cog Railway was a marvel of engineering when it opened. Its little locomotives, tilted so that their boilers stay level on the steep slope, push carriages up a toothed track with grades of more than 37 per cent on the steepest trestle, known as Jacob's Ladder. The railway still runs every summer, now with biodiesel locomotives as well as steam.`,`The 1944 Bretton Woods conference brought together economists and diplomats, including John Maynard Keynes, to plan how the world's money would work after the Second World War. They agreed to tie currencies to the US dollar and the dollar to gold, a system that lasted until 1971. The hotel, built in 1902, still stands at the foot of the mountain, its red roof bright against the green forests.`,`The observatory's summit crew lives in a building designed to withstand winds well beyond the 1934 record. Its resident cats have become minor celebrities — the most famous, Marty, lived there from 2008 to 2020. Researchers study icing, clouds and climate, and the mountain's long weather records are among the most valuable anywhere for understanding how the climate of the northeastern mountains is changing.`]},"yuma-az":{why:`A four-range rain shadow, a clear desert atmosphere, and Colorado River irrigation together make a Sunbelt agricultural district with almost no cloudy days. Winter sun at this latitude, through dry air, is more effective than April light at many higher latitudes. Summer afternoons routinely climb past 40 °C; nights in January still stay well above a hard freeze on the valley floor. Sunshine is the brand. Water is the argument. Without the river there is no city, only creosote and sand. The canals turn a hyperarid Sonoran corner into winter lettuce country; they do not cancel the heat.`,feel:`Yuma feels like the sun's own address. It is among the sunniest places on earth, cloudless on most days of the year, and the dry desert air turns winter into one long, golden afternoon — warm enough for shirtsleeves while the rest of the country shivers. The Colorado River, tamed by dams and canals, spreads a carpet of lettuce fields across the desert floor. Then summer arrives, the furnace door opens, and the town shelters indoors until October.`,seasons:{winter:`The reward season — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 7°C, and sunshine on almost every day. Lettuce, broccoli and spinach fill the green fields that supply most of America's winter salad greens, snowbirds fill the RV parks, and the light is soft and golden. A rare Pacific storm may bring a day of rain; frost is almost unknown.`,spring:`Warm, then fierce — afternoons climbing from about 27°C in March to 36°C by late May, with no rain to speak of, and the last of the winter visitors heading north by April. Desert wildflowers bloom briefly after wet winters, and the lettuce harvest wraps up as the heat arrives.`,summer:`A furnace — afternoons near 42°C and often hotter, nights that barely fall below 27°C, and sun that bakes the asphalt soft. Most activity shifts to early morning and after dark. In late summer the monsoon can push in humid air, dust storms and the occasional violent thunderstorm, but rain stays rare — Yuma averages only a few millimetres in these months.`,autumn:`A slow release — September is still scorching, with afternoons near 40°C and the odd monsoon storm, but by November afternoons have eased to about 26°C, the nights are cool, and the winter visitors and the vegetable crews return.`},history:[`In 1899 a young woman named Pearl Hart and a partner held up a stagecoach in the Arizona desert, one of the last stagecoach robberies in the West. They got lost making their getaway and were caught within days. Pearl, who claimed she had robbed the coach to pay for her sick mother's care, became a newspaper sensation and was sent to the Yuma Territorial Prison, the most feared penitentiary in the territory. Built in 1876 on a bluff over the Colorado River by the convicts themselves, it was a place of stone cells, iron bars and summer heat that inmates called the hellhole of Arizona. Pearl was released early, in December 1902. When the prison closed in 1909, the building became the town's high school, and Yuma's teams have been called the Criminals ever since.`,`The Quechan and Cocopah peoples farmed the floodplain of the Colorado for centuries, planting corn, beans and squash in the silt left by the river's floods. The narrow crossing where the Colorado and Gila rivers met was the only good ford for hundreds of kilometres, and whoever held it controlled the route between Mexico and California. In 1781 the Quechan rose against the Spanish missions built at the crossing, killing the priests and soldiers, including the famous missionary explorer Francisco Garcés, and closing the overland road to California for decades. In the gold rush of 1849, thousands of forty-niners poured through the crossing, and the army built Fort Yuma to protect them.`,`Steamboats came up the Colorado from the Gulf of California, carrying supplies to the mines and forts of the territory, and in 1877 the Southern Pacific railroad bridged the river at Yuma. The Bureau of Reclamation dammed and channelled the river in the early twentieth century, turning the desert valley into some of the most productive farmland in America; today Yuma grows most of the leafy greens Americans eat in winter. The town's dry, sunny air also made it a centre for military testing and aviation, and the Marine Corps flies its jets from an air station on the edge of town.`],deepTitle:`Crossing and prison`,deep:[`The Yuma Crossing was so valuable that it attracted some of the West's most notorious characters. In 1850 a former Texas Ranger and scalp hunter named John Glanton seized the ferry that the Quechan had been operating and began robbing travellers. The Quechan attacked and killed Glanton and most of his gang. The episode later inspired Cormac McCarthy's novel Blood Meridian.`,`The Yuma Territorial Prison housed more than three thousand prisoners in its thirty-three years. Despite its terrible reputation, it was in some ways advanced for its time — it had electricity, a library, and a hospital — and some locals grumbled that it was a country club on the Colorado. Elmore Leonard's story 3:10 to Yuma, about a rancher escorting an outlaw to the train that will take him to the prison, became two famous westerns. The old prison is now a state historic park.`,`Francisco Garcés, the Franciscan who died in the 1781 uprising, was one of the great explorers of the Southwest. He crossed the Mojave Desert, reached the Grand Canyon and visited the Hopi mesas, often travelling alone with Native guides. The two missions at the crossing had been built with little regard for the Quechan's fields and leaders, and their destruction cut California off from overland contact with Mexico for the rest of the Spanish era.`,`Yuma's winter vegetable industry depends on Colorado River water, delivered through a network of canals, and on thousands of farmworkers, many of whom cross the border from Mexico each day before dawn to cut lettuce in the fields. As the river shrinks under drought and over-allocation, the question of how much water Yuma's farms will keep is one of the most important in the West.`]},"south-padre-tx":{why:`South Padre is a barrier island between the Gulf of Mexico and the hypersaline Laguna Madre, so water buffers temperature from both sides. Low latitude keeps winter nights far from freezing; afternoon sea breeze caps summer heat that inland South Texas still suffers. A subsidence inversion and marine layer do additional work on the hottest days. The annual range is narrow for Texas because the island is a sand ribbon, not a prairie. Hurricane tracks across this same corridor are the price of that double-water moderation.`,feel:`South Padre Island feels like the tropics' first outpost in Texas. A thin ribbon of sand between the Gulf of Mexico and the shallow, salty Laguna Madre, it has warm water on both sides and a steady sea breeze that keeps summer bearable and winter mild — palm trees, pelicans and kites in a sky that is almost always moving. Northers can still bring a sharp chill in winter, and from August to October, everyone keeps one eye on the Gulf.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and breezy — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 13°C, and many sunny days broken by the occasional norther that sweeps down from the plains and drops the temperature 10°C in a few hours, with grey skies and gusty winds for a day or two. The winter Texans arrive from the north, birders walk the boardwalks, and the water is cool but swimmable on warm days.`,spring:`Windy and warming — afternoons rising from about 25°C in March to 30°C by late May, with strong southeasterly breezes that make the Laguna Madre one of the best places for kiteboarding and windsurfing in the country. College students flood the island for spring break in March, and sea turtles begin nesting on the beaches by April.`,summer:`Hot, humid and breezy — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 26°C, and a steady Gulf breeze that keeps the island several degrees cooler than the mainland. The Gulf water is as warm as a bath. Thunderstorms are occasional, and the hurricane season looms from June, with the greatest risk in August and September.`,autumn:`Hurricane season's peak, then the island's finest weather — September and early October bring the wettest weeks of the year, with tropical storms and heavy downpours, but by late October afternoons ease to about 28°C, the humidity falls and the water stays warm. Migrating birds and monarch butterflies pass through on their way south.`},residentFit:`It suits winter Texans escaping northern cold, fishermen, birders and surfers who are happy on a narrow barrier island, plan seriously for hurricane season and do not mind spring break crowds for a few weeks each March.`,contrasts:[{label:`Brownsville (≈35 km W, inland)`,note:`Away from the sea breeze, summer afternoons in the Rio Grande Valley run several degrees hotter and the humidity feels heavier.`},{label:`Corpus Christi (≈200 km N)`,note:`Further up the coast, winter northers bite harder and more often, and January nights run a few degrees colder.`}],history:[`In April 1554 a fleet of four Spanish ships, bound from Veracruz to Spain with a cargo of silver, was caught by a storm and three of them were wrecked on Padre Island. About three hundred survivors struggled ashore and began walking south along the beach toward the Spanish settlements at Pánuco, hundreds of kilometres away. Without food or water, and attacked along the way, nearly all of them died. One of the few survivors, a friar named Marcos de Mena, was badly wounded and left for dead — his companions, by tradition, buried him in the sand to protect him from the sun — but he recovered and staggered on to reach safety. In the 1960s treasure hunters began raising the silver, and Texas responded by passing one of the first laws in the nation to protect shipwrecks as historic sites.`,`The Karankawa and other coastal peoples had camped along the island for centuries, fishing and gathering shellfish in the Laguna Madre. In the early 1800s a Spanish priest, Padre José Nicolás Ballí, received a land grant on the island and ran cattle there with his nephew; the island still bears his title. Later John Singer, brother of the inventor of the Singer sewing machine, was shipwrecked here in 1847 and settled down to ranch. When the Civil War broke out, legend says he buried his family's fortune in the dunes before fleeing; it has never been found. The last land battle of the Civil War was fought at Palmito Ranch, near the mouth of the Rio Grande just southwest of the island, in May 1865 — weeks after Lee had surrendered.`,`For most of its history the island was reachable only by boat. The Queen Isabella Causeway opened in 1954, linking it to the mainland at Port Isabel, and a resort town grew up on the island's southern tip. In September 2001 a barge struck the causeway and knocked down part of the bridge, and eight people died when their cars plunged into the gap. Today South Padre Island is a spring-break and winter-Texan destination, with high-rise hotels, fishing piers, and a famous sea turtle rescue centre. Just across the channel at Boca Chica, rockets from SpaceX's Starbase now rise over the beach.`],deepTitle:`Silver and turtles`,deep:[`The 1554 shipwreck is one of the earliest documented in North America. The fleet carried silver coins and bars, along with the possessions of wealthy colonists returning to Spain. The Spanish salvaged part of the treasure soon after, but much remained under the sand. When a company began salvaging the wrecks in 1967, a legal battle over who owned the artefacts led to the Texas Antiquities Code of 1969, which declared that wrecks in state waters belonged to the people of Texas.`,`Padre Ballí's ranch was the first European settlement on the island. Under the Spanish grant he and his nephew Juan José Ballí raised cattle, horses and sheep, and the family's claims to the island were argued over in Texas courts for more than a century. A statue of Padre Ballí greets visitors at the entrance to the island.`,`Ila Loetscher, known as the Turtle Lady, founded Sea Turtle Inc. on the island in 1977 to protect the endangered Kemp's ridley sea turtle, whose population had collapsed to only a few hundred nesting females. She gave talks dressed in costume alongside her turtles and became a beloved local character. Thanks to conservation efforts on both sides of the border, the Kemp's ridley has begun to recover, and the hatchlings are released on the island's beaches each summer.`,`Hurricanes have repeatedly reshaped the island. A storm in 1933 swept away early resort plans, Hurricane Beulah in 1967 cut channels through the island and flooded the lower Rio Grande Valley, and Hurricane Dolly in 2008 and Hurricane Hanna in 2020 brought damaging winds and rain. The Gulf's warming waters make strong hurricanes more likely, and the island's dunes are its first and best defence.`]},"ely-mn":{why:`Ely sits on the Canadian Shield: thin soils over granite, a maze of glacial lakes, and boreal forest that holds snow cover through long radiative nights. There is little terrain to mix the boundary layer, so still, clear winters routinely reach extreme cold. Lakes moderate summer only while they are open; from December to April they are ice, which is to say they stop helping. Continentality, polar-jet access, and a white forest floor combine to make this among the coldest towns in the Lower 48. The Boundary Waters are that climate made into a paddling map.`,feel:`Ely feels like the last town before the wilderness. A former iron-mining town of brick storefronts and canoe outfitters, it sits on the edge of the Boundary Waters, a vast roadless country of lakes, rocky islands and dark spruce and pine forest stretching to the Canadian border, where travel is by canoe in summer and by dogsled in winter. The climate is fiercely continental: winters are long, deep-frozen and among the coldest in the lower forty-eight, while summers are short, warm and buzzing with insects, with long golden evenings on the water.`,seasons:{winter:`Deep cold and dry snow — afternoons near −7°C, nights near −18°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −40°C, making Ely one of the coldest towns in the lower forty-eight. The lakes freeze thick enough to drive on, the snow squeaks underfoot, and dog-sled teams run trails through the forest. The northern lights flicker on clear nights.`,spring:`Late and muddy — afternoons climbing from about 0°C in March to near 18°C by late May, with snow lingering in the woods into April and the lakes not clearing of ice until early May. When the ice goes out, the walleye season opens and canoe outfitters start their season.`,summer:`Warm days and cool nights — afternoons near 24°C, nights around 12°C, and the long northern evenings that make paddling the Boundary Waters a pleasure. Thunderstorms are common, and so are mosquitoes and black flies in June. The lakes warm enough for a quick swim by July, and blueberries ripen in the burned-over forests by August.`,autumn:`Brilliant and brief — September brings crisp days near 18°C, birch and aspen turning gold against the dark spruce, and the first frosts. By late October the first snow falls, the canoes are put away and the lakes start to freeze in November.`},residentFit:`It suits paddlers, canoe outfitters, dogsled mushers and off-grid wilderness people who love the deep cold and the silence, and who are willing to live far from cities, hospitals and shopping malls.`,history:[`In 1930 a young woman named Dorothy Molter came from Chicago on a fishing trip to a resort on Knife Lake, deep in the canoe country north of Ely, and never really left. She took over the resort, cared for its owner until he died, and stayed on alone after the government banned motorboats, roads and new buildings in what became the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness. To earn a living she began brewing root beer from lake water and selling it to passing paddlers, thousands of bottles a summer, and became famous as the Root Beer Lady. The government tried to make her leave, but public support was so strong that she was allowed to stay until she died in 1986. Volunteers then hauled her cabins out by sled, and they now stand in Ely as a museum.`,`The lakes around Ely are the homeland of the Ojibwe, who travelled them by birchbark canoe, harvested wild rice and fished their waters; French fur traders followed the same routes in the 1700s. A short-lived gold rush to Lake Vermilion in 1865 found little gold but did find iron. Underground iron mines opened in the 1880s, the railroad arrived in 1888, and Ely grew into a busy mining town of Slovenian, Finnish, Italian and Scandinavian immigrants. Logging crews cut the great white pine forests around the town, and by the turn of the century, outfitters were already advertising the lakes to fishermen and canoeists.`,`The underground mines could not compete with the cheaper open-pit iron of the Mesabi Range, and the last one closed in 1967. By then a long fight had begun over the wilderness on Ely's doorstep. Starting in the 1920s, conservationists including Ely's own writer Sigurd Olson fought to keep roads, dams and resorts out of the canoe country, and in 1978 Congress created the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness, banning most motorboats and logging. Many locals were furious, but the wilderness gave Ely a new economy of outfitters, guides and nature centres. Today proposals for copper and nickel mines just outside the wilderness have reopened the old argument between mining and wilderness.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Sigurd Olson moved to Ely in the 1920s to teach biology and spent his life guiding canoe trips and writing about the canoe country. Books such as The Singing Wilderness made him one of the most beloved nature writers in America, and his campaigns to protect the lakes made him one of the most hated men in Ely; in the 1970s angry residents hanged him in effigy. He died in 1982 after a heart attack while snowshoeing near his home, and his cabin, Listening Point, is preserved on a lake outside town.`,`In 1949 President Truman signed an order banning planes from flying low over the roadless area, cutting off the fly-in fishing resorts that had sprung up on remote lakes. The government bought out the resort owners, and the buildings were removed. That airspace ban, the first of its kind in the United States, was a key step in making the canoe country a true wilderness.`,`Ely has become a centre for the study and appreciation of wolves and bears. The International Wolf Center, founded in 1985 and opened in Ely in 1993, keeps a small pack of wolves in a large enclosure for visitors to watch, and the North American Bear Center has resident black bears. The forests around Ely have one of the densest wolf populations in the lower forty-eight.`,`Ely's cold has produced its own heroes. In 1986 the Ely explorer Will Steger led a dogsled expedition that reached the North Pole without resupply, and in 1990 he led a team that crossed Antarctica by dogsled. The town now hosts winter festivals with snow sculptures, and dog-sled teams train on its frozen lakes, celebrating the cold that once made mining life so hard.`]},"cannon-beach-or":{why:`Cannon Beach sits on the outer Oregon coast where summer upwelling brings cold Pacific water against the sand, and a marine layer or sea breeze keeps afternoons cool. The Coast Range blocks Willamette Valley heat from the east. Winters are wet and near ice-free because the ocean is the thermostat. Orographic lift on the headlands wrings extra rain from Pacific storms. Haystack Rock is scenery; the mechanism is a cold current plus mountain wall plus latitude 46°, producing one of the most temperature-stable climates in the Lower 48.`,seasons:{winter:`Stormy, wet and dramatic — afternoons near 10°C, nights around 4°C, and Pacific storms that roll in one after another with sideways rain, gales over 100 km/h and surf exploding against Haystack Rock. Frost and snow are rare. Between storms the beach is empty and luminous, and storm watchers sit by fires in the inns to watch the waves.`,summer:`Cool, often foggy and gently sunny — afternoons near 20°C, nights around 11°C, and mornings that frequently start under a blanket of grey marine fog that may or may not burn off by afternoon. While the Willamette Valley bakes in heat, Cannon Beach stays mild. Puffins nest on Haystack Rock, tide pools teem with sea stars, and the beach fills with sandcastle builders and kites.`},history:[`In January 1806, word reached the Lewis and Clark expedition at Fort Clatsop, their winter camp near the mouth of the Columbia, that a whale had washed up on the beach to the south. William Clark set out with a small party to buy some of the blubber, and Sacagawea insisted on coming. She had travelled thousands of kilometres with the expedition, she said, and had not yet seen the great waters; now there was a monstrous fish to see as well, and she thought it very hard that she should not be allowed to see either. Clark took her. They crossed Tillamook Head, looked down on a view he called the grandest and most pleasing he had ever seen, and found the whale already stripped to the bones by the Tillamook people at the creek he named Ecola, after the Chinookan word for whale. He bought about 135 kg of blubber and oil.`,`The Tillamook and Clatsop peoples had lived along this coast for thousands of years, gathering shellfish, fishing salmon and harvesting the occasional stranded whale. In 1846 the US Navy schooner Shark was wrecked at the mouth of the Columbia, and part of its deck, with a small cannon attached, drifted south and washed ashore. Settlers found the cannon decades later, and the beach was named for it. In 1922 the town of Ecola changed its name to Cannon Beach because postal clerks kept confusing it with Eola, a town in the Willamette Valley. Getting there was a struggle: early visitors came by stagecoach or wagon on a muddy road with dozens of hairpin curves.`,`In 1913 Governor Oswald West declared Oregon's ocean beaches a public highway, a clever move that kept them open to everyone, and in 1967 the Oregon Beach Bill confirmed that the whole dry sand belongs to the public. In March 1964 a tsunami from the great Alaska earthquake swept into Cannon Beach and washed away its bridge; that summer, the town held a sandcastle contest to lift its spirits, and it has run ever since. Cannon Beach became an arts colony and resort, famous for Haystack Rock, a 72-m sea stack that featured in the opening of The Goonies. Today its biggest worry is the next tsunami — from the Cascadia fault just offshore.`],deepTitle:`Whale and cannon`,deep:[`The cannon that gave the town its name was found in 1898 near Arch Cape and is now displayed in the town's museum. In 2008 winter storms stripped the sand from the beach and exposed two more old cannons, which archaeologists confirmed came from the Shark. The ship had been surveying the Columbia when it hit the treacherous bar at the river's mouth, a hazard that has sunk hundreds of ships and earned the area the name Graveyard of the Pacific.`,`Out to sea off Tillamook Head stands Tillamook Rock Lighthouse, built on a tiny sea stack between 1879 and 1881 at great cost; a surveyor drowned trying to land on the rock. Keepers called it Terrible Tilly because waves crashed over the top of the lighthouse in winter storms, sometimes hurling rocks through the lantern windows. It was decommissioned in 1957 and for a time was used as a columbarium for storing ashes.`,`Geologists have discovered that the Cascadia subduction zone, a fault running along the coast from northern California to Vancouver Island, produces enormous earthquakes every few hundred years. The last one struck on the night of 26 January 1700, and its tsunami crossed the Pacific and flooded villages in Japan, where records allowed scientists to date it to the hour. Native stories of the coast also describe a great flood and shaking. Cannon Beach has tsunami evacuation routes on every street and has worked on building a city hall designed to survive the next one.`,`Haystack Rock and the other sea stacks are protected as part of the Oregon Islands National Wildlife Refuge, and volunteers patrol the tide pools at low tide to keep visitors from trampling the sea stars, anemones and chitons. Tufted puffins nest in burrows on the rock each spring and summer, and the town's strict building rules have kept the village small, low and shingled.`]},"valentine-ne":{why:`Stabilized dune topography is nationally rare and produces the strongest relief in the central Plains. Interdune blowouts trap cold air in winter and create sharp micro-scale thermal differences. The Ogallala Aquifer surfaces as springs and lakes, supporting wetland mosaics inside an otherwise dry grassland. Skies stay among the darkest in the Lower 48 because Cherry County is so thinly settled. Elevation near 775 m stretches the diurnal range. The inhabited wonder is topographic: a grass-anchored dune sea dissected by a spring canyon, not a generic High Plains grid.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot days, storm-filled evenings — afternoons near 31°C in July, nights cooling to about 16°C, and dramatic thunderstorms building over the grasslands in the late afternoon, sometimes with hail and spectacular lightning. The Niobrara is busy with canoes and inner tubes, the grass is green after the spring rains, and on clear nights the Milky Way blazes over one of the darkest skies in the country.`},history:[`In July 1906 the soldiers of the 25th Infantry, a Black regiment, were transferred from Fort Niobrara, a few kilometres east of Valentine, to Fort Brown in Brownsville, Texas. Within weeks, after a night of gunfire in Brownsville in which a white bartender was killed, the town blamed the Black soldiers. There was no real evidence, and their white officers testified that the men had been in their barracks, but President Theodore Roosevelt dishonourably discharged 167 of them without a trial. It was not until 1972 that the army reversed the discharges, after a congressman reopened the case; by then only one of the soldiers was still alive. The Brownsville Affair remains one of the great injustices in the history of the US Army — and it began in the quiet Sandhills of Nebraska.`,`The Niobrara valley was home to the Ponca, who built earth-lodge villages and grew corn along the river, and the surrounding grasslands were hunting grounds for the Lakota, Pawnee and others. The Sandhills themselves are a sea of grass-covered sand dunes, the largest in the Western Hemisphere, formed by wind-blown sand at the end of the last ice age and held in place by the prairie grasses. In 1880 the army built Fort Niobrara to watch over the Lakota at the nearby Rosebud reservation, and in 1882 the town of Valentine was laid out a few kilometres west, named for a Nebraska congressman. The railroad arrived in 1883, bringing cattle ranchers and homesteaders.`,`Most homesteaders soon discovered that 65 hectares of sandy grassland would not support a farm; in 1904 Congress passed the Kinkaid Act, allowing settlers in western Nebraska to claim 260 hectares, and the Sandhills became ranching country. Fort Niobrara closed in 1906, and in 1912 its land became a wildlife refuge with bison and elk. In 1991 Congress designated part of the Niobrara as a National Scenic River. Today Valentine is a ranching and tourist town, known for its canoeing, its dark skies and its name — every February, people from around the country send their valentines to be remailed with a heart-shaped postmark.`],deepTitle:`Dunes and soldiers`,deep:[`The Brownsville Affair grew out of a Texas town's resentment of Black soldiers. After the shooting, local officials produced spent cartridges they said came from army rifles, but investigators later suggested they had been planted. Roosevelt's decision, announced after the 1906 elections to avoid losing Black votes, caused outrage across the country, and Senator Joseph Foraker of Ohio fought the discharges for years. In 1972 the army cleared the soldiers' records, and the last surviving soldier, Dorsie Willis, received $25,000 in compensation.`,`The Sandhills hold one of the largest stores of fresh water in North America, the Ogallala Aquifer, which lies just below the dunes and rises to the surface in thousands of lakes and wetlands. The sand absorbs rain like a sponge, so few streams flood. That water makes the Sandhills a haven for migrating birds and allows ranchers to raise cattle on the grass; the debate over drilling more wells for irrigation is one of the most important in the region.`,`The Niobrara River valley is a biological crossroads where eastern deciduous forest, northern boreal forest and western pine forest meet, along with several types of prairie. Paper birch, a tree of the north woods, survives in shaded side canyons, a relic of the ice age. Smith Falls, the tallest waterfall in Nebraska, pours over a cliff into the river. Fossil beds along the river have yielded rhinoceroses, camels and horses that roamed the region millions of years ago.`,`Fort Niobrara National Wildlife Refuge began with a gift: in 1912 a Nebraska rancher donated six bison and a herd of elk to restock the land, at a time when bison were nearly extinct. Today the refuge's bison herd numbers several hundred, and visitors can watch them grazing on the hills above the river. Cattle ranching still dominates Cherry County, which is larger than the state of Connecticut and has fewer than six thousand people.`]}},r={"loess-hills-ia":{why:`Wind-laid silt this thick, this steeply dissected, and this linear exists in only two places globally. The result is a string of dry-prairie refugia in the middle of the rain-soaked Corn Belt, with micro-scale contrasts of several degrees between adjacent north and south slopes in summer. Catsteps and razor-backs rise about ninety metres above the Missouri floodplain. South crests dry enough for yucca; north coves keep oak-hickory. The climate is ordinary Upper Midwestern continental. The landform is not, and every land-use choice here is an argument with erosion after the next cloudburst.`,history:[`On 20 August 1804, three months into their journey up the Missouri, the men of the Lewis and Clark expedition buried one of their own on a high bluff above the river. Sergeant Charles Floyd, twenty-two, had fallen violently ill a few days earlier, probably with a burst appendix that no doctor in the world could then have treated. He was the only member of the Corps of Discovery to die on the whole two-year expedition. His grave on the bluff, now in Sioux City, is marked by a white stone obelisk 30 m tall, and in 1960 it became the very first registered National Historic Landmark in the United States. The bluff it stands on is made not of rock but of dust.`,`At the end of the last ice age, glaciers grinding across the northern plains ground rock into fine silt, which meltwater carried down the Missouri and dumped across its floodplain. When the floodplain dried, the prevailing winds picked the silt up and piled it along the eastern edge of the valley, layer upon layer, until it stood as much as 60 m thick. Streams then carved it into knife-edged ridges and steep, terraced slopes. Only in China's Loess Plateau does wind-blown silt form hills as tall and steep. The Ioway, Omaha and Oto farmed the valley and hunted the bluffs, and a thousand years ago people of the Glenwood culture built earth lodges in the southern hills.`,`The Mormon pioneers wintered across the river from Omaha in the 1840s on their way west, and some of their splinter groups stayed behind. In the 1850s a self-proclaimed prophet named Charles B. Thompson led his followers into a hollow north of the Soldier River, named their town Preparation, and persuaded them to sign over their property to him; in 1858 they revolted, and Thompson fled for his life. Further south, the town of Tabor became a station on the Underground Railroad and a base for the abolitionist John Brown. Farmers ploughed the flat floodplain but found the steep loess slopes useless for crops, so the hills stayed in prairie and pasture — which is why some of the largest remnants of native prairie in Iowa survive here today.`],deepTitle:`Hills of dust`,deep:[`Loess is a strange material. Its fine, angular grains lock together so firmly that it can stand in vertical cliffs, yet when it gets wet and loses its plant cover it slumps and washes away with alarming speed. Farmers who tried to plough the hills in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries watched gullies swallow their fields. The ridges' distinctive catsteps, rows of little terraces on the slopes, are formed by the loess slowly slipping downhill.`,`Charles B. Thompson called himself Baneemy, a title he said meant the mouth of God, and promised his followers that their sacrifices would prepare them for the coming end of the world. Many of them gave him their land, livestock and money. When they realised how little they were getting back, they confronted him, and he escaped; the courts later returned much of the property. The village of Preparation faded away, and its site is now part of the Loess Hills State Forest, where hikers walk through Preparation Canyon.`,`Tabor, a town founded by abolitionists from Ohio, sheltered people escaping slavery from Missouri on their way north and stored weapons for John Brown during the struggle over Kansas. Brown and his men drilled there in 1857, and the townspeople's support only cooled when he arrived with freed slaves and stolen horses after a raid in 1858. The John Todd House, a Congregational minister's home where Brown stayed, still stands.`,`The Loess Hills prairies shelter rare plants and animals found nowhere else in Iowa, including the prairie rattlesnake and the plains pocket mouse, and they serve as a corridor for migrating hawks and eagles each autumn. Without the fires that once swept the prairie, cedar trees have been invading the slopes, and land managers now use controlled burns to keep the hills open. The Loess Hills Scenic Byway winds for more than 300 km along the ridges, and in some places, the view across the Missouri valley stretches all the way to Nebraska.`]},"flint-hills-ks":{why:`The Flint Hills preserve the only remaining tallgrass prairie of landscape scale because the Permian limestone is shot through with chert that broke plows. That rock, fire, and grazing together kept the grass unplowed. Aspect-driven contrasts at fine scale sit on a 60–100 m topographic break. The March–April range-burning window produces some of the most intense managed-fire atmospheric signatures in North America. The climate is ordinary continental grassland. The reason the prairie survived here is the rock, and fire is still part of the year, not an interruption.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot, windy and storm-charged — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 20°C, and humid air that builds into towering thunderstorms in late afternoon, with lightning, hail and the occasional tornado sweeping across the open grass. The tallgrass grows shoulder-high by August, wildflowers bloom in waves, and cattle grow fat on the bluestem. Evenings are glorious when the wind drops and the storms pass.`},history:[`On the morning of 31 March 1931, a Fokker airliner carrying the most famous football coach in America, Knute Rockne of Notre Dame, took off from Kansas City bound for Los Angeles. Over the Flint Hills near the village of Bazaar, the wooden wing broke apart in the air, and the plane plunged into a pasture. All eight people on board were killed. The death of Rockne stunned the nation and set off an investigation that led to stricter aircraft inspections and the end of wooden wings in commercial aviation. A stone memorial now stands in the middle of the tallgrass where the plane came down, on private ranch land that visitors can reach only by arrangement with the family that owns it.`,`The Flint Hills got their name from the explorer Zebulon Pike, who crossed them in 1806 and complained of their rough flinty soil. The chert in the limestone is what saved them. The Kaw, Osage, Pawnee and Wichita had hunted bison here for centuries and made tools from the flint, but when settlers arrived with steel ploughs, they found the rocky soil impossible to break. Most of the tallgrass prairie that once covered the Great Plains from Texas to Canada was ploughed under; in the Flint Hills, it survived, and today the region holds most of the tallgrass prairie left on earth.`,`Council Grove, where a treaty with the Osage in 1825 opened the Santa Fe Trail, became the last major outfitting stop for wagon trains heading west, and the Kaw were moved onto a reservation there in 1847, before being removed again to Oklahoma in 1873. Cattlemen took over the grasslands, and the Flint Hills became one of America's great grazing regions, with ranchers buying Texas cattle to fatten on the summer grass. In 1996 Congress created the Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve around the historic Spring Hill Ranch, and since 2006 a symphony orchestra has played each June in the middle of the open prairie.`],deepTitle:`Fire and bluestem`,deep:[`The Flint Hills prairie depends on fire. For thousands of years, lightning and Native hunters set the grass alight, burning off the dead growth and keeping trees from invading. Ranchers continue the tradition each spring, setting controlled burns across hundreds of thousands of hectares; at night the lines of flame stretch across the hills, and the smoke can drift as far as Kansas City and Omaha. Within weeks, fresh green grass sprouts from the blackened ground.`,`Spring Hill Ranch was built in 1881 by Stephen Jones, a cattleman who had made his fortune in Texas and Colorado. Its three-storey limestone mansion, barn and stone fences, built by skilled stonemasons, showed off the wealth that could be made from grass. After decades of private ownership, the ranch was bought by a conservation trust and became the heart of the national preserve, which reintroduced bison in 2009.`,`Chase County, at the centre of the hills, has one of the oldest courthouses in Kansas, a French Second Empire building of 1873 still in use. In 1991 the writer William Least Heat-Moon published PrairyErth, a long, rambling portrait of Chase County built from walks across its grid of townships, and it made the county a literary landmark. Its population is still under three thousand.`,`The Konza Prairie Biological Station near Manhattan, managed by Kansas State University, has studied how fire, grazing and climate shape the tallgrass since 1971. Its researchers burn different patches on different schedules and track how grasses, flowers, bison and birds respond. Their findings have helped ranchers and conservationists manage the prairie, and as the climate warms, the region's long records are watched closely for changes in rain and grass.`]},"eureka-springs-ar":{why:`Few places in the humid eastern United States display Ozark karst relief at this density and verticality. Escarpment position, carbonate bedrock, and spring-line hydrology produce a town where climate changes meaningfully at the scale of a city block. Springs emerge near 13 °C; north-facing limestone slots stay cooler in summer; hollow bottoms pond cold air at night. About 90 m of relief in less than 500 m of horizontal distance is the civic geometry. The humidity is still Ozark. The streets are not a generic plateau grid.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot, humid and leafy — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 20°C, and thick green forests that close over the winding streets like a canopy. Afternoon thunderstorms pop up often, and the steep hollows hold the heat and moisture. Visitors cool off on Beaver Lake or float the Buffalo River, and the evenings bring fireflies and the sound of cicadas.`},history:[`In 1937 the Crescent Hotel, a grand limestone resort perched on the ridge above Eureka Springs, was bought by a man named Norman Baker. Baker was a former vaudeville performer and inventor who had made a fortune broadcasting on the radio and claimed to have found a cure for cancer. He turned the hotel into the Baker Hospital, painted it in garish colours, and invited desperate patients to come and be cured by his secret injections, which turned out to be a mixture of glycerine, alcohol and carbolic acid. In 1940 he was convicted of mail fraud and sent to prison. The hotel survived, and today it bills itself as one of the most haunted hotels in America, with ghost tours of the old hospital rooms.`,`Eureka Springs began with water. The Osage hunted these hills in the eighteenth century, and the springs that bubble out of the limestone hollows were well known to them. In the 1850s a doctor named Alvah Jackson claimed to have cured his son's eye ailment with water from a spring here. In 1879 a judge who believed the water had cured him spread the word, and within months thousands of people flocked to the hollows, camping in tents and shacks. The town was named Eureka Springs, and within a couple of years it was one of the largest towns in Arkansas. In 1883 a railroad arrived, and in 1886 the Crescent Hotel opened, built by a company headed by the former governor Powell Clayton.`,`Eureka Springs' streets wind up and down the steep hillsides, and many buildings have entrances on two levels, one from the street above and another from the street below. When the mineral-water craze faded in the early twentieth century, the town declined but was never torn down. Carry Nation, the hatchet-wielding temperance crusader, retired here in 1908 and lived in a house she called Hatchet Hall. In the 1960s new attractions arrived: a giant Christ of the Ozarks statue and a Passion Play. Artists and later a large LGBTQ community moved in, and in 1980 the architect E. Fay Jones built Thorncrown Chapel, a soaring glass-and-wood church in the woods outside town.`],deepTitle:`Springs and spirits`,deep:[`The springs of Eureka Springs, more than sixty of them, flow from cracks in the limestone. Like most mineral water, their curative powers were greatly exaggerated by promoters, but in the late nineteenth century spas were big business, and Eureka Springs competed with Hot Springs in southern Arkansas for visitors. Basin Spring, at the heart of town, was the first to draw crowds, and several springs have been restored with stone basins and gardens.`,`Norman Baker's radio station had already been shut down by federal authorities in Iowa when he moved to Arkansas. He was a showman who attacked the American Medical Association as a conspiracy and dressed in white suits and lavender shirts. His patients paid large sums for treatments that did nothing; many of them died. He served four years in prison and died in Florida in 1958.`,`Carry Nation spent the last years of her life in Eureka Springs after a career smashing saloons in Kansas with her hatchet. She bought a house, started a school, and continued to campaign against alcohol until she collapsed during a speech in Eureka Springs in 1911; she died a few months later. Her house still stands.`,`Thorncrown Chapel, designed by Fay Jones, a student of Frank Lloyd Wright, is one of the most admired buildings of twentieth-century America. It is made of local wood and more than 400 windows, and its frame of crossed timbers rises 15 m into the oak forest. In 2000 the American Institute of Architects ranked it fourth on its list of the top buildings of the century. Visitors from around the world come to sit in its pews and look out at the trees.`]},"brookings-or":{why:`Brookings is not merely a mild Oregon coast. It is the only town on this shoreline sitting at the seaward mouth of a deep, northeast–southwest river gap that can capture Great Basin northeasterlies and compress them into a localized foehn. Neighboring Gold Beach and Crescent City feel weaker cousins of the same pattern; Brookings gets the full channel. On ordinary marine-layer days the town is cool, wet, and fog-prone like the rest of the outer coast. When the Chetco Effect fires, frost can vanish in minutes and winter afternoons can run tens of degrees warmer than Astoria. That on-off switch, not the annual mean, is the climate that local lore and the forecast office both treat as the place’s signature.`,immersive:`A Brookings winter morning can smell like wet spruce and kelp, the Chetco sliding under a low gray lid, sweaters still honest at noon. Then the gap wind arrives. Northeasterly air dumps off the Siskiyous, strips the marine layer, and the same street that was dripping at breakfast can feel like April by midafternoon, frost gone from windshields while the rest of the Oregon coast stays clenched. Baseline climate is still hyper-maritime: more than two metres of rain, foggy Junes, summer highs near 22°C that rarely ask for more than a light layer. Storms and swell remain the serious weather. The Chetco Effect is the interruption that makes the town a forecast problem, not the climate that most days actually deliver.`,seasons:{winter:`Wet, wild and sometimes startlingly warm — afternoons near 13°C, nights around 6°C, and Pacific storms that dump most of the year's two metres of rain between November and March, with gales and big surf. Then, now and again, an east wind pours down the Chetco valley from the interior, warming as it sinks, and Brookings basks in a sunny 20°C-plus afternoon in January while the rest of the coast stays grey. Frost is rare and azaleas bloom early.`,summer:`Mild and breezy — afternoons near 21°C, nights around 12°C, and the north wind that blows along the coast keeping the air cool and fresh. Fog visits, but less often than further north or south, and rain almost disappears from June to September. Inland, a short drive up the Chetco, the canyon heats up for swimming holes and river picnics; in bad fire years, smoke drifts down from the Siskiyous.`},contrasts:[{label:`Crescent City, CA (≈40 km S)`,note:`Just across the state line, Crescent City is foggier and cooler in summer, without the Chetco valley's occasional warm downslope winds.`},{label:`Grants Pass (≈90 km NE, over the mountains)`,note:`In the inland Rogue Valley, summer afternoons run about 10°C hotter and the year brings well under half of Brookings's rain.`}],history:[`At dawn on 9 September 1942, a Japanese submarine surfaced off the southern Oregon coast and launched a small seaplane from a hangar on its deck. The pilot, Warrant Officer Nobuo Fujita, flew inland over Brookings and dropped two incendiary bombs on the forested slopes of Mount Emily, hoping to set the whole coast range ablaze. It was the first of the only two times an enemy aircraft has bombed the mainland United States. Fire lookouts spotted the smoke, and the rain-soaked forest refused to burn. Twenty years later, Fujita came back to Brookings as a guest of honour, and presented the town with his family's centuries-old samurai sword as a token of peace. When he died in 1997, some of his ashes were scattered at the bomb site.`,`The Chetco people, speakers of an Athabaskan language, had lived in villages along the lower Chetco and Winchuck rivers for thousands of years, fishing salmon and gathering shellfish at the river mouths. In 1853 settlers burned their main villages, and after the Rogue River Wars, most survivors were marched north to the Siletz Reservation in 1856. A small settlement called Chetco grew up at the mouth of the river, but the area remained one of the most isolated stretches of the Oregon coast, reachable only by boat or by rough trails.`,`In 1908 the lumberman John Brookings founded a company town to cut the Chetco valley's redwoods and Douglas fir, and the architect Bernard Maybeck drew up a plan for the town. A long wharf carried lumber to waiting ships, and until the coast highway arrived in the 1920s and 1930s, supply boats were the only lifeline; winter storms could delay them for days. The mill closed, the fishing fleet took over the harbour, and Brookings became a retirement and tourism town famous for its winter warmth. The 2002 Biscuit Fire burned some 2,000 square kilometres in the mountains behind it, and the 2011 Japanese tsunami wrecked its harbour.`],deepTitle:`Bombs and azaleas`,deep:[`Fujita's flight was part of a plan to start huge forest fires in the Pacific Northwest and spread panic. A second raid later that month also failed to start a major fire. The submarine I-25 went on to sink an American tanker off the Oregon coast before returning to Japan. Fujita survived the war and ran a hardware business. On his first visit to Brookings in 1962, some residents protested, but most welcomed him, and he later invited students from Brookings to visit Japan. A trail on Mount Emily leads to the site where his bombs fell.`,`The Chetco River valley holds the northernmost groves of coast redwood, the tallest trees on earth, which reach just a few kilometres into Oregon here. The redwoods, Douglas fir and Port Orford cedar of the valley made Brookings a lumber town, and the old-growth forests were largely cut in the twentieth century. Some groves are now protected in Alfred A. Loeb State Park and along the Redwood Nature Trail.`,`The Chetco Effect, also called the Brookings Effect, happens when high pressure over the interior sends dry east winds down the Chetco valley toward the sea. The air warms as it descends, so Brookings can be the warmest spot on the West Coast on a winter day, with temperatures sometimes approaching 27°C. The same effect makes the area mild enough for flowering plants such as azaleas, which grow wild in Azalea Park, and just across the California line farmers grow most of the Easter lily bulbs in North America.`,`The Biscuit Fire of 2002, sparked by lightning in the Siskiyou Mountains, became one of the largest fires in Oregon's history, and in 2017 the Chetco Bar Fire again threatened the town. On the coast, the 2011 tsunami caused by the earthquake off Japan surged into Brookings's harbour and destroyed docks and boats; the damage revealed ancient village sites near the river mouth, a reminder of the Chetco people who lived here long before the lumber town.`]},"medford-or":{why:`Medford’s distinctness is summer drought at 42°N plus a reliable irrigation network. The Coast Range and Klamaths intercept Pacific moisture, leaving the Rogue floor with sunny, arid summers that feel closer to the Sacramento Valley than to Portland. Bear Creek and Rogue diversions turn that dryness into orchard climate: high light, large diurnal swings, and enough winter chill for pears and grapes. Winter inversions then trap fog and smoke in the same bowl that ripens fruit in August. Nearby Jacksonville is cooler and hillier; the coast is another planet of fog and rain. The rain-shadow sanctuary is the mechanism. Irrigation is what made it a city.`,immersive:`July in Medford smells like hot oak leaves and irrigation water. Afternoons routinely climb through the low thirties, the sky hard and clear because the Klamaths have already wrung the Pacific dry. Nights drop hard enough that valley fog can pool by morning, a gray lid over Bear Creek until the sun burns it off. Winter is the inversion season: cold, sometimes smoky, with pears dormant under a still ceiling. Spring arrives as a white corridor of blossom along the laterals. About 550 mm of rain falls in a typical year, almost none of it in summer. The climate that fills fruit with sugar is the same climate that makes unirrigated lawns a short experiment.`,feel:`Medford feels like a slice of inland California tucked into southern Oregon. The Klamath and Siskiyou mountains block the Pacific's clouds, so summers in the Rogue Valley are long, hot and bone-dry — perfect for pears and wine grapes, less perfect when wildfire smoke settles in the bowl. Winters flip the script: the valley fills with cold, damp fog for days at a time while the surrounding hills sit in sunshine. Spring blossom and autumn harvest are the glorious in-between.`,seasons:{winter:`Cool and foggy — afternoons near 10°C, nights near freezing, and stretches when a lid of cold air traps fog over the valley floor for days at a time, while the hilltops and Mount Ashland bask in the sun above. Rain comes with Pacific storms, snow is uncommon on the valley floor but frequent in the mountains, and the orchards stand bare and quiet.`,spring:`Blossom time — afternoons warming from about 15°C in March to 24°C by late May, the pear orchards bursting into white bloom in April, and a pleasant mix of sun and showers. Frost on clear nights is the orchardists' great worry, and wind machines whir in the orchards to keep the cold air moving.`,summer:`Hot, dry and bright — afternoons near 33°C and often climbing past 38°C, nights cooling to around 13°C in the dry air, and almost no rain from June to September. Wildfire smoke from the surrounding mountains can fill the valley for days or weeks in bad fire years. The Rogue River offers rafting and fishing, and the Oregon Shakespeare Festival in nearby Ashland draws crowds.`,autumn:`Harvest season — afternoons near 28°C in September cooling to about 13°C by November, pears and wine grapes coming in from the orchards and vineyards, and the first rains of October washing the summer's smoke from the air. Oaks and vineyards turn gold, and the mountains get their first snow.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Crater Lake and the Rogue River, rafting and steelhead fishing, the pear orchards and wineries of the Rogue Valley, and the Shakespeare festival a short drive away in Ashland.`,contrasts:[{label:`Ashland (≈20 km SE, ≈150 m higher)`,note:`Up the valley at the foot of the Siskiyous, Ashland runs a little cooler in summer and wetter in winter, with a touch more snow.`},{label:`Crater Lake rim (≈90 km NE, ≈1,700 m higher)`,note:`Up in the Cascades, the rim of Crater Lake averages more than 10 m of snow a year and stays snowbound well into July.`}],history:[`On 11 October 1923, three brothers named DeAutremont jumped aboard a Southern Pacific train as it slowed at the Siskiyou Pass south of Medford, forced the engineer to stop at the mouth of Tunnel 13, and set off a dynamite charge to blow open the mail car, believing it held a fortune in gold. They used so much dynamite that the explosion killed the mail clerk and set the car on fire, destroying anything they might have stolen. In a panic, they shot the engineer, the fireman and a brakeman and fled into the forest with nothing. One of the largest manhunts in American history followed, with wanted posters printed in several languages and sent around the world. The brothers were finally caught in 1927, one of them serving in the army in the Philippines under a false name.`,`The Rogue Valley was home to the Takelma, Shasta and other peoples, who fished salmon in the river and gathered acorns in the oak savannas. In 1851 gold was found in a creek near what became Jacksonville, and miners flooded in. Clashes between miners and Native people grew into the Rogue River Wars of 1853 to 1856; after a massacre of a Takelma village by volunteers in 1855, the fighting turned savage, and by 1856 the survivors had been marched or shipped north to reservations. When the railroad arrived in 1883, it bypassed Jacksonville, and a new town called Medford was laid out on Bear Creek, named after Medford, Massachusetts.`,`Pears made Medford famous. Orchardists discovered that the valley's hot summers and cool nights produced fruit of extraordinary sweetness, and by the 1910s the Rogue Valley's pear orchards were selling around the world. During the Second World War, the army built Camp White just north of town to train some 40,000 soldiers. The orchards later gave way to suburbs and vineyards, and Medford became southern Oregon's commercial and medical centre. In September 2020 the Almeda Fire swept through the towns of Talent and Phoenix just south of Medford, destroying some 2,600 homes in a single afternoon.`],deepTitle:`Pears and robbers`,deep:[`The DeAutremont brothers — twins Ray and Roy, and their younger brother Hugh — had planned the robbery for months. They left behind a pair of overalls, which a pioneering crime scientist in Berkeley, Edward Heinrich, studied with a microscope; from fir pitch, a scrap of paper and the wear on the pockets, he deduced that the wearer was a left-handed lumberjack of a certain height and age. The description helped identify the brothers. All three were sentenced to life in prison. They were eventually released one by one, decades later; Hugh died of cancer within a year of his parole in 1958.`,`In 1934, in the depths of the Depression, two brothers who ran a pear orchard in Medford, Harry and David Rosenberg, could not sell their large, delicate Comice pears. They travelled to New York and San Francisco and personally presented boxes of their fruit to business executives, suggesting they send the pears to clients as Christmas gifts. It worked. The company they built, Harry & David, became one of the biggest mail-order fruit businesses in America, and its Fruit-of-the-Month Club began in 1938.`,`Jacksonville, the old gold-rush county seat a few kilometres west of Medford, lost its importance when the railroad passed it by. Because it was too poor to modernise, its brick and wooden buildings survived almost unchanged, and in 1966 the entire town became a National Historic Landmark. Each summer the Britt Festival holds concerts on a hillside in the old town.`,`In 1935 a teacher named Angus Bowmer staged two Shakespeare plays in a crumbling roofless theatre in Ashland, with boxing matches held in the afternoons to cover the costs. The Oregon Shakespeare Festival grew into one of the oldest and largest professional theatres in the United States, drawing hundreds of thousands of visitors to the valley each year.`]},"friday-harbor-wa":{why:`Friday Harbor sits in a double lee: Olympics to the south-southwest and Vancouver Island to the west, with the island itself shedding cloud in its wake. The result is a measurable drop in precipitation and overcast relative to Everett or Bellingham at similar latitude, while sea breezes still cap summer heat. Garry oak and rain-shadow prairie appear here; they do not appear on the wet west sound. Winters stay mild because the water never lets go. The place is not dry like eastern Washington. It is the maritime Northwest with a dimmer switch on gloom, which is a small difference on a map and a large one in a January week.`,immersive:`August afternoons at Friday Harbor often feel borrowed from a drier latitude: 22°C, a sea breeze off the strait, Garry oaks throwing hard shadows on yellowed grass. Winter is still the Northwest, just a shorter version, with about 650 mm of rain instead of Seattle’s heavier tally and fewer weeks of unbroken gray. Frost rarely bites the shoreline gardens. On the west side of the island, salt spray sculpts the evergreens; the east harbor, in the island’s wake, is kinder to apples and lavender. Orcas use the same channels the ferries do. The climate is cool-summer maritime with a rain-shadow edit, not a different seasonality, and that edit is why people pay island prices to stay.`,feel:`Friday Harbor feels like the Pacific Northwest with the clouds pulled back. Tucked in the rain shadow of the Olympic Mountains and Vancouver Island, San Juan Island gets far less rain than Seattle and plenty more sunshine, so its summers are bright, breezy and cool, with madrone trees and golden grass on the hills. Winters are grey and damp but mild, and on any day of the year the ferry horn, the harbour seals and the chance of a passing orca set the rhythm.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, grey and damp — afternoons near 8°C, nights just above freezing, and steady rain from Pacific storms, though less of it than on the mainland. Snow is rare and brief. Winter storms can churn the straits and disrupt the ferries, and the island quiets to its year-round residents.`,spring:`Soft and slowly brightening — afternoons warming from about 11°C in March to 17°C by late May, wildflowers carpeting the prairies and rocky bluffs, and rain showers giving way to longer sunny spells. Bald eagles nest, and the first orca sightings of the season bring whale-watchers to the west side.`,summer:`Cool, dry and luminous — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 12°C, and very little rain from June through September. Sea breezes keep the island comfortable even when the mainland swelters. Long evenings stretch past ten o'clock, kayakers paddle the shoreline, and the harbour fills with sailboats.`,autumn:`Mellow then stormy — September afternoons near 19°C with crisp, clear air, then the return of the rains by late October. The salmon runs bring orcas close to shore, the madrones shed their bark, and the first gales arrive with November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come by ferry for orca and whale watching, kayaking among the islands, the lavender farm and sculpture park, and the slow pace of an island town in the rain shadow of the Olympics.`,contrasts:[{label:`Seattle (≈100 km SE)`,note:`Outside the strongest part of the rain shadow, Seattle gets roughly half again as much rain, with more cloudy days and warmer summers.`},{label:`Victoria, BC (≈40 km W across Haro Strait)`,note:`Victoria sits in the same rain shadow, with a similar mild, dry-summer climate — the islands and the city share the sunny gap in the clouds.`}],history:[`On 15 June 1859, an American farmer named Lyman Cutlar looked out at his potato patch on San Juan Island and saw a pig rooting through it — again. He shot it. The pig belonged to the Hudson's Bay Company, and its manager demanded payment and threatened to have Cutlar arrested by British authorities. The trouble was that both Britain and the United States claimed the island. Within weeks, an American captain named George Pickett landed with a company of soldiers, and the British sent warships. By August, more than four hundred American soldiers with fourteen cannon faced five British warships carrying some two thousand men. The British admiral refused to start a war between two great nations over a pig. Instead, the two countries agreed to occupy the island jointly — for twelve years. The only casualty of the Pig War was the pig.`,`For thousands of years, the islands had been the home of Coast Salish peoples, including the Lummi, Songhees and others, who fished for salmon in the rich waters around the islands and gathered camas bulbs on the prairies. Smallpox devastated their communities in the late eighteenth century. In 1853 the Hudson's Bay Company set up a sheep farm on the island to strengthen British claims, and hired Hawaiian shepherds to run it. One of them, Peter Friday, was a Hawaiian whose name in his own language was Poalima; the harbour where he kept his sheep became Friday Harbor.`,`In 1872 Kaiser Wilhelm I of Germany, asked to settle the dispute, awarded the islands to the United States. Friday Harbor grew into a small port with a salmon cannery, and lime kilns on the island's north end burned limestone for cement. Ferries replaced the steamers, and summer homes, tourists and artists replaced farms. The island's resident orcas became world-famous, and the town now lives on tourism, marine research and a growing population of retirees and remote workers.`],deepTitle:`The Pig War`,deep:[`The Pig War was caused by an ambiguous line in the 1846 treaty that divided the Oregon Country between Britain and the United States. The treaty set the border along the middle of the channel separating Vancouver Island from the mainland, but there are two main channels, Haro Strait and Rosario Strait, with the San Juan Islands in between. Both sides claimed the islands. During the joint occupation, the British built a comfortable camp with gardens on the north end, and the Americans built theirs on the windswept south end; the two garrisons held sports days and attended each other's celebrations.`,`George Pickett, the American captain, later became a Confederate general, famous for leading the doomed charge at Gettysburg in 1863. The British commander who calmed the crisis, Rear Admiral Robert Baynes, ordered his captains not to fire on the Americans. Today American Camp and English Camp are preserved as San Juan Island National Historical Park, and the British flag still flies over English Camp, one of the few places in the United States where a foreign flag is raised daily by the National Park Service.`,`The University of Washington opened a marine laboratory in Friday Harbor in 1904, and it has become one of the most important marine research stations on the Pacific coast. The Whale Museum, which opened in the town in 1979, was the first museum in the United States devoted to a species living in the wild. Its researchers have followed the Southern Resident killer whales, whose numbers have fallen as salmon runs have declined.`,`On the island's north end, the lime works at Roche Harbor, run by John McMillin from the 1880s, became one of the largest lime operations on the West Coast. The company built kilns, a hotel and a village for its workers, many of them Japanese immigrants. The kilns closed in 1956, and Roche Harbor became a resort, but the old hotel, the chapel and the ruins of the kilns still stand by the water.`]},"winthrop-wa":{why:`Winthrop’s climate is the North Cascades rain shadow plus a north–south trench that traps whatever air falls into it. Storms spend their water on the crest; the Methow gets sun, dry snow, and only about 480 mm of precipitation a year. In winter, cold air drains to the valley floor and stagnates, so nights in town can undercut the benches. In summer the same lid holds wildfire smoke. Nearby western-slope towns drown in rain; the Columbia Plateau is windier and less alpine. The Methow is the pocket where mountain recreation and high-desert dryness occupy the same address, which is why the place feels so unlike Seattle at the same latitude.`,immersive:`A clear January in Winthrop can be bitterly still: valley-floor air pooled below the ponderosa benches, woodsmoke hanging in a line, the Methow steaming at the riffles. Snow here is dry, the kind Nordic skiers travel for, under a sky that goes truly dark once the highway over the crest is gated. July flips the ledger. Afternoons near 30°C, sagebrush scent, and a sun that would be coastal heresy. Then a plume from somewhere in the interior turns the peaks into silhouettes and the air into a throat problem. About 480 mm of water falls in a year. The rest of the weather is rain shadow, inversion, and fire, in that order.`,seasons:{winter:`Snowy, sunny and sharply cold — afternoons around freezing, nights near −6°C, and on clear, calm nights cold air pools on the valley floor and pushes the thermometer below −20°C. A deep, dry snowpack covers the valley from December to March, and the groomed Nordic trails, among the largest network in North America, fill with skiers. Highway 20 over the Cascades closes for the winter, turning the valley into a quiet cul-de-sac.`,summer:`Hot, dry and brilliant — afternoons near 30°C in July and August, nights cooling to about 12°C, and barely any rain from June to September. The Methow River runs cold and clear for floating, the North Cascades Highway reopens, and the night skies are full of stars. Wildfire is the season's great threat; smoke can fill the valley for weeks.`},contrasts:[{label:`Washington Pass (≈50 km W, ≈1,150 m higher)`,note:`Up on the North Cascades crest, snow piles so deep that the highway closes from November to spring, and summer afternoons run much cooler.`},{label:`The west slope at Marblemount (≈90 km W)`,note:`On the wet side of the Cascades, the forest gets roughly four times Winthrop's rain, with grey winters and few hard frosts.`}],history:[`In October 1939, a group of daredevils jumped out of an airplane over the Methow Valley to find out whether parachutists could reach forest fires faster than crews on foot. They practised dozens of jumps into the steep timber of the North Cascades, testing parachutes and padded suits; one of them, Francis Lufkin, a local Forest Service man, became a founder of the new profession. The experiment worked. The Forest Service began using smokejumpers on fires the following year, and the North Cascades Smokejumper Base near Winthrop still calls itself the birthplace of smokejumping.`,`The Methow people, Salish speakers, fished salmon in the Methow and Chewuch rivers, gathered roots and berries, and traded across the Cascades with the coastal peoples. After treaties in the 1850s and later executive orders, they were moved onto the Colville Reservation. Prospectors arrived in the 1880s and 1890s hoping for gold, and in the early 1890s a Harvard-educated trader named Guy Waring opened a store at the junction of the Methow and Chewuch rivers. The town was named after Theodore Winthrop, a writer who had travelled the region in the 1850s and been killed early in the Civil War. Waring's college friend Owen Wister visited on his honeymoon in 1898, and local legend holds that the valley helped inspire his novel The Virginian, the first great cowboy novel.`,`For most of the twentieth century Winthrop was a small ranching and logging village. When the state announced that the North Cascades Highway would finally open in 1972, local businessmen, backed by a wealthy couple named Kathryn and Otto Wagner, decided to rebuild the town's main street in the style of an Old West frontier town to attract the coming traffic. It worked, and Winthrop is now a popular year-round destination for hikers, cross-country skiers, mountain bikers and fishermen. Wildfires have become its great challenge: the Carlton Complex fire of 2014 burned more than 1,000 square kilometres in the valley, and in 2015 three firefighters died in the Twisp River fire.`],deepTitle:`Jumpers and cowboys`,deep:[`Smokejumpers revolutionised firefighting in the rugged forests of the West, where crews could take days to hike to a remote fire. The first jump onto a real fire was made in Idaho in 1940, and during the Second World War, conscientious objectors made up much of the force. The Winthrop base still trains jumpers who are dispatched across the West each summer, and visitors can tour it.`,`Guy Waring was a New Englander who came west to make his fortune and found the Methow a tough place to do it. His store, the Methow Trading Company, served miners, ranchers and Native people, and his log house, which he called the Castle, is now the heart of the Shafer Historical Museum. Waring's Duck Brand Saloon, built in 1891, now serves as Winthrop's town hall.`,`The North Cascades Highway, State Route 20, was the last major mountain highway built across the Cascades. Proposed in the 1890s, it took until 1972 to complete because the terrain was so difficult and the snow so heavy. The road crosses Washington Pass beneath the granite spire of Liberty Bell Mountain and closes each winter because of avalanches; its reopening each spring is a celebrated event in the valley.`,`The Methow Valley's cross-country ski trails, built and maintained by a local non-profit since the 1970s, grew into one of the largest groomed trail systems in North America. In summer the same trails are used by mountain bikers and runners. The valley's population has grown with remote workers and second-home owners, and debates over housing, water and fire protection now shape its future.`]},"leavenworth-wa":{why:`Leavenworth occupies the sharpest climate step on the Highway 2 crossing of the Cascades. West of the crest, forests drip; here, a few dozen kilometres later, summers are hot, skies are often clear, and annual precipitation drops to about 650 mm. Chinooks add a foehn that can erase snow in hours. The Wenatchee River supplies irrigation that the rain shadow withholds, which is why apples and pears still ring the town. Nearby Stevens Pass is a different planet of snow and cloud. The Bavarian overlay is recent. The east-slope rain-shadow hinge is the older, and truer, reason the place feels unlike western Washington.`,immersive:`Summer in Leavenworth can feel inland in a way the peaks deny: 31°C on Front Street, granite walls glaring, the Wenatchee sliding cold through town as if it had not noticed the heat. Winter returns the mountains to the story, snow on the false fronts, then a chinook that strips the streets to wet pavement overnight. Spring is orchard blossom on irrigated terraces. Autumn is larch on the high slopes and a crush of visitors for color and festivals. About 650 mm of precipitation falls in a year, much of it as snow, little of it in July. Wildfire smoke is the August asterisk. The climate is east-slope continental, not Bavarian, and it asserts itself the moment you leave the painted block.`,seasons:{winter:`Snowy and storybook — afternoons a few degrees above freezing, nights near −4°C and colder on still mornings, and snow piling deep on the steep roofs of the Bavarian-style buildings. The town strings up half a million Christmas lights, Stevens Pass skiers pass through, and east winds can sweep cold air through the valley from the interior. Now and then a chinook melts the snow almost overnight.`,spring:`Bright and quickly warming — afternoons rising from about 11°C in March to 21°C by late May, nights still near freezing early in the season, and snowmelt roaring down the Wenatchee River and Icicle Creek. The orchards down the valley blossom in April, balsamroot paints the hillsides yellow, and the town celebrates with its Maifest in May.`,summer:`Hot, dry and sunny — afternoons near 30°C, nights cooling to about 13°C, and almost no rain from late June to September. The Wenatchee River offers rafting and tubing, the trails to the Enchantments fill with hikers, and wildfire smoke is a common late-summer visitor.`,autumn:`Crisp, bright and festive — September afternoons near 24°C cooling to about 8°C by November, apples and pears ripening in the orchards, larches turning gold in the high country, and Oktoberfest filling the streets with lederhosen and brass bands. The first snow usually arrives on the mountains by October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Bavarian-style village and its Oktoberfest and Christmas lights, rafting the Wenatchee River, climbing in Icicle Canyon, and hiking toward the Enchantments, one of the most beautiful alpine basins in the Cascades.`,residentFit:`It suits skiers, climbers, orchardists and families who want hot, dry summers and snowy winters in a mountain town, and who can live with tourist crowds, high housing prices and wildfire smoke in late summer.`,contrasts:[{label:`Stevens Pass (≈40 km W, ≈900 m higher)`,note:`Up on the Cascade crest, the ski area averages more than 10 m of snow a year, and Pacific storms hit with full force.`},{label:`Wenatchee (≈35 km SE, ≈150 m lower)`,note:`Out where the valley opens onto the Columbia, Wenatchee gets barely a third of Leavenworth's precipitation, and its summers run a little hotter.`}],history:[`In 1962 Leavenworth was a dying town. Its sawmill had closed, the railroad had moved away decades before, and its main street was lined with empty storefronts. A committee of local businesspeople, working with the University of Washington, came up with an unusual idea: they would turn the town into a Bavarian village. The inspiration was partly the alpine scenery and partly the success of Solvang, a Danish-themed town in California. One by one, the buildings on Front Street were given steep roofs, painted wooden balconies and murals. The idea worked spectacularly. Today Leavenworth attracts millions of visitors a year for its Christmas lights, Oktoberfest and German food — even the fast-food restaurants must follow the Bavarian design rules.`,`The Wenatchi people had lived at the meeting of Icicle Creek and the Wenatchee River for centuries, catching salmon and gathering camas. An 1855 treaty promised them a fishing reservation here that the government never created, and most were moved to the Colville and Yakama reservations. In 1892 the Great Northern Railway built its line over Stevens Pass, and Leavenworth became a division headquarters, a busy town of railroad workers and sawmill hands. Orchards spread across the valley, irrigated from the river.`,`In 1922 the railroad moved its division point to Wenatchee and rerouted the line, and in 1929 a new 12.5-km Cascade Tunnel, then the longest railroad tunnel in North America, bypassed the old route. The mill closed, and Leavenworth fell into decline. In the 1930s a ski jump on the hills above town drew crowds to championship competitions, but the town's real revival came only with the Bavarian makeover of the 1960s. Behind the costumes, Leavenworth is a real east-slope town of orchards, snowy winters and hot, dry summers.`],deepTitle:`Instant Bavaria`,deep:[`On 1 March 1910, an avalanche swept two snowbound passenger trains off the tracks at Wellington, just west of Stevens Pass, and down into the canyon below. The trains had been stranded for days by a blizzard, and 96 people died — the deadliest avalanche in American history. The Great Northern renamed Wellington as Tye to escape the bad publicity and later built snowsheds and the new tunnel to avoid the dangerous slopes. The ruins can be reached by trail.`,`The Leavenworth Winter Sports Club built a ski jump in the hills above town in 1929, and through the 1930s to the 1960s, it hosted national championships. Norwegian immigrants brought jumping skills to the town, and some jumps set North American distance records. The hill closed in 1978, but the club still runs cross-country and alpine skiing near town.`,`The Enchantments, a basin of granite peaks, larches and turquoise lakes in the Alpine Lakes Wilderness above Leavenworth, are among the most coveted backcountry destinations in the country. Overnight permits are allotted by lottery because demand is so high. The larches, conifers that turn gold and drop their needles each autumn, draw crowds of hikers every October.`,`Leavenworth's Bavarian theme has been both a triumph and a source of tension. The town's population is only about two thousand, but on festival weekends tens of thousands of visitors pack its streets, and housing has become scarce and expensive for workers. Wildfires in the surrounding forests, and the smoke they bring, have become a growing threat to the summer and autumn season.`]},"lone-pine-ca":{why:`Lone Pine’s climate is extreme rain shadow plus a basin deep enough to invert. The Sierra wrings Pacific storms; the valley floor gets about 255 mm of precipitation, howling gap winds, and summer heat near 37°C while Whitney’s summit holds snow. Cold air pools in winter. Dust from the desiccated Owens Lake and smoke from Sierra fires ride the same trapped air. Neighboring west-slope Sequoia country is a rainforest by comparison. The Alabama Hills sit on the same fault that broke in 1872. Nowhere else in the Lower 48 stacks a 4,000-metre wall, a saltbush floor, and a city aqueduct in one view.`,immersive:`Morning in Lone Pine often starts with wind already working the saltbush and a wall of granite so close it distorts scale. Mount Whitney hangs nine thousand feet above the Alabama Hills, which themselves are only the tips of a buried scarp. Summer afternoons on the floor push past 37°C; a short drive to Whitney Portal drops the temperature as if someone opened a cellar door. Winter nights pool cold in the basin while the peaks stay brighter. About 255 mm of rain and snow fall in a year. Dust from Owens Lake and smoke from somewhere over the crest are the haze that makes sunsets red and lungs cautious. This is desert in the hydraulic shadow of the highest Sierra.`,feel:`Lone Pine feels like the front row of the most dramatic stage in America. The Sierra Nevada rises almost 3,300 m straight up from the town's back yard to the summit of Mount Whitney, catching the Pacific's storms and leaving the Owens Valley sunny, windy and dry. Summers are desert-hot, winters brisk and brilliant, and the light on the granite at dawn turns the whole escarpment pink. Across the valley, the dried bed of Owens Lake is a reminder of where the water went.`,seasons:{winter:`Crisp, sunny and windy — afternoons near 14°C, nights just above freezing, and the Sierra crest above town buried in snow. Strong winds can whip through the valley ahead of storms, and occasionally a light snow dusts the Alabama Hills. Most days are cloudless, and the snowy peaks glow pink at sunrise.`,spring:`Warm and gusty — afternoons climbing from about 19°C in March to 28°C by late May, the desert blooming with wildflowers in good years, and strong winds that can raise great clouds of dust from the dry bed of Owens Lake. Snow still caps the high peaks, and climbers start their attempts on Mount Whitney.`,summer:`Hot, dry and blazing — afternoons near 36°C, nights cooling to about 19°C, and very little rain, though thunderstorms can build over the Sierra and the White Mountains on monsoon afternoons. Hikers flock to Whitney Portal, 2,500 m up, to escape the heat and begin the climb to the highest summit in the lower forty-eight.`,autumn:`Warm, clear and golden — September afternoons near 32°C easing to about 18°C by November, cool nights and very little rain. Aspens turn gold in the canyons below the Sierra crest in October, the air is still and clear, and it is one of the best seasons for climbing Mount Whitney before the first snows close the high trails.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Alabama Hills, where hundreds of westerns were filmed, the trailhead to Mount Whitney, the highest peak in the lower forty-eight, and the Manzanar National Historic Site just up the road.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mount Whitney summit (≈20 km W, ≈3,300 m higher)`,note:`The highest peak in the lower forty-eight is an arctic world above town, with freezing nights even in July and snow most of the year.`},{label:`Death Valley (≈100 km E, ≈1,200 m lower)`,note:`Over the Inyo Mountains and down in the sub-sea-level basin, summer afternoons run about 10°C hotter.`}],history:[`At 2:30 in the morning on 26 March 1872, one of the largest earthquakes in California's history tore along the base of the Sierra Nevada and dropped the Owens Valley floor several metres in seconds. Lone Pine's adobe buildings collapsed, and 27 of the town's roughly 250 people were killed; many of them were buried together in a mass grave that can still be seen north of town. In Yosemite Valley, more than 150 km away, the naturalist John Muir was thrown from his bed in his cabin, ran outside and shouted, by his own account, A noble earthquake! The fault scarp it left is still visible along the edge of the Alabama Hills.`,`The Owens Valley Paiute, who call themselves Nüümü and their land Payahuunadü, the land of flowing water, had diverted streams from the Sierra for centuries to irrigate fields of wild seed plants and roots. Settlers came in the 1860s, using the Paiute ditches for their own ranches, and conflict led to the army marching about a thousand Paiute out of the valley in 1863. Many later returned. In 1873, a year after the earthquake, three fishermen from Lone Pine made the first recorded ascent of the highest mountain in the United States, which surveyors had named for the geologist Josiah Whitney; the fishermen, unimpressed, called it Fisherman's Peak.`,`In the early 1900s Los Angeles quietly bought up land and water rights in the Owens Valley and built an aqueduct to carry the water 375 km south; when it opened in 1913 its engineer, William Mulholland, told the crowd: There it is. Take it. Owens Lake dried up within a decade, and in 1924 angry ranchers seized the aqueduct's spillway near Lone Pine and released the water back into the valley. Los Angeles won. During the Second World War, more than ten thousand Japanese Americans were held at the Manzanar camp a few kilometres north. Meanwhile Hollywood had discovered the Alabama Hills, and since 1920 hundreds of films have been shot among their rounded boulders.`],deepTitle:`Quake, water, film`,deep:[`The 1872 earthquake was so powerful that it was felt across most of California and Nevada. Along the fault, the ground dropped and shifted sideways by several metres, and a fence near Lone Pine was offset by about 5 m. The same fault system has lifted the Sierra Nevada and dropped the Owens Valley over millions of years, which is why the escarpment above Lone Pine is the steepest and tallest mountain front in the lower forty-eight.`,`The California water wars were among the bitterest conflicts in western history. Los Angeles agents posed as ranchers to buy water rights in the valley, and once the aqueduct was built, the city pumped more and more groundwater. Ranchers dynamited the aqueduct repeatedly in the 1920s. Owens Lake, once a large salt lake that supported steamboats, became one of the biggest sources of dust pollution in the country; since the 2000s, Los Angeles has spent billions spreading water and gravel over the lake bed to control the dust.`,`The Manzanar War Relocation Center held Japanese Americans from 1942 to 1945 in rows of tarpaper barracks under the Sierra. The photographer Ansel Adams documented life in the camp, and the site is now a national historic site with a reconstructed mess hall and barracks. A white memorial obelisk, built by internees in 1943, stands in the camp cemetery and is the site of an annual pilgrimage.`,`The Alabama Hills, named by Confederate sympathisers after a Confederate warship, became Hollywood's favourite backdrop for westerns. Gunga Din, How the West Was Won and countless Hopalong Cassidy films were shot here, as were scenes from Tremors, Iron Man and Django Unchained. The Museum of Western Film History in Lone Pine tells the story, and each autumn a film festival brings back fans of the old westerns.`]},"page-az":{why:`Page sits on a mesa above Glen Canyon where monsoon storms meet almost bare Navajo sandstone. Rain that would soak into soil elsewhere becomes instant runoff, so flash floods and ephemeral waterfalls are the hydrologic signature. Elevation near 1,310 m keeps winters milder than the high Rockies and summers slightly less brutal than Phoenix, though afternoons still near 37°C. Lake Powell moistens the immediate shoreline without breaking the BSk arid core; annual precipitation is only about 170 mm. Neighboring Lees Ferry and Kayenta share the desert but not the lake. The combination of monsoon-on-slickrock and a mid-century reservoir is why Page does not read as a generic Arizona plateau town.`,immersive:`A July afternoon in Page can feel like a kiln until a monsoon cell builds over the Navajo sandstone and the light goes purple. Rain hits slickrock that stores almost nothing; waterfalls appear on cliffs that were dry an hour earlier, and every slot canyon becomes a serious place. Then the storm walks off and the air smells like wet creosote and hot stone. Winter days are the quiet season: bright, spare, lake water hard blue in the side canyons. Summer highs near 37°C, winter nights near −2°C, and only about 170 mm of precipitation in a year. The lake adds a thin humidity at the marinas. A kilometre inland, the desert takes the weather back.`,feel:`Page feels like a frontier outpost perched on the rim of a drowned canyon. The town sits on a sandstone mesa above the deep blue of Lake Powell, surrounded by slickrock, slot canyons and the endless red desert of the Navajo Nation. The air is dry and the sun relentless; summer days are fierce and the monsoon brings explosive afternoon storms, while spring and autumn are glorious. Everything here — the town, the lake, the jobs — exists because a concrete dam was poured into Glen Canyon in the 1960s.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, clear and quiet — afternoons near 9°C, nights a couple of degrees below freezing, and bright sunshine on most days. A storm can dust the red rock with snow that vanishes by midday. Tourist crowds thin out, Lake Powell is still and cold, and the low winter light makes the canyons glow.`,spring:`Windy and warming — afternoons rising from about 16°C in March to 30°C by late May, with strong spring winds that can whip up sand and dust. The houseboats return to the lake, slot canyon tours fill up, and occasional cold fronts can still bring a chilly day.`,summer:`Hot, dry and then stormy — afternoons near 37°C, nights around 21°C, and from July the monsoon pushes moist air from the south, building towering thunderstorms over the desert by afternoon. Flash floods can rip through the slot canyons with little warning, even from storms many kilometres away, and canyon tours close when storms threaten. The lake is warm and full of boaters.`,autumn:`The best season — September afternoons near 31°C easing to 14°C by November, cool and clear nights, calm air and warm lake water through early autumn. The crowds thin, the light softens, and occasional rains from late monsoon storms or Pacific systems freshen the desert.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the sculpted slot canyons of Antelope Canyon, the bend of the Colorado at Horseshoe Bend, boating on Lake Powell and rafting from Glen Canyon Dam, all within a few kilometres of town.`,residentFit:`It suits river guides, tour operators, Navajo Nation families and remote workers who love canyon country and can cope with fierce summer heat, a falling Lake Powell and long drives to any large city.`,contrasts:[{label:`Lees Ferry (≈25 km SW, ≈350 m lower)`,note:`Down on the river below the dam, at the head of the Grand Canyon, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter and the cold, clear river water is a shock.`},{label:`Grand Canyon North Rim (≈130 km SW, ≈1,200 m higher)`,note:`Up on the Kaibab Plateau, the North Rim is buried in snow from November to May and its summer afternoons run about 12°C cooler.`}],history:[`On the spring equinox of 1981, a small group of environmental activists from a new organisation called Earth First! walked onto the crest of Glen Canyon Dam with a long roll of black plastic. They unrolled it down the face of the dam, a sheet nearly 100 m long, so that from below it looked as if a huge crack had split the concrete. The stunt was inspired by Edward Abbey, whose novel The Monkey Wrench Gang, published in 1975, told the story of a band of eco-saboteurs whose dearest dream was to blow up the dam and set the Colorado River free. Abbey himself was there that day and gave a speech. The dam still stands, but the argument over whether it should has never quite gone away.`,`Glen Canyon was named by the explorer John Wesley Powell, who floated through it in 1869 and found it a place of quiet beauty, with fern-hung alcoves and sandstone walls. It had long been the country of the Navajo and Southern Paiute, who travelled, grazed sheep and held ceremonies among its canyons; Rainbow Bridge, a great natural stone arch, remains sacred to the Navajo. In 1956 Congress approved a dam to store the Colorado's water, and the government acquired a mesa top from the Navajo Nation in exchange for land in Utah to build a town for the construction workers. It was named Page, after a former commissioner of the Bureau of Reclamation.`,`Construction began in 1956, and by 1966 the dam was complete. Lake Powell slowly filled behind it, drowning nearly 300 km of canyon, along with hundreds of archaeological sites. The lake became one of the most popular boating destinations in America, and Page grew into a resort town. In the 1990s Antelope Canyon, a sculpted slot canyon on Navajo land just outside town, became world-famous through photographs, and today it draws visitors from around the world. A long drought in the West has dropped Lake Powell to record lows, exposing canyons that had been underwater for half a century.`],deepTitle:`Damming Glen Canyon`,deep:[`The construction of Glen Canyon Dam was a huge engineering project. Workers drilled diversion tunnels through the canyon walls to reroute the river, then poured more than 3.7 million cubic metres of concrete. Building the bridge across the canyon, so workers and equipment could reach both sides, was itself a feat. Many Navajo men worked on the dam, and the town of Page grew from a temporary camp into a permanent town that incorporated in 1975.`,`For environmentalists, the loss of Glen Canyon became a symbol of what could be destroyed in the name of progress. The Sierra Club's leader, David Brower, had agreed not to oppose the dam in exchange for stopping other dams in Dinosaur National Monument, and he called it the greatest mistake of his life. Brower's regret and Abbey's novel helped inspire a generation of activists who fought to protect wild rivers across the West.`,`Antelope Canyon was formed by flash floods carving through the sandstone over thousands of years, creating narrow, twisting passageways with smooth, flowing walls. Sunbeams shining through openings above create the light shafts that photographers prize. The canyon's beauty hides its danger: in August 1997 a sudden flash flood swept through Lower Antelope Canyon and killed eleven tourists. Visitors must now go with Navajo guides, who watch the skies closely.`,`For more than forty years, the coal-fired Navajo Generating Station near Page supplied power to pump Colorado River water to Phoenix and Tucson and provided hundreds of jobs for the Navajo Nation. It closed in 2019, and its three smokestacks were demolished in 2020. Today the Navajo Nation is developing solar power, and Page's economy rests on tourism and on a lake whose future depends on snow in the Rocky Mountains.`]},"international-falls-mn":{why:`International Falls sits where Arctic air masses arrive unimpeded at a river-and-lake outlet on the Canadian border. The Rainy system organizes persistent cold pools in calm winter highs, so the town’s mean annual temperature ranks among the lowest of any U.S. first-order station. Lakes shave the worst extremes only slightly and add some lake-effect snow. Summer is short and actually mild, which is the surprise the nickname erases. Nearby Duluth is lake-moderated; nearby interior muskeg is even harsher. The Icebox label is marketing built on a real mechanism: northern-interior continentality plus a lowland that collects cold air and holds it.`,immersive:`January in International Falls is a study in dry cold. Breath loudens, the Rainy River becomes a road, and nights in the normals near −20°C feel like a floor, not a novelty. Steam from the mill hangs in a line over town. Then May arrives all at once: long evening light, blackflies, water opening, afternoons that can reach the middle twenties. Summer is brief enough to be cherished and warm enough to garden in a hurry. About 700 mm of precipitation falls across the year, much of the winter portion as snow that the lakes help wring. The Icebox nickname is earned. The pleasant July is real too, and both belong to the same continental interior.`,feel:`International Falls feels like a border town at the edge of the boreal north, where winter is not just a season but an identity. On still January nights, cold air settles into the Rainy River lowland and the thermometer plunges toward −40°C; steam from the paper mill rises in white columns, and the snow squeaks. Then summer arrives — short, green, and full of long northern evenings on the lakes of Voyageurs National Park — and the town that calls itself the Icebox of the Nation turns surprisingly pleasant.`,seasons:{winter:`Bitter and long — afternoons near −7°C, nights near −18°C, and arctic air masses that can push the thermometer below −40°C for several nights in a row. Snow covers the ground from November to April, the lakes freeze thick enough for ice roads and fishing houses, and automakers test their cars and parts in the cold. Clear, frozen nights often bring the northern lights.`,spring:`Late and grudging — afternoons climbing from about 0°C in March to near 18°C by late May, with snowstorms possible into April and ice lingering on the lakes until early May. Then the ice goes out in a rush, the birch and aspen leaf out, and the fishing season opens.`,summer:`Short, green and pleasant — afternoons near 25°C, nights cooling to about 12°C, and long northern evenings when the light lasts past ten o'clock. Thunderstorms are common, and so are mosquitoes. Rainy Lake and the waters of Voyageurs National Park fill with boaters and anglers chasing walleye.`,autumn:`Brief and colourful — September afternoons near 18°C with crisp, clear air, aspens and birches turning gold, and the first frosts by mid-month. By late October the snow begins, and the lakes freeze in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Voyageurs National Park, boating and fishing on Rainy Lake in summer, ice fishing, snowmobiling and the bragging rights of a winter in the self-styled Icebox of the Nation.`,contrasts:[{label:`Duluth (≈230 km SE)`,note:`On Lake Superior's shore, Duluth's winters are a few degrees milder, though the lake brings more cloud and lake-effect snow.`},{label:`Minneapolis (≈400 km S)`,note:`In the south of the state, January runs about 6°C warmer on average, and summers are notably hotter and more humid.`}],history:[`International Falls has spent decades arguing over who owns the cold. For generations the town has called itself the Icebox of the Nation, but so did Fraser, Colorado, a mountain town that often recorded even lower temperatures. The two towns traded claims, letters and legal threats for years: International Falls once paid Fraser to give up the title, then let its own registration lapse, and Fraser tried to take it back. In 2008 International Falls finally secured the federal trademark for itself. Today the town's water tower, its Icebox Days winter festival — complete with frozen turkey bowling — and its thermometer-shaped sign all celebrate a climate that most places would rather hide.`,`The Ojibwe, who call the place Koochiching, lived along the lakes and rivers of the border country, travelling by canoe and harvesting wild rice. In the 1600s French voyageurs began paddling the border lakes, which formed a great water highway for the fur trade between Montreal and the far northwest; in 1731 the explorer La Vérendrye's men built a fort on Rainy Lake. The falls where Rainy Lake drains into the Rainy River threw up a mist that gave the lake and the town their names. A short-lived gold rush to Rainy Lake in the 1890s brought prospectors, and the town grew up around the falls.`,`In the early 1900s a lumber baron named Edward Backus saw the power in the falls and built a dam across them, drowning the waterfall and using its power to run a giant paper mill on the American side and another across the river in Fort Frances, Ontario. Backus wanted to dam the whole chain of border lakes, but a conservationist named Ernest Oberholtzer led a long fight against him, helping to protect the wilderness that later became the Boundary Waters and Voyageurs National Park. The paper mill still dominates the town's economy and its skyline, and the bridge to Canada keeps it busy with border traffic.`],deepTitle:`Icebox of the Nation`,deep:[`The cold of International Falls comes from its location in the heart of the continent, far from any ocean, and from its low-lying river valley, where cold air pools on calm nights. The town's average temperature is among the lowest of any major weather station in the lower forty-eight. The extreme cold has made it a testing ground for car companies, which bring prototypes north each winter to see how batteries, engines and plastics cope.`,`Ernest Oberholtzer spent much of his life on a small island in Rainy Lake, where he lived among books and Ojibwe friends. In the 1920s he became the leading voice against Backus's plan to raise the levels of the border lakes, testifying before Congress and the International Joint Commission. The fight led to the Shipstead-Nolan Act of 1930, which protected the shorelines of the border lakes, and to later wilderness protections. His island home, Mallard Island, is now a retreat for writers and artists.`,`Voyageurs National Park, established in 1975 just east of International Falls, protects the lakes and islands of the old fur-trade route. It is a park best explored by boat in summer and by ski, snowshoe or snowmobile in winter, when ice roads are ploughed across the frozen lakes. The park's name honours the French-Canadian canoemen who paddled these waters with loads of furs and trade goods.`,`International Falls is also the hometown of Bronko Nagurski, one of the strongest and most feared football players in history, who starred for the University of Minnesota and the Chicago Bears in the 1930s. A museum in the town honours him. The town's giant statue of Smokey Bear, erected in 1954, reflects its long ties to forestry and fire prevention.`]},"duluth-mn":{why:`Duluth’s summer cooling is one of the strongest lake-driven anomalies in the interior United States. Lake Superior’s thermal inertia at 46°N keeps July afternoons near 24°C while Minneapolis swelters, and it delays spring with fog and lingering ice. In winter the same water, while open, feeds lake-effect snow onto the South Shore; once iced, the moderating effect weakens and cold snaps harden. Inland, even a short drive off the water, summers warm and nights run colder. The cliff-top city is not a generic Dfb town. It is a continental climate with a cold sea glued to one side, and that glue is the whole local weather.`,feel:`Duluth feels like a seaport stranded in the middle of a continent. The city climbs a steep, rocky hillside above the western tip of Lake Superior, the largest freshwater lake in the world by area, and its harbour is busy with ocean-going freighters loading iron ore and grain beneath a famous lift bridge. The lake shapes everything: it chills and fogs the waterfront in spring and summer and feeds heavy winter snow, so the hilltops can feel like a different climate from the harbour. Winters are long and fierce, summers short and refreshingly cool.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, snowy and lake-chilled — afternoons near −4°C, nights near −13°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −30°C, while the steep streets climbing the hillside become ski runs for the unprepared. Superior steams in the frigid air, ice builds along the shore, and lake-effect snow falls along the North Shore. The ships stop running from January to March.`,spring:`Slow and lake-cooled — afternoons warming from about 2°C in March to 17°C by late May, but an east wind off the still-icy lake can drop the temperature on the waterfront 10°C below the hills in a matter of minutes. Fog rolls in off the lake, and the first freighters of the season arrive when the ice breaks up in late March.`,summer:`Mild and refreshing — afternoons near 25°C up the hill but often cooler by the lake, nights around 13°C, and a lake breeze that makes Duluth an escape for people fleeing the heat of the Twin Cities. Freighters sail under the Aerial Lift Bridge, the Lakewalk fills with walkers, and Superior's cold water tempts only the boldest swimmers.`,autumn:`Crisp, colourful and stormy — September afternoons near 21°C cooling to about 5°C by November, the maples and birches on the hills blazing red and gold by early October, and the gales of November sweeping across the lake, building huge waves that crash over the piers. The first lasting snow usually arrives by late November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the lift bridge and the giant ore boats, the lakewalk and Canal Park, the North Shore drive to waterfalls and lighthouses, tall-ship festivals in summer and blazing fall colour along the hills.`,residentFit:`It suits outdoor people, remote workers and families who want a real city with trails and lake views, cool summers free of heat waves, and who embrace long, snowy winters rather than enduring them.`,history:[`On 27 January 1871, a congressman from Kentucky named J. Proctor Knott stood up in the House of Representatives to speak against a bill granting land to a railroad that would run to Duluth, then a raw frontier town of a few thousand people on the western tip of Lake Superior. His speech was a masterpiece of mockery. He described how he had searched for Duluth for years without finding it on any map, praised the untold delights of the zenith city of the unsalted seas, and suggested that it must be the centre of the universe. The speech was reprinted across the country, and Duluth, instead of being insulted, adopted the phrase Zenith City as a proud nickname and has used it ever since.`,`The Ojibwe called the place Onigamiising, the place of the little portage, where travellers could cross the long sand spit of Minnesota Point from the lake into the St. Louis River. The French explorer Daniel Greysolon, Sieur du Lhut, reached the area in 1679, and the city later took his name. In the 1854 Treaty of La Pointe the Ojibwe ceded the land, and the Fond du Lac Band kept a reservation upriver. Duluth boomed in the late 1860s when the financier Jay Cooke made it the terminus of his railroad, then went bust when his bank failed in the Panic of 1873. In 1871 the town dug a canal through Minnesota Point to create a harbour, by local legend racing to finish before an injunction from its jealous rival, Superior, Wisconsin, could arrive.`,`Iron ore saved Duluth. In the 1890s the Mesabi Range north of the city began shipping iron by rail to Duluth's giant docks, and from there by ship to the steel mills of the lower Great Lakes. Grain, lumber and coal followed, and by the early twentieth century Duluth had more millionaires per person than almost any city in America. In 1905 the city built its famous Aerial Bridge across the canal. A darker chapter came in 1920, when a mob lynched three young Black circus workers falsely accused of rape; the city unveiled a memorial to them in 2003. Bob Dylan was born in Duluth in 1941. Today the city lives on shipping, health care, universities and tourism.`],deepTitle:`Zenith City`,deep:[`The Mesabi iron deposits were discovered by the Merritt brothers, seven sons of a Duluth pioneer family, who found ore so rich and close to the surface that it could be scooped out with steam shovels. They borrowed heavily to build a railroad to Duluth, but in the Panic of 1893 they lost control of their mines to John D. Rockefeller, who later sold them to U.S. Steel. The Mesabi would supply most of America's iron for decades.`,`The Aerial Bridge began as a transporter bridge, with a gondola suspended from a high steel frame that carried people and vehicles across the canal. In 1929 and 1930 it was converted into a lift bridge, its central span rising more than 40 m to let ships pass. Today it lifts thousands of times a year, and watching the thousand-foot freighters slide beneath it is one of Duluth's great spectacles.`,`The lynching of Elias Clayton, Elmer Jackson and Isaac McGhie on 15 June 1920 was one of the most shocking events in Minnesota history. A mob of several thousand people broke into the city jail and hanged the three men from a light pole. The tragedy led Minnesota to pass an anti-lynching law in 1921, and in 2003 the Clayton Jackson McGhie Memorial was dedicated at the corner where they died.`,`On 10 November 1975, the ore freighter Edmund Fitzgerald, which had left the twin port of Superior, Wisconsin, loaded with taconite pellets a day earlier, sank in a violent storm on eastern Lake Superior with all 29 crew. The tragedy became one of the most famous shipwrecks of modern times, immortalised in a ballad by Gordon Lightfoot. Duluth's maritime museum and the Split Rock Lighthouse on the North Shore tell the story of the lake's many wrecks.`]},"erie-pa":{why:`Erie’s distinctness is a two-season lake effect on Pennsylvania’s only Great Lakes shore. In summer, onshore flow and a lake-breeze front keep afternoons milder and spark storms along the boundary. In autumn the warm water delays frost and makes a grape belt possible. In early winter, cold air crossing open Lake Erie buries the city in snow squalls that inland counties never see. Once the lake freezes, the snow machine weakens and continentality returns. Pittsburgh, a few hours south, shares none of this. The Presque Isle peninsula is the local amplifier: harbor, dunes, and fetch all in one sandspit.`,feel:`Erie feels like a beach town that becomes a snow globe. In summer the lake is warm enough for swimming, the sandy peninsula of Presque Isle fills with sunbathers, and a gentle lake breeze takes the edge off the heat. In winter that same lake turns into a snow machine: frigid winds blowing across the open water pick up moisture and dump it on the city in long, narrow bands, sometimes burying Erie under more than a metre of snow while towns 50 km inland stay almost bare.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey, cold and snowy — afternoons just above freezing, nights near −5°C, and lake-effect snow bands that can drop several centimetres an hour when arctic air blows across the open lake. Erie can see several metres of snow in a big winter; over Christmas 2017 it received more than 1.3 m in two days. Once the lake freezes over, usually in late winter, the heaviest snow ends.`,spring:`Cool and slow — afternoons warming from about 6°C in March to 20°C by late May, but the lake's chilly water keeps the shore cooler than inland towns, and fog can roll in on warm days. Ice lingers on the bay into March, and migrating birds stream through Presque Isle.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 17°C, and a lake breeze that keeps the shore pleasant on hot days. The lake warms enough for swimming by July, Presque Isle's beaches fill, and thunderstorms roll across the lake on humid afternoons.`,autumn:`Mild and long — the lake holds its summer warmth, so September afternoons stay near 23°C, grapes ripen in the vineyards along the shore, and frost comes late. By November, cold winds off the lake bring the first bands of rain, then snow.`},residentFit:`It suits Great Lakes people who love summer beaches and boating on Presque Isle Bay, affordable housing and a mid-sized city, and who take lake-effect snowstorms that can bury the city in stride.`,history:[`At sunrise on 10 September 1813, a young American naval commander named Oliver Hazard Perry sailed out to fight the British squadron on Lake Erie in a fleet built in Erie's harbour from green timber cut in the surrounding forest. His flagship, the brig Lawrence, flew a blue battle flag stitched with the dying words of Perry's friend James Lawrence: Don't give up the ship. Within a couple of hours the Lawrence had been shot to pieces and most of its crew killed or wounded. Perry lowered the flag, climbed into a rowboat and, under heavy fire, rowed to his second brig, the Niagara, took command and sailed it straight through the British line. The British surrendered. Perry's message to his commander became famous: We have met the enemy and they are ours.`,`The Erie people, who gave the lake its name, farmed its southern shore until they were destroyed in wars with the Iroquois in the 1650s. In 1753 the French built a fort on the bay sheltered by the long sandy peninsula they called Presque Isle, almost an island; the British took it in 1760, and in Pontiac's War of 1763 Native warriors captured and burned it. Pennsylvania had no lake shore until 1792, when it bought a small triangle of land from the federal government to gain a port. In 1796 General Anthony Wayne, the hero of the Indian wars, died at the fort; his son later dug up the body, boiled the flesh from the bones, and carried the bones home to eastern Pennsylvania, leaving Wayne with two graves, one in Erie.`,`After the War of 1812 Erie grew into a lake port and a railroad town, a place of shipyards, foundries and factories. General Electric built a giant locomotive works here in 1910, and Erie's factories made paper, boilers and engines. The shore east of the city became a grape belt, where the lake's mild autumns allow Concord grapes to ripen; Welch's still processes them nearby. Today Erie is a smaller city than it was in its industrial heyday, but Presque Isle State Park, with its beaches and its birds, draws millions of visitors each year.`],deepTitle:`Perry's lake`,deep:[`Building a fleet in Erie in 1813 was a race against time. Shipwrights from New York, led by Noah Brown, worked through the winter, and Daniel Dobbins, a local sailor, gathered timber and supplies. Getting the brigs out of the bay was another challenge: a sandbar blocked the entrance, and the crews had to lift the ships over it with floating pontoons while the British fleet was briefly away. A reconstruction of the Niagara is docked in Erie today and still sails as Pennsylvania's flagship.`,`Anthony Wayne's double burial is one of the strangest stories in American history. Wayne died of complications from gout in 1796 and was buried at the foot of the fort's flagpole. In 1809 his son came to take the body home to the family church in Radnor, near Philadelphia, but found it surprisingly well preserved; to make it easier to carry, a doctor boiled the remains, and the flesh was reburied in Erie. Legend says some bones fell out along the road home, and that Wayne's ghost rides the route each year on his birthday looking for them.`,`Presque Isle is a 1,300-hectare sand spit that curves out into the lake and is slowly moving east, pushed by the waves. Its beaches, ponds and marshes are a stopover for hundreds of species of migrating birds. Perry built his ships in the sheltered bay behind it, and a monument to him stands at the place where his fleet wintered.`,`Erie's lake-effect snow happens when cold air crosses the lake from the west and northwest, picking up warmth and moisture from the open water. Over Christmas 2017 a band of snow stalled over the city and dropped more than 1.3 m of snow in two days, setting a Pennsylvania record. As Lake Erie freezes less often in a warming climate, the lake-effect season may stretch longer into the winter.`]},"chattanooga-tn":{why:`Chattanooga is a humid subtropical river city threaded through a plateau gap, not a generic Gulf South grid. The Tennessee’s gorge channels diurnal breezes and storm tracks, while Lookout and Signal mountains add aspect contrasts the valley floor then wraps in urban heat. Summers are as sticky as Birmingham’s; the difference is topography that creates overlooks, gap winds, and cooler ridge nights a short drive from the basin. About 1,450 mm of rain and a flood history come with the same river that supplies the scenery. Nearby plateau towns are cooler and foggier; nearby flatlands lack the notch. The gap is the climate mechanism and the historical one.`,immersive:`Summer in Chattanooga is a wet weight. The Tennessee slides through downtown under a haze that the plateau walls seem to hold in place, afternoons near 32°C, nights that do not truly cool in the basin. Drive up Lookout and the air thins a little; the river becomes a silver bend at Moccasin. Spring and fall are the living seasons, thunderstorms marching the fronts, dogwoods and then color on the ridges. Winter is short, with a dusting on the high rims more often than in the streets. About 1,450 mm of rain falls in a year. Humidity is the climate. The gorge is what makes that humidity feel like a room with a view.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, damp and changeable — afternoons near 12°C, nights just above freezing, and long stretches of grey rain broken by bright, crisp days. A light snow or ice storm comes once or twice in most winters and briefly paralyses the city. The ridges around town show their rock outcrops through the bare trees, and fog often fills the river gorge on cold mornings.`,spring:`Lush and stormy — afternoons warming from about 17°C in March to 28°C by late May, dogwoods and redbuds blooming on the ridges, and strong thunderstorms that can bring hail and the risk of tornadoes. Rain is frequent, and the river runs high. By May, the climbers and paddlers are out in force.`,summer:`Hot and steamy — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 20°C, humidity that hangs in the river valley, and afternoon thunderstorms that rumble over the ridges. The river, the climbing crags and the cool caves of Lookout Mountain are the escapes, and summer evenings come alive with fireflies.`,autumn:`Warm then glorious — September afternoons near 29°C give way to cooler, drier days by October, with afternoons around 23°C and leaves turning red and gold on the ridges by late in the month. It's the best season for hiking and for views from the mountaintops.`},contrasts:[{label:`Lookout Mountain summit (≈10 km SW, ≈450 m higher)`,note:`Up on the mountain above the city, afternoons run a few degrees cooler, and the occasional winter snow that misses downtown sticks around.`},{label:`Monteagle, Cumberland Plateau (≈70 km NW, ≈380 m higher)`,note:`Up on the plateau, summers are noticeably cooler and fog and ice are more common than in the river valley.`}],history:[`On 24 November 1863, Union troops climbed the steep, wooded slopes of Lookout Mountain above Chattanooga to attack the Confederate soldiers dug in near the top. A thick fog clung to the mountain that day, and the soldiers fighting in the mist could barely see each other; from the valley below, the battle could only be heard, not seen. When the fog lifted the next morning, Union soldiers raised a giant American flag on the summit. Newspapers called it the Battle Above the Clouds. Later that day Union troops stormed Missionary Ridge and broke the Confederate siege of Chattanooga, opening the way for General Sherman's march into Georgia.`,`Chattanooga stands where the Tennessee River cuts through the mountains, a natural gateway that made it valuable to every power that came. The land south of the river belonged to the Cherokee, and in 1816 John Ross, who would become the Cherokee Nation's principal chief, opened a trading post and ferry here called Ross's Landing. In 1838 Ross's Landing became one of the main departure points for the forced removal of the Cherokee to Oklahoma, the Trail of Tears. A city grew on the site, and the railroads that converged here made it a prize in the Civil War.`,`After the war Chattanooga became an industrial city of iron foundries and mills. In 1899 two local lawyers bought the right to bottle Coca-Cola for almost nothing from the company's owner, who did not believe bottling had a future; their bottling business made them, and Coca-Cola, rich. By the 1960s the city's factories had made its air so polluted that a federal report named it among the dirtiest in America. Chattanooga cleaned up, rebuilt its riverfront around an aquarium and a pedestrian bridge, and reinvented itself as an outdoor-sports city and, as one of the first American cities to offer gigabit internet to every home, as Gig City.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The fighting around Chattanooga in the autumn of 1863 began with a Confederate victory at Chickamauga in September, the bloodiest battle of the war in the West, after which the defeated Union army retreated into Chattanooga and was besieged. General Grant arrived in October, opened a supply route called the Cracker Line, and in late November launched the attacks that broke the siege. In 1890 Congress made the battlefields the first national military park in the United States.`,`The Coca-Cola bottlers, Benjamin Thomas and Joseph Whitehead, signed their contract with Asa Candler, the owner of Coca-Cola, in 1899. Candler reportedly charged them a single dollar for the rights, believing the future of the drink lay in soda fountains. The two men set up a system of franchised bottlers across the country, and by the time the company realised its mistake, the bottling system had made Coca-Cola available everywhere.`,`Chattanooga's famous song, the Chattanooga Choo Choo, was recorded by Glenn Miller's orchestra in 1941 and became the first record ever awarded a gold disc for selling more than a million copies. The blues singer Bessie Smith, the Empress of the Blues, was born in Chattanooga in 1894 and sang on its street corners as a child. A museum and cultural centre in the city honour her.`,`Lookout Mountain has drawn tourists since the nineteenth century. The Incline Railway, opened in 1895, is one of the steepest passenger railways in the world, and in 1928 a cave explorer named Leo Lambert found an underground waterfall deep inside the mountain and named it Ruby Falls, after his wife. Rock City, a garden of giant boulders on the mountaintop, was advertised on the roofs of hundreds of barns across the South with the words See Rock City, one of the most famous roadside campaigns in America.`]},"gatlinburg-tn":{why:`Gatlinburg is a mid-elevation orographic pocket on the west flank of the Great Smokies. Moist southern air lifts on the ridges, dropping about 1,500 mm of rain in town and more above, with morning fog in the coves and a long, elevation-stratified wildflower and color season. That is closer to a temperate rainforest than to Knoxville’s valley climate a short drive away. Aspect splits the same hollow into mesic north walls and drier south slopes. Flooding on steep watersheds and rare, violent fire in drought are the two hazards the rainfall totals do not cancel. Nearby Pigeon Forge shares the tourists, not the same wet walls.`,immersive:`Morning in Gatlinburg often starts inside a cloud. The West Prong of the Little Pigeon sounds louder than the strip, rhododendron dripping, the ridges erased until the sun burns a hole. By afternoon the pavement can still hit 30°C, humid enough to fog a camera lens, while a north-facing cove twenty metres off the road stays moss-cool. Autumn color peels down the mountains a week at a time. Winter is rain mixed with the odd snow on the high balsams. About 1,500 mm of precipitation falls in a year, much of it as quiet orographic rain rather than drama. The mist is the climate. The neon is the overlay.`,feel:`Gatlinburg feels like a mountain carnival at the gates of a wilderness. The town is a narrow strip of pancake houses, fudge shops, moonshine distilleries and wedding chapels squeezed into a valley at the entrance to Great Smoky Mountains National Park, the most visited national park in the United States, and a few minutes up the road the crowds give way to dense, misty forests of hemlock, tulip trees and rhododendron. The climate is mild and humid: summers are warm, hazy and stormy in the valley but cool on the peaks, winters short and damp, and spring and autumn bring wildflowers and blazing colour.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild in town, wintry above — afternoons near 11°C, nights a little below freezing, and frequent rain in the valley while the high peaks of the Smokies above town are capped with snow and rime. The road over Newfound Gap closes for a few days after storms. Fewer tourists come, cabins fill with fireplaces, and the Christmas lights on the strip glow through the mist.`,spring:`Green and blooming — afternoons warming from about 16°C in March to 26°C by late May, frequent showers, and wildflowers carpeting the coves of the national park, with more than a thousand species in bloom over the season. Waterfalls run full, and the trout streams are cold and clear.`,summer:`Warm, humid and misty — afternoons near 30°C in town but much cooler up on the ridges, nights around 18°C, and almost daily afternoon thunderstorms. The famous blue haze hangs over the mountains, and in early June the synchronous fireflies of Elkmont flash in unison in the forest at night.`,autumn:`The peak season — September afternoons near 27°C easing to about 16°C by November, crisp, clear mornings, and the forests of the Smokies turning red, orange and gold from the high peaks downward through October, drawing the year's biggest crowds. October is the driest month, and the first frosts arrive in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Great Smoky Mountains National Park — the waterfalls, the Alum Cave and Chimney Tops trails, black bears in Cades Cove, synchronous fireflies in June — and for the fudge shops, distilleries and pancake houses of the town.`,residentFit:`It suits hospitality workers, hikers and retirees who love the mountains and the mild, misty climate, and who accept heavy tourist traffic, tight valley housing and the memory of the 2016 wildfire that burned into town.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mount Le Conte (≈10 km SE, ≈1,600 m higher)`,note:`Up on the third-highest peak in the Smokies, it's about 10°C cooler, snow is common in winter, and far more rain falls than in town.`},{label:`Knoxville (≈55 km NW, in the valley)`,note:`Out in the broad Tennessee Valley, summer afternoons run a little hotter and the year brings a bit less rain.`}],history:[`In the 1850s a man named Radford Gatlin opened a store in a remote mountain cove called White Oak Flats, and when the post office moved to his store in 1856, the settlement took his name. Gatlin, however, was a quarrelsome man and a supporter of slavery in a community that sided with the Union, and he soon fell out with almost everyone. By the time of the Civil War his neighbours had run him out of town. They kept his name anyway. Gatlinburg, named after a man the town expelled, grew up to become the busiest gateway to the most visited national park in America.`,`The Cherokee had hunted in these mountains for centuries and travelled through the coves on trails across the high ridges. The first settler, William Ogle, came from South Carolina around 1802, cut logs for a cabin with Cherokee help, and went home to fetch his family, but he died before he could return. His widow, Martha Jane Huskey Ogle, brought the family to the cove herself around 1807, and many people in Gatlinburg today descend from them. For a century the mountain families farmed small plots, made their own tools, quilts and whisky, and lived largely cut off from the world.`,`In the late nineteenth century, logging companies built railroads into the mountains and began cutting the great forests. In the 1920s a campaign to protect what remained led to the creation of Great Smoky Mountains National Park, funded partly by schoolchildren's pennies and by five million dollars from John D. Rockefeller Jr. Families were bought out of their land, and the park was dedicated by President Franklin Roosevelt in 1940. Gatlinburg became a tourist town of hotels, craft shops and attractions. In November 2016 a wildfire driven by hurricane-force winds swept down from the mountains into the town, killing fourteen people and destroying more than 2,400 buildings.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Pi Beta Phi, a women's fraternity, opened a settlement school in Gatlinburg in 1912 to educate mountain children. The teachers encouraged local women to weave and craft, and in 1926 they opened Arrowcraft, a shop selling handmade goods to tourists. The craft tradition survives in the Great Smoky Arts and Crafts Community, a loop of studios and shops outside town.`,`LeConte Lodge, near the summit of Mount Le Conte, opened in 1926 and is still reached only by hiking trails; supplies are carried up by llamas. The lodge has no electricity in its cabins, and guests sleep under wool blankets and eat family-style meals. The trails to the lodge are among the most popular in the park.`,`Great Smoky Mountains National Park is the most visited national park in America, drawing more than twelve million people a year. It protects one of the richest forests in the temperate world, with more species of salamanders than almost anywhere on earth and vast stands of old-growth trees. The famous blue haze comes from natural chemicals released by the forest, which scatter blue light.`,`The 2016 fire began as a small blaze on the Chimney Tops, a rocky summit in the park, during a severe drought. Days later, winds gusting at more than 120 km/h carried embers over the ridges and into Gatlinburg, setting off fires across the town within minutes. Thousands of people fled through burning streets. The town rebuilt, and today it remembers the victims with a memorial in the heart of the strip.`]},"nags-head-nc":{why:`Nags Head is a low barrier island in the hurricane recurvature zone with Gulf Stream water just offshore. That combination produces high humidity, sea-breeze-capped summer heat, nor’easter overwash in winter, and a late-summer cyclone risk that inland Carolina never shares. Dune height and sound-versus-ocean exposure change the hazard in a few hundred metres. Jockey’s Ridge’s migrating sand is the geomorphology of the same wind that lifted the Wright glider. Nearby mainland Elizabeth City is hotter in summer, colder in winter, and dry-footed in storms that put Highway 12 underwater. The strip’s climate is exposure itself.`,immersive:`August on the Nags Head oceanfront is a wet heat the sea breeze never quite finishes. Air sticks; the Atlantic is bathwater from the Gulf Stream; afternoon storms walk in from the sound and leave the dunes steaming. Winter is a different island: nor’easters driving sand across Highway 12, cottages groaning, the ridge of Jockey’s Ridge changing shape between visits. Summer highs near 30°C, winter nights near 2°C, and about 1,420 mm of rain in a year. Humidity is constant. Elevation is not. The whole climate shows up in the ankles when a surge tide stands in the parking lot and on the skin when the onshore wind finally turns cool in October.`,feel:`Nags Head feels like a thin ribbon of sand at the mercy of the Atlantic. On the Outer Banks of North Carolina, a chain of barrier islands far out from the mainland, its weathered beach cottages stand on stilts between the ocean and a wide, shallow sound, beside the tallest living sand dune on the East Coast. The wind is almost always blowing, the light is huge, and the weather comes straight off the sea: summers are warm, humid and breezy, winters mild but wild with nor'easters, and the hurricane season looms over late summer and autumn.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, windy and wild — afternoons near 12°C, nights around 4°C, and nor'easters that drive rain, high surf and tides over the dunes and sometimes across the coastal road. Snow is rare and brief. The beaches empty, the wind sculpts the dunes at Jockey's Ridge, and the ocean takes on a steely winter grey.`,spring:`Breezy and slowly warming — afternoons rising from about 15°C in March to 25°C by late May, the ocean still cold enough to chill the beaches when the wind blows onshore. Strong winds make it a favourite season for kite-flying and hang-gliding on the dunes, and the first visitors arrive by Easter.`,summer:`Hot, humid and sea-breezy — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 23°C, and warm ocean water ideal for swimming and surfing. Afternoon thunderstorms are common, rip currents are a hazard, and the islands' population swells several times over with beachgoers.`,autumn:`Hurricane watch, then golden — August through early October is the peak of hurricane season, but by October the humidity drops, afternoons ease to about 23°C, and the ocean stays warm. Fishing peaks as the red drum and striped bass run, and the crowds go home.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for wide Atlantic beaches, the Wright Brothers National Memorial at Kill Devil Hills, hang-gliding off Jockey's Ridge, the wild horses of Corolla and surfing and fishing around Cape Hatteras.`,residentFit:`It suits beach people, fishermen and remote workers who love the wind and the ocean, accept that storms will flood the road and erode the beaches, and are prepared to evacuate when a hurricane comes.`,contrasts:[{label:`Norfolk, VA (≈110 km N, mainland)`,note:`Away from the open ocean, summer afternoons run a little hotter and winter nights colder, with occasional snow that the Outer Banks usually miss.`},{label:`Cape Hatteras (≈90 km S)`,note:`Further out at the elbow of the Outer Banks, near the warm Gulf Stream, winters are milder still — and the hurricane risk is among the highest on the East Coast.`}],history:[`In the bitter wind of 17 December 1903, two bicycle makers from Dayton, Ohio, named Orville and Wilbur Wright laid a wooden rail on the sand at Kill Devil Hills, just north of Nags Head, and set their homemade flying machine on it. They had chosen this remote spot after writing to the weather bureau asking for a place with strong, steady winds and soft sand to land on. With Orville at the controls and a cold wind blowing, the Flyer lifted off on a hop about 37 m long, lasting 12 seconds — the first powered, controlled flight in history. By the fourth flight that day, Wilbur managed one 260 m long. A telegram to their father that afternoon reported: Success four flights Thursday morning.`,`The Carolina Algonquian peoples had fished the sounds behind the islands for more than a thousand years when English ships arrived in 1584. In 1587 Sir Walter Raleigh sent more than a hundred colonists to Roanoke Island, just across the sound from Nags Head, where Virginia Dare became the first English child born in the Americas. Her grandfather, the colony's governor, sailed back to England for supplies; when he finally returned three years later, the colony had vanished, leaving only the word Croatoan carved on a post. What happened to the Lost Colony remains one of the great mysteries of American history.`,`Nags Head became North Carolina's first beach resort in the 1830s, when planters from the mainland brought their families to escape summer fevers. Legend says the town got its name from pirates who hung lanterns around the necks of horses to lure ships onto the shoals, but the story is probably folklore. The shoals themselves were real: so many ships were wrecked off these islands that they became known as the Graveyard of the Atlantic. Today the Outer Banks are lined with beach houses and resorts, and the narrow islands are constantly reshaped by hurricanes and nor'easters.`],deepTitle:`Wind, sand, wrecks`,deep:[`The Wright brothers spent several seasons at Kill Devil Hills, living in a wooden shed and testing gliders before they built their powered Flyer. The local people of Kitty Hawk helped them, and some witnessed the first flight. A tall granite monument now stands on Big Kill Devil Hill, where they made their glider tests, and the Wright Brothers National Memorial marks the spots where the four flights landed.`,`The Outer Banks shipwrecks number in the thousands, caused by shifting sandbars, sudden storms and the meeting of warm and cold ocean currents. To save sailors, the government built lifesaving stations along the coast from 1874. The Pea Island station was manned by the first all-Black crew in the service, led by Richard Etheridge, a formerly enslaved man. In 1896 they rescued the entire crew of the schooner E.S. Newman in a hurricane; a century later, in 1996, the crew were posthumously awarded the Gold Lifesaving Medal.`,`The Lost Colony has inspired many theories. Some historians think the colonists moved to Croatoan Island, the home of a friendly Native nation, and merged with them; others believe they went inland. Archaeologists have found English artefacts at sites in both places, but no proof. Since 1937 an outdoor drama called The Lost Colony has been performed each summer in Manteo, one of the longest-running outdoor plays in America.`,`The islands move. Storms push sand from the ocean side over to the sound side, and inlets open and close. In 1999 the Cape Hatteras Lighthouse, the tallest brick lighthouse in America, was moved nearly 1 km inland to save it from the encroaching sea. The road along the islands, Highway 12, is regularly washed out by storms, and rising seas make the future of the Outer Banks' communities uncertain.`]},"burlington-vt":{why:`Burlington’s climate is Lake Champlain’s north–south fetch plus a lee relative to the Adirondacks. While the lake is open, early-winter air picks up moisture and dumps snow bands on the Vermont shore that inland towns do not see. Spring is delayed by cold water and fog. Summer afternoons, near 28°C, are milder than Boston or Hartford because of lake breezes. Once ice covers the lake, moderation weakens and continental cold returns. Nearby Stowe is colder and snowier in the mountains; the Champlain Islands are even more maritime. The lake is why this Dfb city does not feel like interior New England.`,immersive:`November on the Burlington waterfront can feel like a fifth season: lake fog in the morning, a squall of lake-effect snow by afternoon, the Adirondacks appearing and vanishing as if on a dimmer. Summer is the reward, breezes off Champlain keeping the eighties honest, sails on the water, Intervale farms in full green. Winter nights near −11°C still bite once the lake ices and the buffer thins. About 1,030 mm of precipitation falls in a year, some of it as that early-season lake snow. Fall color hits hard behind Canadian fronts. Life here runs by the lake’s calendar, not the mainland’s, and the lake is never in a hurry to become spring.`,feel:`Burlington feels like a small city with a lake for a front yard and mountains for a back yard. Lake Champlain stretches west to the Adirondacks, softening the summer heat and delaying spring, while the Green Mountains rise to the east. Winters are long, cold and snowy, but the city is a college town that embraces them, with ski slopes less than an hour away. Summer evenings on the waterfront, watching the sun set over the Adirondacks, are the reward.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and bright on the clear days — afternoons near 0°C, nights near −10°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −25°C. Snow covers the ground from December to March, the lake freezes over in cold winters, and skiers head for the Green Mountains, where Stowe and Sugarbush get far more snow than the city.`,spring:`Late and muddy — afternoons warming from about 3°C in March to 20°C by late May, with snowstorms possible into April and a muddy thaw that Vermonters call mud season. Maple sap runs in March, and by May the lilacs bloom and the lake warms slowly.`,summer:`Warm and pleasant — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 15°C, and breezes off the lake that take the edge off hot days. Thunderstorms roll across the lake, sailboats fill the bay, and the waterfront bike path and farmers' markets are full.`,autumn:`Spectacular — September afternoons near 22°C, crisp nights, and a blaze of red and orange foliage across the hills by early October. The lake stays warm into the autumn, and the first snow arrives on the mountains by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Lake Champlain sunsets over the Adirondacks, the pedestrian Church Street Marketplace, the lakefront bike path and the Champlain Islands, and the ski resorts of the Green Mountains within an hour's drive.`,residentFit:`It suits university people, remote workers and outdoor families who want a small, progressive city with a lake and mountains on its doorstep, and who can manage long, cold winters and expensive housing.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mount Mansfield (≈35 km E, ≈1,280 m higher)`,note:`Vermont's highest peak above Stowe gets several metres of snow a year and runs about 8°C cooler than the lakeshore.`},{label:`Montpelier (≈55 km SE, in the mountains)`,note:`Away from the lake's moderating influence, the state capital has colder winter nights, more snow and a shorter growing season.`}],history:[`In October 1776, a makeshift American fleet of gunboats and galleys, hastily built on the shores of Lake Champlain, met a much larger British fleet in the narrow channel behind Valcour Island, about 25 km across the lake from today's Burlington. The Americans were commanded by Benedict Arnold, then one of the Continental Army's most daring generals. His fleet was badly beaten, but in the night Arnold slipped his surviving boats past the British line in the fog, and the fight had delayed the British invasion from Canada for a crucial year. Arnold later turned traitor, but his doomed little fleet is still celebrated by historians as one of the most important battles of the Revolution.`,`The Western Abenaki had farmed the Winooski River valley and fished Lake Champlain for centuries; their name for the river meant land of wild onions. The French explorer Samuel de Champlain sailed down the lake in 1609 and gave it his own name. After the British won the region, New Hampshire's governor chartered Burlington in 1763, and Ethan Allen and his brother Ira, leaders of the rebellious Green Mountain Boys, bought up land and settled here. Ira founded the University of Vermont in 1791, the same year Vermont joined the United States. Ethan, famous for capturing Fort Ticonderoga in 1775, died in Burlington in 1789.`,`In the nineteenth century, canals linking the lake to the Hudson River and the Erie Canal made Burlington one of the busiest lumber ports in the country, and French-Canadian and Irish immigrants came to work in its mills. In the twentieth century IBM opened a plant nearby, and the city became a liberal college town. In 1978 two friends, Ben Cohen and Jerry Greenfield, opened an ice cream shop in an old gas station downtown; in 1981 a socialist named Bernie Sanders won the mayor's race by ten votes; and in 1983 a band called Phish formed at the university. Today Burlington is Vermont's largest city, with fewer than fifty thousand people.`],deepTitle:`Lake of battles`,deep:[`Lake Champlain was a highway of war for two centuries. French, British and American armies sailed up and down its waters, and forts such as Ticonderoga and Crown Point guarded its narrows. In the War of 1812 an American fleet defeated the British at the Battle of Plattsburgh in 1814, across the lake from Burlington, ending a British invasion. The lake bottom is littered with shipwrecks, including Arnold's gunboat Spitfire, discovered in 1997.`,`Lake Champlain has its own legendary monster, Champ, said to be a large, long-necked creature like the Loch Ness monster. Sightings date back to the nineteenth century, and in 1977 a woman named Sandra Mansi took a famous photograph of what appears to be a head and neck rising from the water. Scientists have offered many explanations, from logs to large fish, but the legend thrives, and Burlington's minor-league baseball team once had a mascot named Champ.`,`Ben & Jerry's started with a five-dollar correspondence course in ice-cream making. The first scoop shop, in a renovated gas station on the corner of St. Paul and College streets, celebrated its first anniversary by giving away free cones, a tradition that became the company's annual free cone day. The company grew into an international brand, known for its odd flavours and its social activism.`,`Burlington has been a laboratory for progressive politics. Bernie Sanders served four terms as mayor in the 1980s, and the city became known for its community land trust, its public waterfront and its bike path, built along an old railroad line. In 2014 Burlington became the first city in the United States to get all of its electricity from renewable sources.`]},"bar-harbor-me":{why:`Bar Harbor’s climate is cold-current marine air meeting island mountains. The Labrador Current keeps summers cool and fog-prone; Acadia’s ridges wring extra rain and create pockets of different wind and cloud a few kilometres apart. Winters are milder than interior Maine because of the water, then violent when nor’easters arrive. About 1,370 mm of precipitation and a spruce-fir forest mark a Downeast regime, not a Cape Cod one. Nearby Ellsworth, inland, is warmer in summer and colder in winter. The fjord-like coast and Pemetic’s orography are why the village does not feel like a generic Dfb mill town.`,immersive:`A July afternoon in Bar Harbor can arrive as a wall of fog, the spruce going gray, foghorns on the water, Cadillac erased as if the island had been unplugged. An hour later granite steams in hard sun and the harbor smells of bait and balsam. Winter is nor’easters, mixed snow and rain, streets of granite dust and slush, then a clarity that makes the barren summits look close enough to touch. Summer highs near 25°C, winter nights near −10°C, about 1,370 mm of precipitation in a year. The Labrador Current is in every breath. Interior Maine, an hour away, is a different season on the same day.`,feel:`Bar Harbor feels like the meeting of the North Atlantic and the mountains. Granite peaks rise straight out of the sea on Mount Desert Island, spruce forests run down to the rocky shore, and fog drifts in and out of the harbour on cool summer days. The cold Labrador Current keeps summers mild and refreshing, but winters are long and stormy, and the town, packed with visitors in summer, shrinks to a quiet fishing village when the snow comes.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, stormy and quiet — afternoons near 1°C, nights near −8°C, and nor'easters that bring snow, sleet and wind-driven rain off the ocean. The Park Loop Road closes to cars, the shops shut for the season, and cross-country skiers glide along the snow-covered carriage roads. On clear mornings, sea smoke rises from the cold water of Frenchman Bay.`,spring:`Late, foggy and slow — afternoons warming from about 4°C in March to 16°C by late May, with fog and drizzle common and snow possible into April. The ocean stays cold, keeping the shore chilly, but by late May the forests are green and the first lupines bloom.`,summer:`Cool, bright and foggy by turns — afternoons near 24°C, nights around 13°C, and fog that rolls in off the cold sea and can blanket the harbour while the mountaintops sit in sunshine. Lobster boats work the bay, cruise ships anchor offshore, and Acadia's trails and carriage roads fill with hikers and cyclists.`,autumn:`Crisp and colourful — September afternoons near 20°C, nights cooling fast, and a blaze of red maples and golden birches against the dark spruce by mid-October. Storms grow stronger as the season goes on, and the crowds thin after the leaves fall.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Acadia National Park — sunrise on Cadillac Mountain, the carriage roads, the Park Loop Road and Thunder Hole — along with whale watching, lobster on the pier and the granite-and-spruce coast of Mount Desert Island.`,residentFit:`It suits hikers, hospitality workers and retirees who love the rugged coast and cool summers, and who can handle the long, quiet winter after the visitors leave and the scramble for housing in summer.`,contrasts:[{label:`Bangor (≈60 km NW, inland)`,note:`Away from the cold ocean, summer afternoons in Bangor run a few degrees warmer and winter nights colder.`},{label:`Cadillac Mountain summit (≈5 km SW, ≈460 m higher)`,note:`The highest point on the US Atlantic coast is windier and cooler than town, and from October to March it often catches the first sunrise in the United States.`}],history:[`In October 1947, after the driest summer and autumn anyone in Maine could remember, a fire started in a dump a few kilometres from Bar Harbor. On 23 October, a howling northwest wind drove it across the island toward the town. The flames swept through the forests and the summer estates of some of America's richest families, burning dozens of grand summer cottages along the shore. Residents fled to the town pier, where fishing boats evacuated hundreds by sea, and a convoy of cars escaped through the burning forest after bulldozers cleared a path. The fire burned some 70 square kilometres of the island and ended the golden age of Bar Harbor's millionaires; the forests that grew back are full of birch and aspen, which give Acadia its brilliant autumn colours today.`,`The Wabanaki — Penobscot, Passamaquoddy and other peoples — had come to Mount Desert Island for thousands of years to fish, hunt seals and gather clams, calling it Pemetic. In 1604 the French explorer Samuel de Champlain sailed past and named it the island of the barren mountains, for its bald granite summits. French Jesuits founded a mission on the island in 1613, which an English raiding party from Virginia promptly destroyed. For two centuries the island was a frontier between English and French claims, and fishermen and shipbuilders settled its harbours.`,`In the mid-1800s painters of the Hudson River School, including Thomas Cole and Frederic Church, painted the island's dramatic coast, and their pictures drew tourists, called rusticators, who boarded with local families. By the 1880s the town, then called Eden, had become one of the most fashionable summer resorts in America, where Rockefellers, Vanderbilts and Astors built enormous summer homes they modestly called cottages. Some of those wealthy families helped protect the island's mountains as a national monument in 1916; it became a national park in 1919, the first east of the Mississippi, and was renamed Acadia in 1929. In 1918 the town took the name of Bar Harbor, after the gravel bar that appears at low tide.`],deepTitle:`Cottages and fire`,deep:[`George Dorr, a wealthy Bostonian, spent most of his fortune and his life buying land on Mount Desert Island and lobbying Washington to protect it. With the Harvard president Charles Eliot and others, he donated the land for Sieur de Monts National Monument in 1916, which became Lafayette National Park in 1919 and Acadia National Park in 1929. Dorr became its first superintendent and died nearly penniless in 1944.`,`John D. Rockefeller Jr., who summered on the island, built some 72 km of carriage roads through the park between 1913 and 1940, with granite bridges and stone guardrails nicknamed Rockefeller's teeth. He wanted a way for people to travel by horse and carriage without cars. The roads are now used by walkers, cyclists and cross-country skiers, and they are considered among the finest broken-stone roads in America.`,`The Wabanaki never stopped coming to Bar Harbor. In the late nineteenth century, families camped on the shore each summer and sold baskets woven from sweetgrass and brown ash to tourists. The Abbe Museum, founded in 1928, tells the story of the Wabanaki peoples, and the annual Native American Festival continues the tradition of basket markets.`,`The Jackson Laboratory, founded in Bar Harbor in 1929 by the geneticist Clarence Cook Little with support from summer residents, became one of the world's leading centres of mouse genetics. The 1947 fire destroyed the lab and most of its mice, and scientists around the world sent back mice that had been bred from Jackson's strains to help it rebuild. Today the lab breeds millions of research mice a year and is the largest employer on the island.`]},"lubbock-tx":{why:`Lubbock’s climate is High Plains elevation plus unlimited fetch. At about 980 m on the Llano Estacado, summer sun drives afternoons near 34°C while humidity crashes, then nights cool hard. The dryline uses that flat approach to fire supercells. Blue northers use it to drop winter temperatures in hours. Annual precipitation near 490 mm arrives mostly as convective bursts, not gentle rain. Nearby Palo Duro adds canyon relief; the city itself has none. Cotton exists because of the Ogallala, not because the sky is generous. That combination of wind, dryline, and irrigated steppe is why Lubbock does not feel like the rest of Texas.`,immersive:`A Lubbock spring day can start in shirtsleeves and end in a dust-dark noon, the dryline a visible wall, sirens, then hail hammering cotton country that was planted because the Ogallala sits under the caprock. Summer is a dry oven, 34°C with a wind that never clocks out, skies so hard they look swept. Winter is bright and bearable until a blue norther arrives and the temperature falls off the table. About 490 mm of rain in a year, much of it in those convective minutes. Grit hangs in the air on still evenings. Lightning shows at a distance that would be another county anywhere with hills. The plains are the weather.`,feel:`Lubbock feels like the top of the world on a flat table. The Llano Estacado, the Staked Plains, stretches to the horizon in every direction, a high, treeless tableland where the sky dominates everything and the wind almost never stops. Days swing from hot to cool within hours, dry air makes the sunshine intense, and in spring and early summer the dryline sets off towering supercell thunderstorms that light up the plains with lightning. Cotton fields, grain elevators and the big sky define the place.`,seasons:{winter:`Dry, sunny and changeable — afternoons near 14°C, nights below freezing, and blue northers that can drop the temperature by 20°C in a few hours as arctic air sweeps down the plains. Snow is occasional and usually melts within a day or two, and fierce winds can whip up dust from bare fields.`,spring:`Windy, dusty and stormy — afternoons climbing from about 20°C in March to 30°C by late May, with relentless winds that blow dust across the plains and set the stage for severe thunderstorms. The dryline, where moist Gulf air meets dry desert air, triggers supercells with hail and tornadoes in May and June.`,summer:`Hot and dry with stormy evenings — afternoons near 34°C, nights cooling to about 20°C in the dry air, and evening thunderstorms that roll across the plains bringing much of the year's rain. Irrigated cotton grows green against the brown plains, and the evenings are pleasant once the sun goes down.`,autumn:`The best season — September afternoons near 30°C easing to about 18°C by November, calm winds, clear skies and cool nights. Cotton harvest fills the fields and gins, Texas Tech football fills the stadium, and the first cold fronts arrive in October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Palo Duro Canyon to the north, the Buddy Holly Center, a growing High Plains wine country, Texas Tech football and spectacular spring thunderstorms rolling across the Llano Estacado.`,residentFit:`It suits agricultural researchers, Texas Tech staff, storm chasers and families who want affordable living under huge skies, and who can take wind, dust storms, hail and hot summers.`,contrasts:[{label:`Amarillo (≈190 km N, ≈120 m higher)`,note:`Further up the Panhandle, Amarillo's winters are colder and snowier, with even more relentless wind.`},{label:`Fort Worth (≈450 km E)`,note:`Down off the Caprock in North Texas, the air is far more humid, the year brings nearly twice Lubbock's rain, and winter nights are milder.`}],history:[`Just after midnight on 3 February 1959, a 22-year-old rock-and-roll star from Lubbock named Buddy Holly chartered a small plane to fly between concerts on a freezing winter tour of the Midwest. It crashed in a snowy Iowa cornfield soon after take-off, killing Holly, Ritchie Valens, J.P. Richardson — the Big Bopper — and the pilot. Holly had grown up in Lubbock, playing country music on the local radio before he heard Elvis Presley and switched to rock and roll. In just two years he and his band, the Crickets, recorded That'll Be the Day, Peggy Sue and Not Fade Away, songs that shaped the Beatles and the Rolling Stones. Don McLean later called it the day the music died.`,`People have lived in this part of the Llano Estacado for at least twelve thousand years; at Lubbock Lake, an ancient spring site in Yellow House Canyon, archaeologists have found evidence of hunters of mammoths and ancient bison. In the nineteenth century the plains were Comanche country, the hunting grounds of the Quahadi band, who rode the buffalo herds across this vast tableland. In 1874 the army defeated the Comanche at Palo Duro Canyon, and buffalo hunters slaughtered the herds within a few years. Ranchers moved onto the open range, and in 1890 two rival towns merged to form Lubbock, rolling their hotel across the canyon on logs to the new town square.`,`The railroad arrived in 1909, and farmers discovered that the plains could grow cotton, especially once they began pumping water from the vast Ogallala Aquifer. Texas Technological College opened in 1925, and Lubbock became the Hub City of the South Plains. On 11 May 1970 an F5 tornado tore through downtown, killing 26 people and wrecking much of the city; the 20-storey Great Plains Life Building survived a direct hit and still stands, slightly twisted. Today the Lubbock region is one of the largest cotton-growing areas in the world, and the city has produced a remarkable string of musicians, from Waylon Jennings to Joe Ely and Natalie Maines.`],deepTitle:`Cotton and Crickets`,deep:[`In August 1951, several Texas Tech professors sitting in a backyard saw a V-shaped formation of lights sweep silently across the night sky. Over the following weeks, hundreds of people reported seeing them, and a teenager named Carl Hart Jr. photographed them. The Lubbock Lights became one of the most famous UFO cases of the era, investigated by the US Air Force; the official explanation blamed migrating birds reflecting the city's new streetlights, though not everyone was convinced.`,`The Lubbock Lake Landmark is one of the most complete records of human life on the Great Plains. Layers of soil in the canyon preserve the remains of extinct mammoths, horses and giant bison, along with the tools of the hunters who killed them, and then of later Native peoples up to the Comanche and the buffalo hunters. Excavations have continued since the 1930s.`,`The Ogallala Aquifer turned the South Plains into one of the most productive farming regions in America, but farmers have been pumping its water far faster than rain can replace it. Water tables have fallen sharply, and some wells have gone dry. Many farmers are switching to dryland cotton or more efficient irrigation, and the future of agriculture around Lubbock depends on how long the water lasts.`,`The 1970 tornado led to a new science of wind engineering. Texas Tech researchers studied the damage and became pioneers in designing buildings and storm shelters that could survive tornadoes, and the university's National Wind Institute is now a world leader in the field. The city's annual Buddy Holly celebrations and the Buddy Holly Center keep its most famous son's memory alive.`]},"new-orleans-la":{why:`New Orleans is distinct because a below-sea-level urban bowl sits between a continental river and a warm gulf. Humidity is not a season; it is the medium. Summer heat indices routinely exceed what the 33°C afternoon suggests. About 1,740 mm of rain falls on ground that often cannot drain without pumps. Tropical cyclones then add surge that the levees must perfectly refuse. Nearby higher terraces and the North Shore flood less. The portage that made the city also trapped it. Compound flood, not jazz weather, is the climate mechanism, and the bowl is why this Cfa city is not interchangeable with Houston or Mobile.`,immersive:`A New Orleans August is a wet wool blanket. By nine in the morning the air is already thick; afternoon thunderstorms hammer the Quarter and leave steam on slate roofs; nights do not truly cool. The river, sweet olive, and mildew share the same block. Winter is the mercy season, mild, then a short hard snap that blackens night-blooming jasmine. Summer highs near 33°C, winter nights near 7°C, about 1,740 mm of rain in a year. Humidity writes the felt temperature. The bowl writes the flood. Live oaks hang on to their green as if autumn were a rumor. The Gulf is always in the next weather discussion.`,feel:`New Orleans feels like a city that floats on heat, water and music. Wrapped in a great bend of the Mississippi and surrounded by lakes and swamps, much of it lying below sea level behind its levees, it is humid nearly all year, the air thick with the scent of jasmine, river mud and frying food. Summers are long and steamy, winters short and mild, and a brass band seems to be playing somewhere at any hour. Hurricanes are the price of living in such a place.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and damp — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 8°C, and a mix of grey, drizzly days and bright, crisp ones after cold fronts. A freeze happens once or twice in most winters, and the city goes slightly crazy for Carnival, which peaks with Mardi Gras in February or early March. Camellias and azaleas bloom early.`,spring:`Beautiful and short — afternoons warming from about 23°C in March to 30°C by late May, flowers everywhere, and festival season in full swing, from the French Quarter Festival to Jazz Fest. Strong thunderstorms can bring sudden flooding to low streets, and the humidity creeps back by May.`,summer:`Hot, steamy and stormy — afternoons near 33°C, nights barely dropping below 24°C, and a heat index that often feels like 40°C or more. Almost daily afternoon thunderstorms bring torrential downpours that briefly flood streets. People slow down, find the shade of the oaks and the air conditioning, and wait for the evening.`,autumn:`Hurricane season, then relief — August through October is the peak of hurricane season, and the city watches every storm in the Gulf. By late October, the first real cold fronts bring drier air and afternoons near 26°C, and the city's outdoor life returns with a rush.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for jazz on Frenchmen Street, Mardi Gras and Jazz Fest, Creole and Cajun cooking, the French Quarter and Garden District, and swamp tours through the cypress bayous beyond the levees.`,contrasts:[{label:`Baton Rouge (≈120 km NW)`,note:`Further inland along the river, summer afternoons run a little hotter and winter nights a few degrees colder.`},{label:`Grand Isle (≈80 km S, on the Gulf)`,note:`On Louisiana's only inhabited barrier island, sea breezes take the edge off summer, but the island takes the full force of every Gulf hurricane.`}],history:[`On 8 January 1815, a ragtag American army of regular soldiers, frontier riflemen, free Black militiamen, Choctaw warriors and even pirates under the smuggler Jean Lafitte waited behind a mud rampart on a sugar plantation just below New Orleans. Their commander was Andrew Jackson. A British army of veterans who had fought Napoleon marched across an open field toward them in the morning fog. In less than half an hour the British lost some two thousand men killed, wounded or captured, including their commanding general, while the Americans lost only a handful. The victory made Jackson a national hero and, eventually, president. There was one catch: the war was all but over. A peace treaty had been signed in Belgium two weeks earlier, but the news had not yet crossed the Atlantic.`,`The Choctaw called the place Bulbancha, place of many tongues, a trading ground where many nations met between the river and Lake Pontchartrain. In 1718 the French colonial governor Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville founded a town on a curve of the river and named it after the Duke of Orléans. Spain took over in 1763, and after two great fires burned most of the old city in 1788 and 1794, it was rebuilt in brick and stucco with courtyards and iron balconies — so that the French Quarter today is largely Spanish in its architecture. France took the colony back briefly in 1800, then sold it to the United States in 1803. Refugees from the Haitian Revolution doubled the city's population in 1809, adding to a rich Creole culture.`,`In the nineteenth century New Orleans became one of the largest and richest cities in America, a port for cotton and sugar and the biggest slave market in the country. In Congo Square, enslaved Africans gathered on Sundays to dance and play music, and those rhythms, along with brass bands, blues and Caribbean music, gave birth to jazz at the start of the twentieth century; Louis Armstrong was born here in 1901. The city's famous Mardi Gras parades began in 1857. In August 2005 Hurricane Katrina's storm surge broke the levees, flooding about four-fifths of the city and killing more than a thousand people, and New Orleans has been rebuilding ever since.`],deepTitle:`Below the river`,deep:[`Jean Lafitte was a pirate, smuggler and slave trader who operated from the bayous of Barataria, south of the city. The British offered him money and a commission to help them attack New Orleans; instead he passed their letters to the Americans and offered his men and his supplies of gunpowder and flints to Jackson in exchange for pardons. His gunners served the American artillery in the battle. Afterwards he drifted away to Galveston and disappeared from history.`,`New Orleans has sunk over the past century as its swamps were drained and its soils compacted, and parts of the city now lie several metres below sea level, protected by levees and pumps. Katrina showed what happens when those defences fail. Since then the federal government has built a stronger system of levees, floodwalls and giant pumps, but the city's coast is still losing land to the sea at an alarming rate as the Mississippi Delta erodes.`,`In 1892 a shoemaker named Homer Plessy deliberately boarded a whites-only railroad car in New Orleans to challenge Louisiana's segregation laws. The case, Plessy v. Ferguson, went to the Supreme Court, which ruled in 1896 that separate but equal facilities were legal, a decision that upheld segregation for more than half a century. In 1960 six-year-old Ruby Bridges integrated a New Orleans elementary school, escorted by federal marshals past jeering crowds.`,`Storyville, the city's legal red-light district from 1897 to 1917, was one of the places where early jazz musicians found work. Louis Armstrong grew up nearby, learned to play the cornet in a home for boys, and went on to become one of the most influential musicians of the century. Today the city's music lives on in its clubs, its second-line parades and its jazz funerals, where brass bands play mournful hymns on the way to the cemetery and joyful music on the way back.`]},"ojai-ca":{why:`Ojai’s distinctness is an east–west valley tucked behind the Topatopa Mountains, a rare orientation in Southern California that traps late sun on the north wall and shelters the floor from many Pacific storms. The result is a Mediterranean pocket: wet winters, dry summers, citrus-friendly nights, and evening sea-breeze tongues that take the edge off inland heat. Santa Ana winds are complicated by the enclosing ranges but not abolished. Nearby Ventura is marine and cooler; the Ojai floor is hotter, brighter, and more flammable. The pink moment is the visible geometry of that bowl. Fire is the seasonal tax on the same geometry.`,immersive:`Late sun in Ojai hits the Topatopa face and the whole north wall goes salmon for a few minutes, a geometry so consistent that locals treat it as a clock. Afternoons on the valley floor can reach 31°C, oak shadows black on dry grass, citrus oil in the heat. Then a cooler tongue of sea air often slides up from the west and supper happens outside. Winter is green, the only season the hills look soft, with about 510 mm of rain falling almost entirely then. Summer is dust, irrigation, and fire weather. Nights in winter stay near 4°C. The bowl holds light. It also holds smoke when the chaparral runs.`,feel:`Ojai feels like a hidden valley that time forgot. Tucked behind the coastal hills, away from the fog of the coast, it basks in warm sunshine, its orange groves and oak-dotted meadows ringed by mountains. The valley runs east to west, so at sunset the Topatopa Bluffs at its eastern end glow a deep pink — a nightly event the locals call the pink moment. Summers are hot and dry, winters mild with cool nights, and wildfire is the ever-present shadow.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild days and chilly nights — afternoons near 19°C, nights around 4°C with frost possible in the valley bottoms, and Pacific storms that bring most of the year's rain in a few heavy downpours. The hills turn green, the citrus groves glow with ripening oranges and Pixie tangerines, and the snow occasionally dusts the high peaks above the valley.`,spring:`Fragrant and green — afternoons near 22°C, cool nights, and the scent of orange blossom filling the valley. Wildflowers bloom on the hillsides, the creeks run, and the oak meadows are at their greenest before the long dry season.`,summer:`Hot, dry and golden — afternoons near 31°C and often hotter, nights cooling to about 14°C, and almost no rain from May to October. Unlike the coast just 20 km away, the valley escapes the marine fog, and the hills turn golden brown. Locals retreat to swimming holes in the canyons.`,autumn:`Warm and windy — afternoons near 27°C, clear skies, and the season of the Santa Ana winds, hot and dry gusts from the desert that can drive wildfires through the chaparral at frightening speed. The first rains usually come in November, bringing relief.`},contrasts:[{label:`Ventura (≈20 km S, on the coast)`,note:`Down on the coast, the marine layer keeps summer afternoons about 8°C cooler, with grey mornings that Ojai rarely sees.`},{label:`Upper Ojai (≈10 km E, ≈250 m higher)`,note:`Up the valley, the air is a little cooler, winter nights frostier, and the chaparral wilder.`}],history:[`In 1916 a wealthy glassmaker from Toledo, Ohio, named Edward Libbey looked at the dusty little town of Nordhoff, where he spent his winters, and decided it needed a makeover. He hired architects to build a Spanish-style arcade along the main street, a pergola, and a tall post office tower modelled on a Spanish colonial church, and he paid for much of it himself; when shopkeepers could not afford their share, Libbey quietly covered it. In 1917 the town changed its name to Ojai, from the Chumash word for moon. Many locals believe the valley later served as the model for Shangri-La, the hidden paradise in Frank Capra's 1937 film Lost Horizon — a claim no one has ever quite been able to prove or disprove.`,`The Chumash people had lived in the valley for thousands of years, in villages connected by trails over the mountains to the coast; one village in the upper valley was called 'Awhay. After the Spanish missions were established in the late 1700s, the Chumash were drawn or forced into the mission system, and their population collapsed. In 1874 a town was laid out in the valley and named Nordhoff, after Charles Nordhoff, a journalist whose book about California had promoted the region; his grandson later co-wrote Mutiny on the Bounty. Orange and lemon groves, health resorts and a tennis tournament, founded in 1896, made Nordhoff a winter retreat.`,`In 1922 the Indian philosopher Jiddu Krishnamurti came to Ojai with his brother Nitya, who was seeking a cure for tuberculosis, and had a profound spiritual experience under a pepper tree; he lived in the valley on and off for the rest of his life, and Ojai became a centre for spiritual seekers, artists and musicians. The Ojai Music Festival has been held since 1947. In December 2017 the Thomas Fire, then the largest wildfire in California's recorded history, swept around the valley, threatening the town for days.`],deepTitle:`The pink moment`,deep:[`The pink moment happens because the Ojai Valley runs east to west, an unusual orientation for the mountains of Southern California. As the sun sets in the west, its last rays shine straight up the valley onto the face of the Topatopa Bluffs, which glow pink, orange and red for a few minutes. Locals stop what they are doing to watch it.`,`Edward Libbey made his fortune with the Libbey Glass Company, which produced light bulbs and pressed glass, and later helped develop a machine for mass-producing glass bottles. He also built the Ojai Valley Inn, a golf resort that still operates. His arcade and post office tower survived a fire in 1917 and still define the town's centre.`,`The ceramic artist Beatrice Wood moved to Ojai in 1948 and lived there until her death in 1998 at the age of 105. A friend of the French artist Marcel Duchamp in her youth, she became famous for her shimmering lustre-glazed pottery. She credited her long life to chocolate and young men, and the director James Cameron said she partly inspired the character of the elderly Rose in Titanic.`,`The Ojai Valley has burned many times. Chaparral, the dense shrubland that covers its hills, is adapted to fire, and Santa Ana winds can drive flames through it at astonishing speed. The Thomas Fire burned some 1,100 square kilometres in Ventura and Santa Barbara counties; afterwards, heavy rains on the burned slopes set off deadly mudflows in nearby Montecito. Ojai survived largely because firefighters made a stand at its edges and because years of brush clearing had created a buffer.`]},"hilo-hi":{why:`Hilo’s climate is trade-wind orographic rain on a high volcanic island. Moist northeasterlies lift on Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa and can drop more than three metres of warm rain in a year, while Kona, on the same island, may receive an order of magnitude less. The trade inversion then caps the moisture, so a drive to the summits crosses into dry cold. Hilo Bay’s shape also amplifies tsunamis, a coastal hazard folded into the same topography. Nearby Puna is wet too, without the same urban bay. The windward/leeward asymmetry is the whole Hawaiian climate lesson, and Hilo is its rain-soaked textbook page.`,history:[`Early on the morning of 1 April 1946, people in Hilo noticed that the sea in the bay had drained away, leaving fish flopping on the exposed reef. Some walked out to look. Then the first wave came. An earthquake in the Aleutian Islands, some 3,800 km to the north, had sent a tsunami racing across the Pacific, and it struck Hilo with waves up to 8 m high, smashing the waterfront and killing 96 people in the town. Up the coast at Laupāhoehoe, the sea swept away a seaside school, killing 16 children and 5 teachers. Because it was April Fool's Day, some people who heard warnings thought they were a joke. The disaster led to the creation of the Pacific Tsunami Warning Center three years later.`,`Hilo Bay was the heart of a rich Hawaiian district of taro fields, fishponds and villages, nourished by the rain that falls on the windward slopes of Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa. According to tradition, the young Kamehameha came here and overturned the Naha Stone, a huge rock that legend said only the future unifier of the islands could move; he went on to conquer all the Hawaiian Islands by 1810. American missionaries arrived in the 1820s, and in the late 1830s the preacher Titus Coan led a great religious revival in Hilo, baptising more than 1,700 people on a single day in 1838. In 1880 a lava flow from Mauna Loa began creeping toward the town, and it was still advancing the next summer when Princess Ruth Keʻelikōlani made offerings to Pele, the volcano goddess, at its edge. Within days, the flow stopped.`,`Sugar plantations spread across the windward coast in the nineteenth century, and workers came from China, Japan, Portugal, Puerto Rico, Korea and the Philippines, giving Hilo the rich mix of cultures it still has. Hilo grew into a busy port and railway town. Then, on 23 May 1960, another tsunami, this time from a giant earthquake in Chile, struck the town and killed 61 people. Afterwards the city moved its downtown back from the shore and turned the waterfront into parks. The sugar mills closed in the 1990s, but Hilo has found a new role as the base for the observatories on Mauna Kea and as the home of the Merrie Monarch Festival, the world's most important hula competition.`],deepTitle:`Rain, lava, waves`,deep:[`A clock on a pole near the waterfront still stands with its hands stopped at 1:04, the moment the 1960 tsunami struck Hilo. The waves wrecked the Waiākea neighbourhood near the bay, which had been rebuilt after 1946, and this time the town decided not to rebuild in the danger zone. The Pacific Tsunami Museum in downtown Hilo tells the stories of the survivors and teaches visitors how to recognise the warning signs.`,`Banyan Drive, a road along the Waiākea peninsula, is lined with giant banyan trees planted by famous visitors in the 1930s and later. Babe Ruth, Amelia Earhart, Franklin Roosevelt and Cecil B. DeMille were among those who planted trees, each one marked with a plaque bearing the planter's name. Earhart planted hers in 1935, shortly before her historic solo flight from Hawaii to California.`,`The summit of Mauna Kea, more than 4,200 m above sea level and above most of the clouds and moisture in the atmosphere, is one of the best places on earth to study the stars, and since the late 1960s more than a dozen observatories have been built there. For many Native Hawaiians, the mountain is sacred, the home of gods and ancestors, and plans for a giant new telescope in 2019 led to months of protests by people who called themselves protectors of the mountain.`,`The Merrie Monarch Festival, named after King David Kalākaua, who revived hula in the nineteenth century after missionaries had suppressed it, began in Hilo in 1963 to boost the town's economy after the sugar decline and the 1960 tsunami. It became a hula competition in 1971 and is now the most prestigious hula event in the world, broadcast live across Hawaii every spring. Hilo, one of the wettest cities in the United States, also boasts lush gardens, waterfalls and orchid farms nourished by its rain.`]},"nome-ak":{why:`Nome is a coastal subarctic town whose seasonal sea ice both buffers and betrays. Compared with interior Alaska at 64°N, the Bering shore has a narrower annual temperature range: summers stay cool, near 15°C, and winters, while severe, are less extremely cold than Fairbanks when ice and water moderate the air. When ice is thin or leads are open, Siberian wind delivers vicious chill. Storms rake the treeless coast. About 390 mm of precipitation and polar-night darkness complete a climate that is maritime by Arctic standards and harsh by any temperate one. The ice calendar, not the thermometer mean, is the mechanism residents actually use.`,immersive:`Nome is where North America's cold air masses exit toward the Bering Sea. Winter is long and dark: sea ice damps temperature extremes near shore but storms still rake the coast with wind-driven snow. Front Street faces that fetch; when leads are open, Siberian wind makes the same thermometer reading feel rawer than Fairbanks cold. Spring break-up turns rivers to slush highways; summer brings fog, berries, and cool but pleasant afternoons — t-shirt weather by local standards, near 15°C. The Bering Strait's proximity means climate teleconnections to both the Pacific and Arctic oscillations show up in storm tracks. There is no road out. Freeze-up, break-up, and the first barge are the seasons that matter.`,feel:`Nome feels like the edge of the continent, a gold-rush town perched on a beach at the end of the road — except there is no road; it can only be reached by air or by sea. Winter is long, dark and bitterly cold, with the Bering Sea frozen solid to the horizon and the sun barely rising. Summer is short and cool, with days that seem never to end, and miners still pan for gold on the beach. The town's frontier spirit, and its role at the finish of the Iditarod, give it a character all its own.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, dark and bitterly cold — afternoons near −10°C, nights near −17°C, and arctic air from Siberia that can push the thermometer below −35°C, made worse by winds off the frozen sea. The Bering Sea freezes solid to the horizon, and in December the sun is up for only about four hours. The northern lights blaze across the sky on clear nights.`,spring:`Still winter for most of it — afternoons climbing from about −8°C in March to near 7°C by late May, with snow on the ground into May and the sea ice lingering offshore until late spring. Days lengthen rapidly. In March the town fills with crowds for the end of the Iditarod sled-dog race.`,summer:`Short, cool and bright — afternoons near 14°C, nights around 6°C, and nearly endless daylight in June, when the sun barely dips below the horizon. Rain and fog are common, the tundra blooms with wildflowers, and miners and tourists pan for gold on the beach. Storms can sweep in from the Bering Sea even in summer.`,autumn:`Brief and stormy — September afternoons near 10°C dropping to below freezing by late October, with the year's strongest storms sweeping in off the open sea and driving waves against the seawall. The tundra turns red and gold, the first snow falls, and the sea begins to freeze by November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come to watch the Iditarod sled dogs finish on Front Street in March, see musk oxen and migrating birds along the roads in summer, pan for gold on the beach and see the aurora over the Bering Sea.`,residentFit:`It suits Iñupiat families, miners, researchers and adventurous public servants who are at home in a remote Arctic town with no road to the outside world, months of darkness and fierce winter storms.`,contrasts:[{label:`Fairbanks (≈850 km E, Interior)`,note:`In the heart of Alaska, far from the sea, summer afternoons run about 8°C warmer and winter nights are even colder.`},{label:`Kotzebue (≈290 km N, above the Arctic Circle)`,note:`Further north, the sea ice lasts longer, winters are colder, and the sun truly does not set at midsummer.`}],history:[`In January 1925 a doctor in Nome found that children in the town were dying of diphtheria, a highly contagious disease, and that his supply of antitoxin was out of date. The nearest supply was in Anchorage, more than 1,000 km away, and the only planes in Alaska had been taken apart for the winter. So the serum was sent by train to Nenana, then relayed to Nome by dog sled — twenty mushers and about 150 dogs racing through blizzards and temperatures below −40°C, covering some 1,085 km in five and a half days. Leonhard Seppala and his lead dog Togo ran the longest and most dangerous stretch, but it was Gunnar Kaasen and his lead dog Balto who brought the serum into Nome, and Balto who became famous; a statue of him was unveiled in New York's Central Park that same year. The Iditarod sled-dog race, which since 1973 has run more than 1,500 km from Anchorage to Nome every March, honours the serum run.`,`The Iñupiaq and Yupik peoples have lived along this coast for thousands of years, hunting seals, walrus and whales and fishing the rivers. In 1898 three Scandinavian prospectors, guided by Iñupiaq men, found gold on Anvil Creek, and a year later prospectors discovered that the beach itself was full of gold dust — anyone with a shovel and a pan could get rich. By 1900 some 20,000 people were camped along the beach, making Nome the largest city in Alaska. Among them was Wyatt Earp, of O.K. Corral fame, who ran a saloon here for two years. The name Nome, according to a popular story, came from a mapmaker's mistake: a British officer wrote ? Name next to an unnamed cape, and a later draughtsman read it as C. Nome.`,`The gold rush faded, but mining companies used giant dredges to work the creeks for decades. Fires in 1905 and 1934 destroyed much of the town, and storms off the Bering Sea have repeatedly flooded it, so few gold-rush buildings remain. Today Nome is a town of about 3,700 people, a supply hub for the villages of the Bering Strait region, and a meeting place of Iñupiaq, Yupik and non-Native cultures. As the sea ice shrinks, Nome has become a candidate for a deep-water Arctic port, and cruise ships now sail through the Northwest Passage to its harbour.`],deepTitle:`Gold on the beach`,deep:[`The gold rush brought lawlessness on a grand scale. In 1900 a politician named Alexander McKenzie and a corrupt federal judge, Arthur Noyes, conspired to seize the richest claims on Anvil Creek from their owners, with McKenzie installed as the court-appointed receiver. The miners appealed to a higher court in San Francisco, which ordered the claims returned, and McKenzie was jailed. The scandal inspired Rex Beach's bestselling novel The Spoilers, which was filmed five times.`,`The three prospectors who struck gold at Anvil Creek, Jafet Lindeberg, Erik Lindblom and John Brynteson, became known as the Three Lucky Swedes, though Lindeberg was actually Norwegian. The Iñupiaq men who had guided them to the creek, including Constantine Uparazuck and Gabriel Adams, were denied claims of their own because Native people were not considered citizens under the mining law. Their contribution has only recently been recognised.`,`Leonhard Seppala, a Norwegian immigrant, was the most famous musher in Alaska. He and Togo covered about 420 km of the serum run, including a dangerous crossing of the sea ice of Norton Sound in a storm. Many mushers and historians believe Togo was the true hero, and in recent years books and a film have told his story. Balto and Togo were both later preserved; Togo's mounted body is displayed at the Iditarod headquarters in Wasilla.`,`Nome is one of the last places in America where you can still prospect for gold on a public beach. Each summer recreational miners camp on the sand and dig, and offshore, small dredges vacuum the seabed for gold, a scene featured on reality television. In winter, the frozen sea becomes a highway for snowmachines, and hunters travel across the ice as their ancestors did by dog team.`]}},i={"corpus-christi-tx":{why:`Corpus Christi is not an open Gulf beach town. Barrier islands and a shallow bay sit between the city and deep water, so afternoon sea breezes arrive as a local wind machine rather than a generic coastal draft. West of town, hypersaline Laguna Madre adds another fetch and humidity gradient that inland prairie never sees. When offshore flow kills that breeze ahead of a tropical cyclone, heat and surge stack in the same hours. Bay geometry, not storm category alone, decides how high water climbs through Aransas Pass and into neighborhoods built on fill.`,immersive:`Summer here is a negotiation with Gulf moisture. Heat indices climb into the danger band by late morning, then a bay breeze often knocks the edge off the waterfront while inland streets stay still and sticky. Live oaks and palms hold salt on their leaves. Winters stay mild until a rare freeze blackens citrus and ornamental tropicals overnight. The honest calendar is hurricane season: when a storm is still hundreds of kilometres away, the air can go glassy and the tide already looks wrong. After dark, refinery lights and shrimp-boat work keep the port awake while the lagoon to the west stays dark and windy.`,feel:`Corpus Christi feels like a breezy bayfront city where the sea wind never quite stops. Warm Gulf water wraps around it, palm trees line the seawall, and a steady southeasterly breeze keeps the long, humid summers bearable and makes the bay one of the best windsurfing spots in the country. Winters are short and mild, broken by the occasional cold blast from the north, and from August to October the city keeps a wary eye on the Gulf.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and changeable — afternoons near 20°C, nights around 10°C, and warm, humid spells broken by northers that sweep down from the plains and drop the temperature by 15°C in a few hours, with grey skies and gusty winds. Frost is rare, birdwatchers flock to the coast to see whooping cranes wintering nearby, and the bay is calm on the cool days.`,spring:`Warm, windy and humid — afternoons rising from about 24°C in March to 31°C by late May, strong southerly winds that make the bay a playground for windsurfers and kiteboarders, and occasional thunderstorms. Spring break brings students to Padre Island, and the humidity steadily climbs.`,summer:`Hot, humid and breezy — afternoons near 34°C, nights barely falling below 25°C, and a sea breeze that keeps the bayfront cooler than inland towns. Rain is sparse except for the odd thunderstorm or tropical system. The beaches on Padre and Mustang islands fill with swimmers and fishermen, and the hurricane season looms.`,autumn:`Hot and storm-watched, then pleasant — September is the wettest month and the heart of the hurricane season, with afternoons near 32°C and heavy tropical downpours. By October the first northers bring relief, and by November afternoons ease to about 24°C, with dry, breezy days and migrating birds filling the coastal marshes.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Padre Island National Seashore, sea-turtle hatchling releases, windsurfing and kiteboarding on the bay, the USS Lexington aircraft carrier museum and world-class birding along the coast.`,contrasts:[{label:`Kingsville & the King Ranch (≈60 km SW, inland)`,note:`Away from the bay breeze, summer afternoons run a couple of degrees hotter and the ranchland is drier and dustier.`},{label:`Port Aransas & Mustang Island (≈30 km E)`,note:`Out on the barrier island, ocean breezes trim summer afternoons, but the island takes the full force of Gulf storms.`}],history:[`In the winter of 1845–46, nearly four thousand American soldiers camped on the beach at Corpus Christi, waiting to march into territory claimed by Mexico; it was the largest gathering of the US Army since the War of 1812. To pass the time, the officers built a theatre and staged plays. One young lieutenant, small and slender, was cast as Desdemona in Shakespeare's Othello. His name was Ulysses S. Grant. Unfortunately his Othello, Lieutenant Theodoric Porter, complained that he could not summon any passion opposite a Desdemona who looked so little like a woman, and an actress was hired from New Orleans instead. The army marched south in March 1846, and the Mexican-American War began weeks later.`,`The Karankawa people had lived along the bay for centuries, fishing and gathering shellfish; their shell middens still mark the shoreline. The Spanish explorer Alonso Álvarez de Pineda charted the coast in 1519, and the bay later took the name of the Catholic feast of Corpus Christi. In 1839 a trader named Henry Kinney set up a trading post on the bay to deal with Mexican ranchers and smugglers, and the town grew around it. To the southwest, a steamboat captain named Richard King founded a ranch in 1853 that grew into the King Ranch, larger than the state of Rhode Island.`,`On 14 September 1919, a powerful hurricane drove a storm surge across North Beach and downtown, killing hundreds of people. The city used the disaster to win federal money for a deep-water port, which opened on the storm's seventh anniversary in 1926, and for a great stepped seawall along the bayfront, designed by Gutzon Borglum, the sculptor of Mount Rushmore. Oil, petrochemicals and a naval air station, where a young George H.W. Bush earned his pilot's wings, fuelled the city's growth. In 1995 Corpus Christi lost its most famous daughter, the Tejano singer Selena Quintanilla, who was murdered at the age of 23; a statue of her looks out over the bay.`],deepTitle:`Seawall and Selena`,deep:[`The 1919 hurricane struck with almost no warning. Storm surges pushed debris and wreckage across North Beach, where many families had gathered for the end of the summer season, and bodies were found for weeks along the bay shore and in the marshes. The death toll has been estimated at anywhere from about three hundred to well over six hundred. The seawall, completed in 1941, was designed to stop the next surge, and it has protected downtown through many storms since, including Hurricane Celia in 1970.`,`Selena Quintanilla grew up singing in her family's restaurant and band, and by the early 1990s she had become the most famous star of Tejano music, a blend of Mexican and Texan styles, winning a Grammy in 1994. She was shot by the former president of her fan club in a Corpus Christi motel in March 1995. Her death caused an outpouring of grief across Texas and Mexico, and the Mirador de la Flor, a memorial on the seawall, draws fans from around the world.`,`The King Ranch, south of the city, is one of the largest ranches in the world. Richard King and his partner Mifflin Kenedy built their fortunes on steamboats on the Rio Grande, and King bought the first of his land in 1853. The ranch developed the Santa Gertrudis cattle breed, the first breed developed in the United States, and its running W brand became famous. Many of its cowboys, known as Kineños, were Mexican families who moved north with King's herds.`,`The aircraft carrier USS Lexington, which fought in the Pacific in the Second World War and later trained naval pilots, has been moored in Corpus Christi Bay as a museum since 1992. The port is now one of the busiest in the United States for oil exports, and the city's skyline of refineries and bridges stands next to beaches, birding trails and the long, empty sands of Padre Island National Seashore.`]},"paducah-ky":{why:`Paducah sits where the Tennessee meets the Ohio, so river fog, nocturnal convection, and floodwater are not side notes to a latitude. The confluence concentrates Gulf moisture and channels mesoscale storms along the valleys. Winter is a contest between Arctic outbreaks and overriding Gulf air, which is why freezing rain appears as often as honest snow. Summer humidity is inland-South, not maritime. Hydroclimate, more than the Cfa label, is what a scouting trip should check: how high the wall is, how often the bottoms go under, and how sticky the nights stay after the river has cooled the afternoon only slightly.`,feel:`Paducah feels like a river town through and through. It sits where the Tennessee River pours into the Ohio, a short way upstream from where the Ohio meets the Mississippi, and the rivers shape everything: the morning fog, the barges sliding past the floodwall, the humid summer heat, and the floods that have repeatedly tested the town. Spring is lush and stormy, summers hot and steamy, autumns mellow, and winters short, grey and mild with the occasional ice storm.`,seasons:{winter:`Short, grey and changeable — afternoons near 9°C, nights just below freezing, and a mix of rain, occasional snow and the dreaded ice storms that can coat trees and power lines in glaze, as one did disastrously in 2009. Fog often lies thick over the rivers in the mornings.`,spring:`Lush and stormy — afternoons warming from about 15°C in March to 27°C by late May, flowering dogwoods and redbuds, and the wettest season of the year, with strong thunderstorms and tornado warnings. The rivers rise with spring rains, and every few years they climb high against the floodwall.`,summer:`Hot and steamy — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 21°C, and humidity that hangs over the river bottoms. Afternoon thunderstorms are common, and the long, hot evenings bring out the fireflies. Barges churn up and down the rivers, and boaters head to Kentucky Lake and Lake Barkley.`,autumn:`Mellow and golden — September afternoons near 28°C easing to about 15°C by November, with long spells of settled, sunny weather, with oaks and maples turning colour by late October. Morning fog returns to the rivers as the nights cool.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the National Quilt Museum and the huge quilt show each April, the floodwall murals along the Ohio River, a lively historic downtown and the lakes of the Land Between the Lakes nearby.`,contrasts:[{label:`St. Louis (≈220 km NW)`,note:`Further north on the Mississippi, St. Louis has colder winters with more snow, and similar steamy summers.`},{label:`Memphis (≈260 km SW)`,note:`Further down the river, Memphis runs a degree or two hotter in summer and milder in winter, with heavier Gulf humidity.`}],history:[`On 25 March 1864, the Confederate cavalry general Nathan Bedford Forrest swept into Paducah, which had been held by Union troops since the start of the war, and drove the garrison into its fort by the river. Forrest's men looted supplies and horses but could not take the fort, and pulled out. A few days later, Union-leaning newspapers boasted that the raiders had missed 140 of the finest horses in town, hidden in a foundry. Forrest read the papers. On 14 April he sent his men back to Paducah, found the horses exactly where the newspapers said they were, and rode off with them. The story became a favourite example of why generals should not trust journalists.`,`The land at the meeting of the Tennessee and Ohio rivers was Chickasaw country until 1818, when the tribe ceded western Kentucky in a treaty known as the Jackson Purchase. In 1827 William Clark, of Lewis and Clark fame, laid out a town at the confluence and named it Paducah, probably after the Padouca, a name for the Comanche and other Plains peoples he had met on his western journey, though local legend credits a Chickasaw chief. Steamboats made Paducah a busy port. In September 1861 a Union brigadier general named Ulysses S. Grant seized the town without a fight, securing the mouth of the Tennessee River for the Union.`,`In January 1937 weeks of rain sent the Ohio River to a record height of nearly 18.5 m, flooding almost all of Paducah and forcing some 27,000 people from their homes. Afterwards the government built a massive concrete floodwall, which now displays a long series of painted murals of the town's history. In the 1950s the Atomic Energy Commission built a uranium enrichment plant west of town, which ran until 2013. Paducah also gave the nation a vice president, Alben Barkley, and became, through its National Quilt Museum, a world centre of quilting, recognised in 2013 as a UNESCO Creative City.`],deepTitle:`Floods and quilts`,deep:[`The 1937 flood was one of the worst disasters in the Ohio Valley's history. Ice and heavy rain in January swelled the river until it broke over the town's earthen levees, and for weeks the water stood in the streets; people travelled by boat downtown, and the flood mark is painted high on the walls of buildings. Paducah's floodwall, completed in the late 1940s, has kept the river out ever since, including during the high water of 2011, when its gates were closed.`,`Alben Barkley, who grew up near Paducah, served as a congressman and senator for decades and as Harry Truman's vice president from 1949 to 1953. His grandchildren gave him the nickname the Veep, and the word has been used for vice presidents ever since. Paducah's other famous son, the humorist Irvin S. Cobb, wrote short stories set in a fictional version of his hometown that made him one of the best-known writers in America in the 1920s.`,`The Paducah Gaseous Diffusion Plant, built in the 1950s, enriched uranium for nuclear weapons and later for power plants, and for decades it employed thousands of people. It also left a legacy of contamination, and workers who were exposed to radioactive materials without proper protection later won compensation. Cleanup of the site continues.`,`The American Quilter's Society, founded in Paducah in 1984, holds an annual quilt show that draws tens of thousands of people to the town, and the National Quilt Museum opened in 1991 to display some of the finest quilts in the world. The LowerTown neighbourhood, once run down, was revived by an artist relocation programme that offered cheap houses to artists, making the town a regional centre for crafts.`]},"bismarck-nd":{why:`Bismarck occupies the middle of the continent, so maritime air is a visitor, not a climate. Arctic highs deliver brilliant dry cold; Gulf tropical air in summer is equally temporary and often explosive along the dryline. The Missouri trench and Lake Sakakawea nibble at the edges of that range without canceling it. Chinook warmth can interrupt a January cold spell after crossing the Rockies, then vanish overnight. The lived difference from Fargo or Minneapolis is less snow-lake theater and more wind, sun, and a temperature swing that makes vehicle, garden, and heating decisions feel like logistics rather than lifestyle.`,feel:`Bismarck feels like a capital set down in the middle of an ocean of grass. The Missouri River winds through a broad valley lined with cottonwoods, and beyond it the prairie rolls to the horizon in every direction under a huge sky. The climate is one of extremes: summers are hot, dry and stormy, while winters bring arctic air that sweeps down from Canada with nothing to stop it, pushing temperatures far below zero. Spring and autumn are brief, windy and beautiful.`,seasons:{winter:`Bitter and bright — afternoons near −3°C, nights near −15°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −30°C, with windchills colder still. Snow is light but blows and drifts in the prairie wind, and blizzards can close the interstates. Sunny days are common, and the Missouri freezes over.`,spring:`Windy and abrupt — afternoons climbing from about 3°C in March to 20°C by late May, with late snowstorms possible into April and strong winds that dry the prairie. Snowmelt swells the rivers, migrating geese and cranes stream north, and the grass greens almost overnight in May.`,summer:`Warm, sunny and stormy — afternoons near 29°C, nights cooling to about 14°C in the dry air, and evening thunderstorms that roll across the plains with hail and spectacular lightning. Long days, cool evenings and the river make it a pleasant season. Sunflower fields bloom in late July.`,autumn:`Crisp, golden and short — September afternoons near 23°C cooling to about 5°C by November, cottonwoods turning gold along the river, and the harvest of wheat and sunflowers. The first snow usually falls in October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Missouri River and Fort Abraham Lincoln, where Custer rode out in 1876, the Lewis and Clark trail, the North Dakota Heritage Center and the open prairie under an enormous sky.`,residentFit:`It suits families, state government and energy workers who want a safe, affordable small city, and who accept long, bitterly cold winters with blizzards and arctic air in exchange for bright, sunny summers.`,contrasts:[{label:`Fargo (≈310 km E, Red River Valley)`,note:`In the flat Red River Valley, Fargo gets more rain and more humid summers, and is just as cold in winter.`},{label:`Rapid City, SD (≈450 km SSW)`,note:`Near the Black Hills, chinook winds give Rapid City noticeably milder winter afternoons and less bitter cold.`}],history:[`On 17 May 1876, the Seventh Cavalry rode out of Fort Abraham Lincoln, just across the Missouri from Bismarck, with its band playing The Girl I Left Behind Me. At its head was Lieutenant Colonel George Armstrong Custer. Five weeks later, on 25 June, Custer and more than 260 of his men were killed by Lakota and Cheyenne warriors at the Little Bighorn in Montana. The news came back down the Yellowstone and the Missouri aboard the steamboat Far West, carrying wounded soldiers; its captain, Grant Marsh, drove it some 1,140 km in about 54 hours, a record, reaching Bismarck late on 5 July. A telegraph operator in Bismarck tapped out the news to a stunned nation.`,`For centuries before that, the Mandan people had lived along the Missouri in villages of large, round earth lodges, farming corn, beans and squash on the river bottoms and trading with peoples from across the plains. On-a-Slant Village, south of today's city, was home to perhaps a thousand people before smallpox devastated the Mandan in the late 1700s. Lewis and Clark spent the winter of 1804–05 among the Mandan and Hidatsa upstream, where they met Sacagawea. In 1872 the Northern Pacific Railway reached the river here, and the next year it named the town Bismarck, after the German chancellor Otto von Bismarck, hoping to attract German investors to buy its bonds.`,`The discovery of gold in the Black Hills in 1874 turned Bismarck into a boomtown, the jumping-off point for prospectors. In 1883 it became the capital of Dakota Territory, and in 1889 the capital of the new state of North Dakota. When the old capitol burned in 1930, the state built a nineteen-storey Art Deco tower, known as the Skyscraper of the Prairie, which can be seen for kilometres across the plains. In the 1950s the Garrison Dam upriver flooded the homelands of the Mandan, Hidatsa and Arikara, forcing hundreds of families from their land. Today Bismarck is a government, medical and energy centre for the region.`],deepTitle:`Custer and capitol`,deep:[`Elizabeth Custer, Custer's wife, was waiting at Fort Abraham Lincoln when the news of the Little Bighorn arrived on 6 July. She and the other officers' wives were told of their husbands' deaths that morning. She spent the rest of her long life defending her husband's reputation, writing books that portrayed him as a hero. The Custer house has been rebuilt at the fort, now a state park, along with a reconstruction of the Mandan earth lodges at On-a-Slant.`,`Bismarck's name was a business decision. The Northern Pacific Railway had financed its construction partly by selling bonds in Europe, and it hoped that naming the town after the famous chancellor would attract German investors. The bonds did not sell well, and the railroad went bankrupt in the Panic of 1873, but the name stuck. Bismarck himself never visited.`,`The Garrison Dam, completed in 1953, created Lake Sakakawea, one of the largest reservoirs in the United States. It flooded some 62,000 hectares of the Fort Berthold Reservation, including the fertile river bottoms where the Mandan, Hidatsa and Arikara had farmed for centuries. George Gillette, the tribal chairman, wept openly as he watched the agreement being signed in 1948; a photograph of that moment became a symbol of the costs of the dam.`,`Bismarck's climate is one of the most continental in the United States, with a range of more than 60°C between its hottest summer days and coldest winter nights. In the twenty-first century, the oil boom of the Bakken formation to the west brought growth and traffic, and the city has also coped with floods on the Missouri, especially in 2011, when record releases from the dams upstream pushed the river over its banks.`]},"morgantown-wv":{why:`Morgantown’s weather is a ridge-and-hollow problem more than a latitude problem. Southwesterly flow lifts against the Allegheny Plateau and wrings extra rain and cloud onto the uplands, while the Monongahela trench ponds fog and cold air on still nights. Two hundred metres of local relief can separate a rain evening from a heavy-snow evening. Summers are sticky without being Gulf-coastal. The university town sits where mid-Atlantic coastal storms and Ohio Valley convection both have a vote, which is why neighbors a few hollows apart keep different frost calendars.`,feel:`Morgantown feels like a university town wedged into the folds of the Appalachians. Its steep streets climb from the Monongahela River up wooded hills crowded with brick houses, church steeples and the buildings of West Virginia University, whose students make up much of the population, and a little automated train, the PRT, shuttles them between campuses on its own concrete tracks. The climate is humid and changeable: summers warm and stormy, winters grey, damp and often snowy, with fog filling the river valley on cool mornings.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey, damp and hilly-cold — afternoons near 6°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and a mix of rain, snow and freezing drizzle as storms ride up the ridges. Snow comes often in modest amounts, and the city's steep streets turn treacherous; fog fills the Monongahela valley on still mornings while the ridgetops sit in sun.`,spring:`Changeable and greening — afternoons rising from about 12°C in March to 23°C by late May, with frosty mornings into April, frequent showers, and redbud and dogwood flowering on the hillsides. Late snow is possible in March, and thunderstorms arrive by May.`,summer:`Warm, humid and green — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 16°C, and frequent afternoon thunderstorms building over the hills. The forested ridges keep the city lush, and the rail-trail along the river is busy with walkers and cyclists. Valley fog returns at dawn on calm, muggy mornings.`,autumn:`Mild and colourful — September afternoons near 25°C easing to about 12°C by November, morning fog in the river valley burning off to clear, crisp days, and the hills turning red and gold in October, just in time for football weekends.`},contrasts:[{label:`Allegheny highlands (≈90 km SE, ≈700 m higher)`,note:`Up around Canaan Valley and Davis, winters are far colder and several times snowier, and summer nights can flirt with frost.`},{label:`Pittsburgh (≈95 km N)`,note:`Further down the Monongahela, Pittsburgh's climate is similar but a touch cooler and cloudier, with less of Morgantown's deep valley fog.`}],history:[`In October 1972, a few weeks before a presidential election, President Nixon's daughter Tricia came to Morgantown to ride a futuristic new transport system: driverless electric cars running on a concrete guideway, which passengers could summon like elevators to carry them between downtown and the university's scattered campuses. The federal government had rushed the project to show off American technology. The ceremony went ahead, but the system was not really ready; it broke down repeatedly and did not open to the public until 1975. Half a century later, the Morgantown Personal Rapid Transit is still running, still one of the only systems of its kind in the world, and still a source of local pride and exasperation in roughly equal measure.`,`The Monongahela valley was a contested hunting ground of the Delaware, Shawnee and Seneca in the 1700s, and early settlers who tried to establish themselves here were driven out. In 1759 a settlement founded the year before at the mouth of Deckers Creek was wiped out in a raid. Around 1771 a frontiersman named Zackquill Morgan claimed land at the same spot, and in 1785 the Virginia legislature established a town on his land called Morgan's Town. The Mason-Dixon line, the boundary between Pennsylvania and Virginia, runs just north of the city. Morgantown grew slowly as a river town of farmers, salt makers and boatmen.`,`In 1867 the new state of West Virginia founded an agricultural college in Morgantown, which became West Virginia University the next year. The railroad arrived in 1886, and coal mines, glassworks and oil wells transformed the area; glassmakers from Belgium, France and Germany built a thriving industry, and Morgantown glass was sold across the country. During the Great Depression, Eleanor Roosevelt championed a homestead community for unemployed miners at nearby Arthurdale. Today the university and its hospitals are the city's economic engine. Morgantown is also the hometown of the comic actor Don Knotts, best known as Deputy Barney Fife.`],deepTitle:`Mountaineer town`,deep:[`The Morgantown PRT carries thousands of students a day. Its little cars run along an elevated guideway and stop at five stations; passengers choose their destination at a turnstile, and a car takes them there without stopping. It was meant to be a model for cities across America, but the cost overruns and early breakdowns ended that dream. Engineers still visit to study it.`,`Arthurdale, about 25 km southeast of Morgantown, was the first of the New Deal homestead communities, built in 1933 and 1934 to give unemployed coal miners and their families a fresh start as small farmers and craftsmen. Eleanor Roosevelt visited often and took a personal interest in its school and crafts. The project was criticised as costly, and many of its factories failed, but the village survives and is now a historic site.`,`Morgantown's glass industry was famous for its fine tableware. The Seneca Glass Company, which moved to Morgantown from Fostoria, Ohio, in 1896, and Morgantown Glass Works made hand-blown and cut glass for hotels, restaurants and the White House. Immigrant craftsmen brought skills from Europe, and the neighbourhoods around the factories became home to their communities. The last of the big glass plants closed in the 1980s.`,`West Virginia University's sports teams, the Mountaineers, draw tens of thousands of fans on autumn weekends, and the university and its hospital system have grown into the state's largest employer. The Monongahela River, once used to float coal barges, is now a place for kayaking and fishing, and a long rail-trail follows its banks.`]},"port-orford-cape-blanco-or":{why:`Cape Blanco is Oregon’s westernmost headland, so Port Orford lives inside cold upwelling air rather than a generic Oregon-coast marine layer. Summer fog and wind are not decorations; they are the temperature control. Nearby Coast Range slopes squeeze winter rain until totals look rainforest-wet while the thermometer barely moves. The harbor has no bar to cross, which is a navigation fact with a climate cousin: the same exposure that makes launching easy also throws full-ocean storms at the heads. Shelter is a hedge and a hollow, not a latitude.`,immersive:`Wind arrives here as a physical occupation. On the cape, Sitka spruce lean inland and coastal prairie stays cropped as if grazed by air. A bright afternoon can vanish behind a marine curtain in minutes, dropping the temperature enough that 20°C feels like a heat event worth remarking. Winter is less about snow than about fetch: long-period swell, salt on windows, and nights when the harbor crane is the only upright thing that looks intentional. Gardens survive in the lee of hedges. The town is small enough that the dock still sets the day’s rhythm, and the headland still sets the wind.`,seasons:{summer:`Cool, bright and wind-blasted — afternoons near 20°C, nights around 12°C, and a strong north wind that blows down the coast on most afternoons, stirring up cold water from the depths offshore. Fog banks hover offshore and roll in at night. Rain almost vanishes, the headland grasses turn gold, and when the wind drops, the bay is calm and clear.`},history:[`On 9 June 1851, a steamer dropped nine men on the beach at Port Orford, where its captain, William Tichenor, planned to found a town. The Qua-to-mah people, whose village stood nearby, did not want them there. When the men's small cannon fired into a crowd of warriors, killing several, a siege began. The nine settlers held out for about two weeks on a rocky sea stack at the water's edge, now called Battle Rock, until they slipped away one night and walked north through the forests to safety. Tichenor came back with a larger party a few weeks later, and the town was founded. Battle Rock still stands on the beach beside the highway.`,`The Tututni people had lived along this stretch of coast for thousands of years, in cedar-plank houses near the river mouths, fishing for salmon and gathering shellfish. In 1543 a Spanish expedition sighted the white cliffs of Cape Blanco, the westernmost point in Oregon. The Rogue River Wars of the 1850s ended with the Tututni forced onto reservations to the north. Gold was found on the beaches, and in 1868 a forest fire destroyed most of the young town. Port Orford rebuilt as a lumber and sheep-ranching settlement, shipping a fragrant, rot-resistant wood that came to be called Port Orford cedar.`,`Port Orford's harbour faces the open ocean with no protecting river bar or breakwater on its exposed side, so the fishing boats cannot be left in the water overnight. Instead, giant cranes lift them out of the sea and set them down on trailers on the dock each evening — one of the very few working docks of its kind in the world. In 1870 a lighthouse was built on Cape Blanco; it still stands, the oldest lighthouse standing on the Oregon coast. The town remains a working harbour of about a thousand people, surrounded by wild headlands, rivers and forest.`],deepTitle:`Battle Rock`,deep:[`In 1856 a geologist named John Evans reported that he had found a large meteorite on a mountain near Port Orford, and sent a small sample to the Smithsonian Institution. When the sample was analysed, it proved to be a rare type of meteorite, and Evans claimed the main mass weighed some ten tonnes. He died before he could go back for it, and generations of searchers have hunted for it without success. Some researchers now suspect the whole thing was a hoax.`,`Port Orford cedar, a tall, aromatic tree that grows only in a small area of southwest Oregon and northwest California, was prized for ship-building, boxes and later for arrows and battery separators. In the twentieth century it became especially valuable in Japan, where its wood is used in temples and shrines. Since the 1950s a root disease spread by mud on vehicles has killed many of the trees, and foresters work to protect the remaining stands.`,`The Cape Blanco lighthouse keepers lived a lonely life on one of the windiest points on the West Coast. Mabel Bretherton, the first woman to serve as a keeper on the Oregon coast, served there in 1903. Nearby stands the Hughes House, a Victorian farmhouse built in 1898 for an Irish ranching family, and both are now open to visitors as part of Cape Blanco State Park.`,`The cold waters off Port Orford, fed by upwelling from the deep ocean, are among the richest on the Pacific coast. The Redfish Rocks Marine Reserve, created in 2012, protects a cluster of offshore rocks and their kelp forests, where rockfish, lingcod and sea lions thrive. Local fishermen helped design the reserve, an unusual partnership between the fishing community and conservationists.`]},"klamath-falls-upper-klamath-basin-or":{why:`East of the Cascades, this volcanic basin is a rain-shadow climate with a lake in the middle of it. Upper Klamath Lake stores light and a little heat, then winter inversions trap smoke and cold over the city while Crater Lake’s rim, an hour up, is a different planet of snow. Diurnal range is desert-like; irrigation makes the fields green against sage. The distinct feeling is not Oregon lushness. It is bright air, tule marsh, and a water budget that farmers, endangered fish, and the Klamath Tribes have been forced to share in public.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot, dry and bright — afternoons near 30°C, but in the thin, dry air nights fall to around 9°C, and frost is possible on the valley floor into June. Rain is rare, white pelicans glide over the lake, and the high basin enjoys some of the sunniest weather in Oregon. In bad fire years, smoke from the Cascades and northern California fills the basin.`},history:[`Through the winter of 1872–73, about fifty Modoc warriors and their families, led by a man named Kintpuash — called Captain Jack by the settlers — held off an army of hundreds of US soldiers for nearly six months from a natural fortress of lava trenches and caves south of Tule Lake. The Modoc had refused to stay on the Klamath reservation, where they had been forced to live alongside their old rivals, and tried to return to their homeland. In April 1873, during peace talks, Kintpuash killed General Edward Canby, the only US general killed in the Indian wars. The army brought in reinforcements, and the Modoc were eventually captured. Kintpuash and three others were hanged at Fort Klamath, north of the lake, that October.`,`Upper Klamath Lake and its marshes had been home to the Klamath, Modoc and Yahooskin peoples for thousands of years, a rich land of fish, waterfowl and the seeds of the wocus water lily. In 1864 a treaty created the Klamath Reservation, taking most of their land. In 1867 a settler named George Nurse founded a town on the short Link River between Upper Klamath Lake and Lake Ewauna, calling it Linkville; it became Klamath Falls in 1893. In 1905 the federal government began the Klamath Project, draining marshes and lakes to create farmland, and potatoes, grain and cattle became the basin's mainstays.`,`In the Second World War, Tule Lake, south of the city, became the site of one of the largest camps for Japanese Americans, holding more than 18,000 people at its peak. In 1954 Congress terminated the Klamath Tribes' federal recognition, and their vast forest lands were sold; recognition was restored in 1986. Water has been the basin's great battleground ever since: in 2001 irrigation water was cut off to protect endangered fish, setting off protests, and in 2002 tens of thousands of salmon died downriver. In 2023 and 2024, four hydroelectric dams on the Klamath River were removed — the largest dam removal project in history — to bring the salmon back.`],deepTitle:`Lava, lakes, salmon`,deep:[`The Modoc stronghold, now part of Lava Beds National Monument, is a maze of collapsed lava tubes and trenches that gave the defenders natural cover. The soldiers, unfamiliar with the terrain and fighting in fog and snow, suffered heavy losses in their first attacks. After the war, the surviving Modoc were exiled to Indian Territory in Oklahoma; some were allowed to return to the Klamath Reservation in 1909.`,`Klamath Falls sits on top of a geothermal field, and since the early twentieth century residents have tapped hot water from the ground to heat their homes. The city now runs a geothermal district heating system that warms downtown buildings and melts snow from its sidewalks, and the Oregon Institute of Technology heats its campus with geothermal water.`,`The Klamath basin is one of the most important stopovers for migrating birds on the Pacific Flyway. Millions of ducks and geese pass through each autumn, and the Lower Klamath National Wildlife Refuge, created in 1908 by Theodore Roosevelt, was the first waterfowl refuge in the United States. Bald eagles gather by the hundreds in winter. Draining much of the basin's marshes for farms has left the refuges short of water in dry years.`,`The dam removals on the Klamath River opened hundreds of kilometres of river to salmon for the first time in a century. Within months, Chinook salmon were swimming past the old dam sites into tributaries in Oregon. The Klamath Tribes, who had fought for decades to bring the salmon back, celebrated their return, and scientists are watching closely to see how the river recovers.`]},"los-alamos-pajarito-plateau-nm":{why:`The Pajarito Plateau is not a smooth highland. Volcanic tuff mesas, canyon slots, and the Jemez wall break the town into neighborhoods with different wind, frost, and fire edges. Elevation buys cool nights and occasional snow; it does not buy monsoon immunity or hydrant pressure on a rim. White Rock sits lower, warmer, and drier above the Rio Grande gorge. Santa Fe, close on a map, is a basin city, not a mesa-canyon fragment. Aspect and canyon drainage are the local climate, measured in streets rather than counties.`,immersive:`Morning light on the mesas is thin and exact. Ponderosa duff smells sharp after a cold night; by afternoon the same street can feel like high desert, sun-hard and windy at the rim. Canyons drop away so suddenly that a school run and a firebreak can share a cul-de-sac. Winter snow is real but not reliable, and it leaves fast on south rock. Monsoon cells build over the Jemez and then decide, sometimes in minutes, whether a canyon will run. The town is quiet in the way of badge gates and early bedtimes, with a sky that still looks like the caldera’s leftover.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, bright and thunderous — afternoons near 28°C in June, nights cooling to about 12°C in the thin mountain air, and from July the monsoon builds towering storms over the Jemez Mountains nearly every afternoon, with lightning, hail and brief, drenching rain. June, before the rains, is the most dangerous fire season. The ponderosa forests smell of warm pine, and the canyons offer shade and cool.`},history:[`In November 1942, a physicist named J. Robert Oppenheimer drove up a winding dirt road onto the Pajarito Plateau with an army general to inspect a remote boys' boarding school, the Los Alamos Ranch School. Oppenheimer had spent summers riding horses in the New Mexico mountains as a young man and knew the area. The army needed a secret place to build an atomic bomb, and this mesa, isolated, defensible and beautiful, was it. The school was closed, its students sent home, and within months thousands of scientists and their families moved into a town that officially did not exist; their mailing address was simply P.O. Box 1663, Santa Fe, and babies born there had that address on their birth certificates. On 16 July 1945, the bomb they built was tested in the desert to the south.`,`Long before that, Ancestral Pueblo people farmed the mesas and carved rooms into the soft volcanic rock of the canyon walls, as at Bandelier and Tsankawi; their descendants live today in the Tewa pueblos of San Ildefonso and Santa Clara in the valley below. In the late nineteenth century, Hispanic families from the Rio Grande valley built summer homesteads on the plateau to graze livestock and grow beans and corn. In 1917 a Detroit businessman named Ashley Pond founded the Los Alamos Ranch School, which taught wealthy boys from the East to ride, camp and study in the outdoors; among its students were the writers William S. Burroughs and Gore Vidal.`,`When the army took over in 1942, it bought out the Hispanic homesteaders at low prices, often with little notice; decades later, in 2004, their descendants won a settlement from the government. After the war, Los Alamos remained a closed city behind gates until 1957 and grew into one of the world's leading scientific laboratories. Fire has shaped its recent history: in 2000 a controlled burn in Bandelier National Monument escaped and swept into the town, destroying more than two hundred homes, and in 2011 the Las Conchas Fire burned much of the surrounding forest. Today Los Alamos has one of the highest concentrations of PhDs of any county in America, perched on a mesa that still feels remote.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Manhattan Project's laboratory at Los Alamos, known as Project Y, gathered many of the greatest scientists of the age, including Enrico Fermi, Niels Bohr, Hans Bethe and Richard Feynman, who amused himself by cracking the safes holding atomic secrets. Living conditions were primitive, water was always short, and the army watched everyone closely. The scientists' wives ran the schools, the hospital and the social life of the secret town.`,`Bandelier National Monument, south of Los Alamos, protects the ruins of a large pueblo in Frijoles Canyon and the cliff dwellings carved into the canyon walls, as well as thousands of other sites across the plateau. It is named after Adolph Bandelier, a Swiss-born scholar who was guided there by a Cochiti man in 1880 and wrote a novel set among its ruins. The ancient villages were abandoned by the mid-1500s, as the people moved down to the Rio Grande.`,`The Pajarito Plateau itself was formed by enormous volcanic eruptions about 1.6 and 1.2 million years ago, which blew out the Valles Caldera in the Jemez Mountains and buried the land under thick layers of volcanic ash that hardened into soft tuff. Streams carved the tuff into the finger-like mesas and deep canyons that give the plateau its shape. The caldera, a vast grassy bowl ringed by forested peaks, is now the Valles Caldera National Preserve.`,`The Cerro Grande Fire of May 2000 began as a prescribed burn intended to reduce fire risk. High winds drove it out of control, and it burned into Los Alamos, forcing the evacuation of the entire town and threatening the laboratory. The federal government paid compensation to the victims, and the disaster changed how prescribed fires are planned across the country.`]},"lander-sinks-canyon-wy":{why:`Lander sits where the Wind River foothills meet a dry basin, so the town is neither alpine nor true desert. Sinks Canyon wrings wind and offers a creek corridor; a few miles east the benches are sage and blow. Chinooks can raise winter afternoons, then drop them. Continentality still owns the nights. The particular feeling is a usable edge: mountain access without a resort town’s inversion of prices and snow-making, and enough canyon shelter that cottonwoods along the Popo Agie make a different July than Riverton’s open flats.`,immersive:`Lander is the kind of place people underestimate until they spend a shoulder season there. It is dry, bright, and windy enough to feel unmistakably Wyoming, but the Wind River foothills and Sinks Canyon give it a protected, lived-in edge. Summers are warm without much humidity and nights cool hard. Winters can be severe, then interrupted by downslope warmth that wrecks a snowpack and then returns the cold. Cottonwoods along the Popo Agie make a different July than Riverton's open flats some 35 km northeast, where sage and blow own the benches. The town is not mild; it is crisp, exposed, and useful for people who want mountain access without resort gloss. Wind still taxes that edge every week of the year.`,seasons:{summer:`Hot days and cool nights in thin, dry air — afternoons near 30°C in July, nights dropping to about 12°C, and very little rain except for the afternoon thunderstorms that build over the Wind River Range and sometimes spill down onto the town. Climbers crowd the limestone walls of Sinks Canyon, the river runs cold from the snowfields, and hikers head for the high lakes of the Wind Rivers.`},history:[`In 1894 a young cowboy who called himself George Cassidy stood trial in Lander for stealing a horse. He had a reputation as a charming rogue, and he had spent several years drifting around the ranches and outlaw hideouts of Wyoming. He was found guilty and sentenced to two years in the state penitentiary in Laramie. It was the only time he ever spent in prison. After his release in 1896, supposedly with a promise to the governor not to cause any more trouble in Wyoming, he became known as Butch Cassidy and formed the Wild Bunch, one of the most successful gangs of train and bank robbers in the history of the West.`,`The valley of the Popo Agie River, whose name comes from the Crow language, lies at the foot of the Wind River Range, long the homeland of the Eastern Shoshone. Their great leader, Chief Washakie, negotiated the 1868 treaty that established the Wind River Reservation, one of the few reservations in the West created in a tribe's own chosen homeland. A town grew up nearby, first called Push Root, later named for Frederick Lander, a surveyor who had laid out a wagon road through the region in the 1850s. Gold strikes at South Pass to the south brought miners, and in 1884 the first oil well in Wyoming was drilled near Lander.`,`In 1878 the government moved the Northern Arapaho, old enemies of the Shoshone, onto the Wind River Reservation, supposedly temporarily; they never left, and the two tribes still share it. The railroad finally reached Lander in 1906, and the town promoted itself as the place where the rails end and the trails begin. In 1965 the mountaineer Paul Petzoldt founded the National Outdoor Leadership School in Lander, and it has trained generations of outdoor leaders. Today the town is a hub for climbers, hunters and backpackers heading into the Wind Rivers.`],deepTitle:`Sinks and outlaws`,deep:[`In Sinks Canyon, just outside town, the Middle Fork of the Popo Agie River does something strange: it flows into a limestone cave called the Sinks and disappears. Less than half a kilometre downstream, it reappears in a calm, trout-filled pool called the Rise. When scientists poured dye into the Sinks, it took about two hours to reach the Rise, and more water comes out than goes in, suggesting that the underground river is joined by other streams along the way.`,`Chief Washakie led the Eastern Shoshone for more than half a century and was famous as a warrior and a diplomat who kept the peace with the United States. He died in 1900 and was buried at Fort Washakie with full military honours. Nearby, a grave is marked as that of Sacagawea; Shoshone tradition holds that she lived out her life at Wind River, though most historians believe she died in 1812 at a fur-trading post on the Missouri.`,`Paul Petzoldt was a legendary climber who had attempted K2, in the Karakoram, in 1938. He believed people should learn to travel safely in the wilderness and leave no trace of their passing, and the school he founded in Lander, NOLS, has trained hundreds of thousands of students on expeditions around the world. The town's climbing scene, focused on the limestone and granite walls nearby, grew partly out of that tradition.`,`Since 1940, Lander has hosted the One Shot Antelope Hunt, an event in which teams of celebrities, politicians and astronauts each try to bag a pronghorn with a single bullet. Those who miss must perform a comic penance at the banquet. The event has raised money for wildlife conservation and keeps alive the town's mix of western tradition and outdoor recreation.`]},"mentone-al":{why:`Mentone’s difference is a scarp. Lookout Mountain here sits some 280 m above Wills Valley, so lapse-rate cooling and orographic cloud on moist flow produce afternoons that Alabama’s lowlands do not get. Coves and the plateau cap hold frost in different pockets a short walk apart, which is why orchardists still map by aspect. Gulf humidity still arrives; it just arrives cooler. The village is a discrete mountain climate inside a state stereotyped as torrid, not a different region on the map.`,feel:`Mentone feels like a mountain hideaway the Deep South forgot. The little village sits on the brow of Lookout Mountain in northeastern Alabama, at about 550 m, among forests of oak, hickory and pine beside the clear, rocky Little River, which carves one of the deepest canyons in the eastern United States nearby. It is noticeably cooler than the valleys below: summers are warm but breezy, winters mild with occasional frost and snow, and fog often wraps the mountain. Summer camps, log cabins and antique shops line its quiet roads.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild with a mountain edge — afternoons near 10°C, nights just below freezing, and more frost and the odd dusting of snow than the valley below. Rain comes often, and fog can wrap the brow of the mountain for days. The waterfalls run full, and on clear days the views from the bluffs stretch for kilometres across bare forests.`,spring:`Fresh and flowering — afternoons warming from about 15°C in March to 26°C by late May, rhododendron and mountain laurel blooming along the river, and frequent showers and occasional strong storms. Little River and DeSoto Falls roar with spring rains.`,summer:`Warm but noticeably cooler than the valleys — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 18°C, and afternoon thunderstorms. The breeze along the brow of the mountain and the shade of the forests make it a refuge from the steamy lowlands of Alabama and Georgia, and summer camps fill with children.`,autumn:`Bright and colourful — September afternoons near 26°C cooling to about 15°C by November, crisp nights, and a blaze of red and gold foliage across the plateau by late October, one of the best displays in the Deep South.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for DeSoto State Park and its waterfalls, Little River Canyon, hang-gliding off the brow of Lookout Mountain, antique shops and cabins, and cooler summer air than the valleys below.`,contrasts:[{label:`Fort Payne (≈15 km SW, ≈300 m lower)`,note:`Down in Wills Valley, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter and nights stay muggier.`},{label:`Birmingham (≈130 km SW)`,note:`In the heart of Alabama, summers are longer, hotter and more humid, and winter frosts are less frequent.`}],history:[`In 1884 a woman named Alice Mason looked at the springs bubbling out of the sandstone on top of Lookout Mountain and gave the new resort town her family was building a name: Mentone, after the town of Menton on the French Riviera. That same year the Mentone Springs Hotel opened, a rambling wooden building where families from the steamy cities of the South came to drink the mineral water and breathe the cool mountain air. It stayed open, on and off, for 130 years until it burned down in 2014. By then the village had become something else entirely — a cluster of summer camps, cabins, churches and craft shops on the brow of the mountain.`,`The Cherokee lived in the valleys around Lookout Mountain until the 1830s. At Willstown, in the valley below, a silversmith named Sequoyah is believed to have worked out his system for writing the Cherokee language in the early 1820s, one of the very few times in history that one person created a writing system. In 1838 the Cherokee were rounded up at Fort Payne and other forts and forced west on the Trail of Tears. Local tradition holds that the Spanish explorer Hernando de Soto passed this way in 1540, and the waterfall just outside Mentone is named DeSoto Falls.`,`In the twentieth century Mentone became a place for summer camps; Camp Juliette Low, named for the founder of the Girl Scouts, opened in the 1920s, and several other camps followed. Cloudmont, a resort on the mountain, even opened Alabama's only ski slope, which relied on snowmaking. Below the mountain, the town of Fort Payne became known in the 1990s as the sock capital of the world, and was the hometown of the country band Alabama. Today Mentone's population is only a few hundred, but the plateau draws hikers, paddlers and leaf-peepers to DeSoto State Park and Little River Canyon.`],deepTitle:`Springs and Sequoyah`,deep:[`Sequoyah's syllabary had 85 characters, each representing a syllable in the Cherokee language. It was so easy to learn that within a few years thousands of Cherokee could read and write, and in 1828 the nation began publishing its own newspaper, the Cherokee Phoenix. Sequoyah moved west to Arkansas and later Oklahoma, and died in 1843 on a journey into Mexico. His invention is still used today.`,`Little River, which flows along the top of Lookout Mountain near Mentone, is unusual: it runs for most of its length on the mountaintop, then plunges into one of the deepest canyons in the eastern United States. The Little River Canyon National Preserve, established in 1992, protects the gorge, with its waterfalls, rare plants and swimming holes.`,`The Mentone Springs Hotel was one of the oldest resort hotels in Alabama. Over the years it served as a hotel, a boarding school and a bed-and-breakfast, and its wide porches and creaky floors were beloved by generations of visitors. Its loss in a fire in 2014 was mourned across the region.`,`The band Alabama, formed by cousins from Fort Payne in the 1970s, became one of the most successful country groups of all time, with dozens of number-one hits in the 1980s. They first played in local clubs and at tourist venues before hitting the big time. Their fan club and a museum in Fort Payne drew visitors for years.`]},"norfolk-ct":{why:`Norfolk is Connecticut’s high, inland cold pocket. Elevation, rural exposure, and bowl-like drainage pull minima well below the Hartford basin or the Sound. Lake-effect streamers from Ontario only occasionally reach this far, but when they do they add to snow that the hills already wring from coastal lows. Summers are short and mild. The Dfb label is earned by radiative nights and a late last frost, not by being in Vermont. Gardens and mud seasons run on a Berkshire clock inside a state known for milder suburbs.`,immersive:`Clear winter nights here feel scooped. Cold air slides into the hollows and stays, so a morning walk can start in crunching snow under hemlock while a south-facing stone wall is already dripping. Summers never quite commit to heat; hayfields and the village green stay a few degrees kinder than the Naugatuck valley. When a coastal storm climbs the Taconics, Norfolk’s station, long advertised as the state’s highest rail stop, still behaves like a mountain town. Spring is late. Lilacs and school calendars wait on it, and the Music Shed’s lawn is often cooler than the posters suggest.`,feel:`Norfolk feels like a corner of northern New England tucked into Connecticut. High in the hills of the Litchfield Highlands, it is colder and snowier than almost anywhere else in the state; cold air settles into its hollows on clear nights, frost comes early and leaves late, and the snow can stay on the ground for months. Summers are cool, green and fragrant with hemlock and pine, and the village green with its white churches and grand old houses looks much as it did a century ago.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and quiet — afternoons around freezing, nights near −9°C and far colder on still nights in the hollows, and more snow than almost anywhere else in Connecticut, often lingering on the ground from December into March. The village sees nor'easters and clipper storms, and cross-country skiers and snowshoers head into the woods.`,spring:`Late and muddy — afternoons warming from about 4°C in March to 19°C by late May, with snow possible into April and frosts into late May, weeks after the river valleys below have warmed. Maple sugaring fills March, and the woods burst into leaf and wildflowers in May.`,summer:`Mild and green — afternoons near 25°C, nights cooling to about 14°C, and the occasional thunderstorm. The cool evenings and shady woods make Norfolk a summer refuge, and the music festival fills the old Music Shed with concerts on summer nights.`,autumn:`Brilliant and crisp — September afternoons near 21°C, frost by late September in the hollows, and a blaze of maple, birch and oak colour by early October, often a week ahead of the rest of the state. The first snow can fall by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the summer chamber music of the Norfolk Music Festival, the village green and old churches, hiking and fall colour in the Litchfield Hills, and cross-country skiing in Connecticut's snowiest corner.`,contrasts:[{label:`Hartford (≈45 km SE, ≈400 m lower)`,note:`Down in the Connecticut River valley, summer afternoons run about 4°C hotter, and the city gets barely half of Norfolk's snow.`},{label:`Pittsfield, MA (≈50 km N)`,note:`Up in the Berkshires, Pittsfield shares Norfolk's cold winters and snow, with slightly cooler summers.`}],history:[`In June 1914, the Finnish composer Jean Sibelius, the most famous living composer in the world, sailed to America for the first and only time. He was coming not to New York or Boston but to Norfolk, a small hill town in northwest Connecticut, where a wealthy couple named Carl and Ellen Stoeckel had commissioned a new work from him for their music festival. On 4 June, in a cedar concert hall in the woods called the Music Shed, Sibelius conducted the premiere of his tone poem The Oceanides. The audience gave him a standing ovation, and Yale gave him an honorary doctorate. He remembered his American visit fondly for the rest of his life.`,`When Connecticut's colonial government offered land in these northwestern hills for sale in 1738, few buyers were interested in the rocky, swampy, hemlock-covered country they called the Green Woods. Settlers arrived slowly, and the town was incorporated in 1758 and named for the English county of Norfolk. Farmers found the soil better suited to grazing cattle and sheep than to crops, and the town grew into a community of dairy farms and small mills. In the nineteenth century the railroad brought summer visitors, and wealthy families from New York built grand houses around the village green.`,`The Battell family, who had made a fortune in business, shaped Norfolk's cultural life. Robbins Battell sponsored concerts in the 1880s, and his daughter Ellen and her husband Carl Stoeckel built the Music Shed in 1906 and turned their festival into one of the most remarkable in America, attracting Rachmaninoff, Fritz Kreisler and other stars. When Ellen died in 1939, her will left the estate to Yale University, and since 1941 the Norfolk Chamber Music Festival and Yale's summer school of music have kept the tradition alive. Today Norfolk is a quiet village of about 1,700 people.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Carl Stoeckel spared no expense on the Norfolk festival. He paid composers handsomely for new works, hired the best orchestral players from New York, and hosted his guests at his Whitehouse estate with lavish hospitality. Besides Sibelius, the composers who wrote for Norfolk included the British composer Samuel Coleridge-Taylor, who visited several times and was known as the African Mahler.`,`Norfolk's cold is partly a matter of altitude — the village sits at more than 400 m — and partly of geography. On clear, calm nights, cold air drains off the surrounding hills and collects in the low ground, so that Norfolk often records some of the lowest temperatures in Connecticut. The town's nickname is the Icebox of Connecticut.`,`Haystack Mountain, just north of the village, has a stone observation tower on its summit from which you can see three states on a clear day. Dennis Hill State Park, once the estate of a wealthy doctor, has a stone pavilion with similarly sweeping views. Both are reminders of the summer colony of the early twentieth century, when the wealthy built pleasure grounds on the hilltops.`,`Norfolk's library, a stone building donated by Isabella Eldridge in 1889, and the town's churches and houses around the green are preserved as a historic district. The village has resisted modern development, partly because it is so far from the main highways and partly because its residents like it that way.`]},"lewes-de":{why:`Lewes sits at the mouth of a huge tidal bay, so the day’s wind is an estuary project: onshore when the land heats, offshore at night, and then the occasional nor’easter that treats Cape Henlopen’s dunes as movable scenery. It is flatter than any Piedmont town and more marine than inland Delmarva. Heat is capped compared with Dover; humidity is not. Surge and king-tide flooding are the price of that moderation, written into streets that were laid out for pilots, not for a higher ocean.`,immersive:`Salt marsh edges Lewes the way a yard edges a house. On warm afternoons the bay breeze arrives smelling like mudflat and channel, knocking a few degrees off the brick streets while inland Sussex still shimmers. Nights can go slack and wet. In winter a nor’easter rearranges the Henlopen dunes and leaves the ferry basin pacing. Spring comes earlier than in the Pennsylvania hills, with holly and loblolly holding the maritime forest against the wind. The town is walkable and low. Tide shows in storm drains before it shows as a pretty line at the beach.`,seasons:{winter:`Raw, damp and changeable — afternoons near 9°C, nights just below freezing, and nor'easters that drive wind, rain and high tides across the beaches and marshes. Snow comes a few times a winter but rarely lasts. The town is quiet, the ferry runs on a winter schedule, and seabirds gather in huge flocks at the mouth of the bay.`,spring:`Cool, breezy and slowly warming — afternoons rising from about 13°C in March to 23°C by late May, with chilly sea breezes off the bay that keep the beach colder than inland towns. Horseshoe crabs crowd the bay beaches to spawn in May, drawing migrating shorebirds by the thousands to feast on their eggs.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 19°C, and sea breezes off the bay and the ocean that keep the beaches cooler than inland Delaware. Humidity is high, and afternoon thunderstorms occasionally roll through. The beaches at Cape Henlopen fill with swimmers, and the historic streets are busy with visitors.`,autumn:`Mild and golden — September afternoons near 26°C with the ocean still warm, easing to about 15°C by November. The crowds leave after Labor Day, migrating monarchs and hawks pass along the coast, and the occasional hurricane or nor'easter churns the bay.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Cape Henlopen State Park and its dunes and bunkers, quiet bay beaches, the ferry across the bay to Cape May, historic streets settled by the Dutch in 1631, and horseshoe crabs and shorebirds in May.`,residentFit:`It suits retirees, remote workers and families who want a walkable historic beach town with mild winters, and who can accept summer crowds, rising prices and the risk of coastal storms and flooding.`,contrasts:[{label:`Dover (≈60 km NW, inland)`,note:`Away from the sea breezes, Delaware's capital has hotter summer afternoons and colder winter nights.`},{label:`Cape May, NJ (≈27 km across the bay)`,note:`On the other side of the bay mouth, Cape May has a similar maritime climate, a little cooler in summer thanks to the ocean on two sides.`}],history:[`In 1631 a group of Dutch investors sent 32 men to build a whaling station on Delaware Bay, at a place they named Zwaanendael, valley of swans. The colonists put up a post bearing a piece of tin painted with the arms of the Dutch Republic, as a sign of their claim. According to the account of the Dutch explorer David de Vries, a Lenape man took the shiny tin to make a tobacco pipe. When the Dutch complained, his own people killed him to satisfy them. His friends took revenge, and when de Vries arrived the following year, he found the settlement burned and all its men dead, their bones scattered among the remains of their cattle. A Dutch-style museum built in 1931 for the anniversary now stands near the spot.`,`The Lenape had fished and hunted along the bay for centuries. In 1663 a Mennonite community led by Pieter Plockhoy settled at the same place, but it was destroyed a year later when the English seized New Netherland. In 1682 William Penn took control of the area and named the town Lewes, after a town in Sussex, England. Lewes became a home for the pilots who guided ships through the treacherous shoals of Delaware Bay to Philadelphia, and its residents proudly call it the First Town in the First State, since Delaware was the first state to ratify the Constitution.`,`In the War of 1812, a British squadron blockading Delaware Bay demanded food and water from Lewes. When the town refused, the ships bombarded it for about twenty-two hours on 6 and 7 April 1813. Hundreds of cannonballs were fired, but the only casualties were said to be a chicken killed and a pig wounded, and a cannonball still sticks out of the wall of one house. In 1964 a ferry began running from Lewes to Cape May across the mouth of the bay, and during the Second World War, Fort Miles on Cape Henlopen guarded the entrance with huge guns. Today Lewes is a charming historic town and a favourite of retirees.`],deepTitle:`Pilots and cannon`,deep:[`The bombardment of Lewes inspired local verse. One rhyme mocking the British commander ran that he and all his men had shot a dog and killed a hen. The town's defenders, including militia and local volunteers, had reportedly collected the British cannonballs and fired them back. The Cannonball House, with a ball still lodged in its foundation, is now a maritime museum.`,`The Delaware Breakwater, built between 1828 and 1869 to shelter ships waiting for pilots or riding out storms at the mouth of the bay, was one of the first structures of its kind in the Western Hemisphere. Lewes's pilots, who had to know every shoal and current, were an elite and close-knit group, and their families shaped the town's history.`,`In 1798 the British warship DeBraak sank in a sudden squall off Cape Henlopen, and legends of Spanish gold in its hold drew treasure hunters for two centuries. When the hull was finally raised in 1986, it held no great treasure, but thousands of artefacts that have taught historians much about life aboard an eighteenth-century warship.`,`During the Second World War, Fort Miles was built on Cape Henlopen to defend the entrance to Delaware Bay from German attack. Concrete observation towers were built along the coast, and several still stand on the beaches. In 1945 a German U-boat surrendered to the fort's forces at sea. Cape Henlopen State Park now preserves the fort, the dunes and the beaches.`]},"clayton-ga":{why:`Clayton sits on the Blue Ridge escarpment, so moist southern flow has to rise, cool, and often rain before it ever reaches Atlanta’s heat island. Elevation near 590 m knocks July peaks out of the Deep South’s low-30s habit. North coves stay shockingly cool; south ridges take sun enough for experimental vines. It is still humid. It is not Atlanta. That latitude–elevation decoupling is the whole local climate, audible in waterfall gorges and visible in the cloud cap on Rabun Bald.`,immersive:`Summer afternoons in Clayton often look like a storm is being assembled on purpose. Cloud builds on the escarpment, the air smells of wet rock and cove hardwood, and a shower can drop the temperature while the Piedmont stays white-hot an hour south. Mornings in north-facing hollows carry a chill that feels misplaced until the elevation is noticed. Winters are brief but real, with icing on 441 grades. The town is a crossroads: log trucks, leaf-season traffic, and a courthouse square that still behaves like a county seat rather than a theme village.`,feel:`Clayton feels like the Deep South's mountain hideaway. High on the Blue Ridge in the northeast corner of Georgia, it gets cooler summers than the rest of the state, frequent rain from storms forced up the mountains, and the occasional real snow. Forests of oak, hickory and rhododendron cover the steep slopes, wild rivers rush through the gorges, and the old-time mountain culture of the Appalachians runs deep.`,seasons:{winter:`Cool, wet and sometimes snowy — afternoons near 11°C, nights just below freezing, and frequent rain from storms pushing up the mountains, with a few snowfalls a year that can close the steep roads. On clear days the views from the ridges stretch for many kilometres through the bare trees.`,spring:`Lush and rainy — afternoons warming from about 16°C in March to 25°C by late May, with dogwoods, rhododendrons and flame azaleas blooming in waves up the mountainsides. Rivers run high and the waterfalls are at their best.`,summer:`Warm but tempered — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 17°C, and almost daily afternoon thunderstorms. The mountains offer relief from the heat of Atlanta, and rafters and kayakers head for the Chattooga River.`,autumn:`Glorious — September afternoons near 26°C easing to about 16°C by November, crisp nights, and a riot of red, orange and gold leaves on the mountains by late October. Apples and pumpkins fill the roadside stands.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the waterfalls and trails of the Blue Ridge, the Chattooga River, where Deliverance was filmed, Tallulah Gorge, apple orchards and the cooler mountain summers of Rabun County.`,contrasts:[{label:`Highlands, NC (≈20 km NE, ≈650 m higher)`,note:`Up on the plateau, summer afternoons run about 4°C cooler, and the rainfall is among the highest in the eastern United States.`},{label:`Atlanta (≈150 km SW, ≈300 m lower)`,note:`Down on the Piedmont, summer afternoons run several degrees hotter and muggier, and snow is rare.`}],history:[`On 18 July 1970, the high-wire artist Karl Wallenda, sixty-five years old, stepped onto a cable some 300 m long stretched across Tallulah Gorge, just south of Clayton. About 210 m below him, the river roared over the rocks. Some thirty thousand people watched as he walked across, stopping twice in the middle to stand on his head. It took him about twenty minutes. Wallenda had been performing such stunts for decades, and the gorge was one of his most famous. Eight years later, at seventy-three, he fell to his death from a wire in Puerto Rico. Tallulah Gorge, once called the Niagara of the South for its waterfalls, is now a state park.`,`Five Cherokee trails met at the place where Clayton now stands, a crossroads in the mountains surrounded by Cherokee towns along the creeks. The naturalist William Bartram walked through in 1775, describing the plants and the people. Georgia took the land in an 1819 treaty with the Cherokee, created Rabun County, and gave the land away by lottery to settlers, who farmed the narrow valleys and raised cattle and hogs. In 1882 a railroad reached Tallulah Falls, and trainloads of tourists from Atlanta came to see the gorge. In 1913 the Georgia Railway and Power Company built a dam at the top of the falls, despite a long campaign to save them led by Helen Dortch Longstreet, the widow of the Confederate general James Longstreet.`,`In 1966 a young English teacher named Eliot Wigginton at the Rabun Gap–Nacoochee School, near Clayton, had his students interview elderly mountain people about their crafts, skills and stories, and publish their findings in a magazine they called Foxfire. The project became a series of bestselling books that preserved the knowledge of Appalachian life, from making soap to building log cabins. A few years later, the film Deliverance was shot on the Chattooga River and in Tallulah Gorge, bringing fame — and some unwanted stereotypes — to Rabun County.`],deepTitle:`Foxfire country`,deep:[`The Foxfire books, beginning with The Foxfire Book in 1972, sold millions of copies and inspired a national movement of students recording local history. The Foxfire Museum and Heritage Center near Clayton preserves log cabins, tools and crafts collected by the students. The project's founder later left after a scandal, but the books remain a treasured record of Appalachian folk life.`,`Deliverance, released in 1972, starred Burt Reynolds and Jon Voight as city men whose canoe trip down a wild river turns deadly. The film made the Chattooga famous, and thousands of rafters and canoeists flocked to the river, some unprepared for its rapids; several drowned in the following years. Today the Chattooga is protected as a National Wild and Scenic River, and commercial rafting trips run its rapids every summer.`,`Helen Dortch Longstreet, who married the elderly General Longstreet in 1897, was a pioneering conservationist. She fought for years in the courts and the legislature to stop the Tallulah Falls dam and preserve the falls, which she considered one of the natural wonders of the South. She lost, but her campaign is remembered as one of the first major conservation battles in Georgia.`,`Black Rock Mountain State Park, just north of Clayton, is the highest state park in Georgia, with its summit at about 1,100 m. Its overlooks have views of four states on clear days. The Appalachian Trail passes through Rabun County, and the Bartram Trail follows the route of the eighteenth-century naturalist through the forests.`]},"galena-il":{why:`Galena’s hills are unglaciated Driftless relief inside a glaciated state, so cold air drains, south slopes green early, and a few miles can change frost. The Mississippi adds winter cloud and a little thermal mass without making this a lake town. Lead-bearing limestone is the geologic coincidence that became a city. Flat Illinois corn country cannot organize wind and phenology this way. Dubuque, across the river, shares the Driftless; the prairie counties east do not. The postcard stairs are a climate machine: climbing a side street is a lesson in aspect, one garden in tulips, the next still brown.`,immersive:`Galena’s streets dive into a ravine like a river that forgot to stay water. On still April nights the bottoms frost while brick storefronts halfway up the bluff stay just above. Summer thunder echoes off limestone. In winter the Galena River, once called Fever River, can steam under a Mississippi overcast that feels imported from Minnesota. Climbing a side street is a lesson in aspect: one garden in tulips, the next still brown. The town is small enough that church bells and tour trolleys share the same canyon air, and humid enough that August still belongs to the Midwest, not to a mountain fantasy.`,feel:`Galena feels like a nineteenth-century boomtown preserved in amber. Its main street of red-brick and limestone storefronts curves along the Galena River beneath steep, wooded bluffs, with church spires and mansions climbing the hillsides, in a corner of Illinois that the glaciers of the Ice Age never flattened. The climate is fully continental: winters are cold and snowy, with frigid air pooling in the valley, summers warm, humid and stormy, and spring and autumn short, colourful seasons.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and quiet — afternoons around freezing, nights near −10°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −25°C, with cold air pooling in the narrow valley at the foot of the bluffs. Snow comes in moderate amounts, the brick streets and steep stairways turn icy, and the Mississippi freezes along its edges. Visitors come for cosy inns and Christmas walks.`,spring:`Late and showery — afternoons rising from about 8°C in March to 22°C by late May, frost lingering into April, and rain becoming frequent as thunderstorms return in May. Redbuds and wildflowers bloom on the wooded bluffs, and the farm valleys green up.`,summer:`Warm, humid and green — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 16°C, and thunderstorms that roll across the bluffs, sometimes with flash floods on the Galena River. The wooded hills and farm valleys are lush, and the town's Main Street fills with visitors.`,autumn:`Crisp and colourful — September afternoons near 24°C easing to about 8°C by November, cool nights, and the wooded bluffs turning red, orange and gold in mid-October, when visitors crowd the town for the colour and the harvest.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the remarkably preserved nineteenth-century main street, Ulysses S. Grant's home, the bluffs of the Mississippi Palisades, autumn colour and cosy inns in a corner of Illinois that looks nothing like the flat Midwest.`,contrasts:[{label:`Dubuque, IA (≈25 km NW, across the Mississippi)`,note:`The river city across the water has a nearly identical climate, though its broader valley floor doesn't trap cold air quite as sharply.`},{label:`Chicago (≈230 km E)`,note:`On Lake Michigan's shore, Chicago's summer afternoons are often cooled by lake breezes, and its winters are a touch milder.`}],history:[`In the spring of 1860, a 38-year-old army veteran with a drinking reputation and a string of failed businesses behind him moved his family to Galena to work as a clerk in his father's leather goods store. His name was Ulysses S. Grant, and the townspeople knew him mainly as the quiet man in the faded army coat who sold saddles. A year later, when the Civil War began, Grant helped drill Galena's volunteers and went off to rejoin the army. Four years later he returned as the general who had won the war. In August 1865 the grateful citizens of Galena presented him with a furnished brick house on a hill above the town. He went on to become president, and the house still stands, filled with his family's possessions.`,`Galena is named after the lead ore, galena, that lies close to the surface in the hills around it. The Ho-Chunk, Sauk and Meskwaki mined it for generations, using it for trade and later for bullets. In the 1820s, after the federal government began leasing mining land, thousands of miners rushed into the district, and by the 1840s it produced most of the lead in the United States. Galena became the busiest port on the upper Mississippi, with steamboats crowding its river and wealthy merchants building mansions on the bluffs. The Black Hawk War of 1832, fought nearby, drove the remaining Sauk and Meskwaki from the region.`,`By the late nineteenth century, the lead mines were played out, the Galena River had silted up so badly that steamboats could no longer reach the town, and the railroads had made Chicago the region's capital. Galena stopped growing. Because there was no money to tear down the old buildings and replace them with new ones, the town's nineteenth-century brick downtown survived almost untouched. Beginning in the 1960s, artists and tourists rediscovered it, and today nearly all of Galena is a historic district, its streets lined with shops, inns and restaurants.`],deepTitle:`Lead and Grant`,deep:[`Galena's lead rush began before the gold rushes of the West. Miners dug pits and short shafts called badger holes into the hillsides, and the region's people were nicknamed badgers; Wisconsin, just to the north, is still the Badger State. By 1845 the district produced tens of millions of kilograms of lead a year, much of it shipped down the Mississippi to New Orleans.`,`Galena claims that nine Civil War generals came from the town, including Grant and his chief of staff, John Rawlins, and Ely Parker, a Seneca man from New York who had worked in Galena as an engineer and became Grant's military secretary. Parker wrote out the final copy of the surrender terms at Appomattox in 1865. He later became the first Native American Commissioner of Indian Affairs.`,`The Galena River, which gave the town its harbour, also brought floods. As the surrounding land was cleared for farming and mining, the river silted up and flooded the town repeatedly. In 1951 the town built a levee and floodgates, which are closed when the river rises, and Main Street has stayed dry since.`,`Galena lies in the Driftless Area, a region of hills, bluffs and valleys that was never covered by the ice sheets of the last ice ages. Its rugged landscape is unusual in Illinois, which is mostly flat, and it gives Galena a look more like New England or the hills of southern Wisconsin than the prairie state around it.`]},"beverly-shores-in":{why:`Southern Lake Michigan is a thermal battery. Beverly Shores, on the Indiana shore, gets delayed springs, lake-breeze June, and winter cloud when cold air fetches across open water. Dune and swale stack those effects in a few hundred metres: hot sand, cold wetland, oak shade. It is not Chicago’s heat island and not Michigan’s snowbelt maximum, but it is enough lake to grow boreal relicts and rare orchids beside a Dfa latitude. The train is how people use that microclimate as a suburb.`,feel:`Beverly Shores feels like a quiet beach village hidden in the dunes, an hour from downtown Chicago. Tall dunes of golden sand and oak forests separate the village from the vast blue of Lake Michigan, which cools the summer, softens the autumn and, in winter, sends bands of lake-effect snow drifting over the dunes. The Chicago skyline shimmers on the horizon across the water on clear days.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey and snowy — afternoons just above freezing, nights near −6°C, and lake-effect snow bands that roll in off Lake Michigan when cold northwest winds blow across the open water, sometimes burying the village while towns a few kilometres inland stay clear. The beach turns into a landscape of ice shelves and frozen spray.`,spring:`Cool and slow — afternoons warming from about 6°C in March to 20°C by late May, but a breeze off the cold lake can make the shore much chillier than inland. Migrating birds stream along the lakeshore, wildflowers bloom in the dune forests, and the last ice melts off the lake.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 17°C, and lake breezes that keep the beach cooler than the inland towns. The lake warms enough for swimming by July, and the beaches fill with visitors from Chicago. Thunderstorms roll across the lake on humid afternoons.`,autumn:`Mild and colourful — the lake holds its warmth, so September afternoons stay near 24°C, and the oak and maple forests turn red and gold by October. By November, cold winds bring the first lake-effect rain and snow.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Indiana Dunes National Park, Mount Baldy and the beaches of Lake Michigan, the Century of Progress houses moved here from the 1933 Chicago World's Fair, and spring and autumn bird migrations.`,contrasts:[{label:`Chicago (≈70 km W)`,note:`Across the lake's southern tip, the city is a little warmer at night thanks to the urban heat island, and usually misses the heaviest lake-effect snow.`},{label:`Valparaiso (≈25 km S, inland)`,note:`Away from the lake, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter and winter brings less snow.`}],history:[`In 1935 five houses from the Century of Progress world's fair in Chicago were moved to Beverly Shores, four of them on barges floated across Lake Michigan, where a developer named Robert Bartlett hoped they would help sell building lots in his new resort. The houses had been designed to show off the future of American living: one was built of a new artificial stone, one had walls of steel panels, one was a Florida-style tropical house painted pink. The most famous, the House of Tomorrow, was a twelve-sided glass house with a garage for the family car and a hangar for the family airplane. The houses still stand along the lakeshore, restored as historic landmarks.`,`The Potawatomi lived along the southern shore of Lake Michigan until treaties in the 1830s forced them west. The dunes and swamps remained wild for much of the nineteenth century, though in 1896 the aviation pioneer Octave Chanute tested gliders among the dunes, years before the Wright brothers flew. A few years later, a University of Chicago botanist named Henry Cowles studied how plants colonised the dunes, from bare sand to forest, and his work helped found the science of ecology in America. In 1927 Frederick Bartlett, a Chicago developer, bought about 1,500 hectares here and planned a grand resort; the Depression wrecked his plans, and only a fraction of the lots were ever built on.`,`In the twentieth century, steel mills spread along the Indiana shore, and conservationists fought for decades to save the remaining dunes from industry. Led by activists such as Dorothy Buell and Senator Paul Douglas, the campaign won the creation of the Indiana Dunes National Lakeshore in 1966, which surrounded Beverly Shores. In 2019 it became Indiana Dunes National Park. Today Beverly Shores is a village of a few hundred people, linked to Chicago by the South Shore commuter railway, and its residents share their streets with hikers and birdwatchers.`],deepTitle:`Houses of Tomorrow`,deep:[`The House of Tomorrow, designed by the architect George Fred Keck for the 1933 fair, was one of the first houses in America to use large walls of glass, and it was so sunny that it overheated, inspiring Keck to experiment with passive solar design. It also featured central air conditioning and an automatic dishwasher, innovations at the time. After years of neglect, it is being restored.`,`Henry Cowles's studies of the Indiana Dunes, published in 1899, showed how the plants on a dune change over time, from grasses on bare sand to shrubs and finally to oak and maple forests. The idea of ecological succession, which he helped develop, became one of the foundations of modern ecology, and the dunes became known as the birthplace of the science in America.`,`Mount Baldy, a giant dune near Michigan City, moves inland a few metres a year as the wind blows sand over its crest. In 2013 a six-year-old boy fell into a hidden hole in the dune and was buried under several metres of sand for more than three hours before rescuers dug him out alive. Scientists later found that the holes were caused by buried trees rotting away inside the dune, and much of the dune has been closed to visitors.`,`The dunes of Indiana are one of the most biologically diverse places in the Midwest, with more than 1,100 species of plants, including arctic bearberry and prickly pear cactus growing within a short walk of each other. The park's beaches are also a major stopover for migrating birds, and in spring and autumn thousands of hawks, warblers and waterfowl pass along the shore.`]},"oakland-md":{why:`Garrett County is Maryland’s roof. Oakland sits at 750 m on a plateau that catches Great Lakes moisture on northwest flow and pools cold in glade valleys. Baltimore’s winter is a rumor here. Deep Creek Lake adds local humidity and a breeze, not a true marine climate. Summer is the Mid-Atlantic escape hatch: mild enough to sell lake houses. Spring is late for growers. Snow and frost, not humidity, are the identity that the rest of the state underestimates.`,immersive:`Oakland smells like wet hay and coal-train iron on a still evening. In January the plateau can look Adirondack: hemlock, barn roofs, and a sky that means business. Deep Creek’s water stays open later than the fields, throwing a little steam toward Wisp’s slopes. Summer nights drop enough that blankets make sense after a day that never quite reached the 30s. Fog fills the Youghiogheny glades at dawn, then burns to a hard highland light. The county seat is small, brick, and windy at the corners, with a railroad station that still seems to be waiting for the B&O hotel crowd.`,feel:`Oakland feels like a mountain refuge on the roof of Maryland. The little county seat, with its Queen Anne-style railway station and Victorian houses, sits at about 750 m on a high plateau of the Allegheny Mountains, among hay meadows, Amish farms and forests near the shores of Deep Creek Lake. It is far colder and snowier than the rest of Maryland: winters are long and white, summers cool and green, with refreshing nights that once drew wealthy families escaping the heat of Washington and Baltimore.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and properly mountainous — afternoons near 3°C, nights near −7°C, and enough snow for a ski resort, with lake-effect bands from Lake Erie occasionally reaching this far and upslope storms piling more on the plateau. Cold air pools in the valleys on calm nights, and the county regularly records Maryland's lowest temperatures. Deep Creek Lake freezes hard enough for ice fishing.`,spring:`Late and muddy — afternoons warming from about 8°C in March to 21°C by late May, with snow possible into April and frost into May. The hay meadows green up slowly, wildflowers bloom in the forests, and the waterfalls at Swallow Falls are at their fullest.`,summer:`Cool for Maryland — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 14°C, and refreshing evenings that have drawn visitors from the hot cities since the 1800s. Thunderstorms build over the mountains in the afternoons, and Deep Creek Lake fills with boaters and swimmers.`,autumn:`Early and brilliant — September afternoons near 22°C cooling to about 10°C by November, frost arriving by early October, and the forests and meadows turning red and gold weeks before the lowlands. Showers come often, and the first snow can fall in late October.`},residentFit:`It suits families, skiers and lake-house owners who want real snowy winters and cool summers in the mountains, and who can manage long, icy drives, a small-town economy and the occasional heavy storm.`,contrasts:[{label:`Cumberland (≈60 km NE, ≈550 m lower)`,note:`Down in the Potomac valley, summer afternoons run about 5°C hotter, and the town gets a fraction of Oakland's snow.`},{label:`Deep Creek Lake & Wisp (≈15 km N, ≈150 m higher)`,note:`Around the lake and the ski resort, winters are snowier still, and the lake moderates temperatures slightly.`}],history:[`On 2 June 1886, President Grover Cleveland married 21-year-old Frances Folsom in the Blue Room of the White House — the only president ever to marry in the executive mansion. The newlyweds slipped away for their honeymoon to Deer Park, a resort in the cool mountains of western Maryland, just outside Oakland, where the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad had built a grand hotel to lure wealthy city dwellers into the hills. Reporters followed them, hiding in the bushes and watching the cottage through binoculars, much to the president's irritation. Cleveland was neither the first nor the last president to vacation in the area: Grant, Benjamin Harrison and McKinley also came to escape the summer heat.`,`The high valley where Oakland stands was known as the Glades, a land of meadows and forests used for hunting by Native peoples and later settled by farmers in the early 1800s. In 1849 the land was laid out for a town in anticipation of the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad, which reached it in 1851 after a difficult climb over the Allegheny Front. The town was named Oakland for its oak trees. When Maryland created Garrett County in 1872, it was named after the railroad's president, John W. Garrett, and Oakland became its seat.`,`The railroad turned Oakland into a summer resort, and grand hotels sprang up near the station. The resort era faded in the early twentieth century, and the big hotels were torn down. In 1925 a power company dammed Deep Creek, creating Deep Creek Lake, now the largest lake in Maryland and the centre of a resort area of vacation homes, boating and skiing. Oakland itself remains a small county seat with a restored 1884 railroad station, a reminder of the days when presidents came to the mountains for their health.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Oakland station, built by the B&O in 1884 in the Queen Anne style, with its curved bell-shaped tower, is one of the oldest surviving railroad stations in the country. It served passengers until the 1970s and is now a museum. The trains still rumble past on the tracks behind it.`,`In 1918 and again in 1921, three of the most famous men in America — Henry Ford, Thomas Edison and Harvey Firestone — camped at Swallow Falls, near Oakland, on one of their annual road trips, which they called the Vagabonds' tours. They slept in tents and posed for photographers around the campfire. Swallow Falls State Park, with its waterfalls and old-growth hemlocks, is now one of the most popular parks in Maryland.`,`Deep Creek Lake was created by the Youghiogheny Hydro-Electric Company to generate electricity. The lake, about 20 km long, drowned farms and valleys, and the shoreline was gradually developed with vacation homes. Wisp, a ski resort on the lake's edge, opened in 1955 and is the only ski area in Maryland.`,`Garrett County is the snowiest county in Maryland, with some areas averaging well over 2 m of snow a year. The cold has made it a destination for winter sports, and for summer visitors escaping the heat of Baltimore and Washington, who have been coming to these mountains for more than 150 years.`]},"pittsfield-ma":{why:`Pittsfield sits in a Housatonic valley between Taconic and Berkshire slopes, inland enough to lose Boston’s marine bubble. Winter cold pools in the valley; summer storms follow fronts along the same trenches. Lake-enhanced snow on northwest flow is occasional, not Buffalo-grade. Seasonality is the point: mud, peak color, a real freeze. Cape Cod is the same state and a different climate. Elevation plus storm-track position, not a single mountain, make the difference.`,immersive:`Pittsfield’s downtown brick holds winter longer than it should. Wind slides off Greylock and down the Housatonic, and a January night in the valley can feel scooped out, dry-cold and still. Summer thunderstorms announce themselves on the western ridges before they hit North Street. In September the same ridges go bronze while the mills’ ghost parking lots radiate leftover heat. Tanglewood is a short drive into a greener town; coming home is a return to a small city that still smells faintly of industry on wet days. Mud season is not a joke. Neither is the first honest snow.`,feel:`Pittsfield feels like a working town in the hills of a postcard. The Berkshires roll around it in forested waves, with Mount Greylock, the highest peak in Massachusetts, filling the northern skyline. Far from the ocean's influence, it has real New England winters, with deep snow and sharp cold, and summers that are mild and green, cooled by mountain evenings. Autumn sets the hills on fire with colour, and the region's music, theatre and literary history give it a cultural life far beyond its size.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and long — afternoons near 1°C, nights near −9°C, and more than a metre of snow over the season, from nor'easters and clipper storms. Cold air settles into the valleys on clear nights, and the ski slopes of the Berkshires fill on weekends. Snow often stays on the ground from December to March.`,spring:`Late and muddy — afternoons warming from about 4°C in March to 20°C by late May, with snow possible into April and a muddy thaw. Maple sugaring fills March, and the hills turn green in May, weeks after eastern Massachusetts.`,summer:`Mild and pleasant — afternoons near 26°C, nights cooling to about 13°C, and the occasional thunderstorm. Cool evenings make it the season of outdoor music at Tanglewood, dance at Jacob's Pillow and theatre across the region, and the lakes fill with swimmers and paddlers.`,autumn:`The great show — September afternoons near 21°C, crisp nights with early frosts, and the hills ablaze with red and orange maple leaves by early October. Apples ripen in the orchards, and the first snow falls on Greylock by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Tanglewood and the summer music, dance and theatre of the Berkshires, Hancock Shaker Village, the hiking and fall colour of the Taconic hills and skiing at Jiminy Peak and Bousquet.`,residentFit:`It suits culture lovers, second-home owners and families priced out of Boston who want New England seasons, the Berkshires' arts scene and lower housing costs, and who accept long, snowy winters.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mount Greylock (≈20 km N, ≈750 m higher)`,note:`The highest point in Massachusetts runs about 5°C cooler, wears rime and snow through much of the winter, and catches clouds that pass over the city.`},{label:`Albany, NY (≈55 km W, in the Hudson valley)`,note:`Down in the Hudson valley, summers run a couple of degrees warmer and winters bring somewhat less snow.`}],history:[`On 5 August 1850, a group of writers went on a picnic hike up Monument Mountain, south of Pittsfield. Among them were Nathaniel Hawthorne, the celebrated author of The Scarlet Letter, and a younger writer named Herman Melville, who was summering in Pittsfield and would buy a farm there six weeks later. A thunderstorm drove them into a rock shelter, where they drank champagne and talked. Melville was smitten with Hawthorne's genius, and over the next year he wrote his masterpiece at the farm, which he called Arrowhead. From his study window he could see Mount Greylock, whose long, humped profile, it is often said, reminded him of a great whale. The book, Moby-Dick, published in 1851, was dedicated to Hawthorne. It sold poorly, and it took decades for the world to recognise it as one of the greatest American novels.`,`The Mohican people lived along the Housatonic River, calling the place Pontoosuc, a haven where deer wintered. After the colonial wars, settlers moved in, and the town incorporated in 1761, named for the British statesman William Pitt. A town bylaw of 1791, forbidding anyone to play baseball within 80 yards of the new meeting house to protect its windows, is the earliest known written reference to the game in America. In the nineteenth century Pittsfield became a mill town, making woollen cloth from the fleece of merino sheep, and Shakers at nearby Hancock built a famous round stone barn in 1826.`,`In 1886 an engineer named William Stanley used his new electrical transformer to light the streets of Great Barrington, south of Pittsfield, a key step toward the alternating-current power systems used around the world today. He soon set up a factory in Pittsfield, which General Electric took over in 1903. GE's plant became the city's biggest employer, making transformers, and later plastics and weapons systems. When GE wound down its operations from the 1980s, Pittsfield lost thousands of jobs and was left with contamination from chemicals called PCBs in the Housatonic River. Today the city is reinventing itself as an arts and services centre for the Berkshires.`],deepTitle:`Melville's whale`,deep:[`Melville lived at Arrowhead from 1850 to 1863, writing Moby-Dick, Pierre, The Piazza Tales and other works there while running the farm. He struggled to support his family on his writing and eventually moved back to New York, where he worked as a customs inspector for nearly twenty years. Arrowhead is now a museum, and visitors can stand in his study and look out at Greylock.`,`The Hancock Shaker Village, a few kilometres west of Pittsfield, was home to a community of the Shakers, a religious sect known for their celibacy, simple living and beautifully crafted furniture. Their round stone barn allowed one farmer standing in the centre to feed dozens of cows arranged in a circle. The village was active from about 1790 to 1960 and is now a museum.`,`Pittsfield's 1791 baseball bylaw was found in 2004 by a historian searching the town archives. Its discovery made headlines, since it predated the game's supposed invention by Abner Doubleday in Cooperstown in 1839 by nearly half a century. Pittsfield's minor-league ballpark, Wahconah Park, built in 1919, is famous for facing west, so that games are sometimes halted while the setting sun blinds the batters.`,`General Electric's legacy in Pittsfield is mixed. For decades the company provided good jobs and made the city prosperous; at its peak, GE employed about thirteen thousand people in Pittsfield. But it also dumped PCBs, chemicals used in transformers, into the Housatonic and into landfills around the city. A major cleanup has been underway for years, and the city and region have been working to rebuild a more diverse economy.`]},"ocean-springs-ms":{why:`Mississippi Sound is shallow, so Ocean Springs gets marine moderation without open-Gulf fetch. Afternoon peaks run a little cooler than Hattiesburg; frost is rarer than thirty kilometres inland. Live-oak canopy knits humidity into every summer night. Winter cold fronts are brief and often undone by returning onshore air. The trade is hurricane surge and a long sticky season. It is a maritime Cfa, not a dry one, and the Sound is why.`,immersive:`Live oaks in Ocean Springs hold the day like a vault. Even when the Sound breeze moves, the air under the limbs stays thick, smelling of salt marsh and leaf litter. Summer nights do not cool in any northern sense; they only darken. In January a front can drop the temperature enough for a coat, then the water hands the warmth back. Shrimp boats and the Highway 90 bridge set the soundscape. After rain, the maritime forest drips as if the Gulf were overhead. The town feels small-scale and arts-minded until a hurricane map makes the whole oak canopy look like what it is: a beautiful, breakable windbreak.`,feel:`Ocean Springs feels like a small Gulf Coast art colony shaded by giant live oaks. Its quiet, leafy streets of cottages, galleries and cafés lie along the Mississippi Sound, across the bay from the casinos of Biloxi, and its art has been shaped by the coast's marshes, birds and light, above all by the visionary painter Walter Anderson. The climate is subtropical: summers are long, hot, humid and stormy, winters short and mild, and the hurricane season a constant concern from August to October.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and damp — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 7°C, and a mix of grey, drizzly days and crisp, clear ones after cold fronts. A freeze happens a few times in most winters. Camellias bloom under the live oaks, and the town celebrates Mardi Gras with parades of its own.`,spring:`Warm and blooming — afternoons rising from about 22°C in March to 29°C by late May, azaleas in full colour in March, and strong thunderstorms and occasional tornado warnings. The humidity climbs steadily, and the beaches along the sound begin to fill.`,summer:`Hot, humid and stormy — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 23°C, and almost daily afternoon thunderstorms that bring torrential rain. The sea breeze off the sound helps a little. Locals head out on boats to the barrier islands, and the season's long evenings are spent on screened porches.`,autumn:`Hurricane watch, then sweet relief — August and September are the peak of hurricane season, and the coast watches every storm in the Gulf. By October the humidity drops, afternoons ease to about 27°C, and the Peter Anderson Arts and Crafts Festival fills the streets in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Walter Anderson Museum of Art, the galleries and cafés under the live oaks, boat trips to Gulf Islands National Seashore, fresh Gulf seafood and the Peter Anderson Arts and Crafts Festival in autumn.`,contrasts:[{label:`Biloxi (≈5 km W, across the bay)`,note:`Across Biloxi Bay, the climate is virtually identical, though Biloxi's beachfront casinos sit even more exposed to Gulf storms.`},{label:`Jackson, MS (≈250 km N, inland)`,note:`In central Mississippi, summer afternoons run a degree or two hotter, and winter nights are noticeably colder, with more frequent freezes.`}],history:[`In September 1965, as Hurricane Betsy bore down on the Mississippi coast, a painter named Walter Anderson was alone on Horn Island, a wild, uninhabited barrier island about 19 km offshore, where he often rowed in a small skiff to spend weeks drawing and painting birds, crabs and plants. Instead of returning to the mainland, he stayed, by his own account lashing his boat to a tree and watching the storm pass over the island. He survived, but died of cancer less than three months later. When his family opened a locked room in his cottage in Ocean Springs, they found that he had painted every wall, from floor to ceiling, with a dazzling mural of the coast from dawn to dusk. The Little Room, as it is called, is now displayed at the Walter Anderson Museum of Art in town.`,`In 1699 the French explorer Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville built Fort Maurepas on the shore of Biloxi Bay, near today's Ocean Springs, the first lasting French settlement in what would become the Louisiana colony. The Biloxi and Pascagoula peoples lived along the rivers nearby. The French capital soon moved to Mobile and later to New Orleans, and the area became a quiet coastal settlement. In the nineteenth century, after the railroad arrived, wealthy families from New Orleans built summer homes among the live oaks, and the town became a resort for its supposed health-giving springs.`,`In 1928 Walter Anderson's brother Peter founded Shearwater Pottery on the family estate, and the Anderson family made Ocean Springs an artists' town. Hurricane Camille in 1969 and Hurricane Katrina in 2005 brought devastating storm surges to the coast; Katrina destroyed much of the waterfront, including the bridge to Biloxi. Ocean Springs rebuilt, and today it is a walkable town of shady streets, galleries and restaurants, known as the cultural heart of the Mississippi coast.`],deepTitle:`Anderson's islands`,deep:[`Walter Inglis Anderson suffered from mental illness and spent time in hospitals in the 1930s and 1940s, but he produced an astonishing body of work: thousands of watercolours, drawings, block prints and murals. He believed in close observation of nature and wrote journals about his trips to Horn Island. He also painted a huge mural in the Ocean Springs community centre, which survived Katrina.`,`Shearwater Pottery, founded by Peter Anderson, is still run by the Anderson family. Its pots, figures and tableware, decorated with designs inspired by the coast, are prized by collectors. Peter's brother James McConnell Anderson was also an artist, and the three brothers' work fills the town's museum.`,`Fort Maurepas was built of wood and lasted only until 1702, when the colony's headquarters moved to Mobile. The exact location of the original fort is still debated, but a replica was built on the waterfront in Ocean Springs. Iberville and his brother Bienville went on to found Mobile and New Orleans.`,`Horn Island and the other barrier islands off Ocean Springs are protected as part of Gulf Islands National Seashore, established in 1971. They shelter the mainland from storms, but hurricanes reshape them constantly, eroding some islands and building others. Visitors who make the trip find white sand beaches, dunes and wildlife, much as Walter Anderson saw them.`]},"eminence-mo":{why:`Eminence is a karst town. Springs dump near-constant-temperature water into the Current and Jacks Fork, so summer paddling is a cold-pool experience that ordinary Ozark creeks cannot match. Fog hangs on the water while ridges bake. Elevation is modest; hydrologic complexity is not. Dissected plateau, losing streams, and cave air create a linear oasis climate inside humid Cfa summers. Nearby ridge towns share the oak-hickory heat without the spring-fed air-conditioning. The rivers, not the hills, set the felt temperature, and floods remain the risk that matches the beauty.`,immersive:`Dawn on the Jacks Fork is a cold breath even in July. Spring water, crystal to the gravel, throws fog that fills the bluff line like a lid, then shreds when the sun hits the oak-hickory ridge. By afternoon the gravel bars radiate heat while the river still bites at wading legs. Nights in town are quieter and less cold than the water, but hollows hold damp. Winter is hunting-gray, with the Current running spring-green against bare sycamores. Gravel shifts in the riffles. Wet limestone is the smell. The whole place is louder with water than a map of 215 m elevation would ever suggest.`,feel:`Eminence feels like the heart of the Ozarks, a small town on a clear, cold river surrounded by forested hills. The springs that feed the Current and Jacks Fork rivers flow out of the limestone at an almost constant chill all year, so even on the hottest summer days the rivers are cool enough to take your breath away. Summers are hot and humid, winters short and mild with occasional ice, and the hills blaze with colour in autumn.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and changeable — afternoons near 9°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and a mix of rain, light snow and the occasional ice storm. Mist rises from the springs and rivers on cold mornings, because the spring water is so much warmer than the air. The town is quiet, and bald eagles winter along the rivers.`,spring:`Lush and stormy — afternoons warming from about 16°C in March to 26°C by late May, dogwoods and redbuds blooming in the forests, and the wettest season of the year, with thunderstorms that can send the rivers surging out of their banks.`,summer:`Hot and humid — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 18°C, and afternoon thunderstorms. The rivers are the escape: canoes, kayaks and inner tubes fill the Current and Jacks Fork, and the spring-fed water stays cool enough to refresh even in July.`,autumn:`Mellow and colourful — September afternoons near 28°C easing to about 15°C by November, with long spells of settled, sunny weather, and a blaze of oak, hickory and maple colour across the hills by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the clear spring-fed rivers of the Ozark National Scenic Riverways, canoeing and float trips on the Jacks Fork and Current rivers, wild horses, caves and huge springs such as Alley and Big Spring.`,contrasts:[{label:`Big Spring & the Current River (≈60 km SE)`,note:`The great springs pour out water at about 14°C all year, so the river corridors feel cool in summer and steam on cold winter mornings.`},{label:`Springfield (≈190 km W, ≈200 m higher)`,note:`Up on the Ozark Plateau, Springfield runs slightly cooler and windier, without the shelter of the deep river valleys.`}],history:[`In the 1990s the National Park Service announced that it planned to remove a herd of wild horses that had roamed the hills and river bottoms around Eminence for decades, descendants of farm horses turned loose during the Depression. The people of Shannon County were outraged. They formed a group to defend the horses, held rallies and lobbied their congressman, who took the case to Washington. In 1996 Congress passed a law protecting the wild horses of the Ozark National Scenic Riverways, overruling the Park Service. Today a few dozen horses still roam free in the area, and visitors come hoping to glimpse them along the rivers.`,`Shannon County was named after George Shannon, the youngest member of the Lewis and Clark expedition, who got lost twice on the journey — once for sixteen days, nearly starving before he rejoined the party. The Osage hunted these hills before settlers arrived in the early 1800s, mostly from Tennessee and Kentucky, to farm the narrow valleys and raise hogs in the woods. The first county seat was burned during the Civil War, when guerrillas and bushwhackers fought a vicious war in the Ozarks, and in 1868 a new town of Eminence was laid out on the Jacks Fork River.`,`From the 1880s to the 1910s, lumber companies stripped the Ozark hills of their shortleaf pine forests, building railroads and mills; one company built one of the largest sawmills in the country at nearby Grandin. When the forests were gone, the companies left, and the region slipped into poverty. In the 1930s the Civilian Conservation Corps built parks and replanted forests, and in 1964 Congress created the Ozark National Scenic Riverways, the first national park area in the country set aside to protect a river system. Today Eminence lives on canoeing, horseback riding and tourism.`],deepTitle:`Springs and horses`,deep:[`The springs of the Ozarks are among the largest in the world. Big Spring, near Van Buren, pours out over a billion litres of water a day on average, and Alley Spring, near Eminence, feeds a pool beside a red mill built in 1894. The water flows from underground caves and passages in the limestone, emerging a striking blue-green colour.`,`The Civil War in the Ozarks was a war of raids and ambushes, with both Union and Confederate sympathisers forming guerrilla bands that burned farms, robbed travellers and killed their enemies. Many families fled, and the region was left devastated. Old Eminence, the first county seat, was burned, and the county records were lost.`,`The Ozark National Scenic Riverways protects about 215 km of the Current and Jacks Fork rivers. Its creation was controversial: many local landowners did not want to sell their land to the government, and some still resent the park. But it brought tourism and protected the clear rivers from dams that had been proposed for them.`,`The free-roaming horses of Shannon County are managed by a local association, which works with the Park Service to keep the herd healthy and limited in size. They are often seen near Round Spring and along the Jacks Fork, grazing on the river bottoms, and have become a symbol of the independent spirit of the Ozarks.`]},"mount-charleston-nv":{why:`The Spring Mountains are a sky island on the Mojave’s shoulder. A short drive from Las Vegas climbs through inversion smog and heat into ponderosa, then bristlecone, with summits above 3,000 m. Upslope storms and winter snowpack have no analog on the basin floor. The same inversion that traps the city’s winter pollution isolates the high country as a climatic refuge. Lapse rate is the whole story, stacked in tens of kilometres horizontally and a vertical climate zonation that is among the steepest people actually use in the Southwest.`,history:[`On the evening of 16 January 1942, the film star Carole Lombard boarded a TWA airliner in Las Vegas, heading home to Los Angeles and her husband, Clark Gable, after a tour selling war bonds. Shortly after take-off, the plane flew straight into the side of Potosi Mountain, at the southern end of the Spring Mountains, and all 22 people on board were killed. Gable flew to Las Vegas and waited at the foot of the mountain while search parties climbed through the snow to the wreckage. Thirteen years later, in November 1955, an air force transport carrying workers to a secret base in the Nevada desert, where the U-2 spy plane was being developed, crashed in a snowstorm near the summit of Mount Charleston itself. The wreckage of that flight still lies on the mountain.`,`The Southern Paiute call the Spring Mountains Nuvagantu, where snow sits, and consider them the place of their creation; the mountains remain sacred to them and to neighbouring peoples. The high country gave them pine nuts, game and water in summer, while the valley below was a desert. In the nineteenth century, American surveyors named the highest summit Charleston Peak, apparently after Charleston in South Carolina, and ranchers and timber cutters began using the canyons. Lumber from these forests built early Las Vegas.`,`As Las Vegas grew in the twentieth century, the mountain became its summer escape. In the 1930s Civilian Conservation Corps crews built campgrounds, trails and roads in Kyle Canyon; skiers soon followed to Lee Canyon, where a lift-served ski area opened in the 1960s. Today the mountain, protected as the Spring Mountains National Recreation Area since 1993, draws millions of visitors from the city, who can drive from the desert floor at more than 40°C to a cool pine forest in less than an hour. The small community of Mount Charleston lies in Kyle Canyon, surrounded by the Mount Charleston Wilderness.`],deepTitle:`Island in the sky`,deep:[`The Spring Mountains rise from the Mojave Desert like an island, their summits reaching 3,633 m, almost 3,000 m above the Las Vegas valley. Because the climate changes so dramatically with altitude, the mountain holds a sequence of life zones, from creosote and Joshua trees at the bottom to pinyon and juniper, ponderosa pine, fir and finally bristlecone pines near the top. More than two dozen plants and animals live here and nowhere else.`,`Bristlecone pines are among the oldest living things on earth, and some on Mount Charleston are thought to be several thousand years old. They grow slowly in the harsh, windy conditions near the treeline, twisting into contorted shapes. The bristlecones survive on the mountain as relics of the ice ages, when cooler conditions allowed forests to grow at lower elevations across the region.`,`The Mount Charleston blue butterfly, a tiny insect found only in the high meadows of the Spring Mountains, is one of the rarest butterflies in North America, listed as endangered in 2013. Its survival depends largely on a single small plant, a milkvetch, that grows in open areas created by fires and avalanches; scientists now work to protect its habitat.`,`The Carpenter 1 Fire of 2013, started by lightning, burned more than 11,000 hectares of the mountain, forcing evacuations of the community. Ten years later, in 2023, the remnants of Hurricane Hilary dumped torrential rain on the mountain, washing out the roads into Kyle and Lee canyons and cutting the community off for weeks.`]},"cape-may-nj":{why:`Cape May is almost an island of water: Delaware Bay on one side, Atlantic on the other, a narrow peninsula in between. Sea breezes converge; heat waves that cook inland New Jersey arrive late and weaker. Humidity does not take a holiday. Autumn ocean swimming can outlast inland warmth. Winters flip between marine mildness and Appalachian-channel Arctic air. Tropical cyclones recurving up the coast have a clean shot. Triple-water exposure is the mechanism, not Victorian trim.`,immersive:`Salt is the weather in Cape May. It films porch screens, holly leaves, and the lips of anyone who walks the promenade. On a July afternoon the sea breeze can make 29°C feel usable while Philadelphia wilts; at night the same air just sits, cricket-loud and wet. Autumn keeps the ocean warm enough that locals swim after inlanders have packed sweaters. A nor’easter is a horizontal event: sand on streets, thunder of surf, ferry grounded. In the quiet after, painted ladies look freshly rinsed and slightly out of scale against a sky that still belongs to ships.`,feel:`Cape May feels like a Victorian seaside resort that never quite left the nineteenth century. Its streets are lined with hundreds of gingerbread-trimmed houses painted in candy colours, with wide porches facing the sea, at the southern tip of New Jersey, where Delaware Bay meets the Atlantic. Water on three sides softens the climate: summers are warm and breezy, cooler than inland, winters raw and damp but rarely bitter, and in autumn the peninsula becomes one of the great crossroads of bird migration in North America.`,seasons:{winter:`Raw, damp and windswept — afternoons near 8°C, nights around −1°C, and nor'easters that drive rain, wind and high tides across the beaches. Snow is occasional and rarely heavy, thanks to the surrounding water. The Victorian town glows with Christmas candlelight tours, and migrating seabirds gather offshore.`,spring:`Cool and slow to warm — afternoons rising from about 12°C in March to 22°C by late May, but cold sea breezes and fog often chill the beaches while inland New Jersey basks. Migrating songbirds pour through in May, and the town reopens its porches and inns for the season.`,summer:`Warm and sea-breezy — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 19°C, and breezes off the ocean and the bay that keep the town several degrees cooler than inland New Jersey. Humidity is high, and the occasional thunderstorm rolls in. The beaches, porches and trolleys are packed with visitors, and the ocean is warm enough for swimming.`,autumn:`The bird season — September stays warm, with afternoons near 25°C and a warm ocean, then October brings crisp, clear days and one of the great migration spectacles in North America, as hawks, songbirds and monarch butterflies funnel down the peninsula. Hurricanes and nor'easters are a risk, and the crowds thin after Labor Day.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the painted Victorian houses and inns, quiet beaches, the lighthouse, and one of the greatest bird migrations in North America, when hawks, songbirds and monarch butterflies funnel through the peninsula in autumn.`,residentFit:`It suits retirees, historic-home lovers, birders and innkeepers who want a walkable seaside town with milder winters than inland New Jersey, and who can accept summer crowds and a very quiet off-season.`,contrasts:[{label:`Philadelphia (≈130 km NW)`,note:`Inland, summer afternoons run about 3°C hotter, and winters are colder, with more snow.`},{label:`Lewes, DE (≈27 km across the bay)`,note:`Across the mouth of Delaware Bay, Lewes has a nearly identical climate, slightly warmer in summer with less ocean on its doorstep.`}],history:[`In the summer of 1852, a woman named Harriet Tubman worked as a cook in the hotels of Cape May. She had escaped slavery in Maryland three years earlier, and she was saving her wages to fund her trips back south to lead other enslaved people to freedom. Cape May, at the southern tip of New Jersey, was then America's most fashionable seaside resort, where wealthy families from Philadelphia and the South came to bathe in the sea and parade on the boardwalks; the presence of Southern slaveholders made it a dangerous place for her. She used the money to return to Maryland on one of the missions that earned her the name Moses.`,`The Kechemeche, a Lenape people, hunted and fished on this peninsula between the bay and the ocean. Henry Hudson sailed past in 1609, and the Dutch captain Cornelius Jacobsen Mey charted the area soon after, giving the cape its name. Whalers from New England settled here in the late 1600s, and by the 1760s Philadelphians were coming to Cape May for the sea air, making it one of the oldest seaside resorts in America. In the nineteenth century, presidents including Abraham Lincoln, Franklin Pierce and Ulysses S. Grant visited, and Benjamin Harrison made Cape May Point his summer White House.`,`In 1878 a fire destroyed about fourteen hectares of the town's hotels and houses. Cape May rebuilt in the fashionable styles of the day, with gingerbread trim, turrets and wide porches, and when the resort's popularity faded in the twentieth century, these buildings survived because no one could afford to tear them down. In 1976 the whole town was designated a National Historic Landmark, one of the largest collections of Victorian buildings in America. Today Cape May is a beach resort and a mecca for birdwatchers, who come to see the spring and autumn migrations.`],deepTitle:`Victorian seaside`,deep:[`Off Sunset Beach, at Cape May Point, lie the remains of the SS Atlantus, a ship built of concrete in 1918, when steel was scarce during the First World War. After the war it was used briefly, then abandoned. In 1926 it was being towed to Cape May to be sunk as part of a ferry dock when it broke loose in a storm and ran aground. Its broken hull has been sinking slowly into the sand ever since, and only a small part now shows above the water.`,`Sunset Beach is also famous for Cape May diamonds, pebbles of clear quartz that wash down the Delaware River from the mountains and are polished by the waves. Visitors comb the beach for them, and when cut and polished they sparkle like real diamonds. Every evening in summer, a flag ceremony is held on the beach at sunset, honouring American veterans.`,`Cape May is one of the best places in North America to watch bird migration. Birds flying south along the coast are funnelled down the peninsula and gather at its tip before crossing Delaware Bay, so huge numbers can be seen in a single day. The Cape May Bird Observatory counts tens of thousands of hawks each autumn, and monarch butterflies stop here in their thousands on the way to Mexico.`,`The town's Victorian houses, many of them bed-and-breakfasts, are painted in bright colours and decorated with intricate woodwork. The Emlen Physick Estate, a mansion built in 1879 and designed by the architect Frank Furness, is now a museum. Preservationists fought hard in the 1960s and 1970s to stop the town's old buildings from being demolished, and their success made Cape May a model for historic preservation.`]},"geneva-on-the-lake-oh":{why:`Eastern Lake Erie’s long westerly fetch aims snowbands at this shore. Geneva-on-the-Lake gets the full Great Lakes syllabus: late leaf-out, midsummer lake breeze, and winter cloud that Columbus never sees. June can feel Pacific-Northwest gray until the lake finally warms. Thunderstorms ride outflow boundaries along the shore. It is Ohio’s snowbelt resort, not a generic Dfa inland town, because the water is the weather machine and the Strip is built on top of it.`,immersive:`Off-season, the Strip’s arcades look like they are waiting for a ferry that does not come. Wind off Erie is cold even in May, and the water is the color of steel. When summer finally commits, the same wind is a relief, carrying fryer grease, grape-leaf, and lake smell down State Route 531. Nights stay humid. In January a snowband can erase the amusement lights in an hour, while inland I-90 stays merely wet. Cottages sit close enough to the bluff that surf on rock is the night sound, not a Florida wash. The season is short, loud, and then abruptly gone.`,feel:`Geneva-on-the-Lake feels like a summer that never quite grew up. Ohio's oldest summer resort sits on a bluff above Lake Erie, with a strip of arcades, ice-cream stands, mini-golf and cottages that hasn't changed much since the 1950s. The lake keeps summers mild and breezy and autumns long and warm, perfect for the vineyards inland. In winter the resort empties, and the same lake, cold winds blowing across its open water, sends bands of snow onto the shore.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey, cold and snowy — afternoons just above freezing, nights near −5°C, and lake-effect snow bands that can pile up quickly when cold winds blow down the length of Lake Erie, until the lake freezes over in the coldest months. The strip is closed, and the shoreline turns into a world of ice formations and frozen spray.`,spring:`Cool and late — afternoons warming from about 6°C in March to 20°C by late May, but the cold lake keeps the shore chilly and foggy when the wind blows off the water. Ice can linger on the lake into April, and the vineyards bud late, protected from early frosts by the lake's cooling effect.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 17°C, and lake breezes that take the edge off the heat. The lake warms enough for swimming by July. The strip comes alive with families, motorcyclists and teenagers, and the sunsets over the lake draw crowds to the bluff.`,autumn:`Long and mellow — the lake holds its summer warmth, so September afternoons stay near 23°C, and frosts come late. Grapes are harvested in the vineyards of the Grand River Valley, the leaves turn colour by mid-October, and the first lake-effect rain and snow arrive in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the old-fashioned summer strip of arcades and ice-cream stands, Lake Erie beaches, the wineries of the Grand River Valley and covered bridges of Ashtabula County, and dramatic lake-effect storms in winter.`,contrasts:[{label:`Chardon (≈40 km SW, ≈180 m higher)`,note:`Up on the hills inland, Chardon is the snowiest town in Ohio, receiving far more lake-effect snow than the shoreline.`},{label:`Cleveland (≈70 km W)`,note:`On the west side of the snowbelt, Cleveland gets less lake-effect snow and slightly warmer summer afternoons.`}],history:[`On the snowy night of 29 December 1876, a passenger train called the Pacific Express, carrying about 160 people, was crossing an iron bridge over the Ashtabula River gorge, a few kilometres east of Geneva-on-the-Lake, when the bridge collapsed beneath it. The locomotive at the front made it across, but the rest of the train plunged some 20 m down into the frozen river. The wooden cars caught fire from their stoves and lamps, and 92 people were killed. The bridge's designer and the railroad were blamed for its flawed design, and the railroad's chief engineer, Charles Collins, was found dead in his home soon after. The disaster led to new rules for bridge design across the country.`,`This shore of Lake Erie was part of the Connecticut Western Reserve, land claimed by Connecticut and sold to settlers from New England in the early 1800s. They cleared the forests, farmed the land and burned limestone for lime. On 4 July 1869, two local men opened a picnic grove on Sturgeon Point, a bluff above the lake, and Geneva-on-the-Lake, Ohio's first summer resort, was born. Cottages, dance halls and a carousel followed, and the resort became a getaway for working families from Cleveland, Youngstown and Pittsburgh.`,`In the 1930s and 1940s, big bands played at the resort's dance halls, and an amusement park, Erieview Park, opened on the strip in 1945, with rides and a carousel that drew families until it closed in 2006. Many of the resort's arcades, stands and restaurants have barely changed since the 1950s, giving the strip a nostalgic charm. Inland, the Grand River Valley has become one of Ohio's leading wine regions, making the most of the lake's long, mild autumns.`],deepTitle:`The strip`,deep:[`The Ashtabula bridge was a new kind of iron truss, and its failure, caused by poorly designed and badly made joints, shocked engineers across the nation. The death of Charles Collins, ruled a suicide but suspected by some to be murder, added to the mystery. A monument to the victims stands in Ashtabula's Chestnut Grove Cemetery, where many of the unidentified dead were buried.`,`Ashtabula, just east of Geneva-on-the-Lake, was an important stop on the Underground Railroad before the Civil War. Escapees from slavery hid in houses such as the Hubbard House, near the harbour, waiting for boats to carry them across Lake Erie to freedom in Canada. The Hubbard House is now a museum.`,`Eddie's Grill, a hot-dog and burger stand on the strip since 1950, is one of the resort's most famous institutions. The carousel and other old-fashioned rides, the arcade games and the summer cottages are part of what draws visitors back year after year, many of them the children and grandchildren of earlier vacationers.`,`Lake Erie is the shallowest of the Great Lakes, which means it warms up quickly in summer and often freezes over in winter. When it freezes, the lake-effect snow stops, so the heaviest snowfalls come in early winter, before the ice forms. The shallow lake also makes the summers warm enough for swimming, and its waters support a rich fishery for walleye and perch.`]},"broken-bow-ok":{why:`Broken Bow is an Ouachita foothill town in a plains state. Upslope flow wrings extra rain from Gulf lows, so shortleaf pine and oak replace the pasture geometry of central Oklahoma. Canopy shade knocks the edge off 34°C afternoons without canceling humidity. Mountain Fork and the later reservoir add local moisture. It is still hot. It is not the Panhandle. Orography and land cover, not a different latitude, make the island.`,immersive:`Summer in Broken Bow is a green heat. Pine shade helps until a parking lot, where the humidity stands up without canopy. Afternoon storms build on the ridges and walk toward town, smelling of wet bark and red clay. In winter a rare ice storm glazes US-259 while the lake in the hills stays open and dark. Fall is the kind season: dry enough to see into the woods, cool enough to sleep. Cabin traffic crawls toward Hochatown on Friday nights. The old mill town, down on the highway junction, is quieter, brick-and-timber, with a whistle of trucks where log trains used to be.`,feel:`Broken Bow feels like a piece of the Deep South that wandered into Oklahoma. Instead of open plains, the Ouachita foothills here are covered in pine and oak forests, cut by clear rivers and a long, winding lake, and the climate is wetter and more humid than anywhere else in the state. Summers are hot and sticky, springs stormy, winters short and mild, and autumn turns the forested hills red and gold.`,seasons:{winter:`Short, mild and damp — afternoons near 13°C, nights around freezing, and a mix of grey rain and bright, cool days. An ice storm or light snow comes some winters and briefly brings the hill roads to a halt. Bald eagles winter on Broken Bow Lake, and the cabins fill with visitors looking for fireplaces and quiet.`,spring:`Lush and stormy — afternoons warming from about 19°C in March to 28°C by late May, dogwoods blooming in the pine woods, and the wettest stretch of the year, with powerful thunderstorms, hail and the risk of tornadoes. The rivers run high, and the trout fishing below the dam is excellent.`,summer:`Hot and humid — afternoons near 34°C, nights around 21°C, and air thick with humidity in the forest. The lake, the cold, trout-filled Mountain Fork River below the dam, and the shade of the pines are the escapes, and the cabins around Hochatown are booked solid.`,autumn:`Warm then gorgeous — September afternoons near 31°C easing to about 18°C by November, the humidity dropping, and the hardwoods turning colour among the pines in late October and early November.`},contrasts:[{label:`Oklahoma City (≈320 km NW)`,note:`Out on the plains, the capital is drier, windier and far less wooded, with sharper cold snaps in winter.`},{label:`Hot Springs, AR (≈200 km E)`,note:`Deeper in the Ouachita Mountains, Hot Springs shares Broken Bow's humid, forested climate.`}],history:[`In the winter of 1831–32, thousands of Choctaw people were forced to leave their homes in Mississippi and walk west to new lands in what is now southeastern Oklahoma, the first of the great removals ordered under the Indian Removal Act. They travelled in bitter cold and flood, without enough food, blankets or wagons, and many died of exposure, cholera and starvation. A Choctaw leader is said to have described the journey to a newspaper as a trail of tears and death, and the phrase came to stand for all the forced removals of the era. The survivors rebuilt their nation in the forested hills around Broken Bow, establishing farms, schools and towns, including Hochatown on the Mountain Fork River.`,`For most of the nineteenth century, this was part of the Choctaw Nation, with its own government, laws and courts. After the Civil War, railroads and timber companies pushed into the region, and in 1907 the Choctaw Nation's lands were absorbed into the new state of Oklahoma. In 1911 two lumbermen, the Dierks brothers, founded a sawmill town here and named it Broken Bow, after their former hometown in Nebraska. For decades their company cut the shortleaf pine forests of the Ouachitas, and the town lived on the mill.`,`In 1961 the Army Corps of Engineers began building a dam on the Mountain Fork River, and by 1970 Broken Bow Lake had flooded the old Choctaw town of Hochatown, whose residents were relocated. The lake, the river below the dam and Beavers Bend State Park, built in the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps, turned the area into one of Oklahoma's most popular vacation spots, and thousands of rental cabins now fill the woods. The Choctaw Nation, one of the largest tribes in the United States, remains a major presence in the region.`],deepTitle:`Choctaw hills`,deep:[`The Choctaw Nation rebuilt its government in the west, adopting a written constitution in 1834 and running its own schools, courts and newspapers. During the Irish Potato Famine of 1847, the Choctaw, only a few years after their own suffering on the removal, raised money to send to the starving Irish, a gesture remembered in Ireland to this day; a sculpture of eagle feathers in County Cork commemorates their gift.`,`The Dierks Lumber and Coal Company, founded by brothers from Nebraska, was one of the largest timber companies in the South. It owned vast tracts of forest in Oklahoma and Arkansas and built company towns for its workers. In 1969 Weyerhaeuser bought the company, and the mill in Broken Bow continued to operate. The forests, replanted with pine, still supply timber.`,`Hochatown, before it was flooded by the lake, had a reputation during Prohibition as a centre of moonshine production, with stills hidden in the hollows. The new Hochatown, rebuilt near the lake, is now a busy strip of restaurants, breweries and rental cabins catering to tourists from Dallas and Oklahoma City.`,`The Kiamichi Mountains north of Broken Bow are one of the hotspots of Bigfoot sightings in the United States, and the nearby community of Honobia holds an annual Bigfoot festival. Whether the creature exists or not, the dense forests and remote hollows of southeastern Oklahoma are wild enough to make it seem possible.`]},"block-island-ri":{why:`Block Island is small enough that marine air wins every argument. Heat waves that roast Hartford stall in a windy Cfb. Winters are oceanic and hard rather than bitterly dry. Fog, salt, and a complete lack of inland thermal mass keep the annual range narrow for the latitude. The moor-and-heath vegetation is a salt-spray climax, not a lawn climate. It is southern New England’s clearest island boundary-layer case, and the ferry schedule is part of the meteorology of daily life.`,immersive:`There is no inland on Block Island, only degrees of exposure. On the west, the Great Salt Pond smells of tidal mud and diesel; on the east, Mohegan Bluffs throw clay into a surf that never quite quiets. Summer fog can erase the harbor while the sun still burns exposed skin. Bicycles tick past stone walls and shingled hotels that look English until a tropical storm warning reminds visitors this is hurricane coast. Winter is a different island: fewer people, horizontal rain, and a grocery list planned around the boat. The moors stay low because the wind insists.`,feel:`Block Island feels like a world apart, an hour's ferry ride from the mainland into the open Atlantic. Rolling green moors, stone walls, freshwater ponds and high clay bluffs make up an island that has resisted the overdevelopment of much of the New England coast. The surrounding ocean keeps the summers cool and breezy and the winters milder than the mainland, though fog and wind are constant companions, and storms can cut the island off for days.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, windy and lonely — afternoons near 6°C, nights just below freezing, and gales that whip across the island and sometimes cancel the ferries. Snow comes but rarely stays, thanks to the surrounding ocean. Only about 1,400 people live on the island year-round, and the quiet is profound.`,spring:`Cool and foggy — afternoons warming from about 7°C in March to 17°C by late May, and fog banks rolling in from the cold sea. The shadbush and wildflowers bloom on the moors, migrating birds stop on the island, and the first summer visitors arrive by late May.`,summer:`Mild and breezy — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 17°C, and sea breezes that keep the island several degrees cooler than the mainland. Fog can roll in on any day. The beaches, cycling roads and harbour are full of visitors, and ferries arrive packed from the mainland.`,autumn:`Warm and golden — the ocean holds its heat, so September afternoons stay near 22°C, and autumn is one of the best times to visit, when the crowds are gone. Millions of migrating songbirds pass through, and the hurricane season brings an occasional storm.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come by ferry for the Mohegan Bluffs and Southeast Light, quiet beaches, cycling the island's narrow roads, birding in autumn and a cool, breezy summer far from the crowds of the mainland.`,contrasts:[{label:`Providence (≈70 km N, mainland)`,note:`On the mainland, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter and winter nights noticeably colder, with more snow.`},{label:`Newport (≈40 km NE)`,note:`On Aquidneck Island at the mouth of Narragansett Bay, Newport shares Block Island's maritime climate, with slightly warmer summers.`}],history:[`On a December night in 1738, a ship called the Princess Augusta, carrying German emigrants from the Palatinate region to Philadelphia, ran aground on Block Island after a nightmarish voyage in which disease had killed many of the passengers and much of the crew. The islanders took in the survivors. But a legend grew up that the islanders had lured the ship onto the rocks, looted it and set it on fire with passengers still aboard, and that the burning ship, the Palatine, returned every year as a ghostly light on the horizon. The poet John Greenleaf Whittier made the story famous in 1867. Historians have found no evidence of the crime, but the Palatine Light is still reported off the island.`,`The Manisses people, a branch of the Narragansett, called the island Manisses, the island of the little god, and lived by fishing and farming. In 1614 the Dutch explorer Adriaen Block charted it and left his name. In 1636 the murder of a trader named John Oldham off the island led Massachusetts to send an expedition that burned the Manisses' villages, an attack that helped start the Pequot War. In 1661 a group of English settlers arrived, and the island became the town of New Shoreham. Islanders farmed, fished, and salvaged the many ships wrecked on their shores.`,`In the nineteenth century, steamers brought summer visitors, and grand Victorian hotels rose above the harbour. Two lighthouses, North Light and Southeast Light, guarded the dangerous waters. The 1938 hurricane devastated the island, and the resort declined in the mid-twentieth century. Since the 1970s, islanders have worked to protect their land from development, and today more than 40 per cent of the island is preserved as open space. In 2016 the first offshore wind farm in the United States began turning just off its southern coast.`],deepTitle:`Palatine light`,deep:[`The real story of the Princess Augusta was grim enough. The ship had left Rotterdam with about 340 passengers, but contaminated water and disease killed more than 200 of them, along with the captain. The surviving crew mistreated the passengers, and when the ship struck the island the islanders helped those who came ashore, some of whom stayed and married into island families. The legend may have grown out of the bad conscience of the crew or the jealousy of mainland neighbours.`,`Southeast Light, a brick lighthouse built in 1875 on the edge of the Mohegan Bluffs, was in danger of falling into the sea as the cliffs eroded. In 1993 it was moved back from the edge along a track about 75 m long, a delicate operation that took several weeks. The bluffs below are named for a legend that the Manisses drove a party of invading Mohegans over the cliffs to their deaths.`,`Block Island's conservation movement began in the 1970s, when islanders formed land trusts to buy and protect open land. The Nature Conservancy called the island one of the Last Great Places in the Western Hemisphere, and the island's moors, ponds and bluffs now shelter rare species such as the American burying beetle, which has vanished from most of its former range.`,`The Block Island Wind Farm, five turbines rising from the sea about 6 km off the island's southeast coast, began producing power in 2016. Before it, the island depended on diesel generators, and electricity was among the most expensive in the country. Now the island is connected to the mainland grid by an undersea cable, and the wind farm has become a model for the larger offshore wind projects being built along the East Coast.`]}},a={"spearfish-sd":{why:`Lee of the Black Hills, Spearfish sits where downslope westerlies can replace a valley cold pool faster than a prairie town ever could. The canyon mouth funnels foehn air; adiabatic warming and a humidity crash show up as near-vertical traces on station records. That is not a mild chinook afternoon borrowed from ranges farther west. It is a local replacement of air masses, sometimes in minutes, with snowmelt, road ice, and fire weather changing in the same pulse. Ponderosa on the slopes and grassland on the plains sit close enough that one wind event rewrites both. Neighboring High Plains towns share Arctic outbreaks; few share this valve.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, bright and stormy — afternoons near 30°C in July, nights cooling to about 14°C in the dry air, and thunderstorms that build over the Black Hills in the afternoon and sweep onto the plains with lightning, hail and sudden downpours. Spearfish Canyon's limestone walls and cold creek offer shade and trout, and in early August hundreds of thousands of motorcycles roar through the area for the rally at nearby Sturgis.`},contrasts:[{label:`Lead & Deadwood (≈20 km SE, ≈500 m higher)`,note:`Up in the northern Hills, the old mining towns get far more snow and run several degrees cooler, with a longer, deeper winter.`},{label:`Belle Fourche (≈15 km N, out on the plains)`,note:`Away from the canyon mouth, winter nights can be colder and the chinook's sudden warmth less frequent.`}],history:[`At 7:30 on the morning of 22 January 1943, the thermometer in Spearfish read about −20°C. Two minutes later, it read 7°C. A warm chinook wind had come pouring down off the Black Hills and swept the frigid arctic air away, raising the temperature by 27°C in two minutes — a world record that still stands. Over the next couple of hours, the warm and cold air sloshed back and forth across the town, and the temperature swung up and down like a yo-yo. Windows and windshields cracked from the sudden changes, and people walking from one block to another passed from spring into deep winter.`,`The Black Hills, which the Lakota call Ȟe Sápa, were sacred to them and to other Plains nations, and the 1868 Treaty of Fort Laramie guaranteed them to the Lakota forever. But in 1874 an army expedition led by George Armstrong Custer found gold in the Hills, miners poured in, and in 1877 the government seized the land. Spearfish was founded in 1876 at the mouth of a canyon, to grow food for the gold camps at Deadwood and Lead, and its farms and gardens prospered. A teachers' college founded in 1883 grew into Black Hills State University.`,`In 1896 the federal government opened a fish hatchery in Spearfish to stock the streams of the Black Hills with trout, and the D.C. Booth hatchery still operates as a museum. In 1939 a German actor named Josef Meier brought a Passion Play to Spearfish, and for nearly seventy years it was performed in an outdoor amphitheatre each summer. The canyon south of town, with its limestone cliffs and waterfalls, was used for the winter scenes of Dances with Wolves in 1990. In 1980 the Supreme Court ruled that the Black Hills had been taken illegally and awarded the Lakota compensation; they have refused the money, which now exceeds a billion dollars, insisting on the return of the land.`],deepTitle:`Chinook country`,deep:[`Chinook winds are common along the eastern edge of the Rockies and the Black Hills. When westerly winds blow down the slopes of the mountains, the air is compressed and warms rapidly, melting snow and raising temperatures dramatically. In Spearfish, the canyon mouth funnels the wind, and the town sits right at the boundary where the warm air meets pools of arctic cold on the plains, which is why its temperature swings can be so extreme.`,`The Lakota claim to the Black Hills is one of the longest-running legal battles in American history. In United States v. Sioux Nation of Indians, the Supreme Court agreed that the land had been taken unlawfully and awarded the tribes the 1877 value of the land plus interest. The Lakota have refused to accept payment, saying that the Black Hills are not for sale, and the money sits in a trust account, growing every year.`,`The D.C. Booth Fish Hatchery was established to bring trout to the streams of the Black Hills, which had none of their own. Brown, rainbow and brook trout were raised and released into the creeks, creating a fishery that draws anglers today. The hatchery's historic buildings, ponds and a restored railroad car used to transport fish are now a museum.`,`The Black Hills Passion Play, depicting the last days of Christ, was performed in Spearfish from 1939 to 2008 by a cast that included local residents and a herd of animals. Josef Meier played Jesus for decades. The amphitheatre has since been used for other events, but the play was one of the longest-running outdoor productions in America.`]},"burkes-garden-va":{why:`Garden Mountain is a nearly continuous rim. On clear, calm nights, air cooled on the slopes drains into the limestone bowl and has only Wolf Creek’s gap as an easy exit, so the floor becomes a reservoir of dense air while the rim stays milder. Humidity and soil moisture in this eastern cove change how long the pool lasts, but they do not cancel the geometry. Hay fields on the floor and wooded slopes a few hundred feet up are different climates on the same farm map. Western basin cold pools are famous; this is the humid Appalachian analog, compact enough to walk from frost to thermal belt in a short drive.`,seasons:{summer:`Mild days and cool, misty nights — afternoons near 26°C, but after sunset the cold air drains off the encircling ridges and settles on the valley floor, so nights fall to around 14°C and sometimes below 10°C even in July. Fog lies in the meadows at dawn, hay is cut on the green fields, and the Amish farms bustle with work.`},history:[`In 1748 a party of surveyors working for a land speculator named James Patton camped in a high, oval valley in the mountains of southwest Virginia. One of the men, an Irishman named James Burke, threw the peelings of the potatoes he had cooked into the campfire ashes. When the surveyors returned the next year, they found a crop of potatoes growing where the peelings had been, and they jokingly called the valley Burke's Garden. The name stuck, and so did the valley's reputation for fertility. According to a local story, more than a century later the millionaire George Vanderbilt wanted to buy land here for his country estate, but the farmers refused to sell, and he built Biltmore in North Carolina instead.`,`The valley is a geological curiosity. It sits nearly 1,000 m above sea level, completely surrounded by Garden Mountain, a ring of ridges that forms its rim, and from the air it looks like a giant thumbprint pressed into the mountains, which is why it is sometimes called God's Thumbprint. Millions of years ago, the valley was the top of a dome of rock; the softer limestone at its centre eroded away, leaving the harder sandstone rim. The only natural entrance is a narrow gap where a creek leaves the valley.`,`German farmers settled the valley in the early nineteenth century and built the Lutheran church that still stands in the centre. The fertile limestone soil made Burkes Garden ideal for grazing cattle, and its farmers prospered, building large farmhouses. Isolated by the mountains, the community kept to itself, and in the late twentieth century Amish families moved in, adding their buggies to the country roads. The valley's cold-air pooling gives it some of the coldest temperatures recorded in Virginia, and the Appalachian Trail follows the ridge along its southern rim.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Burkes Garden is the highest valley in Virginia and the largest rural historic district in the state. Its floor, some 80 square kilometres of farmland, is so enclosed by mountains that on calm, clear nights cold air drains down from the ridges and collects there like water in a bowl. As a result, the valley often records frost when surrounding areas do not, and winter nights can be bitterly cold.`,`The story of Vanderbilt and Burkes Garden is popular locally but hard to prove. George Vanderbilt did search the southern mountains for the site of his estate in the 1880s, and Burkes Garden's beauty would have appealed to him; in the end he chose Asheville, where he built the largest private house in America. Whether he ever made an offer in Burkes Garden, the farmers there are proud to tell the tale.`,`The Central Lutheran Church, built in 1875, is the heart of the community. The valley's German settlers came largely from Pennsylvania and the Shenandoah Valley, and their descendants still farm much of the land. The Amish, who began arriving in the 1980s, farm with horses and run small businesses selling baked goods and furniture.`,`The valley's single road enters through a narrow gap, and in winter it can be closed by snow and ice. Hikers on the Appalachian Trail, which runs along the ridge above, look down on the patchwork of fields and woods, and the view from the rim is one of the most celebrated on the trail.`]},"spokane-wa":{why:`East of the Cascades, Spokane receives Pacific storms in modified, rain-shadowed form and still takes Arctic punches that Seattle rarely feels at the same latitude. The Spokane River corridor adds a second layer: valley neighborhoods pool cold air at night while the urban fabric holds heat, so winter minima and summer nights diverge block by block. Occasional chinook intrusions swing winter days harder than a coastal catalog would suggest. Dry summers, a real freeze season, and river-organized drainage are the Inland Empire’s climate, not a milder copy of Puget Sound.`,feel:`Spokane feels like the capital of a dry inland empire. Shielded from the Pacific's rain by the Cascades, it has four distinct seasons: hot, dry summers with clear skies and cool evenings, snowy winters with freezing fog, and short, lovely springs and autumns. The Spokane River runs through the middle of downtown, crashing over a series of waterfalls that gave the city its first purpose, and pine-covered hills and lakes surround it.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and snowy — afternoons near 3°C, nights near −4°C, and long spells of freezing fog and low cloud trapped in the valley, broken by snowstorms and occasional ice storms. The ski areas in the surrounding mountains are a short drive away, and the falls downtown become curtains of ice on cold days.`,spring:`Fresh and quickly greening — afternoons rising from about 10°C in March to 20°C by late May, frosty mornings into April, and passing showers between bright, breezy days. Lilacs bloom in May, when Spokane holds its Lilac Festival, and the river roars over the falls downtown with snowmelt.`,summer:`Hot, dry and sunny — afternoons near 30°C in July and August, but nights cool to around 12°C in the dry air, and rain almost disappears. Wildfire smoke from the Cascades and Canada can fill the sky in bad years. The river, the lakes and the long evenings are the city's summer treasures.`,autumn:`Crisp and golden — September afternoons near 24°C cooling to about 8°C by November, larches turning gold on the mountains and the first rains returning in October. The first snow usually falls in November.`},contrasts:[{label:`Coeur d'Alene, ID (≈50 km E)`,note:`Closer to the mountains, Coeur d'Alene gets more rain and snow and is a little cooler in summer, beside its big lake.`},{label:`Seattle (≈370 km W, across the Cascades)`,note:`On the wet side of the mountains, Seattle has milder, rainier winters and much cooler summers.`}],history:[`In 1909 a woman named Sonora Smart Dodd sat in a Spokane church listening to a sermon on Mother's Day, then a new idea, and thought of her father, a Civil War veteran named William Smart, who had raised her and her five brothers alone after their mother died in childbirth. Why, she wondered, was there no day to honour fathers? She persuaded local churches and the YMCA to support the idea, and on 19 June 1910, Spokane celebrated the first Father's Day. The idea spread slowly across the country, and Father's Day finally became a national holiday in 1972.`,`For thousands of years the Spokane people, whose name is often translated as Children of the Sun, gathered at the falls of the Spokane River to catch salmon, joined each season by neighbouring nations. In 1810 the British fur trader David Thompson built Spokane House, one of the first trading posts in the region. In 1858, after a series of clashes, the US Army under Colonel George Wright defeated the Spokane and their allies, slaughtered some 800 of their horses and hanged several of their leaders. The tribes were pushed onto reservations, and settlers moved in. In 1873 James Glover bought a claim at the falls, betting the Northern Pacific Railway would come; it arrived in 1881.`,`On 4 August 1889 a fire destroyed about 32 blocks of downtown Spokane Falls. The city rebuilt in brick and stone, shortened its name to Spokane, and boomed as the centre of a rich region of wheat farms, timber and mines. The singer Bing Crosby grew up here and attended Gonzaga University. In 1974 Spokane, then a city of about 170,000, became the smallest city ever to host a world's fair, Expo '74, which had an environmental theme; railroads removed the tangle of tracks that had covered the riverfront, and the site became Riverfront Park, where the falls are once again the city's centrepiece.`],deepTitle:`Falls and fire`,deep:[`Spokane Falls, a series of cascades in the heart of downtown, drop some 40 m over basalt ledges. The river was harnessed for hydroelectric power early in the city's history, and the Washington Water Power Company built dams right in the city. In spring, when the snowmelt swells the river, the falls roar, and a gondola carries visitors over them.`,`Colonel Wright's 1858 campaign was brutal. After defeating the tribes at the Battle of Four Lakes and the Battle of Spokane Plains, his men rounded up the Palouse and Spokane horse herds and shot them over two days, a calculated blow to the tribes' way of life. He then hanged several leaders at a creek south of the city, which became known as Hangman Creek. The site of the horse slaughter is marked by a monument east of Spokane.`,`The Davenport Hotel, opened in 1914 by the restaurateur Louis Davenport, was one of the most luxurious hotels in America, with a grand lobby, ballrooms and modern comforts that were the talk of the West. It closed in 1985 and was saved from demolition and restored in 2002. Bing Crosby, born in Tacoma in 1903, grew up in Spokane, and his childhood home now houses a museum at Gonzaga.`,`Expo '74 was opened by President Richard Nixon, and some 5.2 million visitors came. Its theme, celebrating the fresh new environment, was the first environmental theme for a world's fair. The US Pavilion, with its distinctive cable-supported roof, still stands in Riverfront Park, and a giant garbage-eating goat sculpture, built for the fair, still swallows trash in the park today.`]},"napa-ca":{why:`A narrow alluvial trough between the Mayacamas and Vaca ranges heats by day, then takes cool marine air through the Petaluma Gap and as drainage off the hills. Fog and gap flow delay ripening on the floor while benchlands a few tens of meters up change frost risk and hang time. That is why an AVA map here is a mesoclimate map, not a marketing overlay. Inland heat and coastal moisture share a corridor only a few miles wide. Cabernet country is built on nightly cooling as much as on summer sun.`,immersive:`Dawn on the valley floor can still hold fog while the Mayacamas crest is already bright. By mid-morning the stratus burns back toward the gap, and the trough becomes a heat engine until evening drainage returns. Vine rows follow old alluvial fans and volcanic benches; a short walk upslope is a different frost night. Summer afternoons near 30 °C feel dry compared with the Bay, then the marine pulse drops the mercury and the grapes keep their acidity. Winter rain, not summer storms, fills the creeks. Smoke from nearby ranges is now part of harvest math. The place smells like wet bay laurel after a front and like warm oak duff by August. You feel the pocket: Mediterranean in the Köppen table, tightly local in the body.`,feel:`Napa feels like a valley built for grapes. Its long, narrow floor runs north from the cool, foggy shores of San Pablo Bay into hot inland hills, so that within a half-hour's drive the climate changes from misty and mild to baking and dry — exactly the gradient that lets winemakers grow everything from delicate Chardonnay to powerful Cabernet. Summer days are hot and cloudless, nights cool as fog drifts in from the bay, and winters are green, wet and mild.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, green and rainy — afternoons near 15°C, nights around 4°C, and most of the year's rain arriving in a few big Pacific storms. Frost can coat the valley floor on clear nights. The vineyards are dormant and bare, but bright yellow mustard flowers carpet the rows between the vines from January to March.`,spring:`Fresh and fragile — afternoons warming from about 19°C in March to 26°C by late May, the vines budding out and the hills still green. Frost is the great worry: on clear, still nights cold air pools on the valley floor, and growers run giant fans and sprinklers to protect the tender shoots.`,summer:`Hot days, cool nights — afternoons near 30°C on the valley floor and much hotter at the northern end, nights cooling to about 13°C as fog and breezes from San Pablo Bay push up the valley. Almost no rain falls from May to October. The big daily swing in temperature is what gives Napa's grapes their balance of ripeness and acidity.`,autumn:`Harvest season — September afternoons near 30°C, then cooling to about 20°C by November, with the vineyards turning gold and red after the grapes are picked. It is also the season of hot, dry offshore winds that can drive wildfires through the hills, and the first rains usually arrive in late October or November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for wine tasting along Highway 29 and the Silverado Trail, hot-air balloon flights over the vineyards at dawn, world-famous restaurants, the hot springs of Calistoga and harvest season in autumn.`,residentFit:`It suits winemakers, vineyard workers, chefs and retirees who love the Mediterranean climate and the rhythm of the harvest, and who can afford high prices and live with wildfire smoke and the risk of fire.`,contrasts:[{label:`Calistoga (≈40 km N, at the head of the valley)`,note:`Further from the bay's cooling influence, summer afternoons run several degrees hotter, and winter nights are frostier.`},{label:`Carneros & San Pablo Bay (≈10 km S)`,note:`On the bay's edge, fog and breezes keep summers much cooler — ideal for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.`}],history:[`On 24 May 1976, a British wine merchant named Steven Spurrier held a blind tasting in Paris in which nine of France's most respected wine experts compared top French wines with little-known bottles from California. To everyone's astonishment, including the judges', the winners in both categories came from Napa: a 1973 Cabernet Sauvignon from Stag's Leap Wine Cellars and a 1973 Chardonnay from Chateau Montelena. One French judge reportedly demanded her scorecard back. A Time magazine reporter was the only journalist present, and his story made Napa world-famous overnight. The Judgment of Paris, as it became known, changed the wine world forever, and bottles of both winning wines are now in the Smithsonian.`,`The Wappo people lived along the Napa River for thousands of years, gathering acorns, fishing and hunting in the oak woodlands; their population was devastated by disease and violence after Spanish and Mexican settlers arrived. In 1836 the Mexican government granted a ranch in the valley to George Yount, an American trapper, who planted the first grapevines there a few years later. After the gold rush, settlers from Europe and America founded wineries, including Charles Krug in 1861, and by the 1880s Napa was producing wines that won medals at international fairs. The writer Robert Louis Stevenson honeymooned on the slopes of Mount St. Helena in 1880 and called Napa wine bottled poetry.`,`Then came disaster. A root louse called phylloxera destroyed most of the vineyards in the 1890s, and Prohibition, from 1920 to 1933, closed almost all the wineries; a few survived by making sacramental wine for churches. After repeal, a handful of winemakers, including André Tchelistcheff at Beaulieu and later Robert Mondavi, who opened his winery in 1966, rebuilt Napa's reputation for quality. Today the valley has hundreds of wineries and is one of the most famous wine regions in the world. It has also been tested by an earthquake in 2014 and by devastating wildfires in 2017 and 2020.`],deepTitle:`Judgment of Paris`,deep:[`The Paris tasting was organised partly as a publicity event by Spurrier, who ran a wine shop and school in Paris and wanted to introduce the French to California wines. He expected the French wines to win. The judges were so sure they could identify the French wines that they made confident remarks, only to discover they had praised the Californians. A rematch in 2006, using the same wines aged thirty years, also went to California.`,`Robert Louis Stevenson came to California to marry Fanny Osbourne, an American woman he had met in France. Poor and in poor health, the couple spent their honeymoon in 1880 in an abandoned miners' bunkhouse on Mount St. Helena, and Stevenson wrote about the experience in The Silverado Squatters. He visited local winemakers and wrote warmly of their efforts to find the best soils for each grape.`,`Robert Mondavi was the son of Italian immigrants who ran a winery in the valley. After a family feud, he founded his own winery in 1966, the first major new winery in Napa since Prohibition, and championed high-quality wines labelled by grape variety rather than by imitation European names. He also promoted wine tourism, food and the arts, helping to turn Napa into the destination it is today.`,`The wildfires of October 2017, driven by powerful offshore winds, swept through the hills around Napa and Sonoma, killing dozens of people and destroying thousands of homes. In 2020 the Glass Fire burned through the northern valley, destroying several wineries. Smoke damage to grapes, rising temperatures and drought have forced winemakers to rethink how they farm, as the climate that made Napa famous shifts.`]},"palm-springs-ca":{why:`San Gorgonio Pass squeezes basin-and-coast pressure differences into a wind corridor that scours the Coachella Valley. Palm Springs sits in that venturi, in the rain shadow of the San Jacintos, with an oasis hydrology that Cahuilla people organized around a hot spring. Winter sun is reliable because subsidence and rain shadow starve clouds; summer heat is desert-grade, only slightly less fierce than the Imperial Valley floor because of elevation. The resort calendar is a climate calendar. Wind, aridity, and a mountain wall, not landscaping, are the mechanism.`,immersive:`San Jacinto’s granite wall rises more than 3,000 m from the valley, so snow can sit on the ridge while the town bakes in the low desert. Afternoons in July push past 40 °C; people move into shade, pools, and night. Winter days are the product: dry light, sharp shadows, and a breeze that can howl when the pass pressure gradient steepens. Fan palms mark water. The rest is creosote, wash sand, and wind. You hear the corridor before you see a flag stand straight. Nights cool enough, most winters, to sleep without the Gulf Coast’s sticky air. The tradeoff is not subtle. Summer is an indoor season for anyone without a relationship to extreme heat.`,feel:`Palm Springs feels like a desert oasis designed for a cocktail hour that never ends. Tucked at the foot of Mount San Jacinto, which rises more than 3,000 m almost straight up from the town, it basks in sunshine nearly every day of the year. Winter is glorious — warm, dry afternoons and cool nights, with snow on the peaks above the palm trees — while summer is a furnace that sends many residents fleeing. Mid-century modern houses, swimming pools and golf courses define its look.`,seasons:{winter:`The high season — afternoons near 21°C, nights around 8°C, and sunshine almost every day, with snow gleaming on the summit of San Jacinto above the palms. A few Pacific storms bring the year's little rain. Snowbirds fill the resorts and golf courses, and Modernism Week fills the town in February.`,spring:`Warm and windy — afternoons climbing from about 26°C in March to 35°C by late May, wildflowers blooming in the desert after wet winters, and strong winds blowing through the San Gorgonio Pass, spinning the thousands of wind turbines that line it. The music festivals at nearby Indio draw huge crowds in April.`,summer:`Brutally hot — afternoons near 41°C and often well above 45°C, nights that barely fall below 25°C, and sunshine every day. Locals stay indoors or head up the aerial tramway to the cool forests on the mountain above. The occasional monsoon surge brings humidity and a dramatic thunderstorm.`,autumn:`Slowly cooling — September is still very hot, but by November afternoons ease to about 26°C, and the snowbirds return. The desert air is clear, and the town comes back to life after the summer lull.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for winter sun, mid-century modern architecture and Modernism Week, poolside resorts, the aerial tramway up Mount San Jacinto, the Indian Canyons and day trips to Joshua Tree National Park.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mount San Jacinto tramway top (≈10 km W, ≈2,450 m higher)`,note:`At the top of the aerial tramway, temperatures run about 20°C cooler, and winter brings deep snow.`},{label:`Indio (≈40 km SE, ≈150 m lower)`,note:`Further down the Coachella Valley, summer afternoons run a couple of degrees hotter still.`}],history:[`According to a famous Hollywood story, in the 1930s and 1940s the studios had a rule that their stars could not travel more than two hours from Los Angeles without permission, in case they were needed on set at short notice. Palm Springs, a sleepy desert village with a hot spring and winter sunshine, was just within reach, and the stars flocked there. In 1934 the actors Charles Farrell and Ralph Bellamy opened the Racquet Club, which became Hollywood's desert playground. Frank Sinatra built a house there in 1947 with a swimming pool shaped like a piano, Elvis Presley spent his honeymoon there, and Bob Hope, Lucille Ball and dozens of other stars made Palm Springs their winter home.`,`The Agua Caliente Band of Cahuilla Indians has lived around the hot spring at the heart of the town, which they call Séc-he, for thousands of years. In 1876 the federal government created their reservation as a checkerboard, alternating square-mile sections with land given to the railroad. As the town grew, the tribe found itself owning much of the land under the city. For decades they were forbidden to lease their land on long terms, but after federal laws changed in the 1950s, the tribe became one of the wealthiest landowners in the region. In the 1960s, however, the city cleared hundreds of homes of families living on tribal land in Section 14, near downtown, burning many of them.`,`After the Second World War, a building boom brought architects such as Albert Frey, Richard Neutra and William Krisel, who designed sleek, modern houses of glass and steel suited to the desert, and Palm Springs now has one of the largest collections of mid-century modern architecture in the world. In 1963 the Palm Springs Aerial Tramway opened, carrying passengers from the desert floor to the cool forests of Mount San Jacinto. Today Palm Springs is a resort town for retirees, LGBTQ visitors and architecture fans, and the Agua Caliente tribe has opened a museum and spa at the site of the ancient spring.`],deepTitle:`Desert modern`,deep:[`The Palm Springs Aerial Tramway climbs some 2,600 m above sea level in about ten minutes, and since 2000 its cars have slowly rotated as they travel, giving passengers a full view of the cliffs and the desert below. It passes through several climate zones on the way, from desert to alpine forest, and at the top visitors can hike or, in winter, play in the snow.`,`Mid-century modern architecture in Palm Springs uses flat or angled roofs, wide windows, open plans and sliding glass doors to blend indoors and outdoors. Albert Frey's own house, built against a boulder on the mountainside, and Richard Neutra's Kaufmann House of 1946 are among the most famous examples. The Modernism Week festival each February brings tens of thousands of visitors to tour these houses.`,`The demolitions in Section 14 in the 1960s displaced many Black, Mexican American and Native families who rented homes on tribal land. The city, seeking to redevelop the land, ordered the houses cleared, and fire crews burned many of them. In recent years the city has formally apologised and agreed to pay compensation to survivors and their descendants.`,`The San Gorgonio Pass, just northwest of Palm Springs, is one of the windiest places in California, as cool air from the coast rushes through the gap toward the hot desert. Since the 1980s thousands of wind turbines have been built there, forming one of the largest wind farms in the country, and the white turbines spinning against the mountains have become a symbol of the valley.`]},"austin-tx":{why:`Austin sits on the Balcones Escarpment, where Edwards Plateau limestone and Hill Country relief meet Gulf moisture moving inland. The Colorado River corridor adds local humidity and flood routes; urban growth has raised nocturnal temperatures so that nights no longer reset as they did in a smaller town. Drylines and Gulf fetches collide here, which is why summers are both hot and stormy rather than merely one or the other. It is not coastal Texas and not dry west Texas. It is the hinge, with a strengthening heat island on top.`,immersive:`Summer in Austin is a long argument between plateau dryness and Gulf steam. Afternoons commonly near 36 °C, nights that stay sticky, and a sky that builds towers over the hills when a dryline or front arrives. Barton Springs stays in the low 20s °C because it is aquifer water, not lake surface, which is why a swim can feel like a climate change in one body. Winter is brief and bright, then a rare Arctic plunge ices live oaks and pipes. Creeks rise fast on limestone. You smell cedar after rain and exhaust on I-35. The city feels youthful and overheated at once: music on a patio, heat stored in pavement, and a river that still organizes who floods.`,feel:`Austin feels like a Texas city that marches to its own beat. It sits where the green, rocky Hill Country meets the flat coastal plains, with springs, lakes and limestone cliffs on its western edge and a river running through its heart. The climate is hot and humid for much of the year, with long, sultry summers and short, mild winters, broken by violent spring storms and sudden flash floods. Live music, tacos and the evening flight of a million bats are part of the rhythm.`,seasons:{winter:`Short, mild and changeable — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 5°C, and blue northers that can drop the temperature sharply for a day or two. Freezes come a few times a winter, and rare ice storms or snow can paralyse the city, as in February 2021. Many days are sunny and pleasant enough for patios.`,spring:`Beautiful and stormy — afternoons warming from about 23°C in March to 31°C by late May, bluebonnets and other wildflowers carpeting the roadsides in March and April, and powerful thunderstorms that can bring hail and flash floods. The South by Southwest festival fills the city in March.`,summer:`Long, hot and humid — afternoons near 36°C and often above 38°C, nights barely falling below 23°C, and heat that lasts from June into September. Barton Springs pool, fed by cool spring water, and the lakes are the escapes, and at dusk crowds gather on the Congress Avenue Bridge to watch the bats emerge.`,autumn:`Warm and slowly cooling — September is still hot, but by November afternoons ease to about 23°C, and the city enjoys some of its most pleasant weather. Autumn is also the season of heavy rains from Gulf storms, which can bring dangerous floods.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for live music on Sixth Street and South Congress, the South by Southwest festival, barbecue and tacos, swimming at Barton Springs, and the bats emerging from the Congress Avenue Bridge on summer evenings.`,contrasts:[{label:`Fredericksburg & the Hill Country (≈110 km W, ≈350 m higher)`,note:`Up on the Edwards Plateau, the air is drier, nights are cooler, and winter frosts are more frequent.`},{label:`Houston (≈240 km E)`,note:`On the coastal plain, the air is more humid and the summer nights stickier, with more rain and a greater hurricane risk.`}],history:[`On 30 December 1842, a group of men sent by President Sam Houston of the Republic of Texas quietly began loading the government's archives onto wagons in Austin. Houston, who had always hated the frontier capital, wanted to move the government to his own namesake city. The people of Austin were determined to stop him, because they knew that whoever held the archives held the capital. A boarding-house keeper named Angelina Eberly saw what was happening and fired a cannon at the men, blowing a hole in the General Land Office building. The townspeople chased the wagons and recovered the archives. The Archive War, as it was called, kept Austin the capital, and a statue of Eberly firing her cannon now stands downtown.`,`Long before Texas became a republic, the Tonkawa, Lipan Apache and Comanche hunted along the Colorado River and drank from the springs at the foot of the Balcones Escarpment. In 1839 the Republic of Texas chose a tiny settlement called Waterloo as the site of its new capital and renamed it Austin, after Stephen F. Austin, the father of Texas. The city was laid out on a grid between two creeks. In 1888 a new capitol building was completed, built of pink granite and paid for by trading three million acres of land in the Panhandle to the builders; it is taller than the US Capitol in Washington.`,`The University of Texas opened in 1883, and Austin grew as a government and university town. In the 1970s it became a magnet for musicians, as Willie Nelson and other country rebels played at the Armadillo World Headquarters and the television show Austin City Limits began broadcasting in 1974. In 1984 a university student named Michael Dell started a computer company in his dorm room, and Austin became a tech hub. Its population has exploded since the 1990s, bringing traffic, high housing costs and fears that the city is losing the weirdness that made it famous.`],deepTitle:`Capital of weird`,deep:[`Every evening from spring to autumn, about 1.5 million Mexican free-tailed bats emerge from crevices under the Congress Avenue Bridge, forming a dark ribbon across the sky as they head out to feed on insects. The bats moved in after the bridge was rebuilt in 1980, and at first residents feared them, but they have become one of Austin's most popular attractions; it is the largest urban bat colony in North America.`,`Barton Springs, a natural spring-fed pool in Zilker Park, stays at about 20°C all year. It was sacred to Native peoples and became a municipal swimming pool in 1918. Its waters are home to the Barton Springs salamander, an endangered species found nowhere else, and battles over development that might pollute the springs have shaped Austin's politics for decades.`,`The Texas Capitol was built by convicts and stonemasons, including Scottish granite cutters brought over to work the stone. The builders were paid with land in the Texas Panhandle, which became the XIT Ranch, once the largest cattle ranch in the world under a single fence. The capitol's dome rises higher than the US Capitol's, a fact Texans like to point out.`,`South by Southwest, which began in 1987 as a small music conference, has grown into one of the world's largest festivals of music, film and technology. Austin City Limits, the longest-running music programme in American television history, has featured performers from Willie Nelson to Radiohead. The city's unofficial slogan, Keep Austin Weird, was coined in 2000 to support local businesses.`]},"naples-fl":{why:`Southwest Florida’s Gulf shelf is shallow and warm, so Naples has a narrow annual temperature range compared with interior Florida: sea-breeze caps on peak heat, humid nights, and only brief cool-front interruptions. Most rain falls in a summer convective season; winter is the dry tourist pulse. Elevation near 1 m means tide, surge, and sheet-flow flooding are not rare events but the coastal condition. Mangrove estuaries and barrier beaches are the landform. The luxury surface sits on that hydrology.`,immersive:`Gulf water in late summer is bath-warm; the air at night does not dry. Sea-breeze storms build inland while the beach can stay brighter, a daily migration of cloud. Winter is the season people buy: fronts are short, afternoons in the mid-20s °C, and light that makes mangrove edges look sharp. You smell sulfur mud at low tide and jasmine in Old Naples. Sand is everywhere, including in the politics of whose ground floor will take the next surge. Hurricanes are not a theme. They are a rebuilding calendar. Between storms the place feels slow and expensive, a barrier-island quiet that depends on pumps, insurance, and a road out through the Glades.`,feel:`Naples feels like a polished beach town at the edge of a wilderness. On one side lie the white sands and calm turquoise waters of the Gulf of Mexico, with sunsets that draw crowds to the old pier every evening; on the other, the mangroves, cypress swamps and sawgrass of the Everglades stretch away to the horizon. Winters are dry, sunny and warm — the reason everyone comes — while summers are hot and drenched by daily thunderstorms, with hurricanes the great threat.`,seasons:{winter:`The dry, golden season — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 14°C, and sunshine on most days, with only the occasional cold front bringing a cool, breezy spell. The Gulf is cool but swimmable on warm days, the snowbirds fill the town, and wading birds crowd the drying wetlands of the Everglades.`,spring:`Warm, dry and bright — afternoons rising from about 27°C in March to 31°C by late May, with clear skies, calm Gulf waters and rain still scarce until the wet season begins in late May. The winter visitors drift away after Easter, and the Gulf warms for swimming.`,summer:`Hot, humid and thunderous — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 24°C, and towering thunderstorms that build almost every afternoon, dumping much of the year's rain in torrential bursts. Mosquitoes thrive, the Gulf is as warm as bathwater, and the town is quiet, with many winter residents gone north.`,autumn:`Hurricane season at its peak, then the return of the dry season — August through October bring the greatest risk of tropical storms and the heaviest rains, but by November the humidity drops, afternoons ease to about 28°C, and the snowbirds begin to return.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Gulf beaches and sunsets, the boutiques of Fifth Avenue South, golf, boating among the Ten Thousand Islands and airboat rides and wildlife in the Everglades and Big Cypress swamps.`,contrasts:[{label:`Immokalee (≈55 km NE, inland)`,note:`Away from the Gulf breeze, summer afternoons run a little hotter, and on clear winter nights the inland farm country can drop close to freezing.`},{label:`Miami (≈170 km E, Atlantic side)`,note:`Across the Everglades, Miami's winters are slightly warmer and its summers similar, with Atlantic trade winds instead of Gulf breezes.`}],history:[`In the early 1920s, an advertising millionaire named Barron Collier, who had made his fortune selling ads on streetcars, began buying land in southwest Florida — so much of it that he became the largest landowner in the state, with more than half a million hectares. In 1923 he struck a deal with the Florida legislature: if they would create a new county and name it after him, he would pay to finish the Tamiami Trail, a highway across the Everglades from Tampa to Miami that had stalled in the swamps. The legislature agreed. Workers blasted their way through the limestone and sawgrass with dynamite, battling mosquitoes, snakes and heat, and the road opened in 1928. Collier County and its seat, Naples, were finally connected to the world.`,`For centuries this coast belonged to the Calusa, a powerful people who built towns on huge mounds of shell, ruled much of South Florida and resisted the Spanish for two hundred years. In 1896 archaeologists digging at Key Marco, just south of Naples, found a trove of Calusa wooden objects preserved in the mud, including a beautifully carved figure of a panther-like cat now in the Smithsonian. After the Calusa vanished in the 1700s, Seminole and Miccosukee people took refuge in the Everglades. In 1885 two Kentuckians, a newspaper publisher and a former Confederate general, laid out a winter resort here, comparing the bay to the Bay of Naples in Italy, and built a pier in 1888.`,`For decades Naples remained a tiny resort reachable mainly by boat. After the Tamiami Trail and the railroad arrived in the late 1920s, it slowly grew, and after the Second World War, pilots who had trained at its airfield came back to retire. The town became one of the wealthiest communities in America, known for its beaches, golf courses and luxury homes. Hurricanes have repeatedly battered it: Donna in 1960, Wilma in 2005, Irma in 2017 and Ian in 2022, whose storm surge flooded much of the town.`],deepTitle:`Swamp highway`,deep:[`The Tamiami Trail took thirteen years to build. Engineers used dredges to dig canals alongside the route and piled the dredged limestone into a raised roadbed, while workers lived in camps in the swamp. In 1923, to promote the project, a group of motorists called the Trail Blazers tried to drive across the unfinished route; they got lost in the Everglades for weeks, and newspapers reported them missing before they emerged.`,`The Calusa were among the most complex societies in Native North America. They built their power on the rich fisheries of the estuaries rather than on farming, constructed canals and shell islands, and collected tribute from other towns across South Florida. Disease and slave raids destroyed them in the eighteenth century. The Key Marco cat, a small wooden figure about 15 cm tall, is one of the finest pieces of Native American art ever found.`,`The Naples Pier, built in 1888 to bring passengers and freight to the town, has been destroyed and rebuilt several times by hurricanes and fire. It is the town's best-known landmark and a favourite place to watch the sunset and spot dolphins. After Hurricane Ian damaged it badly in 2022, it was rebuilt once more.`,`The Everglades, just east of Naples, are a vast, slow-moving river of grass that once covered much of South Florida. Draining and development shrank them by half, and Collier County's farms grow tomatoes and other winter vegetables on land that was once wetland. Conservation efforts, including Big Cypress National Preserve and Everglades National Park, now protect much of what remains.`]},"buffalo-ny":{why:`Lake Erie’s long west-to-east fetch aims snowbands at Buffalo whenever cold air crosses still-open water. Shoreline convergence and urban roughness help lock streamers in place, so totals are measured in feet on the right synoptic day. Summer reverses the ledger: lake breezes cool the waterfront while convection fires inland along the breeze front. Ice cover, when it forms, shuts the snow machine down. That on-off switch is the climate. A Dfa label without the lake would miss the entire winter identity.`,immersive:`A lake-effect day is a narrow world: gray stratus, stinging snow, and a radar image that looks like a hose aimed at the eastern shore. Streets a few miles apart can differ by a foot of snow. When the lake freezes or the wind turns, the sky can empty. Summer is the other Buffalo—humid, green, and cooled at the waterfront when the breeze is on. You smell the lake and, in older industrial weather, a metallic edge from the river. Wings and grain elevators are the postcard; the body remembers shoveling and the sudden thaw. Niagara’s mist is a day trip. The city’s own climate is Erie’s fetch written onto streets.`,feel:`Buffalo feels like a city that takes its weather personally. At the eastern end of Lake Erie, it is famous for winter snowstorms that roll in off the lake, sometimes burying one part of the region under metres of snow while another stays almost clear. But the same lake makes its summers among the most pleasant of any big American city — breezy, sunny and rarely too hot — and its autumns long and golden. Grand architecture, chicken wings and a fierce civic pride complete the picture.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and snowy — afternoons just above freezing, nights near −6°C, and lake-effect snow bands that can drop a metre or more in a day on narrow strips of the region, especially the southern suburbs. Once Lake Erie freezes, usually by late January, the heaviest snows ease. Blizzards like those of 1977 and 2022 are part of local legend, and the city's snow-removal crews are legendary too.`,spring:`Late and cool — afternoons warming from about 4°C in March to 20°C by late May, with snow possible into April and the lake's cold water keeping the shoreline chilly. Ice jams pile up at the mouth of the Niagara River, and the city's tulips and magnolias bloom in May.`,summer:`Warm, breezy and comfortable — afternoons near 26°C, nights around 16°C, and lake breezes that keep the city cooler than most of the Northeast. Sunny days are common, and the waterfront, parks and festivals are full. Buffalo is one of the few big American cities that has never recorded 38°C.`,autumn:`Long and lovely — the warm lake keeps September afternoons near 23°C, with crisp, clear days and brilliant colour by mid-October. In November the cold winds return, and the first lake-effect snow can arrive in a sudden burst.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Niagara Falls half an hour away, Frank Lloyd Wright's Darwin Martin House, the Olmsted parks, chicken wings at their birthplace, Bills football and the revived waterfront at Canalside.`,residentFit:`It suits families and city people who want an affordable, friendly city with great architecture and a strong community spirit, and who embrace lake-effect snowstorms, grey winters and a short, glorious summer.`,contrasts:[{label:`The Southtowns (≈20 km SE)`,note:`The suburbs south of the city, like Orchard Park, often catch the heaviest lake-effect bands; in November 2014 some got nearly 2 m of snow in three days while downtown got little.`},{label:`Toronto (≈90 km NW, across Lake Ontario)`,note:`On the north shore of Lake Ontario, Toronto gets far less lake-effect snow, though its winters are similarly cold.`}],history:[`In 1901 Buffalo hosted the Pan-American Exposition, a world's fair whose buildings were lit at night by tens of thousands of electric bulbs powered by the new hydroelectric plant at Niagara Falls. Buffalo called itself the City of Light. On 6 September, President William McKinley stood in a receiving line at the fair's Temple of Music shaking hands with visitors. A young anarchist named Leon Czolgosz stepped forward with a revolver hidden under a handkerchief and shot him twice. McKinley died eight days later, and Theodore Roosevelt, who had rushed to Buffalo, was sworn in as president in a friend's house on Delaware Avenue. The house is now a museum.`,`The Seneca, part of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, lived along Buffalo Creek until they were pushed onto reservations in the nineteenth century. Buffalo was a small village until 1825, when the Erie Canal opened, connecting the Great Lakes to the Hudson River and New York City. Buffalo became the place where grain and goods from the West were transferred from lake ships to canal boats, and it boomed. In 1842 a merchant named Joseph Dart built the world's first steam-powered grain elevator, and Buffalo became the greatest grain port in the world. It was also the last stop on the Underground Railroad for many people escaping slavery, who crossed the Niagara River to freedom in Canada.`,`By 1900 Buffalo was one of the largest and richest cities in America, a centre of steel, flour milling and manufacturing, with grand buildings by Louis Sullivan and Frank Lloyd Wright and a park system designed by Frederick Law Olmsted. Two of its citizens, Millard Fillmore and Grover Cleveland, became president. After the Second World War, the St. Lawrence Seaway let ships bypass the city, and the steel mills and factories closed, and Buffalo lost half its population. In recent years it has revived its waterfront and its historic neighbourhoods. In 1964, at the Anchor Bar, a cook named Teressa Bellissimo fried some chicken wings, tossed them in hot sauce and served them with celery and blue cheese, and the Buffalo wing was born.`],deepTitle:`City of Light`,deep:[`The grain elevators of Buffalo, huge concrete silos lining the Buffalo River, were admired by European architects such as Le Corbusier and Walter Gropius as models of modern design. Many still stand, some now used for climbing walls, concerts and art installations. Joseph Dart's invention, a steam-powered conveyor of buckets that lifted grain from ships, made it possible to unload a ship in hours rather than days.`,`Buffalo's architecture is among the finest in the country. Louis Sullivan's Guaranty Building of 1896 was one of the first skyscrapers, and Frank Lloyd Wright designed the Darwin Martin House, a masterpiece of his Prairie style, for a Buffalo businessman. Olmsted's system of parks connected by tree-lined parkways, begun in 1868, was the first of its kind in the country.`,`The Blizzard of 1977 struck on 28 January, when hurricane-force winds whipped the snow already lying on the frozen lake into the city, burying cars and houses in drifts several metres high and killing more than twenty people in the region. In December 2022 another blizzard killed more than forty people in Erie County, many of them trapped in their cars. Buffalonians are used to snow, but these storms are remembered as among the worst in the city's history.`,`Grover Cleveland was a Buffalo lawyer and sheriff who personally hanged two convicted murderers; he became mayor of Buffalo in 1882, governor of New York in 1883 and president in 1885. Millard Fillmore, an earlier Buffalo lawyer, became president in 1850 after Zachary Taylor's death, helped found the University at Buffalo, and is buried in the city's Forest Lawn Cemetery.`]},"scottsbluff-ne":{why:`The North Platte Valley is a watered trench in shortgrass steppe. Irrigation from Wyoming reservoirs and local canals makes beets and beans possible where 380 mm of precipitation would not. Escarpments—Wildcat Hills, Pine Ridge, the bluff itself—add mesoscale wind and rapid chinook replacements of Arctic air that flatter counties to the east feel less often. Elevation near 1,200 m stretches the diurnal range. The town’s climate is river plus scarp, not generic High Plains.`,immersive:`Scotts Bluff stands like a dry mesa at the valley’s edge, a landmark you can read from miles of prairie. Wind is the default weather. Summer afternoons near 33 °C build dryline towers; winter can be −20 °C and then a chinook that smells like dust and thaw. Irrigation laterals cut green rectangles through shortgrass. The light is hard and long. You hear grain trucks and, on still nights, coyotes off the Wildcat Hills. Snow is usually thin except when an upslope event loads the scarp. The body learns sun and wind before it learns trees. This is a river oasis with a famous rock, not a forest town.`,feel:`Scottsbluff feels like the threshold of the West. Out of the rolling plains of western Nebraska, the great sandstone bluff that gave the town its name rises some 250 m above the North Platte River, a landmark that told thousands of pioneers they were leaving the prairie for the mountains. The air is dry, the sky enormous and the wind persistent; summers are hot and sunny, winters cold but often broken by warm chinook days, and storms sweep across the plains in spring and summer.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, dry and windy — afternoons near 6°C, nights near −9°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −25°C, followed by chinook winds that can raise it by 20°C in a few hours. Snow is usually light but blows and drifts in the wind, and the bluffs stand out sharply against the pale winter sky.`,spring:`Windy and unpredictable — afternoons climbing from about 11°C in March to 22°C by late May, with late snowstorms possible into May and strong winds that can whip up dust. Thunderstorms and hail arrive in May, and the river valley turns green.`,summer:`Hot, dry and bright — afternoons near 31°C, often well above 35°C, but nights cool to around 14°C in the dry air. Afternoon thunderstorms sometimes bring hail and violent wind. Irrigated fields of sugar beets, corn and beans fill the valley, green against the brown hills.`,autumn:`Clear and crisp — September afternoons near 26°C easing to about 11°C by November, cool nights and calm, sunny days. The sugar-beet harvest fills the trucks and factories, and the first snow can fall in October.`},residentFit:`It suits farm and ranch families, healthcare and remote workers who want a small, affordable town on the high plains with dry, sunny weather, and who can take wind, hail and cold winters.`,contrasts:[{label:`Cheyenne, WY (≈130 km SW, ≈650 m higher)`,note:`Up on the high plains near the Rockies, Cheyenne is windier and cooler, with more snow.`},{label:`North Platte (≈250 km E, ≈350 m lower)`,note:`Further down the river, North Platte's summers are a little more humid and its year brings more rain.`}],history:[`In 1828 a fur trader named Hiram Scott fell gravely ill on the way back from the annual trappers' rendezvous in the Rocky Mountains. His companions, according to the story later told by fur traders, were supposed to wait for him but abandoned him and pushed on east, reporting that he had died. The following year, trappers passing the great bluff on the North Platte River found a skeleton nearby — Scott's bones, they believed, lying some 100 km from where he had been left. He had crawled all that way, alone, before dying. Washington Irving retold the story, and the bluff has carried Scott's name ever since.`,`Between the 1840s and the 1860s, some 350,000 people travelled the Oregon, California and Mormon trails along the North Platte, and for many of them Scotts Bluff and Chimney Rock, a tall spire about 30 km to the east, were the most memorable sights of the journey; Chimney Rock is the landmark most often mentioned in pioneer diaries. The wagon trains squeezed through Mitchell Pass beside the bluff, and the ruts they wore can still be seen. The Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho, whose hunting grounds the trails crossed, increasingly clashed with the emigrants and the army that followed them.`,`In the early twentieth century, federal irrigation projects brought water from dams in Wyoming to the North Platte valley, and farmers planted sugar beets. In 1910 the Great Western Sugar Company opened a factory in Scottsbluff, and German-Russian, Japanese and Mexican workers came to tend the fields. In 1919 the bluff became a national monument. Today Scottsbluff is the main town of the Nebraska Panhandle, and the monument's museum displays paintings by William Henry Jackson, the pioneer photographer, who passed the bluff as a young bullwhacker on a wagon train in 1866.`],deepTitle:`Trail landmark`,deep:[`William Henry Jackson later became one of the most famous photographers of the American West, and his photographs of Yellowstone helped persuade Congress to create the first national park in 1872. In his old age, he painted scenes of the Oregon Trail based on his memories, and the largest collection of his paintings and sketches is displayed at Scotts Bluff National Monument.`,`Chimney Rock, a slender spire of clay and sandstone rising some 90 m above the plain, was a landmark visible for days to pioneers walking along the trail. Many travellers stopped to carve their names in its base, and some tried to climb it. Erosion has shortened it over the years, but it still stands, now a national historic site.`,`North of Scottsbluff lies Agate Fossil Beds National Monument, where hills of rock are packed with the bones of animals that lived about 20 million years ago, including small rhinoceroses and giant pigs. It also preserves strange spiral fossils called devil's corkscrews, the burrows of an ancient beaver. The rancher who owned the land, James Cook, was a friend of the Lakota chief Red Cloud, and gifts from Red Cloud's people are displayed at the site.`,`The sugar-beet industry brought many different peoples to the valley. German-speaking families from Russia, who had farmed beets on the steppes, arrived in large numbers, followed by Japanese and then Mexican workers. The beet harvest is still a major event each autumn, though most of the old factories have closed.`]},"mobile-al":{why:`Mobile Bay and the Mississippi Sound feed the city a nearly continuous Gulf moisture stream. Sea-breeze collisions with interior heat build daily summer storms; tropical cyclones can unload multi-day rains on a town that already ranks among the wettest large U.S. cities. Elevation of a few meters and an estuarine setting mean surge and river water share the same streets. Live-oak shade and maritime air take the edge off peak heat without making the humidity optional. The climate is water, incoming.`,immersive:`Summer in Mobile is a wet blanket that thunderstorms briefly tear. Afternoons near 33 °C feel heavier than the number; nights stay in the low 20s °C. Live oaks and azaleas hold the older neighborhoods in a green dark. Winter is the relief: camellias, rare light frost, and a sky that can actually stay clear. You smell the bay, paper-mill memory, and rain on hot pavement. Mardi Gras beads in February sit beside a calendar that already knows hurricane names. Floodwater in a low lot is not a surprise. It is the bay reminding the street grid that this was marsh and delta before it was a port.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and damp — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 6°C, and a mix of grey rain and bright, cool days after cold fronts. A few freezes come each winter. Camellias, the city flower, bloom in the gardens, and Mardi Gras parades fill the streets in February with floats, masked riders and showers of beads and Moon Pies.`,spring:`Warm, blooming and stormy — afternoons rising from about 22°C in March to 30°C by late May, azaleas blazing along the streets, and some of the heaviest rains of the year in powerful thunderstorms, with occasional tornado warnings. The humidity climbs steadily toward summer.`,summer:`Hot, steamy and very wet — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 23°C, and thunderstorms nearly every afternoon that make Mobile one of the rainiest cities in the United States. The bay breeze helps a little near the water. Hurricane season looms, and people take refuge in the shade of the live oaks and on the beaches of Dauphin Island.`,autumn:`Hurricane season, then sweet relief — September is hot and humid, with afternoons near 31°C and the greatest risk of tropical storms from the Gulf, but by late October the humidity breaks and the city enjoys clear, warm days and cool nights, with afternoons near 22°C by November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the oldest Mardi Gras in the United States, the battleship USS Alabama, antebellum streets and azalea gardens, Gulf seafood and boat trips into the vast Mobile-Tensaw Delta.`,contrasts:[{label:`Dauphin Island (≈50 km S, at the mouth of the bay)`,note:`Out on the barrier island, the Gulf breeze keeps summer afternoons a couple of degrees cooler, but the island is fully exposed to hurricanes.`},{label:`Montgomery (≈250 km NE)`,note:`Inland, summer afternoons run hotter, winters are colder, and the year brings noticeably less rain.`}],history:[`In 1860 a wealthy Mobile shipbuilder named Timothy Meaher is said to have bet some acquaintances that he could smuggle a shipload of Africans into the city, even though the international slave trade had been illegal in the United States for more than fifty years. He hired a captain, William Foster, who sailed the schooner Clotilda to West Africa and returned in July 1860 with 110 captives. To hide the evidence, Foster burned the ship in the swamps north of Mobile. After emancipation the survivors, unable to return home, bought land and founded their own town, Africatown, where they kept their languages and customs. One of them, Oluale Kossola, known as Cudjo Lewis, told his story to the writer Zora Neale Hurston in 1927. The wreck of the Clotilda was finally found in 2019.`,`In 1702 French colonists founded a settlement on the Mobile River, the first capital of French Louisiana, and moved it to the present site on the bay in 1711. The Mobilian, Choctaw and other peoples traded with the French, and Mobile became a cosmopolitan colonial town. It passed to Britain in 1763, to Spain in 1780 and to the United States in 1813. The French brought their Carnival celebrations, and Mobile claims to have held the first Mardi Gras in what is now the United States, years before New Orleans. In the nineteenth century the city grew rich shipping cotton from the plantations of Alabama and Mississippi.`,`On 5 August 1864, Admiral David Farragut led a Union fleet into Mobile Bay past Confederate forts and a field of underwater mines, then called torpedoes. When one of his ships was sunk by a mine and the others hesitated, Farragut is said to have shouted his famous order: Damn the torpedoes! Full speed ahead! The Union won the battle, closing one of the Confederacy's last major ports. After the war Mobile became a shipbuilding, paper and chemical centre, and the home of two of baseball's greatest players, Satchel Paige and Hank Aaron. Hurricanes, including Frederic in 1979 and Sally in 2020, have repeatedly battered the city.`],deepTitle:`Clotilda, Carnival`,deep:[`Africatown, north of Mobile, became one of the most remarkable communities in the United States, a town founded by Africans who remembered their homeland. Its founders built a church and a school and governed themselves according to their own customs. Zora Neale Hurston's book about Kossola, Barracoon, was not published until 2018. The discovery of the Clotilda wreck brought new attention to the community, which has struggled with industrial pollution from nearby plants.`,`Mobile's Carnival tradition dates back to 1703, when the French colonists held a celebration in their first settlement. After the Civil War, a Mobile clerk named Joe Cain revived the celebrations in 1866 by parading through the streets dressed as a Chickasaw chief, defying the Union occupation. The Sunday before Mardi Gras is still called Joe Cain Day, and Mobile's mystic societies hold balls and parades much like those of New Orleans.`,`The battleship USS Alabama, which fought in the Pacific during the Second World War, is moored as a museum in Mobile Bay. In 1964 schoolchildren across Alabama raised money with nickels and dimes to bring it home, and it opened to visitors in 1965. A submarine and dozens of aircraft are displayed alongside it.`,`Mobile's rainfall, around 1,700 mm a year, comes partly from the warm Gulf of Mexico, which supplies moisture for frequent thunderstorms, and partly from tropical storms. The city lies close to sea level at the head of a shallow bay, so storm surges from hurricanes can push water far inland, as they did during Katrina in 2005, when downtown streets flooded.`]},"savannah-ga":{why:`Savannah sits on a bluff above an estuary, with a broad shelf and warm Gulf Stream water offshore. Sea breezes and a live-oak canopy take the edge off inland Georgia heat, while humidity and tropical moisture still define summer. Winters are mild with occasional fronts. The Georgia Bight’s geometry aims some cyclone tracks at the coast; surge and river flooding share the low wards. It is a maritime Cfa, not Atlanta’s interior version of the same letters.`,immersive:`Spanish moss moves when the sea breeze finally arrives, usually after a still, wet morning. Summer afternoons near 33 °C feel slower under oaks than they do on the asphalt of the industrial east. Night holds the day’s moisture. Winter can be shirt-sleeve and then a brief freeze that browns the palms. You smell pluff mud at low tide, gardenias in April, and diesel from the port. Squares break the grid into rooms of shade. Thunderstorms and the odd tropical system are the price of that long growing season. Tybee’s beach is saltier and windier; downtown is a humid garden with ships at the end of the street.`,feel:`Savannah feels like a city in a slow, romantic dream. Its historic heart is a grid of leafy squares shaded by live oaks draped in Spanish moss, lined with antebellum mansions, churches and cobblestone lanes running down to the river. The climate is warm and humid for much of the year, with long, sultry summers and short, mild winters, and the scent of jasmine and magnolia hangs in the air in spring. Hurricanes and high tides are the price of its beauty.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and pleasant — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 6°C, and many sunny, crisp days with the occasional cold snap. Frost visits a few times a season, and snow is a rare event. Camellias bloom in the gardens, and the squares are quiet and green.`,spring:`Savannah's glory — afternoons warming from about 22°C in March to 30°C by late May, azaleas exploding into colour in March, followed by dogwoods, wisteria and magnolias. St. Patrick's Day brings one of the largest parades in the country, and the humidity gradually returns by May.`,summer:`Hot, humid and stormy — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 23°C, and almost daily afternoon thunderstorms that cool the streets briefly. The sea breeze helps near Tybee Island. People slow down, and the shade of the oaks and the river breeze are precious.`,autumn:`Warm then lovely — August and September are the peak of hurricane season, but by October the humidity drops, afternoons ease to about 26°C, and the city enjoys some of its most comfortable weather. The marshes turn gold, and the oak leaves stay green.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the leafy squares of the historic district, Spanish moss and antebellum mansions, the riverfront, ghost tours, Low Country cooking, the art school's galleries and the beaches of Tybee Island.`,residentFit:`It suits artists, students, retirees and port and hospitality workers who love the history and the mild winters, and who can manage hot, humid summers, hurricanes and rising flood risk.`,contrasts:[{label:`Tybee Island (≈25 km E)`,note:`Out on the beach, ocean breezes keep summer afternoons a few degrees cooler, but the island takes the full force of Atlantic storms.`},{label:`Augusta (≈200 km NW, inland)`,note:`Up the Savannah River at the fall line, summer afternoons run a couple of degrees hotter, and winter nights are colder.`}],history:[`On 22 December 1864, General William Tecumseh Sherman, whose army had just completed its devastating March to the Sea across Georgia, sent a telegram to President Lincoln: I beg to present you as a Christmas gift the City of Savannah, with 150 heavy guns and plenty of ammunition, also about 25,000 bales of cotton. The city's mayor had surrendered without a fight, and Sherman, charmed by its beauty, spared it the destruction he had inflicted on Atlanta. Savannah's elegant squares and mansions survived the war almost untouched, and they are the reason it is one of the most beautiful cities in America today.`,`In February 1733 the English general James Oglethorpe landed on a bluff above the Savannah River with about 120 colonists to found the colony of Georgia. The Yamacraw people and their leader, Tomochichi, welcomed them, and a woman of Creek and English descent, Mary Musgrove, served as interpreter. Oglethorpe laid out the town as a grid of wards, each built around a public square, a plan that is still admired by city planners. Georgia was intended as a buffer against Spanish Florida and a refuge for the poor, and at first it banned slavery, but the ban was lifted in 1751, and rice and cotton plantations spread along the coast.`,`Savannah grew into a busy port shipping cotton and timber. In 1912 Juliette Gordon Low, a Savannah native, gathered eighteen girls in her hometown to found the Girl Scouts of America. In the 1950s, when the city was tearing down its old buildings for parking lots, seven women formed the Historic Savannah Foundation to save the Davenport House from demolition, starting one of the most successful preservation movements in the country. John Berendt's 1994 book Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil, about a murder in one of Savannah's mansions, made the city a tourist magnet. Its port is now one of the busiest container ports in the United States.`],deepTitle:`Squares and moss`,deep:[`Oglethorpe's plan for Savannah divided the town into wards, each with a central square surrounded by lots for houses and public buildings. As the city grew, more wards were added, and eventually there were 24 squares, of which 22 survive. The squares, shaded by live oaks and filled with monuments and fountains, give the city its distinctive, walkable character.`,`Tomochichi, the Yamacraw leader, became a close ally of Oglethorpe and travelled with him to London in 1734, where he met King George II. When he died in 1739, Oglethorpe served as a pallbearer, and he was buried in one of Savannah's squares. A granite boulder now marks his grave in Wright Square.`,`The First African Baptist Church, organised in the 1770s, is one of the oldest Black congregations in North America. Its building, completed in 1859 by enslaved and free Black members, has holes drilled in the floor in a pattern that may have provided air to people hiding beneath it on the Underground Railroad. The church also played a central role in the civil rights movement in Savannah.`,`Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil told the true story of Jim Williams, an antiques dealer who shot his assistant in his mansion, the Mercer House, and was tried four times before being acquitted. The book spent four years on the bestseller list and drew crowds of tourists, who now take tours of the places it describes.`]},"wilmington-de":{why:`Wilmington occupies the fall line, where Piedmont rock meets coastal-plain sediments and Brandywine Creek cuts a gorge steep enough to power mills. The Christina and Delaware rivers bring estuarine moisture; the city is slightly more continental than the New Jersey shore and slightly less hot than interior Piedmont towns on some summer days. Small hills and the gorge organize night drainage. Urban fabric and Chesapeake–Delaware humidity do the rest. It is a Cfa hinge, not a mountain climate and not quite the open bay.`,immersive:`Humidity arrives from the Delaware and the Chesapeake even when the Brandywine gorge feels a degree cooler. Summer afternoons near 30 °C, winter nights that can drop below −3 °C, and a long green season in mixed forest. After rain the creek runs the color of Piedmont clay. You smell tidewater mud downtown and leaf mold in the parks above the millrace. Nor’easters and remnant tropical rains both flood the same low streets. The corporate towers are a dry indoor climate; the lived city is a wet Mid-Atlantic terrace with a rocky creek as its original engine.`,feel:`Wilmington feels like a small city with old money in its hills. Delaware's largest city grew up where Brandywine Creek tumbles off the Piedmont into the Christina River, and the du Pont family's gunpowder mills along the Brandywine became the root of one of America's great industrial fortunes, leaving grand estates, gardens and museums scattered through the wooded valley. The climate is humid and four-seasoned: summers hot and sticky, winters cold, grey and changeable, and spring and autumn long and lovely.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and changeable — afternoons near 7°C, nights just below freezing, and a mix of rain, sleet and snow from nor'easters and clipper storms. Snow comes several times a winter, though it often melts quickly. The Brandywine's gorge holds ice and the bare trees reveal the old mill ruins along its banks.`,spring:`Fresh and flowering — afternoons warming from about 12°C in March to 24°C by late May, the gardens of the Brandywine Valley — Winterthur, Nemours and nearby Longwood — bursting into bloom, and frequent rain showers. Late frosts are possible into April.`,summer:`Hot and humid — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 19°C, and afternoon thunderstorms that roll across the Piedmont. Heat waves can push temperatures above 35°C. The shady Brandywine Park and the riverfront are the city's cool retreats.`,autumn:`Mild and colourful — September afternoons near 26°C easing to about 13°C by November, with crisp, clear days and the forests of the Brandywine Valley turning red and gold by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the du Pont estates and gardens of the Brandywine Valley — Winterthur, Nemours, Hagley and nearby Longwood — the Delaware Art Museum and the riverfront, within easy reach of Philadelphia.`,contrasts:[{label:`Philadelphia (≈45 km NE)`,note:`The bigger city up the Delaware has a nearly identical climate, with slightly warmer nights from its larger urban heat island.`},{label:`Lewes & the Delaware beaches (≈150 km S)`,note:`Down on the coast, ocean and bay breezes keep summer afternoons a couple of degrees cooler, and winters are milder.`}],history:[`For more than forty years, a Quaker merchant named Thomas Garrett ran a hardware store in Wilmington and helped people escaping slavery find their way north. Wilmington was the last major stop before the free state of Pennsylvania, and Garrett sheltered, fed, clothed and guided around 2,700 people to freedom, including many brought by Harriet Tubman, who considered him one of her closest friends. In 1848 a court fined him so heavily for helping a family escape that he was left bankrupt. As he left the courtroom, he reportedly told the judge that if anyone knew of a fugitive who needed help, they should send them to him. When Garrett died in 1871, Black residents of Wilmington carried his coffin through the streets.`,`In 1638 two Swedish ships, the Kalmar Nyckel and the Fogel Grip, landed at a rocky point on the Christina River, and Swedish and Finnish settlers built Fort Christina, the first permanent European settlement in the Delaware Valley. They are credited with bringing the log cabin to America. The Dutch seized the colony in 1655, and the English took it in 1664. A town grew around the fort and was named Wilmington in 1739. In 1802 a French immigrant named Éleuthère Irénée du Pont built a gunpowder mill on the Brandywine River just outside the city, founding a company that would shape Wilmington's history for two centuries.`,`The DuPont company grew from gunpowder into chemicals, and in 1935 its researchers in Wilmington invented nylon, which made its debut in women's stockings in 1939. The city became a corporate capital, especially after Delaware's business-friendly laws made it the legal home of most of America's largest corporations. After the assassination of Martin Luther King Jr. in 1968, riots broke out, and the National Guard occupied the city for nine months, the longest such occupation of an American city since Reconstruction. Wilmington is also the hometown of President Joe Biden, who commuted daily by train to Washington during his decades in the Senate.`],deepTitle:`Powder and nylon`,deep:[`Making gunpowder was dangerous work. The du Pont mills on the Brandywine were built with thick stone walls on three sides and light wooden roofs facing the river, so that any explosion would be directed out over the water. Explosions still happened; one in 1818 killed about forty workers. The mills and the du Pont family home are now part of the Hagley Museum.`,`Nylon was developed by a team led by the chemist Wallace Carothers at DuPont's Experimental Station on the Brandywine. When nylon stockings went on sale nationwide in 1940, women lined up to buy them, and millions of pairs sold in days. During the Second World War, nylon was used for parachutes and ropes, and stockings became scarce.`,`The Swedish and Finnish settlers of New Sweden built their homes from round logs notched at the corners, a technique they brought from the forests of Scandinavia. The log cabin spread across the American frontier and became a symbol of pioneer life. A replica of the Kalmar Nyckel, launched in 1997, sails from Wilmington as Delaware's tall ship.`,`The du Pont family built grand estates around Wilmington, including Winterthur, now a museum of American decorative arts with vast gardens, and Nemours, with formal French gardens. Pierre S. du Pont created Longwood Gardens just over the Pennsylvania line. These estates and gardens make the Brandywine Valley one of the great garden regions of the United States.`]},"mystic-ct":{why:`Mystic sits on a tidal river between Long Island Sound and Block Island Sound, so marine air is the default, not a sea-breeze special. Spring lags inland Connecticut; summer peaks stay milder than Hartford’s. Fog, drizzle, and nor’easter surf set the winter texture. Tropical cyclones are infrequent but the 1938 hurricane is still the local memory of what a surge can do to an estuary village. Dual-sound exposure tightens the maritime influence. The Dfb label hides how much the water paces the year.`,immersive:`Salt is in the air even when you cannot see the sounds. Mystic River turns with the tide under a bascule bridge; mornings can sit in fog while Groton’s hills are clear. Summer highs near 26 °C feel like a gift after a late spring. Winter is gray, wet, and occasionally wild when a nor’easter drives waves into Fishers Island Sound. You smell mudflats, diesel from working boats, and fried seafood from the tourist strip. Oak and pine hold the uplands. The village is small enough that a hurricane evacuation is a real map, not a drill for somewhere else.`,feel:`Mystic feels like a New England seaport frozen in a painting. White clapboard houses and steepled churches line the banks of the Mystic River, a drawbridge lifts for sailboats in the middle of town, and tall ships from the age of sail lie at the docks of a living museum. The sea keeps the summers mild and breezy and the winters milder than inland Connecticut, though nor'easters and the occasional hurricane remind the town of its exposure.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, raw and changeable — afternoons near 5°C, nights below freezing, and nor'easters that bring wind, rain and sometimes heavy snow, though the sea keeps snowfalls lighter than inland. The harbour is quiet, the tall ships are rigged for winter, and the village glows with holiday lights.`,spring:`Cool and slow — afternoons warming from about 8°C in March to 20°C by late May, fog and drizzle common as the sea stays cold, and spring arriving a week or two later than in Hartford. By May, lilacs and dogwoods bloom and the boats return to the water.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 26°C, nights around 17°C, and sea breezes that keep the shore comfortable when inland towns swelter. Sailboats fill the river and the sound, and visitors crowd the seaport, the aquarium and the pizza parlour made famous by a film.`,autumn:`Mild and golden — the sea holds its warmth, so September afternoons stay near 23°C, foliage turns colour by late October, and the hurricane season occasionally sends a storm up the coast.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Mystic Seaport Museum and its historic ships, the Mystic Aquarium, the drawbridge in the village, sailing on the sound, oysters and pizza made famous by the film Mystic Pizza.`,contrasts:[{label:`Hartford (≈75 km NW, inland)`,note:`Up the Connecticut River valley, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter, and winters are colder and snowier.`},{label:`Block Island (≈30 km SE, offshore)`,note:`Out in the open Atlantic, Block Island's summers are a little cooler and its winters milder still.`}],history:[`In 1841 a whaling ship named the Charles W. Morgan was launched in New Bedford, Massachusetts, and over the next eighty years she sailed on 37 voyages to hunt whales across the world's oceans, some of them lasting three years or more. When the whaling industry died, she was laid up and nearly forgotten, until in 1941 she was towed to Mystic, where a new museum of the sea had been founded. Today she is the last wooden whaling ship in the world, and in 2014, after a long restoration, she sailed again on a 38th voyage along the New England coast, the first time in nearly a century that she had been out on the open sea under her own sails.`,`The name Mystic comes from a Pequot word for a large river whose waters are driven into waves by tide or wind. The Pequot were the most powerful nation in this part of New England in the early 1600s, and their conflicts with English colonists exploded into war in 1637. On 26 May English soldiers, with their Mohegan and Narragansett allies, surrounded a fortified Pequot village on a hill above the Mystic River and set it on fire, killing several hundred people, many of them women, children and elders. The massacre broke the Pequot Nation. The survivors were enslaved, scattered or absorbed by other tribes, though the Mashantucket Pequot persevered and regained federal recognition in 1983.`,`From the late 1700s to the early 1900s, the shipyards along the Mystic River built more than six hundred vessels, including some of the fastest clipper ships ever launched; in 1860 the Mystic-built clipper Andrew Jackson sailed from New York to San Francisco in just under ninety days. As wooden shipbuilding faded, a group of local enthusiasts founded the Marine Historical Association in 1929, which grew into Mystic Seaport Museum, a re-created nineteenth-century seaport village. The film Mystic Pizza, set in a real pizza parlour in town, made the village famous in 1988, and nearby casinos on Pequot and Mohegan land now draw millions of visitors.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Charles W. Morgan's voyages took her to the Pacific, the Indian Ocean and the waters off South America in pursuit of sperm whales and right whales. Her crews came from around the world — Cape Verdeans, Pacific Islanders, Azoreans and Native Americans, as well as New Englanders. She brought back thousands of barrels of whale oil, used to light lamps and grease machines before kerosene replaced it.`,`The Mystic Massacre was described in gruesome detail by the English commander John Mason, who saw it as divine judgement. Historians today regard it as one of the darkest episodes of colonial America. A statue of Mason that once stood on the site was later moved to Windsor, Connecticut, after protests from the Pequot and others.`,`Mystic Seaport Museum includes a village of historic buildings moved from across New England, a working shipyard where wooden ships are restored, and a fleet of historic vessels. Its collections of ship models, figureheads, paintings and maritime artefacts are among the largest in the world. Nearby, Mystic Aquarium is known for its beluga whales and research on marine mammals.`,`Mystic is not a town at all but a village divided between the towns of Groton and Stonington by the river, and it has no government of its own. The Mystic River Bascule Bridge, built in 1922, still lifts every hour in summer to let boats pass, stopping traffic on Main Street and drawing crowds of onlookers.`]},"state-college-pa":{why:`Nittany Valley is a Ridge-and-Valley trough: long parallel mountains, a limestone floor, and cold-air drainage on still nights. State College sits in that enclosure, so radiative frosts can bite harder than a ridge a few miles away. Northwesterly flow after Great Lakes storms can drop lake-enhanced snow west of the main Allegheny crest. The university island of light and heat sits inside a farm-and-forest basin. It is central Pennsylvania with a stadium, not a Piedmont suburb.`,immersive:`Autumn is the postcard: ridges in color, a Saturday crowd, and nights that fall fast into the 5 °C range. Winter pools in the valley; a clear January morning can be several degrees colder on the floor than on Tussey Mountain. Summer is humid and green, highs near 28 °C, thunderstorms drifting along the ridges. Spring is late on the flats—maple and apple wait out the last freeze. You smell silage from Centre County barns and leaf smoke in town. The gap winds and the bowl geometry are why a short drive changes the frost date. Happy Valley is a climate name as much as a marketing one.`,feel:`State College feels like a university that grew a town around itself. It sits in the broad Nittany Valley in the heart of Pennsylvania, between long, forested ridges, and its life rises and falls with the academic calendar and the roar of football Saturdays. The climate is properly continental, with cold, snowy winters, warm summers and brilliant autumns, and cold air settles into the valley on still nights, making frost come early and leave late.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and snowy — afternoons near 3°C, nights near −6°C, and frequent snow from clipper storms and nor'easters, with lake-effect flurries occasionally reaching across the Alleghenies. Cold air pools in the valley on clear nights, and the ski slopes of nearby Tussey Mountain open when the cold allows.`,spring:`Slow and changeable — afternoons warming from about 8°C in March to 22°C by late May, with snow possible into April and frosts into May. The campus bursts into bloom by late April, and the trout streams of the valley are among the best in the East.`,summer:`Warm and green — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 15°C, and afternoon thunderstorms. The town is quieter with most students gone, and the summer arts festival fills the streets in July.`,autumn:`Crisp and glorious — September afternoons near 23°C cooling to about 10°C by November, the ridges ablaze with red and orange by mid-October, and football weekends that swell the town with more than a hundred thousand fans.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Penn State football at Beaver Stadium, the Arts Festival in July, hiking and mountain biking in Rothrock State Forest, and the fishing streams and fall colour of the Nittany Valley.`,contrasts:[{label:`Harrisburg (≈110 km SE, in the Susquehanna valley)`,note:`Lower and further from the mountains, Harrisburg has warmer summers, milder winters and less snow.`},{label:`Pittsburgh (≈180 km W)`,note:`West of the Alleghenies, Pittsburgh is cloudier and gets more frequent light snow, with a similar overall climate.`}],history:[`In 1904 a Penn State student named Joe Mason was on a baseball trip to Princeton, whose teams were represented by a fierce Bengal tiger. Penn State had no mascot at all. Thinking fast, Mason told the Princeton crowd that Penn State was represented by the Nittany Lion, a mountain lion of nearby Mount Nittany, the fiercest beast of them all, and that it would beat the tiger. Penn State won the game, and the invented mascot stuck. A limestone statue of the lion was unveiled on campus in 1942, and it is now one of the most photographed sights in Pennsylvania.`,`The Nittany Valley was hunted by the Lenape, Shawnee and Susquehannock peoples, and in the late eighteenth century settlers built iron furnaces in the valleys, fuelled by charcoal from the forests and ore from the ridges. In 1855 the state founded the Farmers' High School to teach scientific agriculture, and two local ironmasters donated land for the campus near their Centre Furnace. The school became the Agricultural College of Pennsylvania and then the Pennsylvania State College, and the town that grew up around it, incorporated in 1896, took its name from the college.`,`In the twentieth century, Penn State grew into one of the largest universities in the United States, famous for its engineering, agriculture and football. Its football stadium, Beaver Stadium, holds more than a hundred thousand fans, making it one of the largest stadiums in the world. The long-time coach Joe Paterno became a legend, until a child abuse scandal involving one of his former assistants, revealed in 2011, shook the university. Today State College is a busy college town of students, researchers and retirees, surrounded by farmland and forested mountains.`],deepTitle:`Lion and creamery`,deep:[`Penn State's Berkey Creamery, which began making dairy products in the 1860s, is famous for its ice cream, made from milk from the university's own cows. Its short course in ice cream making, the oldest of its kind in the country, has trained thousands of people; in 1977 Ben Cohen and Jerry Greenfield took a correspondence version of the course before founding Ben & Jerry's.`,`The iron industry of Centre County flourished in the early nineteenth century. Centre Furnace, built in 1792, used charcoal from the surrounding forests to smelt iron ore, and its iron was hauled to markets in Pittsburgh and Philadelphia. The furnace stack still stands on the edge of campus, and its ironmaster's mansion is now a museum.`,`Mount Nittany, a forested ridge east of town, is said in local legend to be the resting place of a Native maiden named Nita-nee; the story was likely invented in the nineteenth century. The mountain is protected by a conservancy, and its trail to the summit is a rite of passage for Penn State students.`,`The Ridge and Valley region of central Pennsylvania is made up of long, parallel ridges of hard sandstone separated by valleys of softer limestone, formed when ancient rock layers were folded during the building of the Appalachians. The limestone valleys hold rich farmland and cold, spring-fed streams such as Spring Creek, one of the most famous trout streams in the country.`]},"columbia-sc":{why:`Columbia sits at the fall line, inland enough to lose Charleston’s sea breeze and low enough to keep a long, oppressive summer. The Congaree, Broad, and Saluda join here; floodplain forests stay a little milder at night while the sandhills and pavement cook. Tropical remnants still arrive as inland rain and wind. Winters are short with occasional freezes that the coast often misses. It is the South Carolina heat pool, a Cfa capital without a beach to moderate the afternoons.`,immersive:`July in Columbia is a weight. Afternoons near 33 °C, dew points that make shade only a partial relief, and thunderstorms that crack over the Congaree then leave the air unreset. Live oaks and pines hold older streets; the sandhill edges feel hotter and brighter. Winter light is the gift: camellias, a rare ice storm, nights near 1 °C. You smell river mud, hot pine, and, after a summer rain, steam off asphalt. Congaree National Park’s old-growth bottoms, about 30 km southeast, are a different climate—more mosquito, more shade, the same humidity. The city is inland on purpose. The weather agrees.`,feel:`Columbia feels like a Southern capital with no escape from the heat. It sits at the fall line, where the rolling Piedmont drops to the coastal plain, far enough from the ocean to miss its cooling breezes, and its summers are long, steamy and famously hot — the city's slogan is Famously Hot. Springs are lush and colourful, autumns warm and long, and winters short and mild, with only the occasional dusting of snow.`,seasons:{winter:`Short and mild — afternoons near 15°C, nights around 2°C, and a mix of grey, rainy days and bright, cool ones. Frost is common on clear nights, and snow or ice comes perhaps once a winter, causing chaos. Camellias bloom in the gardens.`,spring:`Warm and blooming — afternoons rising from about 20°C in March to 30°C by late May, azaleas and dogwoods in full colour by early April, and pollen coating cars in yellow dust. Strong thunderstorms and occasional tornadoes roll through.`,summer:`Long, hot and humid — afternoons near 33°C and often above 37°C, nights around 21°C, and humidity that makes the heat index soar. Afternoon thunderstorms bring brief relief, and people head for the lakes and rivers or stay in the air conditioning.`,autumn:`Warm and slow to cool — September is still hot, but by November afternoons ease to about 20°C, the humidity drops, and the leaves turn colour late in the season. Football Saturdays at the university fill the city.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Congaree National Park, with its giant old-growth trees and synchronous fireflies, University of South Carolina football, the State House, and kayaking and tubing on the city's three rivers.`,residentFit:`It suits government and university workers, military families from Fort Jackson and young families who want an affordable Southern city, and who can handle long, sweltering summers.`,contrasts:[{label:`Charleston (≈170 km SE, on the coast)`,note:`Down on the coast, sea breezes keep summer afternoons a couple of degrees cooler and winter nights milder.`},{label:`Greenville (≈150 km NW, ≈220 m higher)`,note:`Up in the Piedmont near the mountains, Greenville is a little cooler in summer and gets more winter snow.`}],history:[`On the night of 17 February 1865, the day General Sherman's army entered Columbia, fire swept through the city. By morning about a third of it lay in ashes, including much of the business district. Who started the fire has been argued ever since. Confederate defenders had piled cotton bales in the streets and some had been set alight before they left; Union soldiers, many of them drunk on liquor found in the city, set other fires; and a strong wind spread the flames. Sherman blamed the retreating Confederates, and Southerners blamed Sherman. Columbia had been a symbol of secession: in December 1860 the convention that voted to take South Carolina out of the Union had first met here, before an outbreak of smallpox moved it to Charleston.`,`The Congaree people lived along the river that bears their name before disease and colonial wars scattered them in the early 1700s. In 1786 the state legislature decided to move the capital inland from Charleston to a central location, closer to the growing upcountry, and laid out a new city on a hill above the Congaree River, one of the first planned capitals in the United States. George Washington visited in 1791. The University of South Carolina, founded in 1801, grew up beside the state house, and the city became the centre of the state's government and education.`,`After the war, Columbia rebuilt and became a textile mill town as well as a capital. Woodrow Wilson spent part of his boyhood here, and the city later became a centre of the civil rights movement in South Carolina. Fort Jackson, established in 1917, became the largest training base for new soldiers in the US Army. In October 2015 record rainfall caused catastrophic flooding across the city, breaking dams and washing out roads. Today Columbia is a government, university and military city, gateway to the old-growth forests of Congaree National Park.`],deepTitle:`Famously hot`,deep:[`Congaree National Park, southeast of the city, protects the largest remaining old-growth bottomland hardwood forest in the southeastern United States. Its giant loblolly pines, bald cypresses and oaks are among the tallest trees in eastern North America, and the forest floods regularly from the Congaree River. In late spring, synchronous fireflies flash in unison in the swampy woods.`,`The State House, begun before the Civil War, was still under construction when Sherman's troops shelled it from across the river in 1865; bronze stars on its western wall mark where the cannonballs struck. It was finally completed in 1907. The Confederate flag, which flew on the State House dome and later on its grounds for decades, was removed in 2015 after the Charleston church shooting.`,`Fort Jackson, on the edge of the city, trains about half of all soldiers entering the US Army and more than half of all women. It was founded during the First World War and named after Andrew Jackson, who was born in the Carolinas. The base is one of the largest employers in the region.`,`Columbia's heat is partly a matter of geography: it is far enough inland to miss the sea breeze, and low enough to escape the cooling of the mountains. The city adopted Famously Hot as a tourism slogan in the 2000s, embracing a reputation it could not escape.`]},"des-moines-ia":{why:`Des Moines sits on rolling till at the Raccoon–Des Moines confluence, far from Great Lakes moderation. Summer dew points and CAPE build ahead of fronts; mesoscale convective systems ride moisture tongues across the Corn Belt. Winter brings Arctic outbreaks and ice storms. The rivers flood when snowmelt and heavy rain coincide, as in 1993. Continentality is the mechanism. A Dfa city here has sharper seasons than the same letters on a Great Lakes shore.`,immersive:`August is corn-thick air, cicadas, and a sky that can go from white-hot to green-black in an hour. Afternoons near 30 °C, nights that stay muggy, and a siren culture that takes warnings seriously. January is a different planet: nights near −11 °C, wind across the till plain, and a low sun. Spring is mud and the first severe-weather watches. You smell turned earth, river water, and, in flood years, the sour basement smell of a city that knows the Raccoon. The State Fair is a climate festival as much as a livestock show. Four seasons are not a metaphor. They arrive on schedule and then overdo it.`,feel:`Des Moines feels like the steady heart of the Corn Belt. The city sits where two rivers meet on gently rolling prairie, now almost all farmland, and its climate swings hard between the seasons: winters are cold, windy and grey, summers hot, humid and full of towering thunderstorms that sweep across the plains at night. Spring and autumn are short but lovely, and in August the whole state seems to gather at the fair.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, windy and grey — afternoons around freezing, nights near −9°C, and arctic blasts that can push the thermometer below −25°C, with windchills colder still. Snow comes in moderate storms and blows across the open fields. The rivers freeze along their edges.`,spring:`Lively and stormy — afternoons climbing from about 10°C in March to 23°C by late May, rapid greening of the fields as farmers plant corn and soybeans, and powerful thunderstorms with hail and tornado warnings, especially in May and June. Late frosts are possible into April.`,summer:`Hot, humid and thundery — afternoons near 29°C and often above 32°C, nights around 17°C, and humidity rising off the endless cornfields. Thunderstorm complexes roll across the plains at night with spectacular lightning. The Iowa State Fair fills the city in August.`,autumn:`Crisp and golden — September afternoons near 25°C cooling to about 10°C by November, harvest time on the farms, and trees turning colour along the rivers by mid-October. The first snow usually falls in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Iowa State Fair and its butter cow, the sculpture park and art museum, the Saturday farmers' market, and hundreds of kilometres of bike trails, including the famous High Trestle Trail.`,residentFit:`It suits young families and insurance, finance and agriculture professionals who want an affordable, friendly city with a short commute, and who accept cold winters, humid summers and the odd tornado warning.`,contrasts:[{label:`Minneapolis (≈380 km N)`,note:`Further north, Minneapolis has colder, longer winters and slightly cooler summers.`},{label:`Kansas City (≈280 km SW)`,note:`To the south, Kansas City's winters are a little milder, and its summers a little hotter and more humid.`}],history:[`In the 1930s a young sports announcer at radio station WHO in Des Moines named Ronald Reagan described Chicago Cubs baseball games that he could not see. He sat in a studio, reading brief telegraph reports of each pitch and play, and turned them into vivid live commentary. One day, the story goes, the telegraph line went dead in the middle of a game, with a batter at the plate. Rather than admit the problem, Reagan had the batter hit foul ball after foul ball for several minutes, describing a boy in the stands catching one, until the line came back. In 1937, while covering the Cubs' spring training in California, he took a screen test with Warner Bros., and his career as an actor — and later as president — began.`,`The Sauk and Meskwaki had been forced west into central Iowa when the army built Fort Des Moines at the meeting of the Des Moines and Raccoon rivers in 1843 to keep watch over them. When the tribes were pushed out of Iowa altogether in 1845 and 1846, the fort closed and settlers took over the site. Some Meskwaki refused to stay away and, in 1857, bought land along the Iowa River at Tama, becoming the first Native nation to purchase its own land back in this way. That same year Des Moines became the state capital. The railroad arrived in 1866, and the city grew as a centre of coal mining, publishing and insurance.`,`In 1886 Iowa completed its State Capitol, with a dome covered in gold leaf that glows over the city. During the First World War, Fort Des Moines ran the Army's first officer training camp for Black men, and in 1942 it became the first training centre for the Women's Army Auxiliary Corps. Des Moines grew into one of the insurance capitals of America, and every four years it becomes the focus of national politics during the Iowa caucuses. In 1993 floods on the rivers knocked out the city's water works, leaving some 250,000 people without running water for nearly two weeks.`],deepTitle:`Butter cow country`,deep:[`The Iowa State Fair, held in Des Moines since 1879 and on its present grounds since 1886, draws more than a million visitors each August. Its most famous attraction is the butter cow, a life-size cow sculpted from about 270 kg of butter, first displayed in 1911. The fair also features giant pumpkins, prize livestock and foods on sticks, and it is a traditional stop for presidential candidates eating corn dogs before the caucuses.`,`In October 1979 Pope John Paul II visited Iowa after an Iowa farmer wrote him a letter inviting him to see the heartland. He celebrated Mass before some 350,000 people at Living History Farms, a farm museum on the edge of Des Moines, and spoke about the care of the land. The visit was the first by a pope to rural America.`,`The Fort Des Moines Provisional Army Officer Training School of 1917 trained more than six hundred Black men as officers, who went on to lead Black troops in France during the First World War, at a time when the army was strictly segregated. During the Second World War, the fort trained tens of thousands of women in the Women's Army Auxiliary Corps. A museum on the site tells their stories.`,`The 1993 flood was part of the Great Flood of 1993 that swept the Midwest. The Raccoon River overflowed into the city's water treatment plant, cutting off drinking water for nearly two weeks. Residents lined up for bottled water, and National Guard troops brought in supplies. The flood led to stronger levees and a new water system.`]},"tucson-az":{why:`Tucson occupies a Sonoran basin at about 730 m, ringed by sky-island ranges that can hold snow while the floor stays frost-light. Summer is a two-act play: foresummer heat, then North American Monsoon storms when Gulf of California moisture hits orographic and urban triggers. Winters are cool and brilliant. Elevation-lapse and aspect turn a twenty-minute drive into a different hardiness zone. It is not Phoenix’s lower oven, and it is not a mountain town. It is the basin in between.`,immersive:`June is a dry glare; pavement stores 39 °C afternoons and gives them back after dark. Then July humidity arrives like a rumor, and the Catalinas grow anvil clouds that collapse into dust-first microbursts. The smell of creosote after rain is the city’s signature. Winter mornings can be 4 °C in the basin, colder in washes, with snow visible on Mount Lemmon. Saguaros and palo verde set the silhouette. You hear doves at dawn and monsoon thunder that uses the whole basin as a drum. Hard freezes are uncommon on the floor and ordinary a few hundred meters up.`,feel:`Tucson feels like a desert city that has made peace with its desert. Forests of giant saguaro cactus cover the hills around it, and five mountain ranges ring the valley, one of them high enough to wear snow in winter. The climate is hot and dry for most of the year, with brilliant sunshine and enormous skies, but every July the monsoon arrives, bringing dramatic thunderstorms, the smell of rain on creosote, and a second season of green.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, sunny and lovely — afternoons near 20°C, nights cooling to about 4°C, and occasional Pacific storms that bring gentle rain to the valley and snow to the peaks of the Santa Catalinas above the city. Frosts are possible on clear nights. Visitors flock to Tucson for the gem and mineral show in February.`,spring:`The desert in bloom — afternoons rising from about 25°C in March to 34°C by late May, almost no rain, and the Sonoran Desert flowering: poppies and lupines after a wet winter, then the yellow palo verdes and, by May, the white crowns of the saguaros. By late May the heat is building toward summer.`,summer:`Hot, then stormy — June brings searing, dry heat with afternoons often above 40°C, until the monsoon arrives in early July. Then humid air from the south builds towering thunderstorms over the mountains almost every afternoon, with lightning, wind, dust storms and torrential downpours that flood the washes. Nights stay warm, around 24°C, and the desert turns green.`,autumn:`Warm and golden — September is still hot, with the last monsoon storms, but by November afternoons ease to about 25°C, the skies are clear and calm, and the desert is at its most comfortable. Occasional remnants of Pacific hurricanes bring heavy rain.`},contrasts:[{label:`Mount Lemmon (≈30 km NE, ≈2,000 m higher)`,note:`At the top of the Santa Catalinas, temperatures run about 15°C cooler, pine forests grow, and the southernmost ski area in the United States opens in snowy winters.`},{label:`Phoenix (≈170 km NW, ≈390 m lower)`,note:`Down in the lower Valley of the Sun, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter, and nights stay warmer.`}],history:[`On 22 January 1934, a fire broke out at the Hotel Congress in downtown Tucson. Firefighters helped two guests rescue their heavy luggage from a room upstairs, and were well tipped for their trouble. A few days later, one of the firemen recognised the guests in a detective magazine: they were members of John Dillinger's gang, the most wanted criminals in America, who had come to Tucson to lie low. Within days, Tucson police had arrested the whole gang, including Dillinger himself, without firing a shot. Dillinger escaped from jail in Indiana a few weeks later, but the hotel still celebrates the arrest every January with Dillinger Days.`,`The Tohono O'odham and their ancestors had farmed the Santa Cruz River valley for thousands of years, growing corn, beans and squash at the foot of a black volcanic hill they called Cuk Ṣon, from which the name Tucson comes. The Jesuit missionary Eusebio Kino arrived in 1692 and founded the mission of San Xavier del Bac, whose gleaming white church, completed in 1797, is known as the White Dove of the Desert. In 1775 an Irish officer serving Spain, Hugo O'Conor, founded a walled fort, the Presidio of San Agustín del Tucson. The town passed to Mexico in 1821 and to the United States with the Gadsden Purchase in 1854.`,`Tucson was the capital of Arizona Territory from 1867 to 1877. The railroad arrived in 1880; the University of Arizona was founded in 1885 and held its first classes in 1891. After the Second World War, air-conditioning and the clear desert air drew people and industries, and Davis-Monthan Air Force Base and the astronomical observatories on the surrounding mountains became major employers. Today Tucson is a large university city that keeps a strong Mexican-American heritage, and in 2015 it became the first city in the United States named a UNESCO City of Gastronomy.`],deepTitle:`Saguaro and stars`,deep:[`Davis-Monthan Air Force Base is home to the boneyard, where more than three thousand retired military aircraft are stored in long rows in the desert. The dry air and alkaline soil prevent rust, so the planes can be preserved for spare parts or returned to service. The neighbouring Pima Air and Space Museum displays hundreds of aircraft.`,`Tucson is one of the world's great centres of astronomy. Kitt Peak National Observatory, west of the city, has more than twenty telescopes, and the University of Arizona's mirror laboratory, built under the stands of its football stadium, casts some of the largest telescope mirrors in the world in a spinning furnace. The city has strict lighting rules to keep its night skies dark.`,`The saguaro cactus, which grows only in the Sonoran Desert, can live for more than 150 years and grow over 12 m tall, sprouting its first arms only after fifty years or more. Saguaro National Park protects two forests of them on either side of Tucson. For the Tohono O'odham, the saguaro's fruit harvest in June marks the start of the new year, and its syrup is used to make a wine for the ceremony to bring the rain.`,`The Tucson Gem and Mineral Show, held every February since 1955, has grown into the largest event of its kind in the world, with dealers from dozens of countries setting up in hotels, tents and parking lots across the city to sell minerals, fossils, meteorites and jewellery.`]},"anchorage-ak":{why:`Anchorage is a maritime-modified subarctic city at the head of Cook Inlet, with Chugach peaks immediately east. Inlet water and occasional sea ice moderate winter compared with Fairbanks; mountains wring snow on the hillside neighborhoods. Summer days are long and mild, not hot. Smoke from interior fires can pool when the inlet inversion sets. Few places combine a 300,000-person metro with Dfc winters and a tide flat at the door. That combination is the climate identity.`,immersive:`November light is a low, blue hour that lasts all afternoon. Inlet mudflats at minus tide look like another country; the Chugach wall is close enough to hold alpenglow on a workday commute. Winter nights near −12 °C in town, colder in hollows, with wind off Turnagain Arm that can shut the highway. Summer is 19 °C and twenty hours of usable light, fireweed, and the smell of mud and spruce. You hear ravens and, some years, the tick of freezing rain. Earthquake drills are not theoretical. The 1964 scars are still a map of which bluffs liquefy.`,feel:`Anchorage feels like a city on the edge of wilderness. Its tidy grid of streets sits between the grey waters of Cook Inlet and the jagged Chugach Mountains, and moose wander through the neighbourhoods. Winters are long and dark but milder than the Alaskan interior, thanks to the nearby ocean, while summers are cool, green and nearly endless in their daylight. On clear days the great white mass of Denali floats on the northern horizon, more than 200 km away.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, dark and snowy — afternoons below freezing, nights near −11°C, and only about five and a half hours of daylight at the solstice. Snow covers the ground from October to April, and the cross-country ski trails through the city are among the best anywhere. Warm chinook winds from the mountains occasionally melt the snow, followed by icy streets.`,spring:`Late and bright — afternoons climbing from about 2°C in March to 15°C by late May, rapidly lengthening days, and the snow melting into muddy break-up. Spring is the driest season, and the birch and alder leaf out in a rush in late May.`,summer:`Cool, green and endless — afternoons near 19°C, nights around 10°C, and almost twenty hours of daylight in June. Salmon run in Ship Creek in the middle of town, gardens grow giant vegetables, and residents hike, fish and camp late into the bright evenings. Rain increases in August.`,autumn:`Brief and colourful — birch and aspen turn gold in early September, the first snow dusts the Chugach peaks, and afternoons fall from about 13°C to below freezing by late October. Autumn is the wettest season.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Chugach Mountains on the edge of town, moose on the bike trails, flightseeing over Denali and the glaciers, the start of the Iditarod and the long, light-filled summer days.`,residentFit:`It suits outdoor lovers, military families and oil, aviation and logistics workers who want city services with wilderness at the door, and who can live with dark winters and the risk of earthquakes.`,contrasts:[{label:`Fairbanks (≈420 km N, in the Interior)`,note:`Far from the sea, Fairbanks has much colder winters and warmer summers, with even greater swings in daylight.`},{label:`Whittier & Prince William Sound (≈60 km SE)`,note:`On the wet side of the Chugach Mountains, Whittier gets several times Anchorage's rain and snow.`}],history:[`In April 1915, the US government chose the mouth of Ship Creek, a fishing camp at the head of Cook Inlet, as the construction headquarters for the new Alaska Railroad, and within weeks a tent city of some two thousand people had sprung up on the mudflats. On 10 July 1915 the government auctioned off lots in a new townsite on the bluff above, and the city of Anchorage was born almost overnight, with streets laid out on a neat grid. The railroad took eight years to build, and when it was finished in 1923, Anchorage was a small but busy railroad town.`,`The Dena'ina Athabascans lived around Cook Inlet for centuries, fishing salmon at Ship Creek and hunting in the mountains. In 1778 the British explorer James Cook sailed up the inlet looking for the Northwest Passage. When he discovered that one arm of it led only to mountains and mud, he had to turn back again, and named it Turnagain Arm. During the Second World War the army built Fort Richardson and Elmendorf Field, and Anchorage boomed as a military town. On 27 March 1964 the Good Friday earthquake, the strongest ever recorded in North America, shook the city for about four and a half minutes; neighbourhoods slid into the inlet, and much of downtown was wrecked.`,`Anchorage rebuilt quickly, and the discovery of oil at Prudhoe Bay in 1968 and the construction of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline made it the business and transport centre of the state. Its airport became one of the busiest cargo hubs in the world, a refuelling stop for planes flying between Asia and North America. Today about two in five Alaskans live in the Anchorage area, and the city is the starting point for the Iditarod sled-dog race to Nome each March.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The 1964 earthquake caused some of its worst damage in Anchorage's Turnagain neighbourhood, where the clay beneath the bluff gave way and houses slid toward the sea in a jumble of earth and broken streets. Downtown, a section of Fourth Avenue dropped several metres. The area of the Turnagain slide is now Earthquake Park, where the hummocky ground still shows the scars.`,`Anchorage shares its streets with about 1,500 moose, which browse in yards, wander across roads and occasionally block the doors of schools. Black and brown bears also roam the city's greenbelts and creeks, and residents learn to be careful on trails. Beluga whales feed in the waters of Turnagain Arm, and salmon run up creeks in the city.`,`The Fur Rendezvous winter festival, held each February since 1935, began as a celebration of the return of trappers bringing furs to sell. It now features sled-dog races, a running of the reindeer through downtown streets and other events. The Iditarod's ceremonial start in early March brings mushers and dogs through the city's snowy streets.`,`Turnagain Arm has one of the largest tidal ranges in the United States, rising and falling more than 9 m, and at times a bore tide — a wall of water up to 2 m high — rushes up the inlet with the incoming tide, and surfers ride it for kilometres. The mudflats left by the falling tide are dangerous quicksand, and signs warn people not to walk on them.`]},"honolulu-hi":{why:`Honolulu occupies Oʻahu’s south shore in the lee of the Koʻolau Range. Trades lift rain on the windward side and leave the city drier, while the Waiʻanae Range shadows the west even more. Urban heat raises nighttime minima downtown relative to valleys. The annual range is small; the spatial range across the island is large. Hurricanes are rarer than on Gulf coasts, tsunamis and surge are not theoretical. Trade-wind lee plus UHI is the mechanism, not a generic tropical beach.`,immersive:`Trade clouds stack on the Koʻolau palis while Waikīkī stays brighter. Afternoons near 30 °C, nights near 19 °C, a range so tight that season is more about rain pulses than temperature. You smell plumeria, reef at low tide, and, in town, exhaust in the inversion. Showers can cross from the valleys in ten minutes. Winter surf on the north shore is another climate; the south shore is the lee calendar. Humidity is constant. The body stops expecting autumn. What it learns instead is windward versus leeward, valley versus asphalt, and the sound of an emergency siren test.`,feel:`Honolulu feels like a big city that never forgets it is on a tropical island. Skyscrapers and hotels crowd the shore between Waikiki and the harbour, but the green, rain-streaked ridges of the Koʻolau Range rise directly behind them, and the steady trade winds carry the smell of the sea and plumeria through the streets. The weather barely changes all year: warm, breezy and bright, with brief showers drifting down from the mountains and rainbows over the valleys.`,seasons:{winter:`The slightly cooler, wetter season — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 19°C, and occasional winter storms that bring heavy rain and, on the north shore, giant surf. The trade winds sometimes falter, letting cooler, damp air settle in for a few days. Humpback whales migrate into Hawaiian waters from December to April.`,spring:`Warm and gentle — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 20°C, and steady trade winds with passing showers over the mountains. The flowers of the plumeria and shower trees bloom, and the waters off Waikiki are calm.`,summer:`Warm, breezy and dry — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 23°C, and reliable trade winds that keep the heat comfortable. Rain is scarce on the south shore, surf builds on the south-facing beaches, and the city's festivals and outdoor concerts fill the long evenings.`,autumn:`Warm and gradually wetter — afternoons near 30°C in September easing to about 28°C by November, nights around 22°C, and trade winds that occasionally falter, leaving the air muggy. Showers become more frequent by November, the first big winter swells reach the north shore, and the central Pacific hurricane season, though rarely felt, runs to the end of November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Waikiki Beach and surfing, the USS Arizona Memorial at Pearl Harbor, hiking Diamond Head, Hawaiian culture and food, and the giant winter waves of the North Shore.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mānoa Valley (≈5 km NE)`,note:`Tucked into the Koʻolau Range just behind the city, Mānoa gets several times as much rain as Waikiki, which keeps it lush, misty and rich in rainbows.`},{label:`Kapolei & the leeward coast (≈30 km W)`,note:`Further into the rain shadow, the leeward side of Oʻahu is hotter and drier, with brown hills and only a fraction of the rain.`}],history:[`On 17 January 1893, a group of American and European businessmen, backed by sailors and marines from the US warship Boston who had come ashore in Honolulu, overthrew Queen Liliʻuokalani, the last monarch of the Kingdom of Hawaiʻi. The queen, hoping to avoid bloodshed, yielded her authority, as she wrote, to the superior force of the United States, believing Washington would restore her to the throne once it learned the facts. It did not. In 1895, after a failed uprising by her supporters, she was imprisoned in her own palace, and in 1898 the United States annexed Hawaiʻi. A century later, in 1993, Congress passed a resolution formally apologising for the overthrow.`,`The harbour at Honolulu, whose name means sheltered bay, was little used until the 1790s, when Western ships began calling. In 1795 Kamehameha the Great conquered Oʻahu at the Battle of Nuʻuanu, driving the island's defenders over the cliffs of the Nuʻuanu Pali, and went on to unite all the Hawaiian Islands. Honolulu became a port for whalers and traders, and in 1845 King Kamehameha III moved the capital here from Lahaina. American missionaries and planters followed, and sugar plantations brought workers from China, Japan, Portugal and the Philippines, creating the mixed population that defines Hawaiʻi today. In 1882 King Kalākaua completed ʻIolani Palace, which had electric lights before the White House.`,`After annexation, Honolulu became the headquarters of American power in the Pacific. On 7 December 1941, Japanese aircraft attacked the US fleet at Pearl Harbor, just west of the city, killing more than 2,400 Americans and drawing the United States into the Second World War. Hawaiʻi became the fiftieth state in 1959, and jet travel turned Waikiki into one of the world's most famous beach resorts. Barack Obama was born in Honolulu in 1961. The city now faces some of the highest housing costs in the nation, and a renewed movement for Native Hawaiian culture and rights.`],deepTitle:`Palace and harbour`,deep:[`Queen Liliʻuokalani was a gifted musician and composer who wrote more than 150 songs, including Aloha ʻOe, perhaps the most famous Hawaiian song of all. During her imprisonment in ʻIolani Palace, she composed music and worked on a translation of the Kumulipo, the Hawaiian creation chant. She spent the rest of her life campaigning for the rights of her people and died in 1917.`,`Waikiki was once a marshland of taro patches and fishponds, a retreat for Hawaiian royalty. In the 1920s the Ala Wai Canal was dug to drain it, and hotels rose along the beach. Duke Kahanamoku, a Native Hawaiian swimmer who won Olympic gold medals in 1912 and 1920, popularised surfing around the world, and the beachboys of Waikiki taught generations of tourists to ride the waves.`,`The USS Arizona Memorial at Pearl Harbor spans the sunken hull of the battleship, where more than 1,100 sailors and marines died. Oil still seeps from the wreck, rising to the surface in small drops that visitors call the tears of the Arizona. The memorial is one of the most visited sites in Hawaiʻi.`,`Honolulu's climate is shaped by the trade winds, which blow from the northeast across the islands for most of the year. As the moist air rises over the Koʻolau Range, it drops heavy rain on the windward side and on the mountain crests, then descends, warmer and drier, over Honolulu, which lies in the mountains' rain shadow. That is why it can rain in Mānoa Valley while the sun shines on Waikiki a few kilometres away.`]},"roswell-nm":{why:`Roswell occupies a broad Pecos Valley on the Llano Estacado margin: high, dry, and convective. Summer monsoon moisture and drylines collide here, producing hail and huge diurnal swings. Spring wind and dust are notorious. Irrigation from the Pecos and, historically, artesian wells made a farm island in BSk steppe. Continentality and elevation near 1,100 m do the rest. It is southern High Plains weather with a river, not a mountain-town climate.`,immersive:`The sky is the main landform. Afternoons in June can near 36 °C; nights in January drop below −4 °C with a dryness that makes the cold feel sharp rather than wet. Spring is a wind tunnel: dust, tumbleweed, and a horizontal light. Monsoon cells build over the valley and dump hail on pecan orchards. You smell irrigation, dust, and, after rain, creosote from the edges. Bottomless Lakes are sinkhole oases in gypsum country. The town feels low and wide. Sound carries. Weather arrives as a front you can watch cross the whole bowl.`,feel:`Roswell feels like a quiet ranch and farm town that the rest of the world thinks of as an alien landing site. In the wide, flat Pecos River valley of southeastern New Mexico, where irrigated fields, pecan orchards and dairies meet the dry grasslands of the Llano Estacado, the town has embraced its fame since the mysterious crash of 1947, with flying-saucer street lamps and a UFO museum. The air is dry and the sun strong: summers are hot with monsoon storms, winters mild by day and cold at night, and spring brings strong, dusty winds.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild days and cold nights — afternoons near 15°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and sunshine on most days. Cold fronts sweep down the plains with gusty winds and an occasional light snow that rarely lasts. The dry air makes the stars brilliant on clear nights.`,spring:`Warm, dry and windy — afternoons rising from about 22°C in March to 31°C by late May, cool nights, and strong westerly winds that raise dust storms across the plains. Rain is scarce until late May, and the pecan trees leaf out along the river.`,summer:`Hot, dry and then stormy — afternoons near 35°C and often higher in June, nights cooling to about 18°C, and from July monsoon thunderstorms that build over the mountains to the west and roll across the plains with lightning, hail and short, heavy downpours. The UFO festival in early July fills the town with visitors in alien costumes.`,autumn:`Warm and calm — September afternoons near 31°C easing to about 19°C by November, with clear skies, light winds and cool nights. The pecan harvest begins in the orchards along the river, and the first frost usually arrives in late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the International UFO Museum, the alien-themed street lamps and the UFO Festival each July, the swimming holes of Bottomless Lakes State Park and the Bitter Lake wildlife refuge.`,contrasts:[{label:`Ruidoso (≈110 km W, ≈1,100 m higher)`,note:`Up in the pine-covered Sacramento Mountains, Ruidoso runs about 8°C cooler in summer and gets real winter snow.`},{label:`Carlsbad (≈120 km S, ≈150 m lower)`,note:`Further down the Pecos valley, Carlsbad's summers run a little hotter and its winters a touch milder.`}],history:[`On 8 July 1947, the public information officer at Roswell Army Air Field issued a startling press release: the army had recovered a flying disc that had crashed on a ranch north of town. The story flashed around the world. The next day the army changed its story, saying the debris was nothing more than a weather balloon, and the newspapers moved on. For three decades Roswell was forgotten, until in the late 1970s UFO researchers began interviewing witnesses who claimed to have seen alien bodies and strange materials. In 1994 the US Air Force explained that the debris had come from Project Mogul, a top-secret programme of high-altitude balloons designed to detect Soviet nuclear tests. Few believers were persuaded, and Roswell has become the UFO capital of the world, with a museum, an annual festival and alien-shaped streetlights.`,`The Pecos River valley was the hunting ground of the Mescalero Apache and Comanche. In 1866 the cattlemen Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove herds from Texas up the Pecos to sell to the army, and the rancher John Chisum built a cattle empire along the river. In 1869 two traders built a small adobe store and post office here, and one of them, Van C. Smith, named it after his father, Roswell Smith. The surrounding country saw the violence of the Lincoln County War in the late 1870s, and Pat Garrett, the sheriff who killed Billy the Kid, later settled near Roswell.`,`In the 1890s settlers discovered artesian water beneath the valley, which gushed out of wells without pumping, and Roswell grew into a farming town of orchards, alfalfa and cotton. The New Mexico Military Institute opened in 1891. From 1930 to 1941, the rocket pioneer Robert Goddard conducted his experiments on the plains near Roswell, launching liquid-fuelled rockets that laid the groundwork for the space age. During the Second World War, Roswell Army Air Field trained bomber crews, and in 1945 it became the home of the bomber group that dropped the atomic bombs on Japan.`],deepTitle:`Rockets and aliens`,deep:[`Robert Goddard launched the world's first liquid-fuelled rocket in Massachusetts in 1926, but complaints from neighbours and the need for open space brought him to Roswell, with funding from the Guggenheim family, arranged with help from Charles Lindbergh. In the clear, empty skies of New Mexico, his rockets climbed more than 2 km into the sky at speeds of hundreds of kilometres an hour. The Roswell Museum displays his workshop and rockets.`,`Project Mogul used trains of balloons carrying sensitive microphones to listen for the sound waves of Soviet nuclear explosions. The materials the rancher found — foil, rubber, sticks and tape — matched the balloons, according to the air force. A second air force report in 1997 suggested that stories of alien bodies might have come from memories of crash-test dummies dropped from balloons in the 1950s.`,`Bottomless Lakes State Park, east of Roswell, is a chain of deep, steep-sided lakes formed when underground salt and gypsum dissolved and the ground above collapsed. The lakes, with their blue-green water, were New Mexico's first state park, created in 1933. They are not bottomless, but early cowboys who tried to sound them with ropes believed they were.`,`The New Mexico Military Institute has produced a remarkable list of graduates, including the hotelier Conrad Hilton, the football quarterback Roger Staubach and the broadcaster Sam Donaldson. Roswell is also the birthplace of the singer John Denver and the actress Demi Moore.`]},"rapid-city-sd":{why:`Rapid City sits where the Great Plains meet the Black Hills front. Pacific systems, continental Arctic air, and downslope chinooks can follow one another in hours. Orographic lift on the Hills wrings more moisture than the open prairie gets; the prairie edge still takes dryline storms. Elevation near 1,000 m sharpens the diurnal swing. The gateway-city climate is a frontal position, not a mountain-valley climate like the interior Hills, and not a flat-Plains catalog either.`,immersive:`A January afternoon can feel like April when a chinook arrives, then revert to ice by night. Summer builds thunderheads over the Hills that drift onto the prairie edge. Rapid Creek is a green thread through town—and, in memory, a floodway. You smell ponderosa on the west wind and dust on the east. Nights near −9 °C in winter, afternoons near 30 °C in July. The sky is Plains-wide; the horizon to the west is a dark pine wall. Weather here changes its mind. People who stay learn to check the afternoon again, not the morning forecast alone.`,feel:`Rapid City feels like a gateway town at the edge of two worlds. To the west rise the dark, pine-covered Black Hills, home of Mount Rushmore and sacred to the Lakota; to the east the prairie rolls away toward the Missouri. The weather swings fast between them: arctic cold can be chased away by a warm chinook wind in a matter of hours, summer storms build over the Hills with hail and flash floods, and the dry air makes the sunshine intense.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and changeable — afternoons near 4°C, nights near −9°C, and arctic outbreaks that can drop the thermometer below −25°C, followed by chinook winds that can raise it by 20°C in a few hours and melt the snow. Snow is usually light in town but heavier in the Hills, and the wind can whip up ground blizzards on the plains.`,spring:`Changeable and increasingly wet — afternoons rising from about 9°C in March to 19°C by late May, with spring blizzards possible into April, followed by the wettest stretch of the year as May thunderstorms bring rain and sometimes hail. The Black Hills turn green, and the tourist season begins at Mount Rushmore by late May.`,summer:`Warm and stormy — afternoons near 30°C, nights cooling to about 15°C in the dry air, and afternoon thunderstorms that build over the Black Hills and drift east, sometimes with large hail and flash floods. Tourists stream through on their way to Mount Rushmore, and in early August, motorcycles roar through for the rally at nearby Sturgis.`,autumn:`Clear and crisp — September afternoons near 25°C cooling to about 9°C by November, aspens and birches turning gold in the Hills, and calm, sunny days. The first snow often falls in October, and the buffalo roundup in Custer State Park draws crowds in late September.`},contrasts:[{label:`Spearfish (≈75 km NW)`,note:`At the northern edge of the Black Hills, Spearfish also gets dramatic chinook warm-ups, with more snow and slightly cooler summers.`},{label:`Pierre (≈270 km E, on the Missouri)`,note:`Out on the plains away from the Hills, the state capital has colder winters with fewer chinook breaks and hotter summers.`}],history:[`On the evening of 9 June 1972, a stalled thunderstorm dumped more than 350 mm of rain on the eastern Black Hills in about six hours. Rapid Creek, which runs through the middle of Rapid City, swelled into a torrent, and the Canyon Lake Dam on the west side of town collapsed, sending a wall of water through the neighbourhoods along the creek in the dark. By morning, 238 people were dead and more than 1,300 homes destroyed. Rather than rebuild in the floodplain, the city turned the land along the creek into a long greenway of parks, bike paths and golf courses, which now runs through the heart of the city.`,`The Black Hills, Ȟe Sápa, are sacred to the Lakota, and the 1868 Treaty of Fort Laramie recognised them as part of the Great Sioux Reservation. But after George Armstrong Custer's expedition reported gold in 1874, miners poured in, and in 1876 a group of disappointed prospectors laid out a supply town on Rapid Creek at the edge of the plains, which they named Rapid City. The government seized the Black Hills from the Lakota in 1877. The town grew as a supply centre for the mines and ranches, and the railroad arrived in 1886.`,`In 1927 the sculptor Gutzon Borglum began carving the faces of four presidents into Mount Rushmore, about 30 km southwest of the city, and the monument, completed in 1941, made Rapid City the gateway to one of America's most famous landmarks. In 1948 another sculptor, Korczak Ziolkowski, began carving a giant monument to the Lakota leader Crazy Horse nearby, at the invitation of Lakota elders; it is still unfinished. Ellsworth Air Force Base, east of the city, has long been a major employer. Since 2000 the city has placed life-size bronze statues of the American presidents on its downtown street corners.`],deepTitle:`Faces in the granite`,deep:[`Mount Rushmore was carved mostly with dynamite, which removed about 400,000 tonnes of rock, followed by drills and hand tools for the fine details. Some four hundred workers took part, and remarkably, no one was killed during the fourteen years of work. Borglum died in 1941, and his son Lincoln finished the project. The monument remains controversial for the Lakota, who regard the carving of presidents into their sacred mountains as a symbol of the theft of their land.`,`The Crazy Horse Memorial, when finished, will be the largest sculpture in the world, showing the Lakota warrior on horseback, pointing to the land where his people are buried. Ziolkowski refused government funding, and the work has been carried on by his family with private donations since his death in 1982. Crazy Horse's face, nearly 27 m tall, was completed in 1998.`,`Dinosaur Park, built on a hill above Rapid City in 1936 by the Works Progress Administration, features five life-size concrete dinosaurs painted bright green, which can be seen from much of the city. The Black Hills region is rich in real fossils, and the nearby Badlands have yielded the bones of ancient mammals.`,`The Rapid City Indian Boarding School, which operated from 1898 to 1933, took Native children from their families to assimilate them into white American culture. In recent years, researchers and Lakota descendants have worked to identify the graves of children who died there, and the city has been confronting this painful chapter of its history.`]},"jackson-wy":{why:`Jackson Hole is a high, flat-floored valley between the Teton and Gros Ventre ranges. Cold air drains to the floor and sits; inversions make town colder than some ski bases. Orographic lift on the Tetons loads snow that feeds the Snake. Elevation near 1,900 m shortens the growing season to greens and hay. The Dfc label is the broad stroke; the cold pool plus a wall of rock is the local climate. Summer is the brief reward. Winter is the identity.`,immersive:`January mornings in town can be −20 °C under a lid of ice fog while the Teton peaks shine in sun. Snow squeaks. Elk move through the refuge on the north edge of town. Summer is short and luminous: 25 °C afternoons, big-sky thunderstorms, and a green sage floor that smells like after-rain. You hear the elk bugle in autumn and the rumble of avalanche control in winter. Altitude thins sleep until you adapt. Housing sticker shock is part of the weather now. The valley is a postcard that inverts, freezes, and then briefly becomes perfect.`,feel:`Jackson feels like a cowboy town with a billionaire's address book. Its wooden boardwalks, antler arches and saloons sit at the southern end of Jackson Hole, a high, flat valley ringed by mountains beneath the jagged wall of the Grand Tetons, close to Yellowstone and some of the wildest country in the lower forty-eight. At about 1,900 m, the climate is mountain-cold: winters are long, snowy and bitterly cold on the valley floor, summers short, bright and glorious, with frosty nights possible even in July.`,seasons:{winter:`Deep, cold and brilliant — afternoons below freezing, nights near −13°C, and on still, clear nights cold air sinks to the valley floor and pushes the thermometer below −30°C. Snow piles up steadily in town and far deeper on the mountains, where the ski resort averages around 11 m a season. Thousands of elk winter on the refuge at the edge of town, and the antler arches on the town square wear caps of snow.`,spring:`Late, muddy and fickle — afternoons rising from about 5°C in March to 16°C by late May, with snow still falling into April and nights below freezing well into May. The ski resorts close in early April, the elk leave the National Elk Refuge, and the valley floor greens slowly as bears emerge in Grand Teton.`,summer:`Short, bright and glorious — afternoons near 27°C, but nights that drop to around 6°C, with frost possible even in July on the valley floor. Afternoon thunderstorms build over the Tetons, wildflowers fill the meadows, and the Snake River and the national parks draw crowds. Wildfire smoke can haze the views in late summer.`,autumn:`Brief and golden — September afternoons near 21°C with frosty nights, aspens and cottonwoods turning gold, and elk bugling in the meadows. By late October the first lasting snows arrive, and by November afternoons barely reach 5°C as the valley braces for winter.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Grand Teton and Yellowstone national parks, wildlife in the valley — elk, bison, moose and bears — the steep skiing of Jackson Hole Mountain Resort and the cowboy bars around the town square.`,residentFit:`It suits skiers, climbers, guides and the very wealthy who love mountain wilderness and are willing to pay some of the highest housing costs in the country for it, and to endure long, bitterly cold winters.`,contrasts:[{label:`Rendezvous Mountain summit (≈20 km NW, ≈1,300 m higher)`,note:`At the top of the ski resort's aerial tram, winter brings around 11 m of snow and the air runs about 8°C colder than in town.`},{label:`Teton Valley, Idaho (≈40 km W, over Teton Pass)`,note:`On the far side of the Tetons, Driggs and Victor share the same cold winters and high-altitude summers, at far lower housing costs.`}],history:[`In May 1920 the voters of Jackson, Wyoming, did something almost no American town had done before: they elected an all-woman town government. Grace Miller became mayor, and four women won seats on the town council; the town marshal was a woman too. The press called it the petticoat government. Wyoming Territory had been the first in the nation to give women the vote, in 1869, and the women of Jackson took their election seriously, collecting unpaid taxes, building a cemetery road, installing culverts and cracking down on loose livestock. They served until 1923, and their success helped convince the nation that women could govern.`,`For thousands of years, the Shoshone, Bannock, Crow and other nations came to the high valley beneath the Tetons in summer to hunt elk and bighorn sheep and gather plants, retreating to lower country in winter. Fur trappers arrived in the early 1800s, calling such mountain basins holes, and the valley took the name of one of them, David Jackson. Homesteaders settled in the 1880s and 1890s, raising cattle and hay. As settlement blocked the elk's traditional winter migration routes, thousands starved in hard winters, and in 1912 Congress created the National Elk Refuge on the edge of town to feed and protect them.`,`In the 1920s the oil heir John D. Rockefeller Jr. visited the valley and, alarmed by the gas stations and shacks springing up beneath the Tetons, began secretly buying up ranch land through a front company, intending to donate it to the government. When the plan became public, many locals were furious. In 1943 President Franklin Roosevelt created Jackson Hole National Monument, and ranchers protested by driving cattle across it, led by the film star Wallace Beery. In 1950 the land was added to Grand Teton National Park. The ski resort opened in 1965, and Jackson is now one of the wealthiest resort towns in America.`],deepTitle:`Antler arches`,deep:[`The four arches on Jackson's town square, built from thousands of elk antlers, have become the town's most famous landmark. The antlers are shed naturally each spring by the elk on the refuge, and Boy Scouts gather them and sell them at an annual auction, raising money to help feed the herd. The first arch was built in 1953.`,`The National Elk Refuge supports one of the largest elk herds in North America through the winter, with thousands of animals feeding on the grasslands and supplemental hay. Visitors can take horse-drawn sleigh rides among the elk. Biologists worry that crowding so many animals together spreads disease, and the feeding programme is debated.`,`Rockefeller's Snake River Land Company bought some 14,000 hectares of land in the valley in the late 1920s and 1930s, often from ranchers struggling in the Depression. When he offered to donate the land to the government, Wyoming's congressmen blocked the gift for years. Rockefeller threatened to sell it, and Roosevelt's monument proclamation followed. The national park that resulted protects the valley floor that makes the Teton view so famous.`,`The Grand Teton, rising to 4,199 m, towers some 2,100 m above the valley floor without foothills, which is why the range looks so dramatic. The mountains are among the youngest in the Rockies, lifted along a fault that is still active. The famous barns of Mormon Row, built by Mormon homesteaders around 1900, stand in front of the range and are among the most photographed buildings in the West.`]},"washington-dc":{why:`Washington sits at the Atlantic fall line on the Potomac, where Piedmont rock meets coastal-plain tidewater. Chesapeake moisture and continental air masses take turns; summers are sultry, winters irregularly cold. The urban heat island lifts night minima above the surrounding suburbs. Remnant tropical systems and nor’easters both find the rivers. It is a humid-subtropical capital with a mid-Atlantic storm track, not a New England city and not the Deep South, though summer can feel like a vote for the latter.`,immersive:`July in the District is a damp wool suit: 31 °C afternoons, nights that barely drop, and a sweet-rot smell of the Tidal Basin when the air stalls. Magnolias and brick hold heat after dark. January can be a gray thaw or a brief Arctic event that ices the Mall. You hear helicopters, sirens, and, after a summer storm, the roar of Rock Creek. Cherry bloom is a climate festival with a frost risk. Flooding on the Potomac and in basement apartments is the unglamorous hydrology under the monuments. The city feels important and swampy in the same hour.`,feel:`Washington feels like a grand stage built on a humid river plain. Its broad avenues, white marble monuments and green open spaces sit where the Potomac drops off the Piedmont onto the coastal plain, and its climate is properly Southern in summer — hot, sticky and thundery — while winters bring raw cold and the occasional heavy snow. Spring, when the cherry blossoms bloom around the Tidal Basin, and autumn are the city's finest seasons.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and changeable — afternoons near 8°C, nights just below freezing, and storms that can bring rain, sleet or snow. Most winters bring only a few modest snowfalls, but occasional big storms bury the city and shut down the federal government. Arctic blasts are short-lived.`,spring:`The cherry blossom season — afternoons warming from about 14°C in March to 25°C by late May, the famous Japanese cherry trees around the Tidal Basin bursting into bloom in late March or early April, followed by dogwoods and azaleas. Frequent showers and the occasional late frost keep things changeable.`,summer:`Hot and steamy — afternoons near 31°C and often above 35°C, nights around 21°C, and humidity that hangs heavy over the city. Afternoon thunderstorms are common. Many residents escape to the beaches or mountains, and the monuments are best visited early in the morning or at night.`,autumn:`Mild and pleasant — September stays warm, but by October afternoons ease to about 21°C, the humidity drops, and the trees in Rock Creek Park and along the Potomac turn colour by late October. It is many residents' favourite season.`},contrasts:[{label:`Baltimore (≈60 km NE)`,note:`Up the coast on the Chesapeake, Baltimore has a similar climate, slightly cooler in summer and a little snowier in winter.`},{label:`Shenandoah & the Blue Ridge (≈100 km W, ≈900 m higher)`,note:`Up on Skyline Drive, summer afternoons run about 6°C cooler, and winter brings far more snow and ice.`}],history:[`On the evening of 24 August 1814, British troops marched into Washington after routing the American army at Bladensburg. Hours earlier, the First Lady, Dolley Madison, had refused to flee the President's House until a large portrait of George Washington was cut from its frame and carried to safety. The British officers ate the dinner left on the table, then set the house on fire, along with the Capitol and other public buildings. The next day a violent storm, possibly with a tornado, swept through the city, putting out some of the fires and killing British soldiers. The British withdrew. The burned shell was rebuilt and freshly painted white, and the nickname it had already begun to carry, the White House, stuck for good.`,`The Nacotchtank people farmed and fished along the Anacostia River, and the Piscataway and other Algonquian peoples lived along the Potomac when the English explorer John Smith sailed up the river in 1608. By the eighteenth century, tobacco planters and the port of Georgetown had taken over the region. In 1790 Congress chose a site on the Potomac for the new national capital, and George Washington hired a French engineer, Pierre L'Enfant, to plan it; Benjamin Banneker, a free Black astronomer, helped survey its boundaries. L'Enfant designed a city of grand avenues radiating from the Capitol and the President's House, and the government moved here in 1800.`,`Much of the early city was built by enslaved labourers, and Washington was a major slave market until 1850. In April 1862, eight months before the Emancipation Proclamation, Congress freed the enslaved people of the District. During the Civil War, Washington became an armed camp ringed by forts. The city grew into a world capital in the twentieth century, with the National Mall becoming the nation's stage for protest and celebration: Marian Anderson sang at the Lincoln Memorial in 1939, and Martin Luther King Jr. delivered his I Have a Dream speech there in 1963. In 1912 the mayor of Tokyo gave the city more than three thousand cherry trees, which now bloom each spring.`],deepTitle:`Capital on the river`,deep:[`The Washington Monument was begun in 1848, but construction stopped in 1854 for lack of money and then for the Civil War. When work resumed in the late 1870s, the builders could not get marble from the same quarry, and a visible change in the colour of the stone still marks the height, about a quarter of the way up, where the work had paused. When it was completed in 1884, it was the tallest structure in the world.`,`Washington is often said to have been built on a swamp, but historians point out that most of the city stood on dry ground above the rivers; only some low-lying areas near the Potomac and the tidal flats were marshy. The real problem was the heat and humidity of the summers, which drove Congress and many residents out of the city until air conditioning arrived in the twentieth century.`,`In January 1922 a huge snowstorm, later called the Knickerbocker Storm, dropped some 70 cm of snow on Washington. The weight of the snow on the flat roof of the Knickerbocker Theatre caused it to collapse during a film showing, killing 98 people. It remains one of the worst disasters in the city's history.`,`Marian Anderson, one of the greatest singers of her time, was refused permission to sing at Constitution Hall in 1939 because she was Black. Eleanor Roosevelt resigned from the organisation that owned the hall, and the government arranged for Anderson to sing on the steps of the Lincoln Memorial on Easter Sunday. About 75,000 people came to hear her, and the concert became a landmark of the civil rights movement.`]}},o={"osoyoos-bc":{why:`Pacific storms lose their water on the Coast Mountains and Cascades, then the air sinks and warms as it drops into the Okanagan trench. By Osoyoos the valley floor is low, the benches are open, and summer humidity stays thin. Osoyoos Lake is shallow enough to store heat, so shoreline nights stay milder than sage slopes a kilometre inland. Penticton, fifty kilometres north, is already cooler and a little wetter; Hope, west of the Cascades, takes several times the rain. The gradient is the point: a north-tip desert pocket of antelope-brush and rattlesnakes at 49°N, sitting on irrigation rather than rainfall.`,history:[`On the highway west of Osoyoos lies one of the strangest sights in Canada: a lake that, as it dries out each summer, breaks up into hundreds of small, round pools, each a different shade of blue, green or yellow, separated by walkways of hardened mineral salts. The Syilx people call it Kłlilxw and have long regarded it as a sacred place of healing. During the First World War, settlers mined its salts, which are rich in magnesium sulphate, for making ammunition. In the 1990s the rancher who owned it planned to turn it into a commercial spa, and the Okanagan Nation campaigned for years to stop him. In 2001 they bought the lake back. Visitors can look at Spotted Lake from the roadside, but only the Syilx may walk among its pools.`,`The southern Okanagan valley, a hot, dry trench of sagebrush and antelope-brush that is the northern tip of the Great Basin desert, has been home to the Syilx, or Okanagan people, for thousands of years. They fished for sockeye salmon in the river, gathered bitterroot and saskatoon berries, and crossed the narrows of Osoyoos Lake at a shallow ford. In the early nineteenth century, fur brigades of the Hudson's Bay Company used the valley as a route between the Columbia River and the interior of British Columbia. After the gold rush of 1858, cattle ranchers claimed the grasslands, and reserves were set aside for the Syilx bands.`,`The desert bloomed only after water arrived. After the First World War, the British Columbia government built a long irrigation canal down the valley to create farms for returning soldiers, and orchards of apples, peaches, cherries and apricots spread across the benches. Since the 1980s many orchards have given way to vineyards, and the South Okanagan has become one of Canada's best wine regions. The Osoyoos Indian Band, under its long-time chief Clarence Louie, built a remarkable business empire on its reserve land, including Nk'Mip Cellars, which opened in 2002 as the first Indigenous-owned winery in North America.`],deepTitle:`Pocket desert`,deep:[`The antelope-brush grasslands of the South Okanagan are among the most endangered ecosystems in Canada, home to species found nowhere else in the country, including the Great Basin spadefoot toad, the pallid bat, the burrowing owl and the western rattlesnake. Less than half of the original habitat remains, the rest lost to orchards, vineyards and towns. The Nk'Mip Desert Cultural Centre and several protected areas preserve some of what is left.`,`Osoyoos Lake is said to be the warmest freshwater lake in Canada, reaching more than 24°C in summer. It straddles the border with Washington State, and the town's beaches and resorts fill with visitors in July and August. The lake's narrows, where the Syilx once forded it, are now crossed by a highway bridge.`,`Clarence Louie, chief of the Osoyoos Indian Band since the mid-1980s, became famous across Canada for his blunt insistence that his community should create its own jobs and wealth rather than depend on government. Under his leadership, the band developed a winery, a golf course, a resort, a cultural centre and other businesses, and became one of the most prosperous First Nations in the country.`,`Wildfire is the great danger of the valley's hot, dry summers. In 2003 the Okanagan Mountain Park fire burned hundreds of homes in Kelowna, to the north, and in 2021 the Nk'Mip Creek fire burned across the hills near Osoyoos during a record heat wave. Smoke now regularly fills the valley in late summer.`]},"lethbridge-ab":{why:`Westerlies that have crossed the Rockies descend the eastern slope and warm by compression. In Lethbridge that chinook arrives often enough to interrupt prairie cold rather than merely decorate it: a lenticular arch over the front, then a jump of tens of degrees in hours. Pincher Creek, closer to Crowsnest Pass, is windier still; Edmonton, four degrees farther north and beyond the chinook belt, stays colder for weeks. The same dry descent that makes winter volatile also keeps summer humidity low and the grass short. Wind is not weather here. It is the climate's delivery system.`,history:[`In 1869, after Montana outlawed selling liquor to the Blackfoot, two American traders named John Healy and Alfred Hamilton crossed into what is now southern Alberta and built a trading post where the St. Mary and Oldman rivers meet. They traded watered-down whisky, often laced with tobacco, pepper and worse, for buffalo robes, and their fort became notorious. It was called Fort Whoop-Up. The whisky trade brought violence and misery to the Blackfoot nations, and the reports reaching Ottawa were one of the reasons the Canadian government created the North-West Mounted Police. In 1874 the Mounties made their long march west across the prairies to shut the trade down. When they reached Fort Whoop-Up, the traders were gone.`,`The country around Lethbridge belongs to the Niitsitapi, the Blackfoot Confederacy of the Siksika, Kainai and Piikani nations, who hunted bison across these plains for thousands of years. They called the place Sikoohkotoki, the place of black rocks, for the coal seams exposed in the river coulees. In 1874 a man named Nicholas Sheran began mining that coal, and in the 1880s the company of the Canadian businessman Alexander Galt opened larger mines, building a railway to carry the coal east. The town that grew up was named after William Lethbridge, the company's president, who never set foot in it.`,`Irrigation canals, dug in the 1890s and 1900s partly by Mormon settlers from Utah, transformed the dry prairie into farmland, and Lethbridge became the centre of an agricultural district of wheat, sugar beets and cattle. In 1909 the Canadian Pacific Railway completed the High Level Bridge across the Oldman River valley, at the time one of the longest and highest railway bridges in the world. During the Second World War Lethbridge held one of the largest prisoner-of-war camps in Canada, with more than 12,000 German prisoners. Today it is a university city, famous for its fierce chinook winds.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The High Level Bridge, also called the Lethbridge Viaduct, is more than 1.6 km long and nearly 100 m high, and it still carries trains across the Oldman River coulees. It was built because the original railway line wound slowly down and up the valley; the bridge cut the route short and straight. Seen from the river bottoms, its steel trestles are one of the most dramatic sights on the prairies.`,`The Lethbridge prisoner-of-war camp, known as Camp 133, held German soldiers captured in North Africa and Europe. Many worked on local farms, where they helped with the harvest, and some returned to Canada after the war to settle. The camp was also the scene of violence among the prisoners themselves, when committed Nazis murdered fellow prisoners they considered traitors.`,`During the Second World War, Japanese Canadians were removed from the British Columbia coast, and many families were sent to work on the sugar-beet farms around Lethbridge under harsh conditions. Some stayed after the war, and in 1967 the city opened the Nikka Yuko Japanese Garden, a symbol of friendship and reconciliation that is now one of the finest Japanese gardens in North America.`,`Lethbridge is one of the windiest cities in Canada. Chinook winds blowing down from the Rockies can raise the winter temperature by 20°C or more in hours, melting snow and turning frozen fields into mud. The same winds make southern Alberta one of the centres of wind power in Canada, and rows of turbines now stand on the ridges west of the city.`]},"victoria-bc":{why:`The Olympic Mountains stand sixty kilometres south across the Strait of Juan de Fuca and wring Pacific storms before the air reaches southern Vancouver Island. Victoria therefore takes roughly half Vancouver's rain while sitting in the same marine bath: summers dry and mild, winters rarely below freezing, snow a brief interruption. The Saanich Peninsula extends that rain-shadow pocket northward into Garry oak and camas meadow, the northern edge of a Mediterranean-adjacent plant community. Marine air caps heat that the Interior cannot escape. The mildness is purchased with cloud, housing cost, and a summer that never truly bakes.`,immersive:`Victoria sits in the rain shadow of the Olympic Mountains, 60 km south across the Strait of Juan de Fuca. Annual precipitation is a half to two-thirds of Vancouver's across the strait. Summers are dry and mild; winters are the softest in Canada — rare freezes, almost no snow, daffodils in February. Garry oak meadows and arbutus on southern Vancouver Island are the northernmost examples of a Mediterranean-adjacent plant community in North America, a camas landscape Lekwungen families managed with fire. The Inner Harbour still smells of salt and kelp on a still morning. Marine air caps heat that the Interior cannot escape. The mildness is real, and it is purchased with cloud, a housing market among Canada's tightest, and a summer that never truly bakes.`,history:[`On a Sunday morning in April 1858, the people of Victoria, then a quiet Hudson's Bay Company fort town of a few hundred people, were coming out of church when the American steamer Commodore pulled into the harbour and landed some 450 men with picks, shovels and gold pans. Word had reached San Francisco that gold had been found on the Fraser River, on the mainland of what would become British Columbia, and Victoria was the only port of entry. Over the next few months, tens of thousands of gold seekers poured through the town, buying supplies and mining licences. Tents and shacks sprang up everywhere, prices soared, and Victoria was transformed almost overnight from a fur-trading outpost into a busy colonial city.`,`The Lekwungen people — today the Songhees and Esquimalt Nations — lived around the harbour for thousands of years, harvesting camas bulbs from the oak meadows they managed with fire, and fishing for salmon and herring. In 1843 James Douglas of the Hudson's Bay Company chose the site for a new fort, worried that the company's headquarters on the Columbia River would fall to the United States. Between 1850 and 1854 he signed treaties with the local nations, among the few treaties ever made in British Columbia, paying them mostly in blankets for their lands. In 1871 British Columbia joined Canada, with Victoria as its capital.`,`The young architect Francis Rattenbury designed the Parliament Buildings, completed in 1898, and the grand Empress Hotel, opened in 1908, which together frame the Inner Harbour. But the railway went to Vancouver, which soon outgrew its rival, and Victoria settled into its role as a government town and a retirement haven with a famously English flavour of gardens, afternoon teas and double-decker buses. The painter Emily Carr was born here in 1871, and Jennie Butchart turned her husband's exhausted limestone quarry into the world-famous Butchart Gardens. Today Victoria is known for its mild climate, the mildest of any major Canadian city.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Francis Rattenbury's own story ended in tragedy. After designing many of British Columbia's finest buildings, his reputation and fortune declined, and he moved to England with his second wife, Alma. In 1935 he was beaten to death at their home in Bournemouth by the couple's young chauffeur, George Stoner, who was having an affair with Alma. Stoner was sentenced to death but reprieved; Alma, who was acquitted, took her own life days after the trial.`,`Emily Carr, Victoria's most famous artist, travelled up the coast of British Columbia painting the totem poles and villages of the First Nations and the dense, swirling rainforests. Her work was little appreciated for much of her life, and she ran a boarding house in Victoria to support herself. Recognition came only in her later years, and she is now one of Canada's most celebrated painters.`,`Jennie Butchart began planting around her husband's limestone quarry in 1904, and from 1909 turned the worked-out pit itself into a garden. Using soil brought in by horse and cart, she created a sunken garden on the floor of the quarry, and over the years the gardens grew to include Japanese, Italian and rose gardens. More than a million visitors come each year, and the gardens are a National Historic Site of Canada.`,`Victoria's mild climate comes from its position in the rain shadow of the Olympic Mountains of Washington State and the mountains of Vancouver Island, which block much of the rain carried by Pacific storms. The city gets less than half the rainfall of Vancouver and much more sunshine, and its winters are so mild that flowers bloom in February, when the city holds an annual flower count to show off to the rest of snowbound Canada.`]},"tofino-bc":{why:`Nothing stands between Tofino and the open North Pacific. Winter storms arrive with open-ocean fetch, orographic lift on Vancouver Island's spine, and no rain-shadow shelter. Annual precipitation exceeds 3000 mm. January and July mean highs differ by only about 9°C because the ocean sets the thermostat. Victoria, in the Olympic lee, is a different climate class; even Ucluelet, a short drive south, is slightly more inlet-sheltered. The hyper-maritime pattern is the whole story, not a scenic overlay. Storm surge, Highway 4 closures, and housing cost are how that story is lived.`,immersive:`A winter afternoon on Chesterman Beach is wind, salt, and a sky that does not so much rain as occupy the air. Swells stack against the outer reefs; Sitka spruce lean inland; the temperature sits in a narrow band that makes January feel less like a Canadian winter than a long, loud March. Summer is cool and often fog-bound, the ocean still in charge. People come for surf and storm-watching, then discover that errands, school, and clinic visits all run through a single mountain highway that washes out. The rainforest is not a backdrop. It is the hydrology made visible, dripping from every surface, filling ditches, and reminding anyone who gardens that drainage matters more than drought.`,seasons:{summer:`Cool, misty and occasionally glorious — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 11°C, and mornings that often begin in thick sea fog that may or may not burn off by noon. Rain eases off, but drizzle is never far away. When the sun breaks through, the long beaches fill with surfers, the rainforest steams, and grey whales feed just offshore.`},contrasts:[{label:`Port Alberni (≈90 km E, at the head of an inlet)`,note:`Inland behind the mountains, summer afternoons can run 10°C warmer, and the town gets far less fog.`},{label:`Vancouver (≈190 km E, across the Strait of Georgia)`,note:`Sheltered by Vancouver Island, Vancouver gets less than half of Tofino's rain and noticeably warmer summers.`}],history:[`In June 1811 the Tonquin, a ship sent by the New York fur magnate John Jacob Astor to trade for sea-otter pelts, anchored in Clayoquot Sound near today's Tofino. Its captain, Jonathan Thorn, was a hot-tempered naval officer with little patience for bargaining. When a Tla-o-qui-aht chief pressed him over prices, Thorn lost his temper and struck him in the face with a pelt. The next morning, warriors came aboard as if to trade, then attacked, killing most of the crew. The following day, as people swarmed over the ship to loot it, the Tonquin blew up in a huge explosion — probably set off by a wounded crewman who lit the gunpowder magazine — killing many of those on board. The wreck has never been conclusively found.`,`Clayoquot Sound, a maze of inlets, islands and rainforest on the west coast of Vancouver Island, is the territory of the Tla-o-qui-aht and other Nuu-chah-nulth nations, who have lived here for thousands of years, hunting whales from cedar canoes, fishing salmon and herring and building great plank houses. Spanish explorers named the area after the hydrographer Vicente Tofiño in 1792. Settlers from Norway, Britain, China and Japan arrived in the 1890s to fish and log, but the area stayed remote until a gravel road was pushed through from the east coast of the island in 1959.`,`In the 1960s and 1970s, surfers and counterculture drifters discovered the long beaches south of Tofino, and in 1970 Pacific Rim National Park Reserve was created to protect them. In 1984 the Tla-o-qui-aht declared Meares Island, across the harbour from Tofino, a tribal park to stop it being logged. In the summer of 1993, protesters blockaded logging roads in Clayoquot Sound to stop the clear-cutting of ancient forests; more than 800 people were arrested, at the time the largest act of civil disobedience in Canadian history. In 2000 the sound became a UNESCO biosphere reserve, and Tofino is now Canada's surfing capital.`],deepTitle:`Surf and rainforest`,deep:[`The fate of the Tonquin became one of the most famous stories of the Pacific fur trade. Most of what is known comes from an interpreter from the Quinault nation who survived and told the story later. The disaster ended Astor's plans to dominate the trade on the northwest coast, and for decades few European ships ventured into Clayoquot Sound.`,`The protests of 1993, known as the War in the Woods, began after the British Columbia government decided to allow logging in two-thirds of Clayoquot Sound. Protesters gathered at the Kennedy River bridge every morning to block the logging trucks, and the arrests drew international attention. The government later created a scientific panel that recommended more careful logging, and much of the sound's old-growth forest now stands protected.`,`Tofino's rainforest is home to giant western red cedars, some more than a thousand years old, and Sitka spruces that rise more than 70 m. The forest floor is thick with moss and ferns, and the trees drip with rain for much of the year. Meares Island's Big Tree Trail leads visitors to some of the largest cedars in the world.`,`Winter storms along the outer coast are so spectacular that storm watching has become a tourist season of its own. Waves 6 m high or more come crashing against the rocks at Cox Bay and Chesterman Beach, and visitors gather in hotels with big windows to watch. Tofino's surf is best in the stormy months, when the swells roll in from the open Pacific.`]},"wolfville-ns":{why:`North Mountain, a basalt ridge, stands between the Annapolis Valley floor and the Bay of Fundy's fog factory. South Mountain closes the other side. The result is a sheltered fruit trench: more sun, less fog, and enough summer heat to ripen apples and cool-climate grapes at a latitude that the open Atlantic would keep too raw. Fundy tides still set the regional clock, pushing and pulling huge volumes of water past the valley mouth. Halifax, about an hour's drive southeast, is foggier and more ocean-dominated. The valley is a climatic interior on a maritime peninsula.`,immersive:`Blossom week in the Annapolis Valley smells of apple and wet earth, with North Mountain a dark wall to the north and the Minas Basin a mud-and-silver sheet at low tide. The dykelands are ruler-straight, a seventeenth-century technology still holding back some of the world's highest tides. Summer afternoons can feel almost inland-warm; winter is a proper freeze, not Victoria's shrug. Wine trails and university lawns give Wolfville a soft public face. The honest climate includes ice storms, heavy rain on valley soils, and a Fundy wind that finds any gap in the ridges.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, green and pleasant — afternoons near 24°C, nights around 13°C, and sunny days in the valley while fog often hangs over the Bay of Fundy coast just to the north. Apples swell in the orchards, the vineyards ripen, and the farm markets overflow. The world's highest tides surge in and out of the Minas Basin twice a day.`},history:[`On 5 September 1755, the men and boys of the Acadian village of Grand-Pré, just east of today's Wolfville, were ordered into their parish church to hear an announcement from the British commander, Lieutenant-Colonel John Winslow. He read them an order from the colonial government: their lands, houses and livestock were forfeited to the Crown, and they and their families were to be deported. Over the following weeks, more than two thousand people from the Minas Basin were loaded onto ships and scattered to the British colonies along the Atlantic coast; across Nova Scotia, some ten thousand Acadians were expelled. The Grand Dérangement, as the Acadians call it, became the subject of Henry Wadsworth Longfellow's 1847 poem Evangeline, and Grand-Pré is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.`,`The Mi'kmaq have lived in this part of Nova Scotia, Mi'kma'ki, for thousands of years. French Acadian settlers arrived at Grand-Pré around 1680 and built dykes with clever one-way sluice gates called aboiteaux, which let fresh water drain out of the marshes at low tide but kept the salt tides of the Bay of Fundy from flowing in. Over generations they turned the salt marshes into some of the richest farmland in North America. After the expulsion, the British gave the empty farms to settlers from New England, known as the Planters, who arrived in 1760.`,`One of the Planter families, the DeWolfs, gave their name to the village of Mud Creek, which was renamed Wolfville in 1830, according to local legend because the ladies of the town were embarrassed to write Mud Creek on their letters. Horton Academy, founded in 1828, grew into Acadia University, which still dominates the town. The Annapolis Valley became famous for its apples, shipped by the barrel to Britain, and the Apple Blossom Festival has been held each spring since 1933. In recent decades, vineyards have spread across the valley, and Nova Scotia's crisp white wines have won a following.`],deepTitle:`Dykes and tides`,deep:[`The Bay of Fundy has the highest tides in the world, and in the Minas Basin near Wolfville the difference between high and low tide can exceed 15 m. At low tide the harbour at Wolfville empties completely, leaving boats sitting on the red mud. The aboiteau dykes built by the Acadians are still maintained, protecting farmland that lies below the level of the highest tides.`,`Longfellow's Evangeline tells the story of a young Acadian woman separated from her fiancé on their wedding day by the expulsion, and her lifelong search for him across America. The poem was enormously popular and made Grand-Pré famous; a statue of Evangeline stands in the memorial park there, beside a church built in 1922 on the site of the original.`,`Many of the deported Acadians eventually made their way to Louisiana, then a French and later Spanish colony, where their descendants became known as Cajuns. Others returned to the Maritimes after the war, settling in new communities because their old farms had been given to the Planters. Today Acadian culture thrives in New Brunswick, Nova Scotia and Prince Edward Island.`,`The Annapolis Valley's climate, sheltered by North Mountain from the cold, foggy Bay of Fundy and by South Mountain from Atlantic storms, gives it a longer, warmer growing season than most of Nova Scotia. Apples, pears, cherries and grapes thrive, and Nova Scotia's wineries created a province-wide appellation, Tidal Bay, for crisp white wines that suit the cool climate.`]},"niagara-on-the-lake":{why:`Lake Ontario holds summer heat into autumn and slows the spring warmup, while the Niagara Escarpment blocks cold air draining off the Shield. Between them lies a belt only a few kilometres wide where tender fruit and vinifera grapes survive at a latitude that fails them inland. Winters are mild by Ontario standards, not by Victoria's. Summers are warm and humid. Icewine needs a reliable freeze after a warm enough season to ripen grapes; that double threshold is why the peninsula became famous, and why warming is not an unqualified gift.`,immersive:`A September evening on the Niagara bench smells of grape skin and lake humidity, with the escarpment a wooded step behind the vines and the lake a pale sheet ahead. Mornings can start in fog; afternoons turn muggy. The old town's Georgian streets were laid out as a garrison capital, not a wine village, and they still hold that upright, close-packed feel. Winter is damp-cold rather than prairie-sharp, broken by lake-effect snow when the wind is right. Visitors come for Shaw plays and tasting rooms. Residents count freeze dates, spray schedules, and the weekend traffic that those same tasting rooms generate.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, humid and lake-breezy — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 17°C, and breezes off Lake Ontario that keep the old town comfortable. Thunderstorms roll over the escarpment on hot afternoons. The vineyards are lush, the orchards heavy with peaches and cherries, and the theatres of the Shaw Festival fill every night.`},contrasts:[{label:`Above the Niagara Escarpment (≈15 km S, ≈100 m higher)`,note:`On top of the escarpment, away from the lake's moderating influence, winter nights run colder and frosts come earlier and later.`},{label:`Toronto (≈50 km N, across Lake Ontario)`,note:`On the far shore of the lake, Toronto's climate is similar, with slightly cooler lakeshore summers and a little more snow.`}],history:[`On the bitterly cold afternoon of 10 December 1813, during the War of 1812, the commander of the American troops occupying the town of Newark, as Niagara-on-the-Lake was then called, ordered it burned before his army retreated across the Niagara River. The residents, mostly women, children and the elderly, were given a few hours' warning and turned out into the snow as their houses went up in flames. Almost all of the town's buildings were destroyed. The British were outraged, and within weeks they retaliated by burning Buffalo and other American towns across the river. The following summer, British troops burned Washington, including the White House, partly in revenge for American burnings in Canada.`,`The Neutral people farmed the Niagara Peninsula until they were destroyed in wars with the Haudenosaunee around 1650; later the Mississauga lived in the area. After the American Revolution, Loyalists who had fought for the British, including the members of Butler's Rangers, settled at the mouth of the Niagara River. In 1792 Lieutenant Governor John Graves Simcoe made the town, then called Newark, the first capital of Upper Canada. In 1793 the legislature meeting here passed an Act Against Slavery, the first law in the British Empire to restrict slavery, which banned bringing new enslaved people into the province. The capital moved to York, now Toronto, in 1796, safely out of range of American guns.`,`After the War of 1812, the town was rebuilt, and many of the elegant houses of the early nineteenth century still line its streets, making it one of the best-preserved colonial towns in Canada. In 1962 a group of residents founded the Shaw Festival to perform the plays of George Bernard Shaw, and it became one of the leading theatre festivals in North America. In 1975 two winemakers, Donald Ziraldo and Karl Kaiser, received the first new winery licence in Ontario since Prohibition for their winery, Inniskillin, and in 1991 their icewine won a top prize in France, putting Niagara on the world wine map.`],deepTitle:`Capital and icewine`,deep:[`The Act Against Slavery of 1793 was prompted by the case of Chloe Cooley, an enslaved woman who was violently bound and forced into a boat by her owner to be sold across the river in New York. Her screams were witnessed by others, and the incident moved Simcoe to push for a law to end slavery. The act did not free people already enslaved, but it ensured that slavery would gradually die out in Upper Canada, which later became a refuge for people escaping slavery in the United States.`,`Fort George, built by the British in the 1790s to guard the mouth of the Niagara River, was captured by the Americans in 1813 and later retaken. It has been reconstructed and is now a national historic site, where costumed interpreters fire muskets and cannons. Across the river stands Fort Niagara, on the American side, a reminder of how close the two countries were to each other, and to war.`,`Icewine is made from grapes left on the vine until the first hard freeze of winter, usually in December or January, when temperatures fall below about −8°C. The frozen grapes are picked by hand, often at night, and pressed while still frozen, producing a tiny amount of intensely sweet juice. Niagara's reliably cold winters and warm summers make it one of the world's leading producers.`,`Major General Isaac Brock, the British commander who led the defence of Upper Canada in the War of 1812, was killed at the Battle of Queenston Heights, just south of the town, in October 1812, while leading a charge up the slope. The British won the battle, and Brock became a Canadian hero. A tall column in his honour stands on the heights above the Niagara River.`]},"atlin-lake-bc":{why:`Atlin Lake is a long, glacier-fed body of water on the lee side of the Coast Mountains, so the air is drier than coastal British Columbia and less brutally continental than a treeless Yukon basin. The lake stores cold and delays freeze and breakup, softening the edges of a Dfc climate without erasing darkness, wind, or a short growing season. Whitehorse is larger, drier, and more basin-like; the coast west of the icefields is a different world of snow and rain. Remoteness is part of the climate: the only highway arrives through Yukon, and services thin out as soon as you leave the shore.`,immersive:`Evening light on Atlin Lake lasts, in June, until the idea of night feels theoretical, the water a cold sheet under mountains that still hold snow. Winter is another country: dry cold, aurora, and a town of a few hundred people whose road to anywhere else runs north into Yukon before it can run south. The air is clearer than a coastal rainforest, the silence larger. Gardens happen in raised beds and against south walls. The lake is the reason the settlement exists and the reason it feels less severe than maps of 59°N suggest, without ever becoming gentle.`,seasons:{summer:`Brief, bright and vivid — afternoons near 18°C, nights cooling to about 7°C, and nearly twenty hours of daylight around the solstice. The lake, fed by glaciers, stays bone-chillingly cold, but the slopes above town blaze with fireweed and the light lingers golden late into the evening. A sudden storm can blow up on the lake in minutes, and smoke from wildfires sometimes drifts in from the north.`},history:[`In the summer of 1898, while tens of thousands of gold seekers were struggling over the passes toward the Klondike, two prospectors named Fritz Miller and Kenneth McLaren turned off the trail and headed east to a remote lake, following a rumour of gold. They found it in Pine Creek, just above the lake shore, and by the following year several thousand miners had rushed to Atlin, building a boomtown of hotels, saloons and banks in the wilderness. Unlike most gold-rush towns, Atlin never quite died. Its gold creeks are still being worked today, and the town remains one of the most remote communities in British Columbia.`,`The lake, called Áa Tlein, the big lake, by the Taku River Tlingit, is the largest natural lake in British Columbia, stretching some 130 km north until its tip crosses into the Yukon. The Tlingit and their ancestors used it for hunting, fishing and travel between the interior and the coast for thousands of years, and their village, Wéinaa, stands on the eastern shore. In the 1920s and 1930s, tourists came by steamer and train from Skagway, Alaska, to stay at the Atlin Inn and cruise the lake aboard the MV Tarahne, a passenger boat that now sits on the shore in town as a museum.`,`Atlin is so remote that it is connected to the rest of the world only by a gravel road north to the Alaska Highway in the Yukon, completed in 1950; there is no road south into the rest of British Columbia. In 1973 the province created Atlin Provincial Park, protecting the southern end of the lake and the vast Llewellyn Glacier, part of the Juneau Icefield. Today the town of a few hundred people attracts artists, adventurers and summer visitors, who call it the Switzerland of the North.`],deepTitle:`Gold on the big lake`,deep:[`The Atlin gold rush was complicated by the question of who owned the claims. The BC government passed a law in 1899 banning foreigners from staking claims, aimed at the many Americans who had rushed in, and some disputes dragged on for years. The rush also drew Chinese and Japanese miners. Placer mining tore up the creeks, and the piles of gravel left by hydraulic mining and dredges can still be seen.`,`The MV Tarahne, built in Atlin in 1917, carried tourists across the lake to Scotia Bay, where they boarded a short railway to Taku Arm and continued their journey. It was retired in 1936 and hauled onto the shore, where it has been restored. Visitors can walk its decks and imagine the days when Atlin was a fashionable destination for travellers to the north.`,`The Llewellyn Glacier, which flows into the southern end of Atlin Lake from the Juneau Icefield, is one of the largest glaciers in British Columbia. Its meltwater gives the lake its milky turquoise colour, and the cold water keeps the air around the lake cool even in summer. The glacier has been retreating rapidly in recent decades.`,`Atlin's climate is a blend of the cold, dry interior and the moist coast. The Coast Mountains to the west block most Pacific storms, so the town is sunnier and drier than the coast, but the huge lake moderates the temperature, keeping winters a little milder than other places this far north.`]},"fernie-elk-valley-bc":{why:`Pacific moisture that has already crossed the Columbia and Purcell ranges still has enough water left to be wrung out on the Elk Valley and the Continental Divide. A narrow Rocky Mountain trench, steep relief, and winter storm tracks turn Fernie into a snow-loaded mountain town rather than a dry prairie-foothills cousin. Cranbrook, in the Rocky Mountain Trench, is sunnier and less buried. Sparwood and Elkford, up-valley, are colder and more industrial. Avalanche paths, floodplain, and wildfire smoke are not extras. They are how a snow climate occupies a working coal valley.`,immersive:`Fernie is not a mild BC hideout; it is a snowy, steep, working mountain town. Pacific moisture that survives the Columbia and Purcell ranges is wrung out again on the Elk Valley and Continental Divide, giving the place a deep winter identity and a brick downtown rebuilt after the 1908 fire. Summer is comfortable, green, and active, with warm afternoons and cool nights, then a July ceiling of wildfire smoke when the Rockies burn. Avalanche paths hang above the highway; valley-floor lots flood. The caution is not a footnote: smoke, slope, floodplain choices, and winter maintenance are the lived climate in a coal valley that also learned to sell snow. Cranbrook, in the trench, is the sunnier, less buried cousin.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm days and crisp nights — afternoons near 25°C in July and August, nights cooling to about 8°C as cold air drains off the peaks, and afternoon thunderstorms that build over the mountains. The Elk River runs clear for fly-fishing, the ski hill becomes a mountain-bike park, and wildflowers fill the alpine meadows. Wildfire smoke can fill the valley in hot, dry summers.`},history:[`According to a local legend, the prospector William Fernie learned of the coal seams in the Elk Valley from a Ktunaxa chief, after admiring a necklace of shiny black stones worn by the chief's daughter. Fernie supposedly promised to marry her in exchange for being shown where the coal was found, then broke his promise. The chief, the story goes, put a curse on the valley: it would suffer from fire, flood and famine. Whatever the truth of the tale, the town that took Fernie's name did suffer. A mine explosion in 1902 killed 128 men; fires burned down the town's business district in 1904 and destroyed almost the entire town in 1908; and floods followed. In 1964 a Ktunaxa chief and the town's mayor held a ceremony to lift the curse.`,`The Elk Valley is the homeland of the Ktunaxa, whose territory spans the Rocky Mountains across what is now British Columbia, Alberta, Idaho and Montana. They hunted elk, deer and mountain sheep here and fished the rivers for trout. In the 1880s William Fernie and his partners began exploring the region's rich coal deposits, and in 1898 the Canadian Pacific Railway built a line through the Crowsnest Pass to carry the coal. The town of Fernie grew rapidly as a coal-mining centre, attracting immigrants from Britain, Italy, Eastern Europe and China.`,`After the 1908 fire, which left some 6,000 people homeless, Fernie was rebuilt in brick and stone, and many of those buildings still stand. Coal mining continued, and during the First World War an internment camp nearby held immigrants from the Austro-Hungarian Empire. In 1962 a ski area opened on the slopes above town, famous for its deep powder snow, and Fernie gradually became a ski and outdoor town as well as a mining one. Today the large open-pit coal mines of the Elk Valley still supply steelmakers around the world, while the town's restored downtown fills with skiers and mountain bikers.`],deepTitle:`The Fernie curse`,deep:[`The Coal Creek mine explosion of 22 May 1902 was one of the worst mining disasters in Canadian history. Gas and coal dust ignited deep underground, killing 128 miners. More explosions followed in later years, and the dangers of the mines, along with conflicts with the coal company, made Fernie a centre of union organising in the early twentieth century.`,`On summer evenings, a shadow appears on the face of Mount Hosmer above Fernie in the shape of a rider on horseback, known as the Ghostrider. Local legend links it to the curse, saying it represents the chief's daughter and her father pursuing Fernie, or Fernie himself fleeing. It is caused by the setting sun casting shadows across the rock.`,`The Fernie internment camp of the First World War held men from Ukraine and other parts of the Austro-Hungarian Empire who were classified as enemy aliens, even though many had come to Canada to escape that empire. They were forced to work in the mines and the bush. The internments are now recognised as an injustice, and plaques commemorate them.`,`The open-pit coal mines of the Elk Valley produce steelmaking coal exported mainly to Asia. Mining has released selenium, a toxic element, into the Elk River and downstream into Montana and Idaho, harming fish, and the pollution has become a cross-border environmental issue. Mining companies are building treatment plants to reduce the contamination.`]},"sunshine-coast-bc":{why:`Vancouver Island's Beaufort Range stands between the open Pacific and this stretch of the Strait of Georgia, so Sechelt receives more sun and less rain than Vancouver or the outer Island. It is still a Csb coast: wet winters, dry-ish summers, marine air year-round. The Sunshine Coast name is relative, not Californian. Ferries are part of the climate in practice, because the weather that closes Horseshoe Bay or Langdale rewrites the week. Mildness is real. So is the marine cloud that tourists omit from the brochure.`,immersive:`Sechelt sits on a low isthmus between the Strait of Georgia and Sechelt Inlet, the water never far, arbutus peeling on south rock, Douglas-fir holding the back slopes. Winters are dark-green and wet without being Tofino-wet; summers can run weeks of high cloud and then a dry spell that browns the moss. The ferry wake at Langdale is the region's metronome. People who move here for sun discover a milder, brighter Vancouver rather than a new climate class, and they discover that groceries, specialists, and many jobs still live on the other side of a sailing.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, grey and wet — afternoons near 7°C, nights just above freezing, and Pacific storms that bring heavy rain, though a little less than the North Shore mountains across the water. Snow falls occasionally but rarely lasts at sea level. Storms can delay the ferries, the only link to Vancouver, and the forests drip with rain.`,summer:`Warm, sunny and gentle — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 14°C, and long dry spells with more sunshine than Vancouver, thanks to the rain shadow of Vancouver Island. The ocean warms enough for swimming in sheltered bays, and kayakers explore the inlets while orcas and seals swim past.`},contrasts:[{label:`North Shore mountains above Vancouver (≈35 km E)`,note:`Rising straight from the sea into the path of Pacific storms, the North Shore gets well over half again as much rain, and much more snow up high.`},{label:`Vancouver (≈30 km SE, across Howe Sound)`,note:`The big city across the water has a similar mild climate, a little rainier and cloudier than the Sunshine Coast.`}],history:[`From 1972 to 1990, the town of Gibsons on the Sunshine Coast was the setting for The Beachcombers, a Canadian television series about a log salvager named Nick Adonidas and his rival, Relic, who chased logs that had broken loose from booms in the waters of Howe Sound. The show ran for 387 episodes and was broadcast in dozens of countries, making it one of the longest-running dramatic series in Canadian television history. Its fictional café, Molly's Reach, was a real building on the Gibsons waterfront, and it still operates as a restaurant, drawing fans from around the world.`,`The Sunshine Coast is the traditional territory of the shíshálh, or Sechelt, Nation and the Sḵwx̱wú7mesh, or Squamish, Nation. The shíshálh lived in villages along the inlets, including Pender Harbour, and fished, hunted and gathered along a coast of deep fjords and forested mountains. In the late nineteenth century, missionaries, loggers and fishermen arrived, and settlers founded towns such as Gibsons, named after George Gibson, a retired naval officer who settled there in 1886. In the 1920s a settler named Harry Roberts painted the words Sunshine Belt on the side of his store at Roberts Creek, an early version of the region's sunny name.`,`It is not an island, but it might as well be: no road connects it to the rest of British Columbia, and the only ways in by car are ferries, from Horseshoe Bay near Vancouver or from Vancouver Island via Powell River and the Saltery Bay–Earls Cove crossing. That isolation has kept its towns small and its pace slow. In 1986 the shíshálh Nation became the first Indigenous nation in Canada to leave the Indian Act and govern itself, gaining ownership of its lands. Today the coast is home to retirees, artists, commuters and holiday-makers drawn by its mild climate and quiet coves.`],deepTitle:`Beachcombers' coast`,deep:[`The Skookumchuck Narrows, at the entrance to Sechelt Inlet, have some of the fastest tidal rapids in the world. When the tide changes, water rushes through the narrow passage at up to about 30 km/h, creating whirlpools and standing waves that draw expert kayakers and surfers. Visitors walk through the forest to watch the rapids from the rocks.`,`The shíshálh Nation's self-government agreement of 1986 was a landmark for Indigenous rights in Canada. Under the Sechelt Indian Band Self-Government Act, the nation gained the power to make its own laws and to own its lands outright, rather than having them held in trust by the federal government. It became a model for other First Nations seeking to govern themselves.`,`Logging was the Sunshine Coast's main industry for much of the twentieth century. Logs were cut in the forests above the inlets, floated down to the sea and gathered into booms, which were towed by tugboats to the mills. Logs that broke free from the booms were valuable, and beachcombers collected them for a reward, as in the television series.`,`More sunshine falls here than in Vancouver because it lies partly in the rain shadow of the mountains of Vancouver Island, which catch some of the rain from Pacific storms. Its summers are notably dry and sunny, and it has become a popular retreat for people seeking a milder, quieter life near the city.`]},"salt-spring-bc":{why:`The Olympic rain shadow reaches the Southern Gulf Islands. Salt Spring's valleys and harbours sit in marine air that rarely freezes hard, with summers dry enough for Garry oak and arbutus. Precipitation is far below the outer coast's, though still well above the Okanagan's. Island shelter cuts wind relative to the open strait, but ferry fog and a winter damp remain. The Mediterranean hint is real on south rock and rain-shadow farms. It is not a licence to plant a California garden without a water plan.`,immersive:`Ganges Harbour on a Saturday morning is floatplanes, market stalls, and arbutus bark peeling over tide-line rock. The island smells of fir duff and, in late summer, dry grass. Winter is a long green rain, rarely a snowpack, with ferries cancelled when the strait kicks up. Farms in the valleys hold the older agricultural promise that drew Black, Hawaiian, and later Japanese families. The pace is island-slow until a sailing is missed. Then the climate's mildness is beside the point: the constraint is water, housing, and a dock.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, grey and damp — afternoons near 6°C, nights just above freezing, and steady rain from Pacific storms, though less than on the mainland. A snowfall comes most winters and briefly turns the island's hilly roads into toboggan runs. The farms are quiet, wood smoke drifts from chimneys, and the ferries keep their winter schedule.`,summer:`Warm, dry and golden — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 12°C, and long rainless weeks that turn the meadows of Garry oak and arbutus golden brown. The Saturday market in Ganges overflows with cheeses, lamb, pottery and produce, kayakers paddle the sheltered coves, and fire risk climbs by August.`},contrasts:[{label:`Victoria (≈40 km S)`,note:`Deeper in the Olympic rain shadow, Victoria gets noticeably less rain and a little more sunshine.`},{label:`Vancouver (≈60 km E, across the Strait)`,note:`On the mainland, Vancouver's winters are wetter and its summers slightly cooler and cloudier.`}],history:[`In 1860 a young woman named Sylvia Stark arrived on Salt Spring Island with her husband, Louis, and their children. She had been born into slavery in Missouri, and her family had bought their freedom and moved to California, where new laws soon stripped Black residents of their rights. In 1858 the governor of the colony of Vancouver Island, James Douglas, whose own mother was of mixed African descent, invited Black Californians to settle in his colony, and hundreds accepted. Salt Spring was among the first places they farmed. Sylvia Stark lived on the island for the rest of her long life, working her farm and telling stories of her journey, and died in 1944, more than a hundred years old.`,`The island lies in the traditional territory of Coast Salish peoples, including the W̱SÁNEĆ and Quw'utsun, who harvested camas, clams and salmon around its shores. Its English name comes from the brine springs at its north end. The first settlers who arrived in 1859 were a mix of Black Americans, Europeans and Hawaiians, many of them married to Indigenous women, making the island one of the most diverse communities in the colony. Japanese Canadian families later built some of its most productive farms, until in April 1942 they were rounded up and shipped off the island; their property was sold without their consent, and few returned.`,`After the Second World War, Salt Spring became a haven for artists, craftspeople, back-to-the-landers and, in the 1960s, American draft resisters. The island's Saturday market in Ganges, begun in the 1970s, became famous for its handmade goods, and its farms produce prized lamb, cheese and apples. With no bridge to the mainland, it remains reachable only by ferry or floatplane, and its residents have long resisted development to keep its rural character. Today about 11,000 people live on the island, which has the largest population of the Gulf Islands.`],deepTitle:`Island of newcomers`,deep:[`The Black settlers of Salt Spring faced hardship and danger, including conflicts with neighbouring Indigenous groups and the isolation of the early island. Some families stayed for generations, and their descendants are still part of the island's community. Their story is told in the Salt Spring Island museum and in the island's historical society, which has worked to recover this chapter of British Columbia's past.`,`Salt Spring Island lamb, raised on the island's grassy meadows, has been prized by chefs since the nineteenth century. Its orchards hold many heritage apple varieties planted by early settlers, and the island's apple festival each autumn displays hundreds of varieties. Its cheesemakers and farmers have made it one of the best-known food destinations in British Columbia.`,`The island's Garry oak meadows, with their spring carpets of camas and other wildflowers, are among the rarest ecosystems in Canada. They were once maintained by Indigenous peoples using fire to keep them open for camas harvesting. Today most have been lost to farms and houses, and conservation groups work to protect what remains.`,`Salt Spring's population grew rapidly from the 1960s as people seeking an alternative lifestyle arrived. The island developed a reputation for independence and eccentricity, and in 2001 it began issuing its own currency, the Salt Spring dollar, to support local businesses. Debates over growth, housing and water are constant, as the island's limited freshwater supplies come under pressure.`]},"summerland-bc":{why:`Mid-Okanagan benches sit above a deep lake that slows winter cold, under a rain shadow that keeps summers dry and sunny. South-facing slopes add thermal-belt advantage: cold air drains to the lake while the benches stay a few degrees kinder on radiation nights. Osoyoos is hotter and more desert; Kelowna is larger and a little cooler. Summerland is the orchard-and-vineyard middle, still BSk at heart, still irrigation-dependent. Wildfire is the seasonal tax on that arrangement.`,immersive:`From the Giant's Head side, Okanagan Lake is a long blue trench and the benches are a stacked geometry of orchards, vines, and sage. Summer light is hard; shade is a design problem. Winter is short by Canadian standards, with the lake delaying the worst cold, then giving it back as a damp inversion on still days. Packing-house towns used to smell of apples in September. Now the harvest calendar includes wine grapes and a smoke season that can shut the same patios the climate was supposed to guarantee.`,feel:`Summerland feels like a sunny orchard town perched above a long blue lake. Spread over benches and terraces on the west shore of Okanagan Lake, among orchards, vineyards and dry hillsides of sagebrush and ponderosa pine, it lives up to its name for much of the year, for summers in the Okanagan are hot, dry and brilliant. Winters are a different story, grey and cool, with fog and low cloud trapped over the lake for weeks at a time, though snow rarely lies long on the valley floor.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey, quiet and mild for the interior — afternoons near 2°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and long spells of low cloud trapped over the valley by temperature inversions, while the hills above sit in sunshine. Snow falls lightly and the lake rarely freezes, and nearby ski hills draw residents up into the sunshine.`,spring:`Blossom time — afternoons warming from about 10°C in March to 21°C by late May, the orchards of cherries, apricots, peaches and apples bursting into bloom in April, and occasional late frosts that send growers scrambling to protect their trees. The hills turn green briefly before the summer drought.`,summer:`Hot, dry and sunny — afternoons near 29°C in July and often above 35°C, nights cooling to about 14°C, and very little rain. The lake warms for swimming, cherries and apricots ripen in the orchards, and the vineyards on the benches grow heavy with grapes. Wildfire smoke is the season's great risk.`,autumn:`Harvest season — September afternoons near 23°C, cooling to about 6°C by November, apples and grapes coming in from the orchards and vineyards, and the hills turning gold. The first valley fog of winter creeps in by November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the wineries and fruit stands of the Bottleneck Drive, swimming in Okanagan Lake, the steam train of the Kettle Valley Railway and hot, dry summer days that feel more Californian than Canadian.`,contrasts:[{label:`Penticton (≈15 km S)`,note:`At the south end of Okanagan Lake, Penticton is slightly warmer and drier, with the hot South Okanagan just beyond.`},{label:`Osoyoos (≈60 km S)`,note:`At the bottom of the valley, in Canada's pocket desert, summer afternoons run a few degrees hotter and the land is far drier.`}],history:[`In the early twentieth century, the Canadian Pacific Railway wanted a line through the mountains of southern British Columbia to carry ore from the mines of the Kootenays to the coast and keep American railways out. The job of building it fell to an engineer named Andrew McCulloch, and the Kettle Valley Railway he completed in 1915 was a feat so daring that people called it McCulloch's Wonder. It climbed and twisted through canyons on trestles and tunnels, and at Summerland it crossed Trout Creek on a steel bridge some 73 m high. Most of the line was abandoned in the 1980s, but a stretch near Summerland still runs as a heritage steam railway, crossing the great bridge, and much of the rest has become a cycling trail.`,`Summerland lies in the territory of the Syilx, or Okanagan, people, who fished the lake, gathered roots and berries and wintered in villages along the shore. Their stories tell of N'ha-a-itk, a spirit of the lake, which settlers turned into the legend of the Ogopogo, a lake monster said to live in Okanagan Lake. In 1902 a promoter named John Moore Robinson, backed by the president of the Canadian Pacific Railway, laid out the town, dividing the benches above the lake into small orchard lots and bringing water from the creeks to irrigate them. He named it Summerland for its sunny climate.`,`Orchards spread across the hills, and in 1914 the federal government opened an experimental farm in Summerland to help settlers learn to grow fruit. Its scientists went on to breed some of the world's most important fruit varieties, including the Spartan apple and the Lapins and Sweetheart cherries, now planted in orchards around the world. In recent decades vineyards have replaced many orchards, and Summerland is now part of one of Canada's leading wine regions, its benches dotted with wineries overlooking the lake.`],deepTitle:`Orchards and Ogopogo`,deep:[`The Summerland Research and Development Centre, as the experimental farm is now known, has bred dozens of fruit varieties suited to the Okanagan's climate. Its cherries, developed to be self-pollinating and to ripen late, extended the cherry season and opened export markets in Asia. The farm's ornamental gardens, laid out in the early twentieth century, are open to visitors.`,`Giant's Head Mountain, which rises just behind Summerland's town centre, is the eroded core of an ancient volcano. Seen from certain angles, it resembles a giant face lying on its back. A road and trail lead to the summit, with sweeping views of Okanagan Lake.`,`The Ogopogo has been reported in Okanagan Lake since the nineteenth century, usually described as a long, dark, serpent-like creature with humps. Early settlers, following Syilx tradition, sometimes carried a small animal on their boats to offer to the spirit when crossing the lake. The legend is a popular tourist attraction, and sightings are still reported.`,`The Kettle Valley Railway's route through the mountains included the Myra Canyon near Kelowna, where eighteen trestles cling to the walls of a canyon. Many of those trestles burned in the 2003 Okanagan Mountain Park fire but were rebuilt, and the rail trail is now one of the most popular cycling routes in British Columbia.`]},"grand-manan-nb":{why:`Twelve-metre Fundy tides move enormous volumes of cold water past Grand Manan, so summer sea-surface temperatures stay low and winter water stays relatively warm. The island's climate is fog, wind, and a small temperature range: cool summers, milder winters than inland New Brunswick, and rain that rarely becomes a drought. Mainland Charlotte County heats and freezes harder. The tidal engine is the reason puffins, weirs, and dulse share a shore that feels more oceanic than the latitude suggests.`,immersive:`Fog takes the cliffs and then the road, a wet wool that smells of rockweed and diesel from the ferry at North Head. On a clear evening the Bay of Fundy is a moving plane of cold water, tidal rips drawing lines offshore where whales feed. Winter is raw rather than arctic; summer rarely commits to heat. Herring weirs still stand in a few coves like wooden ribs. The island is 90 minutes of ferry from Blacks Harbour, and that crossing is the climate's other fact: you live here only as far as the sailing allows.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, raw and stormy — afternoons near 1°C, nights near −8°C, and nor'easters that sweep across the Bay of Fundy with snow, sleet and gale-force winds, sometimes cancelling the ferry for days. The surrounding sea keeps the island a little milder than the mainland, but the wind makes it feel colder. Fishing boats wait out the storms in the harbours.`,summer:`Cool, foggy and fresh — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 12°C, and fog that rolls in off the cold Bay of Fundy and can wrap the island for days. When the fog lifts, the views from the cliffs are spectacular. Whales feed in the rich waters, puffins nest on nearby islands, and the beaches at Dark Harbour are covered with dulse drying in the sun.`},contrasts:[{label:`Saint John (≈75 km NE, on the mainland)`,note:`Across the bay, Saint John is one of the foggiest cities in Canada, with similar cool summers but colder winter nights.`},{label:`Fredericton (≈170 km N, inland)`,note:`Away from the sea, Fredericton's summers run several degrees hotter and its winters are much colder and snowier.`}],history:[`In 1922 the American novelist Willa Cather, already famous for books such as My Ántonia, came to Grand Manan for a summer holiday, and fell in love with the island. She and her companion, Edith Lewis, built a simple cottage above Whale Cove, and Cather returned almost every summer for nearly twenty years, writing in the quiet of the island and walking its cliffs and beaches. The cottage was the only house she ever owned. In 1833 another famous visitor, the naturalist John James Audubon, had spent time on the island studying its birds, and Grand Manan has been a destination for birdwatchers and nature lovers ever since.`,`The Passamaquoddy people, whose territory spans the border between Maine and New Brunswick, came to Grand Manan for centuries to fish, hunt seals and gather seaweed. Loyalists fleeing the American Revolution arrived in 1784, led by Moses Gerrish, and settled on the island to fish. Over the following century, fishermen built weirs, large circular traps of stakes and nets in the shallow coves, to catch the herring that swarmed the bay. By the late nineteenth century, Grand Manan had dozens of weirs and canneries packing sardines, and it became one of the herring capitals of the Atlantic.`,`The herring fishery declined in the late twentieth century, and only a few weirs are still worked today. Grand Manan has turned to lobster fishing, aquaculture and tourism, drawing visitors for its whales, birds and cliffs. The island is also famous for dulse, an edible red seaweed harvested at Dark Harbour on its western shore, where the cliffs shelter one of the best dulse beds in the world. Today about 2,500 people live on the island, reached by a ferry ride of about ninety minutes from Blacks Harbour on the mainland.`],deepTitle:`Weirs and dulse`,deep:[`Herring weirs are among the oldest fishing techniques in North America, adapted from methods used by the Passamaquoddy. Fishermen drive tall poles into the seabed in a heart-shaped pattern and hang nets or brush between them; herring swim in on the rising tide and become trapped. Each weir has its own name, some of them dating back more than a century.`,`Machias Seal Island, a small rocky island about 20 km southwest of Grand Manan, is home to a large colony of Atlantic puffins and is one of the last places where Canada and the United States still dispute ownership of land. A Canadian lighthouse has stood there since 1832, and both countries claim the island and the rich fishing waters around it. Boat tours from Grand Manan take visitors to see the puffins.`,`The Swallowtail Lighthouse, built in 1860 on a rocky point near the island's ferry terminal, is one of the most photographed lighthouses in Canada. Visitors reach it by a footbridge and stairs over the cliffs, and from its point they can watch whales, seals and seabirds in the Bay of Fundy.`,`Dulse, a red seaweed eaten dried as a salty snack, is harvested by hand at low tide. Harvesters pick it from the rocks and spread it on the beach or on nets to dry in the sun. Grand Manan dulse is considered among the best in the world, and it is sold across the Maritimes and beyond.`]},"creston-bc":{why:`Creston occupies a broad valley between the Selkirks and Purcells, a partial rain shadow with long summer days and a floor of silt left by the Kootenay River. The river's old flood regime built the soil; dykes and dams now suppress that flood, which is why orchards and grain can occupy former wetland. It is not the Okanagan's heat, and not the West Kootenay's deep-lake gloom. Fertile, sheltered, and still frost-prone, the valley is orchard country by hydrology as much as by sunshine.`,immersive:`Morning on the Creston flats can be a low fog over grain and wetland, the Purcells a wall to the east, the Selkirks to the west. By afternoon the valley is bright and agricultural, cherries dark on the benches, hawks over the reclaimed marsh. Winter is colder than Nelson's lake shore, with inversion nights that pool on the floor. The Wildlife Management Area's boardwalks make the old floodplain visible again: this quiet fruit district is a drained wetland held in place by pumps, dykes, and a river that no longer behaves as it did.`,feel:`Creston feels like a fertile basket set between mountain ranges. In the broad, flat valley of the Kootenay River in southeastern British Columbia, just north of the American border, orchards, farms and one of Canada's most important wetlands spread across the valley floor, ringed by the forested Selkirk and Purcell mountains. The climate is a mild version of the interior: summers hot, sunny and dry enough to ripen cherries and peaches, winters cold and grey with moderate snow, and spring and autumn alive with migrating birds.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and often grey — afternoons near 2°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and moderate snow that lingers on the valley floor, while the surrounding mountains collect far more. Fog and low cloud settle in the valley on still days. Tundra swans and other waterfowl gather on the open water of the wetlands.`,spring:`Blossom and birdsong — afternoons warming from about 9°C in March to 20°C by late May, cherry and apple orchards bursting into bloom, and the wetlands filling with migrating ducks, geese and swans. The Kootenay River rises with the snowmelt, and late frosts are a risk on clear nights.`,summer:`Hot, sunny and dry — afternoons near 28°C and sometimes above 35°C, nights cooling to around 11°C, and fruit stands piled with cherries in July and peaches and apples later. Thunderstorms build over the mountains in the afternoons, and wildfire smoke can fill the valley in August.`,autumn:`Harvest and migration — September afternoons near 22°C, with apples and pears coming off the trees, cooling to about 5°C by November as the rains return and low cloud settles into the valley. Tens of thousands of ducks, geese and swans stop in the wetlands on their way south.`},contrasts:[{label:`Kootenay Pass (≈40 km W, ≈1,170 m higher)`,note:`On the highway over the Selkirks, one of the highest passes in Canada, snow piles metres deep and the road is often closed in winter storms.`},{label:`Nelson (≈80 km NW)`,note:`Deeper in the Selkirk Mountains, Nelson gets more rain and snow and slightly cooler summers.`}],history:[`The Ktunaxa people of the Creston Valley built a canoe unlike any other in North America. Made of a frame covered in pine or birch bark, it had long, pointed ends that sloped down into the water at both bow and stern, a shape known as the sturgeon-nose canoe. Its design was so stable in rough water and so easy to handle in the marshes that it was perfectly suited to the shallow lakes and wetlands of the Kootenay. Strangely, the only other people known to have built canoes of the same shape lived along the Amur River, on the other side of the Pacific in Siberia, and how the two traditions came to resemble each other remains a mystery.`,`The Yaqan Nukiy, the Lower Kootenay Band of the Ktunaxa Nation, lived along the slow, winding Kootenay River where it spreads into wetlands south of Kootenay Lake, fishing, hunting and harvesting roots and berries. In the 1880s an Austrian-English adventurer named William Baillie-Grohman tried to drain the wetlands for farming by diverting part of the Kootenay River into the Columbia through a canal he built at Canal Flats, far to the north. The scheme failed, and the canal was used by only a couple of boats. In the twentieth century, settlers built dykes along the river and drained much of the floodplain, turning it into fields of grain and orchards.`,`Creston grew as a fruit-growing and farming town, shipping cherries, apples and grain across Canada. In 1968 the province created the Creston Valley Wildlife Management Area to protect the remaining wetlands, the first of its kind in British Columbia, and it has become one of the most important stopovers for migrating birds in the interior. The town is also home to the brewery that makes Kokanee beer, named after the landlocked salmon of the region's lakes.`],deepTitle:`Sturgeon-nose canoes`,deep:[`The Creston Valley Wildlife Management Area covers some 7,000 hectares of marshes, lakes and river channels at the south end of Kootenay Lake. More than 300 species of birds have been recorded there, including ospreys and herons, and the wetlands also shelter the endangered northern leopard frog. Boardwalks and canoe routes allow visitors to explore the wetlands.`,`Baillie-Grohman's canal at Canal Flats was completed in 1889 but was never really used, because the government, fearing floods downstream, insisted on locks that made it nearly impassable. Only a couple of steamers ever passed through it, and one had to be dismantled to fit. His grand plans to drain the Kootenay flats came to nothing, but he wrote vivid books about hunting and travelling in the region.`,`The Ktunaxa language is an isolate, unrelated to any other language in the world, and the Ktunaxa people's territory spans the mountains of southeastern British Columbia, Alberta, Montana and Idaho. The Yaqan Nukiy community near Creston has worked to restore wetlands and bring back species such as the white sturgeon, which once thrived in the Kootenay River.`,`Creston's orchards benefit from the valley's sheltered location and hot summers, but they are vulnerable to late spring frosts. The town's Blossom Festival celebrates the flowering of the orchards each spring, and fruit stands along the highway sell cherries, peaches and apples in summer and autumn.`]},"penticton-bc":{why:`Penticton occupies the isthmus between Okanagan and Skaha lakes in a deep Interior rain shadow. Two large lakes store heat and blunt winter; the valley's aridity produces ponderosa, sage, and a summer that can feel Southwestern at 49°N. Osoyoos is hotter and drier still; Kelowna is bigger and less desert-edged. The combination of lake mass and rain-shadow drought is the whole mechanism. Without the lakes this would be a cold desert steppe. Without the rain shadow it would not ripen wine grapes.`,immersive:`On the channel between the lakes, summer is beach towels, irrigation hiss, and a heat that collects on the asphalt before the water knocks it back at night. Hillsides are open ponderosa and bunchgrass, not coastal forest. Winter is short, grey, and lake-moderated, with the occasional cold snap that tests the tender fruit. The town is a service grid on an isthmus, not a hidden village. Smoke in late summer can erase the hills that the climate otherwise puts on display.`,feel:`Penticton feels like a beach town in the middle of the mountains. It sits on a narrow strip of land between two lakes, Okanagan to the north and Skaha to the south, with dry, sage-covered hills rising on either side and vineyards and orchards on the benches. Summers are hot, sunny and long, made for floating the channel between the lakes, while winters are cold, cloudy and quiet. The town's name comes from a Syilx word for a place to stay forever.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and quiet — afternoons near 3°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and long spells of low cloud trapped in the valley by inversions, while the ski hill at Apex Mountain above sits in brilliant sunshine. Light snow comes and goes, and the lakes stay open.`,spring:`Fresh and blossoming — afternoons warming from about 11°C in March to 22°C by late May, orchards and vineyards waking up, and hillsides dotted with yellow balsamroot flowers. Showers are brief, and the beaches begin to fill by late May.`,summer:`Hot, dry and sunny — afternoons near 30°C and often well above 35°C, nights cooling to about 14°C, and very little rain. Thousands of people float down the channel between the lakes on inner tubes, the beaches are packed, and peaches ripen for the August peach festival. Wildfire smoke can fill the valley.`,autumn:`Harvest season — September afternoons near 23°C, warm enough for the beach, as grapes are picked on the Naramata Bench and the fall wine festival fills the valley in early October. By November afternoons cool to about 7°C, and the first valley fog and grey skies of winter settle in.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the beaches on two lakes, floating the channel between them on inner tubes, the wineries of the Naramata Bench, the Ironman-style triathlons and rock climbing at Skaha Bluffs.`,contrasts:[{label:`Apex Mountain (≈20 km W, ≈1,500 m higher)`,note:`Up at the ski resort, winters are snowy and sunny above the valley's inversion clouds, and summers run far cooler.`},{label:`Osoyoos (≈50 km S)`,note:`At the southern end of the valley, in Canada's pocket desert, summers are hotter and drier still.`}],history:[`In March 1955 a team of amateur hockey players from Penticton, the Vees — named after the Okanagan's peach varieties, the Valiant, Vedette and Veteran — travelled to West Germany to represent Canada at the World Ice Hockey Championship. The Soviet Union had shocked Canada by winning the previous year's tournament, and the nation was desperate for revenge. In the final game, the Vees, led by the Warwick brothers, beat the Soviets 5–0. Back home, Canadians who had listened on the radio celebrated in the streets, and the team returned to a heroes' welcome in Penticton. It remains one of the most celebrated wins in the history of Canadian hockey.`,`The Syilx, or Okanagan, people called this place snpintktn, a place where people lived year-round, because fish, game, roots and berries were so plentiful between the two lakes. The Penticton Indian Band still lives on its reserve just southwest of the city. In the 1860s an Irish rancher named Thomas Ellis built a cattle empire that covered much of the southern Okanagan. In 1905 his estate was sold and subdivided into irrigated orchard lots, and in the early twentieth century Penticton became the headquarters of the Kettle Valley Railway, which linked the Okanagan to the coast and the Kootenays.`,`Sternwheelers once carried passengers and fruit up and down Okanagan Lake, and the last of them, the SS Sicamous, built in 1914, is now beached on the shore at Penticton as a museum. After the Second World War, highways replaced the railways and the lake boats, and Penticton became a resort town, famous for its beaches, its peach festival and, from 1983 to 2012, the Ironman Canada triathlon. Today the vineyards of the Naramata Bench, just north of the city, are among the best in Canada.`],deepTitle:`Peaches and Vees`,deep:[`The Penticton Vees' victory in 1955 is still celebrated as one of the great moments in Canadian hockey history. The team was a senior amateur club made up of local players and a few veterans, and they had won the Allan Cup, the national senior championship, to earn the right to represent Canada. The final game was played in Krefeld, West Germany, before a crowd that included many Canadian soldiers stationed in Europe.`,`The SS Sicamous was one of the largest sternwheelers on the inland lakes of British Columbia, with elegant cabins, a dining room and a promenade deck. It carried passengers, mail and fruit until 1936, when roads and railways made it obsolete. The city of Penticton bought it in 1949 for one dollar and has restored it as a heritage museum.`,`Every summer thousands of people float down the Okanagan River Channel, a straightened stretch of the river between Okanagan and Skaha lakes, on inner tubes and air mattresses. The trip takes a couple of hours, and buses carry the floaters back to the start. The channel was built in the 1950s for flood control, replacing the winding natural river.`,`The Naramata Bench, a strip of vineyards and orchards on the eastern shore of Okanagan Lake, has some of the most celebrated wineries in British Columbia. Its hot, dry summers, cool nights and long autumns allow grapes to ripen fully while keeping their acidity, and its lake views draw visitors from around the world.`]},"kamloops-bc":{why:`Kamloops sits at the confluence of the North and South Thompson in a double rain shadow: Coast Mountains first, then the Interior Plateau. Annual precipitation is among the lowest of any Canadian city, often near 300 mm. Sage, bunchgrass, and ponderosa replace coastal forest. Rivers concentrate settlement and a little irrigated green; the hills remain a hot, dry steppe. Summer afternoons commonly reach the low to mid 30s °C. Winters are cold and bright. Fire weather is not a rare exception.`,immersive:`From the Aberdeen hills, Kamloops is a grid in a sage bowl, two rivers meeting in a brown trench, the air smelling of dust and, in July, distant smoke. Heat lifts off the pavement by late morning. Winter can be sharp and blue, the bunchgrass pale, the rivers steaming. This is one of the few Canadian cities where a short hike from downtown enters true steppe. People who love it talk about light and space. People who leave talk about the 2021 heat, the fires, and a summer when the sky did not turn blue for weeks.`,feel:`Kamloops feels like a corner of the American West tucked into British Columbia. It sits where the North and South Thompson rivers meet, in a broad valley of sagebrush, bunchgrass and bare, eroded hills that look more like Nevada than the green forests most people picture in BC. Summers are hot, dry and bright, among the hottest in Canada, while winters are cold but relatively mild, with modest snow and long spells of valley fog.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and dry — afternoons near 2°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and long spells of low cloud and fog trapped in the valley, broken by cold snaps of arctic air. Snow is light compared with the mountains, and skiers head up to Sun Peaks, where the sun shines above the cloud.`,spring:`Fresh and warming — afternoons rising from about 11°C in March to 22°C by late May, the hills turning briefly green and dotted with wildflowers, and the rivers swelling with snowmelt from the mountains. Rain is light, and the dry season is never far away.`,summer:`Hot, dry and blazing — afternoons near 29°C and often above 35°C, nights around 14°C, and very little rain. The rivers and lakes are the escape, with fishing for rainbow trout and swimming in the lakes around the city. Wildfire smoke and record heat waves have become common.`,autumn:`Mild and golden — September afternoons near 23°C cooling to about 5°C by November, cottonwoods turning gold along the rivers, and clear, calm days before the first snow. The salmon return up the Thompson in early autumn.`},contrasts:[{label:`Sun Peaks (≈45 km NE, ≈1,500 m higher)`,note:`Up at the ski resort in the mountains, winters bring deep snow and summer afternoons run much cooler.`},{label:`Vancouver (≈250 km SW, on the coast)`,note:`On the wet side of the Coast Mountains, Vancouver gets more than four times Kamloops's rain, with milder winters and cooler summers.`}],history:[`Late on 8 May 1906, three masked men stopped a Canadian Pacific Railway train at a place called Ducks, east of Kamloops, and robbed the mail car. Their leader was a soft-spoken, silver-haired American named Bill Miner, who had spent much of his life in prison for robbing stagecoaches and trains and was famous for his politeness to his victims. This time, the robbers missed the money and got away with only about fifteen dollars. They were tracked down and arrested by the Royal North-West Mounted Police and the BC Provincial Police a few days later, and Miner was sentenced to life in prison. The next year he escaped from the penitentiary in New Westminster, perhaps with help from people who had come to admire the Gentleman Bandit, and fled to the United States.`,`The meeting of the North and South Thompson rivers has been the heart of Secwépemcúl'ecw, the territory of the Secwépemc, or Shuswap, people, for thousands of years; Tk'emlúps, the name from which Kamloops comes, means the meeting of the waters. Fur traders built a post here in 1812, and it became the Hudson's Bay Company's Fort Kamloops. In 1862 a party of settlers called the Overlanders, who had travelled from Ontario across the prairies and the Rockies, arrived at Kamloops after a gruelling journey; one of them, Catherine Schubert, gave birth to a daughter the day after they arrived. The Canadian Pacific Railway reached Kamloops in 1885, and the town grew as a ranching and railway centre.`,`In 1890 the Kamloops Indian Residential School opened on the Tk'emlúps reserve, and it became one of the largest residential schools in Canada, where thousands of Indigenous children were taken from their families. It operated until 1978. In May 2021 the Tk'emlúps te Secwépemc announced that ground-penetrating radar had detected about 200 potential unmarked burials on the school grounds, a finding that shook Canada and led to a national reckoning with the residential school system. Today Kamloops is a regional centre for the interior of British Columbia.`],deepTitle:`Where rivers meet`,deep:[`Bill Miner had been released from San Quentin in California in 1901 after serving more than twenty years for stagecoach robbery. He moved to British Columbia, where he lived under the name George Edwards and became popular with local ranchers. He is often credited with coining the phrase Hands up! during his robberies. After his escape in 1907, he returned to the United States, where he robbed another train and was caught again. He died in a Georgia prison in 1913. His story inspired the 1982 Canadian film The Grey Fox.`,`The Overlanders of 1862 were a group of about 150 settlers who set out from Ontario to reach the gold fields of the Cariboo. Their journey across the prairies and the Rockies took months, and some drowned rafting down the Fraser and Thompson rivers. Catherine Schubert, who made the journey with three small children while pregnant, became a legend, and her daughter Rose was one of the first children of European descent born in the interior of British Columbia.`,`Kamloops trout, a strain of rainbow trout, are famous among anglers for their fighting spirit, and the region has hundreds of lakes stocked with them. Kamloops calls itself the Tournament Capital of Canada for the many sports events it hosts, and its dry, sunny climate has made it a centre for outdoor recreation, from mountain biking in the hills to skiing at Sun Peaks.`,`The 2021 announcement at the Kamloops Indian Residential School led to searches at other residential school sites across Canada and to a new national day of observance, the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation, held each year on 30 September. The Tk'emlúps te Secwépemc continue to investigate the site and to support survivors and their families.`]},"nelson-bc":{why:`Kootenay Lake is long and deep, a fjord-like trench at the foot of the Selkirks. Its thermal mass takes the edge off winter and caps summer heat in a town that would otherwise behave like a cold Interior mountain valley. Nelson's steep streets face north toward the lake and lose the low winter sun early; the North Shore across the West Arm gets more of it. Kaslo, farther up the lake, is quieter and more fully lake-bound. Castlegar, at the river confluence, is windier and less moderated. The mildness is relative to the Rockies, not to the coast.`,immersive:`Nelson stacks itself up a slope so that Baker Street is a shelf above the lake, brick and tin, with the Selkirks across the water holding snow into June. Winter is real but shorter-tempered than Fernie's: damp cold, lake cloud, then a bright day that feels almost coastal. Summer nights cool off. The town's counterculture reputation is a climate fact as well as a social one: you can live here year-round without the fortress-winter of a high Rockies town, if you accept smoke weeks and a small-city service list.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, bright and lake-cooled — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 12°C, and long sunny days when the West Arm of Kootenay Lake fills with boats and swimmers. Thunderstorms sometimes build over the Selkirks in the afternoon. In bad fire years, smoke from wildfires across the interior settles into the valley for days at a time.`},contrasts:[{label:`Whitewater ski area (≈20 km S, ≈1,100 m higher)`,note:`High in the Selkirks above town, the ski hill gets around 11 m of snow a year, famous for its light, dry powder.`},{label:`Castlegar (≈40 km SW, on the Columbia)`,note:`In the broader Columbia valley, Castlegar's summers run a little hotter and drier, with somewhat less snow in winter.`}],history:[`In the summer of 1986, a Hollywood film crew arrived in Nelson, a small mountain city on Kootenay Lake, to shoot a romantic comedy. Steve Martin starred as a witty fire chief with an enormous nose, in a modern retelling of Cyrano de Bergerac. Roxanne was a hit when it came out the following year, and it made Nelson's steep streets, Victorian brick buildings and lakeside views famous across North America. Nelson's fire hall, where Martin's character worked, still stands. The film helped seal Nelson's reputation as one of the most charming small towns in Canada, a place of heritage buildings, artists, cafés and mountain views.`,`The country around Kootenay Lake is where the territories of the Sinixt, or Arrow Lakes people, the Ktunaxa and the Syilx meet. The Sinixt fished salmon in the rivers until dams on the Columbia blocked the runs. In 1956 the Canadian government declared the Sinixt extinct in Canada, because most of them lived across the border in Washington State; in 2021 the Supreme Court of Canada ruled that the Sinixt still hold Aboriginal rights in their Canadian homeland. In 1886 two prospecting brothers found silver and copper on Toad Mountain above the lake, and a mining camp grew up that was named Nelson, after the province's lieutenant governor.`,`Railways and sternwheelers on the lake made Nelson the commercial centre of the Kootenays, and its prosperous citizens built grand brick and stone buildings on Baker Street. When mining declined, the buildings survived, and in the 1980s the city restored them. Nelson's history includes the Doukhobors, Russian religious dissenters who settled nearby; Japanese Canadians interned in camps in the nearby Slocan Valley in the Second World War; and the American draft resisters and back-to-the-landers who arrived in the 1960s and 1970s and gave the town its creative, independent spirit.`],deepTitle:`Kootenay queen`,deep:[`Nelson has more than 350 heritage buildings, many of them built during the mining boom of the 1890s and early 1900s, and it is often described as the best-preserved heritage town in British Columbia. The city runs its own hydroelectric plant on the Kootenay River, one of the oldest municipal power systems in Canada, and a restored streetcar runs along the lakeshore.`,`The Sinixt people's legal battle centred on Richard Desautel, a member of the Lakes Tribe of the Colville Confederated Tribes in Washington, who shot an elk in British Columbia in 2010 to test whether his people still had hunting rights in their traditional territory. The Supreme Court ruled in his favour, a landmark decision that recognised that Indigenous peoples whose ancestors lived in Canada can hold rights there even if they now live in the United States.`,`During the Second World War, thousands of Japanese Canadians removed from the coast were held in internment camps in the Kootenays, at towns such as New Denver, Slocan City and Kaslo. The Nikkei Internment Memorial Centre in New Denver preserves some of the shacks where families lived. Many stayed in the region after the war.`,`Kootenay Lake, more than 100 km long and over 150 m deep, moderates Nelson's climate, keeping winters milder and summers cooler than other towns in the interior. Its waters hold enormous rainbow trout, called Gerrard trout, which can weigh more than 10 kg, as well as kokanee salmon that turn bright red when they spawn in the autumn.`]},"revelstoke-bc":{why:`Revelstoke occupies the Columbia valley where the Selkirks and Monashees force remaining Pacific moisture out as snow. Valley-level snowfall is already heavy; Mount Mackenzie and the Rogers Pass ranks take several metres more. This is not prairie powder and not a coastal rainforest. It is an interior wet-belt snow climate in a river trench. Summers are green and mild. Winter is the town's occupation. Glacier National Park next door is the same wet-belt machine written at pass scale rather than town scale.`,immersive:`A January street in Revelstoke is a canyon of plowed snow, roofs shedding, the Columbia a dark winter river between white benches. The Selkirks do not look distant. They look like weather about to slide. Summer is a different town: long twilight on the river, meadow flowers up the Meadows in the Sky Parkway, a damp heat that never quite becomes Okanagan. People who come for North America's advertised vertical discover that living here is snow management, avalanche bulletins, and a highway that closes when the pass decides.`,feel:`Revelstoke feels like a town built at the bottom of a snow globe. Deep in the Columbia Mountains, where Pacific storms slam into the Selkirks and Monashees, it lives under some of the heaviest snowfall of any town in Canada, and its surrounding peaks receive even more. Winters are long, snowy and grey, with avalanche danger a constant fact of life, while summers are warm and green, with dense inland rainforests of giant cedars and alpine meadows bursting with wildflowers.`,seasons:{winter:`Buried in snow — afternoons around freezing, nights near −6°C, and storm after storm that drops more than 4 m of snow in town over the winter and far more in the mountains. Roofs groan under the weight, the Trans-Canada Highway through Rogers Pass closes regularly for avalanche control, and the ski resort boasts the longest vertical drop in North America.`,spring:`Slow and slushy — afternoons warming from about 6°C in March to 20°C by late May, the snow melting from the valley floor in April while the mountains stay white well into June. Avalanches thunder down the slopes, and the Columbia River swells with meltwater.`,summer:`Warm, green and lush — afternoons near 26°C, nights around 11°C, and thunderstorms that build over the peaks in the afternoon. The road to the summit of Mount Revelstoke opens to wildflower meadows in late July, mountain bikers and hikers fill the trails, and the cedar rainforests are cool and shady.`,autumn:`Brief and colourful — September afternoons near 20°C cooling quickly, larches and cottonwoods turning gold, and the first snow dusting the peaks by late September. By November the valley is usually white again.`},contrasts:[{label:`Rogers Pass (≈70 km E, ≈880 m higher)`,note:`Up in the Selkirks on the Trans-Canada Highway, a winter can bring around 10 m of snow, among the highest of any road pass in the world.`},{label:`Kamloops (≈210 km SW)`,note:`In the dry interior beyond the mountains, Kamloops gets a fraction of Revelstoke's snow and far more sunshine.`}],history:[`On the night of 4 March 1910, a crew of about sixty railway workers was clearing snow from an earlier avalanche that had buried the Canadian Pacific Railway tracks near the summit of Rogers Pass, east of Revelstoke. Shortly before midnight, a second avalanche came down from the opposite side of the valley and swept over them. Fifty-eight men were killed, many of them Japanese labourers; only one man, a fireman who was thrown clear, survived on the line. It was the deadliest avalanche in Canadian history. The railway gave up on the pass and spent the next six years digging the Connaught Tunnel, more than 8 km long, under Mount Macdonald.`,`The Columbia valley around Revelstoke was used for thousands of years by the Secwépemc, Sinixt and Ktunaxa, who travelled and fished along the river. In 1881 a railway surveyor named Major A.B. Rogers found the pass through the Selkirks that now bears his name, earning a bonus of five thousand dollars; he famously framed the cheque rather than cash it until the railway's manager persuaded him to. The Canadian Pacific Railway built its line through the pass in 1885, and on 7 November 1885 the last spike of Canada's first transcontinental railway was driven at Craigellachie, west of Revelstoke. The town grew up as a railway division point.`,`Revelstoke became famous for ski jumping in the early twentieth century, when Norwegian immigrants built a jump on Mount Revelstoke; in 1925 Nels Nelsen set a world record there. Mount Revelstoke National Park was created in 1914. The Trans-Canada Highway through Rogers Pass opened in 1962, and the Revelstoke Dam on the Columbia was completed in 1984. In 2007 Revelstoke Mountain Resort opened with the longest vertical drop of any ski area in North America, and the town is now a magnet for powder skiers.`],deepTitle:`Avalanche country`,deep:[`Rogers Pass is one of the most avalanche-prone stretches of road in the world. Since 1962, Parks Canada and the Canadian Armed Forces have run an avalanche control programme along the highway, in which soldiers fire artillery shells at the slopes above the road to trigger avalanches under controlled conditions. It is the largest mobile avalanche control programme in the world.`,`Nels Nelsen, a Norwegian immigrant, set a world ski-jumping record on Mount Revelstoke in 1925 with a jump about 73 m long. Revelstoke's jump hosted international competitions for decades, and the town produced many champion jumpers. The ski jump was closed in the 1970s, but its history is honoured in the town's museum.`,`The inland rainforest around Revelstoke is one of the few temperate rainforests in the world located far from the ocean. Western red cedars and hemlocks, some more than five hundred years old, grow in the valleys, nourished by the heavy rain and snow. The Giant Cedars Boardwalk in Mount Revelstoke National Park leads visitors through a grove of these ancient trees.`,`Revelstoke is a centre of avalanche science and forecasting in Canada. Avalanche Canada, the national avalanche warning service, has its headquarters in the town, and researchers, guides and rescue teams train in the surrounding mountains. The town's culture is built around the snow, from heli-skiing operations to backcountry huts.`]},"tofino-ucluelet-corridor":{why:`Ucluelet faces the open North Pacific on the Ucluth Peninsula, with no upwind land to blunt storms. Temperature range over the year is only about 10°C; rain exceeds 3000 mm. Fog and gales are the working sky. Tofino, a short drive north, shares the climate at higher tourism intensity. Barkley Sound's inlets give Ucluelet slightly more harbour shelter, not a different climate class. The hyper-maritime pattern is the reason storm-watching exists as an economy.`,immersive:`The Wild Pacific Trail is cedar root, salt wind, and a horizon that is nothing but swell. In winter the air is loud; in summer it is cool enough that tourists buy fleece in July. Ucluelet's harbour turns its back on the outer coast just enough to hold fishing boats. The rainforest still drips on the town side. Compared with Tofino, the streets are quieter and the storms are the same storms. You come for the outer coast. You stay only if rain on the roof for weeks feels like a condition, not a complaint.`,feel:`Ucluelet feels like the working, wind-blasted end of the wild west coast. Perched on a peninsula facing the open Pacific, with nothing but ocean between it and Japan, it takes the full force of the winter storms that roll in off the sea, sending waves crashing over the rocks along the Wild Pacific Trail. Summers are cool and often foggy, winters mild, wet and stormy, and the dense rainforest grows right down to the water's edge.`,seasons:{winter:`Wet, wild and windy — afternoons near 8°C, nights around 3°C, and storm after storm rolling in off the Pacific with driving rain, gales and huge surf. Over a metre of rain falls between December and February alone. Snow and frost are rare. Visitors come to watch the storms from the cliffs, wrapped in rain gear.`,spring:`Cool and showery — afternoons warming from about 10°C in March to 15°C by late May, rain easing but still frequent, and thousands of grey whales passing close to shore on their migration north from Mexico to Alaska in March and April.`,summer:`Cool, misty and calm — afternoons near 18°C, nights around 11°C, and fog that often rolls in off the cold sea in the mornings. Sunny afternoons bring hikers to the trails and surfers to the beaches. Rain eases off, and salmon fishing boats head out from the harbour.`,autumn:`Increasingly stormy — afternoons near 14°C, with the rains returning in earnest by October and the first big storms of the season arriving. The salmon return to the rivers, and black bears forage along the shores.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for surfing the long beaches of Pacific Rim National Park, winter storm watching from cosy lodges, the Wild Pacific Trail, whale and bear tours and the old-growth rainforest of Clayoquot Sound.`,contrasts:[{label:`Port Alberni (≈80 km E, inland)`,note:`At the head of a long inlet, Port Alberni's summers run about 8°C warmer and much less foggy.`},{label:`Victoria (≈170 km SE)`,note:`In the rain shadow at the southern tip of Vancouver Island, Victoria gets only about a quarter of Ucluelet's rain.`}],history:[`In the stormy darkness of 26 December 1905, the steel sailing ship Pass of Melfort was driven onto the rocks at Amphitrite Point, at the tip of the Ucluelet peninsula, in a violent storm. All of her crew, more than thirty men, were drowned, and wreckage and bodies washed up along the shore. The disaster added to the west coast's grim reputation as the Graveyard of the Pacific, and the next year a lighthouse was built on the point. Today a walking path called the Wild Pacific Trail winds along the cliffs past the lighthouse, and visitors stand where the wreck happened, watching the waves explode against the rocks.`,`Ucluelet, which means people with a safe landing place in the Nuu-chah-nulth language, is the home of the Yuułuʔiłʔatḥ First Nation, who lived on the shores of Ucluelet Inlet and Barkley Sound for thousands of years, hunting whales and seals, fishing for salmon and halibut, and harvesting cedar. European sea-otter traders arrived in the late eighteenth century, followed by fishermen, loggers and missionaries. Japanese Canadian fishermen were a large part of the fleet in the early twentieth century until, in 1942, they were removed from the coast and their boats seized.`,`For most of the twentieth century, Ucluelet was a fishing and logging village, connected to the rest of Vancouver Island only by sea until a road arrived in 1959. The creation of Pacific Rim National Park Reserve in 1970 brought tourists to the nearby beaches, and in recent decades Ucluelet has grown as a destination for hikers, surfers and storm-watchers. In 2011 the Yuułuʔiłʔatḥ and four other nations signed the Maa-nulth Treaty, the first modern treaty on Vancouver Island, giving them self-government over their lands.`],deepTitle:`Storms and whales`,deep:[`The Wild Pacific Trail was the dream of a local man known as Oyster Jim Martin, who began clearing a path along the rugged coastline in 1999. Over the years it has grown into a trail of about 10 km, with lookouts over the ocean, the lighthouse and the rainforest. It is one of the most spectacular coastal walks in Canada, especially during winter storms.`,`Grey whales migrate past Ucluelet every spring on a round trip of some 16,000 km between the lagoons of Baja California, where they give birth, and the feeding grounds of the Bering Sea. Some stay all summer in the bays near Ucluelet and Tofino, feeding on tiny crustaceans in the sand. The Pacific Rim Whale Festival celebrates their passing each March.`,`The Maa-nulth Treaty, which took effect in 2011, gave the five participating nations ownership of their treaty lands, self-government and a share of resources such as fish. For the Yuułuʔiłʔatḥ, it meant the end of more than a century of control by the Indian Act. The nation's village of Hitacu stands across the harbour from the town of Ucluelet.`,`Whaling was once an industry on this coast. In the early twentieth century, a whaling station operated in Barkley Sound, and the ships hunted humpbacks, fin whales and others until the populations collapsed. Today humpback whales have returned in large numbers to the waters off Ucluelet, and whale watching has replaced whaling.`]},"qualicum-bc":{why:`Qualicum Beach lies on Vancouver Island's east coast in the lee of the island's own mountain spine. Tofino, a straight line west, takes three times the rain. Here summers are dry and mild, winters wet but rarely freezing, a Csb pocket that supports Garry oak and a retirement shoreline. The Salish Sea caps heat and cold. It is not Victoria's rain shadow, and not the west coast's rainforest. It is the Island's inside-coast compromise: more sun than Vancouver, less garden privilege than the Saanich Peninsula.`,immersive:`Qualicum Beach and the mid-island east coast lie in the rain shadow of Vancouver Island's mountain spine. Annual precipitation, about 1,100 mm, is little more than a third of Tofino's 3,000 mm across the island. Summers are dry, sunny, and mild — highs near 22°C, lows near 12°C — while winters are wetter but still mild, with the Salish Sea holding both heat and frost. Garry oak savanna, a Mediterranean-affiliated ecosystem rare in Canada, occupies the drier ground. The retirement shoreline follows that comfort, not a Californian drought. Inside-coast rain shadow is leakier than Victoria's Olympic lee: enough sun for a garden with irrigation, not enough to skip a cistern conversation. Walk the same beach in August at two different hours and the marine layer will argue with the brochure.`,feel:`Qualicum Beach feels like a quiet, sunny seaside town on the sheltered side of Vancouver Island. The mountains of the island's spine catch the Pacific rain, leaving the east coast drier and brighter than almost anywhere else in British Columbia; summers are warm, dry and gentle, and winters mild, grey and damp. Wide sandy beaches look out across the Strait of Georgia to the Coast Mountains, and in March the sea turns milky white as herring spawn along the shore.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, grey and wet — afternoons near 7°C, nights just above freezing, and steady rain from Pacific storms, with occasional snowfalls that rarely last more than a few days. The beaches are empty except for dog walkers and the flocks of sea ducks that winter offshore.`,spring:`Fresh and lively — afternoons warming from about 10°C in March to 17°C by late May, and one of nature's great spectacles: the herring spawn in March, when millions of fish turn the water white and draw sea lions, eagles and gulls by the thousand, followed by thousands of migrating brant geese.`,summer:`Warm, dry and sunny — afternoons near 23°C, nights around 13°C, and long rainless weeks that make this one of the sunniest corners of the coast. At low tide the beach stretches far out over warm sand flats, and the water in the shallows warms enough for swimming.`,autumn:`Mellow then wet — September stays warm and dry, with afternoons near 19°C, but by late October the rains return in earnest. Salmon run up the Big and Little Qualicum rivers, drawing anglers and bears.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the long sandy beaches at low tide, the ancient Douglas firs of Cathedral Grove, Milner Gardens, the Horne Lake caves and the drive across Vancouver Island to Tofino and Pacific Rim.`,residentFit:`It suits retirees and gardeners who want some of the mildest winters in Canada, a quiet seaside town and easy access to nature, and who accept grey, rainy winter months and a slower pace of life.`,contrasts:[{label:`Port Alberni (≈45 km W, over the mountains)`,note:`On the other side of the island's spine, Port Alberni gets far more rain in winter and hotter afternoons in summer.`},{label:`Tofino (≈120 km W, on the open Pacific)`,note:`On the outer coast, Tofino gets almost three times Qualicum's rain, with cooler, foggier summers.`}],history:[`In 1856 a Hudson's Bay Company trader named Adam Grant Horne was sent from Nanaimo to find a route across Vancouver Island to the west coast, where the company hoped to open trade with the Nuu-chah-nulth. Guided by Indigenous travellers, he followed the Qualicum River inland to a lake, crossed the mountains and descended to the head of the Alberni Inlet. The lake and the cave system near it still bear his name. On the return journey, according to his own account, his party was involved in a violent clash at the mouth of the Qualicum River, a reminder that the coast was a contested place.`,`The name Qualicum comes from a word in the Pentlatch language meaning where the chum salmon run, and the Pentlatch people gathered camas, dug clams and fished for salmon and herring along this shore for thousands of years. Smallpox, especially the epidemic of 1862, and raids by other nations devastated them, and the Pentlatch language fell silent in the twentieth century. In recent years the Qualicum First Nation has worked to revive it. Settlers arrived slowly, a road reached the area in the 1890s, and the Esquimalt and Nanaimo Railway arrived in 1914.`,`In the early twentieth century, a development company laid out the town and built the Qualicum Beach Hotel, with a golf course, to attract wealthy visitors from Britain and Victoria, and the town gained a genteel, English character. After the Second World War, Qualicum Beach became a favourite place to retire, and today it has the oldest population of any town in Canada, with a median age of over sixty. Its beaches, mild climate and quiet streets attract retirees, artists and families, and its spring herring spawn draws naturalists from around the world.`],deepTitle:`Herring and brant`,deep:[`The Pacific herring spawn along the east coast of Vancouver Island each March is one of the largest wildlife events on the coast. Herring gather in huge schools and spawn in the shallow water, coating seaweed and rocks with eggs, while the males release milt that turns the sea a milky turquoise for kilometres. Sea lions, seals, eagles, gulls and ducks gather to feast, and fishing boats crowd the water.`,`Thousands of brant, small sea geese, stop along the Qualicum shoreline each spring on their migration from Mexico and California to their breeding grounds in the Arctic. They feed on eelgrass and herring eggs, and the Brant Wildlife Festival celebrates their arrival each April. The beaches and estuaries near Qualicum are protected as important habitat.`,`Horne Lake Caves, in the mountains west of Qualicum Beach, are a network of limestone caves carved by an underground river. Visitors can explore them on guided tours, squeezing through narrow passages and climbing past calcite formations. The caves were known to Indigenous people long before Horne's journey.`,`Milner Gardens and Woodland, an estate on a bluff above the sea in Qualicum Beach, features a rhododendron garden beneath ancient Douglas firs. It hosted royal visitors in the twentieth century and is now run by Vancouver Island University as a public garden.`]},"prince-rupert-bc":{why:`Prince Rupert occupies Kaien Island where Pacific storms hit the Coast Mountains at the Skeena mouth. Orographic lift wrings out some of the heaviest rain in North America; fjord walls keep temperatures between a few degrees and the mid-teens most of the year. Sunshine hours are few. Fog and low cloud are the visual climate. Terrace, upriver, is a little more interior. The Haida Gwaii crossing is even more oceanic. This is a harbour climate built for fish and rail, not for gardens that want heat.`,immersive:`Rain on Prince Rupert is not an event. It is the default, beading on yellow cedar, filling gutters, turning the harbour into a grey plane under mountains that disappear by noon. Winter rarely commits to a hard freeze; summer rarely commits to 20°C. The air smells of salt, diesel, and wet forest. When the cloud lifts, the fjord is suddenly enormous. People who stay are usually tied to the port, the fishery, or a decision that sun is negotiable. Everyone else is on the ferry or the plane before the next front.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, dark and drenched — afternoons near 5°C, nights just above freezing, and Pacific storms that bring torrential rain and gales almost continuously; more than 600 mm of rain can fall in the winter months. Snow comes occasionally but rarely lasts. Days are short, barely seven hours of light at the solstice, and the city lights reflect in wet streets.`,summer:`Cool, misty and green — afternoons near 17°C, nights around 11°C, and rain that eases but never quite stops. Sunny days are precious and spectacular, revealing the snowy peaks around the harbour. Cruise ships call, salmon fishing boats crowd the harbour, and humpback whales feed in the channels.`},contrasts:[{label:`Terrace (≈145 km E, up the Skeena)`,note:`Inland behind the Coast Mountains, Terrace gets hotter summers and colder, much snowier winters, with roughly half of Prince Rupert's rain.`},{label:`Ketchikan, Alaska (≈150 km N)`,note:`Just across the border, Ketchikan is even wetter, with nearly four metres of rain a year.`}],history:[`In April 1912 Charles Melville Hays, the president of the Grand Trunk Railway, boarded the Titanic in Southampton to return to North America. He had been in London trying to raise money to complete his great project, a transcontinental railway across northern Canada ending at a new port called Prince Rupert, which he believed would rival Vancouver and become the gateway to Asia. Hays was among those who died when the ship sank on 15 April. Without his leadership and with costs soaring, the Grand Trunk Pacific was completed in 1914 but soon went bankrupt, and Prince Rupert never became the great city he had dreamed of.`,`Kaien Island and the mouth of the Skeena River are the territory of the Ts'msyen, or Tsimshian, people, whose villages and trading networks extended along the coast and up the river for thousands of years. They harvested salmon, eulachon and halibut, and carved towering totem poles and canoes from cedar. In the 1860s a missionary named William Duncan established a model Christian village at Metlakatla, near the future city. In 1905 Hays chose Kaien Island as the terminus of his railway, and the name Prince Rupert, after the first governor of the Hudson's Bay Company, was chosen through a national naming contest.`,`Prince Rupert grew as a fishing and canning town, and during the Second World War it became a busy military port, shipping troops and supplies to Alaska and the Aleutian Islands. In the twenty-first century, its deep, ice-free harbour, the closest major port in North America to Asia, has finally begun to fulfil Hays's dream: a container terminal opened in 2007, and the port now ships grain, coal and containers across the Pacific. It is also known as Canada's rainiest city.`],deepTitle:`Hays's port`,deep:[`The contest to name Prince Rupert in 1906 offered a prize of 250 dollars and drew thousands of entries. The winning name was submitted by Eleanor MacDonald of Winnipeg, who suggested honouring Prince Rupert of the Rhine, a nephew of King Charles I and the first governor of the Hudson's Bay Company. Other people had suggested the same name, but she won the prize.`,`The North Pacific Cannery at Port Edward, just south of Prince Rupert, operated from 1889 to 1981 and is the oldest surviving salmon cannery village on the west coast of North America. Its workers included Indigenous, Japanese, Chinese and European families, each living in separate housing. It is now a national historic site and museum.`,`The Khutzeymateen Grizzly Bear Sanctuary, north of Prince Rupert, was the first area in Canada protected specifically for grizzly bears, established in 1994. It can be reached only by boat or floatplane, and visitors watch the bears from boats as they feed on sedges and salmon along the inlet.`,`Prince Rupert receives well over 2,000 mm of rain in a typical year, and on some days it rains for twenty-four hours without stopping. The city has embraced its weather with the nickname City of Rainbows. Its moss-covered forests and lush gardens thrive in the wet, mild climate.`]},"pincher-creek-ab":{why:`Crowsnest Pass funnels westerlies onto the foothills at Pincher Creek, producing some of Canada's strongest sustained winds and its most frequent chinooks. Winter is a sequence of arctic plunges and sudden thaws, not a steady freeze. Fescue prairie and cottonwood creeks are adapted to that oscillation. Lethbridge, downwind and lower, is still chinook country but less ferocious. Waterton, in the mountains, is wetter and snowier. Wind here is infrastructure, hazard, and, since 1993, electricity.`,immersive:`A chinook arch over the Rockies from Pincher Creek is a hard bright line, the foothills suddenly smelling of dust and chinook, snow vanishing off south slopes while the creek stays iced in the shadows. On other days the same street is a lean-into-it wind that hums in power lines and strips any thought of a calm garden. Summer is dry and moving. Waterton's peaks look close enough to walk to. Ranch trucks and wind turbines share the ridges. The sky is the occupation.`,feel:`Pincher Creek feels like a town where the wind is part of the scenery. It sits in the rolling foothills just east of the Rocky Mountains, where the Crowsnest Pass funnels Pacific air down onto the prairie, and the wind blows hard and often; rows of giant turbines turn on the ridges. Chinook winds can transform a frigid winter day into a mild one in a matter of hours, and summers are warm, sunny and dry, with the mountains always on the western horizon.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, windy and changeable — afternoons around freezing, nights near −10°C, and arctic air that can plunge the thermometer below −30°C, until a chinook arrives and sends it soaring by 20°C or more in a few hours, melting the snow and leaving the fields bare. The wind can be ferocious, gusting well over 100 km/h. A distinctive arch of clear sky over the mountains signals an approaching chinook.`,spring:`Windy and wild — afternoons warming from about 4°C in March to 16°C by late May, but late snowstorms are common, sometimes dumping heavy snow in May. The foothills green up, calving season fills the ranches, and the wind rarely stops.`,summer:`Warm, sunny and pleasant — afternoons near 24°C, nights cooling to about 9°C, and occasional thunderstorms with hail rolling off the mountains. The foothills are green, and visitors head for Waterton Lakes National Park, a short drive to the south.`,autumn:`Crisp and golden — September afternoons near 19°C, aspens turning gold in the foothills, and calm, clear days interrupted by the first snowfalls and strong winds by October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Waterton Lakes National Park, where the prairie meets the Rockies, the wind farms on the ridges, fishing and skiing at Castle Mountain, and the Frank Slide on the road into the Crowsnest Pass.`,contrasts:[{label:`Waterton Lakes (≈45 km SW, in the mountains)`,note:`Where the Rockies rise straight from the prairie, Waterton gets much more precipitation and even fiercer winds.`},{label:`Lethbridge (≈100 km E, on the plains)`,note:`Further out on the prairie, Lethbridge is drier and slightly warmer in summer, but shares the chinooks.`}],history:[`In 1868 a party of prospectors heading north into what is now Alberta camped beside a creek in the foothills and lost a pair of farrier's pincers, the tool used to trim horses' hooves. Ten years later, North-West Mounted Police officers setting up a horse farm nearby found the rusty pincers lying in the creek, and named it Pincher Creek. The town that grew up around the police farm kept the name. Among the colourful characters who passed through the area was John George Kootenai Brown, an Irish-born adventurer who had served in the British Army in India, ridden for the Pony Express, hunted wolves and traded whisky, before settling on the shores of the Waterton Lakes. He campaigned to protect them and in 1910 became the first warden of what became Waterton Lakes National Park.`,`The foothills around Pincher Creek are the traditional territory of the Piikani, part of the Blackfoot Confederacy, who wintered in the sheltered valleys and hunted bison across the plains. After the bison disappeared in the late 1870s, the Piikani signed Treaty 7 in 1877 and settled on a reserve east of the town, where they still live. Large cattle ranches spread across the grasslands, and Pincher Creek became a ranching town, its foothills dotted with cattle and horses. The town incorporated in 1906.`,`Pincher Creek's steady winds, among the strongest in Canada, have given it a new industry. In 1993 the first commercial wind farm in Canada was built at Cowley Ridge, northwest of town, and since then hundreds of turbines have been erected across the region, making it one of the centres of wind power in the country. Today Pincher Creek is a ranching, energy and tourism town, gateway to Waterton Lakes and the Crowsnest Pass.`],deepTitle:`Wind and pincers`,deep:[`Waterton Lakes National Park, created in 1895, joined with Glacier National Park in Montana in 1932 to form the Waterton-Glacier International Peace Park, the first of its kind in the world, celebrating the friendship between Canada and the United States. Kootenai Brown is buried beside the lakes he loved.`,`On 29 April 1903, a huge mass of limestone broke off Turtle Mountain, in the Crowsnest Pass about 50 km west of Pincher Creek, and buried part of the coal-mining town of Frank. About 80 million tonnes of rock swept across the valley in less than two minutes, killing around seventy people. The Frank Slide is one of the deadliest landslides in Canadian history, and the rubble still covers the valley floor.`,`The chinook arch is a band of clear sky over the Rockies, with a layer of clouds above it, that often appears before a chinook. It forms as the warm, dry air descends the eastern slopes of the mountains, and people in southern Alberta watch for it as a sign that warmer weather is coming. The chinooks can raise temperatures so quickly that snow vanishes overnight.`,`The Piikani Nation, whose reserve lies east of Pincher Creek, has invested in wind power, building its own wind farm on reserve land. The nation has also fought to protect the Oldman River, which flows through its territory, opposing a dam built on it in the 1990s.`]},"cypress-hills-sk":{why:`The Cypress Hills are a forested plateau rising hundreds of metres above the surrounding mixed-grass prairie, high enough to be cooler, a little wetter, and ecologically unlike the shortgrass sea around Maple Creek. Lodgepole pine and aspen occupy an island that would not exist without that extra elevation. Winters can still be severe, but the hills block some prairie wind. Summers on the plateau run cooler than the baking plain. It is a sky-island climate in a province famous for not having mountains.`,immersive:`Cypress Hills rises abruptly from the Saskatchewan prairie, reaching 1460 m — higher than Banff. The elevation produces a cool island of lodgepole pine and aspen in a sea of short-grass prairie. Summer afternoons top out around 22°C on the plateau while the plain below bakes at 32°C; the drive from Maple Creek's dry streets into the Centre Block is a biome change without leaving the province. Winters can be cold but are sheltered from the worst prairie winds. Lodgepole at this longitude is a Pleistocene leftover maintained by height and fire. Species drop out a few hundred metres downslope. It is a textbook island refuge: plants and animals present here and nowhere else for hundreds of kilometers, on a ranching plateau that is not a mountain range.`,feel:`The Cypress Hills feel like an island of forest floating on a sea of grass. Rising some 600 m above the surrounding prairie, the hills are the highest ground between the Rocky Mountains and Labrador, and their cool, moist slopes support forests of lodgepole pine, aspen and spruce that look as if they belong in the mountains far to the west. The air is cooler and wetter than on the plains below, winters are long and cold, and the night skies, far from any city, are some of the darkest in Canada.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, cold and snowy — afternoons below freezing, nights near −14°C, and arctic air that can push the thermometer below −35°C. The hills catch more snow than the prairie below, and cross-country skiers glide through the pine forests. Chinook winds occasionally bring a thaw.`,spring:`Late and changeable — afternoons warming from about 0°C in March to 15°C by late May, with snow possible well into May. The grasslands and forests green up, and the hills echo with the calls of birds returning from the south.`,summer:`Cool and green — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 9°C, and thunderstorms that build over the hills in the afternoon. The hills are noticeably cooler and greener than the hot prairie below, and campers and cabin-dwellers come for the lakes, the forests and the stars.`,autumn:`Crisp and golden — September afternoons near 16°C, aspens turning gold among the dark pines, and elk bugling in the meadows. The first snow often falls in October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for a forested plateau rising out of the prairie, the dark-sky preserve and its star parties, Fort Walsh and the history of the North-West Mounted Police, and lodgepole pine and wildlife far from the mountains.`,residentFit:`It suits ranching families and park workers who love open country and big skies, and who are content with a remote, quiet life and cold prairie winters softened by the occasional chinook.`,history:[`On 1 June 1873, a group of American wolf hunters, who poisoned wolves for their pelts, rode into the Cypress Hills looking for horses they believed had been stolen. They came upon a camp of Nakoda, or Assiniboine, people near two whisky trading posts, and after a dispute fuelled by alcohol, the wolfers opened fire. At least twenty Nakoda men, women and children were killed. When news of the Cypress Hills Massacre reached Ottawa, it helped spur the government to create the North-West Mounted Police, and in 1875 the Mounties built Fort Walsh in the hills to keep order. The fort has been reconstructed as a national historic site.`,`The Cypress Hills have been used by people for at least 7,000 years. In the nineteenth century they were a wintering ground for the Nakoda, Cree, Blackfoot and Métis, a wooded refuge with water, firewood and game when the plains were frozen and windswept. The Métis called them the Montagne de Cyprès, mistaking the lodgepole pines for jack pines, which they called cyprès. After the Battle of the Little Bighorn in 1876, the Lakota leader Sitting Bull and thousands of his followers fled north into Canada, and the Mounties at Fort Walsh and nearby posts dealt with them until they returned to the United States in 1881.`,`After the bison disappeared, ranchers moved into the hills, and the town of Maple Creek grew up below them as a cattle town, known as the old cow town. In 1931 Saskatchewan created a provincial park in the hills, and Alberta followed with its own park in 1951. In 1989 the two provinces joined their parks to create Canada's first interprovincial park, and in 2004 the area was designated a dark sky preserve, where visitors can see the Milky Way stretching across the sky.`],deepTitle:`Island in the plains`,deep:[`The highest parts of the hills were never covered by the last ice sheet, which flowed around them, leaving the plateau as an island of ancient land. This is one reason the hills support plants and animals unusual on the prairie, including lodgepole pine, a remarkable variety of orchids, and plants and animals more typical of the mountains far to the west.`,`Fort Walsh was named after James Morrow Walsh, the Mountie superintendent who became famous for his dealings with Sitting Bull. Walsh rode into Sitting Bull's camp with only a few men to explain Canadian law, and the two men developed a mutual respect. The Canadian government, wanting the Lakota to leave, eventually transferred Walsh away, and Sitting Bull surrendered in the United States in 1881.`,`The massacre of 1873 is now remembered as a turning point in the history of the Canadian West. The wolfers were tried in Canada and the United States, but none were convicted. The Nakoda victims are commemorated at the massacre site near Fort Walsh, and the event is recognised as a national historic event.`,`The dark skies of the Cypress Hills are among the best in Canada, thanks to their distance from cities and their high, dry air. The park hosts star parties and astronomy events, and on clear nights visitors can see thousands of stars, the Milky Way and sometimes the northern lights.`]}},s={"leamington-on":{why:`Lake Erie stores summer heat into autumn and delays hard freeze along this 42° north shore, at the same latitude as California's northern border. That thermal lag, plus clay-loam lakebed soils, is why frost-free weeks stretch longer here than anywhere else on the Canadian mainland. The same water that lengthens the season also feeds lake-effect snow squalls and severe-storm tracks off the warm, shallow basin. Greenhouse glass now treats the lake as both a light well and a heat-exchange bath.`,immersive:`Monarchs stack in Point Pelee’s hackberry and cedar before the lake crossing, and the same narrow spit funnels warblers in May. Leamington’s fields sit on the Carolinian fringe: peach orchards, tomato rows, and kilometre after kilometre of greenhouse glass that glow at night. Lake Erie keeps nights milder than inland Essex in January and holds warmth into October, which is why outdoor crops and heated ranges share one shoreline. The honest cost is humidity, lake-effect squalls, and a storm fetch that can turn a summer afternoon into a severe-weather watch. This is Canada’s warmest growing season, not a postcard beach town.`,feel:`Leamington feels like the sunniest, greenest corner of Canada. On the north shore of Lake Erie, at the southern tip of the country, it is surrounded by flat, fertile farmland and vast greenhouses that glow at night, and at its doorstep Point Pelee juts out into the lake, a long spit of marsh, forest and sand that is the southernmost point of mainland Canada. The lake and the latitude give it the warmest growing season in the country: summers are hot and humid, autumns long and mild, and winters damp and grey but milder than almost anywhere east of the Rockies.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild for Canada, but grey and damp — afternoons just above freezing, nights near −5°C, and frequent cloud off Lake Erie, with occasional lake-effect snow when cold winds blow across open water. The glow of thousands of hectares of greenhouses lights up the winter night sky, where tomatoes and cucumbers grow all year.`,spring:`Cool, damp and then bursting — afternoons rising from about 6°C in March to 19°C by late May, the lake keeping spring chilly and frost lingering into April. In May, warblers and other songbirds pour into Point Pelee after crossing Lake Erie, in one of the greatest bird migrations in North America.`,summer:`Hot, humid and long — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 17°C, and the longest, warmest growing season in Canada. Thunderstorms roll in off the lake on muggy afternoons. Field tomatoes ripen by the truckload, and the beaches of Point Pelee fill with swimmers.`,autumn:`Long and mellow — the warm lake keeps September afternoons near 23°C and holds off frost into late October. Monarch butterflies gather by the thousands at the tip of Point Pelee before crossing the lake on their way to Mexico, and the tomato harvest winds down.`},contrasts:[{label:`Point Pelee (≈10 km S, the tip of the mainland)`,note:`Jutting into Lake Erie, the point is even more strongly moderated by the water, with cooler summer afternoons and milder winter nights.`},{label:`Toronto (≈300 km NE)`,note:`Further north and east, Toronto has colder winters, more snow and a growing season about three weeks shorter.`}],history:[`In 2014 the H.J. Heinz Company closed the ketchup factory it had run in Leamington for more than a century, putting hundreds of people out of work in a town that called itself the Tomato Capital of Canada. Two years later, a man from Ontario posted on social media that the French's company was now making its ketchup with Leamington tomatoes, processed in the old Heinz plant, and urged Canadians to switch brands. The post went viral. Canadians cleared French's ketchup off the shelves, stores struggled to keep it in stock, and the little ketchup war became a national story about loyalty to local farmers.`,`Leamington lies on the north shore of Lake Erie, at the southern tip of the Canadian mainland, in the traditional territory of the Caldwell First Nation, Anishinaabe people who lived at Point Pelee and along the lakeshore for generations. Settlers began farming the rich soils of the lake plain in the early nineteenth century, and the town, named after Leamington Spa in England, was incorporated in 1890. Its warm climate, the mildest in Ontario, made it ideal for growing tobacco, fruit and especially tomatoes, and early in the twentieth century Heinz opened its plant in the town.`,`Waves of immigrants shaped Leamington: German-speaking Mennonites, Italians, Portuguese and Lebanese families, and more recently Mexican Mennonites and seasonal workers from Mexico and the Caribbean who now keep the farms and greenhouses running. Since the 1960s Leamington and the neighbouring town of Kingsville have built the largest concentration of commercial greenhouses in North America, growing tomatoes, cucumbers and peppers all year round. Point Pelee National Park, established in 1918 on a sandy spit just south of town, protects a rare Carolinian forest and marshes, and is one of the best places in North America to watch migrating birds and butterflies.`],deepTitle:`Tomato capital`,deep:[`Point Pelee is a long, narrow spit of sand that stretches about 10 km into Lake Erie, ending in a sharp point that is the southernmost tip of mainland Canada. It lies at the same latitude as northern California, and its Carolinian forest of hackberry, sassafras and black walnut is home to species found nowhere else in Canada. In spring, exhausted songbirds crossing the lake land here by the thousands, and birdwatchers gather to see them.`,`Pelee Island, a short ferry ride from Leamington, is the southernmost inhabited place in Canada, and Middle Island, a tiny island nearby, marks the southernmost point of the country. During Prohibition, Pelee Island's location made it a centre for smuggling liquor into the United States. In the 1860s the island was home to Canada's first commercial winery, and vineyards still cover much of it today.`,`Leamington's tomato tourist booth, a giant red tomato on the main street, welcomes visitors to the self-styled Tomato Capital of Canada. The town's processing plant still makes tomato products under new owners, and the region's greenhouses cover more than a thousand hectares, lighting the night sky with a pinkish glow that can be seen from far away.`,`The Caldwell First Nation spent nearly two centuries without a reserve after their land was taken in the early nineteenth century. In 2010 they reached a settlement with the federal government, and in recent years they have acquired land near Leamington and established their own community, ending a long struggle for a homeland.`]},"thunder-bay-on":{why:`Lake Superior is a cold inland sea. At Thunder Bay it delays freeze, delays spring, and throws lake-effect snow when winds are right, so the shoreline is milder than the Shield a few kilometres inland. Continentality still wins the long winter. The Sibley Peninsula and the Kaministiquia mouth add shelter that Port Arthur and Fort William used as harbours. The result is a boreal port climate, not a generic northwestern Ontario average.`,immersive:`Sleeping Giant’s mesa sits across the bay like a breakwater of diabase, and the grain elevators still read as a second skyline. Superior stays cold enough that July afternoons on the waterfront feel marine while the boreal forest behind town is already full of mosquitoes and fireweed. Winter is long; the lake only softens the edge. When northwest flow is right, snow squalls stream off open water onto the shore communities. This is a working harbour at the seam of Great Lakes moderation and Shield continentality, with Fort William First Nation as neighbour, not backdrop.`,feel:`Thunder Bay feels like a frontier city at the top of the Great Lakes. Its harbour faces the vast cold expanse of Lake Superior, guarded by the long, rocky peninsula of the Sleeping Giant, and behind the city the boreal forest stretches north for a thousand kilometres. Winters are long, snowy and bitterly cold, as arctic air pours down from the north, while summers are short, pleasant and cooled by the lake. Grain elevators line the waterfront, and the city's Finnish saunas and pancakes are part of its character.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, cold and snowy — afternoons near −5°C, nights near −15°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −30°C. Snow covers the ground from November into April, and the harbour freezes over. Skiers and snowmobilers head into the hills, and the Sleeping Giant lies white across the bay.`,spring:`Late and chilly — afternoons warming from about 1°C in March to 15°C by late May, with snow possible into May and the cold lake keeping the shore chilly and foggy when the wind blows off the water. The ice breaks up in April and the first ships arrive.`,summer:`Mild and pleasant — afternoons near 23°C, nights around 11°C, and a lake breeze that keeps the city comfortable. Long evenings, cool nights and occasional thunderstorms. Hikers climb the Sleeping Giant for views over the lake, and the blueberries ripen in the bush by August.`,autumn:`Crisp and colourful — September afternoons near 18°C cooling quickly, the boreal forest turning gold with aspens and birches, and the first snow arriving by late October. The last grain ships of the season hurry out before the Seaway closes.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Sleeping Giant rising out of Lake Superior, Kakabeka Falls, Fort William Historical Park and its fur-trade re-enactments, amethyst mines, and Finnish pancakes at the Hoito.`,residentFit:`It suits outdoor families, students and workers in health care, mining and forestry who love the boreal forest and the lake, and who accept long, cold winters and distance from other cities.`,contrasts:[{label:`Duluth, Minnesota (≈300 km SW)`,note:`At the western tip of Lake Superior, Duluth has a similar climate, slightly milder in winter and with more lake-effect snow.`},{label:`Nipigon (≈100 km NE)`,note:`Further from the lake's influence, Nipigon has colder winter nights and warmer summer afternoons.`}],history:[`On 1 September 1980, a 22-year-old named Terry Fox stopped running on the Trans-Canada Highway just east of Thunder Bay. Fox had lost his right leg to cancer three years earlier, and in April he had dipped his artificial leg in the Atlantic at St. John's, Newfoundland, and set out to run across Canada to raise money for cancer research, covering about 42 km — a marathon — every day. After 143 days and some 5,373 km, he felt pain in his chest; the cancer had spread to his lungs. He was forced to end his Marathon of Hope near Thunder Bay, and he died the following June. Terry Fox runs held each year around the world have since raised hundreds of millions of dollars for cancer research, and a monument stands on the highway near where his run ended.`,`The mouth of the Kaministiquia River has been a gathering place for the Anishinaabe for thousands of years. In 1803 the North West Company, the great fur-trading rival of the Hudson's Bay Company, built a huge post here called Fort William, which became the scene of an annual summer rendezvous where canoe brigades from Montreal met the traders from the far northwest to exchange goods and furs, with weeks of feasting and dancing. After the two companies merged in 1821, the rendezvous faded. The Fort William First Nation still holds a reserve on the city's southern edge.`,`In the late nineteenth century, the town of Port Arthur grew up beside Fort William, and the two became rival ports, shipping grain from the prairies down the Great Lakes to the East; their waterfronts filled with enormous grain elevators. Finnish immigrants arrived in large numbers, creating one of the largest Finnish communities outside Finland. In 1970 the two towns merged to form Thunder Bay, a name chosen by the residents in a vote. The port still ships grain, and the city remains the main centre of northwestern Ontario.`],deepTitle:`Giant and grain`,deep:[`The Sleeping Giant, a long rocky peninsula across the bay from the city, looks like a giant lying on its back. According to Anishinaabe legend, it is Nanabijou, a spirit who was turned to stone after the location of a rich silver mine, which he had revealed to his people, was discovered by white men. The peninsula is now a provincial park, and the trail to the top of the giant's chest offers spectacular views from cliffs more than 200 m above the lake.`,`Fort William Historical Park, a reconstruction of the North West Company post on the Kaministiquia River, is one of the largest living history sites in North America. Costumed interpreters re-create the life of the fur traders, voyageurs and Indigenous people at the time of the great rendezvous, and each summer the park re-enacts the gathering.`,`The Hoito, a restaurant in the basement of the Finnish Labour Temple, has served thin Finnish pancakes since 1918, when it was opened as a co-operative to feed Finnish workers. The Finnish community also brought saunas, which are found all over the city. Many Finnish immigrants worked as lumberjacks and miners and were active in labour unions and socialist movements.`,`Kakabeka Falls, west of the city, makes a plunge about 40 m high over a cliff into a gorge on the Kaministiquia River, and is sometimes called the Niagara of the North. Voyageurs had to carry their canoes around the falls on their way west. Anishinaabe legend tells of a chief's daughter who led an attacking war party over the falls to save her people.`]},"gaspe-qc":{why:`Gulf of St. Lawrence water stays cold well into summer, so Gaspé’s tip keeps fog, wind, and cool afternoons that an inland 48° latitude station would not. Appalachian ridges wring extra snow and rain from Atlantic systems. Winter is still long; the sea only blunts the worst continental outbreaks. Forillon’s cliffs and Gaspé Bay’s fetch make wind the daily variable. This is maritime-modified Dfb, not a generic Québec interior winter.`,immersive:`Salt spray and boreal krummholz meet on Forillon’s headlands, where the Appalachians finally drop into the Gulf. Summer fog can sit on Gaspé Bay for days while the Chic-Chocs, a few valleys inland, are already in bright sun. Winter storms arrive as wind-driven snow and ice, not as a quiet boreal hush. Cap-des-Rosiers and the bay villages still smell of tide and diesel. The peninsula’s eastern tip is one of eastern Canada’s sharpest land-sea climate borders: cool, wet, and windy enough that gardens and fishing stages have always shared the same short season.`,feel:`Gaspé feels like the end of the road in eastern Quebec, a place where the Appalachian Mountains finally run into the sea. Sheer cliffs drop into the Gulf of St. Lawrence, fishing villages cling to the coves, and the cold ocean keeps summers cool and often foggy, while winters are long, snowy and stormy. The light is clear and the air smells of salt and spruce; whales feed in the bay, and seabirds wheel around the great rock at Percé.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, cold and snowy — afternoons near −4°C, nights near −12°C, and storms off the Gulf that bring heavy snow and fierce winds. The bay freezes, the villages hunker down, and snowmobiles replace cars on many trails. The mountains inland collect deep snow for skiers.`,spring:`Late and grudging — afternoons warming from about 0°C in March to 12°C by late May, with snow on the ground into April and ice lingering in the Gulf. Fog rolls in off the cold water, and the first whales return to the bay in May.`,summer:`Cool, bright and foggy by turns — afternoons near 20°C, nights around 11°C, and sea fog that can roll in suddenly from the Gulf. Visitors flock to Percé, the gannet colony on Bonaventure Island and the cliffs of Forillon, where whales feed close to shore.`,autumn:`Crisp and colourful — September afternoons near 17°C, the forests turning red and gold against the sea, and the first storms of the season rolling in. Fishing boats bring in the last catch, and the first snow falls in the mountains by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Forillon National Park at the end of the Appalachians, the Percé Rock and the gannet colony of Bonaventure Island, whale watching, seafood and the fishing villages of the Gaspé coast.`,residentFit:`It suits people who love the sea and the quiet, including fishermen, artists and remote workers, and who can live with long, snowy winters, a short tourist season and great distances from Quebec City and Montreal.`,contrasts:[{label:`Chic-Choc Mountains (≈150 km W, ≈1,250 m higher)`,note:`In the Gaspésie's high mountains, snow lingers into June, the summits are alpine tundra, and the last caribou herd south of the St. Lawrence survives.`},{label:`Quebec City (≈600 km W, inland up the river)`,note:`Up the St. Lawrence, summers run several degrees hotter, and winters are colder but less stormy.`}],history:[`On 24 July 1534, the French explorer Jacques Cartier, on his first voyage to North America, landed on the shore of Gaspé Bay and planted a wooden cross about 10 m tall, bearing a shield with the fleur-de-lis and the words Long live the King of France. He was claiming the land for France. Watching was a party of Iroquoian people from Stadacona, near today's Quebec City, who had come to the bay to fish; their leader, Donnacona, made it clear by gestures that the land was his, and that the strangers should not have erected the cross without permission. Cartier persuaded, or forced, two of Donnacona's sons to sail back to France with him. The landing is remembered as the birthplace of New France, and a granite cross stands near the spot in Gaspé today.`,`The Mi'gmaq call this land Gespe'gewa'gi, the last land, and it forms one of the seven districts of their traditional territory; the Mi'gmaq communities of Gespeg, Gesgapegiag and Listuguj still live on the peninsula. Long before Cartier, Basque, Breton and Norman fishermen crossed the Atlantic to catch the cod that swarmed in these waters. After the British conquest of New France in 1760, merchants from the island of Jersey, led by Charles Robin, built a fishing empire on the Gaspé coast, buying salted cod from local fishermen and exporting it to Europe and the Caribbean.`,`For centuries the peninsula's villages lived on the cod fishery, and Gaspé grew into the region's port and commercial centre. In 1970 the federal government created Forillon National Park on the peninsula's tip, expropriating about two hundred families from their homes and villages, a painful episode still remembered in the region. The cod fishery collapsed in the early 1990s, and Gaspé turned to tourism, attracting visitors to its cliffs, whales and the famous pierced rock at Percé. In 2024 the Mi'gmaq of Gespeg and the government signed an agreement to share the management of the park.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Percé Rock, a huge limestone block about 400 m long and nearly 90 m high, rises from the sea off the village of Percé, south of Gaspé. Waves have carved an arch through it, which gives it its name, the pierced rock. At low tide, visitors can walk out to the rock along a sandbar. The rock is slowly crumbling; a second arch collapsed in 1845.`,`Bonaventure Island, a short boat ride from Percé, is home to one of the largest colonies of northern gannets in the world, more than 100,000 birds nesting on its cliffs each summer. Visitors can walk among the nesting birds and watch them dive into the sea from great heights to catch fish.`,`The Robin company, founded by Charles Robin of Jersey in the 1760s, controlled much of the Gaspé fishery for more than a century. It advanced supplies to fishermen on credit and bought their catch at prices it set, leaving many families permanently in debt. The company's buildings at Paspébiac are now a historic site.`,`The Forillon expropriations of 1970 forced families out of villages such as Grande-Grave, Petit-Gaspé and Cap-des-Rosiers, many of whom had lived there for generations. Former residents and their descendants have long campaigned for recognition of the injustice. Today the park's restored buildings at Grande-Grave tell the story of the fishing families who once lived there.`]},"st-johns-nl":{why:`The Avalon sits where the Labrador Current’s cold water meets the North Atlantic storm track, so St. John’s is foggy, windy, wet, and snowy in the same year. Summers stay cool because the sea does. Winters are milder than the Canadian interior at this latitude, but gales and wet snow make the mildness expensive. The Narrows and Signal Hill concentrate wind. This is a hyper-maritime city, not a foggy version of Halifax.`,seasons:{winter:`Raw, stormy and changeable — afternoons just above freezing, nights near −5°C, and a constant parade of storms off the Atlantic bringing snow, freezing rain, rain and gales, sometimes all in one day. Snowfall is heavy, more than two metres over the season, and the city's steep streets become treacherous. In January 2020 a single blizzard dumped more than 75 cm and shut the city down for a week.`,summer:`Cool, foggy and fickle — afternoons near 19°C, nights around 10°C, and fog that rolls in from the cold Labrador Current and can hang over the harbour for days. When the sun breaks through, the colourful houses glow and everyone heads outdoors. Icebergs drift past the coast in early summer, and humpback whales feed off Cape Spear.`},contrasts:[{label:`Cape Spear (≈11 km SE)`,note:`The easternmost point of North America is even foggier and windier than the city, fully exposed to the open Atlantic.`},{label:`Gander (≈330 km NW, inland)`,note:`In central Newfoundland, away from the sea, summers run a few degrees warmer and winters noticeably colder.`}],history:[`On 12 December 1901, in a stone building on Signal Hill above St. John's harbour, the Italian inventor Guglielmo Marconi sat with a telephone receiver pressed to his ear, listening. Outside, his assistants struggled to keep a kite aloft in the gale, trailing a long wire antenna into the sky. Just after noon, Marconi heard three faint clicks, the letter S in Morse code, sent from a transmitter in Cornwall, England, more than 3,000 km across the Atlantic. It was the first wireless signal ever received across an ocean, and it launched the age of radio. Eighteen years later, in June 1919, two British airmen, John Alcock and Arthur Whitten Brown, took off from a field in St. John's and made the first non-stop flight across the Atlantic, landing in an Irish bog.`,`St. John's harbour, a narrow inlet protected by steep cliffs, was one of the first places in North America used by Europeans. Fishermen from England, France, Portugal and Spain came to the Grand Banks for cod from the early 1500s, and in 1583 Sir Humphrey Gilbert claimed Newfoundland for England here. The Beothuk people, who lived on the island's coasts and in its interior, were gradually driven from their lands and died out in the early nineteenth century; the last known Beothuk, a woman named Shanawdithit, died in St. John's in 1829. The city grew slowly as a fishing station, and fires repeatedly destroyed it, the worst in 1892, when most of the town burned.`,`Newfoundland was a separate British dominion until 1949, when, after two referendums, it voted narrowly to join Canada. St. John's became a provincial capital, and its economy remained tied to the cod fishery until 1992, when the government banned cod fishing after the stocks collapsed, throwing some 30,000 Newfoundlanders out of work. Offshore oil, discovered on the Grand Banks, later brought new money. Today St. John's is famous for its brightly painted row houses, its lively pubs on George Street and the welcoming custom of the screech-in, which makes visitors honorary Newfoundlanders.`],deepTitle:`Signal Hill`,deep:[`Cabot Tower, on Signal Hill, was built in 1900 to commemorate the four-hundredth anniversary of John Cabot's voyage of 1497, when he became the first European since the Vikings to reach North America, and Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee. The hill was also the site of the last battle of the Seven Years' War in North America, in 1762, when British troops retook St. John's from the French.`,`Shanawdithit was captured with her mother and sister in 1823 and lived as a servant in a settler household. In the last months of her life, she drew maps and pictures of Beothuk life for a man named William Cormack, who was trying to record what was known about her people. Her drawings are among the few records of Beothuk culture. She died of tuberculosis in 1829, and the Beothuk are remembered as one of the great tragedies of North American history.`,`The cod moratorium of 1992 ended a fishery that had sustained Newfoundland for nearly five hundred years. Overfishing by large trawlers had reduced the northern cod stock to a tiny fraction of its former size. The fishery has been slow to recover, and many outport communities lost their young people to jobs elsewhere in Canada. St. John's, with its university, hospitals and oil industry, fared better than the rural coves.`,`St. John's is one of the windiest, foggiest and snowiest cities in Canada. Its weather is shaped by the meeting of the cold Labrador Current and the warm Gulf Stream offshore, which produces dense fog, and by its position in the path of Atlantic storms. Locals take it in stride, and the city's colourful houses were partly painted, legend says, so that sailors could find their way home in the fog.`]},"whitehorse-yt":{why:`The Coast Mountains wring Pacific moisture before it reaches the Yukon Plateau, so Whitehorse sits in a rain shadow unusual for 60° north. Winters are cold but dry and often sunny; inversions pool in the river valley. Summer daylight stretches toward twenty hours and pushes a short, intense growing window. Continentality remains. The dryness is why aurora and ice fog can share the same winter, and why this capital does not feel like a coastal subarctic town.`,immersive:`Whitehorse sits at 60.7° latitude but in the deep rain shadow of the Coast Mountains, making it one of the driest subarctic climates on the continent — well under 400 mm of precipitation a year. Winters are long and cold, with January lows near −19°C, but also sunny and strikingly dry; inversions still pool ice fog and wood smoke in the Yukon River valley on still mornings. Summer daylight approaches 20 hours; afternoon highs reach the low 20s, and gardens sprint under that light rather than under mildness. The dryness produces clearer winter skies than most of the Canadian north, which is why aurora is generous here. Potatoes and greens stop hard at frost. Dryness is not warmth. It is why the cold feels bright.`,contrasts:[{label:`Dawson City (≈530 km N, in the Klondike)`,note:`Further north and deeper in the interior, Dawson's winters are much colder and its short summers hotter.`},{label:`Skagway, Alaska (≈170 km S, over the White Pass)`,note:`On the other side of the Coast Mountains, at sea level, Skagway's climate is mild and maritime, with far more rain and much warmer winters.`}],history:[`In 1904 a young bank clerk named Robert Service was transferred by the Canadian Bank of Commerce to its branch in Whitehorse. In the long winter evenings, he listened to the tall tales of old prospectors and began to write ballads about the gold rush, a few years earlier, and the harsh northern wilderness. One night, at a party, he heard a story about a man who had cremated his friend's body; inspired, he wrote The Cremation of Sam McGee, which begins with the lines There are strange things done in the midnight sun, by the men who moil for gold. His poems, published in 1907 as Songs of a Sourdough, became international bestsellers, and Service became known as the Bard of the Yukon. His cabin, though, is in Dawson City, where the bank later sent him.`,`Whitehorse sits on the Yukon River in the traditional territories of the Kwanlin Dün First Nation and the Ta'an Kwäch'än Council, Southern Tutchone people who have fished for salmon at Miles Canyon for thousands of years. In 1898 tens of thousands of gold seekers heading for the Klondike floated down the Yukon River in homemade boats, and had to run the dangerous rapids of Miles Canyon and the White Horse Rapids, whose foaming waters were said to look like the manes of white horses. Many boats were wrecked, until the North-West Mounted Police required qualified pilots to steer them through. In 1900 the White Pass and Yukon Route railway arrived from Skagway, and Whitehorse grew as the place where passengers and freight transferred to riverboats bound for Dawson City.`,`During the Second World War, the US Army built the Alaska Highway through Whitehorse in just eight months in 1942, and the town boomed with thousands of soldiers and construction workers. In 1953 the territorial capital moved from Dawson City to Whitehorse, which had become the Yukon's transport hub. A hydroelectric dam built in 1958 tamed the White Horse Rapids. Today about seven out of ten Yukoners live in Whitehorse, a small city of rivers, mountains and northern lights, where the Yukon Quest sled-dog race and the Sourdough Rendezvous festival celebrate the winter.`],deepTitle:`Bard of the Yukon`,deep:[`The SS Klondike, a sternwheeler built in 1937, carried freight and passengers between Whitehorse and Dawson City until the 1950s, when the new highway made riverboats obsolete. It is now beached on the riverbank in Whitehorse as a national historic site, one of the last survivors of the hundreds of sternwheelers that once plied the Yukon River.`,`The Alaska Highway, about 2,200 km long, was built by some ten thousand American soldiers, including several regiments of Black soldiers who faced discrimination but performed some of the hardest work in the swamps and permafrost. The road linked Alaska to the rest of the United States through Canada, at a time when Japan had invaded the Aleutian Islands and threatened the coast.`,`The Kwanlin Dün and Ta'an Kwäch'än signed modern land-claim and self-government agreements in the 2000s, giving them control over their lands and their own governments. The Kwanlin Dün Cultural Centre on the Whitehorse waterfront celebrates their history and culture, and has helped revive traditional practices such as canoe-making.`,`Whitehorse lies in the rain shadow of the Coast Mountains and is one of the driest cities in Canada. Its winters are cold but sunny, with dry snow, and its summers have nearly twenty hours of daylight in June. The aurora borealis appears frequently from late August to April, drawing visitors from around the world.`]},"yellowknife-nt":{why:`Great Slave Lake is a thermal flywheel only while it is open. Once ice locks the north arm, Yellowknife is a dry continental subarctic station at 62° north, with January darkness and severe cold. Short, warm summers and a huge annual range follow. Shield bedrock and lake fetch shape wind and ice fog. Aurora frequency is a sky consequence of that dry, dark winter, not a separate climate. Inland muskeg without the lake is even harsher in autumn; the locked lake is why January feels fully continental. Open-water autumn and locked-ice winter are two different towns on the same rocks.`,immersive:`Old Town’s rock and ice roads sit on Shield knobs that drop straight into Great Slave Lake, and in January the lake is a white plain rather than a moderating sea. Woodsmoke and ice fog hang on still mornings; the sun sketches a low arc and leaves. Summer reverses the script: long light, warm afternoons, and mosquitoes in every muskeg pocket. Dettah’s lights across the ice are a reminder that this is Dene water first. The aurora is statistically generous here because the air is dry and the nights are long, not because the cold is decorative.`,feel:`Yellowknife feels like a frontier city on the edge of the Arctic. The capital of the Northwest Territories stands on the rocky shore of Great Slave Lake, among stunted spruce and ancient granite, where gold miners, Dene families, bush pilots and government workers live in a mix of glass offices, houseboats and the shacks of Old Town. The climate is extreme: winters are long, dark and brutally cold, with some of the best northern lights on earth, and summers short but bright, with the sun barely setting in June.`,seasons:{winter:`Brutally cold and dark — afternoons near −18°C, nights near −27°C, and cold snaps that drop the thermometer below −40°C. The days shrink to about five hours of light around the solstice, but the long nights are lit by the aurora borealis, which dances overhead on most clear nights. Great Slave Lake freezes thick enough to carry ice roads, and houseboats on the bay are locked in the ice.`,spring:`Late but bright — afternoons rising from about −10°C in March to 15°C by late May, with sunshine returning in force and snow lingering into May. March and April bring long, sunny days of snowmobiling and ice fishing, while the ice on the lake doesn't break up until June.`,summer:`Short, warm and endless — afternoons near 20°C, nights around 10°C, and nearly twenty hours of daylight around the summer solstice, when the sun barely sets. Boaters and paddlers take to the lake, and the town celebrates with festivals. Wildfire smoke is a growing threat.`,autumn:`Brief and colourful — September afternoons near 10°C, the tundra and birches turning gold and red, and the first snow by early October. The aurora returns as the nights lengthen, and the lake begins to freeze by late November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for some of the best aurora viewing on earth from late August to April, dog sledding, the ice road across Great Slave Lake in winter, the houseboats and fish fry of Old Town and the midnight sun in June.`,contrasts:[{label:`Fort Smith (≈300 km S, on the Alberta border)`,note:`Further south, Fort Smith's summers are warmer and its winters a little milder, though still bitterly cold.`},{label:`Inuvik (≈1,100 km NW, above the Arctic Circle)`,note:`In the far north, Inuvik has colder winters with weeks of polar night and a true midnight sun in summer.`}],history:[`On 24 January 1978, a Soviet satellite called Kosmos 954 fell out of orbit and broke up over northern Canada, scattering radioactive debris across a long swathe of the Northwest Territories, from Great Slave Lake east into the Barrens. The satellite had been powered by a small nuclear reactor. Canadian and American teams launched Operation Morning Light, searching the frozen wilderness by plane and on foot in temperatures far below zero, and recovered dozens of radioactive fragments, some of them dangerously hot. Canada billed the Soviet Union for the cleanup, which eventually paid about half. Yellowknife, as the base for the search, briefly found itself at the centre of a Cold War drama.`,`The Dene shore of Great Slave Lake was home to the T'atsaot'ine, known to traders as the Yellowknives for the copper knives they made, and to the Tłı̨chǫ, or Dogrib, people. A Yellowknives leader named Akaitcho guided the British explorer John Franklin on his first Arctic expedition in 1820. Gold was found on the shore of Yellowknife Bay in the 1930s, and prospectors and mining companies rushed in; the Con Mine opened in 1938 and the Giant Mine in 1948, and the town grew around them. In 1967 Yellowknife became the capital of the Northwest Territories.`,`The gold mines closed in the early 2000s, but by then diamonds had been discovered in the Barrens north of Yellowknife, and the city became the supply base for some of the world's richest diamond mines, reached in winter by ice roads hundreds of kilometres long. In August 2023, as wildfires approached, the entire city of about 20,000 people was evacuated, by road and air, in one of the largest evacuations in northern Canadian history; the fires stopped short, and residents returned weeks later. Today Yellowknife is a government and mining city, and a destination for visitors from around the world who come to see the northern lights.`],deepTitle:`Gold and diamonds`,deep:[`The Giant Mine left one of the worst environmental legacies in Canada. Roasting the gold ore released arsenic, and about 237,000 tonnes of toxic arsenic trioxide dust was stored in underground chambers. To keep it from leaking into the groundwater and Great Slave Lake, the government is freezing the ground around the chambers, a solution that will need to be maintained indefinitely. The cleanup is expected to cost billions of dollars.`,`Diamonds were discovered north of Yellowknife in 1991 by two prospectors, Chuck Fipke and Stewart Blusson, after years of searching for the tiny indicator minerals that trace diamond-bearing rock. The Ekati mine opened in 1998, followed by others, making Canada one of the world's leading diamond producers. The mines are supplied by winter ice roads, built across frozen lakes, which featured in the television series Ice Road Truckers.`,`Yellowknife sits under the auroral oval, a ring around the North Pole where the northern lights are most frequent, and visitors can see them on about 240 nights a year. Thousands of tourists, many from Japan and China, come each winter to watch the lights from heated cabins and teepees outside the city.`,`The houseboats of Yellowknife Bay are home to a small, independent community that lives on the water in summer and on the ice in winter, reaching shore by boat, snowmobile or on foot. The Old Town, on a rocky peninsula, preserves the cabins and shacks of the gold-rush era, and the bush planes that serve the North take off from the bay.`]},"churchill-mb":{why:`Hudson Bay is a seasonal ocean. In winter it freezes and Churchill becomes brutally continental; from late summer into autumn open water feeds moisture, fog, and heavy snow at a latitude that would otherwise be dry. Polar bears wait on the coast for freeze-up because that is when the ice platform returns. Inland boreal Manitoba is drier and lacks the autumn marine pulse. The town sits at a tundra–boreal edge where marine and continental air take turns. There is no road. The freeze calendar, not a single mean, is the climate residents actually use.`,immersive:`Grain elevators and a short main street sit between boreal outposts and the open bay, and in October the coast belongs to bears walking the tidal flats. Summer is brief, cool, and loud with insects; belugas use the Churchill River estuary when the water is open. Winter erases the maritime feeling: the bay is ice, the wind is dry and severe, and darkness is long. There is no road. The train and the plane are the town’s climate as much as the thermometer. This is a meeting shore — Cree, Dene, Inuit, Métis, and a port — not a wilderness set.`,feel:`Churchill feels like a town at the edge of the world, where the boreal forest gives way to the tundra and the land meets the vast, frozen expanse of Hudson Bay. There are no roads in; you arrive by train or plane. Winters are fiercely cold and dark, with blizzards sweeping off the frozen bay, and the polar bears that gather each autumn to wait for the ice wander right through town. Summers are short and cool, with belugas filling the river and wildflowers carpeting the tundra.`,seasons:{winter:`Arctic in all but name — afternoons near −19°C, nights near −27°C, and blizzards that whip off the frozen bay with windchills below −45°C. Days are short, about six hours of light at the solstice, and the northern lights blaze across the long nights, among the best displays in the world.`,spring:`Arctic until late — afternoons rising from about −13°C in March to 5°C by late May, blizzards still possible into May, and the bay frozen solid. The days lengthen fast, mother polar bears lead their cubs out of their dens toward the sea ice, and the river ice finally breaks up in late May or June.`,summer:`Short, cool and alive — afternoons near 17°C, nights around 7°C, and nearly eighteen hours of daylight. Thousands of beluga whales gather in the mouth of the Churchill River to feed and calve, the tundra blooms with wildflowers, and birdwatchers come for the nesting shorebirds. Mosquitoes and black flies are fierce. Cold winds off the ice can drop the temperature sharply.`,autumn:`Polar bear season — afternoons falling from about 10°C in September to below freezing by late October, and hundreds of polar bears gathering along the coast near town to wait for the bay to freeze so they can hunt seals on the ice. Tourists ride tundra buggies to watch them. The first snow arrives in October, and the bay freezes by mid-November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come to see polar bears gathering along the coast in October and November, thousands of beluga whales in the Churchill River in July and August, the northern lights in winter, and the history of the fur trade at Prince of Wales Fort.`,contrasts:[{label:`Thompson (≈400 km SW, in the boreal forest)`,note:`Inland in the forest, Thompson's summers are warmer and its winters similarly cold, but without the bay's fierce winds.`},{label:`Arviat, Nunavut (≈250 km N, up the bay)`,note:`Further north on the treeless tundra, Arviat's winters are even colder and longer, with less snow-sheltering forest.`}],history:[`In the autumn of 1619, a Danish expedition of two ships and 64 men under Captain Jens Munk, searching for a route to Asia, sailed into the mouth of the Churchill River and prepared to spend the winter. It was a catastrophe. Scurvy and other diseases, made worse by the bitter cold and poor food, killed the crew one by one. By the spring of 1620 only Munk and two others were still alive. Somehow, the three survivors managed to sail the smaller of the two ships back across the Atlantic to Norway, arriving in September. Munk wrote an account of the voyage, one of the most harrowing in the history of Arctic exploration, and archaeologists have found remains of the expedition near Churchill.`,`People have lived along this coast for some four thousand years, hunting caribou, seals and whales and fishing the rivers. Cree, Dene, Inuit and Métis families have all met here, where the Churchill River enters Hudson Bay. In 1717 the Hudson's Bay Company built a trading post at the river mouth, and in 1731 it began building Prince of Wales Fort, a massive stone fortress, to guard the fur trade. When a French fleet under the Comte de Lapérouse arrived in 1782, the governor, the explorer Samuel Hearne, with only a few dozen men, surrendered without firing a shot. The French spiked the cannons and partly destroyed the walls, which still stand.`,`In 1929 the Hudson Bay Railway reached Churchill, and a port was built to ship prairie grain to Europe. During and after the Second World War, the United States and Canada built a military base and a rocket research range near the town. In 1956 the government forcibly moved the Sayisi Dene from their homeland at Little Duck Lake to Churchill, where they suffered terrible poverty; Manitoba and the federal government later formally apologised. Today Churchill, a town of fewer than a thousand people, is famous as the polar bear capital of the world, drawing visitors from around the globe each autumn.`],deepTitle:`Polar bear capital`,deep:[`Samuel Hearne was one of the great explorers of the Canadian North. In 1770–72, guided by the Dene leader Matonabbee, he walked from Churchill to the mouth of the Coppermine River on the Arctic Ocean and back, a journey of thousands of kilometres, becoming the first European to reach the Arctic coast of North America overland. His surrender of Prince of Wales Fort was criticised in Britain, but with so few men he had little choice.`,`Polar bears come ashore along the western coast of Hudson Bay each summer when the ice melts, and spend the following months fasting on land. In autumn they gather near Churchill, where the ice forms earliest, waiting to return to the bay. Bears that wander into town are captured and held in a polar bear holding facility, nicknamed the polar bear jail, until the ice forms and they can be released.`,`Each summer about three thousand beluga whales gather in the mouth of the Churchill River, attracted by the warmer water and the fish. Visitors watch them from boats or kayaks, and the whales often come close, their white backs gleaming in the water. Churchill is one of the best places in the world to see belugas.`,`The Hudson Bay Railway, Churchill's only land link to the south, was cut by severe flooding in 2017, leaving the town without a rail connection for more than a year. Food and fuel had to be flown in at great cost. The railway was repaired and reopened in 2018, and the port and railway are now owned by a group of First Nations and northern communities.`]},"prince-edward-co-on":{why:`A near-island in Lake Ontario, cut off in practice by the Murray Canal, sits in lake air on a limestone plateau that warms and drains fast in spring. Cool nights and thin soils favour Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Riesling more than inland heat. Lake-effect snow and freeze–thaw still define winter. Inland Quinte is hotter in summer and colder on still spring nights; the County’s benches get Ontario’s thermal mass and a frost delay that is real and incomplete. Peninsula geometry, not a marketing wine trail, is the climate mechanism. Wind machines in May are the honest proof.`,immersive:`Sandbanks’ dunes and the County’s limestone backroads share one lake. On still May mornings the water is a pale sheet and the vineyards on shallow soils are already fighting frost with wind machines. Picton’s harbour and Wellington’s shore sit only a few metres above Ontario, so humidity and lake breeze are daily, not occasional. Winter is still Ontario-cold, just delayed and snowier when the lake is open. This is a working limestone peninsula with beaches, barns, and a young cool-climate wine industry, not a mild-coast transplant.`,feel:`Prince Edward County feels like an island of farms, vineyards and beaches floating in Lake Ontario. Almost entirely surrounded by water, this rolling peninsula of limestone fields, old stone farmhouses, small villages and wineries has become one of Ontario's favourite escapes, with the great sand dunes of Sandbanks at its western edge. The lake moderates the climate: summers are warm and breezy, autumns long, and winters cold and snowy but a little milder than inland.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, grey and lake-tempered — afternoons around freezing, nights near −8°C, and snow from storms and lake-effect bands when cold winds cross Lake Ontario. The lake keeps temperatures a little milder than inland Ontario, but the wind across the open fields bites. The wineries and inns are quiet, and the vines are buried in soil to protect them from the cold.`,spring:`Slow and lake-chilled — afternoons rising from about 5°C in March to 18°C by late May, with the lake keeping the shore cool and frost lingering into May. The vineyards bud late, the apple orchards bloom in May, and migrating birds rest on the peninsula on their way north.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 15°C, and lake breezes that keep the peninsula comfortable. The beaches and great sand dunes of Sandbanks fill with swimmers, the wineries and farm stands are busy, and cyclists pedal the quiet country roads.`,autumn:`Long and mellow — the lake keeps September afternoons near 22°C and holds off frost into October, while the grape harvest fills the wineries and the maples blaze. By November afternoons cool to about 8°C, and the winds off the lake turn raw.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the beaches and great sand dunes of Sandbanks, wineries and farm stands along the county roads, cycling the Millennium Trail and the restaurants and galleries of Picton and Wellington.`,residentFit:`It suits winemakers, farmers, chefs, artists and retirees who want a rural, lake-moderated way of life within reach of Toronto, and who accept rising prices and quiet, windy winters.`,contrasts:[{label:`Kingston (≈60 km E)`,note:`At the eastern end of Lake Ontario, Kingston has a similar climate, slightly colder in winter and windier off the lake.`},{label:`Toronto (≈200 km W)`,note:`The big city is a little warmer year-round, thanks to its size and its more southerly position on the lake.`}],history:[`In the 1860s and 1870s, the farmers of Prince Edward County discovered that their limestone soils and lake-moderated climate grew superb barley, exactly the kind American brewers wanted for beer. Schooners carried the barley across Lake Ontario to Oswego and other New York ports, and the County grew rich; farmers built the grand brick and stone farmhouses that still dot its roads, and the era became known as the Barley Days. Then, in 1890, the United States passed the McKinley Tariff, which placed a heavy duty on imported barley, and the trade collapsed almost overnight. The County turned to canning tomatoes, corn and peas, and by the early twentieth century it had dozens of canning factories and called itself the Garden County of Canada.`,`The County, as it is known, is a peninsula almost surrounded by Lake Ontario, and its shores were the territory of the Mississauga people, part of the Anishinaabe, with the Haudenosaunee also using the area. After the American Revolution, Loyalists who had fought for Britain arrived in 1784 and settled the townships, clearing farms and building towns such as Picton and Wellington. The Loyalist heritage is still visible in the County's old houses, churches and cemeteries, and in the names of its roads and villages.`,`In the twentieth century, the canning industry declined, and the County's young people moved away. Then, around 2000, winemakers discovered that the limestone soils and cool climate were ideal for growing Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, and dozens of wineries opened; in 2007 Prince Edward County became an official wine region. Artists, chefs and city dwellers followed, and the County is now one of the most popular getaways from Toronto, famous for its wineries, its farm-to-table restaurants and the sand dunes of Sandbanks Provincial Park.`],deepTitle:`Barley and dunes`,deep:[`Lake on the Mountain, near the village of Glenora, is a small lake perched about 60 m above the Bay of Quinte, with no obvious source of water. For centuries people wondered how it was fed; the Mohawk believed it was the home of spirits, and settlers thought it might be connected to the Great Lakes by underground channels. Geologists now believe it is fed by underground springs. The view from the lake over the bay below is one of the most striking in Ontario.`,`The dunes of Sandbanks Provincial Park are the largest freshwater baymouth bar and dune system in the world, rising more than 25 m above the lake. They were formed by winds blowing sand from the lake bottom onto the shore, and they continue to shift. The park's long, sandy beaches are among the most popular in Ontario.`,`Grapevines in Prince Edward County must be protected from the winter cold, which can kill them. Growers bury the vines under soil in the autumn, a technique called hilling, and dig them out again in the spring. The extra work, along with the cool climate and limestone soil, gives the County's wines their distinctive character.`,`The County's canning industry once employed thousands of people, and its factories processed tomatoes, peas and corn for markets across Canada. The last canning factories closed in the 1990s, but the County's farm heritage lives on in its farmers' markets, its local produce and its restaurants that celebrate the land.`]},"iqaluit-nu":{why:`Frobisher Bay freezes from late autumn into early summer, so Iqaluit loses most maritime moderation in the cold half-year and sits in a true Arctic regime on southern Baffin Island. The Labrador Current keeps nearby water cold even when open. Polar day and polar night set the solar calendar. Tundra, permafrost, and katabatic flow off the highlands do the rest. This is ET climate with a capital’s logistics, not a slightly colder Yellowknife.`,feel:`Iqaluit feels like a frontier capital on the edge of the Arctic. It sits on rocky tundra at the head of Frobisher Bay on Baffin Island, surrounded by low hills and bare rock, with no roads connecting it to the rest of Canada. Winters are long, dark and bitterly cold, with blizzards sweeping across the frozen bay, while summers are short, cool and bright, with the sun barely setting. Inuktitut is spoken on the streets, and snowmobiles outnumber cars in winter.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, dark and brutal — afternoons near −17°C, nights near −25°C, and blizzards that can shut down the city for days, with windchills below −50°C. Days shrink to about four hours of light at the solstice. The bay freezes solid, and hunters travel across the sea ice by snowmobile and dog team.`,spring:`Bright but frozen — afternoons climbing from about −16°C in March to 0°C by late May, with the sun returning in force and long, brilliant days on the snow. It is the season for travelling on the ice, ice fishing and the Toonik Tyme festival in April, which celebrates Inuit traditions.`,summer:`Short, cool and luminous — afternoons near 10°C, nights just above freezing, and nearly twenty-one hours of daylight around the solstice. The tundra blooms with tiny wildflowers, the sea ice breaks up, and the annual sealift ships arrive with a year's supply of goods. Fog and drizzle are common.`,autumn:`Brief and blustery — September afternoons near 6°C, the tundra turning red and gold, and the first snow arriving by late September. Storms sweep in off the bay, and by November the sea is freezing again.`},residentFit:`It suits Inuit families, government workers, teachers, nurses and researchers who are drawn to life in the Arctic and to Inuit culture, and who can manage long, dark winters, high prices and flights as the only link to the south.`,contrasts:[{label:`Pangnirtung (≈300 km N, on Cumberland Sound)`,note:`Nestled among towering fjord walls near the Arctic Circle, Pangnirtung is windier and gets more dramatic mountain weather.`},{label:`Kuujjuaq, Quebec (≈650 km S)`,note:`Across Hudson Strait in northern Quebec, Kuujjuaq's summers are a little warmer, with scattered trees at the edge of the forest.`}],history:[`In 1576 the English explorer Martin Frobisher sailed into the long bay that now bears his name, hoping it was the start of a Northwest Passage to China. He found no passage, but one of his men picked up a black rock that an assayer in London declared contained gold. Excited investors, including Queen Elizabeth I, funded two more voyages in 1577 and 1578, and Frobisher's ships carried back more than a thousand tonnes of the black rock. It turned out to be worthless; the ore was eventually used to repair roads and walls in England. Frobisher also kidnapped several Inuit and took them to England, where they soon died. Inuit oral history still remembers the strangers who came to Frobisher Bay.`,`The place called Iqaluit, place of many fish, was a fishing camp at the mouth of the Sylvia Grinnell River for centuries, used by the Inuit and their Thule ancestors. In 1942 the United States built an air base nearby as a stopover for planes flying to Europe, and an Inuk guide named Nakasuk helped choose the site. The base, and later a radar station of the Distant Early Warning Line, drew Inuit families into a settlement named Frobisher Bay. On 1 January 1987 the town reclaimed its Inuktitut name, Iqaluit.`,`On 1 April 1999 Canada created the new territory of Nunavut, our land in Inuktitut, carved out of the Northwest Territories after decades of Inuit negotiation, and Iqaluit became its capital. It is the only Canadian capital not connected to the rest of the country by road, and nearly everything it needs arrives by air or on the annual summer sealift. In 2021 fuel contamination in the city's water supply forced residents to drink bottled and river water for weeks. Today Iqaluit is a young, growing city where most residents are Inuit and where Inuktitut is an official language of government.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Frobisher's three voyages were among the earliest English attempts to explore the Arctic. On the third voyage, a fleet of fifteen ships carried miners to Baffin Island to dig ore, and they even planned a colony there. The ruins of their mines and a small stone house they built can still be seen on Kodlunarn Island in Frobisher Bay, which the Inuit call Qallunaat Island, the island of the white people.`,`St. Jude's Anglican Cathedral in Iqaluit was built in the shape of an igloo, with a white dome. It was destroyed by an arson fire in 2005 and rebuilt in the same shape, reopening in 2012. Its interior features Inuit art, including an altar shaped like a sled and a cross made from narwhal tusks.`,`The creation of Nunavut was the result of the largest Indigenous land-claim agreement in Canadian history, signed in 1993. It gave the Inuit ownership of about 350,000 square kilometres of land and a share of resource revenues, and it created a territory whose government reflects Inuit values and language. Nunavut covers about a fifth of Canada's land area but has fewer than 40,000 people.`,`The annual sealift, when ships arrive in summer after the ice breaks up, is a major event in Iqaluit. The ships bring everything that is too heavy or bulky to fly in, from cars and building materials to a year's supply of canned food. Because the bay has huge tides, rising and falling more than 11 m, ships unload onto barges that are beached at high tide.`]},"inuvik-nt":{why:`North of the Arctic Circle on the Mackenzie Delta, Inuvik has polar day and polar night as calendar facts. Continentality dominates once the delta freezes; innumerable lakes only slightly ease summer. The range between warmest and coldest extremes is among the largest on the planet. Permafrost at shallow depth forces utilidors and piles. Yellowknife is south of the Circle and on a Shield lake, not a delta of silt and ice. This is a planned subarctic town in a taiga–tundra mosaic, not a slightly colder Yellowknife. Fifty-six days of sun above the horizon is a work calendar, not a novelty.`,feel:`Inuvik feels like a town built for the extremes of the Arctic. Perched on the edge of the vast Mackenzie Delta, a maze of channels and lakes, it lies about 200 km north of the Arctic Circle, where the sun does not set for nearly two months in summer and does not rise for about a month in winter. Its houses stand on stilts above the permafrost, linked by insulated pipes called utilidors, and its round church is shaped like an igloo. Winters are long and fiercely cold, summers short, bright and surprisingly warm.`,seasons:{winter:`Dark and bitterly cold — afternoons near −20°C, nights near −28°C, and cold snaps that drop the thermometer below −45°C. For about a month around the winter solstice, the sun does not rise at all, and the only light is a twilight glow at midday and the aurora at night. Ice roads are built across the frozen delta, connecting the communities.`,spring:`Bright and frozen — afternoons climbing from about −15°C in March to 5°C by late May, the sun returning with astonishing speed until it never sets. The ice road season ends, and the town celebrates the return of the sun with festivals and snowmobile races. The river ice breaks up with a roar in late May or June.`,summer:`Short, warm and endless — afternoons near 17°C and sometimes reaching 30°C, nights that never get dark, and fifty-six days of continuous sunlight from late May to July. The tundra and willows burst into green, mosquitoes swarm, and the community greenhouse grows vegetables in the long light.`,autumn:`Fast and fleeting — September afternoons near 8°C, the tundra turning red and gold, and the first snow by late September. The days shorten rapidly, the delta channels freeze, and by early December the sun sets for the last time until January.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the drive up the Dempster Highway, the midnight sun, the Igloo Church, the Gwich'in and Inuvialuit cultural festivals, the Mackenzie Delta, and now the road on to Tuktoyaktuk and the Arctic Ocean.`,residentFit:`It suits Gwich'in and Inuvialuit families, researchers and public servants at home in the Far North, who can handle a month without sunrise in winter, extreme cold and long distances to the south.`,contrasts:[{label:`Tuktoyaktuk (≈140 km N, on the Arctic Ocean)`,note:`On the treeless coast, Tuktoyaktuk's summers are much cooler and foggier, and winter winds off the frozen ocean bite even harder.`},{label:`Dawson City, Yukon (≈760 km S by the Dempster Highway)`,note:`Further south in the Yukon's interior, Dawson's summers are warmer and its days shorter, with real nights in midsummer.`}],history:[`In the winter of 1931–32, a mysterious trapper who called himself Albert Johnson became the target of the most famous manhunt in Canadian history in the Mackenzie Delta country. When two Mounties came to his cabin on the Rat River to question him about tampering with Indigenous traplines, he shot one of them. Over the following weeks, in temperatures as low as −40°C, a posse of police, trappers and Gwich'in guides pursued him through the mountains and frozen rivers; he killed another Mountie and evaded them by walking backwards in his own tracks and crossing the mountains in a blizzard. For the first time in a Canadian manhunt, the police used an aircraft to track him. He was finally cornered and shot dead on the Eagle River in February 1932. Who he really was has never been confirmed.`,`The Mackenzie Delta has been home to the Gwich'in and the Inuvialuit for thousands of years, hunting caribou, muskrat and whales and fishing in the rivers and lakes. The main settlement in the delta was Aklavik, a trading post on a channel of the river. But Aklavik was built on unstable ground, prone to flooding and erosion, and in the 1950s the government decided to build a new town on higher ground. Construction began in 1954, and Inuvik, from an Inuvialuktun word meaning place of people, was officially opened in 1961. Many of Aklavik's residents refused to move, and their town adopted the motto Never Say Die.`,`Inuvik was built as a model Arctic town, with buildings on piles to keep the heat from melting the permafrost and utilidors to carry water and sewage above ground. It became a centre for government, the military and, during booms, oil and gas exploration in the Beaufort Sea. The Dempster Highway, completed in 1979, connected it to the Yukon, and in 2017 a new all-weather road reached Tuktoyaktuk on the Arctic Ocean, the first public road in Canada to reach the ocean's shore. Today Inuvik is a town of about 3,000 people, mostly Gwich'in and Inuvialuit.`],deepTitle:`Town on stilts`,deep:[`Our Lady of Victory Church, known as the igloo church, was built by Catholic missionaries in 1960 in the shape of a giant igloo. Its round wooden dome sits on a gravel pad that keeps the permafrost beneath it frozen. Inside, its walls are decorated with paintings of the Stations of the Cross by the Inuvialuk artist Mona Thrasher, showing Christ in an Arctic setting.`,`The Inuvik Community Greenhouse, converted from an old hockey arena in the late 1990s, is one of the most northerly commercial greenhouses in North America. In the long summer days, residents grow vegetables, herbs and flowers in the twenty-four-hour sunlight, a welcome alternative to expensive food flown in from the south.`,`The Inuvik to Tuktoyaktuk Highway, about 140 km long, was built across the tundra on a thick bed of gravel to protect the permafrost below. Before it opened in 2017, Tuktoyaktuk could be reached by land only in winter, on an ice road built across the frozen Mackenzie River and Arctic Ocean. The road allows visitors to drive all the way from southern Canada to the Arctic Ocean.`,`The Mackenzie River, Canada's longest, ends in a huge delta of channels, lakes and islands before flowing into the Beaufort Sea. Its waters, warmer than the surrounding land, help explain why Inuvik's summers can be surprisingly warm. The delta is a rich habitat for muskrat, fish and birds, and the Gwich'in and Inuvialuit still hunt and trap there.`]},"haida-gwaii-bc":{why:`A hundred kilometres off the British Columbia mainland, Haida Gwaii is owned by the North Pacific. The Queen Charlotte Mountains lift west-coast rain past 4,000 mm in places while the east shore sits in a local rain shadow nearer 1,300 mm. Summers stay cool; east-coast winters rarely hard-freeze. Fog, swell, and Sitka spruce are the daily facts. Vancouver Island’s west coast is a cousin, not a twin: these islands sit farther offshore, with unique subspecies from post-glacial isolation. This is hyper-maritime rainforest, not a slightly wetter Vancouver Island, and ferry schedules are part of the climate.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, wet and stormy — afternoons near 7°C, nights just above freezing, and Pacific storms that bring gales, torrential rain and huge seas, especially on the exposed west coast. Snow falls occasionally on the mountains but rarely at sea level. The ferries to the mainland are often cancelled, and the islands turn inward.`,summer:`Cool, misty and green — afternoons near 16°C, nights around 11°C, and frequent fog and drizzle, broken by sunny spells when the islands glow. Bald eagles perch everywhere, whales pass offshore, and kayakers paddle the remote coasts of Gwaii Haanas to visit ancient village sites.`},contrasts:[{label:`The west coast of Moresby Island (≈60 km SW)`,note:`Facing the open Pacific, the outer coast takes the full force of the storms and gets roughly twice the rain of the sheltered east-coast villages.`},{label:`Prince Rupert (≈150 km NE, on the mainland)`,note:`Across Hecate Strait, Prince Rupert is wetter still and a little colder in winter, at the foot of the Coast Mountains.`}],history:[`In the autumn of 1985, Haida elders stood on a logging road on Lyell Island, in the south of Haida Gwaii, and refused to move. Logging companies were clear-cutting the island's ancient forests of cedar and spruce, and the Haida, who had lived on the islands for more than ten thousand years, were determined to stop them. Over the following weeks, Mounties arrested seventy-two people, including elders, some in their seventies and eighties, who wept as they were led away. The protest made headlines across Canada. In 1993 the Haida and the federal government agreed to create Gwaii Haanas, a national park reserve and Haida heritage site managed jointly by both, a model for cooperation between Indigenous nations and the government.`,`The Haida built a remarkable civilisation on these islands, with large villages of cedar plank houses, towering carved totem poles and great ocean-going canoes in which they travelled hundreds of kilometres to trade and raid. European fur traders arrived in the 1780s, and in 1862 a smallpox epidemic swept up the coast, killing most of the Haida; their population fell from perhaps ten thousand or more to fewer than a thousand. The survivors gathered in two villages, Skidegate and Old Massett, and many of the old villages were abandoned, their totem poles left to the forest or carried off to museums.`,`In the late twentieth century the Haida led a cultural renaissance, carving new poles and canoes and reclaiming their history. The sculptor Bill Reid, whose mother was Haida, created works such as The Spirit of Haida Gwaii, a huge bronze canoe filled with mythical creatures, which appeared on the Canadian twenty-dollar bill. In 2010 the islands' colonial name, the Queen Charlotte Islands, was officially changed to Haida Gwaii, islands of the people; the Haida formally returned the old name to the province in a carved bentwood box.`],deepTitle:`Island people`,deep:[`SGang Gwaay, an abandoned Haida village on a small island at the southern tip of the archipelago, has some of the finest standing totem poles in the world. Its mortuary poles, which once held the remains of high-ranking Haida, stand weathered and grey along the shore. In 1981 it was named a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and Haida watchmen live there in summer to protect it.`,`Haida Gwaii's long isolation, at the edge of the continental shelf, has produced unique species found nowhere else, earning the islands the nickname the Galápagos of the North. They include a subspecies of black bear larger than mainland bears, a distinctive saw-whet owl and several plants that survived the ice age on small unglaciated areas.`,`For centuries a rare golden-needled Sitka spruce, known to the Haida as K'iid K'iyaas, grew beside the Yakoun River. The tree, sacred to the Haida, was about three hundred years old when, in 1997, a forest engineer named Grant Hadwin cut it down in protest against the logging industry. He vanished while kayaking to his trial and was never found. Cuttings from the tree have been grown into new saplings.`,`On 27 October 2012, a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck off the west coast of Haida Gwaii, one of the largest ever recorded in Canada. It triggered a small tsunami and briefly stopped the flow of some hot springs in Gwaii Haanas, which had been a sacred place for the Haida. The springs later partly recovered.`]},"medicine-hat-ab":{why:`Southeastern Alberta sits in the Rockies’ rain shadow, then in a second lee behind interior ranges, so Medicine Hat is dry, sunny, and prone to chinook breakthroughs that can lift winter into thaw. Short-grass prairie and a river valley add local warmth. Summer heat is real; annual precipitation is scant. This is BSk steppe with a Canadian winter still attached, closer to a high plains station than to a stereotypical prairie blizzard town.`,feel:`Medicine Hat feels like a sun-baked prairie city that has learned to love its weather. It sits in the valley of the South Saskatchewan River, below dry, grassy plains and badland coulees, and claims to be the sunniest city in Canada. Summers are hot, dry and bright; winters cold but frequently interrupted by warm chinook winds; and the big prairie sky dominates everything. The city's old brick buildings and the world's tallest teepee give it a character all its own.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, dry and sunny — afternoons a little below freezing, nights near −13°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −30°C, interrupted by chinook winds that bring sudden thaws. Snow is light, and the sunshine is frequent even in the depths of winter.`,spring:`Bright, windy and changeable — afternoons rising from about 5°C in March to 19°C by late May, with chinooks bringing warm spells and late snowstorms still possible into April. The prairie greens with the May rains, crocuses bloom on the coulees, and the wind barely stops.`,summer:`Hot, dry and blazing — afternoons near 28°C and often above 35°C, nights cooling to about 12°C in the dry air, and very little rain except for occasional thunderstorms with hail. The river valley offers shade and swimming, and the long, sunny evenings stretch past ten o'clock in June.`,autumn:`Clear and pleasant — September afternoons near 22°C cooling to about 5°C by November, calm winds and bright, crisp days. The cottonwoods along the river turn gold, and the first snow usually falls in October or November.`},residentFit:`It suits retirees, gardeners and families who want the sunniest, warmest summers on the Canadian prairies, affordable housing and a river valley full of trails, and who accept dry heat, wind and cold snaps in winter.`,history:[`In 1907 the British writer Rudyard Kipling, travelling across Canada by train, stopped in Medicine Hat, a small prairie town sitting on top of huge deposits of natural gas that the townspeople burned freely for heat and light; the streetlights were left on all day because it was cheaper than paying someone to turn them off. Kipling was delighted, writing that the place seemed to have all hell for a basement. A few years later, when some residents campaigned to change the town's unusual name to something more businesslike, Kipling wrote them a long letter urging them to keep it, arguing that the name had a history and a magic that no other could match. The townspeople voted to keep Medicine Hat.`,`The river valley was the winter home of the Blackfoot peoples, and several legends explain the city's name. According to one, a Blackfoot medicine man lost his eagle-feather headdress, or saamis, in the river during a battle with the Cree; another says that a spirit in a hole in the river ice gave a young man a magical headdress in exchange for the sacrifice of his wife. The Canadian Pacific Railway arrived in 1883, and while drilling for water, its crews struck natural gas. Medicine Hat became a city in 1906, and its cheap gas lured industries such as brick works, potteries and glass factories.`,`By the early twentieth century Medicine Hat was known as the Gas City, and its potteries, especially Medalta, produced crocks, jugs and dishes sold across Canada. The city also became the home of a large military research station at Suffield, nearby. In 1991 the city built the Saamis Teepee, originally made for the 1988 Calgary Winter Olympics and now the world's tallest teepee, on the edge of the river valley. Today Medicine Hat is a city of about 65,000 people, known for its sunshine, its historic Clay District and its industrial heritage.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Medalta Potteries, founded in 1916, became one of the largest pottery makers in Canada, shipping its stoneware crocks and dishes across the country and supplying dishes to the Canadian Pacific Railway's hotels. The company used local clay and cheap natural gas to fire its kilns. It closed in the 1950s, but its factory has been restored as the Medalta Museum in the Historic Clay District.`,`The Saamis Teepee is about 65 m tall, built of steel, and decorated with circular paintings telling the stories of the region's Indigenous peoples. It stands near an important archaeological site where Indigenous peoples camped and processed bison for thousands of years. It was moved to Medicine Hat after the Calgary Olympics.`,`Natural gas made Medicine Hat unusual among prairie towns. The city owned its own gas utility, selling gas to residents and businesses at low prices, and the revenue helped fund city services. As gas prices fell in recent decades, the city closed many of its old wells, but it still owns its gas and electric utilities.`,`Medicine Hat's sunny climate comes from its location in the rain shadow of the Rocky Mountains, on the dry short-grass prairie. It receives very little rain, and its summers are among the hottest in Canada. The city's slogan, the sunniest city in Canada, is disputed by other prairie cities, but its sunshine is real.`]},"twillingate-nl":{why:`Notre Dame Bay sits in the Labrador Current’s path, so icebergs, fog, and near-freezing water in June are normal. Pack ice drifts in some late winters, but open water for most of the season keeps winter milder than the island interior. The annual range is narrow for the latitude. Fog in early summer is warm air over cold water. Gander and the Exploits interior freeze harder and heat more; this shore stays in a cool maritime band that gardens potatoes, berries, and greens rather than corn. This is Iceberg Alley as a current, not as a brand, and the current is why the growing season never much resembled the rest of Canada.`,feel:`Twillingate feels like the edge of the North Atlantic, a fishing outport of brightly painted houses scattered across rocky islands and coves, where giant icebergs drift past the lighthouse each spring. The cold Labrador Current keeps summers cool and often foggy, and winters long, snowy and stormy. When the sun comes out on a calm summer day, with a humpback whale breaching in the bay and an iceberg glittering offshore, few places on earth feel so wild and beautiful.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, snowy and stormy — afternoons just below freezing, nights near −7°C, and heavy snow, more than two and a half metres over the season, driven by gales off the Atlantic. Sea ice drifts down from the north in late winter, locking the harbours. The community hunkers down, and snowmobiles are the main way to get around.`,spring:`Late, cold and iceberg-strewn — afternoons warming from about 0°C in March to 10°C by late May, with snow lingering into April and the first icebergs arriving from Greenland in April and May. The sea ice breaks up, and fog banks roll in off the cold water.`,summer:`Cool and bright — afternoons near 19°C, nights around 10°C, and a mix of fog and brilliant sunshine. Icebergs still drift past into June, humpback and minke whales feed in the bays, and puffins and other seabirds nest on the cliffs. The Fish, Fun and Folk Festival fills the town in July.`,autumn:`Mild then wild — September afternoons near 16°C, the barrens turning red and gold, and the first big storms arriving in October. The tourists leave, the fishing boats are hauled out, and the first snow comes in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come to see icebergs drifting down Iceberg Alley in May and June, humpback whales in summer, the Long Point Lighthouse, cod fishing trips and the music and fish and brewis of the outport kitchens.`,contrasts:[{label:`Gander (≈90 km S, inland)`,note:`Away from the cold sea, Gander's summers run a few degrees warmer and its winters colder, with less fog.`},{label:`St. John's (≈250 km SE)`,note:`On the Avalon Peninsula, St. John's is even more exposed to Atlantic storms, with more freezing rain and slightly milder winters.`}],history:[`In the 1880s a fishing merchant's daughter from Twillingate named Georgina Stirling went abroad to study singing in Paris and Milan. She had a remarkable voice, and under the stage name Marie Toulinguet — the old French name for her home islands — she sang in opera houses in Europe and America, including, by local tradition, at La Scala in Milan. She became known as the Nightingale of the North. Her career was cut short when she lost her voice, and she returned to Twillingate, where she lived quietly until her death in 1935. Her story, of a girl from a remote outport who sang on the great stages of Europe, is still told with pride in the town.`,`People have lived on Twillingate's coves for thousands of years; archaeologists have found tools left by Maritime Archaic people from about 3,500 years ago, and later the Beothuk hunted seals and fished for salmon along this coast. French fishermen named the islands Toulinguet, after a point in Brittany, and in the eighteenth century English merchants from Poole, especially John Slade, built Twillingate into the main fishing centre of the northeast coast. In 1819 a Beothuk woman named Demasduit was captured by settlers, and her husband was killed trying to rescue her; she was brought to Twillingate and named Mary March, and she died of tuberculosis the following year, as the Beothuk people faded toward extinction.`,`For two centuries, Twillingate lived on the cod fishery and the seal hunt, its merchants sending schooners to the Labrador coast each summer to catch and salt cod. The Long Point Lighthouse was built on the cliffs above the sea in 1876. When the cod fishery collapsed and was closed in 1992, Twillingate lost much of its economy, and many young people left. Today the town has turned to tourism, attracting visitors who come to watch the icebergs, whales and seabirds of what is known as Iceberg Alley.`],deepTitle:`Iceberg Alley`,deep:[`The icebergs that drift past Twillingate each spring break off glaciers in western Greenland and travel south on the Labrador Current, a journey that can take two or three years. Some are larger than a city block and contain ice that is more than ten thousand years old. Local businesses harvest small pieces of icebergs to make vodka, beer and bottled water.`,`Demasduit's capture was part of the tragic last chapter of the Beothuk. She was taken from her camp at Red Indian Lake, and the settlers who held her hoped to use her to make contact with her people. She was treated kindly in Twillingate and taught some English, but she died before she could be returned home. Her body was carried back to Red Indian Lake and left there. Her niece, Shanawdithit, the last known Beothuk, died in St. John's in 1829.`,`The merchant houses of Poole, in southern England, dominated the Newfoundland fishery in the eighteenth century, sending ships each spring with supplies and taking back salted cod to sell in Europe. John Slade, one of the most successful, made Twillingate his base, and his company's records give historians a detailed picture of life in the early outport.`,`The Long Point Lighthouse stands on a cliff about 100 m above the sea at the tip of the North Twillingate Island, one of the best places in Newfoundland to watch icebergs and whales. The lighthouse was built in 1876 and is still in operation, and its keepers' house is now a museum.`]},"kelowna-bc":{why:`The Coast Mountains cast a rain shadow over the Okanagan, so Kelowna is dry enough for bunchgrass and ponderosa while Okanagan Lake stores heat and knocks the edge off summer nights. Cold air drains off the east benches into the valley; inversions trap winter smog. A few kilometres of elevation and aspect separate lakeshore frost from vineyard belt. This is an interior desert-lake climate, not a mild West Coast city.`,immersive:`Ponderosa bark and irrigated orchard rows share the east-shore benches, and on a July afternoon the lake looks like a second sky. Heat builds in the valley until a lake breeze or a night drainage flow resets it. Winter can sit under a dirty inversion while the highlands are bright. Wildfire smoke has become a recurring summer ceiling. Cherries, apples, and wine grapes are the agricultural proof of the rain shadow; the 2003 Okanagan Mountain Park fire is the proof that dryness has a cost. This is a fast-growing city in a narrow, sun-filled trench.`,feel:`Kelowna feels like a lakeside resort that grew into a city. It sits on the eastern shore of long, deep Okanagan Lake, with vineyards and orchards climbing the benches above town and dry, pine-covered hills beyond. Summers are hot, sunny and dry, made for boating, beaches and wine tasting, while winters are cold, grey and quiet, with valley cloud trapped beneath the inversion. Its big weakness is fire: the dry forests around it burn in hot summers, and smoke can fill the valley.`,seasons:{winter:`Grey, cold and damp — afternoons near 3°C, nights a few degrees below freezing, and long spells of low cloud trapped in the valley while the ski hills above enjoy sunshine. Snow falls lightly in town, much more heavily at Big White, and the lake stays open, steaming on cold mornings.`,spring:`Blossom and green — afternoons warming from about 11°C in March to 22°C by late May, apple and cherry orchards in full bloom in April, and hills briefly green and dotted with yellow balsamroot. Late frosts are the orchardists' worry.`,summer:`Hot, dry and sunny — afternoons near 30°C and often above 35°C, nights cooling to about 14°C, and almost no rain. The lake fills with boats and swimmers, the beaches with sunbathers, and the vineyards with visitors. Wildfire smoke can turn the sky orange in bad years.`,autumn:`Harvest and colour — September afternoons near 23°C cooling to about 6°C by November, the grape and apple harvests in full swing, and the cottonwoods and larches turning gold. Valley cloud returns by November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Okanagan Lake beaches and boating, more than forty wineries, skiing at Big White in winter, golf, and cycling the Kettle Valley Rail Trail across the trestles of Myra Canyon.`,residentFit:`It suits retirees, tech and remote workers and wine-industry people who want hot, dry summers and mild valley winters by a lake, and who can afford high housing prices and live with wildfire smoke in some summers.`,contrasts:[{label:`Big White (≈55 km SE, ≈1,400 m higher)`,note:`Up at the ski resort, winters bring deep, dry snow, and the summit often basks in sunshine above the valley's grey inversion.`},{label:`Vernon (≈45 km N)`,note:`At the north end of the lake, Vernon is slightly cooler and snowier in winter, and a little wetter.`}],history:[`In 1924 a British music-hall song called The Ogo-Pogo, about a mythical creature whose mother was an earwig and whose father was a whale, became a hit, and a few years later the people of the Okanagan Valley borrowed its name for the monster said to live in their lake. Sightings of a long, dark, serpent-like creature had been reported for decades, and Syilx stories told of N'ha-a-itk, a spirit of the lake that demanded offerings from those who crossed. Kelowna embraced the legend, putting up a statue of a friendly green Ogopogo on its waterfront, and sightings are still reported every few years, usually on calm summer days.`,`The Syilx, or Okanagan, people lived along the lake for thousands of years, fishing, hunting and gathering roots and berries, and burning the hillsides to keep the grasslands open. In 1859 a French Catholic priest named Charles Pandosy founded a mission near what is now Kelowna, and planted the first apple trees in the valley. Settlers followed, and the town grew up on the lakeshore; its name comes from a Syilx word for grizzly bear, said to be the nickname given to a hairy early settler. Ranchers, then orchardists, spread across the benches, and steamboats carried fruit down the lake.`,`In the twentieth century Kelowna became the largest city in the Okanagan, growing on fruit, forestry and, later, tourism and retirement. A local hardware merchant, W.A.C. Bennett, became premier of British Columbia from 1952 to 1972. Vineyards replaced many orchards from the 1980s, and Kelowna is now the centre of Canada's second-largest wine region. In August 2003 the Okanagan Mountain Park fire swept into the city's southern neighbourhoods, destroying more than two hundred homes and forcing tens of thousands to flee; wildfires threatened the city again in 2023.`],deepTitle:`Ogopogo's lake`,deep:[`Father Pandosy's mission, the first permanent European settlement in the Okanagan, included a church, a school and farm buildings, some of which still stand at the Father Pandosy Mission Heritage Site. Pandosy and his fellow missionaries grew apples, grapes and vegetables, starting the agricultural tradition that would define the valley.`,`Okanagan Lake is about 135 km long and more than 230 m deep, and its huge volume of water moderates the climate of the surrounding valley, keeping winters milder and allowing fruit to grow. The William R. Bennett Bridge, opened in 2008 to cross the lake at Kelowna, is a floating bridge, resting on pontoons, because the lake is too deep for conventional supports.`,`The 2003 Okanagan Mountain Park fire began with a lightning strike in the park south of Kelowna and was driven by high winds into the city's suburbs. About 27,000 people were evacuated, and the fire burned some 25,000 hectares, including many of the historic trestles of the Kettle Valley Railway in nearby Myra Canyon. It changed how British Columbia prepares for wildfires in communities.`,`Kelowna has become one of Canada's fastest-growing cities, attracting retirees, families and remote workers drawn by its sunny climate, lake and outdoor lifestyle. The University of British Columbia's Okanagan campus opened in 2005, and the city's airport is one of the busiest in Canada for its size. Growth has brought traffic, high housing costs and debates over how to protect the farmland and lake.`]},"squamish-bc":{why:`Howe Sound is a fjord. At its head, Squamish River freshwater meets Strait of Georgia marine air in a narrow valley that funnels outflow winds when interior high pressure builds. Orographic lift on the surrounding granite dumps heavy winter rain and, sometimes, snow. Summers are mild and marine. Vancouver, a short drive south, is wet too but lacks the gap-wind corridor and the floodplain pinch. The Chief’s granite and the estuary together make a climate of rain, outflow, and flood risk. The gap-wind corridor is why kite sailors and cloud watchers use the same spit.`,immersive:`The Chief’s granite wall throws afternoon shade onto the valley floor while Howe Sound still glints to the south, and the outflow can arrive as a wall of cold air that kite sailors wait for. Winter is the Wet Coast: long rain, low cloud, and occasional Arctic outbreaks that stick snow on the Chief while the town sits in a mix of slush and wind. Summer warmth is real by coastal standards and still maritime. Logging trucks and climbing vans share the highway. This is a fjord-head town, not a Vancouver suburb that happens to have a monolith.`,feel:`Squamish feels like a mountain town at the head of a fjord, where the sea, the rivers and the granite walls meet. The Stawamus Chief, a huge granite dome, towers over the town; snowy peaks rise all around; and Howe Sound stretches south toward Vancouver. Winters are wet and grey, with heavy rain and snow on the mountains, while summers are warm and sunny, made for climbing, biking and kiteboarding in the strong winds that funnel up the sound.`,seasons:{winter:`Wet, grey and sometimes wild — afternoons near 6°C, nights near freezing, and heavy rain from Pacific storms, with snow on the surrounding peaks and occasionally in town. When cold, dry air builds over the interior, fierce outflow winds roar down the Squamish valley toward the sea. Thousands of bald eagles gather along the rivers to feed on spawning salmon.`,spring:`Fresh and dramatic — afternoons warming from about 10°C in March to 19°C by late May, waterfalls thundering off the cliffs with snowmelt, and frequent showers. Climbers return to the granite walls as soon as they dry.`,summer:`Warm, sunny and breezy — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 13°C, and a strong afternoon wind that blows up Howe Sound from the sea, making it one of the best kiteboarding spots in Canada. Climbers swarm the Chief, mountain bikers fill the trails, and smoke from interior wildfires sometimes drifts in.`,autumn:`Wet and colourful — September stays warm and sunny, but by October the heavy rains return. Salmon run up the rivers, and the eagles begin to arrive. The first snow dusts the peaks by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for climbing the granite of the Stawamus Chief, riding the Sea to Sky Gondola, kitesurfing on Howe Sound, mountain biking and watching thousands of bald eagles gather in winter at Brackendale.`,residentFit:`It suits climbers, mountain bikers, kiters and young families who commute or work remotely from Vancouver, and who accept heavy winter rain, rising prices and a fast-growing town.`,contrasts:[{label:`Whistler (≈60 km N, ≈670 m higher)`,note:`Up the Sea-to-Sky Highway, Whistler gets deep snow in winter and slightly cooler summers, with less rain in town but much more on the mountains.`},{label:`Vancouver (≈45 km S)`,note:`At the mouth of Howe Sound, Vancouver gets a good deal less rain than Squamish, which sits where storms pile up against the mountains.`}],history:[`In the summer of 1961 two climbers, Jim Baldwin and Ed Cooper, set out to climb the Grand Wall of the Stawamus Chief, a sheer granite cliff more than 500 m high that rises directly above the highway into Squamish. Using pitons and bolts, they inched their way up over more than forty days spread across several weeks, sleeping on ledges and returning to the ground to rest. Crowds gathered on the highway to watch, and traffic jams formed as drivers stopped to stare. When the two men reached the summit, they became celebrities, and their climb launched Squamish's reputation as one of the great rock-climbing centres of North America.`,`The Skwxwú7mesh, or Squamish, people have lived at the head of Howe Sound for thousands of years, fishing for salmon in the rivers and gathering in villages along the estuary. In their stories, the Stawamus Chief is Siy̓ám̓ Smánit, a longhouse turned to stone by the Transformer Brothers. In the late nineteenth century, settlers began logging the forests, and in 1914 the Pacific Great Eastern Railway was built north from Squamish into the interior. It took decades to finish, and people joked that its initials stood for Please Go Easy or Prince George Eventually. In 1923 sixteen Squamish villages joined together as one nation.`,`For most of the twentieth century, Squamish was a logging and mill town, cut off from Vancouver by the steep cliffs of Howe Sound until a highway was blasted along the shore in 1958. The Sea-to-Sky Highway was rebuilt for the 2010 Winter Olympics in Whistler, and Squamish began to grow rapidly as an outdoor recreation town and a commuter suburb for Vancouver. Today it is famous for climbing, mountain biking, kiteboarding and its winter gathering of bald eagles.`],deepTitle:`Granite and eagles`,deep:[`The Stawamus Chief is one of the largest granite monoliths in the world, rising about 700 m above Howe Sound. More than a thousand climbing routes now cover its walls, and hikers can reach its three summits by a steep trail. The rock was formed about 100 million years ago, and glaciers of the last ice age sculpted it into its present shape.`,`Each winter, thousands of bald eagles gather near the village of Brackendale, just north of Squamish, to feed on chum salmon that have spawned and died in the rivers. In 1994 volunteers counted 3,769 eagles in a single day, one of the largest gatherings of bald eagles ever recorded. The area is now protected as the Brackendale Eagles Provincial Park.`,`A squamish is a word used on the British Columbia coast for a strong, cold wind that blows down a mountain inlet from the interior in winter. These outflow winds can reach gale force and drop temperatures sharply. In summer, the opposite happens: the sun heats the land, and cool air rushes up Howe Sound from the sea, creating the steady afternoon winds that kiteboarders and windsurfers love.`,`The Woodfibre pulp mill, across Howe Sound from Squamish, operated for nearly a century before closing in 2006, and its waste had polluted the sound for decades. Since its closure, the waters have recovered remarkably, and orcas, dolphins and humpback whales have returned to Howe Sound after decades of absence.`]},"banff-ab":{why:`At 1,383 m in the Bow Valley, Banff is mountain-continental: long cold winters, short mild summers, and chinooks that can erase snowpack in hours when Pacific air spills over the divide. Elevation steepens the lapse rate; valley geometry pools cold at night. The front ranges wring some moisture, then the lee dries the rest. Calgary, down-valley, is prairie-chinook; the icefields are a harsher alpine. This is a park town climate, not a prairie one and not an icefield one.`,immersive:`Tunnel Mountain’s shadow slides across the Bow while Sulphur Mountain leaks steam on cold mornings, and a chinook arch can turn a −20°C dawn into a dripping afternoon. Summer is brief, bright, and busy: afternoon convection over the peaks, cold rivers, and a tourist street that never quite smells like a residential town. Winter is the real resident season — ice on the Bow, squeaking snow, and the occasional thaw that wrecks a ski base. Elk on the lawns are a park fact. This is a leased townsite inside a mountain climate, not a village that happens to be scenic.`,feel:`Banff feels like a town set inside a postcard. Rugged limestone peaks rise straight up from the edge of town, the turquoise Bow River winds through the valley, and elk graze on the lawns. At nearly 1,400 m, the air is thin and clear; winters are cold and snowy but often broken by mild chinook winds, while summers are short, warm by day and cool at night, with afternoon thunderstorms over the peaks. Everything here exists because of the national park around it.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and crisp — afternoons a little below freezing, nights near −13°C, and cold snaps that can push the thermometer below −30°C, followed by chinook winds that bring sudden thaws. Snow blankets the valley and the ski hills, the Bow River steams in the cold, and the hot springs are at their most magical.`,spring:`Late and changeable — afternoons warming from about 3°C in March to 15°C by late May, with snow possible into May and the high lakes frozen until June. Bears emerge from their dens, and the valley turns green while the peaks remain white.`,summer:`Warm days and cold nights — afternoons near 22°C, nights falling to around 6°C, and afternoon thunderstorms that build over the peaks. The lakes thaw to their famous turquoise colours, alpine meadows bloom, and the town fills with visitors from around the world.`,autumn:`Golden and crisp — September afternoons near 17°C, larches turning gold in the high valleys, and elk bugling in the meadows during the rut. The first snow dusts the peaks by September and reaches the town by late October.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the turquoise lakes of Lake Louise and Moraine Lake, skiing at Sunshine Village and Lake Louise, the hot springs, the Icefields Parkway and wildlife such as elk and bears in the national park.`,residentFit:`It suits park staff, hospitality workers and mountain guides who want to live in a national park, and who meet Parks Canada's rule that residents must work in town, along with high costs and long winters.`,contrasts:[{label:`Lake Louise (≈55 km NW, ≈150 m higher)`,note:`Further up the Bow Valley, Lake Louise is colder and snowier, with its famous lake frozen from November to June.`},{label:`Calgary (≈125 km E, ≈300 m lower)`,note:`Out on the foothills, Calgary gets more frequent chinooks and warmer summers, with the mountains on its western horizon.`}],history:[`In November 1883, three Canadian Pacific Railway workers, Frank McCabe and the brothers William and Tom McCardell, climbed down into a cave on the slopes of Sulphur Mountain and found a pool of warm, steaming mineral water. Sensing a fortune, they tried to claim the hot springs and build a health resort. Their claim, and a tangle of rival claims, led the federal government to step in, and in 1885 it set aside a reserve around the springs to protect them for the public. Two years later the reserve was expanded into Rocky Mountains Park, the first national park in Canada and the third in the world. The railway workers had not, of course, discovered the springs: the Stoney Nakoda, Siksika, Ktunaxa and other peoples had used them as a gathering and healing place for generations.`,`The Canadian Pacific Railway built its line through the Bow Valley in 1883, and the town that grew up near the hot springs was named Banff, after Banffshire in Scotland, the home county of the railway's president, George Stephen. The railway's general manager, William Van Horne, saw that the mountains could pay for the railway, saying that if the company could not export the scenery, it would import the tourists. In 1888 the CPR opened the Banff Springs Hotel, a grand castle-like resort above the Bow River, and wealthy travellers came from around the world. Mountain guides from Switzerland were brought in to lead climbers up the peaks.`,`The park's creation also meant the exclusion of the Indigenous peoples who had hunted there, though from 1889 the Stoney Nakoda were invited to Banff Indian Days, an annual summer festival that ran for decades. During the First World War, hundreds of Ukrainian and other immigrants classified as enemy aliens were held in internment camps in the park and forced to build its roads. Today Banff is one of the most visited national parks in the world, with millions of visitors a year, and the town works to balance tourism with protecting the wildlife around it.`],deepTitle:`First national park`,deep:[`The Cave and Basin, where the hot springs were found, is now a national historic site, preserving the cave and the pool where early visitors bathed. A tiny snail, the Banff Springs snail, lives only in these hot springs and nowhere else in the world, and it is protected as an endangered species. Parks Canada now acknowledges the long Indigenous history of the springs, and a mural by a Stoney Nakoda artist welcomes visitors.`,`The Trans-Canada Highway through Banff National Park was a deadly barrier for wildlife until the 1990s, when the park built a series of wildlife overpasses and underpasses, covered with trees and soil, to let animals cross safely. Bears, wolves, elk and cougars now use them regularly, and the Banff crossings have become a model for wildlife corridors around the world.`,`The Banff Springs Hotel was rebuilt in the 1920s in stone after a fire, in the style of a Scottish baronial castle. Legends tell of ghosts in the hotel, including a bride who fell on the stairs on her wedding day and a bellman who still helps guests decades after his death. The hotel remains one of the most famous in Canada.`,`Lake Louise and Moraine Lake, north of Banff, owe their brilliant turquoise colour to rock flour, fine particles ground by glaciers and suspended in the water, which reflect blue and green light. The lakes are so popular that the park now limits private cars and requires visitors to take shuttle buses in summer.`]},"halifax-ns":{why:`Chebucto Bay faces the Atlantic at a latitude where leftover tropical storms and nor’easters both arrive. Marine air keeps winters milder and summers cooler than inland Nova Scotia; Truro and Windsor freeze harder and heat more. Fog and humidity are frequent because cool water meets milder air. The harbour’s complex shoreline creates small differences between Halifax, Dartmouth, and Bedford, but none of them escape the ocean. Annual precipitation is high. Gardens do hardy apples, berries, and greens. This is a humid maritime city on a hurricane-and-gale track, not a foggy Montreal and not a dry capital.`,immersive:`Salt in the fog at Point Pleasant, a drumlin under spruce, and the harbour working under it — that is a Halifax morning in June. Summer heat is real inland and often trimmed at the waterfront by onshore flow. Autumn can bring a former hurricane’s rain. Winter is slush, freeze, and the occasional blizzard that arrives as a nor’easter rather than as prairie still-cold. Citadel Hill’s wind is a reminder that this was sited as a fortress on an ice-free harbour. The climate is wet, maritime, and busy, not dramatic in the Newfoundland sense and not continental in the Québec sense.`,feel:`Halifax feels like a seafaring city with its face to the Atlantic. Built on a hilly peninsula beside one of the largest natural harbours in the world, it is a city of naval ships, ferries, old stone forts and wooden houses painted in bold colours. The ocean keeps summers mild and often foggy, and winters raw and stormy, with a mix of snow, freezing rain and rain. Its history of shipwrecks, explosions and wartime convoys gives it a weight beyond its size.`,seasons:{winter:`Raw, stormy and changeable — afternoons near 2°C, nights near −7°C, and storms that alternate between heavy snow, freezing rain and rain, sometimes all in the same day. Nor'easters can bring gale-force winds and high seas, and the harbour steams with sea smoke on the coldest mornings.`,spring:`Late and foggy — afternoons warming from about 4°C in March to 15°C by late May, with fog rolling in off the cold sea and snow possible into April. The lilacs and apple blossoms bloom in late May, and the city's parks and gardens come alive.`,summer:`Mild and pleasant — afternoons near 23°C, nights around 13°C, and frequent fog, especially in early summer. The waterfront boardwalk fills with visitors, tall ships and cruise ships call, and the beaches outside the city draw swimmers on warm days.`,autumn:`Warm, then stormy — September and early October bring some of the best weather of the year, with afternoons near 20°C, but also the risk of hurricanes and tropical storms, which have struck the city several times. The trees turn colour in October, and the first snow usually falls in November.`},residentFit:`It suits students, military families and workers in health care, government and the ocean industries who want a lively, historic port city on the Atlantic, and who accept damp, changeable weather and snowy winters.`,contrasts:[{label:`Peggy's Cove (≈40 km SW, on the open coast)`,note:`On the exposed granite coast, Peggy's Cove is foggier and windier, with waves that can sweep the rocks even on calm days.`},{label:`Truro (≈100 km N, inland)`,note:`Away from the open Atlantic, Truro's summers run a few degrees warmer and its winters colder, with more snow.`}],history:[`At about 8:45 on the morning of 6 December 1917, the French cargo ship Mont-Blanc, loaded with nearly 3,000 tonnes of explosives for the war in Europe, collided with the Norwegian ship Imo in the narrow strait leading into Halifax harbour and caught fire. Crowds gathered on the shore to watch. About twenty minutes later, at 9:04, the Mont-Blanc exploded in the largest human-made blast before the atomic bomb, flattening the north end of Halifax, shattering windows kilometres away and sending a tsunami over the shore. About two thousand people were killed and some nine thousand injured. A train dispatcher named Vince Coleman stayed at his post to send a warning that stopped an incoming passenger train, and died at his telegraph key. The city of Boston sent doctors, nurses and supplies, and since 1971 Nova Scotia has sent Boston a giant Christmas tree every year in thanks.`,`The Mi'kmaq call the harbour Kjipuktuk, the great harbour, and had used its shores for thousands of years. In 1749 the British founded Halifax as a naval base to counter the French fortress of Louisbourg, under Governor Edward Cornwallis, who issued a bounty on the scalps of Mi'kmaq people; his statue was removed from a Halifax park in 2018. The city became a key base of the Royal Navy, guarded by the star-shaped Citadel on the hill above the harbour. In 1912 ships from Halifax recovered the bodies of victims of the Titanic, and about 150 of them are buried in the city's cemeteries.`,`In both world wars, Halifax was the main port for convoys carrying troops and supplies across the Atlantic. From 1928 to 1971, Pier 21 was the gateway for about a million immigrants to Canada, and it is now the Canadian Museum of Immigration. In the 1960s the city demolished Africville, a Black community on the shore of Bedford Basin founded in the 1840s, relocating its residents in city dump trucks; the city apologised in 2010. Today Halifax is the largest city in Atlantic Canada, home to universities, the navy and a lively waterfront.`],deepTitle:`The great harbour`,deep:[`The Halifax Explosion devastated the neighbourhood of Richmond and the Mi'kmaq community of Turtle Grove across the harbour, which was never rebuilt. The next day a blizzard struck the city, hampering rescue efforts and adding to the suffering of the homeless. The disaster led to advances in the treatment of eye injuries, as hundreds of people were blinded by flying glass.`,`The Halifax Citadel, a star-shaped fort on the hill above the city, was completed in 1856 and never fired a shot in battle. A noon gun has been fired from the Citadel every day for more than 150 years, and soldiers in nineteenth-century uniforms reenact the life of the garrison for visitors.`,`Africville was a close-knit community of Black Nova Scotians, many of them descendants of people who had escaped slavery in the United States. The city denied it basic services such as running water and sewers, and placed a dump and other unwanted facilities nearby. In the 1960s, in the name of urban renewal, the city bought or expropriated the land and bulldozed the homes and church. A replica of the church now stands on the site, and former residents and their descendants gather each summer for a reunion.`,`Peggy's Cove, a fishing village on the granite coast southwest of Halifax, with its red-and-white lighthouse perched on bare rock, is one of the most photographed places in Canada. In 1998 Swissair Flight 111 crashed into the sea nearby, killing all 229 people on board, and the people of Peggy's Cove and neighbouring villages joined the recovery effort.`]},"charlottetown-pei":{why:`Prince Edward Island is a sandbar in the Gulf of St. Lawrence: water warms the summer and, when the Gulf ices, winter turns windy and continental again. Seasonal lag versus mainland New Brunswick is the island effect. Red sandstone soils drain and erode. Tropical remnants and nor’easters both find the shore. Charlottetown’s harbour is a slightly sheltered version of that Gulf climate, not a different one. Summers stay mild and windy rather than hot; winters bring drift and a wind that makes the Confederation Bridge a weather report. The capital’s services do not cancel Gulf ice.`,immersive:`Red soil on a wet tire, a southwest breeze off the harbour, and potato blossom in July — Charlottetown’s summer is mild and windy rather than hot. North-shore beaches can feel cooler than a mainland heat wave because the Gulf is right there. Winter reverses it: drift, freeze, and a wind that makes the Confederation Bridge a weather report. Spring fog is common. This is a small capital on an island that still farms, with a climate you feel in the soil colour and the salt, not only on a thermometer.`,feel:`Charlottetown feels like a small, gracious capital set in a garden by the sea. Prince Edward Island's rolling fields of red soil, potatoes and grain run right up to the edge of town, and the harbour opens into the warm, shallow waters of the Gulf of St. Lawrence. Summers are mild, breezy and green, with some of the warmest ocean water north of the Carolinas, while winters are long, cold and snowy, with the gulf freezing over and storms sweeping across the island.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and windswept — afternoons just below freezing, nights near −8°C, and storms that bring heavy snow and drifting across the open fields. The Gulf of St. Lawrence freezes over, and ice piles up along the shores. The Confederation Bridge sometimes closes in high winds.`,spring:`Late and cool — afternoons warming from about 1°C in March to 15°C by late May, with the sea ice breaking up in April and fog common along the coast. Farmers begin planting potatoes in May, turning the fields red.`,summer:`Warm and breezy — afternoons near 23°C, nights around 14°C, and sea breezes that keep the island comfortable. The beaches on the north shore, with water warmer than most of Atlantic Canada, fill with swimmers, and the theatres stage the musical Anne of Green Gables.`,autumn:`Mild and mellow — the warm gulf keeps September afternoons near 19°C, the potato harvest fills the fields, and the trees turn colour in October. Storms grow stronger, and the remnants of hurricanes sometimes sweep across the island.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the red-sand beaches of Prince Edward Island, Green Gables and the world of Anne of Green Gables, lobster suppers and mussels, golf, and the Confederation Trail across the island.`,residentFit:`It suits families, retirees and newcomers who want a small, friendly capital surrounded by farms and beaches, and who accept snowy winters, the occasional post-tropical storm and a slower pace of life.`,contrasts:[{label:`Moncton, New Brunswick (≈110 km SW, on the mainland)`,note:`Away from the moderating sea, Moncton's summers run a little warmer and its winters colder.`},{label:`The north shore beaches (≈30 km N)`,note:`Along the Gulf of St. Lawrence, the sand dunes and beaches are breezier, with warm water in summer and pack ice in winter.`}],history:[`In September 1864, delegates from the British colonies of Nova Scotia, New Brunswick and Prince Edward Island gathered in Charlottetown to discuss uniting the three Maritime colonies. Politicians from the Province of Canada, including John A. Macdonald, asked to join, and arrived on the steamer Queen Victoria, reportedly with a large supply of champagne. They found the town so full of visitors for a travelling circus that there were few rooms to be had. Over a week of meetings, dinners and balls, the Canadians persuaded the Maritimers to consider a much bigger union, which became the Dominion of Canada in 1867. Charlottetown is now proudly called the birthplace of Confederation. Prince Edward Island itself, however, refused to join at first, and entered Canada only in 1873, when it was nearly bankrupt from building a railway.`,`The Mi'kmaq call the island Epekwitk, cradled on the waves, and in their stories the spirit Glooscap shaped it from red clay. The French named it Île Saint-Jean and settled Acadian farmers there; in 1758 the British deported most of them. In 1767 the British government divided the entire island into 67 lots and gave them away by lottery to absentee landlords in Britain, who charged tenant farmers rent for more than a century. The Land Question, the farmers' long struggle to own their land, dominated the island's politics until the 1870s, when the government bought out the landlords.`,`Charlottetown grew as a small port and the capital of the island, a city of churches, wooden houses and tree-lined streets. In 1908 Lucy Maud Montgomery, who grew up in Cavendish on the north shore, published Anne of Green Gables, which became one of the best-loved books in the world and made the island famous. In 1997 the Confederation Bridge, almost 13 km long, connected the island to the mainland, ending its dependence on ferries. Today Charlottetown is a small capital of about 40,000 people, with a lively waterfront, a university and a busy summer of festivals.`],deepTitle:`Confederation cradle`,deep:[`The Charlottetown Conference was held in Province House, the island's legislature, which was completed in 1847 and is a national historic site. Its Confederation Chamber, where the delegates met, has been preserved as it was in 1864. A building beside it, the Confederation Centre of the Arts, opened in 1964 for the conference's hundredth anniversary.`,`Anne of Green Gables tells the story of an orphaned girl with red hair and a vivid imagination who is adopted by an elderly brother and sister on a farm in Avonlea, a fictional village based on Cavendish. The book has been translated into dozens of languages, and it is especially popular in Japan. Thousands of visitors each year visit Green Gables, the farmhouse that inspired the story, now part of a national park.`,`Prince Edward Island's red soil gets its colour from iron oxide, rust, in the sandstone that forms the island. The soil is ideal for growing potatoes, and the island produces about a quarter of Canada's potatoes. The red cliffs and beaches, eroding into the sea, give the island its distinctive look, and the island is slowly shrinking as the waves wear away its shores.`,`The Confederation Bridge, which opened in 1997, crosses the Northumberland Strait between Prince Edward Island and New Brunswick. It is one of the longest bridges in the world over ice-covered water, built to withstand the moving ice that fills the strait in winter. Before it opened, islanders depended on ferries, and the decision to build the bridge was hotly debated in a plebiscite.`]},"baie-saint-paul-qc":{why:`Baie-Saint-Paul sits in the Charlevoix hills’ lee at the Gouffre’s mouth on a cold St. Lawrence estuary, where marine air trims summer heat and Laurentian lift wrings snow and rain. A meteorite structure some 54 km across, identified in 1965, shapes the basin’s relief. Aspect and river flats create orchard pockets inside a harsh regional winter. Québec City, upriver, is more urban and less hill-sheltered. This is a sheltered north-shore town, not a mild coast and not the capital’s climate.`,seasons:{summer:`Warm, bright and fresh — afternoons near 23°C, nights around 12°C, and a cool breeze off the broad St. Lawrence estuary, which here is already salty and tidal. Fog sometimes drifts in from the river in the morning. The galleries and terraces fill with visitors, farms sell cheese, berries and cider, and whales feed in the estuary further downstream.`},history:[`In 1984 a group of street performers from Baie-Saint-Paul — stilt walkers, jugglers and fire-breathers who called themselves Les Échassiers de Baie-Saint-Paul — took their show to Quebec City as part of celebrations for the 450th anniversary of Jacques Cartier's arrival. One of them, Gilles Ste-Croix, had earlier walked about 90 km on stilts from Baie-Saint-Paul to Quebec City to persuade the government to fund their project. With Guy Laliberté, a fire-eater and accordionist, they formed a new circus without animals, built around acrobats, music and theatre. They called it the Cirque du Soleil. It grew into one of the largest entertainment companies in the world, with shows in Las Vegas and on every continent, all born in a small town on the St. Lawrence.`,`The north shore of the St. Lawrence estuary, where the Gouffre River meets the great river, was a seasonal hunting and fishing ground for the Innu and other Indigenous peoples long before the French arrived. Samuel de Champlain named the bay in the early 1600s, and French farmers settled the valley in the late seventeenth century. Charlevoix's dramatic landscape of hills and valleys, sloping down to the river, gave it a reputation for beauty, and in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries painters such as Clarence Gagnon and A.Y. Jackson of the Group of Seven came to paint its farms, churches and snowy hills.`,`Baie-Saint-Paul became known as an artists' town, with more galleries per person than almost anywhere in Canada. In 1988 Charlevoix was designated a UNESCO biosphere reserve, recognising its unusual landscape, which is partly the result of a giant meteorite that struck the area about 450 million years ago, leaving a crater some 54 km wide. Today Baie-Saint-Paul is a town of about 7,000 people, a centre for art, local food and tourism, and the gateway to the whale-watching and resorts of the Charlevoix coast.`],deepTitle:`Crater and circus`,deep:[`The Charlevoix crater is one of the largest impact craters on earth. The meteorite, about 2 km across, struck with enormous force and created a ring-shaped depression, with a central peak at Mont des Éboulements. Baie-Saint-Paul lies within the crater, whose outline can still be traced in the shape of the hills and valleys around it.`,`The Charlevoix region is one of the most seismically active areas in eastern Canada. The ancient crater weakened the earth's crust, and earthquakes occur regularly. In 1663 a powerful earthquake, perhaps magnitude 7, shook the region, triggering landslides along the St. Lawrence; another in 1925 damaged buildings in Baie-Saint-Paul and Quebec City.`,`Clarence Gagnon, a Montreal painter, spent many years in Baie-Saint-Paul in the early twentieth century, painting the villages, farms and snowy hills in bright colours. His illustrations for the novel Maria Chapdelaine became famous. The Musée d'art contemporain de Baie-Saint-Paul and the many galleries in town continue the tradition.`,`The Cirque du Soleil's founders grew up in a Quebec culture of street performance and festivals, and their early shows toured Quebec villages before travelling across Canada and to Los Angeles in 1987, where they became a sensation. The company's headquarters is now in Montreal, but Baie-Saint-Paul remembers its role as the circus's birthplace.`]},"dawson-city-yt":{why:`Far inland near the Arctic Circle, Dawson sits at the Yukon–Klondike confluence where cold air pools in a basin and summer daylight is extreme. Continentality is the main driver; the rivers only slightly ease the edges. Winters are severe and dark; summers are short, dry, and biologically frantic. Permafrost underpins the wooden streets. Whitehorse, down-territory, is milder in services and slightly less of a frost hollow. This is a frost-hollow subarctic town, drier and more seasonal than any coastal north, and the midnight sun does not cancel a basin that holds severe cold until a breeze mixes it.`,immersive:`Permafrost heaves the old boardwalks, and on a June midnight the Yukon still holds a low sun while swallows work the river. Winter is another country: ice fog, squeaking snow, and a cold that pools in the basin until a breeze mixes it. Dust at breakup, mosquitoes at green-up, and an early gold autumn complete a year with almost no mild middle. The town is small, historic, and expensive to keep level on thawing ground. This is a Hän river junction with a gold-rush grid on top, not a theme park that happens to be cold.`,feel:`Dawson City feels like a gold-rush town frozen in time. Its dirt streets, wooden boardwalks and false-fronted buildings, some tilting as the permafrost beneath them shifts, look much as they did in 1898, when tens of thousands of people poured in seeking fortune. The climate is one of extremes: winters are long, dark and bitterly cold, with temperatures plunging toward −40°C, while summers are short, warm and bright, with the sun barely setting at midnight.`,seasons:{winter:`Long, dark and brutally cold — afternoons near −17°C, nights near −26°C, and cold snaps that push the thermometer below −40°C, with ice fog hanging over the town. Days shrink to about four hours of light. The Yukon River freezes solid, and an ice bridge carries traffic across it to the west bank.`,spring:`Bright and slushy — afternoons warming from about −5°C in March to 15°C by late May, with snow melting quickly in April and May. The ice on the Yukon River breaks up with a roar in early May, an event so eagerly awaited that townspeople bet on the exact time it will happen.`,summer:`Short, warm and endless — afternoons near 22°C and sometimes above 30°C, nights cool, and nearly twenty-four hours of daylight around the solstice, when the sun barely dips below the hills. Tourists fill the boardwalks, the saloons and the casino, and miners work the creeks around the clock.`,autumn:`Brief and brilliant — September afternoons near 12°C, the birches and aspens turning gold, and the first snow arriving by late September. The river ferry stops running in October, and the town settles in for winter.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Klondike gold rush streets and saloons, Diamond Tooth Gerties' gambling hall, the Sourtoe Cocktail, the Midnight Dome at midsummer, the drive along the Top of the World Highway and the aurora in autumn.`,contrasts:[{label:`Whitehorse (≈530 km S)`,note:`In the rain shadow of the Coast Mountains, Whitehorse has milder winters and slightly cooler summers.`},{label:`Old Crow (≈450 km N, above the Arctic Circle)`,note:`In the Yukon's far north, Old Crow's winters are even colder and longer, with weeks of polar night.`}],history:[`On 16 August 1896, three men panning for gold in a creek off the Klondike River found a nugget-rich streak so thick that, as one of them later said, it lay between the flat pieces of rock like cheese in a sandwich. They were George Carmack, an American, and two Tagish men, Skookum Jim and Dawson Charlie. They staked their claims on the creek, which they renamed Bonanza, and the news spread. When ships carrying miners and their gold reached San Francisco and Seattle in July 1897, the Klondike gold rush began. Some 100,000 people set out for the Klondike, struggling over the Chilkoot and White passes and floating down the Yukon River, and perhaps 30,000 or 40,000 reached Dawson City, which in 1898 was briefly one of the largest cities in western Canada.`,`The Hän people, the Tr'ondëk Hwëch'in, had a fishing camp called Tr'ochëk at the mouth of the Klondike River, where they caught salmon each summer. When the gold seekers arrived, they were pushed aside, and Chief Isaac moved his people downriver to a new village at Moosehide. Dawson City, named after the geologist George Mercer Dawson, became the capital of the new Yukon Territory in 1898. It was a town of saloons, dance halls, gambling houses and theatres, where gold dust was the currency and prices were astronomical. The North-West Mounted Police kept order, requiring each gold seeker to bring a year's supply of food, about a tonne of goods, into the country.`,`The rush ended almost as quickly as it began; in 1899 news of gold at Nome, Alaska, drew many miners away. Large companies took over the creeks with giant dredges, and Dawson shrank to a small town. In 1953 the territorial capital moved to Whitehorse. But the old buildings survived, preserved by the cold, and the town became a historic site and a tourist destination. In 2023 Tr'ondëk-Klondike, including Tr'ochëk and Moosehide, was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site, recognising the Hän people's history alongside the gold rush.`],deepTitle:`Klondike gold`,deep:[`Writers made the Klondike famous. Jack London spent the winter of 1897–98 near Dawson, and his stories of the North, such as The Call of the Wild, drew on his experiences there. Robert Service, who came to Dawson as a bank clerk in 1908, wrote poems about the gold rush in a small cabin that still stands. Pierre Berton, one of Canada's most popular historians, grew up in Dawson, and his childhood home is now a writers' residence.`,`In 1978 construction workers digging in Dawson City found more than five hundred reels of silent films, buried in an old swimming pool that had been filled in and used as a hockey rink. The films, from the 1910s and 1920s, had been sent to Dawson at the end of their distribution chain and never returned, and they had been preserved by the permafrost. Many were the only surviving copies, and the Dawson City Film Find has been called one of the greatest discoveries in film history.`,`The Downtown Hotel in Dawson serves the Sourtoe Cocktail, a drink containing a real, mummified human toe. The tradition began in 1973 when a local man found a frostbitten toe preserved in a jar in an old cabin. Customers who drink the cocktail and let the toe touch their lips join the Sourtoe Club. Over the years several toes have been swallowed or stolen, and replacements have been donated.`,`Diamond Tooth Gertie's, which opened in 1971, was the first legal casino in Canada. It features can-can dancers and gambling in the style of the gold rush, and its profits help fund the town's heritage. Dawson's old buildings, including the Palace Grand Theatre and the Commissioner's Residence, are preserved by Parks Canada as part of a national historic site.`]},"morden-mb":{why:`Southern Manitoba’s latitude plus the Pembina Escarpment’s modest relief give Morden more heat units than much of the province, with lee-side dryness relative to the Red River lowland. Summers can ripen corn and even hardy grapes; winters remain fully prairie-severe. A few tens of metres of step, and a position west of the wetter lake-bottom flats, change drainage, frost, and wind enough for a federal horticulture station to treat this as a garden belt. This is a thermal and sun pocket, not a mild one. Continentality is still the year, and the Parkland roses prove July, not January.`,immersive:`Shelterbelts of caragana and the escarpment’s long grade are the first climate you see driving in from the Red River flats, and in July the research-station roses look almost impolite this far north. Heat units accumulate on the prairie step that becomes Manitoba’s garden belt. January still bites. The Pembina Hills catch a little more snow and offer a little more aspect than the lake bottom to the east. This is a working agricultural town with a federal horticulture legacy, not a secret wine country. The honesty is heat in summer and prairie winter without lake moderation.`,feel:`Morden feels like a garden town on the prairie. It sits at the foot of the Pembina Escarpment, the ancient shoreline of a vast glacial lake, in one of the warmest corners of Manitoba, where orchards, flower gardens and tall shade trees thrive in spite of the bitter prairie winters. Summers are warm and sunny, with dramatic thunderstorms; winters are long, cold and windy. The surrounding fields of corn, sunflowers and canola stretch to the horizon.`,seasons:{winter:`Long and bitter — afternoons near −8°C, nights near −18°C, and arctic outbreaks that can push the thermometer below −35°C, with windchills far colder. Snow blows across the open prairie, and blizzards can close the roads. Bright, sunny days are common between storms.`,spring:`Late and rapid — afternoons climbing from about 0°C in March to 19°C by late May, the snow melting in a rush in April, and the orchards and gardens bursting into bloom in May. Late frosts and spring floods on the Red River are risks.`,summer:`Warm, sunny and stormy — afternoons near 26°C, nights around 13°C, and thunderstorms that roll across the prairie, sometimes with hail and tornadoes. Fields of corn and sunflowers ripen, and the Corn and Apple Festival in August fills the town.`,autumn:`Crisp and golden — September afternoons near 19°C cooling quickly, the harvest in full swing, and the trees along the escarpment turning colour. The first snow often falls in October.`},residentFit:`It suits gardeners, farm families and retirees who want one of the longest growing seasons on the Manitoba prairie, a safe, tidy small town and easy access to Winnipeg, and who accept cold winters and summer storms.`,contrasts:[{label:`Winnipeg (≈100 km N)`,note:`In the heart of the flat Red River Valley, Winnipeg has similar summers and slightly colder, longer winters.`},{label:`Brandon (≈170 km NW)`,note:`Further west on the prairie, Brandon is drier, with colder winter nights and a shorter growing season.`}],history:[`In 1974 bentonite miners near Thornhill, just west of Morden, came across fossil bones in the shale of the Pembina Escarpment. They belonged to a mosasaur, a giant marine reptile with flippers and powerful jaws that lived about 80 million years ago, when a shallow sea covered the middle of North America. The mosasaur, nicknamed Bruce, turned out to be about 13 m long, and he is now displayed at the Canadian Fossil Discovery Centre in Morden as the largest mosasaur on public display in the world, recognised by Guinness World Records. The shale around Morden has yielded the bones of many other sea creatures, including plesiosaurs, turtles and fish.`,`The Pembina Escarpment, a steep slope rising west of the Red River Valley, marks the old shoreline of glacial Lake Agassiz, a huge lake that covered much of Manitoba at the end of the last ice age. The land around it was used for thousands of years by the Anishinaabe, Dakota, Cree and later the Red River Métis, who hunted bison and travelled by ox cart along trails at the foot of the escarpment. In 1871 Treaty 1 ceded the land to the Crown, and from 1874 settlers arrived, including Mennonites from Russia, who settled a large reserve west of the Red River, and English-speaking settlers from Ontario.`,`In 1915 the federal government opened an experimental farm at Morden to develop fruit trees, shrubs and flowers that could survive the harsh prairie climate. Its scientists bred hardy apples, plums, cherries and roses, and the Morden roses, able to survive bitterly cold winters, are now grown across Canada and in cold climates around the world. Morden became known as a garden town, and its Corn and Apple Festival has been held each August since 1967. Today it is a small city of about 10,000 people, the centre of a rich farming region.`],deepTitle:`Sea monsters, roses`,deep:[`The Western Interior Seaway, which divided North America in two during the age of dinosaurs, was home to mosasaurs, plesiosaurs, giant fish and sharks. When the sea retreated, the sediments on its floor became the shale of the Pembina Escarpment, preserving the bones of the creatures that lived in it. The Canadian Fossil Discovery Centre runs digs where visitors can help excavate fossils.`,`The Morden Research Station developed the Parkland series of roses, hardy shrub roses with names like Morden Blush and Morden Sunrise that bloom through the summer and survive temperatures below −35°C. Its scientists also bred apples and other fruits for the prairies. The station's arboretum and gardens are open to visitors and display many of the plants developed there.`,`The Mennonites who settled the West Reserve, west of the Red River, came from colonies in Russia in the 1870s, seeking religious freedom and exemption from military service. They built villages in the traditional pattern, with houses and barns along a single street, and farmed the land communally at first. Their descendants still live around Morden and the nearby town of Winkler.`,`Glacial Lake Agassiz, which formed as the ice sheets melted about 13,000 years ago, was larger than all the Great Lakes combined. When it drained, it left the flat, fertile Red River Valley and the beaches and shorelines that form the Pembina Escarpment. Some scientists believe its sudden draining into the ocean may have triggered a period of cold climate across the Northern Hemisphere.`]},"sutton-qc":{why:`Modest Appalachian relief at the Vermont border wrings extra snow on Mont Sutton while valley floors pond cold air on clear nights. South-facing benches carry apples, vines, and maple. The village is wetter and hillier than Montréal, less severe than the Laurentian north. Orographic lift, drainage, and aspect pack several microclimates into a few minutes’ drive. A ski hill and an orchard bench can be different climates at dawn. This is a piedmont town, not a generic Eastern Townships average, and frost hollows in the flats are why growers still map by elevation as carefully as by variety.`,immersive:`Maple on the slope and a frost hollow in the flats can be the same dawn in Sutton, with Mont Sutton holding cloud while the village street is already in sun. Winter snow at the ski hill is a different climate than the orchard benches. Summer is humid and green, not hot in the Montréal-island sense. Autumn colour is a topographic event: elevation and hardwoods, not a marketing week. This is a bilingual hill town with Loyalist street memory and a ski economy, in Appalachians that still behave like mountains even when the map calls them modest.`,feel:`Sutton feels like a mountain village of maple woods and covered bridges in Quebec's Eastern Townships. Its main street of old brick and clapboard houses, cafés and galleries sits at the foot of the Sutton Mountains, part of the Appalachians, a few kilometres from the Vermont border, among sugar shacks, vineyards and rolling farmland. The climate is snowy and four-seasoned: winters cold and white, perfect for skiing, summers warm and green, and autumns brilliant with maple colour.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, snowy and bright — afternoons a little below freezing, nights near −12°C, and plenty of snow, especially on Mont Sutton, where the ski area is known for its gladed runs through the forest. Cold air sinks into the valleys on clear nights, making the village colder than the slopes above. Cross-country skiers and snowshoers explore the trails.`,spring:`Sugaring season, then mud — afternoons rising from about 3°C in March to 19°C by late May. In March and April, freezing nights and thawing days set the maple sap running, and the sugar shacks fill with families eating pancakes and maple taffy poured on snow. The snow melts slowly, the back roads turn to mud, and the forests leaf out in May.`,summer:`Warm, green and leafy — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 13°C, and afternoon thunderstorms building over the hills. Hikers climb the Sutton Mountains, cyclists ride the quiet roads, and the vineyards and orchards in the valleys ripen.`,autumn:`Brilliant and crisp — September afternoons near 21°C, and the maple forests on the hills turning red, orange and gold by early October, among the best displays in Quebec. Orchards fill with apple pickers, and the first snow arrives in November.`},contrasts:[{label:`Mont Sutton summit (≈5 km E, ≈750 m higher)`,note:`At the top of the ski area, winters are much snowier and colder, and the snow lasts into April.`},{label:`Montreal (≈100 km NW)`,note:`On the St. Lawrence lowlands, Montreal's summers are a little warmer and its winters bring less snow.`}],history:[`In June 1866 about a thousand Irish-American veterans of the US Civil War, members of the Fenian Brotherhood, crossed the border from Vermont into Quebec at Pigeon Hill, some 25 km west of Sutton. They hoped to capture Canada and trade it back to Britain for Irish independence. They looted farms and taverns in the area for a few days, but when British troops and Canadian militia approached and the US government cut off their supplies, they retreated across the border. Four years later, in 1870, the Fenians tried again at Eccles Hill nearby and were driven back in a short battle. The raids helped persuade the British colonies to unite in Confederation in 1867.`,`The hills around Sutton were part of Ndakina, the homeland of the Western Abenaki, who hunted, fished and travelled through the valleys between Missisquoi Bay and the St. Lawrence. After the American Revolution, Loyalists and other settlers from New England moved north into the area, which became known as the Eastern Townships. They cleared the forests for farms, raised dairy cattle and built villages in a New England style, with white churches and covered bridges. Sutton was surveyed in 1792 and settled in the early 1800s.`,`For much of the twentieth century, the Sutton area was a quiet farming region, close to the border and to Vermont. In 1960 the Boulanger family opened the Mont Sutton ski area, which became known for its natural glade skiing through the trees, and the village became a destination for skiers, artists and city people seeking a country home. Today Sutton is a mostly French-speaking town with an English-speaking heritage, surrounded by forests, vineyards and orchards.`],deepTitle:`Fenians and maples`,deep:[`The Fenian Brotherhood, founded in the United States in 1858, aimed to free Ireland from British rule. After the Civil War, thousands of Irish-American veterans joined, and the organisation launched several raids into Canada between 1866 and 1871. Although they failed, the raids alarmed Canadians and helped build support for a united Canada able to defend itself.`,`The Eastern Townships, or Cantons-de-l'Est, were settled largely by English-speaking Loyalists and New Englanders, unlike the rest of Quebec, which was settled by the French under the seigneurial system. Over the twentieth century, French-speaking Quebecers moved into the area, and today it is mostly French-speaking, but the old English names, churches and cemeteries remain.`,`Maple syrup is a major product of the Sutton hills. Each spring, when warm days follow cold nights, sap flows in the maple trees, and producers collect it and boil it down in sugar shacks, or cabanes à sucre, where families gather for traditional meals of ham, eggs and pancakes covered in syrup. Quebec produces most of the world's maple syrup.`,`Mont Sutton's ski area pioneered glade skiing, in which skiers weave through the trees rather than on cleared runs, preserving the forest. Its trails wind through maple and birch forests, offering a quieter, more natural skiing experience than larger resorts.`]}},c={"oaxaca-mx":{why:`Seventeen degrees north would read as tropical heat if the Valles Centrales sat near sea level. At about 1555 m the lapse rate knocks afternoon highs into the low-to-mid 20s °C for most months, while the Sierra Madre de Oaxaca and Sierra Madre del Sur blunt Gulf and Pacific moisture except when summer monsoon convection builds on the ridges. East–west valley axes still funnel Isthmus air; orographic lift then dumps short, hard afternoon storms. Dry-season light is hard and clear. Wet-season evenings usually recover after the cells collapse.`,seasons:{summer:`The green, rainy season — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 14°C, and clouds that build over the mountains by midday and break into heavy afternoon thunderstorms, washing the streets and turning the dry hills of the valley green. Mornings are usually sunny and fresh. In July the city celebrates the Guelaguetza, when dancers from the state's many Indigenous communities fill the auditorium on the Cerro del Fortín.`},history:[`In January 1932 the Mexican archaeologist Alfonso Caso and his team were excavating the ancient hilltop city of Monte Albán, overlooking Oaxaca, when they opened a tomb that had been sealed for centuries. Tomb 7, as it was called, had originally been built by the Zapotecs, but it had later been reused by the Mixtecs for the burial of a noble, and it was packed with treasures: gold jewellery and pectorals, pearls, turquoise, jade, rock crystal goblets and bones carved with delicate scenes. It was one of the richest burials ever found in the Americas, and the discovery made headlines around the world. Its treasures are now displayed in the museum in the former convent of Santo Domingo in Oaxaca.`,`The Zapotecs built Monte Albán about 500 BCE, levelling the top of a mountain above the three valleys of Oaxaca to create a great plaza surrounded by temples, palaces and a ball court. For more than a thousand years it was one of the great cities of ancient Mexico, until it declined around 800 CE. The Mixtecs later moved into the valleys, and in the fifteenth century the Aztecs conquered them and built a fort at a place called Huaxyacac. In 1521 Spanish conquistadors arrived, and in 1529 they laid out a new town on a grid, which became Antequera and later Oaxaca. Dominican friars built churches and monasteries, including the magnificent Santo Domingo, with its gilded interior.`,`Oaxaca's most famous son was Benito Juárez, a Zapotec boy from the mountain village of Guelatao who was orphaned young, worked as a shepherd and came to Oaxaca at twelve speaking only Zapotec. He became a lawyer, governor of Oaxaca and, in 1858, the first Indigenous president of Mexico, leading the country through civil war and the French invasion. The city added his name, becoming Oaxaca de Juárez, in 1872. Today Oaxaca is one of the most culturally rich cities in Mexico, famous for its food — mole, mezcal, chocolate and chapulines, or toasted grasshoppers — its crafts and its many Indigenous languages.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Monte Albán was one of the first cities in Mesoamerica, and one of the earliest to use writing and a calendar. Among its most mysterious monuments are the Danzantes, stone slabs carved with figures in contorted poses, once thought to be dancers but now believed to depict captured and sacrificed enemies. The site was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site, together with the historic centre of Oaxaca, in 1987.`,`Every year on 23 December, Oaxaca celebrates the Night of the Radishes, a festival that began in 1897, in which local farmers and artists carve giant radishes into elaborate scenes of nativities, saints, dancers and animals. The radishes, specially grown for the event, can be very large and oddly shaped. Thousands of people line up in the main square to see them before they wilt.`,`The Guelaguetza, held on two Mondays in July, has its roots in Indigenous traditions of giving and reciprocity, and in Catholic celebrations of the Virgin of Carmen. Today dancers from the eight regions of Oaxaca perform their traditional dances in colourful costumes, and at the end they throw gifts of fruit, bread and crafts to the audience. The festival draws visitors from across Mexico and abroad.`,`Oaxaca is the most ethnically diverse state in Mexico, home to sixteen officially recognised Indigenous peoples, including the Zapotecs, Mixtecs, Mazatecs and Mixes, who speak dozens of languages. Many villages around the city specialise in a particular craft: black pottery in San Bartolo Coyotepec, woven rugs in Teotitlán del Valle and brightly painted wooden figures called alebrijes in San Martín Tilcajete.`]},"san-cristobal-mx":{why:`At 16–17°N this basin should feel like lowland Chiapas heat. Elevation near 2200 m and a closed highland bowl reverse that: July afternoons often stay near 20–23 °C, while calm winter nights pool cold air on the floor until mornings brush frost. Cloud-forest ridges at 2400–2800 m wring Gulf and Pacific moisture into mist and a long wet season. The result is a cool, cloudy highland climate, not the Cuernavaca-style spring that tourism copy prefers.`,history:[`In the early hours of 1 January 1994, the day the North American Free Trade Agreement came into force, several thousand masked rebels, most of them Tzotzil and Tzeltal Maya, seized the town hall of San Cristóbal de las Casas and several other towns in the highlands of Chiapas. They called themselves the Zapatista Army of National Liberation, after the revolutionary hero Emiliano Zapata, and they demanded land, justice and rights for Mexico's Indigenous peoples. Their spokesman, a pipe-smoking masked man known as Subcomandante Marcos, became an international celebrity. After twelve days of fighting with the army, a ceasefire was declared, and the Zapatistas withdrew into the jungle and mountains, where they still govern their own autonomous communities.`,`The highland valley of Jovel, where San Cristóbal stands, has been home to the Tzotzil and Tzeltal Maya for centuries. In 1528 the Spanish conquistador Diego de Mazariegos founded a town here, and it became the colonial capital of Chiapas. In 1544 a Dominican friar named Bartolomé de las Casas became bishop of Chiapas. He had once been a colonist and slave owner himself, but he became the most famous defender of Indigenous peoples in the Spanish empire, denouncing the cruelty of the conquest and arguing for their rights before the king. The city later took his name. For centuries, San Cristóbal was a town of Spanish and mestizo landowners and merchants, surrounded by Maya villages whose people were forced to work on their estates.`,`In 1892 the state government moved to the warmer lowland city of Tuxtla Gutiérrez, and San Cristóbal became a quiet provincial town, its colonial churches and houses preserved. In the twentieth century, anthropologists and travellers came to study the Maya communities of the highlands, and after 1994 the Zapatista uprising drew activists, journalists and tourists from around the world. Today San Cristóbal is a lively city of cobblestone streets, colourful houses, markets and cafés, where Maya women in traditional dress sell textiles in the plazas.`],deepTitle:`Highland Maya`,deep:[`Na Bolom, a house in San Cristóbal, was the home of the Danish archaeologist Frans Blom and his Swiss wife, Gertrude Duby Blom, a journalist and photographer, from 1950. They devoted their lives to studying and defending the Lacandon Maya of the Chiapas rainforest and campaigning against the destruction of the jungle. Their house is now a museum and research centre, and guests can stay in its rooms.`,`In the Tzotzil village of San Juan Chamula, just outside San Cristóbal, the church is a centre of a unique blend of Catholic and Maya religion. There are no pews; the floor is covered with pine needles, and families kneel among hundreds of candles, praying in Tzotzil, sometimes with offerings of eggs, chickens and soft drinks. Taking photographs inside is strictly forbidden.`,`The Zapatistas' autonomous communities, known as caracoles, run their own schools, health clinics and courts, independent of the Mexican government. The movement inspired activists around the world with its rejection of globalisation and its slogans, such as a world where many worlds fit. Its relationship with the government remains tense.`,`San Cristóbal's market is one of the busiest in southern Mexico, where Maya traders from the surrounding villages sell vegetables, flowers, textiles and pottery. The weavers of the highlands produce brightly coloured fabrics, and each village has its own patterns, worn proudly by its women. The city also has museums of Maya medicine, textiles and amber, a fossil resin found in Chiapas.`]},"cuernavaca-mx":{why:`South-facing volcanic benches at about 1510 m sit below Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl yet above the true tierra caliente of Morelos. That elevation trims Mexico City’s chill while the slope aspect adds winter sun the higher basin does not get. Afternoons run warmer than the capital; nights still drop into the teens °C. Summer convection off the volcanoes rinses the air most evenings. The eternal-spring reputation is a slope-and-lapse-rate fact later turned into a slogan.`,history:[`On the Day of the Dead in 1938, a former British consul named Geoffrey Firmin drinks himself to destruction in a Mexican town called Quauhnahuac, under the shadow of two volcanoes. He is the hero of Malcolm Lowry's novel Under the Volcano, published in 1947 and now considered one of the great novels of the twentieth century. Quauhnahuac was Cuernavaca, where Lowry himself had lived, drinking heavily, in the late 1930s. Lowry was only one of many foreigners drawn to the city's mild climate, flowers and gardens; Cuernavaca, just south of Mexico City but some 700 m lower, has been a retreat for the powerful and the restless for five hundred years.`,`The Tlahuica people built a town here called Cuauhnáhuac, near the trees, in the fertile valleys on the southern slopes of the volcanoes, and the Aztecs later conquered it and collected tribute of cotton and other goods. After the Spanish conquest, Hernán Cortés claimed the region as his own estate and built a fortified palace in Cuernavaca in the 1520s, on top of a Tlahuica temple; he planted sugar cane, and the valley became a land of sugar plantations worked by enslaved Africans and Indigenous labourers. In the eighteenth century a silver magnate, José de la Borda, built lavish gardens in the town, and in 1803 the German scientist Alexander von Humboldt called it the city of eternal spring.`,`In the 1860s Emperor Maximilian, installed on the throne of Mexico by France, made Cuernavaca his favourite retreat, staying in the Borda gardens and building a country house. Fifty years later, in 1911, the peasant leader Emiliano Zapata issued his Plan de Ayala in the state of Morelos, demanding that the land of the haciendas be returned to the villages, and his armies fought for years in the mountains around Cuernavaca. In the twentieth century, wealthy residents of Mexico City and foreigners built weekend homes in the city, and it became famous for its language schools, gardens and swimming pools.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Palace of Cortés is one of the oldest surviving colonial buildings in the Americas. It now houses a museum of the history of Morelos, and its balcony is decorated with murals painted by Diego Rivera in 1929 and 1930, depicting the conquest, the sugar plantations and Zapata's revolution. The murals were commissioned by Dwight Morrow, the American ambassador to Mexico, as a gift to the people of Cuernavaca.`,`Emiliano Zapata, born in the village of Anenecuilco in Morelos in 1879, became one of the most famous leaders of the Mexican Revolution. His slogan, land and liberty, inspired peasants across Mexico. He was ambushed and killed in 1919 at the hacienda of Chinameca, but his ideas shaped Mexico's land reform, which broke up many of the great estates into communal farms called ejidos.`,`The Jardín Borda, built by the silver magnate José de la Borda and his son in the late eighteenth century, was a Baroque garden of fountains, terraces and an artificial lake. Maximilian and his wife Carlota stayed there often, and legend says Maximilian met a local woman, called La India Bonita, in the gardens. The garden is now a public park and cultural centre.`,`Cuernavaca's mild climate, with warm days and cool nights all year, comes from its position at about 1,500 m on the southern slopes of the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt, sheltered from cold northern air but high enough to avoid the heat of the lowlands. Its gardens grow bougainvillea, jacaranda and tropical fruit trees, and its many swimming pools and weekend houses make it a favourite escape for residents of Mexico City.`]},"valle-guadalupe-mx":{why:`Twenty-five kilometres inland from Ensenada, the valley still catches Pacific marine air through coastal gaps without living inside full upwelling fog. Granitic and alluvial soils drain fast. Summer afternoons run warm enough to ripen wine grapes; nights cool as the marine layer creeps up-valley. Annual rain is thin, on the order of a couple of hundred millimetres, so groundwater and drought—not temperature—set the ceiling. Mexicali’s interior heat and Ensenada’s fog belt both fail a few tens of kilometres away.`,immersive:`Morning air often arrives already cooled, a faint salt edge moving up the Guadalupe corridor while granite shoulders stay dry and bright. By afternoon the same benches can feel inland-hot, vines and olive rows radiating heat until the marine layer returns and outdoor tables become possible again. Winter storms are brief and precious; most of the year the light is hard, the ground pale, and dust lifts on the access roads. Fog rarely owns the day the way it does on the Ensenada waterfront, yet the valley is not desert-interior either. Water trucks, well talk, and fire season are part of ordinary weather, not background. Vintage nights can drop sharply. The climate works for grapes and olives because drainage and diurnal swing cooperate—and because someone is pumping an aquifer that does not refill on a Napa schedule.`,history:[`In 1905 about a hundred families of Russian religious dissenters called Molokans, who rejected the Orthodox Church and refused to serve in the tsar's army, arrived in this dry valley in Baja California after a long journey through Los Angeles. They had fled Russia to escape persecution and conscription, and the Mexican government sold them land in the Valle de Guadalupe. They built adobe houses and planted wheat, and some of them grew grapes. For decades the valley was known for its bearded Russian farmers, its samovars and its borscht, and their descendants still live there; a small museum in the village of Francisco Zarco tells their story. They were among the first to plant the vineyards that would one day make the valley Mexico's most famous wine country.`,`The Kumiai, or Kumeyaay, people lived in this granite valley for thousands of years, gathering acorns, agave and seeds and moving between the coast and the mountains with the seasons; their descendants still live in the community of San Antonio Necua. In 1834 Dominican friars founded the mission of Nuestra Señora de Guadalupe del Norte in the valley, the last mission founded in the Californias, and planted olives, fruit and grapevines with Kumiai labour. In 1840 a Kumiai leader named Jatñil, who had once worked with the friars, turned against them and led a revolt that forced the mission's abandonment.`,`Commercial winemaking in Baja California began at Santo Tomás, south of Ensenada, in 1888, and in the twentieth century Italian immigrant families, such as the Cettos, built large wineries producing brandy and table wine. In 1987 a group of winemakers founded Monte Xanic, which began producing high-quality bottled wines, and the valley's reputation grew. Today it has more than a hundred wineries, many of them small and family-run, and its restaurants, boutique hotels and annual harvest festival draw visitors from across Mexico and the United States. The valley's biggest challenge is water: its aquifers are overdrawn, and rain is scarce.`],deepTitle:`Molokans and vines`,deep:[`The Molokans got their name, which means milk-drinkers, because they drank milk during the Orthodox fasts, when it was forbidden. They lived simply, worshipped in plain meeting houses and refused to bear arms. In Mexico they kept their language and traditions for several generations, but many of their descendants later moved to the United States or married into Mexican families, and today only a few families still speak Russian.`,`The Valle de Guadalupe's climate is similar to that of the Mediterranean or California's wine country: hot, dry summers and mild, rainy winters. Cool breezes and fog from the Pacific, about 25 km away, flow into the valley through gaps in the hills, moderating the summer heat and allowing the grapes to keep their acidity. The valley grows a wide range of grapes, including Nebbiolo, Tempranillo and Cabernet Sauvignon.`,`The Kumiai of San Antonio Necua and other communities have kept alive their language and traditions, including basket weaving, pottery and songs. They have worked with researchers to record their language, which has very few remaining speakers, and to protect their rock art sites in the region. Kumiai territory once stretched from what is now San Diego to the mountains of northern Baja California.`,`The Fiestas de la Vendimia, or harvest festival, held each August since 1990, celebrates the grape harvest with wine tastings, concerts and dinners in the vineyards. The festival has helped turn the valley into one of the most popular food and wine destinations in Mexico, and the restaurants of chefs who pioneered Baja California's cuisine attract food lovers from around the world.`]},"patzcuaro-mx":{why:`A highland lake at about 2140 m sits inside the volcanic Meseta Tarasca, so lapse-rate coolness and water-body moderation work together. Daytime highs stay in the low-to-mid 20s °C; winter nights can fall to single digits. Afternoon wet-season storms nucleate on the rim and cross the water, then nights clear. Nearby Morelia is lower and more urban-warm; Uruapan drops further into avocado heat. The lake is the climate machine, not only the view.`,immersive:`Pátzcuaro's climate is shaped by altitude and by the great lake at its center. At about 2140 m, daytime highs hover in the low-to-mid 20s °C year-round; nights cool into the single digits in winter, low teens in summer. The wet season brings afternoon storms that build on the volcanic rim and roll across the water, then nights clear enough for a sweater on the plaza. Nearby Morelia is lower and more urban-warm; Uruapan drops further into avocado heat. The Meseta Tarasca — the Purépecha highlands — remains one of the most culturally continuous and climatically mild regions in Mexico. The lake's falling level is the quieter climate story: less water means less moderation, and the shoreline towns feel that before any plaza does.`,history:[`In the 1530s a Spanish judge and lawyer named Vasco de Quiroga, who had read Thomas More's Utopia, came to the shores of Lake Pátzcuaro and tried to put More's ideal society into practice. The region had been devastated by the conquest, and Quiroga, who became the first bishop of Michoacán, gathered the scattered Purépecha people into communities called hospital-towns, where they shared work and property, cared for the sick and elderly, and learned skills. He gave each village around the lake a craft of its own: copper working in Santa Clara del Cobre, guitar making in Paracho, lacquerware in Uruapan, pottery and weaving elsewhere. Nearly five hundred years later, many of these villages still practise the crafts he assigned them, and the Purépecha still call him Tata Vasco, Father Vasco.`,`The lake basin was the heart of the Purépecha, or Tarascan, empire, one of the most powerful states in ancient Mexico. Its capital, Tzintzuntzan, the place of hummingbirds, stood on the eastern shore of the lake, and its rulers built great stepped platforms called yácatas. In the 1470s the Purépecha defeated an invading Aztec army, one of the worst defeats the Aztecs ever suffered, and their empire was never conquered by the Aztecs. But it fell quickly to the Spanish, and in 1530 the conquistador Nuño de Guzmán, notorious for his cruelty, had the last Purépecha king, Tangáxuan II, tortured and executed.`,`Pátzcuaro grew into a colonial town of whitewashed adobe houses with red-tiled roofs, built around two large plazas, and it kept much of that character. In 1817, during the war of independence, a local woman named Gertrudis Bocanegra, who had supported the rebels, was executed by firing squad in the main plaza. Today the town and the villages around the lake are famous for their crafts, their food and, above all, their Day of the Dead celebrations, when thousands of people gather on the island of Janitzio and along the shores to watch families keep candlelit vigils in the cemeteries through the night.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Day of the Dead on Lake Pátzcuaro is one of the most famous in Mexico. On the night of 1 November, Purépecha families carry flowers, candles and food to the cemeteries and keep watch over the graves of their loved ones until dawn. The cemetery on the island of Janitzio, lit by thousands of candles, draws crowds of visitors, and the lake's fishermen paddle canoes holding their distinctive butterfly-shaped nets.`,`The fishermen of Lake Pátzcuaro once used large, delicate nets shaped like butterfly wings to catch the lake's famous white fish, pescado blanco. Pollution, overfishing and the introduction of other species have made the fish scarce, and the nets are now used mostly for demonstrations for tourists. The lake itself has shrunk and become polluted, and efforts are underway to restore it.`,`The Purépecha defeat of the Aztecs, in about 1478, came when the Aztec emperor Axayácatl invaded with a large army and was crushed near the Purépecha border. The two empires remained enemies until the Spanish conquest, and the Purépecha refused to help the Aztecs against Cortés. The Purépecha language, unrelated to any other language in Mexico, is still spoken by more than 100,000 people.`,`The Gertrudis Bocanegra Library in Pátzcuaro, housed in a former church, contains a huge mural painted by Juan O'Gorman in the early 1940s, depicting the history of the Purépecha and the region from ancient times to the Mexican Revolution. Bocanegra herself is a national heroine, and a statue of her stands in the plaza named after her.`]},"valle-de-bravo-mx":{why:`At about 1850 m, pine-oak slopes already cut lowland heat. The mid-century reservoir then added a local water body that damps daily swing near shore and feeds wet-season humidity. Volcanic rim geometry organizes afternoon convection. Colorines and lower outlets run warmer; Toluca’s higher basin is frostier. Weekend traffic is not climate, but slope aspect, fog on the dam, and storm cells on the rim are. The lake is engineered; the highland comfort is not.`,history:[`In September 1971 an estimated 200,000 young Mexicans converged on the woods near Avándaro, outside Valle de Bravo, for what was supposed to be a motor race with a music festival on the side. The Festival Rock y Ruedas de Avándaro became the Mexican Woodstock: two days of rock bands, rain, mud, long hair and freedom, which was broadcast live on the radio until the station cut off the feed. Conservative politicians and newspapers were scandalised, and the government, fearful of youth rebellion after the student massacre of 1968, effectively banned large rock concerts for years afterwards. Avándaro became a legend, a symbol of a generation of Mexican youth.`,`The valley was home to the Mazahua and Matlatzinca peoples, who farmed maize and beans on the slopes and in the river valley, and the Aztecs conquered the region in the 1470s. Around 1530 Franciscan friars founded a settlement here, and it grew into a quiet colonial town of adobe houses with red-tiled roofs, cobblestone streets and white churches, surrounded by pine forests and farmland. For centuries it lived on farming and timber.`,`In the 1940s the federal electricity commission built dams in the valley to generate power, flooding the farmland below the town and creating a large reservoir. The lake transformed Valle de Bravo: wealthy families from Mexico City built weekend houses on its shores, and it became a centre for sailing, water sports and paragliding. Today the reservoir is also part of the Cutzamala System, which supplies much of Mexico City's drinking water, and in recent droughts it has fallen to alarmingly low levels. Valle de Bravo was named a Pueblo Mágico, a magic town, in 2005.`],deepTitle:`Lake and monarchs`,deep:[`Each winter, millions of monarch butterflies migrate thousands of kilometres from Canada and the United States to the oyamel fir forests in the mountains of central Mexico, including the Piedra Herrada sanctuary near Valle de Bravo. They cluster in huge masses on the trees, turning the branches orange, and fly up in clouds when the sun warms them. The forests are protected, but logging and climate change threaten their habitat.`,`The rock outcrop called La Peña, rising above Valle de Bravo, offers a sweeping view over the town and the lake. Nearby hills, especially El Peñón, are among the best places in the world for paragliding and hang gliding, thanks to reliable thermals and winds, and the town has hosted international competitions.`,`The Cutzamala System pumps water from a series of reservoirs, including Valle de Bravo, up more than 1,000 m over the mountains to Mexico City. It is one of the largest water supply systems in the world. During the drought of 2023 and 2024, the reservoir at Valle de Bravo fell to less than a third of its capacity, exposing its muddy banks and raising fears for Mexico City's water supply.`,`Valle de Bravo's historic centre preserves its colonial character, with adobe houses, red-tiled roofs, wooden balconies and a market where Mazahua women sell crafts and food. The town's Day of the Dead and its festival of lights draw visitors, and its restaurants and galleries cater to the weekenders from Mexico City who make up much of its economy.`]},"zacatlan-de-las-manzanas-mx":{why:`The Sierra Norte de Puebla stands in the Gulf’s path. Elevation near 2040 m cools a tropical latitude into apple weather, while orographic lift wrings mist, drizzle, and a greener wet season than the drier Puebla basin. Nights drop enough for chill hours; days stay mild rather than hot. Fog and slope aspect decide which orchards work. Puebla city, a few hours away, is sunnier, dustier, and less cloud-forest. Dampness and landslide risk are the price of that green.`,immersive:`Zacatlán is one of those places a flat climate map undersells. It sits high enough, near 2040 m, for cool nights and apple culture, but close enough to the humid Gulf-facing mountains that fog, drizzle, and wet-season clouds keep the landscape green. Afternoons are mild, winters are cool rather than harsh, and the town has a lived orchard identity rather than just a pretty mountain label. Fog and slope aspect decide which rows pay; cider and the August fair are processing strategies for surplus and damaged fruit. Puebla city, a few hours away, is sunnier, dustier, and less cloud-forest. The tradeoff is dampness: landslides, cloudy stretches, and road-and-slope exposure are part of the climate, not an inconvenience on the way to the apples.`,seasons:{summer:`Cool, green and misty — afternoons near 24°C, nights around 12°C, and the rainy season in full swing, with moist air from the Gulf of Mexico climbing the Sierra Norte and wrapping the town in fog by afternoon, followed by heavy showers. The apple orchards are heavy with fruit by August, waterfalls thunder in the canyons, and the Apple Fair fills the town in mid-August.`},history:[`In 1909 a young man named Alberto Olvera Hernández, who had taught himself mechanics, built a clock by hand in his workshop in Zacatlán, a small apple-growing town high in the mountains of Puebla. It was the beginning of Relojes Centenario, which grew into one of the most important clock factories in Latin America, making the great clocks that tell time from church towers and town halls across Mexico. In 1986 the company built a giant floral clock, its face planted with flowers, in Zacatlán's main square, and it has become the town's emblem. The family still runs the factory, and visitors can tour its museum of clocks.`,`The mountains of the Sierra Norte de Puebla were home to the Totonac people, and later Nahua and Chichimec groups moved into the region; the town's name, Zacatlán, means place of grass in Nahuatl. After the Spanish conquest, the region's peoples were gathered into towns under Spanish control, and Franciscan friars built a monastery in Zacatlán in the sixteenth century, one of the oldest in the Americas. In the early seventeenth century, apple trees were brought to the town and found the cool, moist mountain climate ideal, and orchards spread across the slopes.`,`Zacatlán became famous for its apples, ciders and fruit wines, and the town added de las Manzanas, of the apples, to its name. The Apple Fair, held every August since 1941, celebrates the harvest with a parade and an apple queen. Zacatlán was also the birthplace of Luis Cabrera, a lawyer and politician who wrote much of the land reform law of the Mexican Revolution. It was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2011, and its misty canyons, waterfalls and orchards draw visitors from Puebla and Mexico City.`],deepTitle:`Apples and clocks`,deep:[`The Barranca de los Jilgueros, a deep canyon on the edge of Zacatlán, drops hundreds of metres below the town, and its walls are often filled with mist. A glass-floored viewing platform juts out over the edge, giving visitors dizzying views of the waterfalls below. On clear days, the canyon is a sea of green forest.`,`The Valle de Piedras Encimadas, about 25 km from Zacatlán, is a valley of huge rock formations, stacked boulders and pinnacles shaped by erosion over millions of years, surrounded by pine forest. Local legends tell of the rocks as petrified giants and animals, and the valley is a popular place for hiking and camping.`,`The Franciscan monastery of Zacatlán, founded in the 1560s, is one of the oldest surviving religious buildings in the Americas, its simple stone walls and arches still standing in the town centre. The Franciscans played a major role in converting the Indigenous peoples of the region, and many villages in the surrounding mountains still have strong Nahua and Totonac traditions.`,`Zacatlán's cool, humid climate comes from its location on the slopes facing the Gulf of Mexico. Moist air blowing inland from the Gulf rises up the mountains, cooling and forming fog and clouds, which bring rain to the slopes. The climate allows apples, pears and plums to grow at a latitude where most of Mexico is too warm for them.`]},"coatepec-mx":{why:`The windward Gulf slope of the Sierra Madre Oriental lifts moist air until it condenses as cloud and drizzle at about 1250 m. That is montane cloud forest weather: mild afternoons, soft nights, and rain totals far above the dry Mexican highlands. Coffee prefers exactly this band—cool enough for quality, wet enough to grow. Xalapa shares the mist; the Veracruz coastal plain below does not. When the cloud deck thins, the town feels ordinary subtropical. When it sits, the forest drips.`,immersive:`Cloud arrives as a temperature drop you feel on the skin before you see it, a grey lid sliding down from the Cofre de Perote massif and snagging on coffee shade. Streets glisten; tile roofs darken; the smell is wet leaf and roast, not dust. Summer afternoons can still reach the upper 20s °C, but the air is thick rather than searing, and thunder often spends itself on the slope before night. Winter mornings are cool enough for a sweater in the parque, rarely a freeze in town. Annual rain is heavy by highland Mexican standards, so mold, mosquitoes, and slick cobbles are ordinary. Sunbreaks feel earned. The coffee belt is a cloud machine with a town inside it.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, damp and grey — afternoons near 24°C on bright days but often cooler under low cloud, nights around 11°C, and the chipi-chipi, a fine misty drizzle that blows in with cold northern winds called nortes. The coffee harvest is in full swing, and the smell of roasting coffee fills the streets.`,summer:`Warm, humid and very wet — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 16°C, and heavy rain almost every afternoon during the rainy season, sometimes with tropical storms from the Gulf. The forests are lush and green, the rivers run high, and orchids bloom in the gardens.`},contrasts:[{label:`Xalapa (≈10 km N, ≈150 m higher)`,note:`The state capital, up the slope, is a little cooler and even mistier, known as the City of Flowers for its gardens.`},{label:`Veracruz port (≈90 km E, at sea level)`,note:`Down on the Gulf coast, the port city is hot and steamy all year, without the cool mountain air.`}],history:[`In 1984 the Hollywood film Romancing the Stone, starring Michael Douglas and Kathleen Turner, shot several of its jungle scenes near Coatepec, including a spectacular sequence at the Texolo waterfall in the nearby town of Xico, standing in for the jungles of Colombia. The film introduced the waterfalls, cloud forests and coffee plantations of this region to audiences around the world. But the region's real fame had come long before, from the coffee that grows on the misty slopes of the mountains between the Gulf of Mexico and the high volcanoes.`,`The name Coatepec means hill of the serpents in Nahuatl, and the area was home to the Totonac people before the Aztecs conquered it. After the Spanish conquest, the town was refounded as San Jerónimo Coatepec, and colonial estates grew sugar cane, tobacco and citrus on the fertile slopes. Around 1808 coffee plants brought from Cuba were planted in the area, and they flourished in the cool, misty climate of the cloud forest. By the late nineteenth century, large haciendas such as La Orduña were producing coffee for export, and Coatepec became known as one of Mexico's finest coffee towns.`,`Coatepec grew rich on coffee, building handsome houses with red-tiled roofs, balconies and courtyards. In the twentieth century, falling coffee prices and plant diseases hurt the industry, and many plantations were turned into pastures or orange groves. In 2012 and 2013 a coffee rust epidemic devastated the plantations. Today Coatepec remains a coffee town, with small farms producing shade-grown coffee beneath the trees of the cloud forest, and it was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2006. Its coffee shops, orchid gardens and colonial streets attract visitors from nearby Xalapa and beyond.`],deepTitle:`Coffee in the clouds`,deep:[`Shade-grown coffee, planted beneath the canopy of native trees, is one of the most environmentally friendly ways of growing coffee, preserving habitat for birds, orchids and other wildlife. The coffee farms around Coatepec form part of a band of cloud forest that is one of the most biologically diverse ecosystems in Mexico, home to hundreds of species of birds, many of them migrants from North America.`,`The jalapeño pepper takes its name from Xalapa, the capital of Veracruz, just north of Coatepec, where it was traded in the market. The peppers are grown in the warm lowlands of Veracruz, and they are often smoked and dried to make chipotles. Xalapa is also known for its university, its anthropology museum, which holds giant Olmec stone heads, and its gardens.`,`The Texolo waterfall, near the town of Xico, plunges into a deep, forested gorge, and a bridge across the gorge gives a spectacular view. Xico is also famous for its mole, a rich sauce made with chillies, chocolate and spices, and for its festival in July, when bulls run through the streets.`,`Coatepec's climate is shaped by its position on the slopes facing the Gulf. Moist air rises up the mountains, forming clouds and fog that keep the forests damp and the temperature mild all year. In winter, cold northern winds called nortes bring drizzle and chilly days, while in summer the rainy season brings heavy afternoon showers.`]},"alamos-mx":{why:`Foothills of the Sierra Madre Occidental sit at 406 m, low enough for hot pre-monsoon afternoons, yet they catch a reliable North American Monsoon pulse from July into September. Winters stay dry, bright, and mild by northern Mexican standards. Tropical deciduous forest greens almost overnight when the storms arrive, then browns again. That dry–wet flip, not a gentle spring, is the climate. Heat and drought are the constraints; the colonial stone is the shade strategy.`,immersive:`May and June can feel like a furnace in the arroyos, cicadas loud, the Sierra de Álamos a bare brown wall. Then monsoon cells stack over the foothills and the tropical deciduous forest leafs out in days: palo santo, amapa, and understory going from dust to shade. Afternoons in the wet months still run hot, but nights ease and the town’s portales hold cooler air. Winter days are the easy season—sun, dry streets, nights near 10 °C rather than frost. Birders work the green months; snowbirds work the dry ones. Dust, well depth, and a 37 °C afternoon are ordinary, not exceptions. The climate is a two-act year, and the architecture is a thermal mass response to the first act.`,feel:`Álamos feels like a sleepy colonial jewel tucked into the hills of southern Sonora. Its cobblestone streets, arcaded plaza and whitewashed mansions, built with silver from the surrounding mountains in the eighteenth century, sit at the edge of a tropical dry forest that turns lush green with the summer rains and grey and bare in the dry season. Winters are warm, sunny and dry — the reason many Americans and Canadians spend the season here — while late spring is scorching and summer brings thunderstorms.`,seasons:{winter:`The sweet season — afternoons near 27°C, nights cooling to about 9°C, and sunshine almost every day. The dry forest around the town is leafless and silvery, but the gardens bloom with bougainvillea. Winter visitors fill the town, and the Alfonso Ortiz Tirado music festival in late January draws crowds to the plaza.`,spring:`Hot and dry — afternoons climbing from about 30°C in March to over 36°C by late May, with almost no rain and the hills turning brown and dusty. The jumping beans that the region is famous for begin to stir in their seed pods, and residents retreat into shade during the hottest hours.`,summer:`Hot, humid and thundery — afternoons near 37°C, nights around 22°C, and the monsoon arriving in late June or early July, bringing towering thunderstorms over the Sierra Madre and torrential afternoon downpours. Within days the dry forest bursts into green, and the streams run again.`,autumn:`Green and gradually cooling — the last monsoon storms fade in September, and by November afternoons ease to about 30°C, with clear skies and pleasant evenings. The forest stays green until the dry season sets in, and the winter visitors begin to return.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the colonial mansions and arcaded plaza, birding in the tropical dry forest, the Alfonso Ortiz Tirado music festival in January and a warm, dry winter far from the crowds of the beach resorts.`,contrasts:[{label:`Navojoa (≈50 km W, on the coastal plain)`,note:`Down on the flat, irrigated plain, Navojoa is hotter and drier, without the hills and forest that shelter Álamos.`},{label:`Hermosillo (≈350 km NW, in the Sonoran Desert)`,note:`Further north in the desert, Hermosillo's summers are even hotter, among the hottest in Mexico, with much less rain.`}],history:[`On 8 April 1914, a girl named María de los Ángeles Félix Güereña was born on a ranch near Álamos, in the hills of Sonora. She grew up in the town, then moved to Guadalajara and Mexico City, where a film director spotted her on the street. As María Félix, she became the greatest star of the golden age of Mexican cinema in the 1940s and 1950s, famous for her beauty, her strong-willed characters and her independence, and was known simply as La Doña. She was painted by Diego Rivera, loved by the composer Agustín Lara and admired across the Spanish-speaking world. Álamos still celebrates its most famous daughter, and her childhood home is a museum.`,`The Mayo people lived along the river that bears their name, farming in the valleys and gathering in the dry forest, and the Yaqui lived to the north. In 1683 Spanish prospectors found rich silver veins in the hills, and a mining town grew up around them. Álamos became one of the richest towns in northwestern New Spain, and its mine owners built grand houses with thick adobe walls, high ceilings, courtyards and arched porches around the plaza. Mayo, Yaqui and other Indigenous workers, along with Spaniards and people of African descent, worked in the mines.`,`In the nineteenth century the silver began to run out, and wars and revolutions drove many families away; by the early twentieth century Álamos was a half-abandoned town of crumbling mansions. In the late 1940s Americans began buying and restoring the old houses, and the town became a winter haven for retirees from the United States and Canada. Álamos was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2005. Since 1984 it has hosted an annual music festival honouring Alfonso Ortiz Tirado, a doctor and celebrated tenor who was born in the town.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Álamos claims to be the jumping bean capital of the world. The beans are not beans at all, but seed capsules from a shrub that grows in the hills around the town, inhabited by the larva of a small moth. When the capsule is warmed, the larva inside twists and jerks, making the bean jump. Local people collect them in early summer and sell them around the world.`,`Alfonso Ortiz Tirado, born in Álamos in 1893, was a doctor who became one of the most famous tenors of his time, singing romantic songs on radio and in concerts across Latin America in the 1930s and 1940s. He used his earnings to fund medical work, including treating the painter Frida Kahlo. The music festival named after him brings orchestras, opera singers and folk musicians to the town each January.`,`The Mayo people, or Yoreme, still live in villages along the Mayo River near Álamos, keeping their language, ceremonies and dances, including the deer dance, in which a dancer wearing a deer's head mimics the movements of the animal. Their Easter celebrations, mixing Catholic and Indigenous traditions, draw visitors to the region.`,`The tropical dry forest around Álamos is the northernmost of its kind in the Americas, home to hundreds of species of plants and animals, including jaguars, which still roam the Sierra de Álamos. The forest is green for only a few months a year, during and after the summer rains, and spends the rest of the year leafless, waiting for the monsoon.`]},"todos-santos-mx":{why:`A freshwater oasis on Baja’s Pacific side sits in Sonoran Desert latitudes but next to a cool current. Upwelling and onshore flow trim summer peaks relative to the Sea of Cortez shore a short drive east. Winters are dry, bright, and mild. Rain is scarce; hurricanes and tropical storms are the wild cards, especially in late summer. Springs from the Sierra de la Laguna, not rainfall on site, explain the orchards. Drought is the baseline; the oasis is the exception.`,immersive:`Pacific air reaches the arroyo as a cooler, salt-tinged breeze while the Sierra de la Laguna holds a darker green line inland. Midday in town can still feel desert-sharp, glare off plaster, but the thermometer stays below the Cortez-side punishment, and nights drop into a sleepable range. Winter is the generous season: dry light, warm water for swimming if you accept Pacific chill, orchards working because someone is still irrigating. Late summer humidity and the hurricane calendar are the honest counterweight. When a storm comes, the oasis shows as arroyo, not postcard. Most days the story is simpler: desert light, a spring, and a wind that remembers the California Current.`,contrasts:[{label:`La Paz (≈75 km NE, on the Sea of Cortez)`,note:`On the sheltered gulf side of the peninsula, La Paz has hotter, more humid summers without the Pacific's cooling breeze.`},{label:`Cabo San Lucas (≈75 km S, at the tip of the peninsula)`,note:`Where the Pacific meets the Sea of Cortez, Cabo is a touch warmer in winter and more exposed to hurricanes.`}],history:[`For decades a small hotel on the main street of Todos Santos, the Hotel California, did a roaring trade with tourists convinced it was the inspiration for the Eagles' 1976 song of the same name. Visitors bought T-shirts and souvenirs and sang the chorus in the bar. The hotel, founded in 1947 by a Chinese immigrant and named Hotel California in 1950, had nothing to do with the song, which the band said was about the excesses of Los Angeles. In 2017 the Eagles sued the hotel's owners for trademark infringement, claiming they were misleading customers into thinking the band had endorsed it; the case was settled the following year. The rumour has never quite died, and the hotel remains one of the town's best-known landmarks.`,`Todos Santos grew up around a freshwater spring on the Pacific coast of the Baja California peninsula, one of the few reliable sources of water in the desert. The Guaycura and Pericú peoples gathered here, fishing and harvesting the fruit of the desert. In 1724 Jesuit missionaries established a farm at the spring to supply their mission at La Paz, and in 1733 founded the mission of Santa Rosa de las Palmas. A year later the Pericú rose in revolt against the missions, killing two Jesuit priests, and epidemics devastated the Indigenous population over the following decades.`,`In the nineteenth century the spring irrigated sugar-cane fields, and Todos Santos became a prosperous town with several sugar mills. When the spring weakened and sugar prices fell in the mid-twentieth century, the mills closed and the town declined. In the 1980s the paving of the highway from La Paz to Cabo San Lucas brought artists and surfers, who restored the old adobe houses and mill buildings and opened galleries and cafés. Todos Santos was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2006, and today it is a town of artists, surfers and organic farmers, with long empty beaches where sea turtles nest.`],deepTitle:`Oasis on the Tropic`,deep:[`The Tropic of Cancer crosses the Baja California peninsula just south of Todos Santos, and the town sits at the edge of the tropics, though its climate is cooled by the cold California Current offshore. The current keeps the Pacific coast's summers milder than the Sea of Cortez side, and the town is noticeably cooler than La Paz in summer.`,`The sugar mills of Todos Santos, called trapiches, crushed cane to make piloncillo, a dark brown sugar shaped into cones, which was shipped to the mainland. Some of the old mill buildings and chimneys still stand, and the town's historic centre preserves the brick and adobe houses built during the sugar boom.`,`The beaches around Todos Santos, including Playa Los Cerritos and San Pedrito, are popular with surfers, especially in winter, when big swells roll in from the North Pacific. Strong currents and powerful waves make many of the beaches dangerous for swimming. Volunteers protect the nests of leatherback and olive ridley sea turtles and release hatchlings into the sea.`,`The Pericú revolt of 1734 was one of the largest Indigenous uprisings in Baja California. The Pericú, angered by the missionaries' efforts to end their traditional customs, killed two Jesuit priests and attacked several missions, and the Spanish needed soldiers from the mainland to suppress the rebellion. Within a few decades, disease had nearly wiped out the Pericú and Guaycura peoples.`]},"tapalpa-mx":{why:`A volcanic plateau near 2020 m lifts western Jalisco out of the warm lakes basin. Pine-oak cover and elevation keep summer afternoons often in the low 20s °C, nights cool enough for frost on open flats. Guadalajara’s heat and Colima’s lower valleys do not reach this bench. Wet-season storms and dry-season sun are the two textures. Cabin climate still depends on aspect: a north-facing pine lot is not a cleared ridgetop. The plateau is a lapse-rate island in a generally warmer state.`,immersive:`Pine shade on the Sierra de Tapalpa cuts the glare that rules the lakes country below. Afternoons in town are walkable when Sayula’s basin is not; nights ask for a blanket even in summer, and winter can film the open campos with frost. Wet months turn the plateau loud with runoff—Salto del Nogal and the gullies running hard—then the dry season returns pale grass, dust, and weekend traffic from Guadalajara. Las Piedrotas sit in full wind, a reminder that forest and exposure are different climates a few minutes apart. Wood smoke on cold mornings is ordinary. The highland trick is simple: two kilometres of elevation and a pine lid, not a different latitude.`,contrasts:[{label:`The Sayula basin (≈20 km E, ≈650 m lower)`,note:`Down on the floor of the dry lake basin below the sierra, summers are much hotter and the land is dusty and salty.`},{label:`Guadalajara (≈130 km N, ≈450 m lower)`,note:`Mexico's second city is several degrees warmer, especially in the hot, dry spring before the rains.`}],history:[`The writer Juan Rulfo, whose novel Pedro Páramo, published in 1955, is considered one of the masterpieces of Latin American literature, was born in 1917 in this corner of southern Jalisco, and spent part of his childhood among the haciendas and villages of the sierra around Tapalpa. His family lost much of its land and several of its men in the violence of the Mexican Revolution and the Cristero War that followed, and the young Rulfo was sent to an orphanage. Pedro Páramo, a haunting tale of a man who goes looking for his father in a village of ghosts, drew on the landscapes and memories of this region. Gabriel García Márquez said that he could recite the whole book by heart.`,`The Sierra de Tapalpa, a high volcanic plateau covered in pine and oak forest, was the home of Otomí-speaking peoples, who were part of a lordship centred on the salt-rich lake of Sayula below. In the late fifteenth century, the people of the region fought the Purépecha empire in a war over the salt flats, known as the Salt War. After the Spanish conquest, the sierra's villages were placed under Spanish control, and cattle haciendas spread across its meadows. The town's church and plaza date from the colonial period, and in the nineteenth century a paper mill was built in a nearby valley; its ruins still stand.`,`For much of the twentieth century, Tapalpa was a quiet mountain village of white adobe houses with red-tiled roofs and wooden balconies, living on farming and cattle. As Guadalajara grew, its residents discovered the cool pine forests of the sierra, and Tapalpa became a weekend retreat, with cabins, restaurants and horseback rides. It was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2002. Its most famous landmark is Las Piedrotas, a valley of huge, rounded boulders scattered across a meadow, which have inspired many legends.`],deepTitle:`Pines and Piedrotas`,deep:[`Las Piedrotas, a few kilometres outside Tapalpa, are giant rounded rocks, some the size of houses, standing in a grassy valley. Geologists think they are the remains of volcanic rock eroded over millions of years. Local legends say they were thrown by giants or dropped by the devil, and visitors climb them and picnic in their shade.`,`The Salt War, fought in the 1480s and 1510s, pitted the Purépecha empire, which wanted control of the salt deposits of the Sayula basin, against the local lordships. Salt was a precious resource, used for preserving food and for trade across western Mexico. The people of the region fought off the Purépecha, keeping their independence until the Spanish arrived.`,`The waterfall of El Salto del Nogal, near Tapalpa, plunges more than 100 m into a forested canyon, one of the highest waterfalls in Jalisco. Hikers reach it by a steep trail, and it is at its most dramatic during the summer rainy season.`,`Tapalpa's cool climate comes from its altitude, about 2,000 m above sea level, which makes it much cooler than the lowlands around it. Its pine forests, mild days and cool nights draw visitors seeking relief from the heat of Guadalajara, especially in the hot, dry months of April and May, and in winter, frosts sometimes whiten the meadows.`]},"xilitla-mx":{why:`Windward Sierra Madre Oriental at 600 m still intercepts Gulf moisture hard enough to drip. Cloud forest, waterfalls, and 1500 mm-class rainfall follow. Afternoons can be warm-humid, nights mild; this is not a cool highland at 2,000 m. Orographic lift is the whole trick: the same latitude’s interior is far drier. Landslide and flood risk ride with the green. Las Pozas is a garden in that machine, not the machine itself.`,immersive:`Gulf air hits the Huasteca escarpment and turns into noise: water on leaves, water in gullies, water on the convent’s old stone. Xilitla’s streets stay slick when the interior plateau is dust. At 600 m the heat never fully leaves—summer afternoons in the low 30s °C are real—but cloud and shade cut the burn, and nights hold in the teens. Orchids, ferns, and coffee tell the same story as the ruined Augustinian fortress: this slope is wet enough to rot wood and grow almost anything. When a norte or a stalled tropical wave sits on the range, the forest drips for days. Sun is a break, not a default. Bring mold strategy along with the walking shoes.`,feel:`Xilitla feels like a dream lost in the jungle. Perched on the steep, green slopes of the Sierra Madre Oriental, where moist air from the Gulf of Mexico rises and condenses into clouds, the town is surrounded by cloud forest, coffee plantations and waterfalls. The air is warm and humid all year, mist drifts through the trees in the mornings, and heavy rains fall from late spring to autumn. Hidden in the forest nearby is one of the strangest creations in the world: a surrealist garden of concrete towers and staircases leading nowhere.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and misty — afternoons near 26°C on clear days, nights around 12°C, and periods of fog and drizzle brought by cold northern winds. The forest stays green, and the coffee harvest is under way on the slopes around town.`,spring:`Warm and gradually wetter — afternoons rising from about 29°C in March to 33°C by May, with the first heavy rains arriving in late spring. Orchids and bromeliads bloom in the forest, and the rivers are clear and blue.`,summer:`Hot, humid and very wet — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 20°C, and heavy rain most afternoons, sometimes with tropical storms from the Gulf. The waterfalls roar, the jungle is at its lushest, and the paths through Las Pozas are slick with moss.`,autumn:`Wet then clearing — September and October bring some of the heaviest rains of the year, with the risk of floods and landslides, before the weather cools and dries in November. The coffee berries ripen red on the bushes.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Las Pozas, Edward James's surreal concrete garden in the jungle, along with the turquoise rivers, waterfalls and swallow-filled pit caves of the Huasteca Potosina.`,residentFit:`It suits artists, coffee growers and nature lovers who want a small town in the cloud forest, and who accept heavy rains, muggy summers and a long, winding road to the nearest city.`,contrasts:[{label:`Ciudad Valles (≈90 km N, in the lowlands)`,note:`Down on the coastal plain, Ciudad Valles is hotter and drier, without the mist of the cloud forest.`},{label:`Jalpan de Serra (≈50 km SW, in the Sierra Gorda)`,note:`In a valley on the drier side of the mountains, Jalpan's climate is warmer and less rainy, with dry scrub on the hills.`}],history:[`In the 1940s an eccentric British millionaire named Edward James, who had been a patron of the surrealist painters Salvador Dalí and René Magritte, came to Mexico looking for orchids and found Xilitla, a small town in the cloud forest of the Huasteca. He bought a coffee plantation in a valley full of waterfalls and pools, and for nearly forty years, until his death in 1984, he spent much of his fortune building a surreal garden in the jungle. With local workers, he built concrete sculptures shaped like giant flowers, columns, arches, staircases that spiral into the sky and lead nowhere, and buildings with no walls. He called it Las Pozas, the pools, and he lived in a house in the town, surrounded by parrots, snakes and other animals.`,`The Teenek, or Huastec, people lived in the mountains and valleys of the Huasteca for centuries before the Aztecs reached the region, and Nahua peoples later settled in the area; the name Xilitla comes from Nahuatl. After the Spanish conquest, Augustinian friars arrived in 1537, and in the mid-sixteenth century they built a fortified convent in Xilitla, one of the oldest colonial buildings in the state of San Luis Potosí. The mountainous region remained remote, and its Teenek and Nahua communities kept much of their culture and language.`,`Through the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, coffee and citrus became the region's main crops, grown on the steep slopes around the town. After Edward James's death, Las Pozas fell into disrepair, until a foundation bought it in 2007 and began restoring it. In 2012 the Mexican government declared it an artistic monument, and it has become one of the most visited sites in the Huasteca. Today Xilitla is a Pueblo Mágico, a base for exploring the region's waterfalls, rivers and caves.`],deepTitle:`Surrealist jungle`,deep:[`Edward James was born in 1907 into one of the richest families in Britain, and rumoured to be an illegitimate grandson of King Edward VII. He funded the surrealist movement in the 1930s, buying works by Dalí and Magritte, who painted him in a famous portrait showing the back of his head reflected in a mirror. He sold much of his art collection to pay for Las Pozas.`,`Las Pozas covers about 30 hectares of forest, with more than thirty sculptures and structures built of reinforced concrete, many of them shaped like flowers, bamboo and other plants. Water from waterfalls flows through the garden into a series of natural pools. The concrete structures, overgrown with moss and vines, seem to blend into the forest.`,`The Sótano de las Golondrinas, the Cave of Swallows, about 40 km from Xilitla, is one of the deepest open-air pit caves in the world, a vertical shaft about 370 m deep. Every morning, thousands of white-collared swifts and green parakeets spiral out of the pit in a huge flock, and every evening they dive back in, a spectacle that draws visitors from around the world.`,`The Huasteca Potosina, the region around Xilitla, is famous for its turquoise rivers and waterfalls, such as Tamul, which plunges more than 100 m into a canyon, and the pools of Micos and Minas Viejas. The water's colour comes from limestone dissolved from the surrounding mountains.`]},"morelia-mx":{why:`The Guayangareo valley at about 1920 m on the western Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt sits in the classic Mexican highland band: afternoons often 24–28 °C, nights 7–13 °C, a June–September thunderstorm season, and a long dry-bright winter. Surrounding relief sharpens local variation, but the city itself is a basin climate, not a canyon microclimate like Guanajuato. Pátzcuaro’s lake sits higher and cooler; Uruapan drops toward avocado heat. Elevation plus subtropical latitude is the whole mechanism.`,immersive:`Pink cantera takes the dry-season sun and gives it back as a warm wall at dusk, while the air itself stays highland-thin and easy to walk. Winter nights bite enough for a jacket in the plaza; they do not bury the city in frost the way Toluca can. From June, towering clouds build over the volcanic rim and drop hard, short rains that leave the cathedral wet and the rest of the evening clear. Dust and brilliance rule the other eight months. Street trees and the aqueduct’s arches make shade, not a forest climate. You feel a capital’s traffic in the same envelope that makes outdoor dinners ordinary. The comfort is real. So is the dry-season thirst of a growing basin.`,feel:`Morelia feels like a city carved from rose-coloured stone. Its cathedral, palaces and arcaded plazas, built of pink volcanic cantera in the colonial era, glow in the clear highland light, and an old aqueduct of 253 arches marches into the centre. At nearly 2,000 m, the climate is mild all year: dry, sunny winters with cool nights, a hot, dusty spring, and a rainy summer when afternoon storms wash the streets and turn the surrounding hills green.`,seasons:{winter:`Dry, sunny and mild — afternoons near 25°C, nights cooling to about 6°C, and clear blue skies almost every day. Frosts occasionally touch the outskirts. It is the season for visiting the monarch butterfly sanctuaries to the east, where millions of butterflies spend the winter in the fir forests.`,spring:`Hot, dry and dusty — afternoons climbing to about 31°C by May, the warmest of the year, with hazy skies, jacaranda trees blooming purple in the plazas, and the countryside parched as everyone waits for the rains.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 13°C, and clouds building by midday into heavy afternoon thunderstorms, often with hail. The hills turn green, the air is fresh after the rains, and the evenings are cool.`,autumn:`Green and gentle — afternoons near 26°C, the rains easing in October, and clear, crisp days returning by November. The Morelia International Film Festival fills the city's theatres in October, and Day of the Dead brings candlelit vigils to the region's cemeteries.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the pink-stone cathedral and colonial centre, the aqueduct, the Morelia film festival in October and the monarch butterfly sanctuaries a few hours to the east in winter.`,residentFit:`It suits students, families, professionals and retirees who want a mild, eternal-spring climate in a beautiful colonial city with universities and good food, and who accept the security concerns that affect parts of Michoacán.`,contrasts:[{label:`Pátzcuaro (≈55 km SW, ≈220 m higher)`,note:`Up in the lake basin, Pátzcuaro is a little cooler, with chillier nights and frequent winter frosts.`},{label:`Uruapan (≈110 km W, ≈300 m lower)`,note:`On the wetter slopes descending toward the coast, Uruapan is warmer and much rainier, the heart of Mexico's avocado country.`}],history:[`In 1785 a terrible drought and frost destroyed the harvests of central Mexico, and famine spread across the region. In the city then called Valladolid, the bishop, Fray Antonio de San Miguel, decided to give the starving people work by paying them to rebuild the city's old aqueduct. The result was a magnificent stone aqueduct of 253 arches, about 1.7 km long, which carried water into the city until the early twentieth century and still stands as one of its landmarks. The bishop's project was an early example of public works used to relieve hunger, and it helped make Valladolid one of the most beautiful cities in New Spain.`,`The valley where Morelia stands was on the edge of the Purépecha empire, and there was no large Indigenous city on the site when the Spanish arrived. In 1541 the viceroy of New Spain ordered a new Spanish town founded here, called Valladolid, as a rival to Vasco de Quiroga's Pátzcuaro, and it became the capital of the province of Michoacán. Its wealthy Spanish families built churches, convents, palaces and the great cathedral, completed in 1744, all of pink stone. In 1765 a boy named José María Morelos was born in the city; he became a priest and, after Miguel Hidalgo's capture in 1811, the leader of the war for Mexican independence.`,`Valladolid was a centre of the independence movement: in 1809 conspirators met there to plan a revolt, and in October 1810, after Hidalgo's rebel army took the city, its intendant issued one of the first decrees abolishing slavery in the Americas. Morelos was captured and executed by the Spanish in 1815, and in 1828 the city was renamed Morelia in his honour. Today Morelia is the capital of Michoacán and a university city, and its historic centre, with more than a thousand historic buildings, was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1991.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`José María Morelos was the son of a carpenter and studied at the Colegio de San Nicolás in Valladolid, where Miguel Hidalgo was rector. After Hidalgo's execution, Morelos led the independence war for four years, winning many battles and calling a congress that declared Mexico's independence in 1813. His Sentiments of the Nation called for the abolition of slavery and the caste system. He was captured in 1815 and shot.`,`Morelia's cathedral, built between 1660 and 1744, combines Baroque, Herrerian and Neoclassical styles, and its twin towers stand nearly 67 m high. On Saturday evenings, the cathedral is lit up and fireworks are set off from its towers. The city's churches and convents house fine collections of colonial art.`,`Michoacán is the heart of the monarch butterfly's winter range. Each autumn millions of monarchs fly up to 4,500 km from Canada and the United States to the oyamel fir forests in the mountains east of Morelia, where they cluster on the trees until spring. The Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.`,`Morelia is famous for its sweets, especially ate, a firm fruit paste made from guava, quince and other fruits, and for its colonial-era candy shops. The Museo del Dulce displays traditional sweet-making, and the city's markets sell candied fruits, milk sweets and chongos zamoranos, a dessert of curdled milk in syrup.`]},"guanajuato-mx":{why:`A steep canyon at about 2000 m in the Sierra de Guanajuato creates block-by-block sun and shade that a flat Bajío city never sees. Elevation supplies the mild highland regime: dry most of the year, wet from June to September, afternoons often in the 20s °C. South-facing stairs bake; tunnel mouths stay cool. Continentality is modest compared with Zacatecas farther north. The geometry is the local climate. The mines explain why anyone built inside that geometry.`,immersive:`Shade and sun trade every few dozen steps. A callejón can hold last night’s cool air while the next landing, turned to the afternoon, radiates stored heat from painted walls. At 2000 m the sky is hard; dry-season dust hangs in the cañada until a summer cell dumps on the hills and the subterranean streets show why they exist. Nights drop into jumper weather most of the year. Stairs are the daily climate test as much as the thermometer: mobility, shade, and lungs. When the rain comes, you hear it first in the tunnels. The town is a highland mine camp folded into a ravine, and you feel the fold all day.`,feel:`Guanajuato feels like a city poured into a ravine. Its brightly painted houses climb the steep walls of a narrow canyon, linked by tiny alleys and stairways, and its main roads run through tunnels beneath the city where a river once flowed. At 2,000 m, the air is dry and the light brilliant; winters are mild and sunny, spring hot and dusty, and summer brings afternoon storms. Students, musicians and the ghosts of silver barons fill the plazas.`,seasons:{winter:`Dry, sunny and crisp — afternoons near 23°C, nights cooling to about 6°C, and clear skies nearly every day. The plazas fill with students and visitors in the afternoon sun, and the evenings call for a jacket.`,spring:`Hot and dry — afternoons climbing to about 30°C by May, the hills around the city brown and dusty, and jacarandas blooming purple. It is the warmest and driest time of the year, before the rains arrive.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 13°C, and thunderstorms building over the sierra by afternoon, sometimes sending torrents down the steep streets. The surrounding hills turn green, and the evenings are cool and fresh.`,autumn:`Mild and lively — afternoons near 25°C, the rains easing in October, and the city bursting with music and theatre during the Festival Internacional Cervantino, which draws artists and crowds from around the world.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the colourful houses climbing the canyon, the tunnels beneath the city, the Mummy Museum, the Alley of the Kiss and the Cervantino festival, which fills the city with music and theatre each October.`,residentFit:`It suits students, artists and cultural expats who love a compact, walkable university city with a mild climate, and who are happy to climb steep stairways and lanes every day.`,contrasts:[{label:`León (≈50 km W, ≈200 m lower)`,note:`Out on the open plains of the Bajío, the industrial city of León is a little warmer and windier, without the shelter of the canyon.`},{label:`San Miguel de Allende (≈55 km E)`,note:`On a hillside above a broad valley, San Miguel has a similar mild highland climate, slightly drier.`}],history:[`In 1865 the city of Guanajuato introduced a tax on graves: families had to pay a fee to keep their relatives buried in the municipal cemetery, and if they could not pay, the bodies were dug up. When workers exhumed the first bodies, they found that many had been naturally mummified by the dry soil and minerals of the ground, preserving their skin, hair and even clothes. The mummies were stored in a building at the cemetery, and by the early twentieth century people were paying to see them. Today the Museo de las Momias displays more than a hundred of them, including a mummified baby said to be the world's smallest mummy, and the American writer Ray Bradbury, who visited in 1945, was so horrified that he wrote a story about it.`,`The Otomí and Chichimec peoples lived in the region before the Spanish arrived. In the 1550s Spanish prospectors found rich silver deposits in the mountains, and a mining camp grew up in the narrow canyon. By the eighteenth century Guanajuato was one of the richest cities in the world: the La Valenciana mine, which began producing in the 1760s, was for a time the most productive silver mine on earth. Its owners built magnificent churches, including La Valenciana, with its gilded altarpieces, and grand houses lining the canyon.`,`On 28 September 1810, at the start of the war of independence, Miguel Hidalgo's rebel army attacked the Alhóndiga de Granaditas, a granary where the Spanish of Guanajuato had taken refuge. According to legend, a miner nicknamed El Pípila tied a stone slab to his back to protect himself from bullets and set fire to the door, letting the rebels storm in and massacre those inside. The following year, after Hidalgo and three other leaders were executed, their heads were hung in cages at the corners of the Alhóndiga, where they remained for ten years. The painter Diego Rivera was born in Guanajuato in 1886, and the city was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1988.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Guanajuato's underground streets were originally the channel of the Guanajuato River, which flowed through the canyon and flooded the city repeatedly. After a disastrous flood in 1905, the river was diverted, and its old tunnels were later turned into roads, which now carry most of the city's traffic beneath the historic centre.`,`The Callejón del Beso, the Alley of the Kiss, is so narrow that the balconies of houses on opposite sides almost touch. According to legend, two lovers, a rich young woman and a poor miner, kissed across the balconies, until her father discovered them and killed her. Couples who kiss on the third step of the alley are said to be guaranteed years of happiness.`,`The Festival Internacional Cervantino, founded in 1972, grew out of the tradition of students performing short plays by Miguel de Cervantes in the city's plazas. It is now one of the largest arts festivals in Latin America, with music, theatre and dance from around the world filling the city every October.`,`Diego Rivera, born in Guanajuato in 1886, became one of the most famous painters of the twentieth century, known for his murals of Mexican history and workers. His family left Guanajuato when he was a boy, but his childhood home is now a museum displaying his early works.`]},"zacatecas-mx":{why:`At about 2440 m, this interior mining ravine sits hundreds of metres above Bajío cities and farther from tropical moisture. Summer afternoons often stall in the low 20s °C; winter nights routinely frost, with hard freezes possible. Annual precipitation stays in a semi-arid band, near 400 mm. Continentality and elevation, not a lake or a marine layer, make the cool. Guadalupe’s flatter edge runs a little warmer. The Bufa is a wind and sun instrument as much as a landmark.`,immersive:`Thin air on the cañada is the first fact: stairs cost more, sun bites harder, and shade is a civic resource. Summer does not cook the stone the way Saltillo’s lower desert can; a 25 °C afternoon feels like the ceiling, not a pause before 35. Winter mornings can glaze the cathedral’s pink cantera with frost, and a norther makes the Bufa cruel. Dry-season light is mineral-sharp; the short wet season smells of dust turned to mud in the alleys. Nights are the reason blankets exist in Mexican hotels. You came for silver Baroque. You stay, if you stay, because the highland is genuinely cool.`,feel:`Zacatecas feels like a silver city clinging to the side of a mountain. Its pink-stone cathedral, churches and mansions fill a narrow ravine beneath the rocky crest of the Cerro de la Bufa, at nearly 2,500 m, one of the highest cities in Mexico. The air is thin and dry, the sun intense; summers are mild and stormy, winters cool and frosty, and the surrounding countryside is a high plateau of mesquite, cactus and wind.`,seasons:{winter:`Cool, dry and frosty — afternoons near 19°C, nights near 4°C and often dipping below freezing, with bright sunshine and occasional cold, windy spells. Snow falls on rare occasions. The plazas are sunny by day and chilly by night.`,spring:`Warm, dry and windy — afternoons climbing to about 27°C by May, with dust storms blowing off the plateau and the countryside parched. It is the warmest time of year, before the rains.`,summer:`Mild and stormy — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 12°C, and afternoon thunderstorms that sweep across the plateau, sometimes with hail. The surrounding fields of beans and maize turn green, and the city's festivals fill the streets.`,autumn:`Cool and clear — afternoons near 22°C, the rains ending in October, and crisp, clear nights. The first frosts arrive in November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the pink-stone cathedral, the cable car to the Cerro de la Bufa, the Mina El Edén silver mine, the mask museum and the history of Pancho Villa's great victory.`,residentFit:`It suits students, professionals and retirees who want a historic city with cool summers and crisp, sunny winters, and who can handle thin air, cold nights and a dry climate.`,contrasts:[{label:`Aguascalientes (≈120 km S, ≈550 m lower)`,note:`Lower on the plateau, Aguascalientes is several degrees warmer, with milder winter nights.`},{label:`Fresnillo (≈60 km NW)`,note:`Out on the high plain, the mining town of Fresnillo, home to one of the world's largest silver mines, is drier and windier.`}],history:[`On 23 June 1914, Pancho Villa's Division of the North, the most powerful army of the Mexican Revolution, attacked the federal troops of the dictator Victoriano Huerta, who had fortified the hills around Zacatecas. Villa's artillery pounded the Cerro de la Bufa, and his cavalry and infantry stormed the city from several directions. By evening the federal army had collapsed, and thousands of soldiers were killed, many of them shot as they fled. It was the bloodiest battle of the revolution, and it broke Huerta's regime, which fell within weeks. A museum on the summit of La Bufa now commemorates the battle, and a statue of Villa on horseback looks over the city.`,`The Zacatecos, a Chichimec people, lived on the high plains around the mountain the Spanish called La Bufa. In September 1546 a Spanish captain named Juan de Tolosa was shown silver ore at the foot of the mountain, and a rush of prospectors followed. Zacatecas quickly became one of the richest silver cities in the Spanish empire, and its silver was carried south along the Camino Real to Mexico City and across the oceans to Europe and China. The city's wealthy miners built a cathedral with one of the most elaborate Baroque façades in Mexico, completed in 1752, and a maze of churches and mansions in the narrow ravine.`,`Zacatecas continued to mine silver through the colonial era and independence, and its mines still produce today. The city has been its state's capital since the 1820s and grew into a university town in the twentieth century, and its historic centre, carved into the mountainside, was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1993. Today visitors can ride a cable car from one hill to another across the city, explore the tunnels of an old mine, and visit the museums of the brothers Pedro and Rafael Coronel, two painters whose collections of masks and art are among the finest in Mexico.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Mina El Edén, which operated from the sixteenth century until 1960, runs beneath the city, and visitors can ride a small train into its tunnels to see how silver was mined, often by Indigenous and enslaved workers in terrible conditions. Part of the mine was once turned into a nightclub, deep underground.`,`The cathedral of Zacatecas, built of pink stone between 1730 and 1752, is considered one of the finest examples of Mexican Baroque architecture. Its main façade is covered with intricate carvings of saints, angels, flowers and vines — a riot of stone that took more than twenty years to carve.`,`The Museo Rafael Coronel, housed in a former Franciscan convent, contains thousands of masks from across Mexico, used in Indigenous dances and festivals, along with puppets and pre-Hispanic pottery. Its founder, the painter Rafael Coronel, was married to Diego Rivera's daughter Ruth.`,`Zacatecas's cool, dry climate comes from its height, nearly 2,500 m above sea level, and its location on the high central plateau, far from the sea. It gets less rain than the cities further south, and its winters bring frosts that are rare in most of Mexico.`]},"saltillo-mx":{why:`A Chihuahuan basin at about 1600 m sits only tens of kilometres from Monterrey yet several degrees cooler on summer afternoons because of that extra elevation. Dry air and continentality mean hot days still happen, but nights and winters cool hard, with frost possible. Sierra Madre Oriental foothills at Arteaga wring a little more moisture and orchard weather. The livable pocket is elevation plus a spring. The surrounding desert is the default.`,feel:`Saltillo feels like Monterrey's cooler, drier older sibling. An hour's drive up the Sierra Madre Oriental from the steamy industrial capital of Nuevo León, the city sits at about 1,600 m in a high Chihuahuan Desert basin, and the climb buys it summer afternoons several degrees kinder and nights that actually cool off. The air is thin and dry, the light hard and bright, the hills around town khaki-coloured and studded with yucca and agave. Winters are sunny and crisp, with the odd frost, and the rain, what little there is, comes in late-summer thunderstorms.`,seasons:{winter:`Clear, dry and crisp — afternoons near 20°C, nights dropping to about 5°C, and frost on the coldest mornings, when a blast of northern air can spill over the plains. Most days are sunny and still; a sweater in the morning, shirtsleeves by lunch. The pine-covered Sierra de Arteaga just east of town sometimes wears a dusting of snow.`,spring:`Warm, dry and windy — afternoons climbing from about 25°C in March to 30°C by May, dust blowing off the desert on gusty days, and barely a drop of rain. The yuccas send up tall stalks of creamy white flowers, and the evenings stay pleasantly cool.`,summer:`Warm days and cool nights — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 16°C, and thunderstorms building over the mountains in the afternoons, bringing most of the year's scant rain in short, heavy bursts. Down in Monterrey it is several degrees hotter and sticky; up here the desert air dries the sweat as fast as it forms.`,autumn:`Mild and briefly green — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 11°C, and September the wettest month of the year, when the desert scrub flushes green. By November the skies have cleared and the first cold fronts arrive from the north.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Churrigueresque cathedral and colonial centre, the Museo del Desierto and its dinosaurs, sarapes and Saltillo pan de pulque, and the orchards and pine forests of the Sierra de Arteaga nearby.`,contrasts:[{label:`Monterrey (≈85 km E, ≈1,050 m lower)`,note:`Down on the plain beyond the mountains, Monterrey runs several degrees hotter on summer afternoons, with heavy, humid air that Saltillo's altitude escapes.`},{label:`Sierra de Arteaga (≈30 km E, ≈1,000 m higher)`,note:`Up in the pine and fir forests of the sierra, summers are cool, winters bring hard frosts and occasional snow, and the valleys are full of apple orchards.`}],history:[`On the morning of 22 February 1847, General Antonio López de Santa Anna, president of Mexico and veteran of the Alamo, arrived in the mountain pass of La Angostura, a few kilometres south of Saltillo, with an army of some 15,000 men he had marched across the desert from San Luis Potosí. Waiting for him at the hacienda of Buena Vista was the American general Zachary Taylor with fewer than 5,000. For two days the armies fought across the gullies and plateaus of the pass. Santa Anna's troops came close to breaking the American line, a battalion of Irish deserters called the San Patricios pounded the American lines with its cannon, and a young colonel named Jefferson Davis held a crucial position with his Mississippi riflemen. Then, on the second night, Santa Anna withdrew, claiming victory. Taylor, the hero of Buena Vista, rode the fame of it to the White House the following year.`,`Long before the armies came, Saltillo was a lonely Spanish outpost on the edge of Chichimeca country. Around 1577 a Portuguese-born captain named Alberto del Canto founded the Villa de Santiago del Saltillo beside a spring and a small waterfall, the salto that gave it its name, in a high desert roamed by nomadic hunter-gatherers who resisted the newcomers fiercely. The village barely survived until 1591, when the viceroy sent families of Tlaxcalans, the Indigenous allies of the Spanish conquest, to found a town of their own next door, San Esteban de Nueva Tlaxcala, with special privileges, including the right to bear arms and govern themselves. For more than two centuries Saltillo and San Esteban were twin towns separated by a single street, the Spanish one smaller than its Tlaxcalan neighbour.`,`The Tlaxcalans brought the weaving that gave the world the Saltillo sarape, a blanket woven with bright bands and a central diamond, and Saltillo's great annual fair on the royal road north became one of the busiest markets of northern New Spain. After independence the city was the capital of the vast state of Coahuila y Tejas, which meant that for a time Texas was governed from Saltillo. In 1913 Venustiano Carranza, governor of Coahuila, launched the revolution against the dictator Victoriano Huerta from the Hacienda de Guadalupe, some 100 km to the north. In the late twentieth century General Motors, Chrysler and their suppliers built huge plants around the city, and Saltillo became one of the centres of Mexico's car industry.`],deepTitle:`Sarapes and sierras`,deep:[`The San Patricio Battalion was made up mostly of Irish Catholic immigrants who had deserted from the American army, many of them angered by the anti-Catholic prejudice of their officers, and joined Mexico. At Buena Vista their artillery fought with distinction. Six months later, after their capture at the Battle of Churubusco, around fifty of them were hanged as deserters. In Mexico and Ireland they are still remembered as heroes.`,`Saltillo's cathedral of Santiago, built between 1745 and 1800, has one of the finest Churrigueresque façades in northern Mexico, its pink-grey stone carved with saints, shells and twisting columns. Its tower gives sweeping views across the city to the mountains. Nearby, the Museo del Desierto displays the dinosaur fossils, cacti and wildlife of the Chihuahuan Desert, and Coahuila is one of the richest dinosaur-hunting grounds in Mexico.`,`The Saltillo sarape, woven of wool in brilliant stripes, was the fashionable cloak of nineteenth-century Mexican horsemen, and its pattern became a symbol of Mexico itself. The city also gave its name to Saltillo tile, the unglazed terracotta floor tile made from local clay and dried in the desert sun, which became a staple of houses in the American Southwest.`,`The Sierra de Arteaga, east of the city, rises to more than 3,500 m and catches enough moisture from the Gulf of Mexico to support forests of pine and fir. Its valleys are full of apple orchards and weekend cabins for families from Saltillo and Monterrey, and in winter its peaks are among the few places in northeastern Mexico where snow falls regularly.`]},"cuauhtemoc-mx":{why:`Central Chihuahua’s plateau at about 2060 m is high, dry, and continental: strong sun, cold winters that can snow, and summers hot by day but cool at night. Chill hours and dry air suit apples once irrigation exists. Rain is modest, near 500 mm, so orchards are a groundwater and well story. The Sierra Tarahumara to the west is a different, more dissected climate. This is temperate-fruit weather in a Mexican latitude because elevation and winter cold say so.`,immersive:`Winter mornings on the plateau can steam at the mouth; frost lies on fence wire and the Sierra Tarahumara is a white-toothed wall to the west. Summer afternoons reach the 30s °C, then the dry air dumps the heat after dark so orchard rows go quiet and cold. Wind is a constant companion on the flats. When rain comes it is often a monsoon burst, not a long drizzle, and the fields answer in a week of green. Apple harvest smells like a northern crop in a Chihuahuan light. Dust, well talk, and a hard freeze risk are the texture. This is not a colonial patio climate. It is a high desert that learned to farm like a cold country.`,feel:`Cuauhtémoc feels like the Canadian prairie dropped onto a Mexican plateau. At about 2,060 m on the high plains of central Chihuahua, under an enormous sky, the city is surrounded by flat, rectangular fields of oats and maize, neat rows of apple trees, grain silos and farmhouses with tidy gardens, where farmers in denim overalls and straw hats speak Low German. The air is dry and the sun fierce, but the altitude brings cold: winter nights freeze hard, and even in summer, when afternoon thunderstorms roll across the plains, the evenings are cool.`,seasons:{winter:`Sunny, dry and cold at night — afternoons near 19°C, but nights falling to around freezing and well below it during cold snaps, with frost white on the fields and an occasional snowfall. The apple orchards are bare, the trees banking the winter chill they need to fruit.`,spring:`Warm, bone-dry and windy — afternoons climbing from about 24°C in March to 30°C by May, almost no rain at all, and dust blowing across the fields. Late frosts threaten the apple blossom, and farmers watch the skies anxiously.`,summer:`The monsoon season — afternoons near 30°C, nights cooling to about 15°C, and thunderstorms rolling in most afternoons from July, bringing nearly two-thirds of the year's rain. Hail is the great enemy of the orchards, which are covered with black netting, and the plains turn green.`,autumn:`Harvest time — afternoons near 26°C, nights cooling fast, the rains fading in September, and the apple harvest in full swing, with trucks loaded with fruit heading south. By November the first frosts return.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Mennonite colonies — their cheese, bakeries and museum — the apple orchards, and the road west into the Sierra Tarahumara and the Copper Canyon on the Chepe railway.`,contrasts:[{label:`Chihuahua City (≈85 km E, ≈600 m lower)`,note:`Down in the state capital, summers are noticeably hotter and the land is true desert, too dry for the orchards that thrive on the plateau.`},{label:`Creel (≈105 km SW, ≈300 m higher)`,note:`Up in the pine forests of the Sierra Tarahumara, Creel is colder and wetter, with regular winter snow and the canyons of the Barranca del Cobre beyond.`}],history:[`In March 1922 the first of a series of chartered trains pulled into a lonely station on the high plains of Chihuahua called San Antonio de los Arenales. Out climbed hundreds of Mennonite farmers from the Canadian prairies, the men in dark clothes and beards, the women in long dresses and headscarves, followed by their horses, cows, ploughs and wagons. They belonged to the Old Colony church, descendants of Dutch and German Anabaptists who had wandered from the Netherlands to Prussia, Russia and Canada in search of a place where they could live by their own rules. When Manitoba and Saskatchewan made English public schooling compulsory, they sent delegates south, and President Álvaro Obregón, eager to settle farmers on the land, promised them their own schools, freedom from military service and the right to run their own communities. Several thousand made the journey that decade.`,`The land they bought had belonged to the Zuloaga family's vast Hacienda Bustillos, in a basin where mestizo ranchers and tenant farmers had worked for generations, and the Rarámuri people of the Sierra Tarahumara to the west had long passed through. The first years were brutal: crops planted by a Canadian calendar failed in the dry spring, and the newcomers had to learn to sow with the summer rains. They built villages called campos, each with a number, laid out along a single street like the villages of their ancestors in Russia, and in 1927 the growing town beside them became the municipality of Cuauhtémoc, named after the last Aztec emperor.`,`The Mennonites prospered, becoming famous for their cheese and growing oats, beans and maize, and in the second half of the twentieth century, with deep wells and irrigation, they and their mestizo neighbours planted the orchards that made Chihuahua Mexico's leading apple-growing state. Cuauhtémoc grew into a busy commercial city, with a strip of Mennonite shops, dealerships and factories along the road to the colonies. Today the community is divided between the traditional, who still drive horse-drawn buggies and refuse rubber tyres, and the modern, who drive pickups. The wells have drained the aquifer, and conflicts over water with neighbouring farmers have flared into protests.`],deepTitle:`Mennonite orchards`,deep:[`The Mennonites take their name from Menno Simons, a sixteenth-century Dutch priest who joined the Anabaptists, Protestants who believed in adult baptism and refused to bear arms. Persecuted across Europe, they moved again and again, and the Old Colony Mennonites of Chihuahua still speak Plautdietsch, a Low German dialect from the Vistula delta, read the Bible in High German and learn Spanish as a third language.`,`Queso menonita, a mild, creamy cheese first made by the Mennonite dairies around Cuauhtémoc, is now sold across Mexico, and is so popular that the name has come to mean a style of cheese. The colonies' cheese factories, bakeries and farm shops are a draw for visitors, and the Mennonite Museum on the road to the campos shows how the first settlers lived.`,`Hail is such a threat to the apple orchards of the Chihuahua plateau that growers cover them with black anti-hail netting, and some have used hail cannons, machines that fire booming blasts of air into the clouds in the hope of breaking up the hailstones. Neighbouring farmers have accused the cannons of driving away the rain, and some towns have tried to ban them, though scientists find no evidence that they work either way.`,`Cuauhtémoc lies on the Chihuahua al Pacífico railway, the Chepe, one of the most spectacular train journeys in the world, which climbs from the plateau into the Sierra Tarahumara and winds through the Copper Canyon on its way to the Pacific coast. The canyons, deeper in places than the Grand Canyon, are home to the Rarámuri, famous for their long-distance running.`]},"real-catorce-mx":{why:`At 2756 m the Sierra de Catorce is a sky island in Chihuahuan steppe: thin air, cool days, winter nights near freezing, and little rain. Surrounding basins bake; the town does not. Continentality keeps skies clear and diurnal swing wide. The Ogarrio tunnel is access, not climate, but the mountain’s elevation lapse is why a desert state holds a cold town. Wirikuta below is hotter and spiritually central. The refuge is orographic.`,feel:`Real de Catorce feels like a silver boomtown that the world forgot on a mountaintop. At about 2,750 m in the Sierra de Catorce, reached only through a single-lane tunnel bored through the mountain, its cobbled streets climb between roofless stone mansions, an old mint and a great parish church, while far below the Chihuahuan Desert stretches flat and pale to the horizon. The air is thin, cool and very dry, the light dazzling and the night sky packed with stars. Days are mild all year, the nights cold, and the silence, broken by the clatter of horses' hooves, is the loudest thing in town.`,seasons:{winter:`Bright and chilly — afternoons near 17°C in the sun, but nights dropping to about 2°C, often below freezing, and wind whistling through the ruins. Skies are clear almost every day, and a dusting of snow is not unheard of. Bring a heavy coat for the evenings.`,spring:`Mild, dry and windy — afternoons reaching about 23°C by May, the warmest month, with dust blowing off the desert below and the cactus and yuccas flowering. It is dry and clear, second only to midwinter, and the stars at night are spectacular.`,summer:`Cool and stormy — afternoons near 23°C, nights around 9°C, and thunderstorms building over the sierra by afternoon, sometimes with hail. The mountainsides turn green, the desert below blooms after the rains, and the town escapes the heat of the lowlands entirely.`,autumn:`Crisp and clear — afternoons near 20°C, the last rains falling in September, and cold nights returning by November. Early October brings the pilgrimage of San Francisco, when tens of thousands of the faithful climb the mountain to the church.`},contrasts:[{label:`Matehuala (≈25 km E, ≈1,150 m lower)`,note:`Down in the plain below the sierra, the busy market town of Matehuala is hotter and drier, with hot summers and mild winters.`},{label:`Wirikuta desert floor (≈10 km W, ≈1,000 m lower)`,note:`On the flat, cactus-covered plain beneath the mountain, sacred to the Wixárika, days are much hotter and the desert more exposed to wind and sun.`}],history:[`In 2000 Brad Pitt and Julia Roberts came to a half-ruined silver-mining town high in the mountains of San Luis Potosí to film The Mexican, and the crews had to squeeze their trucks through a 2.3 km tunnel just wide enough for a single vehicle. The town's residents watched Hollywood transform their streets. It was not the first time Real de Catorce had been invaded by strangers. For centuries, the Wixárika, or Huichol, people have made pilgrimages here from the mountains of Jalisco and Nayarit, several hundred kilometres away, to Wirikuta, the sacred desert at the foot of the mountain where they believe the sun was born. There they gather hikuri, the peyote cactus, and climb the peak of Cerro Quemado to leave offerings.`,`The Guachichil, a nomadic Chichimec people, roamed the desert and resisted the Spanish for decades. In 1772 prospectors struck silver on the mountain, and within a few years thousands of miners had rushed in; the town was formally founded in 1779 as the Real de Minas de la Purísima Concepción de los Catorce. The name, say legends, came from fourteen soldiers, or fourteen bandits, killed in the mountains. The silver made it one of the richest mines in New Spain, and by the late nineteenth century Real de Catorce had perhaps 15,000 inhabitants, a cockfighting arena, a theatre, a mint and shops selling imported luxuries from Europe. In 1901 the Ogarrio tunnel was opened through the mountain to bring in machinery and haul out ore.`,`Then the price of silver collapsed, the Mexican Revolution swept through the region, and the mines closed. Within a few decades the population had fallen to a few hundred, and Real de Catorce became a ghost town, its mansions roofless and its streets empty. What kept it alive was faith: pilgrims kept coming to the church to venerate its statue of Saint Francis, and in the late twentieth century artists, hippies and travellers came too, some in search of peyote. The town was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2001, and today hotels and restaurants occupy its restored buildings, though many ruins remain. When a Canadian company acquired silver-mining concessions in Wirikuta around 2010, the Wixárika and their supporters launched a national campaign, and the mining plans were largely frozen.`],deepTitle:`Wirikuta's silver`,deep:[`The parish church of the Purísima Concepción is home to a statue of Saint Francis of Assisi, known affectionately as Panchito, believed to perform miracles. Its walls are covered with retablos, small paintings on tin offered by the faithful in thanks for miracles: survivors of car crashes, illnesses, shootings and migrations to the United States. On the saint's feast day, 4 October, tens of thousands of pilgrims fill the town.`,`Peyote, called hikuri by the Wixárika, is a small spineless cactus containing mescaline, a powerful hallucinogen. For the Wixárika it is a sacred plant, eaten during ceremonies to commune with the gods, and their pilgrimage to Wirikuta, on foot and by road, is one of the oldest surviving religious traditions in the Americas. Its collection by outsiders is illegal and deeply resented.`,`The Ogarrio tunnel, named after a family of mine owners, runs straight through the mountain. Traffic passes in one direction at a time, controlled by attendants with radios. Before it was opened, the only way into the town was a steep mule track over the mountain.`,`Old Willys jeeps, some dating from the 1950s, carry visitors down a steep, rocky track from Real de Catorce to the desert below and the old railway town of Estación de Catorce. Others ride on horseback up the mountain to the Pueblo Fantasma, the roofless ruins of a mining settlement, with the desert spread out beneath them.`]},"la-paz-mx":{why:`Bahía de La Paz faces the warm Gulf of California, so winter days stay near 25 °C and nights mild, while summer puts 35 °C afternoons together with rising humidity. Pacific-side Baja, including Todos Santos, stays cooler in the same months because of upwelling. Hurricane tracks from August into October are a real season, not a footnote. At 10 m elevation there is no lapse-rate rescue. The bay’s shelter helps boats; it does not cancel heat.`,immersive:`Winter light on the malecón is the easy Mexico people advertise: dry, bright, water in view, a sweater at night. By July the same bay feels close and hot; the Cortez does not cool like the Pacific, and the breeze can arrive already warm. Mangroves at the margins smell of salt mud at low tide. Storm season is a calendar you watch, not a rumor—choppy water, then the long wait on a forecast. Most years the rain still totals little, so dust and desalination talk never leave. Espíritu Santo sits on the horizon as a marine fact: clear water, sea lions, and a city that lives between desert and a warm gulf.`,feel:`La Paz feels like the desert running straight into a turquoise bay. The capital of Baja California Sur sits on a sheltered inlet of the Sea of Cortez, ringed by bare, rust-coloured mountains and cactus-studded hills, and its long seafront promenade, the malecón, faces west across the water, so the sun sets over the bay every evening. It almost never rains. Winters are warm, dry and glorious; summers are fiercely hot, tempered on good evenings by a cool southerly breeze the locals call the coromuel, and late summer and autumn bring the threat of hurricanes.`,seasons:{winter:`Near-perfect — afternoons near 26°C, nights around 12°C, almost no rain, and day after day of clear blue sky. Northwesterly winds sometimes whip the bay into whitecaps, and whale sharks gather in the plankton-rich water, drawing snorkellers from around the world.`,spring:`Hot and parched — afternoons climbing from about 29°C in March to 34°C by May, with almost no rain at all and the desert at its driest. The evening coromuel breeze starts to blow in from the Pacific, and the water clears to its most dazzling blue.`,summer:`Scorching — afternoons near 37°C, nights around 24°C, and the bay as warm as bathwater. The coromuel cools the evenings, and the town lives outdoors after dark, but by August the air grows humid and the first tropical storms can roll up from the south.`,autumn:`Hot and storm-watched — September is the wettest and most dangerous month, when hurricanes from the Pacific can bring a year's rain in a single night. By November afternoons ease to about 30°C, the skies clear and the snowbirds and sailors return.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for swimming with whale sharks in the bay, playing with sea lions at Espíritu Santo, kayaking and sailing in the Sea of Cortez, sunsets on the malecón and fish tacos.`,residentFit:`It suits snowbirds, sailors, divers, marine biologists and retirees who want a relaxed, safe seaside capital with glorious winters, and who accept blistering summers, scarce water and the risk of hurricanes.`,contrasts:[{label:`Todos Santos (≈75 km SW, on the Pacific coast)`,note:`Across the peninsula on the Pacific side, the cold California Current keeps Todos Santos several degrees cooler in summer, with fresher, foggier mornings.`},{label:`Cabo San Lucas (≈140 km S, at the tip of the peninsula)`,note:`At the meeting of the Pacific and the Sea of Cortez, Cabo has slightly milder summers but sits more squarely in the path of hurricanes.`}],history:[`In 1940 the novelist John Steinbeck sailed into the bay of La Paz aboard a sardine boat with his friend, the marine biologist Ed Ricketts, on a voyage to collect specimens in the Sea of Cortez. In the town, he heard a story about a poor Indian boy who found an enormous pearl, thought that it would make him rich and free, and discovered that it brought him only greed, violence and grief, until he threw it back into the sea. Steinbeck turned the tale into a novella, The Pearl, published in 1947, which has been read by generations of schoolchildren. The story touched something real. For four centuries the pearls of La Paz had drawn adventurers, merchants and divers to this desert bay, and made a few men rich at the expense of many others.`,`The Pericú and Guaycura peoples fished and gathered pearls in the bay long before Europeans arrived. In 1533 a Spanish mutineer named Fortún Ximénez landed here and was killed by the locals, but his surviving crew brought pearls back to the mainland, and in 1535 Hernán Cortés himself came to found a colony, which failed within two years for lack of food and water. The explorer Sebastián Vizcaíno named the bay La Paz, peace, in 1596, for the friendly welcome he received, and a Jesuit mission was finally founded in 1720, only to be destroyed in the great Pericú revolt of 1734. In 1830, after a hurricane damaged the old capital of Loreto, La Paz became the capital of the territory.`,`In November 1853 an American adventurer named William Walker, a filibuster who dreamed of carving out his own empire, captured La Paz with a small private army and proclaimed himself president of the Republic of Lower California. He soon moved his headquarters north, and within a few months his ragged force had been driven back across the border. Through the nineteenth century the pearl beds of the bay made fortunes, as divers in heavy copper helmets harvested the oysters, until overfishing and disease wiped them out by the 1940s. Baja California Sur became a state in 1974, with La Paz as its capital, and today the city lives on government, fishing and tourism, the pearls replaced by whale sharks, sea lions and the islands of the bay.`],deepTitle:`Pearls of the bay`,deep:[`In the early twentieth century a local businessman named Gastón Vives founded what is often called the world's first commercial pearl farm, on the island of Espíritu Santo, raising millions of pearl oysters in a system of canals and cages. The farm produced pearls for the markets of Europe, but it was destroyed during the Mexican Revolution, and the ruins of its canals can still be seen on the island.`,`Espíritu Santo, a short boat ride from La Paz, is a desert island of red cliffs, white beaches and turquoise coves, protected as part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site covering the islands of the Gulf of California. Visitors swim with a colony of playful sea lions at Los Islotes, and the explorer Jacques Cousteau is said to have called the Gulf the aquarium of the world.`,`The coromuel, the breeze that cools La Paz on spring and summer evenings, blows from the Pacific across the narrow neck of the peninsula. Legend says it is named after an English pirate, or a ship, called Cromwell, which used the wind to slip in and out of the bay. Without it, the summer nights would be far more oppressive.`,`On 1 October 1976 Hurricane Liza struck La Paz, and the earthen dam of El Cajoncito, above the city, burst, sending a wall of water and mud through the poor neighbourhoods built in the dry streambed below. Hundreds of people were killed, making it one of the deadliest disasters in the history of Baja California.`]},"puerto-escondido-mx":{why:`Oaxaca’s Pacific coast at 15–16°N is tropical wet–dry: sea-breeze afternoons in the low 30s °C, nights that barely cool, and a hard split between a bone-dry winter half and a convective, hurricane-tinged summer half. Warm water is year-round. Zicatela’s surf is bathymetry plus persistent swell and wind, not a climate classification. Inland Mixtec highlands are cooler; this strand is isothermal coast. Humidity, not a cold month, is the constraint. Puerto Vallarta, farther north on a tighter bay, is a different Pacific geometry.`,immersive:`Dry-season mornings start brown and sharp, the lagoon down, the hills the color of dust, the Pacific a relief you walk toward. By afternoon a sea breeze takes the edge off 32 °C without ever feeling highland. From May the same hills blacken with cloud and then explode green; streets steam, mosquitoes return, and a distant cyclone can rearrange the week. Water stays bath-warm. Nights in the dry months are the only time the air feels generous. Wet-season nights stay close. Fishermen still read the sky the way surfers read the buoys. The year has two textures, not four seasons, and both smell of salt.`,feel:`Puerto Escondido feels like a sleepy fishing village that has been taken over by surfers and never quite decided what it wants to be. On the hot Pacific coast of Oaxaca, a small bay of fishing boats and palm-thatched restaurants sits at one end of town, and at the other stretches Zicatela, a long, golden beach where enormous waves explode onto the sand with a roar that can be heard across town. It is hot every month of the year, with afternoons near 34°C. Winters are sunny and bone-dry, and from May the summer rains turn the brown hills behind the town a riotous green.`,seasons:{winter:`Hot, dry and glorious — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 18°C, the coolest of the year, and almost no rain from November to April. The sea is warm, the sky cloudless and the evenings perfect for dinner on the sand, and the town is at its busiest with visitors from North America and Europe.`,spring:`The hottest season — afternoons near 35°C, nights around 20°C, the hills brown and the air hazy with smoke from burning fields. Big swells from the Southern Hemisphere begin to arrive, and by late May the first thunderstorms crackle over the sierra.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 34°C, nights around 22°C, and heavy downpours most afternoons and evenings, especially in June and September. The jungle bursts into green, the humidity is thick, Zicatela's waves are at their biggest and most dangerous, and hurricanes are a real threat.`,autumn:`Wet then clearing — September and October bring some of the heaviest rains of the year and the peak of the hurricane season, before the skies clear in November and the dry season returns, with lush hills and warm water.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the giant waves of Zicatela, quieter swimming beaches, sea-turtle releases, the bioluminescent lagoon of Manialtepec and the cheap, laid-back surf town life.`,contrasts:[{label:`Oaxaca City (≈135 km N, ≈1,550 m higher)`,note:`Up in the central valleys beyond the Sierra Madre del Sur, Oaxaca enjoys a mild, spring-like highland climate with cool nights all year.`},{label:`Huatulco (≈100 km E, on the same coast)`,note:`Further along the coast, the planned resort of Huatulco has a similar hot climate but calmer, sheltered bays and drier scrub forest.`}],history:[`Sometime in the late 1960s, a few wandering American surfers bumped down a new highway along the Pacific coast of Oaxaca to a tiny fishing village and coffee port, where they found something extraordinary. At Zicatela beach, a long underwater sandbar caused the swells rolling in from the open Pacific to rise up and break in thick, hollow barrels that could tower several times the height of a man. They called it the Mexican Pipeline, after the famous wave in Hawaii. Word spread among surfers around the world, and the village began to fill with board-carrying travellers, palm-thatched cabañas and cheap restaurants. Today Zicatela is one of the most famous and feared beach breaks on earth, where the world's best surfers come to ride waves of 6 m high or more, and visitors can watch them from the sand.`,`The coast was part of the domain of the Mixtec kingdom of Tututepec, a powerful state inland that resisted the Aztecs, until the Spanish conquistador Pedro de Alvarado conquered it in 1522. The coast itself remained thinly settled for centuries, with fishing villages and small farming communities in the hills. The name, the hidden port, comes from a legend about a young woman named La Escondida, kidnapped by pirates, who escaped and hid in the forest behind the bay.`,`By the early twentieth century the bay had become a small port for shipping coffee grown on the plantations of the Sierra Madre del Sur, which was carried down to the coast by mule and loaded onto ships from canoes. When roads reached the coast in the 1960s, the port's trade died, and the surfers arrived. In 1970 the federal government expropriated hundreds of hectares for a planned resort that never became another Cancún, and today Puerto Escondido has grown into a busy, rambling town of fishermen, surfers, hotels and backpackers.`],deepTitle:`The Mexican Pipeline`,deep:[`Zicatela's waves are created by a deep underwater canyon offshore that funnels the energy of the Pacific swells onto the beach, where a shallow sandbar makes them rise and break with extraordinary force. The biggest swells arrive between May and October, from storms in the Southern Hemisphere and hurricanes to the south, and big-wave competitions are held here.`,`The Laguna de Manialtepec, west of Puerto Escondido, is a mangrove lagoon famous for its birds and its bioluminescence. On dark nights, plankton in the water glow blue-green when disturbed, so that swimmers and boats leave trails of light. Further west, the Lagunas de Chacahua National Park protects lagoons, mangroves and beaches where sea turtles nest.`,`The beach of La Escobilla, east of Puerto Escondido, is one of the most important nesting grounds in the world for the olive ridley sea turtle. During mass nesting events, called arribadas, hundreds of thousands of turtles come ashore over a few nights to lay their eggs in the sand. Nearby San Agustinillo was once the site of a slaughterhouse for turtles, until Mexico banned their capture in 1990.`,`In May 2022 Hurricane Agatha came ashore near Puerto Ángel, east of Puerto Escondido, as the strongest hurricane on record to make landfall on Mexico's Pacific coast in May. Its torrential rains caused floods and landslides in the mountains behind the coast, a reminder that the region's beauty comes with the risk of powerful storms.`]},"merida-mx":{why:`A flat limestone plateau a few metres above sea level has no orographic coolness. Winters are warm and dry; summers are hotter and humid with afternoon rains from May through October. Cenotes and caves swallow surface water, so hydrology is underground. Inland position weakens some hurricane winds relative to Cancún or the Campeche coast without removing the risk. Trade-wind dryness in winter is the comfort season. Karst, not elevation, is the local mechanism.`,immersive:`January in the centro is glare and a dry easterly, shirtsleeves at night, the limestone still giving back day’s heat from church walls built of older Maya stone. June turns the same streets into a wet-season machine: 34 °C afternoons, a hard shower, steam rising off the calzada. There is no hill to catch a breeze except the one you make with a fan. Cenote air, when you drop into it, is the only reliably cool indoor-outdoor climate. Hurricane weeks are a shutter-and-wait practice even this far inland. The year divides into drinkable evenings and endurance middays. Shade trees on Paseo de Montejo are infrastructure.`,feel:`Mérida feels like a colonial city slowly baking on a limestone griddle. Built on the flat, scrubby plain of the Yucatán Peninsula, with no mountains, rivers or lakes in sight, the White City is a grid of pastel houses, shady plazas, grand old mansions and horse-drawn carriages, where Maya is spoken in the markets and the water lies underground in cenotes. It is hot all year: winters are warm and mostly dry, spring is blistering, and the summer rains bring sticky, steaming afternoons. Life slows to a crawl at midday and comes alive in the evenings, when the plazas fill with music and dancing.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm and pleasant — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 17°C, and mostly dry, sunny weather, broken by cold fronts from the north, called nortes, which bring wind, a little rain and a couple of refreshingly cool days. It is the most comfortable time of year, and the busiest with visitors.`,spring:`Blistering — afternoons climbing to about 36°C by May, the hottest month, and heat waves that can push the thermometer past 40°C. The countryside is parched, the cenotes are the best place to be, and everyone who can escapes to the beach at Progreso.`,summer:`Hot, humid and stormy — afternoons near 34°C, nights around 23°C, and heavy thunderstorms most afternoons, followed by steamy evenings. The jungle around the city turns green, and the hurricane season is under way.`,autumn:`Steamy then easing — September and October are wet and humid, with the greatest risk of hurricanes, before the first nortes arrive in November, bringing drier, fresher weather and afternoons near 30°C.`},contrasts:[{label:`Progreso (≈35 km N, on the Gulf coast)`,note:`On the shore of the Gulf of Mexico, Mérida's beach town is cooled by sea breezes, a few degrees less brutal on summer afternoons.`},{label:`Valladolid (≈160 km E)`,note:`Deeper in the peninsula toward the Caribbean, Valladolid is a little wetter and greener, with forest closer to town and the ruins of Chichén Itzá nearby.`}],history:[`On 12 July 1562, in the town square of Maní, south of Mérida, a Franciscan friar named Diego de Landa staged a great ceremony to punish Maya nobles accused of secretly worshipping their old gods. Before the assembled crowd he burned thousands of sacred images and the painted hieroglyphic books of the Maya, which recorded their history, astronomy and religion. The Maya, Landa wrote, regretted it to an amazing degree and it caused them much affliction. Only four Maya books are known to have survived the conquest. Landa was sent back to Spain to face charges over his brutality, but he was cleared and returned as bishop of Yucatán. The great irony is that the account he wrote of Maya life and writing, including a garbled Maya alphabet, later became one of the keys used by scholars in the twentieth century to decipher the very script he had tried to destroy.`,`Mérida was founded on 6 January 1542 by the conquistador Francisco de Montejo the Younger on the site of the Maya city of T'Hó, whose stone pyramids reminded the Spaniards of the Roman ruins of Mérida in Spain. The Spaniards tore down the pyramids and used their stones to build the cathedral, one of the oldest on the American mainland, and the houses around the main square. For three centuries Mérida was the capital of a Spanish colony where the Maya worked the land under forced labour, and revolts were crushed; in 1761 the rebel leader Jacinto Canek was publicly executed in the main square. In 1847 the Maya rose in the Caste War, and in 1848 their armies came so close to Mérida that the city prepared to flee, when, the story goes, the Maya soldiers went home to plant their maize.`,`In the late nineteenth century Mérida grew rich on henequen, the fibre of an agave grown on vast plantations around the city, which was made into twine for the harvesting machines of the American Midwest. The fibre was called green gold, and the city was said to have more millionaires per head than anywhere in the world; its planters built French-style mansions along the Paseo de Montejo. The workers, mostly Maya, lived in near-slavery. In 1937 President Lázaro Cárdenas broke up the plantations, and synthetic fibres later destroyed the henequen trade. Today Mérida is one of the safest and fastest-growing cities in Mexico, popular with retirees and digital nomads, and Maya is still spoken in its streets.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Felipe Carrillo Puerto, the socialist governor of Yucatán in the early 1920s, gave land to Maya peasants, gave women the vote and promoted the Maya language. He fell in love with an American journalist, Alma Reed, and commissioned the song Peregrina for her, which is still one of the most beloved songs in Yucatán. They planned to marry, but in January 1924, during a military rebellion, Carrillo Puerto was captured and shot.`,`The Casa de Montejo, built in 1549 on the main square for the family of the conquistador, has a carved stone façade showing armoured Spanish soldiers standing on the heads of defeated figures, a vivid and unsettling image of conquest. It is one of the finest examples of colonial civil architecture in Mexico, and is now a museum.`,`The cenotes of Yucatán, natural wells formed when the limestone collapses into underground rivers, were the only source of fresh water for the Maya, and many were sacred. Around Mérida they form a great arc, called the Ring of Cenotes, which traces the buried rim of the Chicxulub crater, the scar left by the asteroid that struck here 66 million years ago and wiped out the dinosaurs.`,`In 1905 more than a thousand Korean immigrants arrived in Yucatán to work on the henequen plantations, recruited with promises of good pay but bound by contracts that left them in near-slavery. By the time their contracts ended, Korea had fallen under Japanese rule, and few could go home. Many of their descendants still live in Yucatán, and a small museum in Mérida tells their story.`]},"bacalar-mx":{why:`A long freshwater lagoon on Yucatán’s Caribbean slope sits in tropical wet–dry weather with little annual temperature range. Trade winds in the dry months keep humidity tolerable; wet-season storms and hurricane remnants arrive from either coast. Karst water chemistry, not dye, makes the color banding. Stromatolites and a shallow basin store heat. Chetumal’s bay is saltier and more urban; the reef coast is saltier still. Fresh water at 18°N is the local exception.`,immersive:`Morning on the lagoon is a sheet of stacked blues, almost still, the town a thin limestone edge. Air stays warm at night; a dry-season easterly is what makes the heat usable. After a wet-season cell the surface chops, the jetty smells of rain on warm stone, and the seven colors go milky until the silt drops. There is no hill and no cold month. Shade, water, and a fan are the toolkit. Hurricane weeks empty the malecón and fill the radio. When the weather is ordinary, the lagoon is the air conditioner—narrow, local, and only as healthy as the septic tanks and mangrove fringe around it.`,feel:`Bacalar feels like a sleepy village beside a lagoon painted in impossible blues. On the southern edge of the Yucatán Peninsula, near the border with Belize, a long, narrow lagoon of fresh water shimmers in bands of turquoise, cobalt and ink, so clear that the white limestone bottom shines through. Wooden docks, hammocks and palm-thatched huts line its shore, and an old Spanish fort guards the town on its low bluff. It is hot and humid every month of the year, with afternoons above 30°C, but the trade winds keep the dry season comfortable, and the summer rains arrive in thunderous bursts.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm, breezy and mostly dry — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 19°C, and a steady trade wind rippling the lagoon. Occasional cold fronts from the north bring cloud and showers, but most days are bright and the water is at its clearest.`,spring:`Hot and increasingly humid — afternoons climbing to about 34°C by April and May, the lagoon still and glassy in the mornings, and the first heavy showers arriving in late May. It is the peak of the heat, best spent floating in the water.`,summer:`Hot, sticky and very wet — afternoons near 34°C, nights around 23°C, and thunderstorms rolling in most afternoons, sometimes clouding the lagoon with runoff from the surrounding jungle. The forest is lush and loud with birds and insects.`,autumn:`Wet and stormy — September and October bring heavy rains and the peak of the hurricane season, which can drive storms in from the Caribbean. By November the rains ease, the trade winds return and the lagoon clears again.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Lagoon of Seven Colours, kayaking and paddleboarding at dawn, the stromatolites, Cenote Azul, the Fuerte de San Felipe and a slower, quieter alternative to the Riviera Maya.`,residentFit:`It suits tropical lovers, small hotel owners and remote workers who want a lakeside village with warm weather all year, and who accept humidity, mosquitoes, hurricanes and the strain tourism puts on the lagoon.`,contrasts:[{label:`Chetumal (≈25 km S, on the Bay of Chetumal)`,note:`The state capital, on a shallow bay of the Caribbean at the Belize border, has a similar hot climate with sea breezes, and a long history of hurricane damage.`},{label:`Mahahual (≈70 km E, on the Caribbean coast)`,note:`Out on the open Caribbean, the fishing village of Mahahual is a little cooler and breezier, with the coral of the Mesoamerican Reef just offshore.`}],history:[`In February 1858, in the middle of the long war that the Maya of Yucatán had launched against the white and mestizo population, an army of Maya rebels stormed the fort of San Felipe at Bacalar and took the town. The rebels were followers of the Talking Cross, a cross in the jungle town of Chan Santa Cruz that was said to speak to its believers and give them orders. Many of the town's surviving residents were taken prisoner. When British envoys came from Belize, across the border, with money to ransom the captives, the Maya commander refused the deal, and most of the prisoners were killed. The Maya rebels held Bacalar and the lagoon for decades, and the war did not officially end until the Mexican army took Chan Santa Cruz in 1901.`,`The Maya called the place Bakhalal, which is often translated as surrounded by reeds, and its lagoon was part of a canoe route linking the Caribbean coast with the interior of the peninsula. The Spanish founded a town here, Salamanca de Bacalar, in the 1540s, but it was so isolated that it barely survived, and in the seventeenth century it was repeatedly attacked by pirates and finally abandoned. In the 1720s and 1730s the Spanish rebuilt the town and constructed the Fuerte de San Felipe, a small stone fort with a moat and bastions, to guard against pirates and the British logwood cutters who had settled in what is now Belize.`,`After the Caste War, Bacalar was a quiet village in the jungle, living on timber, chicle and fishing, in the remote territory of Quintana Roo, which became a state only in 1974. In the late twentieth century, travellers began to discover its lagoon, and it was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2006. Today hotels, hostels and kayak tours line the shore, and since 2024 a station on the Tren Maya has brought even more visitors, raising fears for the fragile lagoon.`],deepTitle:`Seven-colour lagoon`,deep:[`The Laguna de Bacalar, more than 40 km long, is famous for its seven colours, which come from the varying depth of the water over a bottom of white limestone sand. It is also home to some of the largest freshwater stromatolites in the world: rock-like structures built up over thousands of years by microbes, related to some of the oldest life forms on earth. Boat traffic, pollution and runoff threaten them.`,`The Cenote Azul, just south of the town, is a huge circular sinkhole about 90 m deep, filled with deep blue water and fringed by jungle, while the Cenote Negro, inside the lagoon itself, plunges to a similar depth. In 2020 heavy rains from tropical storms washed sediment into the lagoon and turned its famous blues a murky brown for months, a warning of how fragile it is.`,`Gonzalo Guerrero, a Spanish sailor shipwrecked off the coast of Yucatán in 1511, was enslaved by the Maya, but he won his freedom, married the daughter of a Maya lord at Chetumal, south of Bacalar, and became a war leader. When Cortés's expedition offered to rescue him in 1519, he refused to leave his wife and children, and he is said to have died fighting the Spanish. In Mexico he is honoured as the father of the first mestizo family.`,`The Fuerte de San Felipe, completed in the 1730s, is now a museum of the history of the region, with exhibits on the pirates who once raided the coast, the logwood trade and the Caste War. From its walls there are sweeping views over the lagoon, especially at sunset.`]}},l={"tequila-mx":{why:`Blue agave concentrates on volcanic slopes around Volcán de Tequila, where mineral-rich soils, warm days, cool nights, and a summer monsoon let the plant store sugars over a multi-year cycle. Elevation near 1,180 m strips tropical heat without pushing the crop into regular frost. That envelope is why the fields occupy a band between the volcano and the Río Grande de Santiago, and why the same plant fails at meaningful scale only a short drive into wetter or cooler country.`,feel:`Tequila feels like a whole landscape devoted to a single plant. On the slopes of an extinct volcano in western Jalisco, row after row of spiky blue-grey agave marches across red volcanic soil to the horizon, and the cobbled town at the centre smells of roasting agave hearts from its distillery chimneys. At about 1,180 m, the days are warm to hot and the nights cool; winter and spring are bone-dry and brilliant, and from June the summer rains roll in, turning the hillsides between the agave rows a vivid green.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm, dry and golden — afternoons near 28°C, nights cooling to about 10°C, and clear skies almost every day. It is harvest season in the fields, where the jimadores cut the agave in the cool of the morning, and the best time to walk among the rows.`,spring:`Hot and parched — afternoons climbing from about 32°C in March to 35°C by May, almost no rain, and the hills around the agave fields brown and dusty, with the volcano hazy behind. Distillery courtyards and shady plazas are the places to be by midday.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 18°C, and thunderstorms building over the volcano most afternoons, bringing nearly two-thirds of the year's rain. The landscape turns lush green, the air smells of wet earth and cooking agave, and rivers rush through the barrancas.`,autumn:`Green and gently warm — afternoons near 30°C, the rains tapering through October, and clear, fresh days returning by November, when the fields are at their most beautiful, the blue agave set against green grass and the volcano sharp on the horizon.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the blue agave fields, distillery tours and tastings at Cuervo, Sauza and smaller houses, the tequila train from Guadalajara and the cobbled streets of a UNESCO-listed landscape.`,residentFit:`It suits agave growers, distillery workers and families tied to the tequila trade, and newcomers who want small-town life in a warm, sunny valley within easy reach of Guadalajara.`,contrasts:[{label:`Guadalajara (≈55 km SE, ≈380 m higher)`,note:`Up on the Atemajac plateau, Jalisco's capital is a touch milder, with slightly cooler afternoons in the hot spring months.`},{label:`Volcán de Tequila summit (≈10 km S, ≈1,700 m higher)`,note:`Up on the flanks of the volcano, pine and oak forest replaces the agave, and nights are cold enough for frost in winter.`}],history:[`In 1795 King Carlos IV of Spain granted a man named José María Guadalupe de Cuervo the first official licence to produce vino mezcal, a spirit distilled from the roasted hearts of the blue agave, in the town of Tequila. The Spanish crown had spent decades trying to suppress the drink, which competed with imported Spanish brandy, but it was impossible to stop: the people of the region had been making it, legally or not, for generations. Cuervo's family had been granted land for agave fields in 1758, and the company still bearing their name became one of the largest spirit makers in the world. Over the next century the drink from Tequila became so famous that it took the town's name, and by the 1870s a rival, Cenobio Sauza, was shipping it across the border to the United States.`,`The people of this valley had been fermenting agave and using its fibres for thousands of years. Some two thousand years ago, the people of the Teuchitlán tradition built remarkable circular temples, called guachimontones, in the hills to the south, and buried their dead in deep shaft tombs. After the Spanish conquest in the 1530s, Franciscan friars founded the town of Santiago de Tequila at the foot of the volcano. The Spanish brought the copper still, and Indigenous knowledge of cooking and fermenting agave combined with European distilling to produce a new drink. By the seventeenth century, large plantations of agave were being planted around the town.`,`In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries the haciendas of Tequila grew into great distilleries, with brick ovens, stone mills and tall chimneys. In 1974 Mexico declared a denomination of origin, which means that only spirit made from blue agave in Jalisco and a few neighbouring regions may legally be called tequila. In 2006 UNESCO declared the agave landscape and the old distilleries of Tequila a World Heritage Site, and the town, named a Pueblo Mágico in 2003, now receives hundreds of thousands of visitors a year, many arriving from Guadalajara on a tequila train.`],deepTitle:`Blue agave country`,deep:[`The blue agave takes six to eight years to mature, storing sugars in its heart, or piña, which can weigh as much as a grown man. When the plant is ready, a skilled worker called a jimador cuts away the long spiky leaves with a sharp, round-bladed tool called a coa. The piñas are slowly roasted in ovens, crushed, fermented and distilled twice to make tequila.`,`The Guachimontones, near the town of Teuchitlán, are unlike any other ancient sites in Mexico: concentric circles of platforms around a stepped central pyramid, thought to have been used for ceremonies in which a priest climbed a pole at the centre. They were rediscovered by the American archaeologist Phil Weigand, who spent decades studying them.`,`The blue agave's botanical name, Agave tequilana, was published in 1902 by the French botanist Frédéric Weber, whose name follows it as the author. Agave fields can be harvested only once, as the plant dies when its heart is cut, and growing plants from cuttings of a single variety has left the crop vulnerable to disease, prompting scientists to seek more genetically diverse plants.`,`Tequila's climate, with warm days, cool nights, a long dry season and summer rains, suits the agave perfectly. The plant stores water in its thick leaves to survive the dry months, and the volcanic soil, rich in minerals and iron, drains quickly. Growers say the soil and altitude give each region's tequila a different flavour, sweeter from the highlands and earthier from the valley around the town.`]},"mazamitla-mx":{why:`At 2,220 m on a subtropical latitude, Mazamitla is a lapse-rate town: summer afternoons stay in the mid-20s °C while winter nights can approach freezing. The Sierra del Tigre pine-oak canopy shades ridges that would otherwise feel like southern Jalisco’s hotter valleys. Rainfall near 900 mm arrives mainly in summer, feeding waterfalls and also saturating cabin roads. Coolness here is not a Swiss import; it is elevation doing ordinary thermodynamic work above the Lake Chapala basin.`,immersive:`Pine shade on the Sierra del Tigre ridges is the first climate fact in Mazamitla, not a slogan. At 2,220 m, summer afternoons rarely push past the mid-20s °C, and winter nights can near freezing under clear highland air. The Cwb pattern is a wet-summer, dry-winter highland: about 900 mm of rain, concentrated when afternoon storms build over the forest. Cabin belts sit in pockets where sun, drainage, and road access disagree within a few hundred metres. Valle de Juárez, not far below, is warmer and more agricultural, which is the contrast that makes the pine belt feel like a refuge rather than a postcard. Fire weather in the late dry season and saturated slopes in the wet months are the practical limits.`,seasons:{winter:`Cool, dry and clear — afternoons near 23°C in the sun, but nights dropping to about 4°C, sometimes to freezing, so cabin fireplaces are lit every evening and the pine forest smells of woodsmoke. Skies are blue nearly every day.`,spring:`Dry, bright and the warmest season — afternoons reaching about 28°C by April and May, the forest floor crunchy with pine needles and the risk of wildfires high. The nights stay cool, and visitors come up from Guadalajara to escape the lowland heat.`,summer:`Wet, green and misty — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 12°C, and heavy rain most afternoons, with mist curling through the pines afterwards. The waterfall of El Salto runs full, wild mushrooms sprout in the forest, and the mountain roads turn muddy.`,autumn:`Fresh and golden — afternoons near 24°C, the rains easing through October, and crisp, clear days and chilly nights by November, when the forest is still green and the weekenders return to their cabins.`},contrasts:[{label:`Lake Chapala shore (≈30 km N, ≈700 m lower)`,note:`Down by Mexico's largest lake, the climate is several degrees warmer, with mild winters and no frost.`},{label:`Tamazula de Gordiano (≈35 km SW, ≈1,100 m lower)`,note:`In the hot valley below the sierra, sugar cane grows in a tropical climate, with hot, humid summers far removed from Mazamitla's pine-scented chill.`}],history:[`In 1522 a Spanish captain named Cristóbal de Olid, one of Hernán Cortés's most trusted lieutenants, led an expedition west from the newly conquered Aztec capital into the mountains of what is now southern Jalisco, where Nahua lords paid tribute to the town of Tzapotlán. The highland villages fell under Spanish control, and in 1537 a royal decree named this one San Cristóbal Mazamitla. Olid did not live to see it: in 1524 Cortés sent him to conquer Honduras, where Olid declared himself independent, and his former comrades captured him and cut off his head. The sierra he had marched through stayed quiet, forested and remote for the next four hundred years.`,`The name Mazamitla comes from Nahuatl and is usually translated as the place of deer and arrows, recalling its hunters. The Nahua people lived here from at least the twelfth century, on a frontier between their own lordships and the expanding Purépecha empire, which fought for control of the salt flats of the Sayula basin to the north. In the colonial era, the people of the sierra lived in adobe houses with steep roofs of wooden shingles, called tejamanil, cut from the forest, and lived by farming, logging and raising cattle.`,`For much of the twentieth century, Mazamitla was a small farming and timber town, reached by rough roads. As highways improved and Guadalajara boomed, its residents discovered the cool pine forests of the Sierra del Tigre, and the town became a weekend escape, with log cabins, restaurants and horseback rides. Its steep roofs, wooden balconies and pine-covered hills earned it the nickname the Switzerland of Mexico, and it was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2005. The growth has brought problems too: forest cleared for cabins, traffic jams on weekends and strains on the town's water.`],deepTitle:`Mexico's Switzerland`,deep:[`The parish church of San Cristóbal, on Mazamitla's main square, is one of the most unusual in Mexico, with towers topped by curving, tiered roofs that give it the look of a Chinese pagoda. Its white walls and dark roofs match the rest of the town, where the town has worked to keep its houses in the traditional style.`,`The waterfall of El Salto, a short walk from town through the forest of the Arroyo Encantado, plunges into a pool surrounded by ferns and pines. It is at its most spectacular in the rainy season, when the stream swells with runoff from the summer storms, and almost disappears by the end of the dry season.`,`Mazamitla's cool climate comes from its height, about 2,200 m above sea level, in the Sierra del Tigre, a range that rises steeply from the hot valleys of southern Jalisco. The pine and oak forests catch the summer rains, and the dry winter brings cold nights with occasional frost.`,`The town is known for its fruit preserves and sweets, made with fruits from the surrounding orchards, and for ponche, a hot fruit punch sold on cold evenings. Its cheese, cured meats and pan de leña, bread baked in wood-fired ovens, are sold in the markets around the plaza.`]},"la-ventosa-mx":{why:`Across the Isthmus of Tehuantepec the continental ranges flatten to a low pass, and Caribbean high pressure against a relatively lower Pacific forces air through that gap. From roughly October through February the Tehuano can hold at 40–70 km/h for days, with gusts that stop trucks. That geometry is one of a handful of major gap-wind regimes on Earth, kin to Papagayo and Panama. It also strips moisture, which is why this lowland is tropical-dry while much of upland Oaxaca stays wetter. Elevation near 40 m offers no lapse-rate rescue. The wind is the climate.`,feel:`La Ventosa feels like living inside a wind tunnel. The name means simply the windy one, and it is no exaggeration: this small town in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec, where the mountains of Mexico dip to their lowest point, sits in the path of a northerly gale, the Tehuano, that pours through the gap from the Gulf of Mexico to the Pacific for days at a time. Trees grow bent and stunted, dust and plastic bags fly past, trucks are sometimes blown off the highway, and around the town stand hundreds of tall white wind turbines, their blades turning almost constantly. It is hot all year, dry and windy in winter, and wet in summer.`,seasons:{winter:`Hot, dry and blasting — afternoons near 31°C, nights around 19°C, and the Tehuano blowing hard for days at a stretch, with gusts strong enough to topple trucks. Skies are clear, the air is dusty, and almost no rain falls. The wind turbines spin at full speed.`,spring:`The hottest season — afternoons climbing to about 35°C by May, nights warm, and the wind gradually easing as the season turns. The land is parched and brown, and the first thunderstorms arrive in late May.`,summer:`Hot, humid and stormy — afternoons near 34°C, nights around 23°C, and heavy afternoon thunderstorms, especially in June and September. The wind drops, the humidity rises, and the countryside turns green.`,autumn:`Wet then windy — September and October bring heavy rains and the risk of tropical storms, before the first northerly gales roar back through the gap in late October, drying the land and blowing away the humidity.`},residentFit:`It suits wind-farm technicians and the Zapotec farming families who have lived in the Isthmus for generations, and few others: the near-constant wind, heat and dust make it a hard place to settle for comfort.`,history:[`On 23 January 1907, President Porfirio Díaz rode a train across the Isthmus of Tehuantepec to open a railway that, he hoped, would make Mexico the crossroads of world trade. Here, between the Gulf of Mexico and the Pacific, the continent narrows to about 200 km, and the mountains drop to a pass only about 250 m high. For centuries dreamers had imagined a canal or a railway across it; in the 1880s the American engineer James Eads even proposed carrying ocean-going ships across on a railway. Díaz's railway, built by a British firm, was briefly one of the busiest in the world, carrying sugar from Hawaii and goods between the oceans. Then, in 1914, the Panama Canal opened, and the trade vanished almost overnight.`,`The Isthmus has been home for centuries to the Zapotec people, who call themselves the Binnizá, and to the Ikoots, or Huave, who fish in the lagoons along the Pacific coast. The Zapotec towns of Juchitán and Tehuantepec are famous for their markets, run by women in brightly embroidered blouses and long skirts, the costume that the painter Frida Kahlo made famous around the world. After the Revolution, agrarian reform gave much of the land to communal farms called ejidos, and the people of the Isthmus have long been known for their fierce independence.`,`The wind that had always plagued the Isthmus became its newest resource. In 1994 the national electricity commission built a small pilot wind farm at La Venta, and after studies in the early 2000s confirmed that the gap had some of the best wind in the world, companies raced to lease land. Since 2009 dozens of wind farms have been built around La Ventosa, making the region one of the largest concentrations of wind power in Latin America. Some landowners welcomed the rent; others protested that the contracts were unfair and that turbines had been planted near their homes without proper consultation, and the conflicts continue. In 2017 a powerful earthquake devastated the Zapotec city of Juchitán nearby.`],deepTitle:`The Tehuano wind`,deep:[`The Tehuano blows when cold, high-pressure air spreads south across the Gulf of Mexico behind cold fronts from North America and finds the gap in the mountains at Tehuantepec. Squeezed through the pass, the wind accelerates and roars out over the Pacific, where it can churn up the sea for hundreds of kilometres offshore, a hazard for ships and small boats.`,`The women of the Isthmus, the Tehuanas, wear a costume of a short blouse, called a huipil, and a long, flowing skirt, both richly embroidered with flowers, with gold coin jewellery for festivals. Frida Kahlo adopted the dress as a symbol of Mexican identity and wore it in many of her self-portraits. The Zapotec towns hold lavish festivals, called velas, with dances, parades and feasts.`,`Juchitán is also famous for its muxes, people who are born male but live as women or as a third gender, accepted for generations by Zapotec society. They play important roles in the family, in crafts and in festivals, and their annual Vela of the Intrepid Seekers of Danger draws thousands of visitors.`,`Since 2023 a new Interoceanic Railway has again linked the Pacific port of Salina Cruz with Coatzacoalcos on the Gulf, part of a government plan to turn the Isthmus into a trade corridor to rival the Panama Canal, more than a century after Díaz's railway faded.`]},"creel-mx":{why:`A pine-oak plateau at 2,338 m sits almost on top of subtropical canyon floors more than 1,800 m below. January afternoons on the rim can feel mild while nights drop well below freezing in dry, thin air; light snow is ordinary enough to surprise travelers who packed for tropical Mexico. A few tens of kilometres horizontally, Batopilas stays in citrus country. That vertical collapse, not a state-line, is the climate fact.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold and brilliantly clear — afternoons near 20°C in the thin, sunny air, but nights dropping to around freezing and often well below it, with light snow most winters dusting the pines and the rooftops. A few hours' drive below, the canyon floors are warm enough for bananas.`,spring:`Dry, windy and warming — afternoons climbing from about 23°C in March to 28°C by May, the forest dry and the risk of wildfire high. The last frosts linger into April, and the canyons below are already baking.`,summer:`The monsoon — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 11°C, and thunderstorms building over the sierra most afternoons from July, bringing most of the year's rain. The plateau turns green, waterfalls swell, and the Rarámuri plant their maize.`,autumn:`Crisp and golden — afternoons near 25°C, the rains fading in September, and clear, cold nights returning by November. The oaks turn, the air is clean and the views across the canyons are at their sharpest.`},history:[`In 2009 an American journalist named Christopher McDougall published Born to Run, a book about the Rarámuri, or Tarahumara, people of the canyons around Creel, who could run hundreds of kilometres through the mountains in sandals made of old tyres. At its heart was a race, organised in 2006 by a reclusive American runner called Micah True, known as Caballo Blanco, the White Horse, in the canyon town of Urique, in which Rarámuri runners competed against some of the best ultramarathon runners in the United States. The book became a worldwide bestseller, and sent a wave of runners in search of the Rarámuri and their secrets. The Rarámuri themselves had been running long before anyone wrote about it, in races that last a day and a night, kicking a wooden ball across the mountains.`,`The Rarámuri have lived in the Sierra Tarahumara for centuries, moving with the seasons between the cold plateau, where they plant maize in summer, and the warm canyon floors, where they shelter in winter. Jesuit missionaries arrived in the seventeenth century, and the Rarámuri rebelled several times against the missions and the Spanish mines. In April 1907 the tracks of the Kansas City, Mexico and Orient Railway reached this high plateau, and a station was opened, named after Enrique Creel, the governor of Chihuahua and an investor in the railway, one of the richest and most powerful men in the state. In 1906 Creel's government passed a law that claimed to civilise the Rarámuri but opened their lands to colonists and timber companies.`,`The railway took decades more to finish. In 1961 the Chihuahua al Pacífico railway, the Chepe, was finally completed through the canyons to the Pacific coast, crossing 37 bridges and passing through 86 tunnels, and Creel became the gateway to the Copper Canyon. Logging and tourism became the town's main industries, and today Creel is a busy town of hotels, craft shops and tour companies, where Rarámuri women in colourful dresses sell baskets and dolls in the streets.`],deepTitle:`Canyon gateway`,deep:[`The Copper Canyon is a system of six major canyons carved by rivers into the volcanic rock of the Sierra Madre Occidental. Several are deeper than the Grand Canyon, and the Urique canyon drops almost 1,900 m from rim to river. Because the canyons are so deep, their floors have a subtropical climate where oranges, mangoes and bananas grow, while the rims above are covered with pine forests and snow.`,`Arthur Stilwell, the American businessman who founded the Kansas City, Mexico and Orient Railway, dreamed of a route from Kansas City to the Pacific through the Sierra Madre. He claimed to receive his business advice from spirits, which he called brownies, who appeared to him in dreams. His railway went bankrupt long before it reached the Pacific.`,`Batopilas, a silver-mining town at the bottom of one of the canyons, reached by a hair-raising road from Creel, was once run by Alexander Shepherd, a former governor of Washington, DC, who moved there in 1880 to manage the silver mines and lived there until his death in 1902. He built the town's first hydroelectric plant, and the ruins of his mansion still stand by the river.`,`Around Creel, the Rarámuri lands are dotted with strange rock formations, such as the Valley of the Monks, tall pinnacles of eroded volcanic rock, and the Valley of the Frogs and Mushrooms. Lake Arareko, a short drive from town, is surrounded by pine forest and rocks, and the waterfall of Cusárare plunges into a canyon nearby.`]},"ensenada-mx":{why:`The California Current’s cold upwelling reaches this far south, so summer highs on the Ensenada waterfront stay near the mid-20s °C under a marine layer that often burns off by noon. Thirty kilometres inland, Valle de Guadalupe jumps to hotter afternoons and cooler nights, the diurnal swing wine grapes need. Winter rain is modest, on the order of 200 mm in town, and summers are nearly rainless. Nowhere else in Mexico stacks a cool-summer Pacific strip against a dry-farmed wine valley this cleanly.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, fresh and the rainy season, such as it is — afternoons near 21°C, nights dropping to about 5°C, and Pacific storms that bring most of the year's meagre rain and turn the brown hills briefly green. Gray whales pass offshore on their way south, and clear days after a storm are crisp and sparkling.`,spring:`Bright, breezy and greening — afternoons near 24°C, cool nights, wildflowers on the chaparral slopes, and grey mornings of marine fog in May and June that burn off by midday. The vines in the valleys inland burst into leaf.`,summer:`Dry and sunny — afternoons near 30°C away from the water, a few degrees cooler on the breezy waterfront, nights around 12°C, and almost no rain from June to September. Morning fog often hugs the bay, and inland in the wine valleys it is hotter still. Wildfire is the season's danger.`,autumn:`Warm and golden — afternoons near 27°C, the grape harvest under way in the valleys, and the occasional hot, dry Santa Ana wind blowing from the desert to the sea. The first rains usually return in November.`},history:[`In October 1930 a grand Spanish-style hotel and casino opened on the waterfront of a sleepy Mexican fishing port about 100 km south of the American border. The Hotel Playa Ensenada was built to lure Americans who, under Prohibition, could not drink legally at home, and its public face was the former heavyweight boxing champion Jack Dempsey. Hollywood stars came down to drink and gamble in its ballrooms, and legend says that a young dancer named Margarita Cansino, later known as Rita Hayworth, performed there. But Prohibition ended in 1933, and in 1935 President Lázaro Cárdenas banned casino gambling in Mexico. The hotel soon closed, and today, renamed the Riviera del Pacífico, it is a cultural centre with gardens, a museum and a bar where, some claim, the margarita was invented.`,`The Kumiai, or Kumeyaay, people lived along this coast for thousands of years, fishing, gathering shellfish and acorns, and moving between the shore and the mountains. In 1542 the navigator Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo, sailing for Spain, anchored in the bay, and in 1602 the explorer Sebastián Vizcaíno named it the Ensenada de Todos Santos, the bay of All Saints. Dominican missionaries built missions in the valleys inland, but the bay itself remained a ranch for most of the next three centuries. In the 1870s gold was discovered in the mountains to the east, and a port grew up to serve the mines.`,`On 15 May 1882 Ensenada became the capital of the northern territory of Baja California, and an American company, followed by a British one, bought vast tracts of land and tried to sell lots to settlers. The capital moved to Mexicali in 1915, and for a time Ensenada declined. What revived it was its American neighbours, first the thirsty visitors of Prohibition, and then tourists, sport fishermen and cruise ships. Today Ensenada is a busy port and fishing city, the gateway to the vineyards of the Valle de Guadalupe, and the starting line for the Baja 1000, one of the world's most famous off-road races.`],deepTitle:`Casino by the sea`,deep:[`Hussong's Cantina, founded in 1892 by a German immigrant named Johann Hussong, claims to be the oldest cantina in the Californias, and it too claims to be the birthplace of the margarita, mixed, so the story goes, by a bartender in 1941 for a woman named Margarita Henkel. It still has sawdust on the floor and mariachis at the door.`,`La Bufadora, on the Punta Banda peninsula south of Ensenada, is one of the largest marine blowholes in North America. As waves surge into a sea cave, the trapped air and water explode out of an opening in the rock, sending a plume of spray high into the air with a roar. The name means the snorter.`,`Ensenada claims to be the birthplace of the fish taco: strips of fish fried in batter, wrapped in a corn tortilla and topped with cabbage, salsa and cream. Street stands around the fish market serve them, along with fresh ceviche, sea urchins, clams and shrimp, to queues of locals and visitors.`,`Ensenada's mild climate comes from the cold California Current offshore, which cools the coast and creates frequent morning fog, especially in late spring and early summer. Inland, behind the coastal hills, the summers are much hotter, and the difference between hot days and cool nights in the valleys is what makes them ideal for growing grapes.`]},"xalapa-mx":{why:`Warm Gulf air is forced up the eastern escarpment of the Sierra Madre Oriental and condenses in a narrow cloud-forest band. At 1,427 m, Xalapa sits in that isohypse: days near 22 °C, nights in the low teens °C, humidity rarely below 70 percent, and rain or drizzle possible in any month. The bosque mesófilo de montaña is not a garden metaphor. It is persistent cloud contact, and it is why coffee, moss, and mildew share the same walls.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, grey and misty — afternoons near 23°C on bright days, nights around 10°C, and long spells of chipi-chipi, the fine drizzle carried in by the cold northern winds called nortes, when the city disappears into cloud and everyone reaches for a jacket. Between fronts, the sun breaks through and the air is fresh and clean.`,spring:`Warm and brightening — afternoons climbing to about 28°C by May, the warmest and sunniest month, with the city's gardens and parks in flower. Thunderstorms begin in late spring, and the air grows heavy and humid.`,summer:`Warm and very wet — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 15°C, and heavy rain almost every afternoon, often with fog rolling up the mountainside. The forests and gardens are lush and dripping, and moss grows on every wall.`,autumn:`Wet then misty — September brings heavy rains and the tail ends of tropical storms from the Gulf, and as autumn goes on the rains ease into drizzle and fog. Afternoons settle to about 23°C by November, and the nortes return.`},contrasts:[{label:`Coatepec (≈10 km S, ≈150 m lower)`,note:`Down the slope among the coffee plantations, Coatepec is a little warmer, and almost as misty and green.`},{label:`Perote (≈35 km W, ≈1,000 m higher)`,note:`Over the mountains on the high plateau, in the rain shadow of the Cofre de Perote, the climate is cold and dry, with frosty winter nights and bare hills.`}],history:[`On 21 February 1794 a boy named Antonio López de Santa Anna was born in Xalapa, and he grew up to become the most notorious figure in nineteenth-century Mexico. A soldier who switched sides whenever it suited him, he was president of Mexico on eleven separate occasions, lost Texas at the Battle of San Jacinto in 1836, and lost half the country to the United States in the war of 1846 to 1848. In 1838 he lost a leg fighting the French, and when he was president again, he had the leg dug up and buried in Mexico City with full military honours; when he fell from power, a mob dug it up again and dragged it through the streets. In 1847 American troops defeated him at Cerro Gordo, just outside Xalapa, and captured his spare cork leg, which is still displayed in a museum in Illinois. His country estate, the Hacienda El Lencero, outside the city, is now a museum.`,`Xalapa grew from four Totonac villages that, according to tradition, joined together around 1313 near the springs on this sandy slope; its Nahuatl name means water in the sand. The Aztecs conquered the area in the fifteenth century, and in August 1519 Hernán Cortés passed through on his march from the coast to Tenochtitlan. Under Spanish rule, Xalapa lay on the main road between the port of Veracruz and Mexico City, and from 1720 the Spanish crown ordered that goods arriving from Spain be sold at great trade fairs held in Xalapa, away from the deadly yellow fever of the coast. The fairs made the town rich.`,`Xalapa became the capital of the state of Veracruz, and in 1892 it added the name of a liberal governor, Juan de la Luz Enríquez, becoming Xalapa-Enríquez. In the twentieth century it grew into a centre of culture and learning, home to the Universidad Veracruzana, founded in 1944, a renowned symphony orchestra and a superb museum of anthropology, and it became known as the Athens of Veracruz. Today it is a lively university city, surrounded by the cloud forests and coffee plantations of the mountain slopes.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`In his old age, exiled in New York in the 1860s, Santa Anna brought a supply of chicle, the gum of the sapodilla tree, which he chewed, hoping to sell it as a substitute for rubber. An American inventor named Thomas Adams experimented with it, failed to make rubber, and instead started selling it as chewing gum, launching an industry that would conquer the world.`,`The Museum of Anthropology of Xalapa, opened in a striking modern building in 1986, holds one of the finest collections of ancient art in Mexico, including seven colossal Olmec heads, carved from basalt boulders some three thousand years ago, and the smiling clay figures of the Totonac culture. Its galleries step down a hillside through gardens.`,`The cloud forest that surrounds Xalapa is one of the most endangered ecosystems in Mexico. It grows in a narrow band on the mountain slopes where moist air from the Gulf condenses into fog, and it shelters orchids, tree ferns, bromeliads and hundreds of species of birds. Much of it has been cleared for coffee, pasture and the spreading city.`,`In the middle of Xalapa rises the Macuiltépetl, an extinct volcano covered in forest, now a park with paths spiralling to the summit, where there are views over the city to the snow-capped Pico de Orizaba, Mexico's highest mountain, on clear days.`]},"san-miguel-de-allende-mx":{why:`At 1,910 m on the Bajío’s dry edge, subtropical latitude is compressed into bright afternoons in the low-to-mid 20s °C and nights that can near freezing in winter. Humidity stays low outside the summer monsoon, so evening cooling is sharp. Pre-monsoon May heat is the annual peak; once rains arrive, afternoons ease and the hills green. That dry highland swing, not colonial paint, is why the town became a migration magnet.`,feel:`San Miguel de Allende feels like a stage set for a colonial romance. Its cobbled streets, lined with walls painted ochre, terracotta and saffron, tumble down a hillside to a plaza dominated by a fantastical pink stone church like a wedding cake, and behind the heavy wooden doors are courtyards full of bougainvillea, galleries and rooftop bars. At about 1,900 m, the air is dry and the sunlight dazzling. Days are warm all year and nights cool, winters are dry and sunny, spring hot and dusty, and summer brings afternoon thunderstorms that rattle the rooftops.`,seasons:{winter:`Dry, sunny and crisp — afternoons near 23°C, nights dropping to about 5°C, and clear blue skies nearly every day. Houses without heating feel chilly at night, and fireplaces are lit in the evenings, but the days are perfect for walking. The town fills with winter visitors from the north.`,spring:`Hot, dry and brilliant — afternoons climbing to about 30°C by May, the hills around town brown and dusty, and jacarandas blooming purple. It is the warmest time of year, and the Easter processions fill the streets.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 13°C, and thunderstorms building in the afternoons, sometimes sending water streaming down the cobbled streets. The countryside turns green, and the evenings are fresh and cool.`,autumn:`Mild and festive — afternoons near 25°C, the rains easing in October, and crisp, clear nights by November. In late September the town celebrates its patron saint, Michael the Archangel, with dancers, processions and fireworks.`},residentFit:`It suits artists, writers, retirees and remote workers who want a beautiful, walkable colonial town with a mild, dry climate and a large English-speaking community, and who can afford rising prices.`,history:[`In 1938 a young American named Stirling Dickinson, who had fallen in love with a sleepy, half-ruined colonial town in the hills of Guanajuato, helped found an art school in an old convent there. After the Second World War, hundreds of American veterans came to study at the school under the GI Bill, which paid for their education, and the town filled with painters, writers and dreamers. The muralist David Alfaro Siqueiros taught there, and began a mural that he never finished. Later came the Beats: in February 1968 Neal Cassady, the model for Dean Moriarty in Jack Kerouac's On the Road, was found unconscious beside the railway tracks outside the town after a wedding party, and died the next day. By then San Miguel de Allende had become a magnet for American artists and retirees, and it has never stopped.`,`The Chichimec and Otomí peoples lived in this region when, in 1542, a Franciscan friar named Juan de San Miguel founded a mission near a spring. In 1555 the town was refounded as San Miguel el Grande, to protect the road along which silver from the mines of Zacatecas was carried to Mexico City, and it grew rich on trade, textiles and ranching, building fine churches and mansions. In 1769 a boy named Ignacio Allende was born in a house on the main square. He became a captain in the Spanish army and one of the leaders of the conspiracy for Mexican independence, and in September 1810 he joined the priest Miguel Hidalgo in launching the revolt. Allende was captured and executed in 1811, and in 1826 the town was renamed San Miguel de Allende in his honour.`,`In the nineteenth century San Miguel's fortunes declined, and by the early twentieth century it was a quiet provincial town, its colonial buildings preserved because nobody could afford to replace them. The arrival of the artists after the 1930s, and later of the American and Canadian retirees, transformed it into one of the most famous towns in Mexico. In 2008 it was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site, together with the sanctuary of Atotonilco nearby. Today foreigners make up a substantial share of the population, and rising prices have pushed many local families out of the centre.`],deepTitle:`Allende's town`,deep:[`The Parroquia de San Miguel Arcángel, the pink parish church on the main square, owes its fairy-tale neo-Gothic façade to Zeferino Gutiérrez, a self-taught Indigenous stonemason who rebuilt it in the 1880s. According to local legend, he had never seen a Gothic cathedral and based his design on postcards of the cathedrals of Europe. The result has become the symbol of the town.`,`The Sanctuary of Jesús Nazareno de Atotonilco, a few kilometres outside San Miguel, is often called the Sistine Chapel of Mexico, its walls and ceilings covered with vivid eighteenth-century murals. In September 1810, Hidalgo stopped there with his rebel army and took a banner of the Virgin of Guadalupe from the church, which became the flag of the independence movement.`,`San Miguel's dry, sunny climate, with warm days and cool nights all year, is one of its great attractions. At about 1,900 m on the central plateau, it is warm in the sun and cool in the shade, and its winters are mild enough that many northern visitors spend the season here, though few houses have heating.`,`San Miguel's festivals are among the most spectacular in Mexico. During the fiesta of San Miguel Arcángel in September, Indigenous dancers in feathered headdresses perform in the streets, and the alborada, a dawn battle of fireworks, lights up the parish church. Its Holy Week processions and its parade of giant papier-mâché figures, the mojigangas, draw crowds.`]},"cuatrocienegas-mx":{why:`A closed Chihuahuan Desert basin stores old, phosphorus-poor groundwater that surfaces as pools, marshes, and gypsum-edge wetlands. Rainfall is only about 224 mm a year, so the oasis is not a gift of storms. It is aquifer plumbing. Endemic fishes, turtles, snails, and living stromatolites depend on that flow remaining connected. Surrounding Coahuila scrub is ordinary BSh heat without the pools. Canals and alfalfa wells are the climate story as much as the temperature: most remaining surface water is exported, while deep wells tap the same system the stromatolites need.`,immersive:`White gypsum dunes and water the color of shallow tropical lagoons sit inside a hot Coahuila box of limestone ranges. Days in late spring routinely climb into the high 30s °C; nights drop fast under desert skies, with winter lows near 4 °C. Annual rain is a thin 224 mm, often in brief storms that barely wet the gypsum. The lived shock is not the heat. It is standing at a spring-fed pool while the surrounding scrub looks lifeless. Salinity, canal draw, and well depth decide which pools still hold endemic fish. This is a desert with a hidden hydrology, not a spa.`,feel:`Cuatro Ciénegas feels like a desert that hides a secret. Ringed by bare, crumpled limestone mountains in the heart of the Chihuahuan Desert, the valley looks at first like any other stretch of scrub and dust, but scattered across it are hundreds of springs and pools of startlingly clear turquoise water, fringed with reeds and full of tiny fish found nowhere else on earth, and in one corner rise dunes of pure white gypsum. The air is dry, the light blinding, and the summers ferociously hot, while winters are mild by day and cold at night.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, dry and sunny — afternoons near 22°C, nights falling to about 5°C, and frost on the coldest mornings. Cold fronts from the north occasionally bring wind and grey skies. It is the best season for hiking across the dunes and among the pools.`,spring:`Hot and getting hotter — afternoons climbing from about 28°C in March to 35°C by May, almost no rain, and dust storms blowing across the valley. The desert flowers bloom briefly, and the pools are the place to be.`,summer:`Scorching — afternoons near 37°C, nights around 21°C, and the occasional thunderstorm building over the mountains. The gypsum dunes glare white in the sun, and the hot season is best spent indoors at midday, venturing out in the early morning and evening.`,autumn:`Warm and easing — afternoons near 30°C in September, falling to about 25°C by November, with occasional storms bringing some of the year's scarce rain and turning the desert briefly green.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the turquoise pools and springs, the white gypsum dunes, the stromatolites and endemic fish and turtles, the wineries of the town, and the birthplace of Venustiano Carranza.`,residentFit:`It suits ranchers, conservationists and scientists drawn by one of the strangest ecosystems on earth, and who can live with fierce summer heat and the ongoing struggle over the valley's water.`,contrasts:[{label:`Monclova (≈65 km E, ≈150 m lower)`,note:`Beyond the mountains, the steel city of Monclova has a similarly hot desert climate, but without the springs and pools that make Cuatro Ciénegas an oasis.`},{label:`Saltillo (≈200 km SE, ≈850 m higher)`,note:`Up on the high desert plateau, Saltillo is several degrees cooler, especially on summer afternoons.`}],history:[`In the early hours of 21 May 1920, in a hut in the mountain village of Tlaxcalantongo, in the state of Puebla, the president of Mexico, Venustiano Carranza, was shot dead by rebel soldiers. He had been fleeing Mexico City with his government and its gold on a train that was ambushed, and had continued on horseback into the mountains, where he was betrayed. Carranza had been born in 1859 in Cuatro Ciénegas, a small ranching town in a desert valley of Coahuila, and he had risen to become governor of his state, leader of the revolutionary forces that overthrew the dictator Victoriano Huerta, and president of Mexico, under whom the constitution of 1917, still in force today, was written. His birthplace is now a museum.`,`The springs of Cuatro Ciénegas had drawn hunter-gatherers to the valley for thousands of years, and the Spanish named the place after its four marshes. In 1800 a Spanish governor, Antonio Cordero y Bustamante, founded a town here, and through the nineteenth century its people lived on ranching and farming. In 1898 a canal was dug to drain the valley's water to irrigate farms and ranches to the east, and in the twentieth century more canals followed, drying up some of the wetlands.`,`In the 1960s an American biologist named W. L. Minckley began to study the pools of Cuatro Ciénegas and discovered that they were full of fish, snails, turtles and other creatures found nowhere else in the world. Scientists compared the valley to the Galápagos Islands, and in 1994 the Mexican government declared it a protected area. In the 2000s NASA and Mexican scientists studied its stromatolites and microbes, some of which resemble the earliest life on earth. But the valley's water is still pumped out to irrigate alfalfa for cattle, and some of its pools, such as Churince, have dried up, threatening the species that live in them.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The pools of Cuatro Ciénegas are home to more than seventy species found nowhere else, including several kinds of pupfish and the Coahuilan box turtle, the only box turtle in the world that lives mostly in water. Many of the pools are connected by underground channels, and their water, rising from deep in the earth, is warm and rich in minerals.`,`The stromatolites of Cuatro Ciénegas are rock-like structures built up layer by layer by colonies of microbes, similar to those that dominated the earth's oceans billions of years ago. The Mexican microbiologist Valeria Souza found that many of the microbes in the pools are more closely related to marine organisms than to those of other lakes, as if they had been trapped here since the valley was covered by an ancient sea.`,`The Dunas de Yeso, the gypsum dunes of Cuatro Ciénegas, are among the few white gypsum dune fields in the world, formed as water evaporates from shallow lakes and leaves behind crystals of gypsum, which the wind piles into dunes. The sand stays cool underfoot even in the summer heat, because gypsum reflects sunlight.`,`The Poza Azul, one of the most famous pools, is a deep blue spring surrounded by reeds, and visitors can see fish and turtles swimming in its clear water from a boardwalk. Swimming is now banned in most of the pools to protect them, but a few spots, such as the clear, slow-flowing Río Mezquites, remain open for bathing.`]},"parras-de-la-fuente-mx":{why:`At about 1,520 m in a Coahuila rain shadow, hot growing-season afternoons meet cool nights that keep acidity in grapes. Rainfall is irregular and near 250 mm, so springs and irrigation, not monsoon surplus, make the oasis. Surrounding sierras would be hopeless for dryland maize at this latitude; the acequias are the real climate. Valle de Guadalupe is a different Mexican wine: marine-cooled, nearer the Pacific. This warm-day, cool-night envelope is one of Mexico’s strongest desert viticulture niches outside Baja, and it is older than the country’s coastal wine boom. Water accounting is the resident skill.`,immersive:`Vines in Parras sit in a high desert bowl where afternoons in the growing season push past 30 °C and nights fall hard enough to preserve grape acidity. Winter mornings can near 4 °C. Rain is a thin 246 mm, arriving without a reliable calendar, so the valley’s springs and acequias are the real climate. Dust and bright light define the dry months; irrigation timing defines the vintage. Surrounding sierras throw a rain shadow that would be hopeless for dryland maize at this latitude, yet the oasis has carried wine, brandy, and orchard patches since the late sixteenth century. Water accounting is the resident skill.`,feel:`Parras feels like a green oasis that the desert forgot to swallow. In a valley of southern Coahuila ringed by bare, rocky sierras, springs bubble up from the ground and feed stone-lined pools and irrigation channels, and the town is shaded by walnut and fig trees, with vineyards stretching out beyond the last houses. At about 1,500 m, the air is dry and clear and the sun intense; the summers are hot by day but cool at night, winters are mild and sunny, and rain is scarce at any time of year.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, dry and sunny — afternoons near 21°C, nights dropping to about 5°C, with frost on the coldest mornings and sharp, clear skies. The vines are bare and pruned, and the town is quiet.`,spring:`Warm, dry and windy — afternoons climbing from about 26°C in March to 32°C by May, with dust blowing off the desert and the vines bursting into leaf. Almost no rain falls, and the spring-fed pools are the coolest places in town.`,summer:`Hot by day, cool by night — afternoons near 31°C, nights around 17°C, and occasional thunderstorms building over the sierras. The grapes ripen in the heat, and the harvest begins in August, celebrated with the town's grape fair.`,autumn:`Warm and gradually cooling — afternoons near 27°C, September bringing a little of the year's rain, and clear, crisp nights by November, when the vineyards turn gold.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for Casa Madero, the oldest winery in the Americas, the August grape fair, the spring-fed pools, the birthplace of Francisco Madero and the walnut sweets sold around the plaza.`,residentFit:`It suits winemakers, growers and families who want a quiet, green oasis in the desert with mild winters and dry air, and who accept hot summers and a small-town pace.`,contrasts:[{label:`Saltillo (≈120 km E, ≈80 m higher)`,note:`The state capital, on the high desert to the east, is cooler and a little wetter, without Parras's springs and orchards.`},{label:`Torreón (≈120 km W, ≈400 m lower)`,note:`Down in the cotton country of the Comarca Lagunera, Torreón is noticeably hotter in summer, one of the hottest cities in northern Mexico.`}],history:[`In 1873 a boy named Francisco Madero was born on a hacienda near Parras, into one of the richest families in Mexico, owners of cotton fields, mines, banks and vineyards. He studied in France and California, became a vegetarian and a spiritist who believed he received messages from the dead, and in 1910 he did something nobody expected of a wealthy young man: he challenged the dictator Porfirio Díaz for the presidency. Thrown in prison, he escaped to Texas and called for a revolution, and in 1911 Díaz fell. Madero became president, but in February 1913 he was overthrown by his own general, Victoriano Huerta, and shot. His death plunged Mexico into years of civil war, and he is still remembered as the apostle of democracy.`,`The springs of Parras had drawn the Indigenous peoples of the desert, the Irritilas and other groups, long before the Spanish arrived and found wild grapevines growing beside the water; parras means grapevines. Jesuits founded a mission here in 1598, and Tlaxcalan settlers came to farm the land. On 19 August 1597 a Spanish captain named Lorenzo García was granted land in the valley to plant vines, founding the Hacienda San Lorenzo, which became the oldest winery in the Americas. Even when the Spanish crown banned the planting of new vineyards in New Spain, to protect the wine trade of Spain, Parras kept producing wine and brandy, protected by its remoteness.`,`In the late nineteenth century the Madero family bought the Hacienda San Lorenzo, renamed it Casa Madero, and brought in European winemakers and new vines. Parras became a prosperous town of haciendas, textile mills and orchards, famous for its wine, brandy and sweets made from walnuts and fruit. In 2004 it was named a Pueblo Mágico. Today Casa Madero, still owned by the family, produces some of Mexico's most celebrated wines, and the town's colonial churches, old haciendas and spring-fed pools draw visitors from across the north.`],deepTitle:`Madero's vineyards`,deep:[`Casa Madero, founded in 1597, is the oldest winery in the Americas still in operation. Its cellars, stone buildings and vineyards can be visited, and its wines have won international awards. The valley's high altitude and cool nights preserve the acidity of the grapes, even in the hot desert summer.`,`Francisco Madero wrote a spiritist manual under a pseudonym, and he believed that the spirit of his dead brother Raúl had told him to fight for democracy. His opponents mocked him as a dreamer, but his book The Presidential Succession in 1910 and his campaign inspired millions of Mexicans to rise against Díaz.`,`The spring-fed pools of Parras, called estanques, are fed by underground water from the surrounding mountains and used for swimming and for irrigating the vineyards and orchards. Their water flows through a network of channels, some built in colonial times, that carry it to every garden in the town.`,`The church of the Santo Madero, perched on a rocky hill above the town, gives a sweeping view over Parras, its vineyards and the desert beyond. Reached by a long staircase, it was built in the nineteenth century to house a relic of the True Cross, which is where its name, the holy timber, comes from: it has nothing to do with the famous family, whatever visitors assume.`]},"toluca-mx":{why:`At 2,663 m, Toluca is high enough to subtract roughly a climate zone from its 19 °N latitude. Peak-sun afternoons often stay near or below 23 °C; winter nights regularly frost, with tens of mornings a year glazing windshields. Basin inversions pond cold air and urban emissions until the sun breaks them. Mexico City, 400 m lower, is the nearest lesson that a few hundred metres of elevation is a different winter.`,feel:`Toluca feels like a city on the roof of Mexico. At about 2,660 m, it is the highest major city in North America, sprawling across a broad, flat valley of maize fields and factories beneath the great volcano of the Nevado de Toluca, whose summit is often dusted with snow. The air is thin, cool and bright, the sun burns but the shade is chilly, and every evening the temperature plunges. Winters are dry and frosty, summers cool and rainy, and no month is ever truly hot.`,seasons:{winter:`Cold, dry and brilliant — afternoons near 21°C in the strong sun, but nights dropping to about 2°C and often below freezing, with heavy frosts in the valley and the occasional dusting of snow. The Nevado gleams white on the horizon. Houses are rarely heated, so residents bundle up at night.`,spring:`Mild and windy — afternoons near 25°C, the warmest of the year, nights still chilly, and dust blowing across the dry fields until the first thunderstorms arrive in May.`,summer:`Cool and wet — afternoons near 22°C, nights around 9°C, and heavy rain most afternoons, often with hail. The valley turns green, the maize grows tall, and the city sees more grey skies than sunshine.`,autumn:`Fresh and gradually drying — afternoons near 22°C, the rains easing in October, and cold, clear nights returning by November, when the first frosts touch the fields.`},residentFit:`It suits families, factory and government workers who want a cool, high-altitude city near Mexico City without its prices, and who are content with cold nights, frosty winters and summer rains.`,history:[`In the 1470s the Aztec emperor Axayácatl led his armies over the mountains from Tenochtitlan into the Toluca valley, a land of lakes and maize fields shared by the Matlatzinca, Otomí, Mazahua and Nahua peoples. At the town of Xiquipilco an Otomí warrior wounded the emperor in the leg, and he barely escaped with his life. But the Aztecs conquered the valley, and its people were forced to pay tribute in maize, beans and warriors. Flushed with victory, Axayácatl then marched west against the Purépecha empire, and suffered one of the worst defeats in Aztec history. The Matlatzinca capital, Calixtlahuaca, with its circular temple to the wind god Ehécatl, still stands on a hill just outside Toluca.`,`After the Spanish conquest, the Toluca valley became part of the vast estates granted to Hernán Cortés by the king, and Franciscan friars built churches and converted its people in the 1520s. The town grew into a market centre and granary for Mexico City, and in 1830 it became the capital of the State of Mexico. In 1861 it added the name of the liberal politician Miguel Lerdo de Tejada, becoming Toluca de Lerdo. Its long arcaded streets, the portales, built in the nineteenth century, still shelter shops selling local sweets, cheeses and the famous chorizo of Toluca.`,`In the twentieth century, highways and railways linked Toluca to Mexico City, only about 60 km away, and the valley filled with factories, including car plants, breweries and food processors. Its population boomed, and today it is the centre of one of the largest metropolitan areas in Mexico. The Nevado de Toluca, which looms over the valley, was declared a national park in 1936, and every weekend crowds of city dwellers drive up its slopes to walk around the lakes in its crater.`],deepTitle:`Under the Nevado`,deep:[`The Nevado de Toluca, or Xinantécatl, rises to about 4,680 m, the fourth highest peak in Mexico. Its crater holds two lakes, the Lake of the Sun and the Lake of the Moon, at more than 4,200 m above sea level. Archaeologists diving in the icy water have found ancient offerings of copal incense, carved wooden sceptres and other objects, left by people who believed the lakes were sacred.`,`The Cosmovitral, in the centre of Toluca, is an old market hall transformed in 1980 into a botanical garden, its walls filled with huge stained-glass windows designed by the artist Leopoldo Flores. The windows, covering thousands of square metres of glass, depict the struggle between light and darkness, and as the sun moves through the sky they bathe the garden's plants in coloured light.`,`Toluca's chorizo is famous across Mexico, especially the green chorizo, coloured with chillies, tomatillos and herbs, and the city's sweets, such as candied fruits and alfeñique, sugar figures made for the Day of the Dead, are sold under the portales. The Alfeñique Fair, held each October, fills the arcades with sugar skulls and animals.`,`Toluca's cool climate comes from its great height. At about 2,660 m, it is some 400 m higher than Mexico City and a few degrees colder, with frosts on many winter nights. The valley's rich volcanic soil and cool, wet summers are ideal for growing maize, and also for flowers, which are grown in greenhouses in the surrounding towns and sent all over the country.`]},"monterrey-mx":{why:`A semi-arid basin at 540 m opens toward Gulf moisture while the Sierra Madre Oriental walls the west. Summer convection slams into that escarpment; winter norte fronts can still arrive near the Tropic of Cancer. Urban heat and industrial inversions make nights warmer than the surrounding thornscrub. Annual rain is only about 475 mm, so most of the year is bright, dusty, and thirsty. Chipinque, an hour uphill, is oak-pine, a different climate, which is the local relief in a single sentence. The sierra does not cool the valley floor. It only offers an exit.`,immersive:`Cerro de la Silla’s saddle is the first thing the eye reads, but the skin reads heat. Monterrey’s basin floor spends long stretches with afternoon highs in the mid-30s °C, then a summer storm against the sierra that can throw hail and a flash of cool air. Winter nights near 7 °C feel like a norte, not like highland Mexico. Annual rain is only about 475 mm, so most of the year is bright, dusty, and thirsty. Inversion mornings smear the skyline. The Santa Catarina channel is dry until it is not. Elevation contrast, not a sea breeze, is the escape hatch.`,feel:`Monterrey feels like an industrial powerhouse built in a frying pan. Mexico's wealthiest big city spreads across a basin at the foot of the Sierra Madre Oriental, ringed by jagged limestone mountains, among them the twin-humped Cerro de la Silla, the saddle, which towers over the skyline of glass office towers, steelworks and breweries. The heat is the defining fact: summers are long and blistering, often humid, and the mountains trap the haze, while winters are mild and dry, interrupted by sudden, biting cold fronts from Texas. Late summer and autumn bring the year's rain, sometimes in torrents.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild and changeable — afternoons near 23°C on most days, nights around 8°C, but every so often a cold front sweeps down from Texas and drops the temperature sharply, with grey skies, wind and drizzle. Between fronts, it is sunny and pleasant.`,spring:`Hot and getting hotter — afternoons climbing from about 28°C in March to 34°C by May, with dry, dusty air, the occasional violent thunderstorm, and the long summer heat already arriving by late spring.`,summer:`Blistering — afternoons near 36°C, nights around 22°C, and spells of humid heat that push the thermometer past 40°C. The mountains trap the haze, the air conditioners hum, and occasional thunderstorms bring brief relief.`,autumn:`Hot then wet — September is the rainiest month, when tropical storms and hurricanes from the Gulf can unleash floods down the Santa Catarina River, before the weather cools to about 25°C by November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the dramatic mountains around the city — Cerro de la Silla, the Huasteca canyon, the García caves and Cola de Caballo waterfall — Parque Fundidora, cabrito and the city's football rivalries.`,residentFit:`It suits professionals, engineers and families drawn by one of the strongest job markets in Mexico and excellent universities, and who can handle long, blistering summers, smog and traffic.`,contrasts:[{label:`Saltillo (≈85 km W, ≈1,050 m higher)`,note:`Up on the high desert beyond the mountains, Saltillo is several degrees cooler on summer afternoons, with drier air and cooler nights.`},{label:`Chipinque (≈10 km S, ≈800 m higher)`,note:`Up on the forested slopes of the Sierra Madre above the city, the park of Chipinque is cooler and greener, a refuge from the heat of the basin.`}],history:[`In 1579 King Philip II of Spain granted a Portuguese-born adventurer named Luis de Carvajal y de la Cueva a vast territory in the northeast of New Spain, called the New Kingdom of León, and Carvajal brought with him settlers from Spain and Portugal, many of them conversos, Jews who had been forced to convert to Christianity. Some secretly kept their old faith. The Inquisition arrested Carvajal for failing to denounce them, and he died in prison in 1591. In December 1596 his nephew, Luis de Carvajal the Younger, his mother and several of his sisters were burned at the stake in Mexico City for practising Judaism. That same year, on 20 September 1596, Carvajal's former lieutenant, Diego de Montemayor, founded a small town beside the springs of Santa Lucía, at the foot of the Sierra Madre, and named it Monterrey.`,`For two centuries Monterrey was a small, isolated frontier town, threatened by floods from the Santa Catarina River and by the nomadic peoples of the north, who fought the Spanish slave raids and settlements. In September 1846, during the war with the United States, the American general Zachary Taylor attacked the city, and after three days of house-to-house fighting, its defenders surrendered. In the second half of the nineteenth century, Monterrey grew rich from trade with Texas, especially during the American Civil War, when Confederate cotton was smuggled across the border.`,`With the arrival of the railway in the 1880s, Monterrey became Mexico's first great industrial city. In 1890 the Cuauhtémoc brewery was founded, followed by glassworks, cement plants and, in 1900, the Fundidora, the first steelworks in Latin America. Its business families built powerful companies that still dominate the Mexican economy. The Fundidora closed in 1986, and its site is now a park, its old blast furnaces turned into a museum. Today Monterrey's metropolitan area is Mexico's second-largest, home to multinational companies, universities and a booming manufacturing industry.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`In September 1988 Hurricane Gilbert dumped torrential rain on the mountains around Monterrey, and the normally dry bed of the Santa Catarina River became a raging torrent that swept away buses, cars and people. Hundreds of people died across the region. In 2010 Hurricane Alex brought similar floods, and the river, now lined with roads and parks, is a constant reminder of the danger.`,`The Cerro de la Silla, the saddle mountain, rises east of the city to about 1,800 m, its two peaks resembling a horse's saddle. It is the symbol of Monterrey, and hikers climb its steep trails for views over the city. To the west, the Huasteca canyon and the caves of García offer dramatic limestone landscapes within an hour's drive.`,`Monterrey's signature dish is cabrito, young goat roasted slowly over coals, a tradition that some trace to the Sephardic conversos who settled the region. The city is also famous for its carne asada, eaten at weekend barbecues, and for its beer, brewed in the city since the nineteenth century.`,`Monterrey's heat comes from its position in a low basin, far from the sea, shielded by mountains. In summer the air stagnates, and haze and pollution are trapped against the mountains, making Monterrey one of the most polluted cities in Mexico. The same mountains, though, offer relief: the forested heights of Chipinque are only a short drive from the city centre.`]},"hermosillo-mx":{why:`Continental Sonoran Desert heat at 200 m and 29° north produces some of North America’s hottest state-capital afternoons, with summer maxima near 40 °C. The North American monsoon arrives as scattered convective cells, not as a soaking season; annual totals stay near 310 mm. Westward, Bahía de Kino adds Gulf of California humidity without turning the city maritime. Winter is the outdoor season outsiders underestimate: bright, dry, and mild enough for streets to fill. Shade and air conditioning are infrastructure. This is a BWh administrative basin built on captured rivers, not a coastal desert town.`,immersive:`Hermosillo’s light is desert-hard from March onward, the kind that makes shade a civic right. Afternoon highs in the peak months sit near 40 °C on the official record, with nights that still cool when skies stay clear. July and August can throw dust, virga, and a local downpour that barely dents the yearly 309 mm. Winter days are the bargain: bright, dry, and mild enough for streets to fill. The city is not on the sea. Bahía de Kino is the humidity gradient, a day trip west, not a breeze in the centro. Air conditioning is infrastructure here, not a luxury opinion.`,feel:`Hermosillo feels like a city built inside an oven. The capital of Sonora spreads across the flat floor of the Sonoran Desert, among saguaro cactus, mesquite and dusty hills, and for months of the year the heat is the first and last thing anyone talks about. Summer afternoons regularly approach 40°C, and the nights barely cool; the monsoon brings thunderstorms and sticky humidity in July and August. But from November to March the desert is glorious, with warm, sunny days and cool nights, and the city lives outdoors around its carne asada grills.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm, sunny and glorious — afternoons near 26°C, nights cooling to about 7°C, and clear skies almost every day. The occasional winter storm from the Pacific brings a day or two of rain. It is the best time of year in the desert, and the season for the city's outdoor festivals.`,spring:`Hot and bone-dry — afternoons climbing from about 30°C in March to 36°C by May, with almost no rain and the desert parched. The heat builds week by week, and by late May it is already ferocious.`,summer:`Infernal — afternoons near 40°C, nights around 25°C, and June the hottest month, with late spring bone-dry until the monsoon. From July the monsoon brings towering thunderstorms, dust storms and humid heat. Life moves indoors to air-conditioned rooms, and the streets empty at midday.`,autumn:`Hot then gentle — September is still blazing, with the last of the monsoon storms and the risk of tropical storms from the Pacific, but by November afternoons ease to about 30°C, and the desert breathes again.`},residentFit:`It suits families, engineers and factory workers drawn by jobs at the Ford plant and other industries, and people who love dry heat and winter sunshine, but the scorching summers are a real test.`,contrasts:[{label:`Bahía de Kino (≈105 km W, on the Gulf of California)`,note:`On the coast, sea breezes take the edge off the summer heat, although the humidity is higher; it is Hermosillo's favourite beach escape.`},{label:`Phoenix, Arizona (≈500 km N)`,note:`Across the border in the same desert, Phoenix has similarly scorching summers but noticeably colder winter nights.`}],history:[`In October 1852 a French count named Gaston de Raousset-Boulbon marched into Sonora at the head of some 250 French adventurers, most of them failed gold miners from California. He dreamed of seizing the rich silver mines of the region, and perhaps of founding a French colony. His men stormed Hermosillo and took the town after a short battle, but the count fell ill with dysentery, and his little army withdrew to the coast. Two years later he returned with a new force and attacked the port of Guaymas, where he was defeated, captured and executed by firing squad in August 1854. He was one of several foreign adventurers, known as filibusters, who tried to carve out kingdoms of their own in northern Mexico in the decade after the war with the United States.`,`The Pima Bajo people farmed at the meeting of the Sonora and San Miguel rivers, in a settlement called Pitic, and the Seri, or Comcaac, people roamed the desert and coast to the west. In 1741 the Spanish built a fort here, the Presidio of Pitic, to guard against the Seri, who resisted Spanish rule fiercely for decades. Canals were dug to irrigate fields and orchards along the river, and the settlement grew. In 1828 it was renamed Hermosillo after José María González Hermosillo, a hero of the war of independence, and in 1879 it became the capital of Sonora.`,`In the twentieth century Hermosillo grew into a modern city, with dams on its rivers and wells that turned the desert around it into farmland for wheat, grapes and cattle. In 1986 Ford built a large car plant here, and manufacturing joined farming and government as the city's main industries. Today Hermosillo is one of the largest cities in northern Mexico, but water is its constant worry: its reservoir has run dry in droughts, and the city depends on wells and an aqueduct that brings water from the Yaqui River.`],deepTitle:`The desert capital`,deep:[`The Seri, who call themselves the Comcaac, live in two villages on the coast west of Hermosillo, at Punta Chueca and El Desemboque, facing Isla Tiburón, the largest island in Mexico, which they consider their ancestral territory. They are famous for their ironwood carvings and their baskets, and for their language, which is unrelated to any other language in the region.`,`Bacanora, a spirit distilled from wild agave in the mountains of Sonora, was banned by the state government in 1915, and for more than seventy years it was made in secret stills in the hills. The ban was lifted in 1992, and bacanora is now a protected regional spirit, a cousin of tequila and mezcal.`,`Hermosillo is famous for its food: carne asada from the cattle ranches of Sonora, flour tortillas so large and thin they are almost transparent, and the Sonoran hot dog, wrapped in bacon and piled with beans, tomatoes, onions and mayonnaise, sold from street stands after dark.`,`Hermosillo's heat comes from its position on the floor of the Sonoran Desert, far enough inland that the sea breeze rarely reaches it, under a sinking air mass that keeps the skies clear for much of the year. In summer the North American monsoon brings moist air from the south and thunderstorms, but they offer only brief relief from the heat.`]},"puerto-vallarta-mx":{why:`Banderas Bay’s curve and the Sierra Madre immediately inland focus afternoon convection and trap a warm, humid marine layer. Winter is the dry high season; summer is muggy and sits on a Pacific hurricane track. Sea breezes keep the beach cooler than inland Guadalajara, but they do not cancel storm surge or slope runoff. About 1,070 mm of rain falls mostly in those wet months. Mismaloya and the southern coves show how quickly headlands change exposure. Bay geometry plus cyclone exposure is the whole coastal mechanism, and the mountain wall is scenery and a rainfall engine at once.`,immersive:`Warm Pacific water is the winter argument for Puerto Vallarta, and humidity is the summer one. Dry-season days feel golden and walkable; once the rains lock in, afternoons near 34 °C come with a wet weight and thunderstorms that build against the sierra. About 1,070 mm of rain falls mostly in those months. The malecón can be ten degrees kinder than inland Jalisco at the same hour, thanks to a bay breeze, while Mismaloya and the southern coves show how quickly headlands change exposure. Hurricane watches are not theoretical on this coast. The mountain wall is scenery and a rainfall engine at once.`,feel:`Puerto Vallarta feels like a Mexican village that Hollywood turned into a resort without quite erasing the village. White houses with red-tiled roofs and bougainvillea climb the steep, jungle-covered hills above a cobbled old town, the Río Cuale tumbles down to the sea, and a long seaside promenade, the malecón, curves along the edge of the Bay of Banderas, one of the largest bays in Mexico. It is warm and sunny every month: the winters are dry, breezy and close to perfect, and the summers hot, humid and stormy, with towering thunderclouds over the Sierra Madre and the threat of hurricanes in the autumn.`,seasons:{winter:`The high season — afternoons near 30°C, nights cooling to about 15°C, almost no rain, and steady sunshine with a fresh sea breeze. Humpback whales arrive in the bay to breed and give birth, and the town is crowded with visitors escaping the northern winter.`,spring:`Warm, dry and calm — afternoons near 32°C, nights around 18°C, and clear blue skies. The hills behind town turn brown and dusty, the whales head north in March, and the sea warms up for swimming.`,summer:`Hot, sticky and stormy — afternoons near 34°C, nights around 23°C, and the rainy season in full swing from late June, with thunderstorms most evenings that drench the streets and turn the jungle on the hills a vivid green. The humidity is oppressive, and the town is quiet.`,autumn:`Wet then wonderful — September is the rainiest month and the peak of the hurricane season, before the rains end in October and the town greens, dries and fills with visitors again by November.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the malecón and its sculptures, the beaches of the Bay of Banderas, humpback whales in winter, the Zona Romántica, boat trips to Los Arcos and Yelapa, and the jungle-covered mountains behind town.`,contrasts:[{label:`San Sebastián del Oeste (≈40 km NE, ≈1,400 m higher)`,note:`Up in the pine-clad Sierra Madre, the old silver-mining village is cool and misty, with chilly nights even in summer.`},{label:`Guadalajara (≈200 km E, ≈1,560 m higher)`,note:`Up on the central plateau, Jalisco's capital has a mild, dry highland climate, with cool nights and none of the coast's humidity.`}],history:[`In the autumn of 1963 the director John Huston brought a film crew to a remote cove called Mismaloya, south of the small fishing town of Puerto Vallarta, to shoot The Night of the Iguana, based on the play by Tennessee Williams, starring Richard Burton and Ava Gardner. The real drama, however, was off the set. Burton was accompanied by Elizabeth Taylor, who was still married to someone else, and the scandalous affair between the two most famous stars in the world drew reporters and photographers from around the globe to a town that, until a few years before, could only be reached by boat, plane or mule. Burton bought Taylor a house in the hills above the town, Casa Kimberly, and built a bridge connecting it to his own house across the street. The world discovered Puerto Vallarta, and it has been coming ever since.`,`The Bay of Banderas was home to Indigenous peoples who fished, farmed and traded along the coast and in the river valleys long before the Spanish arrived. In the colonial era, the bay was visited by Spanish ships and pirates, but no town was founded on its shores. On 12 December 1851, the feast of the Virgin of Guadalupe, a man named Guadalupe Sánchez Torres founded a settlement at the mouth of the Río Cuale, called Las Peñas de Santa María de Guadalupe, to supply salt to the silver mines in the mountains, which used it to refine ore.`,`In 1918 the town was renamed Puerto Vallarta in honour of Ignacio L. Vallarta, a liberal lawyer and former governor of Jalisco. For decades it remained a quiet village of fishermen and farmers, connected to the rest of Mexico only by sea and air. After the success of The Night of the Iguana, roads and an international airport were built, and hotels spread along the shores of the bay. Today Puerto Vallarta is one of Mexico's most popular resorts, with a large community of foreign residents, and it is known as one of the most welcoming destinations in Latin America for gay travellers.`],deepTitle:`The Iguana effect`,deep:[`The church of Our Lady of Guadalupe, the symbol of Puerto Vallarta, has a tower topped by a large crown held up by angels, said to be modelled on the crown of the Empress Carlota, wife of Maximilian. The original crown fell in an earthquake in 1995 and was replaced by a lighter copy. Every December, pilgrims parade to the church for the feast of the Virgin.`,`Each winter, from December to March, humpback whales migrate from the cold waters of the North Pacific to the warm waters of the Bay of Banderas to mate and give birth. Whale-watching boats leave from the marinas, and the whales can sometimes be seen leaping from the water from the beaches and the malecón.`,`In October 2002 Hurricane Kenna, one of the most powerful hurricanes to strike Mexico's Pacific coast, made landfall north of the bay and sent huge waves crashing over Puerto Vallarta's malecón, damaging hotels and restaurants. The malecón was later rebuilt and is now lined with sculptures, including a famous bronze of a boy riding a seahorse.`,`The village of Mismaloya, where The Night of the Iguana was filmed, has become a tourist attraction, and the ruins of the film set still stand on the headland. Nearby is Los Arcos, a group of granite islands with natural arches, a marine park where visitors snorkel and dive among tropical fish.`]},"mazatlan-mx":{why:`At 23 °N on a long Pacific-and-Gulf of California opening, Mazatlán is wet-dry tropics without Baja’s desert rain shadow. Winters are the bright, dry season that snowbirds buy; summers are humid and convective, with a cyclone history distinct from the peninsula’s. About 606 mm of rain is less than Puerto Vallarta’s, but the storm fetch is long and straight. Sea breeze moderates, it does not aridify. Inland sierras wring moisture that does not fall evenly on the sand. Carnival week is a dry-season civic peak. August is another city, and that seasonal flip is the climate.`,immersive:`Winter light on Mazatlán’s malecón is the climate product people fly for: dry air, warm water, and nights that can dip near 13 °C. Summer inverts the deal. Afternoons near 35 °C arrive with a wet-season weight, mangrove humidity, and the knowledge that Pacific storms have used this coast before. Rain totals near 606 mm are modest for the tropics, concentrated when the monsoon is on, so the beach can look rainless for months and then run. Inland sierras wring moisture that does not fall evenly on the sand. Carnival week is a dry-season civic peak. August is another city.`,feel:`Mazatlán feels like a working port that never stopped throwing a party. Its long seafront promenade runs for kilometres past beaches, hotels and rocky headlands, and at its southern end lies a historic centre of pastel nineteenth-century houses, iron balconies and plazas where brass bands play loud, brassy banda music. Fishing boats and shrimp trawlers crowd the harbour, open-air taxis buzz along the shore, and the city smells of the sea. Winters are warm, dry and sunny; spring is glorious; and summer is hot, humid and stormy, with the hurricane season running into autumn.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm, dry and sunny — afternoons near 28°C, nights cooling to about 14°C, and clear skies nearly every day, with a refreshing breeze off the sea. The water is cool for swimming, and it is the season when snowbirds from the north fill the city and Carnival fills the streets.`,spring:`Warm, dry and calm — afternoons climbing from about 29°C in March to 33°C by May, almost no rain, and the sea warming. It is the driest and sunniest time of year, and the beaches fill during Easter week.`,summer:`Hot, humid and stormy — afternoons near 35°C, nights barely cooling below 25°C, and thunderstorms rolling in from July, bringing most of the year's rain. The sea is as warm as a bath, and the air is sticky and heavy.`,autumn:`Steamy then easing — September and October are hot and humid, with the greatest risk of hurricanes, before the weather turns drier and fresher in November, with afternoons near 32°C.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the restored historic centre and the Ángela Peralta theatre, one of the world's biggest carnivals, fresh shrimp and aguachile, the cliff divers, the lighthouse climb and the long beaches of the Golden Zone.`,contrasts:[{label:`Durango (≈180 km NE, ≈1,890 m higher)`,note:`Up over the Sierra Madre on the high plateau, Durango has a cool, dry climate with frosty winter nights, a world away from the steamy coast.`},{label:`Culiacán (≈200 km NW, inland)`,note:`The state capital, inland on the coastal plain, is noticeably hotter in summer, without Mazatlán's sea breeze.`}],history:[`In August 1883 Ángela Peralta, the most celebrated soprano in Mexico, who had sung at La Scala in Milan and been called the Mexican nightingale, arrived by ship at Mazatlán with her opera company to perform at the city's new theatre. But a yellow fever epidemic had broken out in the port. Within days, members of her company began to fall ill, and so did Peralta. On her deathbed she married her longtime companion, and she died on 30 August, aged thirty-eight; she was buried hastily that night. The epidemic killed thousands in the city. The theatre where she was due to sing was later named the Teatro Ángela Peralta, and, restored after decades of ruin, it is the heart of Mazatlán's cultural life.`,`The Totorame people lived along this coast, fishing, farming and gathering salt and shells, and the Spanish who passed through in the sixteenth century named the area Mazatlán, from the Nahuatl for place of deer. For centuries it was a small fishing and military outpost, harassed by pirates, until the 1820s, when it grew into a port. Its location, on the route between Mexico and the California gold fields and close to the silver mines of the Sierra Madre, made it the most important port on Mexico's Pacific coast in the nineteenth century, and it was occupied by American forces in 1847 and by the French in 1864.`,`German merchant families dominated the port's trade in the late nineteenth century, and in 1900 Germans founded the Pacífico brewery, whose beer is now sold around the world. They also introduced brass instruments, which local musicians blended with Mexican traditions to create the loud, brassy banda music of Sinaloa. Mazatlán's Carnival, held since 1898, grew into one of the largest in the world. In the twentieth century tourism arrived, and the city became one of Mexico's first beach resorts, alongside its fishing fleet, which made it one of the biggest shrimp ports in the country.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The lighthouse of Mazatlán, El Faro, stands on top of the Cerro del Crestón, a rocky hill at the entrance to the harbour, at about 157 m above sea level, making it one of the highest natural lighthouses in the world. Visitors climb a steep path to the top for views over the city and the ocean.`,`The pulmonías, open-air taxis built on the frame of a small car or golf cart, were invented in Mazatlán in 1965 and are found nowhere else. Their name means pneumonia, from the joke that riding in one in the cool evening breeze would give you a chill.`,`At the cliff-diving platform on the malecón, divers plunge from a rock into a narrow channel of churning water, timing their leap to the waves, as visitors watch. The tradition dates back to the mid-twentieth century and is a smaller cousin of the famous cliff divers of Acapulco.`,`Mazatlán sits just south of the Tropic of Cancer, and its climate is a mix of tropical and desert influences: winters are dry and mild, like the deserts to the north, while summers bring tropical heat, humidity and rain. Cool northwesterly winds blowing down the Gulf of California keep the winters fresher than in towns further south.`]},"campeche-mx":{why:`Western Gulf tracks, not Caribbean ones, set Campeche’s cyclone file, while summer waves still deliver monsoon-like deluges on a flat limestone plain. Sea breeze cuts the peak heat without cutting humidity. Surge and street flooding follow from that lack of relief. Cancún faces a different fetch; Mérida sits inland without the wall. The walled centro is a pirate-era answer to a port that could not hide; it is not a climate wall. About 1,080 mm falls mostly in the wave-and-hurricane months, onto streets with nowhere to drain but the mangrove.`,immersive:`Heat in San Francisco de Campeche is Gulf heat: afternoons near 36 °C with a humidity that a sea breeze only stirs. Winter is the kind season, nights near 18 °C and less rain, which is when the ramparts are easiest to walk. About 1,080 mm falls mostly in the wave-and-hurricane months, onto a coastal plain with nowhere to drain but the streets and the mangrove. The ochre walls hold a colonial plan; they do not hold back surge. Compared with Caribbean Cancún, this is a slower, hotter, more historically armed shore, with the same tropical-cyclone arithmetic under a different compass heading.`,feel:`Campeche feels like a fortress that turned into a jewel box. Behind thick stone walls and bastions built to keep out pirates, the old city is a grid of quiet streets lined with low colonial houses painted in every shade of pastel, from pink and lemon to turquoise, their windows protected by iron grilles. Beyond the walls, a long seafront promenade faces the shallow, muddy-green Gulf of Mexico, where the sunsets are spectacular. It is hot all year: winters are warm and pleasant, spring brutal, and summers humid and stormy, with the threat of hurricanes.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm and pleasant — afternoons near 30°C, nights around 18°C, and mostly dry weather, interrupted by cold fronts from the north, the nortes, that bring wind, choppy seas, cloud and a refreshing drop in temperature. It is the most comfortable time to walk the walls.`,spring:`The hottest season — afternoons climbing to about 36°C by May, nights warm and still, and the countryside parched. The heat is intense in the middle of the day, and the city comes to life in the evenings, when the sea breeze blows.`,summer:`Hot, humid and stormy — afternoons near 35°C, nights around 23°C, and thunderstorms most afternoons, bringing torrential downpours that flood the streets. The air is heavy and the jungle inland lush.`,autumn:`Wet and storm-watched — September and October bring heavy rain and the greatest risk of hurricanes from the Gulf or the Caribbean, before the first nortes arrive in November and cool the air.`},residentFit:`It suits families, history lovers and remote workers who want a safe, quiet, walled colonial city on the Gulf with a low cost of living, and who accept heavy heat, humidity and hurricane season.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mérida (≈160 km NE, inland)`,note:`The capital of Yucatán, inland on the flat limestone plain, is a touch drier, with a little less of Campeche's sea breeze on summer afternoons.`},{label:`Ciudad del Carmen (≈190 km SW, on the Gulf)`,note:`Further down the coast, the oil town on its island in the Laguna de Términos is wetter and more humid, surrounded by mangroves.`}],history:[`In July 1685 a fleet of pirates led by the Dutch buccaneer Laurens de Graaf and the Frenchman Michel de Grammont sailed into the bay of Campeche, landed hundreds of men and took the town after a short fight. They held it for nearly two months, looting houses and churches, torturing residents to reveal hidden treasure, and demanding ransom for their prisoners. When the Spanish governor refused to pay, Grammont is said to have executed several of them. Before they left, the pirates burned much of the town. It was the last straw for a city that had been sacked again and again for a century and a half, and the following year the Spanish began to build the great hexagonal wall, with eight bastions, that still surrounds the old city.`,`The Maya town of Can Pech stood on this shore when, in 1517, the expedition of the Spanish explorer Francisco Hernández de Córdoba landed here to take on water, one of the first Spanish landings on the mainland of Mexico. On 4 October 1540, Francisco de Montejo the Younger founded the Spanish town of San Francisco de Campeche, and it became the main port of the Yucatán Peninsula, shipping logwood, a tree used to make dye, as well as salt, timber and other goods to Spain. Its wealth made it a target for English, French and Dutch pirates, who sacked the town repeatedly in the seventeenth century.`,`The walls, built between 1686 and 1704, made Campeche one of the most strongly fortified ports in the Spanish Americas, and in the late eighteenth century two forts were added on the hills overlooking the town. In the nineteenth century, Campeche lost its trade to other ports and became the capital of its own state, separated from Yucatán in 1863. Parts of the walls were demolished to let the city breathe, but much survived, and in 1999 the historic fortified town was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today its restored streets, bastions and museums draw visitors, and the surrounding state is home to the great Maya ruins of Calakmul and Edzná.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`The Fuerte de San Miguel, on a hill south of the old city, now houses a museum of Maya archaeology, displaying jade funerary masks found in the royal tombs of Calakmul, one of the most powerful Maya cities, hidden in the jungle in the south of the state. The masks, made of hundreds of pieces of green jade, are among the finest treasures of the ancient Maya.`,`Logwood, a tree that grows in the swamps of Campeche and Belize, was one of the most valuable products of the colonial Americas, because its heartwood produced a dark dye used to colour cloth black, blue and purple in Europe. English loggers settled illegally along the coast to cut it, and the trade drew pirates and smugglers to the region.`,`When Francisco Hernández de Córdoba's expedition sailed on from Campeche in 1517, it was attacked by the Maya at Champotón, further down the coast. More than half of his men were killed, and Hernández de Córdoba himself died of his wounds soon after returning to Cuba. The bay was later called the Bay of Bad Fight.`,`Campeche's malecón, a promenade several kilometres long along the Gulf, is the place to watch the sunset and to escape the heat of the old city in the evening breeze. The shallow, warm waters of the Bay of Campeche are rich in fish and shrimp, and the city's seafood, such as pan de cazón, layers of tortilla and shark meat in tomato sauce, is famous across Mexico.`]},"palenque-mx":{why:`Lowland rainforest at 150 m under northern Chiapas foothills puts Palenque in persistent heat and in one of Mexico’s wetter pins, near 1,800 mm. Afternoon convection explodes over the canopy; mornings can sit in mist. A drier winter window is relative, not dry. Orographic lift toward San Cristóbal is the upslope exit, not a local breeze. The ruins’ shade is a forest microclimate on top of an Af baseline, and the town’s ordinary afternoon is still a humid low 30s °C under that canopy.`,immersive:`Steam is the afternoon medium in Palenque. Mornings may hang in mist under the canopy; by midday the air at 150 m is a humid low 30s °C, and thunder is a reasonable bet in the wet months. Nearly 1,800 mm of rain supports tropical moist forest that swallowed Lakamha and still crowds the town. Winter is the tourist compromise: less rain, not coolness. Nights near 18 °C never become highland. Howler monkeys and parrots are ecology, not decoration. The walk from town to the ruins is a humidity transect before it is an archaeological one.`,feel:`Palenque feels like a lost city still half-swallowed by the jungle. On the first green ridges of the Chiapas mountains, where they rise from the flat plains of Tabasco, the temples and palaces of an ancient Maya city stand in clearings of emerald lawn, surrounded by towering rainforest that rings with the roar of howler monkeys at dawn. It is hot and humid every month of the year, and it rains a great deal: the air is heavy, the paths slippery, and mist hangs in the trees after storms. The modern town below is a hot, scrappy, friendly place of hostels and hammocks.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm, humid and showery — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 18°C, and regular rain from cold fronts, the nortes, which stall against the mountains and bring days of grey drizzle. Between fronts, mornings are misty and the ruins are at their most atmospheric.`,spring:`The hottest and driest season — afternoons climbing to about 35°C by May, with sticky heat, clearer skies and fewer storms. The rivers run low and turquoise, and the jungle steams in the midday sun.`,summer:`Hot, sticky and very wet — afternoons near 33°C, nights around 22°C, and thunderstorms nearly every afternoon, sometimes torrential. The rainforest is at its loudest and lushest, and the waterfalls nearby roar.`,autumn:`The wettest time of year — September and October bring relentless rains, flooding rivers and the occasional tropical storm, before the nortes take over in November with cooler, drizzly days.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Maya ruins, among the most beautiful in Mexico, walks in the rainforest of the national park, howler monkeys at dawn, and the waterfalls of Misol-Ha and Agua Azul.`,contrasts:[{label:`San Cristóbal de las Casas (≈100 km S, ≈2,050 m higher)`,note:`Up in the Chiapas highlands, a few hours' drive away, the air is cool, nights are chilly and the jungle gives way to pine forest.`},{label:`Villahermosa (≈110 km NW, on the Tabasco plain)`,note:`Down on the flat, swampy lowland, the capital of Tabasco is even hotter and more humid, without the forested hills.`}],history:[`In 1949 the Mexican archaeologist Alberto Ruz Lhuillier noticed that one of the stone slabs in the floor of the Temple of the Inscriptions, the tallest pyramid at Palenque, had holes in it, plugged with stone stoppers. When he lifted it, he found a staircase leading down into the pyramid, deliberately packed with rubble. It took his team four seasons of digging to clear it. On 15 June 1952, they reached a sealed chamber deep inside the pyramid, and inside it found a huge stone sarcophagus with a carved lid. Within lay the skeleton of a king, covered in jade jewellery, his face hidden by a mask made of jade mosaic. He was K'inich Janaab Pakal, who had ruled Palenque for almost seventy years in the seventh century. It was the first royal tomb ever found inside a Maya pyramid.`,`The Maya called the city Lakamha, big water, for the springs and streams that flow from the hills, and it was one of the great cities of the Classic Maya, rising to its height under Pakal, who came to the throne at the age of twelve in 615 and ruled until 683, and his sons. Its builders created some of the most elegant architecture and sculpture of the ancient Americas, and a stone aqueduct that channels a stream beneath the main plaza. But around 800 CE, like many Maya cities, it was abandoned, and the jungle grew over it. In 1567 a Dominican friar named Pedro Lorenzo de la Nada founded a village nearby for the Ch'ol Maya, which he named Palenque, meaning a fortified place.`,`Spanish officials heard rumours of the ruins in the eighteenth century, and in 1787 King Charles III sent an expedition to explore them. In the 1830s an eccentric artist calling himself Count Waldeck lived for more than a year in one of the temples, and in 1840 the American writer John Lloyd Stephens and the artist Frederick Catherwood made drawings that revealed Palenque to the world. Today it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, protected within a national park of rainforest, and one of the most visited ruins in Mexico.`],deepTitle:`Pakal's tomb`,deep:[`The lid of Pakal's sarcophagus is carved with an image of the king falling into the jaws of the underworld, beneath a cross-shaped world tree. In his 1968 bestseller, Chariots of the Gods?, the Swiss writer Erich von Däniken claimed that it showed an ancient astronaut at the controls of a spaceship, a theory archaeologists dismiss but which still draws curious visitors.`,`In 1994 archaeologists opened another tomb, in the temple next to Pakal's, and found the remains of a noblewoman covered in bright red cinnabar, wearing a mask of malachite. Known as the Red Queen, she is thought to have been Pakal's wife, Tz'akbu Ajaw.`,`John Lloyd Stephens was so taken with Palenque that he considered buying the site, but found that only a foreigner married to a Mexican could own land. He wrote that he briefly considered finding a wife among the local women, but decided against it, and moved on to other ruins.`,`The jungle around Palenque is full of waterfalls, such as Misol-Ha, which plunges over a cliff into a deep pool, and the cascades of Agua Azul, where turquoise water tumbles over limestone terraces. Howler monkeys, toucans and scarlet macaws live in the forest of the national park.`]},"taxco-mx":{why:`Lapse rate is the entire trick: at 1,773 m, 18 °N yields spring-like nights while Acapulco, not far in latitude, swelters at sea level. Steep folds of the Sierra Madre del Sur create aspect-by-aspect rainfall and wind. Summer storms arrive with theatrical lightning on the ridges, dumping close to 980 mm, mostly in those months. Karst and mine drainage complicate water. Nearby Iguala is hotter and lower. The cobbles are pretty because the slope is unforgiving, and every stair is a local climate of sun, shade, and wet-season runoff.`,immersive:`Balconies in Taxco hang on a grade that makes the climate local to each stair. At 1,773 m, afternoons in the upper 20s °C feel like highland spring, and nights near 10 °C want a wall. Summer thunderheads stack on the Sierra Madre del Sur and dump close to 980 mm, mostly in those months, onto streets too steep for standing water and too old for easy drainage. Bougainvillea on stone is real; so is the knee tax of every errand. Silver shops occupy a mining town that learned to live with slope. Acapulco’s heat is a few hours down the same latitude, which is the whole lesson.`,feel:`Taxco feels like a silver town poured down a mountainside. Its whitewashed houses with red-tiled roofs cling to steep slopes in the Sierra Madre del Sur, linked by narrow cobbled lanes that twist up and down so sharply that the white Volkswagen Beetle taxis seem to climb walls, and at its heart rises the pink Baroque church of Santa Prisca, its twin towers glowing at sunset. Hundreds of silver shops line the streets. At about 1,800 m, the climate is mild all year, with warm, sunny, dry winters, a hot spring, and summers of afternoon thunderstorms that send rain rushing down the lanes.`,seasons:{winter:`Warm, dry and sunny — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 11°C, and blue skies almost every day. It is the best time to explore the steep streets, and the silver fair in late November and the Christmas festivities fill the town.`,spring:`Hot and dry — afternoons climbing to about 31°C by April and May, the warmest of the year, with hazy skies and the hills brown. Holy Week is the high point of the year, with solemn processions of penitents through the streets.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 15°C, and heavy thunderstorms most afternoons, sending water cascading down the steep lanes. The hills turn green, and the air is fresh in the evenings.`,autumn:`Green and mild — afternoons near 27°C, the rains easing in October, and clear, pleasant days returning by November, when the town celebrates the Day of the Dead and, soon after, the curious festival of the jumil.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the silver shops and workshops, the Baroque church of Santa Prisca, the steep cobbled lanes and white Beetle taxis, the National Silver Fair and the cable car to Monte Taxco.`,residentFit:`It suits silversmiths, artisans and retirees who love a beautiful hill town with a mild climate, and who accept steep streets, crowded weekends and the security concerns that affect parts of Guerrero.`,contrasts:[{label:`Iguala (≈25 km S, ≈1,050 m lower)`,note:`Down in the hot valleys toward the Balsas River, Iguala is several degrees hotter, with sweltering spring afternoons.`},{label:`Cuernavaca (≈55 km NE, ≈300 m lower)`,note:`The city of eternal spring has a similar mild climate, a little warmer, and the same summer rains.`}],history:[`In 1929 an American architect named William Spratling, who had been teaching at Tulane University in New Orleans, arrived in Taxco to write a book. He found a sleepy, half-forgotten mining town, where the silver was dug out of the mountain and shipped away, and nobody made anything of it. Encouraged, the story goes, by the American ambassador, Dwight Morrow, he opened a workshop in 1931, hired local craftsmen, and began designing silver jewellery and tableware inspired by pre-Hispanic art. His designs became a sensation in the United States, and his apprentices opened workshops of their own, until Taxco had hundreds. Within a generation the town had become the silver capital of Mexico, and Spratling, who died in a car crash in 1967, is remembered as the father of Mexican silver.`,`The Nahua town of Tlachco, the place of the ball game, stood near here before the Spanish conquest, and its people mined metals and paid tribute to the Aztecs. Soon after the conquest, Hernán Cortés's men found tin and silver in the mountains, and in the 1530s a mining town was established. Its greatest boom came in the eighteenth century, when a French-born miner named José de la Borda struck a fabulously rich vein of silver. Borda became one of the richest men in New Spain, and between 1751 and 1758 he built the church of Santa Prisca, one of the masterpieces of Mexican Baroque, with its riot of gilded altarpieces. His motto, it was said, was God gives to Borda, and Borda gives to God.`,`After Borda, the mines declined, and by the early twentieth century Taxco was a quiet village. A new highway from Mexico City to Acapulco, which passed through the town in the late 1920s, brought travellers, and Spratling's silver brought the world. Taxco was protected as a historic town, and its building rules preserved its whitewashed houses and red roofs. It is named Taxco de Alarcón, after the playwright Juan Ruiz de Alarcón, said to have been born here around 1581, one of the great writers of Spain's Golden Age.`],deepTitle:`Borda's silver`,deep:[`Santa Prisca's façade of pink stone is covered with carved saints, columns and cherubs, and its interior holds twelve gilded altarpieces in the extravagant Churrigueresque style. Borda's fortune did not last: he was later forced to sell a golden monstrance he had given the church to pay his debts, before striking it rich again in the mines of Zacatecas.`,`Taxco's National Silver Fair, held each year, draws silversmiths from across Mexico, who compete for prizes for the finest jewellery and designs. The town's hundreds of silver shops and workshops range from tiny family businesses to grand showrooms, and a market of silver stalls fills the streets on Saturdays.`,`On the first Monday after the Day of the Dead, the people of Taxco climb the hill of Huixteco to collect jumiles, small stink bugs that live in the oak forest, and eat them, sometimes alive, with salsa in tacos. The jumil is believed to carry the spirit of the ancestors, and its taste is said to be like cinnamon or mint.`,`A cable car carries visitors up from the edge of town to the hotel of Monte Taxco, with views over the red rooftops, and a giant statue of Christ on the hill of Atachi, above the town, offers another panorama. The white Volkswagen Beetle taxis, called vochos, are among the last of their kind, still climbing the steep, narrow streets where larger cars cannot go.`]},"puebla-mx":{why:`A basin at 2,150 m east of Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl sits in the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt’s rain shadow and rain catch at once. Summer monsoon waters the oak–pine farms; winter inversions trap urban air under cobalt dry-season skies. Ash is an episodic neighbor, not a metaphor. Mild highs near 28 °C and winter nights near 5 °C are the highland default. The volcanoes are the western wall of the climate.`,immersive:`Popocatépetl’s plume, when it is working, is Puebla’s western clock. The city at 2,150 m takes the edge off 19 °N: summer afternoons near 25–28 °C, winter nights that can near 5 °C, and about 814 mm of rain packed into the monsoon. Dry-season mornings can be inversion-grey even when the volcano is white. Talavera tiles flash in a light that is highland-sharp. Volcanic soils still grow maize and flowers in the hinterland while the centro breathes traffic. Students and factories share a springlike mean that winter smog and ashfall can cancel for a week at a time.`,feel:`Puebla feels like a city glazed in tile. Its colonial heart, one of the grandest in Mexico, is a grid of churches, convents and mansions decorated with bright Talavera tiles in blue, yellow and white, set among red brick and white stucco, and above it rise the twin towers of a massive cathedral. Beyond the city, three great volcanoes line the horizon, among them the smoking Popocatépetl. At about 2,150 m, the air is clear and cool; winters are dry, sunny and frosty at night, spring warm, and summer brings afternoon thunderstorms and cool, damp evenings.`,seasons:{winter:`Dry, sunny and crisp — afternoons near 23°C, nights dropping to about 5°C, and clear skies that show off the snowy volcanoes on the horizon. Frost touches the outskirts on the coldest mornings, and the city is busy with Christmas and Epiphany celebrations.`,spring:`Warm and dry — afternoons near 28°C, the warmest of the year, with hazy skies and the jacarandas blooming purple. Thunderstorms begin to break in May, and on 5 May the city celebrates its most famous victory with a parade.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 25°C, nights around 11°C, and thunderstorms most afternoons, often with hail. The countryside is green, and the evenings cool and fresh. In August the first chiles en nogada appear in the restaurants.`,autumn:`Mild and drying — afternoons near 24°C, the rains easing in October, and clear, cool days returning by November, when the city's markets fill with marigolds and sugar skulls for the Day of the Dead.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Talavera-tiled churches and mansions, the gilded Rosary Chapel, mole poblano and chiles en nogada, the Great Pyramid of Cholula next door and views of the smoking Popocatépetl.`,residentFit:`It suits students, professionals, factory workers and families who want a big, historic, affordable city with a cool highland climate, and who accept smog, traffic and the occasional fall of volcanic ash.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mexico City (≈105 km NW, ≈90 m higher)`,note:`Over the volcanoes in the Valley of Mexico, the capital has a very similar highland climate, with more smog and a larger urban heat island.`},{label:`Atlixco (≈30 km SW, ≈300 m lower)`,note:`In a warmer valley at the foot of Popocatépetl, Atlixco has a milder climate, famous for its nurseries and flower gardens.`}],history:[`On the morning of 5 May 1862, a French army of some 6,000 men, among the best soldiers in the world, marched up to the hills outside Puebla, confident of an easy victory. The French had invaded Mexico to collect its debts and, the emperor Napoleon III hoped, to install a friendly monarchy. Defending the city was a smaller, poorly equipped Mexican army under General Ignacio Zaragoza, entrenched in the forts of Loreto and Guadalupe. Three times the French stormed the hills, and three times they were thrown back. That evening they retreated. Among the Mexican officers was a young general named Porfirio Díaz, who would later rule Mexico for three decades. The victory did not win the war, for the French returned the following year and took the city, but the Fifth of May, Cinco de Mayo, became a symbol of Mexican resistance, celebrated today more widely in the United States than in Mexico.`,`Puebla was founded on 16 April 1531 on an empty plain between the Indigenous cities of Tlaxcala and Cholula, as a town for Spanish settlers. Legend says that its site was revealed to Bishop Julián Garcés in a dream, in which angels laid out the streets with ropes, and it was named Puebla de los Ángeles, the town of the angels. It grew into the second city of New Spain, rich from farming, textiles and trade along the road between Veracruz and Mexico City. Its craftsmen, taught by potters from Talavera de la Reina in Spain, developed the colourful glazed pottery called Talavera, and its convents created some of Mexico's most famous dishes.`,`In November 1910 a Puebla shoemaker named Aquiles Serdán and his family, who had been plotting against the dictator Porfirio Díaz, fought a gun battle with the police at their house, two days before the revolution was due to begin. Serdán was killed, but the revolution followed. In the twentieth century Puebla became an industrial city, and in 1967 Volkswagen opened a huge factory there, which built the last of the original Beetles in 2003. The historic centre was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, and today Puebla is Mexico's fourth-largest city.`],deepTitle:`More of the story`,deep:[`Mole poblano, the rich, dark sauce made with chillies, chocolate, spices and nuts, is said to have been invented by the nuns of the convent of Santa Rosa in Puebla, who created it in a hurry to honour a visiting viceroy or archbishop. Another Puebla dish, chiles en nogada, green chillies stuffed with meat and fruit, covered with a white walnut sauce and sprinkled with red pomegranate seeds in the colours of the Mexican flag, is said to have been created for the independence hero Agustín de Iturbide in 1821.`,`The Rosary Chapel, in the church of Santo Domingo, completed in 1690, is one of the most dazzling interiors in the Americas, every surface covered with gilded plaster, carvings and sculptures. In its day it was called the eighth wonder of the world.`,`The Great Pyramid of Cholula, just west of Puebla, is the largest pyramid in the world by volume, bigger than the Great Pyramid of Giza, though it now looks like a grassy hill, crowned by a colonial church built by the Spanish on its summit. Tunnels dug by archaeologists run through its interior.`,`Popocatépetl, the smoking mountain, rises about 45 km west of Puebla to more than 5,400 m, and it is one of the most active volcanoes in Mexico. Since it reawakened in 1994, it has regularly puffed out clouds of ash, which sometimes dust the streets of Puebla and close its airport.`]},"ajijic-lake-chapala-mx":{why:`Lake Chapala’s thermal mass, 1,530 m of elevation, and a mountain wall on the north shore combine to trim tropical heat and blunt night extremes. The lake is warmer and less urban than Guadalajara, less dry than the Bajío, and far less cold than Mazamitla’s pine belt. Afternoon storms in the wet season green the Sierra de San Juan Cosalá. Water quality and lake level are part of the climate, not a separate civic topic.`,history:[`In May 1923 the English novelist D. H. Lawrence and his wife Frieda rented a house in the lakeside town of Chapala, a few kilometres east of Ajijic, and Lawrence sat under the trees by the water every morning writing the first draft of a novel about a revolution in Mexico led by the reborn Aztec gods. Published in 1926 as The Plumed Serpent, it is one of his strangest books. Lawrence stayed only a couple of months, but he was not the last writer or artist to fall for the light on Lake Chapala. Twenty years later an American adventurer and travel writer named Neill James, recovering from an injury, came to Ajijic, then a village of fishermen and farmers with dirt streets and no electricity, and stayed for the rest of her life. She wrote a book about it, Dust on My Heart, and the foreigners began to follow.`,`Ajijic, from a Nahuatl name meaning the place where the water springs forth, stands on the north shore of Lake Chapala, the largest lake in Mexico, in a land where the Coca and other peoples fished the lake and farmed its shores for centuries. Franciscan friars arrived in the 1530s, and the village grew up around its chapel and plaza, living on fishing, farming and weaving. In the early twentieth century the president, Porfirio Díaz, spent holidays at Chapala, and wealthy families from Guadalajara built lakeside villas there, but Ajijic remained a quiet village.`,`Neill James helped start a local embroidery and weaving industry, opened a library and a school of art, and after her death left her house and garden to the Lake Chapala Society, which became the heart of a growing community of foreign residents. From the 1950s, American and Canadian retirees, drawn by the mild climate, the low cost of living and the beauty of the lake, settled in Ajijic and the nearby villages, which now host one of the largest communities of foreign retirees in the world. The village has kept its cobbled streets, colourful murals and festivals, though rising prices and traffic have changed it, and the lake itself has suffered from pollution and falling water levels.`],deepTitle:`Lawrence's lake`,deep:[`Lake Chapala, about 80 km long but only a few metres deep on average, supplies much of the drinking water for Guadalajara, Mexico's second city. In the drought of the early 2000s, it shrank alarmingly, its shoreline retreating hundreds of metres, before heavy rains refilled it. Water hyacinth, a floating weed, periodically chokes its shores.`,`Every winter, flocks of American white pelicans fly south from Canada and the northern United States to spend the season on Lake Chapala, and they gather in huge numbers near the town of Petatán, on the southern shore, where fishermen feed them the remains of their catch.`,`Ajijic's streets are decorated with murals painted by local and foreign artists, depicting the history, legends and wildlife of the lake. The village's patron saint, Saint Andrew, is celebrated at the end of November with nine days of processions, fireworks and dances.`,`Lake Chapala's mild climate, often described as one of the best in the world, comes from its altitude, about 1,500 m above sea level, and from the lake itself, which tempers the heat of the day and the cold of the night. The dry season, from November to May, is sunny and warm, and the rainy season brings afternoon thunderstorms and green hills.`]},"queretaro-mx":{why:`Highland elevation near 1,820 m on the Bajío’s dry edge yields bright winters, large day-night swings, and only about 484 mm of rain, most of it in afternoon storms. That is drier and often warmer than Mexico City, less humid than the lowland tropics. Urban growth now stores nighttime heat and loads winter air with traffic. The BSk/Cwb label is the average; the lived pattern is a fast-growing basin modifying its own nights.`,immersive:`Clear dry-season light is Querétaro’s first impression: sharp shadows on cantera, laundry that dries by noon, nights that fall toward 6–7 °C. Summer afternoons near 28–30 °C break when storms build, then the streets smell of wet dust and strained drains. Annual rain is only about 484 mm, so the green is seasonal and the hills go beige again. Juriquilla’s lawns are an irrigated climate, not the Bajío’s. The centro’s walkable core still belongs to a highland city; the ring road belongs to a heat-island metro. Services are unusually complete for this dryness. Water is the quiet argument.`,feel:`Querétaro feels like a colonial city that went to work in a factory and did very well out of it. Its historic centre is a calm, prosperous grid of pink-stone churches, Baroque mansions, shady plazas and pedestrian streets, crossed by a monumental aqueduct, while all around it sprawl new suburbs, industrial parks and aerospace plants. At about 1,800 m on the edge of the central plateau, the air is dry and the sun bright; winters are mild and sunny, spring is the warm season, and summer brings afternoon thunderstorms.`,seasons:{winter:`Dry, sunny and mild — afternoons near 24°C, nights cooling to about 7°C, and clear blue skies almost every day. The evenings are chilly enough for a jacket, and the plazas glow with Christmas lights.`,spring:`Warm and dry — afternoons climbing to about 30°C by May, the warmest of the year, with hazy skies, dusty winds and jacarandas blooming purple along the avenues. The first storms arrive in late May.`,summer:`The rainy season — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 14°C, and thunderstorms most afternoons, sometimes with hail. The surrounding hills turn green, and the evenings are fresh and cool.`,autumn:`Mild and clear — afternoons near 26°C, the rains ending in October, and crisp, sunny days returning by November, with cooler nights.`},contrasts:[{label:`San Miguel de Allende (≈50 km NW, ≈90 m higher)`,note:`The artists' town in the hills has a very similar highland climate, a touch cooler at night.`},{label:`Jalpan de Serra (≈120 km NE, ≈1,050 m lower)`,note:`Down in the Sierra Gorda, Jalpan is hot and humid, surrounded by tropical forest, a world away from the dry plateau.`}],history:[`At dawn on 19 June 1867, Maximilian of Habsburg, the Austrian archduke who had been installed as emperor of Mexico by the French, was led up the Cerro de las Campanas, the hill of the bells, outside Querétaro, with two of his generals, Miguel Miramón and Tomás Mejía. Abandoned by the French army, he had been besieged in the city for months by the republican forces of Benito Juárez, and captured when it fell. Before the firing squad, Maximilian gave each soldier a gold coin and asked them to aim at his heart, not his face, so that his mother could recognise him. His last words were reported as a hope that his blood would bring peace to Mexico. A small chapel built by the Austrian government now stands on the spot, beside a giant statue of Juárez.`,`Querétaro was founded, according to tradition, in 1531, when an Otomí leader named Conín, baptised Fernando de Tapia, allied with the Spanish to subdue the Chichimec peoples of the region. Legend says that the battle was fought with fists, not weapons, and that it ended when the apostle Saint James appeared in the sky during an eclipse. The town grew into a rich centre of textiles and trade on the road to the silver mines of the north, and in the eighteenth century it built splendid churches and an aqueduct of 74 arches. In 1810 Querétaro was the centre of a conspiracy for independence, and when the plot was discovered, Josefa Ortiz de Domínguez, the wife of the town's magistrate, managed to warn the conspirators, who launched the revolt early.`,`The city played a central role in Mexican history again and again. In 1848 the Mexican congress, meeting in Querétaro, ratified the treaty that ended the war with the United States and gave up half of Mexico's territory. In 1917 the constitution that still governs Mexico was signed in its theatre. In 1996 the historic centre was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and in recent decades Querétaro has become one of the fastest-growing cities in Mexico, home to car, appliance and aerospace factories, including plants that build parts for airliners.`],deepTitle:`An emperor's end`,deep:[`The aqueduct of Querétaro, built between 1726 and 1738, carried water from springs in the hills to the city across 74 stone arches, some of them more than 20 m high. It was paid for by the Marquis of the Villa del Villar del Águila, and legend says he built it out of love for a nun of the convent of Santa Clara, who had complained of the city's bad water.`,`Josefa Ortiz de Domínguez, known as La Corregidora, was locked in her room by her husband, the magistrate, when the conspiracy was discovered, but, according to legend, she stamped on the floor to signal to a trusted messenger to ride and warn the rebels. Her warning reached Miguel Hidalgo in Dolores, who launched the war of independence on 16 September 1810.`,`The Peña de Bernal, about 60 km northeast of Querétaro, is one of the largest monoliths in the world, a huge tower of volcanic rock rising above a small, colourful village. Climbers scale its sheer walls, and the village is known for its wool weavers and its sweets.`,`Querétaro's vineyards and cheese makers, in the valleys around Tequisquiapan and Ezequiel Montes, form a wine and cheese route that has become popular with weekend visitors from Mexico City, and the region is known for its sparkling wines, which suit the cool highland nights.`]},"durango-mx":{why:`Interior elevation near 1,880 m east of the Sierra Madre Occidental keeps nights crisp and winters capable of near-freezing mornings, while summer storms build off the sierra in a true monsoon-edge pattern. Rainfall near 435 mm is less than the Bajío’s wetter pockets and far less than the Gulf slope. Basin inversions stain winter dawns. Mazatlán, a few hours west and down, is humid Pacific; this is highland dry Mexico, not a tropical capital that happens to be inland.`,immersive:`Durango’s plateau light is hard and clean, the kind that makes distant sierra look closer than it is. At 1,880 m, summer afternoons can reach the low 30s °C before a monsoon cell dumps on one barrio and misses the next. Winter nights near 2 °C are ordinary; frost is not a surprise. About 435 mm of rain falls in a few stormy months, leaving a long bright dust season. The Guadiana valley ponds cold air on still mornings. Pine-oak starts west toward the Tepehuán highlands; the city itself is grassland-and-scrub elevation. Drought is the slow hazard. Thunder is the summer one.`,feel:`Durango feels like the set of a Hollywood western, which is exactly what it has often been. The capital of the state of the same name sits on a high, dry plateau on the eastern side of the Sierra Madre Occidental, surrounded by plains of grass, mesquite and cactus under a vast blue sky, and its stately colonial centre, built on silver and iron, is anchored by a great Baroque cathedral. At about 1,880 m, the air is thin and dry; winters bring sunny days and frosty nights, spring is windy and dusty, and summer is the season of thunderstorms.`,seasons:{winter:`Sunny, dry and cold at night — afternoons near 22°C, but nights dropping to about 3°C and often below freezing, with frost on the fields. Snow falls occasionally, especially in the Sierra Madre to the west, which can whiten the peaks.`,spring:`Warm, dry and windy — afternoons climbing to about 31°C by May and June, the warmest time of year, with dust storms blowing across the plains and almost no rain. The countryside is parched and brown.`,summer:`The monsoon season — afternoons near 29°C, nights around 14°C, and thunderstorms building over the sierra most afternoons from July, bringing most of the year's rain. The plains turn green, and the air is fresh after the storms.`,autumn:`Mild and clear — afternoons near 26°C, the rains ending in September and October, and crisp, cool nights returning by November, when the first frosts arrive.`},travelerFit:`Visitors come for the Hollywood western sets on the plains, the cathedral and colonial centre, the pine forests of the Sierra Madre and the spectacular highway and Baluarte Bridge to Mazatlán.`,contrasts:[{label:`Mazatlán (≈180 km SW, ≈1,880 m lower)`,note:`Over the Sierra Madre on the Pacific coast, Mazatlán is hot and humid, with tropical warmth all year.`},{label:`El Salto (≈80 km W, ≈700 m higher)`,note:`Up in the pine forests of the Sierra Madre, the logging town of El Salto is much colder, with hard frosts and occasional heavy snow.`}],history:[`In 1954 a Hollywood film crew arrived in Durango to shoot a western called White Feather, drawn by the landscapes of the region: vast plains, rocky canyons, pine-covered mountains and crumbling haciendas, under a sky almost always clear. Others followed, and over the next few decades more than a hundred films were made around the city. John Wayne came to make several of his late westerns, including The Sons of Katie Elder and Chisum, and liked it so much that he bought land here. Hollywood built entire western towns on the plains, some of which still stand, and visitors can watch staged gunfights on their dusty streets. Durango still calls itself the land of cinema.`,`The Tepehuán and other peoples lived in the valleys and mountains before the Spanish arrived. In 1552 a Spanish captain named Ginés Vázquez de Mercado led an expedition north in search of a legendary mountain of silver. He found a mountain, but it was made of iron, and he died soon after of wounds received in a fight with Indigenous warriors. On 8 July 1563 a young Basque captain, Francisco de Ibarra, founded a town near the mountain and named it Durango, after his home town in the Basque Country, and it became the capital of the vast province of Nueva Vizcaya, which stretched across much of northern Mexico. In 1616 the Tepehuán rose in one of the largest Indigenous revolts in the colonial north, burning missions and killing settlers.`,`Silver mines in the surrounding mountains made Durango rich in the colonial era, and its cathedral, palaces and churches were built with their wealth. In 1878 a boy named Doroteo Arango was born on a hacienda in the state of Durango; as Pancho Villa, he became the most famous general of the Mexican Revolution. In the twentieth century, Durango lived on mining, logging and cattle, and since 2013 a spectacular highway across the Sierra Madre has linked it to Mazatlán and the Pacific coast in a few hours.`],deepTitle:`The land of cinema`,deep:[`Durango is famous across Mexico for its scorpions, called alacranes, which thrive in its dry climate. Some species are highly venomous, and the city's football team, souvenir shops and even its nickname, the city of scorpions, celebrate them. Visitors can buy key rings and paperweights with real scorpions set in resin.`,`The Cerro de Mercado, the mountain that Ginés Vázquez de Mercado mistook for silver, is a huge deposit of iron ore, and it was mined for centuries. It rises on the northern edge of the city, and its iron fed Mexico's first steel mills in the twentieth century.`,`The Baluarte Bridge, on the highway between Durango and Mazatlán, was the world's highest cable-stayed bridge when it opened in 2012, its deck more than 400 m above the river at the bottom of the gorge. The road crosses the Sierra Madre through dozens of tunnels and bridges, replacing an old, winding road known as the Devil's Backbone.`,`Pancho Villa, born Doroteo Arango, became an outlaw as a young man, according to legend after he shot a landowner who had assaulted his sister. As a general in the Mexican Revolution, he commanded the Division of the North, the most powerful army of the revolution. He was assassinated in Parral, Chihuahua, in 1923.`]},"orizaba-mx":{why:`Windward of the Sierra Madre Oriental and below Pico de Orizaba (Citlaltépetl), Gulf air is lifted into cloud and heavy rain. At 1,230 m the city is cooler than Veracruz port and far wetter than Puebla’s basin, with about 1,410 mm a year. Coffee and citrus mark the transition band. Flooding on the Río Blanco is the cost of that orographic gift. A few tens of kilometres change the entire vegetation.`,immersive:`Cloud sliding off Citlaltépetl is Orizaba’s ordinary sky. At 1,230 m on the wet side of Mexico’s highest peak, afternoons stay mild — highs near 29 °C are a hot day — and nights near 10 °C want a wall. Rain, about 1,410 mm, is not a brief thunderstorm hobby; it is a season that soaks streets and feeds the Río Blanco. Coffee on the slopes and citrus in warmer pockets are the agricultural translation of that cloud forest edge. Compared with Puebla’s dry light, this is a green, humid highland. Compared with the port of Veracruz, it is a relief. Flood marks on walls are part of the architecture.`,feel:`Orizaba feels like a green, rain-washed valley at the foot of a giant. The city lies in a fertile valley on the mountain slopes between the Gulf coast and the central plateau, surrounded by steep, forested hills, and to the north, on clear days, rises the snow-capped cone of the Pico de Orizaba, the highest mountain in Mexico. Rivers and springs run through the town, the air is soft and humid, and fine drizzle or heavy downpours fall much of the year. At about 1,230 m, the climate is mild, never very hot or cold, but rarely dry.`,seasons:{winter:`Mild, grey and drizzly — afternoons near 25°C on sunny days, nights around 10°C, and spells of chipi-chipi, the fine misty drizzle carried by the cold northern winds called nortes. On clear mornings the volcano gleams white over the city.`,spring:`Warm and gradually wetter — afternoons climbing to about 30°C by May, the warmest of the year, with the first heavy thunderstorms arriving in late spring. The valley is lush and flowering.`,summer:`Warm and very wet — afternoons near 28°C, nights around 15°C, and torrential rain most afternoons, sometimes from tropical storms sweeping in from the Gulf. The rivers rise, the forests drip, and the waterfalls on the hills roar.`,autumn:`Wet then misty — September and October bring heavy rains and the risk of floods and landslides, before the nortes return in November with cooler, drizzly weather.`},contrasts:[{label:`Córdoba (≈20 km E, ≈400 m lower)`,note:`Down the slope in the coffee and sugar country, Córdoba is warmer and steamier, with hotter summer afternoons.`},{label:`Pico de Orizaba summit (≈25 km N, ≈4,400 m higher)`,note:`The highest mountain in Mexico, at more than 5,600 m, is capped with glaciers and snow, and its summit is freezing all year.`}],history:[`In January 1907 the workers of the great textile mill at Río Blanco, just outside Orizaba, owned by French businessmen, were locked out by their employers after a wave of strikes across the country's mills. On 7 January, angry and hungry, they gathered outside the mill, and when the company store refused them credit, they looted and burned it. The government of Porfirio Díaz sent in troops, who opened fire on the crowds and hunted down strikers in the streets and hills, killing dozens, perhaps hundreds, of workers. The massacre of Río Blanco became a symbol of the injustice of the Díaz regime, and when the Revolution came three years later, the workers' demands helped shape the labour rights written into the constitution of 1917.`,`The Nahua town of Ahuilizapan, the place of pleasant waters, stood in this valley beneath the volcano the Aztecs called Citlaltépetl, the mountain of the star, and paid tribute to the Aztec empire. After the Spanish conquest, Orizaba became an important stop on the road between the port of Veracruz and Mexico City, where travellers paused to recover from the heat and fevers of the coast, and in the eighteenth century the Spanish crown made the region one of the few places in New Spain where tobacco could legally be grown. The tobacco factory, mule trains and sugar mills made the town prosperous.`,`In 1873 the new railway from Veracruz to Mexico City opened through Orizaba, and the rivers powered textile mills, making it one of the first industrial towns in Mexico. In 1896 the Moctezuma brewery was founded here, and the following year its German brewmaster created a beer called Dos Equis to celebrate the new century. In 1973 an earthquake damaged much of the region. Today Orizaba is a tidy, busy city, with a cable car that climbs to the summit of the Cerro del Borrego for views over the valley and the volcano.`],deepTitle:`Star mountain`,deep:[`The Pico de Orizaba, or Citlaltépetl, rises to more than 5,600 m, the highest mountain in Mexico and the third highest in North America, after Denali and Mount Logan. It is a dormant volcano, its glaciers shrinking fast, and it is climbed by mountaineers from around the world. It last erupted in the mid-nineteenth century.`,`The Palacio de Hierro, the Iron Palace, on Orizaba's main square, is a building of iron and steel prefabricated in Belgium and shipped to Mexico in pieces in the 1890s. It is often attributed to the workshop of Gustave Eiffel, the designer of the Eiffel Tower, and it now houses museums, including one devoted to beer.`,`On the night of 13 to 14 June 1862, a few weeks after the Mexican victory at Puebla, French troops climbed the Cerro del Borrego, the hill overlooking Orizaba, and surprised a Mexican force camped on its summit, routing it. The battle helped the French hold Orizaba as their base until they returned to take Puebla the following year.`,`Orizaba's rainy climate comes from its position on the slopes facing the Gulf of Mexico. Moist air from the sea rises up the mountains, cooling and condensing into clouds and rain, which fall on the valley for much of the year, while the high plateau beyond the mountains, in the rain shadow of the Pico de Orizaba, is dry and cold.`]}};function u(e,t){return{id:`${e}-site-history`,title:t.deepTitle,paragraphs:[...t.deep]}}function d(e,t){return(e??[]).some(e=>e.id===`${t}-site-history`||/(?:^|-)site-history$/.test(e.id))}function f(e,t){return!t?.deep.length||d(e.deepSections,e.id)?e:{...e,deepSections:[u(e.id,t),...e.deepSections??[]]}}var p={...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l};export{f as n,p as t};